{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "10 Summaz", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/2-chainz-10-summaz-lyrics", "genius_title": "10 Summaz", "genius_id": 215204, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "[Chorus]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This track isn’t Rick Ross rapping from his personal life in any way, he’s rapping through the eyes of a slave, moreover, through the eyes of Django.\n\nThe whole track, with exception from a couple of bars, is a visualization of a slave’s perspective and how he sees what’s going around him in this dark chapter of the history of African-Americans in America.", "annotation_id": "1488615", "url": "https://genius.com/1488615", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "“Oh, I’m gonna walk in the moonlight with you.”", "vault_lines": ["\"Oh, I'm gonna walk in the moonlight with you.\""], "meaning": "When the slavers wanted to kill a black man, they would take him for a “walk” in the dead of night so as not to get in any kind of trouble and alert other slaves. This was a threat to Django.", "annotation_id": "1417123", "url": "https://genius.com/1417123", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "[Intro] “Oooh, now you are one lucky nigga.” “You better listen to your boss white boy.”", "vault_lines": ["\"You better listen to your boss white boy.\""], "meaning": "A quote from the movie Django Unchained. One of Candie’s white cronies tells Django he is lucky for not being killed by the white men. Django replies with a shift of power by telling the white man to listen to his master.", "annotation_id": "1393901", "url": "https://genius.com/1393901", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "I seen a hundred women burn", "vault_lines": ["I seen a hundred women burn"], "meaning": "Rick Ross has seen lotsa bad shit happen to lotsa good people, many of whom are women.\n\nAs with the first verse’s bar, the song continues from a slave’s point of view. Ross may not have seen 100 women burn personally, but as a slave it’s VERY possible.", "annotation_id": "1348545", "url": "https://genius.com/1348545", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "What did she do to deserve? Put me on the farm", "vault_lines": ["What did she do to deserve? Put me on the farm"], "meaning": "Given the nature of the perspective, Django (Ross) is questioning the severity of punishment that a slave owner is doling out. It is not unreasonable to think that this is referencing the scene wherein the Brittle brothers punish Little Jody for breaking eggs by whipping her. In addition, this could also reference the scene where Django begs for the Brittle brothers to punish him in place of his wife.", "annotation_id": "1348538", "url": "https://genius.com/1348538", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "Bitch cause I’m coming", "vault_lines": ["bitch cause I'm coming"], "meaning": "In the movie Django Unchained this song plays over footage of Django arriving at the farm where his wife is being kept as a slave – he’s coming to get her.", "annotation_id": "1348413", "url": "https://genius.com/1348413", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "All I want is my woman, such a wonderful mother, (mama!)", "vault_lines": ["All I want is my woman, such a wonderful mother, (mama!)"], "meaning": "One of the plot-points of the film is that Django is looking for his beloved wife  Brunhilda Von Shaft", "annotation_id": "1341044", "url": "https://genius.com/1341044", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "Is you a cat or a mouse? Keep them rats out the house", "vault_lines": ["Is you a cat or a mouse? Keep them rats out the house"], "meaning": "Snitches get stitches or get killed. He doesn’t want the Rats/Mouse in the house. He wants the superiors aka Cats.\n\nAlso, clever wordplay and a cruel euphemism referencing black slaves. “Rats” are an inhuman and condescending reference to black slaves. In this line, a white slave owner is ordering that black slaves must be kept outside in the slave quarters and out of the house.", "annotation_id": "1268270", "url": "https://genius.com/1268270", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "Our revenge is the sweetest,", "vault_lines": ["Our revenge is the sweetest,"], "meaning": "2Pac once talked about the sweetness of revenge.\n\nAlso, in the context of the movie, Django is extremely eager to punish the white slavemasters once he joins Dr. Waltz as a bounty hunter. He takes pleasure in getting his revenge on the Brittle Brothers, who whipped his wife, Broomhilda.", "annotation_id": "1267620", "url": "https://genius.com/1267620", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "A lotta scars on my back,", "vault_lines": ["A lotta scars on my back,"], "meaning": "Ross is referring to the scars that especially are the ones that Brümhilda (Django’s wife) have on her back. They come from slave-owners and their employees whipping the slaves' back when they are working or when they are being punished.", "annotation_id": "1267611", "url": "https://genius.com/1267611", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "I see your praying to Jesus, but will that help ease the pain?", "vault_lines": ["I see your praying to Jesus, but will that help ease the pain?"], "meaning": "In the movie Django Unchained, there is a scene in which Django kills Big John Brittle, one of the notorious Brittle Boys at Big Daddy’s plantation. At the beginning of the scene, Big John is seen holding a Bible and quotes Genesis 9:2 (he even has torn pages of the Bible sewn to his shirt?!! You got that right, he is that religious!) before he begins punishing, by means of whipping, a slave girl Little Jody. Some slave owners believed that they were doing God’s work by enslaving-taming African people. Hence the reference to praying to Jesus — a religious Christian figure. At the end of the scene Django utters the classic quote\n\nI like the way you die, boy\n\n http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RzmgKmM77cg", "annotation_id": "1266439", "url": "https://genius.com/1266439", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "Yet I post on the block, look like I’m Big Daddy Cane", "vault_lines": ["Yet I post on the block, look like I'm Big Daddy Cane"], "meaning": "Big Daddy Cane refers to the sugar plantations, and the character Big Daddy who is also a slave owner in the film. Big Sugar and Big Oil are nicknames of their industries.\n\n\n\nThis also could a reference to Big Daddy Kane, the Grammy award winning rapper.", "annotation_id": "1266004", "url": "https://genius.com/1266004", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "Any questions, they hang ’em, better pray for Dj-Django", "vault_lines": ["Any questions, they hang 'em, better pray for Dj-Django"], "meaning": "Back in the day of the slaves, if you asked too many questions, you’d probably get hanged. So Ross is praying that they don’t hang Django\n\nIn the movie, Django tries to save his wife from being whipped, hanged, or worse", "annotation_id": "1265984", "url": "https://genius.com/1265984", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "12 gauge, shotgun, chest full of carbon (boom-boom)", "vault_lines": ["12 gauge, shotgun, chest full of carbon (boom-boom)"], "meaning": "The caliber of shotguns is measured in terms of gauge  or bore. The gauge number is determined by the weight, in fractions of a pound, of a solid sphere of lead with a diameter equal to the inside diameter of the barrel. So, a 10 gauge shotgun nominally should have an inside diameter equal to that of a sphere made from one-tenth of a pound of lead. By far the most common gauges are 12 (0.729 in, 18.5 mm diameter) and 20 (0.614 in, 15.6 mm), although .410 (= 67), 32, 28, 24, 16, and 10 (19.7 mm) gauge and 9 mm (.355 in.) and .22 (5.5 mm) rimfire calibres have also been produced.\n\nChest full of carbon= Carbon  is a chemical element. It is one of the many elements used to make bullets. A chest full of carbon would signify a chest full of bullets.", "annotation_id": "1264740", "url": "https://genius.com/1264740", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "Money on his head, bitch, I’m trying to make a fortune", "vault_lines": ["Money on his head, bitch, I'm trying to make a fortune"], "meaning": "A reference to Django/Schmidt’s bounty hunting exploits: before they go to track down Django’s wife, they spend the winter hunting down criminals with money [rewards] on their heads to make some money (‘trying to make a fortune’)", "annotation_id": "1264295", "url": "https://genius.com/1264295", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "I broke off the chains only the realest remain", "vault_lines": ["I broke off the chains only the realest remain"], "meaning": "Given that the perspective of this song is from that of Django, I think this is likely clever wordplay. Obviously, Django (Ross) is a freeman for all but the prologue of the movie. However, I do not think that this line examines only the physical chains of slavery. Despite his status as a freeman, I think he is examining both the psychological scars of slavery as well as the stigma of being a free black man in 1850’s America. As the narrator discusses later, the psychological scars inflict pain in his psyche long after he frees himself from bondage. Although Django broke free from his physical bondage, moreover, his status as a free man does not release him from his color in a racially divided America. Therefore, only the “realest” chains against freedom remain: 1850’s racism.", "annotation_id": "1264190", "url": "https://genius.com/1264190", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "I need a hundred black coffins for a hundred bad men A hundred black graves, so I can lay they ass in I need a hundred black preachers, with a black sermon to tell From a hundred black Bibles, while we send them all to hell I need a hundred black coffins, black coffins, black coffins (Oh, Lord!) I need a hundred black coffins, black coffins, black coffins (Oh, Lord!) Black coffins! (I need a hundred...)", "vault_lines": ["I need a hundred black coffins for a hundred bad men", "A hundred black graves, so I can lay they ass in", "I need a hundred black preachers, with a black sermon to tell", "From a hundred black Bibles, while we send them all to hell", "I need a hundred black coffins, black coffins, black coffins (Oh, Lord!)"], "meaning": "In the movie, Django starts working with Dr. Schultz as a bounty hunter, tracking down and killing wanted criminals (“bad men”) for the rewards. Later, Django gets the opportunity for revenge and sends a lot of bad men who have been abusing slaves “to hell”. The hook was written by Jamie Foxx himself, so it is more personal to the story of Django than if Rick Ross wrote it from the outside in.", "annotation_id": "1263986", "url": "https://genius.com/1263986", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "line": "In the middle of the night Killers coming for you life, all you wanna do is shine?", "vault_lines": ["Killers coming for you life, all you wanna do is shine?"], "meaning": "This is a reference to the “midnight walks” slave owners would take with slaves, to kill them.", "annotation_id": "1263943", "url": "https://genius.com/1263943", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Black Coffins", "status": "done", "targets": 18, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-100-black-coffins-lyrics", "genius_title": "100 Black Coffins", "genius_id": 100277, "source": "api"}
