{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "Underboss I’ll just play the holes like a golf course", "vault_lines": ["Underboss I'll just play the holes like a golf course"], "meaning": "Rozay plays with the P.A.M. holes just like Tiger Woods plays with golf holes (and, who am I kidding, Tiger plays with the other holes too)!", "annotation_id": "311765", "url": "https://genius.com/311765", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "Over hospital gown white", "vault_lines": ["over hospital gown white"], "meaning": "Another displays of Pusha’s epic double entendre’s about coke. He is saying how he brutally murders people for the best cocaine. But he uses the vivid imagery of a bloody red murder over the whitest coke. Any body who has been to a hospital can appreciate this metaphor explaining how pure and white his coke is.", "annotation_id": "299434", "url": "https://genius.com/299434", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "The feds’ dragnet couldn’t take nothin’ from me", "vault_lines": ["The feds' dragnet couldn't take nothin' from me"], "meaning": "The FBI’s best orchestrated efforts or dragnets cant even stop Pusha from slangin yayo. He proceeds to explain how he gets through a road block with a decoy car that smashes through like a “crash test dummy” clearing the path for his van packed with 100 bricks.", "annotation_id": "299432", "url": "https://genius.com/299432", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "Got her for cheap, compare the price", "vault_lines": ["Got her for cheap, compare the price"], "meaning": "Reference to The Clipse’s catchphrase/mixtape series “Got It 4 Cheap”", "annotation_id": "298987", "url": "https://genius.com/298987", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "Walked the mud jungle where they grinded out the leaves", "vault_lines": ["Walked the mud jungle where they grinded out the leaves"], "meaning": "The mud jungles in Bolivia and Colombia (and Peru, Ecuador, etc) are where workers pick the coca leaves used to make cocaine.", "annotation_id": "275066", "url": "https://genius.com/275066", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "The Iron Man Audi", "vault_lines": ["The Iron Man Audi"], "meaning": "The Audi R8 has been featured in every Iron Man movie as Tony Stark’s mode of transportation (When not saving the world as Iron Man, of course).", "annotation_id": "273390", "url": "https://genius.com/273390", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "Man when Blade died my city cried a hundred nights", "vault_lines": ["Man when Blade died my city cried a hundred nights"], "meaning": "When rapper Blade Icewood died in a drive-by, Detroit cried a hundred nights\n\nBlade Icewood Drive-by shooting\n\n\n\n“Cried for a hundred nights” is also a reference to Rick Ross’s “Hustlin”", "annotation_id": "266939", "url": "https://genius.com/266939", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "From where they rob Pintos and could afford Enzos", "vault_lines": ["From where they rob Pintos and could afford Enzos"], "meaning": "The city where people break into and steal from, or steal the actual Ford Pintos (relatively crappy and inexpensive cars) but with the money they make from illegal activities they could afford to purchase Ferrari Enzos (relatively high end expensive cars).", "annotation_id": "262575", "url": "https://genius.com/262575", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "Colombian necktie", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A Colombian necktie is a method of execution in which the victim’s throat is slashed (with a knife or other sharp object) and his or her tongue is pulled out through the open wound. It was a frequent method of killing during the period of Colombia called La Violencia.", "annotation_id": "262571", "url": "https://genius.com/262571", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "Low key, Barry White", "vault_lines": ["My nigga keep that low key, Barry White"], "meaning": "Barry White sang pretty low. however low key could mean he doesn’t keep a lot of cocaine on him. or also could mean he keeps a low profile so the cops don’t bust him\n\nBarry White is a singer, who sings in a low key. Sean, is referencing back to his previous line “Got her for cheap, compare the price.” by saying “My nigga keep that low key, Barry white.” Meaning: He get keys for a low price compared to others, due to his friend having that deal for him.", "annotation_id": "261965", "url": "https://genius.com/261965", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "My gesundheit could make a whole city sneeze", "vault_lines": ["My gesundheit could make a whole city sneeze"], "meaning": "“Gesundheit” means bless you in German. So the whole city would sneeze just to receive his blessing cause he’s that big and rich.\n\nThis also pertains to the nose candy that he’s pushing.", "annotation_id": "259453", "url": "https://genius.com/259453", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "Call ’em penny loafers, they a pretty penny too I’m tippin’ strippers but I call it penny pinchin’", "vault_lines": ["Call 'em penny loafers, they a pretty penny too", "I'm tippin' strippers but I call it penny pinchin'"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is expressing his wealth in describing his penny loafers (shoes) costing a pretty penny (large sum).\n\nTipping strippers is often associated with singles, whereas here Rick Ross is describing the singles and “penny pinching” meaning it’s use as a tip has no effect on his pocket. Once again describing his wealth through his clever wording.", "annotation_id": "259446", "url": "https://genius.com/259446", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "Triple beam dreams", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Rozay is recalling a time when he had aspirations of making it big with coke and/or rap.\n\n\n\nBehold, a triple beam scale, commonly used for weighing coke and money. ‘Tis handy for hustlers.\n\nAlso the name of a more recent song by Ross.", "annotation_id": "257783", "url": "https://genius.com/257783", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "Cause everybody knows keys open doors, but bricks open windows", "vault_lines": ["Cause everybody knows keys open doors, but bricks open windows"], "meaning": "Pulling the hook from the Clipse’s track “Keys Open Doors.\n\nDouble meaning built off the previous line: (1) The literal meaning pertains to the physical objects – keys are used open the doors while bricks are be used to open/break/bust through windows for a robbery. (2) The slang drug terms, “key” being a kilogram of cocaine aka “brick”.\n\nBeing a successful drug dealer or trafficker opens new doors and windows (sights) for the supplier.", "annotation_id": "257360", "url": "https://genius.com/257360", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "line": "The game a bitch, but at times she sweet like cantaloupe", "vault_lines": ["The game a bitch, but at times she sweet like cantaloupe"], "meaning": "The Rap game (or the crack game) is a bitch, because there is endless competition,hate, criticism. but at times the game is as sweet as Cantaloupe. Cantaloupe is a sweet fruit. The rap game is sweet at times because of the money, cars, jewelry, women etc.", "annotation_id": "249164", "url": "https://genius.com/249164", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Keys", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Big-sean-100-keys-lyrics", "genius_title": "100 Keys", "genius_id": 51372, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Million", "line": "We the best", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "We the best is the name of DJ Khaled’s second studio album. It has also turned in DJ Khaled’s catchphrase and he has used it many times in an intro, such as in We Takin' Over.", "annotation_id": "1739904", "url": "https://genius.com/1739904", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Million", "line": "Cash Money Millionaires (Millionaires) Cash Money Billionaires (Billionaires) Cash Money Trillionaires, we rich (Trillionaires, we rich) We ain’t never gon’ stop, never! (Stop, never) We got money nigga", "vault_lines": ["Cash Money Millionaires (Millionaires)", "Cash Money Billionaires (Billionaires)", "Cash Money Trillionaires, we rich (Trillionaires, we rich)", "We ain't never gon' stop, never! (Stop, never)"], "meaning": "The Cash Money Millionaires were a group consisting of Birdman, Juvenile, Lil Wayne, Mannie Fresh, B.G.. and Young Turk. Yeah.\n\n\n\nCash Money Millionaires? That’s old man. They’re never going to stop grinding, those millionaires are about to become trillionaires. Word. Regardless of this part being by DJ Khaled, I still want to make the Birdman “brrr” birdcall.\n\nhttp://youtu.be/dYDIPVd4l-Y\n\nThat’s better.", "annotation_id": "1437523", "url": "https://genius.com/1437523", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "100 Million", "line": "That 100 million dollar money", "vault_lines": ["That cash money, that 100 million dollar money"], "meaning": "This is in reference to the 100 million dollar check written to Cash Money Records in 2010.\n\nThe interview he said this during can be found here. http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yVp2ce0wz8k&feature=results_video&playnext=1&list=PL3E9AE04229BB3BFB", "annotation_id": "428434", "url": "https://genius.com/428434", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
