{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "800 Karats", "line": "Mozambique, hit up my dog, we diggin’ for some diamonds", "vault_lines": ["Mozambique, hit up my dog, we diggin' for some diamonds"], "meaning": "Diamonds are mined in certain regions of the country, although Mozambique is not one of the world’s largest diamond producers, unlike Botswana, South Africa, or Angola. Historically, diamond deposits in Mozambique have attracted investors and international companies, and control over them has sometimes been a factor in political and economic tensions.", "annotation_id": "37723871", "url": "https://genius.com/37723871", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "800 Karats", "line": "Rohan Marley, I’m smokin’ godly, pettin’ on my leopard", "vault_lines": ["Rohan Marley, I'm smokin' godly, pettin' on my leopard"], "meaning": "Rick Ross shouts out Rohan Marley, a Jamaican entrepreneur who is the son of legendary reggae artist Bob Marley. Like his late father, Rohan is a Rastafarian, a member of the Abrahamic religion of Rastafari. Rastafarians see cannabis or weed as a religious sacrament, believing it frees the mind with a euphoria. Ross claims he is “smoking godly,” perhaps an allusion to the religious ties Rohan and others associate with cannabis.\n\nRick Ross mentions petting a leopard in this line, a nod to his love of exotic animals. He notably owns an Atlanta compound with 280 acres of land that is home to bison, horses, and a bull. In a line on the intro track to Too Good To Be True, “SHAQ & KOBE,” Ross likewise acknowledges his menagerie:\n\nI got a pool of parrots and some buffalos\n\nIt is worth noting that he was also pictured petting a baby leopard in 2013:", "annotation_id": "30078380", "url": "https://genius.com/30078380", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
