{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "Accident murderer, act like you killed on purpose", "vault_lines": ["Accident murderer, act like you killed on purpose"], "meaning": "When I listen to this hook, I think of bystanders getting hurt and the shooter claiming the kills for intimidation alone. The shooter has a target in mind, but “his guns’ a virgin” speaks of inexperience and lack of control. Just blasting away, fingers crossed, hoping he hit his target. But in the end, his lack of control has led to collateral damage that’ll blast back. And by then the original target is lost. Now this freshly new murderer is caught up. Constructed his own demise by his design. Accident murderer, because if he really was about that life, he’d accurately and discreetly hunt down his target. Figuratively speaking of course 🤓", "annotation_id": "859745", "url": "https://genius.com/859745", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "[Verse 2: Rick Ross]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Rick Ross is the sole rap artist featured on Life Is Good.\n\nIn an interview with Vibe, Nas explained:\n\nI didn’t want a lot of people on the album since it’s been four years for me. Next album I’ll do more features, but on this one I didn’t want to have a lot of people on the album. I also have Mary J. Blige on vocals. I’m drawn to that soulful sound.\n\nPrior to this songs release the pair have collaborated on “Usual Suspects”, “Tower Heist” and “Triple Beam Dreams”. After this songs release they also collaborated on “One of Us” and “Powers That Be”.", "annotation_id": "13495786", "url": "https://genius.com/13495786", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "[Produced by No I.D.]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Accident Murderers” incorporates pipe organ in its production, and its lyrics addresses senseless violence with a rags-to-riches narrative by Rick Ross.\n\nThe producers from tracks 2 and 3 collaborates on track 4 here to bring us a perfect sound bed for Rick Ross’ feature. More piano, organs, choir and rap-cinema flavor, with our first true hook at 4 records in. It has an instrumental break after first hook which leads into verse 2 and the more patented story-telling Nas, a la “I Am”.\n\nWorth noting that despite several singing features (Victoria Monet, Mary J. Blige, the late Amy Winehouse), and Large Professor’s appearance on Loco-Motive, Rozay is the only guest RAPPER on the album.\n\nOn the reasons why he only had one guest rapper, Nas said:\n\nI didn’t want a lot of people on the album since it’s been four years for me. Next album I’ll do more features, but on this one I didn’t want to have a lot of people on the album. I also have Mary J. Blige on vocals. I’m drawn to that soulful sound.", "annotation_id": "2764386", "url": "https://genius.com/2764386", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "You ain’t mean to kill him", "vault_lines": ["You ain't mean to kill him"], "meaning": "Nas is subtly saying that these other killers had a code they lived by, and they weren’t afraid to strike when necessary.\n\nBut it’s different with these other guys– they’re unprofessional, killing by accident, and killing at any provocation.\n\nBasically– there’s a G-code that street violence is inherently sanctioned by, and new kids are just imitating it without having understood how it works. Violence is sometimes necessary, but these kids don’t have the wisdom to tell the difference.", "annotation_id": "1483542", "url": "https://genius.com/1483542", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "Seated in foreign cars, constantly getting weeded", "vault_lines": ["Seated in foreign cars, constantly getting weeded"], "meaning": "The kids that used to waste time by tagging everything have grown up to ride around in expensive cars and constantly getting high.\n\n“Constantly getting weeded” here also means that they were always being segregated against just because they were black. So the cops would “weed” them out from the rest.", "annotation_id": "917222", "url": "https://genius.com/917222", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "You cocked back You thought you had it planned, you thought you had your man He saw you coming, he ran when you tried to blast that man Missed him by inches, he sprinted Some of his boys on the corner was who your bullets entered Two of ’em pull through, but one didn’t, son’s finished", "vault_lines": ["Two of 'em pull through, but one didn't, son's finished"], "meaning": "The guy pulled his gun when he saw the guy he wanted to kill. In his mind, it was all gonna play out perfectly. But when his target saw him, he ran off, causing the guy to start shooting to try and get him before he gets too far.\n\nBut he missed the target and instead hit some nearby civilians. Two of the people the bullets hit survive, but one of them died.\n\nNas really sells the song’s concept here with language– we get an avalanche of ‘you’s,’ ‘your’s,’ ‘he’s,’ and ‘him’s’, confusing listeners trying to attach real people to these floating pronouns– a kind of parallel to a shooter trying to ‘pick out’ one man from a crowd to shoot at.\n\nbut what’s really cool is the slick, imagistic pun at the end of this little sequence… let’s do some poetic analysis:\nthe line that sets it up:\n\n“some of his boys on the corner was who your bullets entered”\n\nnotice the cluttered syntax–‘the boys was who the bullets entered’ instead of the much clearer ‘the bullets entered the boys.’ this twist isn’t just a trick to fit into a rhyme scheme (nas has plenty of internal rhyming here to spare, plus ‘sprinted’/‘entered’ isn’t even a great fit). looking at the next lines:\n\n“two of ‘em pulled through, but one didn’t/ son’s finished”\n\nit’s clear this was intentional, because now it’s unclear what “two” and “one” are referring to:\n\n\n“pulled through” seems to imply “survived,” ie, two boys survived, and “one” was “finished,” ie, “one boy died.” so we put together that there were three boys, all of whom got shot, and one of whom died. BUT:\n“pulled through” could have a literal meaning, ie, “two bullets pulled through [a body], and one bullet didn’t [pull through].” this interpretation is helped by the fact that “two of ‘em” refers more readily to the last noun in the previous sentence (“bullets”) rather than the noun at the front of it (“boys”).\nthe full picture for the second meaning plays off the first one– we’re focusing on the one boy who didn’t make it when two bullets passed cleanly through him and one didn’t.\n\n\n\nNas takes punning even further here by having the images themselves be visual puns as in a movie– we get a very cinematic sense of the chaos and moral ambiguity of street violence.", "annotation_id": "917122", "url": "https://genius.com/917122", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "We the mob, Bob Marley Marl’ing all through the night", "vault_lines": ["We the mob, Bob Marley Marl'ing all through the night"], "meaning": "This is a reference to both Bob Marley, the reggae legend, and Marley Marl, Queens legend and golden age hip-hop producer.\n\nBoth are well known smokers and use marijuana to sustain late night sessions.", "annotation_id": "912909", "url": "https://genius.com/912909", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "Think of my nigga, Drawz", "vault_lines": ["Think of my nigga, Drawz"], "meaning": "Clarence Shawn Williamson, a.k.a. Drawz or DU, was a Queensbridge street legend who was killed in 2011.  See an interview with him below:\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-XjwQv9Z2ac", "annotation_id": "868878", "url": "https://genius.com/868878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "Pay your tithes, stay alive, can’t be dodging my clique", "vault_lines": ["Pay your tithes, stay alive, can't be dodging my clique"], "meaning": "Tithing is the Biblical practice of dedicating a percentage of your income to God. In this case, Ross refers to the monetary tribute that other hustlers owe to his crew. If they pay their tithes when his clique comes to collect instead of trying to dodge them, they’ll stay alive.", "annotation_id": "864315", "url": "https://genius.com/864315", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "Can’t play with these little niggas, gangsta little niggas Can’t hang with these little niggas, they’re killing, they’re reckless", "vault_lines": ["Can't play with these little niggas, gangsta little niggas", "Can't hang with these little niggas, they're killing, they're reckless"], "meaning": "The use of “little” in these lines indicates that even kids are murderers, because they learn from their environment & just do what they see\n\nWhile most kids just “play” or “hang” out with their friends, these kids kill with their friends\n\nThis epistrophe is reminiscent of Jay-Z’s in Trouble. Whereas Jay-Z was condescending and threatening with it, Nas' attitude is more wary", "annotation_id": "862097", "url": "https://genius.com/862097", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "And for that girl who never made it home, shot in the dome How they gon’ kill that beautiful sister?", "vault_lines": ["And for that girl who never made it home, shot in the dome", "How they gon' kill that beautiful sister?"], "meaning": "Shout out to Rap Genius for getting this. This line is referencing “Jerome’s niece” from One Love:\n\nGuess who got shot in the dome-piece?\nJerome’s niece, on her way home from Jones Beach", "annotation_id": "859843", "url": "https://genius.com/859843", "state": "verified", "votes": 207, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "Addicted to wealth, never cold turkey to war", "vault_lines": ["Addicted to wealth, never cold turkey to war"], "meaning": "He’s obsessed with getting money, and is never gonna fully quit fighting\n\nAlso, in reference to the last line before, Marlboro = most well-known cigarette brand in the world. Cold Turkey line can be a play on the line before.\n\nPlay on Cold War and the country Turkey. Turkey was involved in the cold war and at the time a U.S. ally. Turkey is also 98% Muslim country, which goes with next line “that’s Insh'Allah”!", "annotation_id": "859630", "url": "https://genius.com/859630", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "Now hollow heads getting heated", "vault_lines": ["We grew up doing graffiti, now hollow heads getting heated"], "meaning": "Now guns are being fired (“hollow” refers to hollow point which means a type of bullet that has a lead core and a swagged jacket but leaves the front portion of the projectile exposed)\n\nIt’s also talking about how they grew up together “doing graffiti”, but the victim got shot because the shooter’s “hollow head” got “heated” and his anger made him pull the trigger.", "annotation_id": "859617", "url": "https://genius.com/859617", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "Some real killers, I think of Wayne Perry", "vault_lines": ["Some real killers, I think of Wayne Perry"], "meaning": "Wayne ‘silkk’ Perry is often thought of as the most dangerous hitman in history\n\n\n\nOr could Nas be referring to this Wayne Perry, recently elected National President of the Boy Scouts of America?\n\n\n\nYou decide…", "annotation_id": "859607", "url": "https://genius.com/859607", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Accident Murderers", "line": "Yo, for my nigga that got killed, got hit up Vodka spills on the concrete, light a Swisher, we miss ya", "vault_lines": ["Yo, for my nigga that got killed, got hit up", "Vodka spills on the concrete, light a Swisher, we miss ya"], "meaning": "Nas pays his respect to the victims of accident murders. Nas has previously dedicated a whole song to people who died for various reasons.\n\nAlso, swisha is slang for a Swisher Sweet cigar, commonly made into a blunt with marijuana.", "annotation_id": "859598", "url": "https://genius.com/859598", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
