{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "I’m what you used to be (Ha)", "vault_lines": ["I'm what you used to be (Ha)"], "meaning": "So he’s referring to how humans have always mixed different races of humans and there is no ‘pure white’ human like white supremacists religiously believe- if you go back far enough, every white man has a descendant who was black as Charles Darwin points out (but all black men have a far back ancestor who was white, skin colour is just an adaptation for survival to the climate). To me, this song is a diss towards capitalists like Donald Trump as it is about Rick Ross (an African American descendant of slaves from the South) who has been through more oppression from the US state based on his ethnicity, taking the position of capitalist and ‘whipping’ his own ‘work’ like Nike, taking the religious, Christian idea of God back from the American ruling class, declaring that he ‘built it ground up’ & American capitalists just ‘bought it renovated’, Rick Ross is a gangster like any other capitalist, infact Ross is less so one yet Rick Ross is much cooler than most cowardly billionaires.", "annotation_id": "33057369", "url": "https://genius.com/33057369", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "Funny, you claimin’ the same bitch that I’m penetrating", "vault_lines": ["Funny, you claimin' the same bitch that I'm penetrating"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is saying that he can have sex with any girl he chooses. While the average man might try to date (or claim) a certain girl, Rick Ross is of such a high status, that he can convince that girl to have sex with him even though she is dating that man.\n\nThis line portrays the same theme that Ross uses in the previous few lines: Rick Ross is superior to you (the listener/some average male). He says previously, “I built it ground up, you bought it renovated.” His point is that his stuff is superior thatn the average person, built custom made “from the ground up,” like a house, rather than just renovated from a previous owner.\n\nJust like his house is better than the average guy, his sex life is better, allowing him to get any girl, even one who another guy is already claiming.", "annotation_id": "19980220", "url": "https://genius.com/19980220", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "And it’s so white, I just might charge you double", "vault_lines": ["And it's so white, I just might charge you double"], "meaning": "100% pure cocaine can be “ stepped ” on or diluted and doubled in value sometimes several times if its pure enough. Ross is flexing his connection is so direct that it comes from the ground straight to him without any dilution to the product. So in practice he should charge you double for it.", "annotation_id": "15846891", "url": "https://genius.com/15846891", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "[Produced by Lex Luger]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This song’s electronic symphonic build up with the thundering orchestral timpani echoing in the intro not only foreshadows this song’s epic proportions, but sets up the stage for one of the most recited hooks of 2010.  In combination with Rick Ross’s husky voice, menacing horn section, schizophrenic hi-hat patterns, and trunk rattling 808’s and sub woofer bumping; its hard not to act ignorant to this 2010 hood anthem.\n\nThe song was produced by the Virginia beatsmith Lex Luger, who was still in his teens when he helmed the cut. He told MTV News:\n\nTo take something that a lot of people thought was nothing and to turn it into another dimension of sound was crazy to me. The reaction from people that I saw was crazy to me. That’s when I really knew this is what I wanted to do.\n\nThis video below shows the process that Lex Luger typically goes through in making a beat.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DZsiT2BX8Xc", "annotation_id": "2494265", "url": "https://genius.com/2494265", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "So fuck a nigga (Fuck a nigga), I’m self-made (Self-made)", "vault_lines": ["So fuck a nigga (Fuck a nigga), I'm self-made (Self-made)"], "meaning": "He doesn’t need anyone to help him. Hence the phrase “Self Made”\n\nRozay is promoting his MMG Group cuts Self Made Volumes 1 & 2", "annotation_id": "960558", "url": "https://genius.com/960558", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "Self-made, you just affiliated", "vault_lines": ["Self-made, you just affiliated"], "meaning": "Rick Ross established himself, while others relied on partnerships with established rappers to gain entrance into the Rap game.\n\nThis could be a shot at Jeezy, seeing as Jeezy was affiliated with the Black Mafia Family, which propelled him into rap stardom.\n\nThe line would go on to eventually become the kick-start to MMG’s Self Made compilation series.", "annotation_id": "385954", "url": "https://genius.com/385954", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "My Rolls Royce triple-black, I’m Geechee, ho", "vault_lines": ["My Rolls Royce triple-black, I'm Geechee, ho"], "meaning": "“I’m Geechee, hoe” as in I’m “country”, i.e. from the south.  Geechee is a term used often by those in the south to represent those who are direct descendants of african slaves and often used in a playful manner to describe those who speak in a dialect often not understood by northerners or most Americans for that matter. How do I know this? My dad’s family is Geechee.", "annotation_id": "194937", "url": "https://genius.com/194937", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "Look at yourself, now look at me (Me!)", "vault_lines": ["Look at yourself, now look at me (Me!)"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is making a reference to a popular online video meme.\n\nRoss wants his haters to compare themselves to Ross…because Ross will win that battle.", "annotation_id": "68452", "url": "https://genius.com/68452", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "[Chorus: Rick Ross]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“B.M.F.” actually stands for Black Mafia Family – the cocaine trafficking organization in which Big Meech was involved\n\n..but he obviously can’t just put that in the song, hence the “KFC”-style abbreviation", "annotation_id": "35960", "url": "https://genius.com/35960", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "Always gettin’ money, my nigga, crime pays (Crime pays)", "vault_lines": ["Always gettin' money, my nigga, crime pays (Crime pays)"], "meaning": "Possibly a play on the phrase “crime pays” in which Ross is saying that people make a lot of money through criminal acts rather than the original phrase used to denote one would pay a harsh penalty for committing such acts.", "annotation_id": "32865", "url": "https://genius.com/32865", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "A eighth of that girl’ll knock your mommy and your daddy off (You know that!)", "vault_lines": ["A eighth of that girl'll knock your mommy and your daddy off (You know that!)"], "meaning": "“EGG” = an ounce of “Hard” (crack cocaine)\n\n“GIRL” = “Cocaine” (‘70s slang)\n\n“KNOCK” = Kill", "annotation_id": "32204", "url": "https://genius.com/32204", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "And did I mention: guns from Red Dead Redemption? (Did I?) Nine-mils, fifty-clip extensions (A long clip)", "vault_lines": ["And did I mention: guns from Red Dead Redemption? (Did I?)", "Nine-mils, fifty-clip extensions (A long clip)"], "meaning": "Red Dead Redemption is a Western-themed video game made by Rockstar, the company most famous for the Grand Theft Auto games.  There are a lot of guns in it.", "annotation_id": "31512", "url": "https://genius.com/31512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "Blunt tip orange like caviar", "vault_lines": ["Blunt tip orange like caviar"], "meaning": "Referring to the color of his “blunt” when he is puffing on the reefer madness. Real caviar is black. He’s probably thinking of salmon roe, which is the domestic sparkling wine to black caviar’s champagne. Salmon roe’s still good though…", "annotation_id": "31505", "url": "https://genius.com/31505", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "Talkin’ plenty capers, nothing’s been authenticated", "vault_lines": ["Talkin' plenty capers, nothing's been authenticated"], "meaning": "Ross refers to rappers that mention criminal activities but haven’t really committed any.\n\nIt’s a bold line from a guy whose credibility has been questioned by Jeezy, 50 Cent, Kool G. Rap, Ice-T, Shyne, etc.", "annotation_id": "31499", "url": "https://genius.com/31499", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "Coke is like a mattress in the hood: I’m flippin’ on it (I’m flippin’)", "vault_lines": ["Coke is like a mattress in the hood: I'm flippin' on it (I'm flippin')"], "meaning": "To “flip” coke is to re-sell it (at a profit).\n\nAlso a reference to an older Lauryn Hill lyric:\n\nFlipping in the ghetto on a dirty mattress", "annotation_id": "31488", "url": "https://genius.com/31488", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "Wildin’ out, fishtailin’, Subaru, rally car (Fishtailin’)", "vault_lines": ["Wildin' out, fishtailin', Subaru, rally car (Fishtailin')"], "meaning": "The Subaru STI is a reasonably-priced, not particularly flashy car that provides very high performance for the money\n\n\n\nKind of an odd choice for a rapper\n\n“Fishtailing” is when a car’s rear wheels lose traction, resulting in oversteer.  The Subaru’s Variable Torque Distribution and Vehicle Dynamics Control systems are supposed to minimize that kind of thing", "annotation_id": "31486", "url": "https://genius.com/31486", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "I build it ground-up, you bought it renovated", "vault_lines": ["I build it ground-up, you bought it renovated"], "meaning": "Ross is noting that he’s created something new for the game and he built his music empire from the ground up. He didn’t do it the easy way like others who have ‘bought’ their success, or just ‘renovated’ what has already been established.", "annotation_id": "31482", "url": "https://genius.com/31482", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "Rozay—that’s my nickname", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The playful nickname “Ricky Rozay” is a pun on the French slang word, Rozay – Ricky’s favorite type of champagne.", "annotation_id": "31230", "url": "https://genius.com/31230", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "Them thirty-six holes leave you bleedin’, family (Bleedin’) Word to them thirty-six O’s in a kilogram (Ha)", "vault_lines": ["Them thirty-six holes leave you bleedin', family (Bleedin')", "Word to them thirty-six O's in a kilogram (Ha)"], "meaning": "Referring to the fact that if you’re in the drug game, dealing with that much weight, you’ll end up dead at some point (36 holes in you), and that ultimately your demise will be caused by the fact you’re selling drugs. In other words: you’ll end up dead, thanks to the drugs you’re selling.\n\nThere are 35.2739619 ounces in a kilogram, but that’s a lot harder to fit into a rhyme", "annotation_id": "30314", "url": "https://genius.com/30314", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "One nation under God", "vault_lines": ["One nation under God"], "meaning": "A play on words, in reference to Larry Hoover’s “God Body”. Also, BMF and GD were massive organizations, one nation.", "annotation_id": "30093", "url": "https://genius.com/30093", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "You can’t see a nigga,", "vault_lines": ["You can't see a nigga,"], "meaning": "Probably a reference to a phrase that his Lil Wayne uses on a regular basis. Wayne often says that people can’t see him because “the money in the way”. This concept is often used in rap to express how immensely successful the artist has become and cannot even be bothered by those with smaller bank accounts.", "annotation_id": "29679", "url": "https://genius.com/29679", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "Cocaine runnin’ in my big veins", "vault_lines": ["Cocaine runnin' in my big veins"], "meaning": "Whether selling it or snorting it, Ross is a master with the yayo.\n\nRoss might also be using some wordplay, meaning cocaine “running (shipping)” is something he grew up with, and that the cocaine is, literally, circulating through his veins.\n\nThe biggest vein in the human body is the vena cava", "annotation_id": "29674", "url": "https://genius.com/29674", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "Look at my timepiece It’s an Audem-eer, hundred racks, at least", "vault_lines": ["It's an Audem-eer, hundred racks, at least"], "meaning": "Audemars Piguet is a manufacturer of expensive Swiss watches. Ross' AP costs at least $100,000.", "annotation_id": "25967", "url": "https://genius.com/25967", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "(Ugh!) I got that Archie Bunker", "vault_lines": ["(Ugh!) I got that Archie Bunker"], "meaning": "Archie Bunker is a fictional character in the long-running and top-rated American television sitcom All in the Family and its spin-off Archie Bunker’s Place\n\n\n\nMore importantly, Archie Bunker was known for his pro-white racial stances. So… Rick Ross is referring to cocaine", "annotation_id": "25824", "url": "https://genius.com/25824", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "Larry Hoover", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Larry Hoover is the reputed leader of the Gangster Disciples gang in Chicago. He has been in prison since 1973.", "annotation_id": "25822", "url": "https://genius.com/25822", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "line": "I think I’m Big Meech (Ugh),", "vault_lines": ["I think I'm Big Meech (Ugh),"], "meaning": "Demetrius “Big Meech” Flenory is the founder of the Black Mafia Family (BMF), a multi-million dollar drug trafficking organization founded in Detroit. He is currently serving a 30-to-life sentence for operating a continuing criminal enterprise, and is scheduled for release in 2031.\n\n\n\nBMF also has a large hub in Atlanta and, in its guise as music promotion group BMF Entertainment, has a long association with the career of Young Jeezy.\n\nJeezy took exception to Ross' use of Meech’s name on this track and released two freestyle responses, one with a cameo by Meech himself.", "annotation_id": "25821", "url": "https://genius.com/25821", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "status": "done", "targets": 26, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-bmf-blowin-money-fast-lyrics", "genius_title": "B.M.F. (Blowin’ Money Fast)", "genius_id": 844, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "BCGMMG (Remix)", "line": "Real intelligent ish too, somethin’ niggas’ll pay for", "vault_lines": ["Real intelligent ish too, somethin' niggas'll pay for"], "meaning": "A reference to his mixtape, Lincoln Way Nights: Intelligent Trunk Music. It was such a good mixtape, it was on par with other artists' albums – it’s quality music that people would pay for.", "annotation_id": "1451156", "url": "https://genius.com/1451156", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "BCGMMG (Remix)", "line": "Your work high, I’m low key", "vault_lines": ["Your work high, I'm low key"], "meaning": "Double meaning: Meek is saying he is a low-key (ie laid back) person, and also that while his adversary’s drugs (also known as ‘work’) is high-priced (seen as being a sign of a low-status dealer), Meek’s carries a small price tag (a sign of clout as a drug dealer.) Here, ‘key’ is a play on ‘kilo’ or 1000 grams, which is a quantity of cocaine that is commonly used for trade.", "annotation_id": "1388202", "url": "https://genius.com/1388202", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "BCGMMG (Remix)", "line": "I started off with like four quarters and it was tie game, I went OT", "vault_lines": ["I started off with like four quarters and it was tie game, I went OT"], "meaning": "Started dealing drugs with an ounce (4 quarters = 1 oz) and when things got tied up on his block he went and sold drugs out of town (OT: out of town).", "annotation_id": "1037162", "url": "https://genius.com/1037162", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "BCGMMG (Remix)", "line": "Like the Re-Up Gang I had two clips", "vault_lines": ["Like the Re-Up Gang I had two clips"], "meaning": "Amazing word play by Meek: Re-Up Gang is affilated with the rap duo Clipse (clips), consisting of Pusha T and Malice. The clips Meek is referring to are guns.", "annotation_id": "833833", "url": "https://genius.com/833833", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
