{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Beautiful", "line": "My car is camouflage Speakin’ of camouflage", "vault_lines": [", my car is camouflage"], "meaning": "While in a place or in the places mentioned above the Game has his car camouflaged with other like cars making it difficult to find out his car up until he uses the car’s alarm system to unlock the car to get it.\n\n\n\nWhile talkin about camouflage the Game goes on to pay homage to his homey in the followin line.", "annotation_id": "4915368", "url": "https://genius.com/4915368", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Beautiful", "line": "I got a condo up in the sky, ’fore I fake it, I’d die Foreign bitch, no lie, man, that bitch beautiful", "vault_lines": ["I got a condo up in the sky, 'fore I fake it, I'd die", "Foreign bitch, no lie, man, that bitch beautiful"], "meaning": "A condo is short for a condominium: a luxurious, individually owned apartment. As such, condominium complexes have levels: Jeezy is talking about his lavish establishment being on the top floor. The next line “fore I fake it, I’d die” means that he is certain of his ownership rights and emphasizes that this condo is quite expensive. Foreign bitches in this case are women not of Jeezy’s native land and are normally attracted to rich condominium owners and rappers: they thus dwell in Jeezy’s apartment too and are in this way exquisite providers of amusement. The final line in this hook emphasizes how fantastic all of this is.", "annotation_id": "4048987", "url": "https://genius.com/4048987", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Beautiful", "line": "[Produced by Black Metaphor]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Black Metaphor sampled Cortex’s “Chanson D'un Jour D'Hiver,” a French jazz instrumental. He took the sample, sped it up, lowered the pitch, and looped it throughout the song.\n\nSample origin\n\nSamle usage\n\nThis sample was also used in Rick Ross' group, Triple Cs‘ song “Diamonds & Maybachs Pt. 2,” so Jeezy knew that Ross would sound good on it.", "annotation_id": "3983340", "url": "https://genius.com/3983340", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Beautiful", "line": "Came up in the projects, dreamin’ ’bout them mansions Wit’ the coke white walls, flow like bowlin’ balls", "vault_lines": ["Came up in the projects, dreamin' 'bout them mansions", "Wit' the coke white walls, flow like bowlin' balls"], "meaning": "Jeezy’s a well known d-boy and was in fact implicated in a cocaine-trafficking case.\n\nGrowing up poor on the projects instead of a big house he had his house walls filled with cocaine to sell, therefore the “white walls” pun.\n\nThe “flow” refers to how fast he would move the drugs, just as fast as bowling balls move.\n\n#SNOWMAN!", "annotation_id": "3953445", "url": "https://genius.com/3953445", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Beautiful", "line": "Meal ticket wrapped in plastic, that bitch beautiful", "vault_lines": ["Meal ticket wrapped in plastic, that bitch beautiful"], "meaning": "The “meal ticket” here is his gun. Meaning he gon' get the meals a.k.a money by any means possible.\n\nRick Ross had a similar line on “Triple Beam Dreams”.\n\nBrand new S Class with a meal ticket; Nigga cocaine white as Tommy Hilfiger.", "annotation_id": "3953430", "url": "https://genius.com/3953430", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Beautiful", "line": "Bury me on the left, right next to John Gotti", "vault_lines": ["Bury me on the left, right next to John Gotti"], "meaning": "John Joseph Gotti was an American mobster who became the Boss of the famous Gambino crime family in New York City.\n\nJeezy has been successful at dealing drugs that he feels like he deserves to be buried along such a real man.\n\nPeep Gotti’s legendary FBI mugshots :", "annotation_id": "3953414", "url": "https://genius.com/3953414", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Beautiful", "line": "Bought my first Caprice, put fifty in the motor", "vault_lines": ["Bought my first Caprice, put fifty in the motor"], "meaning": "The Chevrolet Caprice is a full-sized automobile produced by the Chevrolet Division of General Motors in North America. It was a common practice especially for rap lovers to put money in the hood or motor, meaning upgrading the car’s motor.\n\nThese days whoever as mentioned on his verse, Jeezy’s upgraded to much better cars that need no upgrading at all.", "annotation_id": "3953409", "url": "https://genius.com/3953409", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Beautiful", "line": "Ray Charles in these Ray Bans, why? Yeah, I touched them keys with both hands", "vault_lines": ["Ray Charles in these Ray Bans, why?", "Yeah, I touched them keys with both hands"], "meaning": "A smart metaphor here, Jeezy is referring to himself as the blind piano player and one of the greatest artists of all time — Ray Charles.\n\nA “key” is slang word for a kilogram of cocaine. Jeezy moves his drugs as smoothly as Ray used the play the piano keys.", "annotation_id": "3931713", "url": "https://genius.com/3931713", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
