{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bel Air", "line": "Bel Air, Fresh Prince Jazzy Jeff, forty bricks Uncle Phil, he was never there Now we got the baddest bitches laying everywhere Call your friends over, poetic justice Janet Jackson braids, time to show me something Introduce you to my cousin M Maybe we could shoot a film To all my Jewish friends, L’chaim", "vault_lines": ["Jazzy Jeff, forty bricks", "Uncle Phil, he was never there", "Now we got the baddest bitches laying everywhere", "Call your friends over, poetic justice", "Janet Jackson braids, time to show me something", "Introduce you to my cousin M", "Maybe we could shoot a film", "To all my Jewish friends, L'chaim"], "meaning": "Continuing the theme of Rozay living the life, as ‘The Fresh Prince’ did.\n\n\n\nHe alludes to many characters of the show in the hook, including Uncle Phil who acted as a dad for Will Smith. Rick Ross is saying that he didn’t have a father figure like Uncle Phil in his life. This is not the first time a rapper has alluded to Uncle Phil, J Cole did it too in Role Modelz.", "annotation_id": "7740065", "url": "https://genius.com/7740065", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bel Air", "line": "This is the very best of the best This is top shelf, the caviar of hip-hop Bel Air", "vault_lines": ["This is top shelf, the caviar of hip-hop"], "meaning": "Starting off the vibe of the song of Rozay being rich and prosperous, living his life.\n\nAlluding to the Fresh Prince of Bel Air, he talks about his life being amazing. He talks about him coming up from the ghetto from a poor lifestyle, as ‘The Fresh Prince’ did, to living the rich life.", "annotation_id": "7739794", "url": "https://genius.com/7739794", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
