{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Benediction", "line": "And I ran through the streets ’til my feet got tired ’Cause I ain’t wanna have my shoes on them power lines", "vault_lines": ["'Cause I ain't wanna have my shoes on them power lines"], "meaning": "He was trying to run from the drugs and the violence. Shoes hanging from power lines either means that drugs are sold there or a murder has occurred there.", "annotation_id": "2948097", "url": "https://genius.com/2948097", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Benediction", "line": "Started off in the streets We would take collection from the fiends", "vault_lines": ["We would take collection from the fiends"], "meaning": "August Alsina started off in the streets, or was born to the ghetto – he was born and raised for a while in the poorer parts of New Orleans, Louisiana. After the huge Hurricane Katrina devastation and disaster, he moved to Houston, Texas for a fresh start, also due to the fact that both his father and step-father were dealing with addition problems from crack-cocaine. It was then that his father died, and fights with his mother would occur to a point where he was eventually “kicked out”. On the last day of August, five years after he moved, his brother was shot and killed; resulting in moving to Atlanta, Georgia, soon after. He has been through much struggle, and much of his music revolves around these subjects. Similar to Drake, he started from the bottom, and now he’s here, and was raised by the streets, like Jay Rock.\n\n\n\nHere, August uses the religious theme of benediction to flip the meaning of collections, in which case would be tithes of a church, into substance/drug purchases from addicts to drug dealers.", "annotation_id": "2946625", "url": "https://genius.com/2946625", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
