{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch I’m From Dade County", "line": "Epidemic", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Epidemic Records is a record label founded by Hip-Hop Producers Cool & Dre in 2003. They have produced a lot of DJ Khaled work like:\n\n\nHolla at Me\nWatch Out\nNew York\nI’m On\nand much more", "annotation_id": "3169268", "url": "https://genius.com/3169268", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch I’m From Dade County", "line": "Cash Money", "vault_lines": [", Dope Ridas, Cash Money"], "meaning": "Cash Money Records is a record label found by Birdman (a person DJ Khaled is associated with) in 1991.\n\n\n\nOther acts include:\n\n\nAce Hood\nLil' Wayne\nDrake\nMavado\nand much more", "annotation_id": "3168546", "url": "https://genius.com/3168546", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch I’m From Dade County", "line": "Slip-N-Side", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Slip-n-Slide Records is a record label founded by Ted Lucas in 1994. The first person who was signed to the label was Trick Daddy, a person who DJ Khaled is associated with.\n\n\n\nOther acts include:\n\n\nRick Ross\nTrina\nPlies", "annotation_id": "3168505", "url": "https://genius.com/3168505", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch I’m From Dade County", "line": "Dade County", "vault_lines": ["Dade County, let it do what it do"], "meaning": "If they don’t say “305”, they say Dade County. Either way, they are repping where they are from", "annotation_id": "3166294", "url": "https://genius.com/3166294", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch I’m From Dade County", "line": "You are whatchu eat, young pussy, go brush ya teeth", "vault_lines": ["You are whatchu eat, young pussy, go brush ya teeth"], "meaning": "Playing on the old saying designed to get kids to eat healthy food. Foods with sugar in them are bad for your teeth, so presumably C-Ride’s calling you sweet (derogatory term meaning coward).", "annotation_id": "2195677", "url": "https://genius.com/2195677", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch I’m From Dade County", "line": "Dade County, you name it (whudd?!)", "vault_lines": ["Dade County, you name it (whudd?!)"], "meaning": "Dade County encompasses a major portion of Miami. This county includes many of the rough neighborhoods of Miami where Trina, Khaled, Ross, Flo-Rida, and Brisco all reside from. Check the crime data.", "annotation_id": "2076022", "url": "https://genius.com/2076022", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch I’m From Dade County", "line": "P-O-E", "vault_lines": ["Triple C's, 305, P-O-E"], "meaning": "P.O.E. Is an acronym for Pills Over Everything, referencing the Coriciden- an over the counter cough medicine- mentioned moments earlier.\n\nP-O-E could also be referencing POE Boy music group which Rick Ross used to be signed to.", "annotation_id": "1685094", "url": "https://genius.com/1685094", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch I’m From Dade County", "line": "Come on the opposite side of the beach", "vault_lines": ["Come on the opposite side of the beach"], "meaning": "Flo-Rida is referring to the other side of the McArthur Causeway from Miami Beach, where his home town Carol City and the rest of Miami’s historically black and inner city neighborhoods are located.", "annotation_id": "915277", "url": "https://genius.com/915277", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch I’m From Dade County", "line": "Like EA Sports, I’m in the game", "vault_lines": ["Like EA Sports, I'm in the game"], "meaning": "This is a reference to video game manufacturer EA Sports, which uses the slogan, “if it’s in the game, it’s in the game.”\n\nAlthough this probably refers to the rap game, Ross played football during his time at Carol City High School and signed a football scholarship with Georgia’s Albany State University, though he later dropped out without graduating.", "annotation_id": "838360", "url": "https://genius.com/838360", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch I’m From Dade County", "line": "Rest in peace to Uncle Al", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "DJ Uncle Al was a Miami based DJ who was murdered in 2001.", "annotation_id": "404908", "url": "https://genius.com/404908", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch I’m From Dade County", "status": "done", "targets": 10, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dj-khaled-bitch-im-from-dade-county-lyrics", "genius_title": "Bitch I’m From Dade County", "genius_id": 4363, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch, Don’t Kill My Vibe (Remix)", "line": "Rappers wanna be ballers, ballers wanna be rappers", "vault_lines": ["Rappers wanna be ballers, ballers wanna be rappers"], "meaning": "Two of the worlds largest sources of entertainment, Hip Hop music and professional basketball, are professions dominated by African Americans. From Kurtis Blow to French Montana, pictured here with JR Smith, they have always been linked.\n\nOne is not superior to the other – they feed, borrow, and inspire each other. There is a mutual respect between Rappers and NBA players because they have reached the pinnacle of their craft, besting the countless number of young men and beating the odds.\n\nAt the core, basketball and hip hop are the 2 most profitable “ways out” of innercity life. Why do these seem like the only 2 options? Use this link as a jumpstart on reading about “ways out”.\n\n\n\nFurther evidence of this partnership is found in basketball video games such as NBA Street vol. 1, 2, & 3.", "annotation_id": "1536681", "url": "https://genius.com/1536681", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch, Don’t Kill My Vibe (Remix)", "line": "I watched everything change in a blink of an eye", "vault_lines": ["I watched everything change in a blink of an eye"], "meaning": "There are moments in the life of a Bawse when everything changes instantaneously. Rozay is speaking of when in a home invasion, his best friend, P-Nut, was shot and killed. The death of Nut reminded Rozay of the transient nature of life on this planet and motivated Ross to grind even harder.", "annotation_id": "1524045", "url": "https://genius.com/1524045", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch, Don’t Kill My Vibe (Remix)", "line": "When the liquor endorsements worth more than your masters Here’s a toast to Ciroc,", "vault_lines": ["When the liquor endorsements worth more than your masters", "Here's a toast to Ciroc,"], "meaning": "It’s a crazy world when Ross makes more money off his Ciroc deal alone, than someone who earned a Master’s degree.\n\n\n\nAlso, perhaps a reference to the masters rights to one’s music. Most big label artists don’t own the “master rights” to their own music – but the label does. This means that don’t have full control over their music and cannot control where it licensed and published. Owning your masters is a big deal and usually not granted in major label deals, if at all.", "annotation_id": "1457280", "url": "https://genius.com/1457280", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch, Don’t Kill My Vibe (Remix)", "line": "Some how I pray for you niggas at the very same time See I was raised in the South, you helped your brother to shine", "vault_lines": ["Some how I pray for you niggas at the very same time", "See I was raised in the South, you helped your brother to shine"], "meaning": "Despite the fact that he wants his enemies to die, Ross prays for them. Deep down, Ross is a nice guy. This is “Father forgive them, for they know not what they do” kind of thing. And of course, we all know God Forgives….but Rozay doesn’t.\n\nHe was born in Coahoma County, Mississippi but raised in Carol City, Florida. Ross was taught Southern Hospitality, a stereotype of residents of the Southern United States as particularly warm, sweet, and welcoming to visitors & each other. Ross wants to see his brothers shine.", "annotation_id": "1457255", "url": "https://genius.com/1457255", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch, Don’t Kill My Vibe (Remix)", "line": "Used to call me dyslexic it was a challenge to read Mother held me at night until I finally achieved", "vault_lines": ["Used to call me dyslexic it was a challenge to read"], "meaning": "Ross noted in the Rolling Stone story that he struggled in school and got by “on his popularity”.\n\nWhile Ross was not dyslexic (a learning disability that impairs a person’s fluency or comprehension accuracy in being able to read), he had trouble reading so people thought he was dyslexic. His mother eventually helped him become the Boss he is now.", "annotation_id": "1457246", "url": "https://genius.com/1457246", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch, Don’t Kill My Vibe (Remix)", "line": "I die for my charm", "vault_lines": ["I had to struggle to make it, I die for my charm"], "meaning": "“Charm” refers to his pendant, but this is a double entendre:\n\n\nHe’ll die for his money/jewels.\nRoss has an MMG pendant, meaning he will die for his MMG comrades.", "annotation_id": "1457241", "url": "https://genius.com/1457241", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch, Don’t Kill My Vibe (Remix)", "line": "Soundtracks, straight up", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Rick Ross' music is the soundtrack to a Boss' Life. After all, he was named GQ’s Bawse of the 2012.\n\nThis could be a reference to his song “100 Black Coffins” featured on the Django Unchained Soundtrack.", "annotation_id": "1457232", "url": "https://genius.com/1457232", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch, Don’t Kill My Vibe (Remix)", "line": "Sometimes I look in the mirror I can’t believe I’m alive Disrespectful to niggas who wanna take me alive", "vault_lines": ["Sometimes I look in the mirror I can't believe I'm alive", "Disrespectful to niggas who wanna take me alive"], "meaning": "This freestyle was probably recorded before the shooting events on Rick Ross' birthday, but these lines seem to address that situation.\n\nRicky could be shocked he’s still alive for a couple reasons.\n\n• Ross suffered two seizures in October 2011, which he attributed towards his lack of sleep. He came out unscathed through his seizure fiasco.\n\n\n\n• Rozay is quite the rotund fellow, he himself maybe surprised he hasn’t keeled over from a heart attack or some other health issue.\n\n\n\n• On his 37th birthday, just several hours before this freestyle was released, Ross was targeted by a gunman and shot at 20 times. Miraculously, all shots missed but the gunfire did cause Ross did crash his Rolls Royce into a Fort Lauderdale apartment building.", "annotation_id": "1455666", "url": "https://genius.com/1455666", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Bitch, Don’t Kill My Vibe (Remix)", "line": "Wishing death on you niggas we never see eye to eye", "vault_lines": ["Wishing death on you niggas we never see eye to eye"], "meaning": "Ross is wishing death upon his enemies, whether that’s 50 Cent, Young Jeezy, his haters, or the gunmen who tried to shoot him down, he’ll never be cool with any of them. After all, God Forgives, Rozay Don’t.", "annotation_id": "1455664", "url": "https://genius.com/1455664", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
