{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Bottles", "line": "A nigga takin’ chains, Deebo (Gimmie that)", "vault_lines": ["A nigga takin' chains, Deebo (Gimmie that)"], "meaning": "Deebo, portrayed by Tom Lister Jr, is a fictional character from the 1995 movie Friday and its 2000 sequel Next Friday. In the films, Deebo is a thug who steals whatever he wants and doesn’t take “No” for an answer. When the guys see Deebo, they immediately hide all of their jewelry so he won’t steal it. This tactic doesn’t always work, as Deebo stole Red’s chain that his grandma gave him.", "annotation_id": "25661694", "url": "https://genius.com/25661694", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Bottles", "line": "Pitching hard ball and they hit it, call ’em Mark McGwire (Uh)", "vault_lines": ["Pitching hard ball and they hit it, call 'em Mark McGwire (Uh)"], "meaning": "Quavo refuses to meet in the middle when discussing deals. He compares the other party to former MLB slugger Mark  McGwire because they “hit” what he is “pitching”", "annotation_id": "8805316", "url": "https://genius.com/8805316", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Bottles", "line": "Trappin’ and dabbin’, I trap in the rain, sleet, or snow", "vault_lines": ["Trappin' and dabbin', I trap in the rain, sleet, or snow"], "meaning": "A reference to the unofficial creed of the United States Postal Service as written on the front of the James Farley Post Office.\n\nNeither snow nor rain nor heat nor gloom of night stays these couriers from the swift completion of their appointed rounds\n\nOften misquoted to include the words “rain, sleet or snow”, Takeoff borrows this quote to show that he sells drugs 24/7, whatever the weather.", "annotation_id": "6716658", "url": "https://genius.com/6716658", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Bottles", "line": "Migo! Walkin’ out the bando with a 4-5 (Bow, bow)", "vault_lines": ["Migo! Walkin' out the bando with a 4-5 (Bow, bow)"], "meaning": "Quavo has used this line before on their “Nawfside” song off of Rich Nigga Timeline.", "annotation_id": "5136581", "url": "https://genius.com/5136581", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Bottles", "line": "Whippin’ up Alicia Keys (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["Whippin' up Alicia Keys (Woo)"], "meaning": "Here, Offset is using a slang term for Crack Cocaine, “girl.”\n\nSince Alicia Keys is obviously a “girl” (/female), she’d fit as a substitute for the actual term.\n\n\n\nSimply Offset is cooking (or in this case “whippin'”– referring to the object used to create the supplement) Crack Cocaine.", "annotation_id": "4664137", "url": "https://genius.com/4664137", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Bottles", "line": "My neck is flooded and my double cup is mudded (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["My neck is flooded and my double cup is mudded (Woo)"], "meaning": "When Offset says his “neck is flooded”, he means the chains on his neck are are literally flooded with diamonds, and when he says his “cup is mudded”, he means his cup is full of lean, also known as purple drank, which is also referred to as mud due to its thick texture.", "annotation_id": "4247715", "url": "https://genius.com/4247715", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Bottles", "line": "Talkin’ ’bout you on the block, Macaulay Culkin, home alone (Damn)", "vault_lines": ["Talkin' 'bout you on the block, Macaulay Culkin, home alone (Damn)"], "meaning": "Takeoff doesn’t like rappers who say they were in the streets selling drugs when they were really at sitting at home. He references Macaulay Culkin, the lead actor from the classic movie “Home Alone”.", "annotation_id": "4247316", "url": "https://genius.com/4247316", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Bottles", "line": "My wrist whip the magic, Quavo Hardaway (Whip)", "vault_lines": ["My wrist whip the magic, Quavo Hardaway (Whip)"], "meaning": "The way he works his wrist while cooking crack is so unbelievable Quavo thinks it might be magic. Penny Hardaway, a former NBA player, played for the Orlando Magic.", "annotation_id": "4247303", "url": "https://genius.com/4247303", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Bottles", "line": "They watchin’ a nigga like Blu-Ray (Watchin’ me)", "vault_lines": ["They watchin' a nigga like Blu-Ray (Watchin' me)"], "meaning": "Blu-Ray is a digital optical disc data storage format designed to replace the DVD format, capable of storing videos with a full HD resolution. The content of a Blu-Ray disc is supposed to be watched, just like the haters watch Offset.", "annotation_id": "4246872", "url": "https://genius.com/4246872", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Bottles", "line": "(Takeoff!) 32 bricks of gold, Karl Malone (Damn)", "vault_lines": ["32 bricks of gold, Karl Malone (Damn)"], "meaning": "“Gold brick” is slang for gold bar, so Takeoff is saying he has 32 gold bars, just like the basketball player Karl Malone, player for the Utah Jazz who has the number 32 on his jersey.", "annotation_id": "4246843", "url": "https://genius.com/4246843", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Bottles", "line": "I gave my shone a student loan (Haha) That bitch’s mouth made the honor roll (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["I gave my shone a student loan (Haha)", "That bitch's mouth made the honor roll (Woo)"], "meaning": "Ross attended Albany State University on a football scholarship, so we aren’t so sure he knows a lot about student loans and honor rolls. That being said, he uses these two phrases to describe a girl he is having sex with. The phrases indicate that she is still in college. Shone is Miami slang for a promiscuous female.\n\nHe pays her for her services with enough money to pay off her student loans, which is actually a lot of money.\n\nHe loves the oral sex with her so much that he thinks it belongs on the honor roll, which is usually reserved for those with the highest grades.", "annotation_id": "4244229", "url": "https://genius.com/4244229", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Bottles", "line": "Black bottles and the gold chains", "vault_lines": ["Black bottles and the gold chains"], "meaning": "Rozay contrasts two colors, black and gold, in order to demonstrate his immense wealth.\n\nThe black bottles he’s referring to, as seen in the album art, are expensive alcoholic beverages like Belair Rose.\n\nThis drink has become Ross' staple drink. Rose is pronounced just like Rozay.\n\n\n\nRoss has enough money to afford this drink and the many gold chains he wears.", "annotation_id": "4244157", "url": "https://genius.com/4244157", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
