{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Superhero Car", "line": "Headlights beaming, she all excited Can’t wait for me to touch her, give her that Midas", "vault_lines": ["Headlights beaming, she all excited", "Can't wait for me to touch her, give her that Midas"], "meaning": "Mythologically, Midas was a Greek king who had the ability to turn anything he touched into gold. DZA explains this woman’s sexual desire for him and expresses equal intent by claiming to have such powers.", "annotation_id": "11088528", "url": "https://genius.com/11088528", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Superhero Car", "line": "Corns and callouses with pride in my Gucci slippers", "vault_lines": ["Corns and callouses with pride in my Gucci slippers"], "meaning": "Corns and callouses occur on the feet after walking long distances in uncomfortable shoes. Rick Ross has come a very long way in way in the rap game from virtually nothing. The corns and callouses are evidence of this, and he’s proud of them because they reflect all the work he’s put in. Now he’s got a pair of Gucci slippers to reflect the life he’s living.", "annotation_id": "10945722", "url": "https://genius.com/10945722", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Superhero Car", "line": "Food stamps and Medicaid raised every nigga I know", "vault_lines": ["Food stamps and Medicaid raised every nigga I know"], "meaning": "Rick Ross contrasts the previous line by saying eventhough he’s rich, he was raised like everyone else. Throughout the late 1900’s, most African-American families relied on Food Stamps to buy food and social healthcare for treatment as a result of low income.", "annotation_id": "10945692", "url": "https://genius.com/10945692", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Superhero Car", "line": "What makes you a soldier? How quick you climb out a hole Threw us under the basement, now it’s penthouses and homes", "vault_lines": ["What makes you a soldier? How quick you climb out a hole", "Threw us under the basement, now it's penthouses and homes"], "meaning": "Rozay’s implying he’s one hell of a fighter. Before he was rich, he lived out the lowest part of his life in a basement. A quite literal hole in the ground. He contrasts this by saying how he now lives on the highest floor of his condo(s) and life’s good.", "annotation_id": "10945657", "url": "https://genius.com/10945657", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Superhero Car", "line": "I’m from this lil part of Harlem, shit is like Iraq", "vault_lines": ["I'm from this lil part of Harlem, shit is like Iraq"], "meaning": "Throughout the 1900’s, Harlem was regarded as (And to a lesser degree, still is) a dangerous neighbourhood. Rappers have compared it to the Middle East in the past, just ask Rocky: https://genius.com/3062383", "annotation_id": "10945641", "url": "https://genius.com/10945641", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Superhero Car", "line": "Take Eric Gardner killers, give me Sean Price back", "vault_lines": ["Take Eric Gardner killers, give me Sean Price back"], "meaning": "Eric Garner died in July of 2014 as a result of police brutality. He was choked to death after a cop suspected he was selling contraband cigarettes.\n\nSean Price, an influential rapper from Brownsville, died last year in August.\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sean_Price\n\n\n\nBasically what DZA’s getting at is “Fuck The Police”", "annotation_id": "10945560", "url": "https://genius.com/10945560", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Superhero Car", "line": "Smokestradamus, I be visioning things", "vault_lines": ["Smokestradamus, I be visioning things"], "meaning": "Clear reference to the 14th century French physician, Nostradamus. He’s known for shocking many with his disturbingly accurate predictions of the near future and its possible end.", "annotation_id": "10945533", "url": "https://genius.com/10945533", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Black Superhero Car", "status": "done", "targets": 8, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Smoke-dza-and-pete-rock-black-superhero-car-lyrics", "genius_title": "Black Superhero Car", "genius_id": 2916649, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Still .45 strapped", "vault_lines": ["Real recognize real, still .45 strapped"], "meaning": "‘Till this day Scarface still carries a pistol with him for protection because he can’t risk getting caught slipping.\n\n.45 describes the size of the bullet cartridge/shell which is used in Colt revolvers and semi automatic pistols.", "annotation_id": "8901330", "url": "https://genius.com/8901330", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Dopeman, dopeman yellin’ Cook it ’til it’s rock hard, bag it up, sell it", "vault_lines": ["Cook it 'til it's rock hard, bag it up, sell it"], "meaning": "Scarface pays homage to N.W.A. by alluding to the hook from their song “Dopeman”:\n\nDopeman, dopeman!\nHey man give me a hit\nDopeman, dopeman!\nYo man fuck that shit\n\nHe describes how drug dealers operate: They cook cocaine into crack rocks, bag it up, and serve it to the dope fiends.", "annotation_id": "8703345", "url": "https://genius.com/8703345", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Took shots, shed tears, that’s war", "vault_lines": ["Took shots, shed tears, that's war"], "meaning": "There are two different wars here:\nThere’s the war in the streets with cops and other gangstas:\n\n\n\nAnd the war inside yourself.\n\n“Took shots” can either mean taking shots at a rival or taking shots of alcohol to drown out the pain inside. After either one, you shed tears for your loss.", "annotation_id": "7173819", "url": "https://genius.com/7173819", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "”You was doin’ 50 in the 35\", the endin’ is: ”You fit the description of the subject in this incident We gon’ have to take you downtown for some questionin’”", "vault_lines": ["\"You fit the description of the subject in this incident", "We gon' have to take you downtown for some questionin'\""], "meaning": "At first, Scarface is stopped for speeding, a relatively harmless crime, but as it happens, he just happens to match the description of a crime that happened earlier in the day. The racial profiling Scarface is subject to by the police officer who pulls him over leads to a very questionable arrest.\n\nBlack’s have always had a problem with being arrested for little to no reason. A similar situation happens with this black man who was arrested for drinking tea.", "annotation_id": "3398815", "url": "https://genius.com/3398815", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Catch a nigga slippin’, put a bullet in his temple Homie, it’s the same rules, money talks, simple My kinfolk,", "vault_lines": ["Homie, it's the same rules, money talks, simple"], "meaning": "In the hood, being caught “slipping”, being caught with your guard down, is one of the most dangerous things one can do, as it can easily lead to death. Here, Scarface let’s us know that killing people isn’t something he does for personal enjoyment. Scarface only kills because of money.\n\nIn the next lines, Scarface goes on about how “only if [his family] knew what he had to do to [keep his] mind right”, which shows us that Scarface is remorseful about his immoral deeds. Killing, drug dealing, etc. are mentally taxing, and Scarface let’s us know that he’s sorry about partaking in such immoral activities, but they had to be done.", "annotation_id": "3398799", "url": "https://genius.com/3398799", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Call it what I’m livin’ like the high life Only if he knew what I had to do to keep my mind right", "vault_lines": ["call it what I'm livin' like the high life", "Only if he knew what I had to do to keep my mind right"], "meaning": "“Never Surrender”, this songs prequel, appeared on DJ Khaled’s album Suffering from Success\n\n\n\n‘Face summarized the whole concept of Khaled’s album in these 2 bars.\n\nThere is a price to be paid for success. That price is peace of mind.", "annotation_id": "3157577", "url": "https://genius.com/3157577", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Fuck the police, stop runnin’ White boys terrorize nigga’ neighborhoods, gunnin’", "vault_lines": ["Fuck the police, stop runnin'", "White boys terrorize nigga' neighborhoods, gunnin'"], "meaning": "In these bars, ‘Face raps about the relationship between black Americans living in urban areas and the largely white police force that uses violent methods to punish and control black people.\n\nThis idea is expounded on by former Black Panther’s leader Bobby Seale  in a speech that was later used in his conspiracy trial and sampled in “Tears of Joy” by Ross from Teflon Don:\n\nDo you know what we are going to do? We are going to defend ourselves because Huey P. Newton says that, “Power is the ability to define phenomena and make it act in a desired manner.” Power is the ability to define phenomena and make it act in a desired manner. Power: if we have the ability to define it, the only next thing to do is get organized. So when a pig walks up to you, or when a pig gets to jiving with the people you be so organized you be learning some tactics, you be learning some revolutionary principles, you be having some guns hid out somewhere, you have some proper tactics that when the pig gets to jiving with you, the pig is wrong, you whip your guns out on him, blow him a-way, and then you have made the ability, in fact, you have made that pig act in a desired manner\n\n\n\nSeale going to trial\n\n\n\nA court reporter’s rendition of Seale gagged in the courtroom for disturbing the trial\n\nOr in the words of Ross,\n\nMoving white? Better look out for them white boys!", "annotation_id": "3148427", "url": "https://genius.com/3148427", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Damn, a blessin’ in disguise if you ask me", "vault_lines": ["Damn, a blessin' in disguise if you ask me"], "meaning": "A coincidence saved ‘Face from being a murder victim. Freedom for a black person in America is full of complications. But another day free from the words of snitches and handcuffs of police is something to be thankful for.\n\n\n\nThe Geto Boys – Scarface is on the bottom left", "annotation_id": "3148372", "url": "https://genius.com/3148372", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Accept collect calls, tell our dawgs that we did it", "vault_lines": ["Accept collect calls, tell our dawgs that we did it"], "meaning": "This is an allusion to the line in “Drug Dealers Dream” where Ross raps,\n\nMiddle of the night, my nigga wanna Skype, I just count money for him, that shit just get him hype\n\nRoss' friend that went to jail because of a snitch receives the good news from the BO$$ – WE MADE IT", "annotation_id": "3146126", "url": "https://genius.com/3146126", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Every time I turn around, the guilty be pointin’ fingers at me", "vault_lines": ["Every time I turn around, the guilty be pointin' fingers at me"], "meaning": "Former associates and friends who snitch when they catch heat are a big reason why it is dangerous to live the street life. Getting caught is not the issue – it is part of the game – where Z-Ro takes issue is when people turn their guilt into the guilt of their former friends. Snitching is why Z-Ro is happier alone.", "annotation_id": "3145999", "url": "https://genius.com/3145999", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Artificial homies, worse than the agents", "vault_lines": ["Artificial homies, worse than the agents"], "meaning": "At least Federal agents are openly the enemy of black America. Fake friends destroy empires. Going back to the era of American slavery, slaves who were planning to escape or rebel would sometimes be snitched on by fellow slaves looking to gain the favor of “master”.\n\nFurthermore, all the FBI needs is one high level gang leader to get caught, accept the plea bargain to incriminate his fellow “family” members, and the power structure of the organization falls apart. This is what happened in Chicago during the 80’s and 90’s and is a major reason why Chicago is so disorganized and violent now. See Worldstarhiphop’s documentary on this topic here:\nhttp://www.worldstarhiphop.com/videos/video.php?v=wshh7F3lC244E21i1n8C", "annotation_id": "3145058", "url": "https://genius.com/3145058", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "On that lean, you know I fuck her fantastic", "vault_lines": ["On that lean, you know I fuck her fantastic"], "meaning": "As Rozay raps in “In Vein”:\n\nSip syrup so I fuck slow\n\nWhile lean is known to decrease sex drive, it can increase sexual stamina.", "annotation_id": "3145051", "url": "https://genius.com/3145051", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Death to them niggas gettin’ caught go and tellin’", "vault_lines": ["Death to them niggas gettin' caught go and tellin'"], "meaning": "‘Face spits an excellent verse on DJ Khaled’s “Never Surrender” about snitching where he raps,\n\nBut them pictures say them works we ain’t try to stomach, the homie didn’t keep in one hundred\n\n“Death to them niggas getting caught go an telling” is the conclusion to his verse from “Never Surrender”: the punishment for betrayal is death.", "annotation_id": "3145037", "url": "https://genius.com/3145037", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Hieroglyphics keep the rhymes tight", "vault_lines": ["See it in the distance, hieroglyphics keep the rhymes tight"], "meaning": "The hieroglyphics is rap group formed in the 1990’s that contained some of the most renowned left-field rappers of the West Coast.", "annotation_id": "2921612", "url": "https://genius.com/2921612", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Underneath the white tee, Zimmerman get shot down", "vault_lines": ["Underneath the white tee, Zimmerman get shot down"], "meaning": "This line refers to the shooting of Trayvon Martin by George Zimmerman. Seems Scarface has a fantasy of shooting down Zimmerman. Others rappers possess a similar dream.", "annotation_id": "2921590", "url": "https://genius.com/2921590", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Plenty money orders, that what uncle Kenny taught us", "vault_lines": ["Plenty money orders, that what uncle Kenny taught us"], "meaning": "Uncle Kenny refers to Kenneth Williams, the leader of the Boobie Boys gang – a gang Rozay was once apart of. Throughout the album, Ross references his time with the Boobie Boys.", "annotation_id": "2875207", "url": "https://genius.com/2875207", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Work white as Rita Ora in that plastic", "vault_lines": ["Work white as Rita Ora in that plastic"], "meaning": "Interesting wordplay from Rick Ross, he mentions several things which can be interpreted in many ways.\n\nHe mentions the British singer, Rita Ora. A combination of her and plastic can be picked up in two different ways:\n\n\nPlastic as in plastic surgery, which she is rumoured to have used.\nThis could also refer to Rita’s clothing choice, as she has been pictured numerous times wearing ‘plastic’ outfits.\n\n\n\n\n\nHe also mentions the word ‘white’.\nWhite is mostly used to refer to drugs\nAnd then again we have plastic, which is used to carry drugs in.\n\nIn short: a clever line by Rick Ross which can be picked up many different ways.", "annotation_id": "2872943", "url": "https://genius.com/2872943", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Sunny south Florida, a cold motherfucker", "vault_lines": ["Sunny south Florida, a cold motherfucker"], "meaning": "Nice contrast by Ross, explaining the situation in Florida (or  to be precise Miami). While Floride is sunny and warm most of the year, there are still ghetto’s where people will kill (being cold).", "annotation_id": "2869062", "url": "https://genius.com/2869062", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Raymond passed away, here’s somethin’ for his mother It never is enough, tryna show her that I love him", "vault_lines": ["Raymond passed away, here's somethin' for his mother", "It never is enough, tryna show her that I love him"], "meaning": "Raymond was a childhood and close friend of Rick Ross, they met in elementary school. Raymond and Ross would write lyrics til 3 AM and always thought of a plan for success in their early years.\n\nRaymond was killed in Mid-December, 2010.\n\nHe was shot to death in front of his three young children and wife. His kids were 3, 5 and 7. He was intentionally shot by 2 men, after he gave them whatever he had, they still shot anyways. I don’t think this case was ever solved, but I personally believe they just want to rob and kill him because he hung out with Rick Ross and Waka Flocka Flame and was on the come up.\n\nHe mentions ‘nut or peanut’ (Raymond), in another song from Mastermind.\n\nRoss' love for peanut doesn’t stop here: he made a song called She Crazy, from his 2010 mixtape Ashes To Ashes where Peanut is mentioned in the intro.\n\nHe will continue talking about his relationship with Raymond later this verse.", "annotation_id": "2865416", "url": "https://genius.com/2865416", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "I’ve been knowin’ Ross since he was Teflon", "vault_lines": ["I've been knowin' Ross since he was Teflon"], "meaning": "Rozay’s original rap name was “Teflon Da Don”. That was way back in the day, ergo Scarface is emphasizing how long their friendship has lasted.\n\nFace ain’t lyin – Ross and Brad have collaborated before, on the song “Dear Lord”, which appeared on Ross' compilation album Rise To Power, a compilation of unreleased Ross tracks that were recorded from when he first signed to Suave House.", "annotation_id": "2862694", "url": "https://genius.com/2862694", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Cracks in the wall", "vault_lines": ["Cracks in the wall"], "meaning": "Ross is describing his home town Carol City, Florida. This is a very clever line.\n\n\nHe is alluding to the fact that their is actual cracks in the walls because the city is in such poor condition.\nHe means there is actual crack cocaine in the walls. His city could have been hit hard by the crack epidemic in the 80’s like many cities were.\n\n\n\n#ROZAY STAYS EATIN", "annotation_id": "2855302", "url": "https://genius.com/2855302", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "I, I tell you I got 20/20 hindsight", "vault_lines": ["I, I tell you I got 20/20 hindsight"], "meaning": "Alluding to the saying “Hindsight is 20/20,” Scarface will be honest with you and say that the only thing he can be certain about is what’s already happened. Wise words, since you never know what’s going to come next in life.", "annotation_id": "2855118", "url": "https://genius.com/2855118", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Blessing in Disguise", "line": "Ready for a war,", "vault_lines": ["Ready for a war,"], "meaning": "A nod to album track “War Ready”.", "annotation_id": "2853794", "url": "https://genius.com/2853794", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
