{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Born Stunna (Remix)", "line": "Oh, you mad, ho? You ain’t headline", "vault_lines": ["Oh, you mad, ho? You ain't headline"], "meaning": "People are jealous of Nicki because she is winning, and they are not. Headline could be alluding to the way Nicki is always making headlines in the news and tabloids. It’s more likely, however, a reference to the fact that she headlined Summer Jam (an event that she didn’t end up performing at).", "annotation_id": "882028", "url": "https://genius.com/882028", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Born Stunna (Remix)", "line": "It ain’t working out, it ain’t crunch time", "vault_lines": ["It ain't working out, it ain't crunch time"], "meaning": "Wordplay: You do crunches while working out… so if you’re not working out, “it ain’t crunchtime.”\n\nWhat ain’t working out?\n\n\nWhen other rappers see her winning, they wanna hurry (i.e., crunch time) and try getting there stuff together because they wanna win to, but “it ain’t working out.”\nNicki is trying to teach these bitches how to be a good punchline rappers. But they’re not very good students; they lack intelligence and talent. This learning moment ain’t working out so well.", "annotation_id": "882024", "url": "https://genius.com/882024", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Born Stunna (Remix)", "line": "Out in Tokyo, they calling me Nicki-san Everywhere you go, they calling you Nicki’s son", "vault_lines": ["Out in Tokyo, they calling me Nicki-san", "Everywhere you go, they calling you Nicki's son"], "meaning": "Reference to Nicki recently being in Japan. There her Japanese Barbs called her Nicki-San.\nNicki’s Son play on the words son and san (both sound the same). They are called Nicki’s son because they are just a worse version her.", "annotation_id": "882014", "url": "https://genius.com/882014", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Born Stunna (Remix)", "line": "You my son, bitch, and it’s bedtime", "vault_lines": ["You my son, bitch, and it's bedtime"], "meaning": "A nod to her classic “all these bitches is my sons” line from her 2011 hit “Did It On ‘Em.”", "annotation_id": "882010", "url": "https://genius.com/882010", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Born Stunna (Remix)", "line": "Roll a kush up It’s a chess game, but fuck a push-up", "vault_lines": ["Roll a kush up", "It's a chess game, but fuck a push-up"], "meaning": "Doing push-ups is exercise to improve your upper body muscles, essentially a chest game; she pronounces it the same was as chess game, something simple to a mastermind such as a herself.\n\nThe biggest significance of this line is her (not-so) subliminal diss to Pusha T (read: “Fuck a Pusha”); here she shows her loyalty to her mentor Lil Wayne by assisting him in his feud with the GOOD Music rapper.", "annotation_id": "881984", "url": "https://genius.com/881984", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
