{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Can’t Feel My Face", "line": "I can’t feel my face when I’m with you But I love it, but I love it, oh I can’t feel my face when I’m with you But I love it, but I love it, oh", "vault_lines": ["I can't feel my face when I'm with you", "But I love it, but I love it, oh"], "meaning": "Abel smiles a lot when he’s with this special person, and so his whole face goes a little numb. Abel isn’t the first singer to compare the euphoria of love to that provided by drugs – just ask Ghostface.\n\nWhile many drugs are associated with numbness, including novocaine and Ketamine, the classic face-numbing drug is cocaine. For years cocaine was used by doctors as a numbing agent and local anaesthetic. Although it’s rarely used in healthcare anymore, the suffix “caine,” which originally just meant “of the coca plant,” is still used in pharmaceutical contexts to indicate a numbing compound, e.g. novocaine.\n\nThe personification of cocaine in the song climaxes in the hook. In recent singles, Abel has referenced his addiction to drugs – and the reason he can’t stop is the numb-euphoric rush he gets from narcotics. This suggests a kind of death, a rigor mortis, and a dying-off of his fundamental identity (his face) when he’s with his love.", "annotation_id": "14675047", "url": "https://genius.com/14675047", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Can’t Feel My Face", "line": "She told me, “Don’t worry about it” She told me, “Don’t worry no more” We both know we can’t go without it She told me you’ll never be alone, oh, oh, woo", "vault_lines": ["She told me, \"Don't worry about it\"", "She told me, \"Don't worry no more\"", "We both know we can't go without it", "She told me you'll never be alone, oh, oh, woo"], "meaning": "Abel channels his lover’s voice as she manipulates him. She convinces him both that she has what he needs (drugs) and that he won’t be able to find something better so that he doesn’t leave her to try.\n\n\n\nIn telling Abel not to worry, she (the drug) joins the club of Bob Marley, who said “Don’t Worry About a Thing,” Bobby McFerrin, who said “Don’t Worry, Be Happy,” and the Talking Heads, who said “Don’t Worry About Me,” in their song “Don’t Worry About the Government.”\n\nNobody worry about anything!", "annotation_id": "14675034", "url": "https://genius.com/14675034", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Can’t Feel My Face", "line": "But at least we’ll both be beautiful and stay forever young This I know, yeah, this I know", "vault_lines": ["But at least we'll both be beautiful and stay forever young", "This I know, yeah, this I know"], "meaning": "While Abel is optimistic about the future early in the song, the narrative moves from optimism to misery by the end of the second verse. If he takes an overdose and dies, at least he’ll stay forever young in everyone’s memory, as if living until he’s old is a crueler fate. It’s an ironic sacrifice: life in exchange for the immortality of fame.\n\nAlphaville’s song “Forever Young” is often associated with death at a young age.", "annotation_id": "14675027", "url": "https://genius.com/14675027", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Can’t Feel My Face", "line": "And I know she’ll be the death of me, at least we’ll both be numb And she’ll always get the best of me, the worst is yet to come", "vault_lines": ["And she'll always get the best of me, the worst is yet to come"], "meaning": "Abel personifies a drug (likely cocaine) as a beautiful woman. While he’s aware the relationship (with either the drug, the woman, or both) has a negative influence on him, the feeling he gets is too overwhelming to ignore.\n\n\n\nThe use of antithesis (“best” and “worst”) shows the breadth of his feelings in this relationship. The same is true of the word “numb”: it’s the high cocaine provides, but it’s also the cold feeling of death.", "annotation_id": "14675014", "url": "https://genius.com/14675014", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
