{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "D.O.P.E.", "line": "I just spent ten bricks on a foreign car", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A “brick” in drug lingo refers to a pound or kilogram—making 10 bricks equivalent to anywhere from $150,224 – $225,280.\n\nFuture is most likely referring to his 2014 Bentley Flying Spur he recently purchased at the price of $210,000.", "annotation_id": "9097191", "url": "https://genius.com/9097191", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "D.O.P.E.", "line": "We bury money in the fields", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is a reference to drug baron Pablo Escobar burying his billions in farmer’s fields, as depicted on Netflix’s Narcos.\n\n\n\nRoss is a big fan of Narcos. He told Time:\n\nI love Narcos. Oh my God. It’s amazing. I watched that in, like, three days. It was dope.\n\nRoss is now working on a movie inspired by Narcos.", "annotation_id": "8361224", "url": "https://genius.com/8361224", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "D.O.P.E.", "line": "I’m making money got me Geechi My rolex so Geechi Presidentials so Geechi See my whips they Geechi Roll up Back’s they Geechi Racks on racks they Geechi And my whole hood Geechi", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Rick Ross released a song on Black Dollar named “Geechi Liberace”.\n\n\n\nGeechi originally referred to the Gullah people, a population of African-Americans that live on the Sea Islands off the coast of South Carolina and Georgia. They are a unique group in that they speak their own creole tongue that still bears traces of West African languages.\n\nBecause of the quaintness of Gullah to standard English speakers,  the term “Geechi” or “Geechee” came to be a epithet for rustic, rough, uneducated or rural since the early 20th century. It’s now used by southern rappers in a similar way as “hood” or “ghetto.”", "annotation_id": "8331616", "url": "https://genius.com/8331616", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "D.O.P.E.", "line": "Twenty chains got dope on me VVS’s that’s dope on me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "All of the chains Future is wearing are looking very dope. “VVS” stands for “Very Very Slightly Included\"—it’s an extremely high grade of diamond and that has almost no imperfections. It once meant \"Very Very Slightly Imperfect,” but it was changed in the 1990s for marketing reasons.", "annotation_id": "8331514", "url": "https://genius.com/8331514", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "D.O.P.E.", "line": "I got time on my wrist and it’s an Audemar", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "He’s wearing an Audemar Piguet timepiece. Its very expensive. Mainly millionaires can afford this watch. Future mentions this watch very often.\n\nSome manufacturers of the watch don’t use the term “watch” because it doesn’t sound classy or professional. They would prefer to say “timepiece because it sounds more luxurious.", "annotation_id": "8324029", "url": "https://genius.com/8324029", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
