{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Dear Lord", "line": "You give your life to God, you’ll be forgiven for that The muslims fucked around and went to prison for that", "vault_lines": ["The muslims fucked around and went to prison for that"], "meaning": "9/11 attacks, Terrorism , Pictures of Mohammed leading to murder is also leading to jail therefore because they gave their souls to the Muslim God, they are also eglible for a prison Sentence as well.", "annotation_id": "4812034", "url": "https://genius.com/4812034", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Dear Lord", "line": "Bible contradicts", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The bible has its own interpretations on Racial, Homosexual, Religious issues etc.\nMany would say the bible contradicts itself and its teaching. This kind of explains why there are like 8 different kinds of Christians for like one book and one God and one Christ.\n\nhttps://encrypted-tbn3.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcSHkP6rzhVZebCS3Gto0bk3drmJC5mxDZvLANdjn2Z-86QF8tcb.\n\nExample Infidel.org\n\nFor instance, Genesis 1 and 2 disagree about the order in which things are created, and how satisfied God is about the results of his labors. The flood story is really two interwoven stories that contradict each other on how many of each kind of animal are to be brought into the Ark–is it one pair each or seven pairs each of the “clean” ones? The Gospel of John disagrees with the other three Gospels on the activities of Jesus Christ (how long had he stayed in Jerusalem–a couple of days or a whole year?) and all four Gospels contradict each other on the details of Jesus Christ’s last moments and resurrection. The Gospels of Matthew and Luke contradict each other on the genealogy of Jesus Christ’s father; though both agree that Joseph was not his real father. Repetitions and contradictions are understandable for a hodgepodge collection of documents, but not for some carefully constructed treatise, reflecting a well-thought-out plan.", "annotation_id": "4811978", "url": "https://genius.com/4811978", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Dear Lord", "line": "Is the God you’re praying to the same God cuz mine ain’t white With a chokehold on niggas in charge of all lightness", "vault_lines": ["With a chokehold on niggas in charge of all lightness"], "meaning": "When people believe in God typically Christians, They believe God is great and he created everything in existence. But it is kind of contradictory that he is white, white like the white people who enslaved and had racial bias on Niggas and he is black looking up to a white man that created this.\n\nhttp://media2.giphy.com/media/GgWEEEeNc3Rle/giphy.gif.", "annotation_id": "4811953", "url": "https://genius.com/4811953", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Dear Lord", "line": "When my daddy died, I came close to not giving a FUCK!", "vault_lines": ["When my daddy died, I came close to not giving a FUCK!"], "meaning": "Not the first one to critique his father on a song. There could’ve been many reasons why Rick Ross didn’t like his father – his father could’ve been abusive, an alcoholist – we don’t know his full story, but we do have one reason why Ross is angry at his father because this is what Ross told us in his own song, Maybach Music III, off his Teflon Don album:\n\nStreet scholar, graduated no father figure\n\nSo he told us he didn’t have a father who could’ve given him advice about life which would’ve helped him a lot. Probably even keeping Ross off the streets, which would have prevented Ross from hustling on the streets. Ross is probably still furious with his father for letting him down. This could be why Ross sounds so agressive and full of anger in this line.\n\nBut Ross told us in the song All The Money In The World his father died in 1999 – when Rick Ross was 23 – by the cause of cancer:\n\n\nPassed in ‘99 cancer all in his liver\nShit different since we last spoke; his son’s a lil richer\nI’d never rap again if I could tell him that I miss him\n\nCrying in my mansion as I’m holdin on his picture\n\n\nHe’s contradicting himself – in this song he’s saying he didn’t give a fuck when his father died, and a few years later, in 2010, Ross says he misses him.\n\nThis could either mean:\n\n\nHe really didn’t give a fuck back then, considering the place he was in. When he became successful, it’s likely that he let go of some of the resentment he held in the past.\nSomething we yet don’t know about..", "annotation_id": "3111028", "url": "https://genius.com/3111028", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
