{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Def R", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Werewolf-boy-def-r-lyrics", "genius_title": "Def R", "genius_id": 9114215, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "You should have a travel agent, ’cause you a trip You should make your own toilet tissue since you the shit", "vault_lines": ["You should have a travel agent, 'cause you a trip", "You should make your own toilet tissue since you the shit"], "meaning": "In his lines Kanye makes a play on words with the expressions “you’re a trip” and “you’re the shit”.\n\nTo be a trip is essentially a wild or crazy person, and Kanye uses the word “trip” to compare to a travel agent on a plane trip.\n\nTo be the shit it’s when people think you’re cool or when people respect you, but Kanye is literally comparing this girl to a piece of shit (also a term for being a bad person), so she should make her own toilet tissue to clean herself up,", "annotation_id": "36665586", "url": "https://genius.com/36665586", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "[Verse 6: Rick Ross]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Compared to the other verses, Ross’s seems to be one of the only ones that focuses entirely on himself after the breakup that the song is about.\n\nDrake is saddened by the breakup, Kanye is angry, and 3 Stacks is thinking about it in a introspective way, while Ross is only thinking about his lifestyle, which parallels when Kanye said:\n\nYou gon' be hot a little while, I'ma be rich forever", "annotation_id": "36422864", "url": "https://genius.com/36422864", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "Your wrist and fingers glisten, ice cold like Michigan", "vault_lines": ["Your wrist and fingers glisten, ice cold like Michigan"], "meaning": "Michigan is a pretty cold place, the lowest temperature ever recorded in Michigan was 51°F/-46°", "annotation_id": "36422809", "url": "https://genius.com/36422809", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "All that bullshit’s for the birds You ain’t nothin’ but a vulture-ure-ure-ure", "vault_lines": ["You ain't nothin' but a vulture-ure-ure-ure"], "meaning": "The difference between a bird and vulture is that vultures are more suited for hunting and or killing, sometimes taking food.\n\nBirds are less hunting based and as aggressive as vultures.\n\nChris is calling a girl in particular a vulture and other people birds, as if she’s different from everyone else, but in a negative way.", "annotation_id": "36422771", "url": "https://genius.com/36422771", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "Now I’m ghost, baby, four-door Rizzy Royce", "vault_lines": ["Now I'm ghost, baby, four-door Rizzy Royce"], "meaning": "There is a car known as a Rolls-Royce Ghost, which Rocky indirectly references earlier in the song. It is a full-sized luxury car manufactured by Rolls-Royce Motor Cars. The “Ghost” nameplate, named in honour of the Silver Ghost, a car first produced in 1906.", "annotation_id": "35545468", "url": "https://genius.com/35545468", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "I’m stupid, but I won’t get my drama on What I’m dealin’ with is too real for me to comment on", "vault_lines": ["What I'm dealin' with is too real for me to comment on"], "meaning": "Kanye acknowledges his past mistakes of speaking without thinking (ie. the Taylor Swift VMA incident), but he knows when to draw the line. What you hear him rapping about is nowhere close to what he deals with on a daily basis. He doesn’t put all his shit on blast, unlike other rappers who tell all their secrets and leave nothing up to the imagination.", "annotation_id": "35128738", "url": "https://genius.com/35128738", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "It’s hard to throw up the deuces ’Cause when you know it’s juicy", "vault_lines": ["'Cause when you know it's juicy"], "meaning": "3000 professes that when ‘it’s juicy’ (i.e the sex is good, girl’s pussy game is tight), it’s hard to end a relationship or say goodbye to a partner. Andre finds himself making seemingly deep conscientious excuses, which connects to the next line.\n\nThe conscientious excuses is reminiscent of the ancient Chinese philosopher and writer Confucius, made famous for his one-line reflections on society and his surroundings.\n\n3000 realises there excuses are just a cover for him not wanting to let go of an exciting and addictive sexual nymph.", "annotation_id": "35128640", "url": "https://genius.com/35128640", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "Accordin’ to my old bish, I be on some new ish She was on some old ish, now I got a new bish Think I give two ish? You ain’t gonna do ish", "vault_lines": ["Accordin' to my old bish, I be on some new ish", "She was on some old ish, now I got a new bish", "Think I give two ish? You ain't gonna do ish"], "meaning": "Apparently, F-A-B-O-L-O-U-S somewhat censors himself to begin his verse. Although if given the choice, the uncensored version would look like this:\n\nAccordin' to my old bitch, I be on some new shit\nShe was on some old shit, now I got a new bitch\nThink I give two shits? You ain’t gonna do shit", "annotation_id": "31809169", "url": "https://genius.com/31809169", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "What it entails is hard to say like sellin’ seashells By the seashore", "vault_lines": ["What it entails is hard to say like sellin' seashells"], "meaning": "No doubt that break ups are difficult and can leave one tongue-tied.\n\nIt’s also a reference to the tongue-twister: She sells seashells by the seashore", "annotation_id": "21859228", "url": "https://genius.com/21859228", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "But I’ll fuck you right, I will, I fuck you right, I will I’ll fuck you like no one has ever, ever made you feel", "vault_lines": ["But I'll fuck you right, I will, I fuck you right, I will", "I'll fuck you like no one has ever, ever made you feel"], "meaning": "This is a nod to Usher’s bridge lyric on his 1997 slow jam “Nice & Slow”, except Drake goes full-time explicit with it.\n\n\n\nNow tell me, do you wanna get freaky?\n‘Cause I’ll freak you right, I will\nI’ll freak you right, I will\nI’ll freak you like no one has ever, ever made you feel\n\nThis wasn’t the first time Usher’s work was mentioned on “Deuces”, but it makes it the 2nd time noted on the remix version.", "annotation_id": "21859196", "url": "https://genius.com/21859196", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "K-K-K-K-K-K-Mac", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The intro incorporates the producer tag for California writer and producer Kevin McCall, who is most famous for his collaborations with main artist Chris Brown. They’ve worked together many times before, on tracks like November 2011’s “Strip” and June 2012’s “2012.”\n\nMcCall actually appeared on the first version of the track and rapped the third verse, along with handling production duties. His vocals are still present throughout the song, as well.", "annotation_id": "18526649", "url": "https://genius.com/18526649", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "Go on, got me hot, smokin’ like a chimney", "vault_lines": ["Go on, got me hot, smokin' like a chimney"], "meaning": "Double Entendre:\n\n\nSmoking like a chimney could mean because he is so mad(hot) that he is smoking.\nThis could also mean that he is trying to cope with this stress by smoking a lot of weed.", "annotation_id": "1718365", "url": "https://genius.com/1718365", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "Got a bucket full of ice, and a watch to go with it (Ugh!)", "vault_lines": ["Got a bucket full of ice, and a watch to go with it (Ugh!)"], "meaning": "#Triple entendre\n\n\n\n“Ice” can mean “diamonds”. He has a lot of jewels, consequently it’s a duty to possess a diamond watch to go with that status\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBut “ice” can also mean “crystal meth”.\n\n\n\n\n\nA “bucket” of meth represents much money, so it goes with a luxury watch\n\n\n\nOr just a bucket full of ice cubes with that watch !", "annotation_id": "1632230", "url": "https://genius.com/1632230", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "R-O-Z-A-Y on the wall in that Hall of Fame (Maybach Music, Ugh!)", "vault_lines": ["R-O-Z-A-Y on the wall in that Hall of Fame (Maybach Music, Ugh!)"], "meaning": "“Rozay” : that’s my nickname\nB.M.F.\n\nIt comes from the Rick’s favorite champagne, “François de Rozay”, made in Rozay-en-Brie", "annotation_id": "1631030", "url": "https://genius.com/1631030", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "So when you talk ’bout, “You know who”", "vault_lines": ["So when you talk 'bout, “You know who”"], "meaning": "Once again he is talking about Amber Rose. When he hears something about her, he’s not going to comment on it, because he’s learned to keep his mouth shut about such things. (Can anyone say, “Taylor Swift?”)\n\nJay Z had a similar line on his song “Takeover” about Nas' then girlfriend and mother of his first child, Carmen:\n\nBecause you-know-who did you-know-what with you-know-who\nBut let’s keep that between me and you (for now)", "annotation_id": "56030", "url": "https://genius.com/56030", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "You start to sound like Confucius when makin’ up excuses", "vault_lines": ["You start to sound like Confucius when makin' up excuses"], "meaning": "Confucius was an ancient Chinese philosopher known for his inspirational quotes regarding personal and government morality. Example:\n\nAn oppressive government is more to be feared than a tiger\n\n\n\nNo word as to whether or not he could rap like Three Stacks, though.", "annotation_id": "51685", "url": "https://genius.com/51685", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "I gotta find me a new locomotive, stop makin’ sad songs", "vault_lines": ["I gotta find me a new locomotive, stop makin' sad songs"], "meaning": "Andre feels he needs to find himself a new girl (or “locomotive”, carrying on from the Caboose metaphor used in the previous line), so that he can once again be happy and no longer feel the need to make sad songs.\n\nThe “stop making sad songs” part may well be a reference to Eddie Murphy’s character in Dreamgirls, at the point in which he stops singing sad R'n'B songs and starts rapping (something which Andre could well have parallels with considering how little rapping was on The Love Below).", "annotation_id": "49660", "url": "https://genius.com/49660", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "[Verse 4: Kanye West]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This verse refers to his issues with his ex, Amber Rose.\n\nWest also featured on (and co-produced) “Down”, a song on Brown’s previous album Exclusive.\n\nThey later collaborate for the third time on West’s song, “Waves”.", "annotation_id": "49657", "url": "https://genius.com/49657", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "And the hardest part about the fuckin’ business is mindin’ your own", "vault_lines": ["And the hardest part about the fuckin' business is mindin' your own"], "meaning": "A play on the phrase “mind your own business” that illustrates the difficulty of Drake’s situation. Drake’s talking about the “fucking” business, literally. The hardest part about sex is making sure you get the most out of it, not your partner.\n\nThis line is also talking about the entertainment business, the hardest part about it is to learn to mind your own business and stay focused on the music. Also since he’s famous he’s in the spotlight and everyone is concerned about the things hes doing and the girl he is dating, when they need to be focused on their own selves.", "annotation_id": "49650", "url": "https://genius.com/49650", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "Miss me a little when I’m gone", "vault_lines": ["Know you gon'miss me a little when I'm gone"], "meaning": "Sampled directly from the Drake (feat. Lil Wayne) track “Miss Me”. Since there was no official acapella made, it explains why it sounds weird. Since Drake appears on this song, one wonders why Fab just didn’t ask him to use the original masters.", "annotation_id": "41664", "url": "https://genius.com/41664", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "line": "Jay finally got it through my head not to run my mouth", "vault_lines": ["Jay finally got it through my head not to run my mouth"], "meaning": "Jay Z has made a career out of being subliminal and for the most part composed about issues, however Kanye is the very opposite. Though Jay has probably offered his fair share of advice for Ye, this is probably about when Kanye was on the tonight show and Jay Leno asked what Kanye’s mom would think about the stunt he pulled when he took the mic from Taylor swift. Kanye seemed very emotional about the question.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A6bgSITBrVQ&feature=player_embedded", "annotation_id": "41662", "url": "https://genius.com/41662", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Deuces (Remix)", "status": "done", "targets": 21, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Chris-brown-deuces-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "Deuces (Remix)", "genius_id": 1414, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Haven’t said a word, haven’t said a word to me this evening", "vault_lines": ["Haven't said a word, haven't said a word to me this evening"], "meaning": "Smokey Robinson’s line “You don’t have to say a word…” is repeated in the sample throughout the song. Kanye sings along with an altered lyric, as he has been known to do in songs from “Through the Wire” to “30 Hours.”", "annotation_id": "30129349", "url": "https://genius.com/30129349", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Uh Uh", "vault_lines": ["The way you look should be a sin, you my sensation, uh"], "meaning": "Uh. One of Kanye’s simplest yet most iconic sounds. He uses his voice in a way that is truly incredible. Kanye uses “uh” on hits like Devil In a New Dress, Good Morning, Barry Bonds, Heard ‘Em Say, and so many more hits.\n\nUh", "annotation_id": "28469057", "url": "https://genius.com/28469057", "state": "pending", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "She putting on her make-up, she casually allure", "vault_lines": ["She putting on her make-up, she casually allure"], "meaning": "Ye seems to be praising this girl’s natural beauty, how she’s casually allure, effortlessly gorgeous.\n\nHowever he’s also underlining how she seems to be a natural temptress, casually putting her charms to work as if it was nothing, hinting at a certain lack of loyalty and possibly unfaithfulness.", "annotation_id": "18386429", "url": "https://genius.com/18386429", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "[Instrumental Interlude: Mike Dean Guitar Solo]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The interlude is only on the album version. The interlude consists of a guitar solo performed by producer Mike Dean.", "annotation_id": "7481926", "url": "https://genius.com/7481926", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1024, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "[Produced by Bink! and Mike Dean]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In one of the many gems from the diamond mine that is My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy, Bink! flips a smooth, soulful sample: Smokey Robinson’s version of the classic Carole King song, “Will You Love Me Tomorrow? Add in a beautifully composed guitar solo, electric guitar progression, a fine-tuned piano, and strings, and then you have one of the best beats of the past 5 years.\n\nIn an interview with VIBE, Bink! briefly spoke on the history of this beat. As quoted directly from the article:\n\nAlmost immediately after Bink! played his first instrumental for ‘Ye, he knew that the record that would become “Devil In A New Dress” was a keeper. “The reaction I got from him after I played the beat for the first time was real intense,“ Bink! remembers. \"I mean 'Ye just doesn’t react to music if it’s not genuine.”\n\nLooks like we weren’t the only people who had that reaction after hearing this beat.\n\nBelow is an official Producer Genius SoundCloud breakdown of the track. First off, both of the portions of the song that were chopped are played. Then, they are played together, in sequence, at the original tempo. Finally, the sample is sped up to the final 79.6 bpm, and EQ’d to bring out the beautiful bassline.\n\nhttps://soundcloud.com/producergenius/breakdown-of-kanyes-devil-in-a", "annotation_id": "2484803", "url": "https://genius.com/2484803", "state": "accepted", "votes": 238, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "[Chorus: Kanye West]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This song samples Smokey Robinson’s “Will You Still Love Me Tomorrow”, itself a cover of The Shirelles' original. Ye’s song contains lyrical themes of lust and heartache, in addition to love and religion.\n\nThere doesn’t appear to be a straightforward narrative in this song; rather, Kanye touches upon the theme of fame, and how it molds one as a person. Ye discusses a woman he knows, who was once fairly religious, but began to lose her faith as time passed by.\n\nHer personality shift causes strain in their relationship, and Kanye finds himself upset with her aspects that he once found endearing. In his typical Kanye fashion, he also describes himself as a person, and how he looks down on himself as an asshole after a few controversies and rough life experiences.", "annotation_id": "1821635", "url": "https://genius.com/1821635", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "I love it, though I love it, though, huh, you know? Uh", "vault_lines": ["I love it, though, huh, you know?"], "meaning": "“Devil In a New Dress” is the only song on My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy that features no production from West himself. In spite of this, Kanye can’t help but love the beat.", "annotation_id": "366114", "url": "https://genius.com/366114", "state": "accepted", "votes": 302, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Cat got your tongue?", "vault_lines": ["Cat got your tongue?"], "meaning": "Kanye has finally wised up to her bullshit, so she keeps her mouth shut rather than give him more ammunition to use against her.\n\nIt’s interesting to note that right after this line, the instrumental is played to reflect her being silent, while at the same time building up to Ross’s epic verse.", "annotation_id": "333144", "url": "https://genius.com/333144", "state": "accepted", "votes": 270, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "[Verse 3: Rick Ross]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Although Rick Ross was not initially intended to be featured on West’s album, he was invited to Hawaii to work with West, as Kanye was set to produce the song “Live Fast, Die Young” off Ross' album. While there, he walked in while West was recording “Monster” and Ross was so impressed with what he heard and asked if he could deliver an intro to the song. West enjoyed what Ross brought to the song and invited him back to his studio during the final stages of the album, to record a guest verse on this song.\n\nAccording to Ross, the verse was added the day before the album had to be turned into the record label. In an interview with MTV discussing the composition of the song, Ross commented:\n\nI got a call, they wanted me to be a part of that record. It was actually the last day before Kanye had to turn the record in, and I think that pressure just made it that much more special to me. So I just sat there, approached the record openly and as straightforward as I could. When I laid the verse, 30 minutes later, I was extremely happy. I sent it to him, and he was too. I think it was one of the dopest verses I did this year.\n\nRoss favorably compared the recording sessions to his past in school. He mused:\n\nIt’s like everybody is a student: You walk into a session, and it’s all about the subject at hand.\n\nThis verse is often referred to as one of Ross' best verses of his career, and it’s pretty clear Ross outshines Kanye on this track.", "annotation_id": "320437", "url": "https://genius.com/320437", "state": "accepted", "votes": 569, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "I never needed acceptance from all you outsiders", "vault_lines": ["I never needed acceptance from all you outsiders"], "meaning": "Rick Ross knows that the opinions of people that aren’t living his type of lifestyle are irrelevant. They aren’t in the inner circle, so their opinions don’t matter. Furthermore, as he states in the next line, even Kanye has been in his inner circle before both of them even got famous, which makes other people’s opinion about him even more irrelevant.\n\nThe interesting thing about this line is the idea of being accepted by outsiders. This is the opposite of what we would normally expect to be true, so in a way it insinuates that Rick Ross is the outsider from everyone else’s point of view, but it doesn’t matter to him because he’s not being rejected by them, they’re being rejected by him.", "annotation_id": "300849", "url": "https://genius.com/300849", "state": "accepted", "votes": 144, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "I shed a tear before the night’s over God bless the man I put this ice over (Uh)", "vault_lines": ["I shed a tear before the night's over", "God bless the man I put this ice over (Uh)"], "meaning": "These lines can be seen as a prayer – Ross is thanking God for his continual blessings, and ‘pays back the favor’ by encrusting Jesus as his diamond-encrusted pendant.\n\nIt’s also possible that these lines are more of a plea to pray for a man he just killed, or saw die.  Dead bodies are often referred to as being put “on ice”. “Shedding a tear” could refer to a death-related teardrop tattoo under his eye, or for literally crying because of committing a terrible sin.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/rickyrozay/status/273590283936100353", "annotation_id": "91272", "url": "https://genius.com/91272", "state": "accepted", "votes": 172, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Stretch limousine, sipping rosé all alone", "vault_lines": ["Stretch limousine, sipping rosé all alone"], "meaning": "The obvious meaning is that he is enjoying the lifestyle of a rapper, and drinking champagne on his limo.\nBut the second is that there is a girl with him on the limo “sipping” on Rozay, which is his nickname. Either way, would be an example for the lavish lifestyle of a rap star.\n\n\n\nAlternatively, the lyric could be read as, “stretch limousine, sipping, Rozay all alone.” In the video of just Ross' verse, he’s by himself. This helps convey a sense of isolation, a contemplative state, that is prevalent throughout the album.", "annotation_id": "91268", "url": "https://genius.com/91268", "state": "accepted", "votes": 165, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Climbing up the Lord’s ladder", "vault_lines": ["climbing up the Lord's ladder"], "meaning": "Kanye was seriously injured in a car accident just before his career took off. Ross assumes that ‘Ye was on his way up to heaven before the car crash.\n\nThis might be a reference to Jacob’s ladder that appears in Genesis 28:10-19:\n\nAnd he dreamed, and behold a ladder set up on the earth, and the top of it reached to heaven: and behold the angels of God ascending and descending on it.", "annotation_id": "82545", "url": "https://genius.com/82545", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Cherry-red chariot, excess is just my character All-black tux, nigga’s shoes lavender", "vault_lines": ["Cherry-red chariot, excess is just my character", "All-black tux, nigga's shoes lavender"], "meaning": "Everything about Ross is grandiose: from his lavish lifestyle, to his larger-than-life persona, to his excessively large body.\n\n“Chariot” is yet another example of the Greek mythology theme in previous lines (Medusa, hydra), here being a metaphor for a red sportscar.", "annotation_id": "78117", "url": "https://genius.com/78117", "state": "accepted", "votes": 117, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "You love me for me, could you be more phony?", "vault_lines": ["You love me for me, could you be more phony?"], "meaning": "“I love you for you” is a cliche; Kanye knows the girl is attracted to his social capital.\n\nKanye is also referring to his own imperfections and superficiality (a theme throughout the album). He is literally saying that he is so “phony” (read: egotistic, superficial, jerk-ish, as alluded to earlier in the song as well as throughout the album) that if you love him for him, you must be those things yourself.", "annotation_id": "78106", "url": "https://genius.com/78106", "state": "accepted", "votes": 282, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "When it come to tools, fool, I’m a Pep Boy When it came to dope, I was quick to export Never tired of ballin’, so it’s on to the next sport", "vault_lines": ["When it come to tools, fool, I'm a Pep Boy", "When it came to dope, I was quick to export", "Never tired of ballin', so it's on to the next sport"], "meaning": "Ross quickly sums up the “sports” he’s good at: handling his guns, dealing drugs, and making money.\n\nFirst, Pep Boys is a car (repair) store, so the people who work there know their way around with tools. Ross also knows how to handle his “tools”, but he uses different kind of tools…\n\nBut he’s not just good with guns: as a drug dealer he used to quickly export drugs to his clientele.\n\nFinally, “ballin'” generally refers to two things: playing basketball, and being rich. Ross references the sports meaning of ballin' while actually referring to the “being rich” meaning. He goes from “sport” to “sport”, because he is never tired of making money.\n\nThis line could also be a reference to Ross playing college football scholarship to Albany State, and changing sports – going from football to hip-hop.", "annotation_id": "78090", "url": "https://genius.com/78090", "state": "accepted", "votes": 114, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Spinnin’ Teddy Pendergrass vinyl as my J burns", "vault_lines": ["Spinnin' Teddy Pendergrass vinyl as my J burns"], "meaning": "Smoking a spliff and listening to some freak-nasty R&B:\n\n Let’s take a shower, shower together..\nTonight I’m in a sexy mood, babay \n\n Would you rub me down in burning hot oils, baby?", "annotation_id": "74533", "url": "https://genius.com/74533", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Different traps I was gettin’ mail from Polk County, Jacksonville, right in Melbourne Whole clique appetites had tapeworms", "vault_lines": ["Different traps I was gettin' mail from", "Polk County, Jacksonville, right in Melbourne", "Whole clique appetites had tapeworms"], "meaning": "A “trap” is a drug-dealing spot\n\nHe keeps running his fancy cars through speed traps (thus getting tickets in the mail)\nRoss is receiving mail from his prison homies (Polk County, Jacksonville and Melbourne are all home to famous correctional facilities), a trap being a prison.\n\n\n\nRoss' claims his whole crew ended up in prison because they were too “hungry” for money, much like someone who has a tapeworm in their intestines. A tape worm eats away at your insides, causing you to always be hungry for more.\n\nOn the other hand tapeworms can’t explain Rick Ross' physique. Fat motherfucker, now look who’s in trouble…", "annotation_id": "69453", "url": "https://genius.com/69453", "state": "accepted", "votes": 170, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "New Mercedes sedan, the Lex’ sport So many cars, DMV thought it was mail fraud", "vault_lines": ["So many cars, DMV thought it was mail fraud"], "meaning": "Having all these car registrations from one person must have made someone at DMV (Department of Motor Vehicles) think it’s a part of a scam. As if anyone at DMV actually thinks about anything.\n\nRoss may also be playing off the previous line: aside from going from “sport to \"sport,” he also buys a new sports car very frequently.", "annotation_id": "68822", "url": "https://genius.com/68822", "state": "accepted", "votes": 304, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Double-headed monster with a mind of his own", "vault_lines": ["Double-headed monster with a mind of his own"], "meaning": "If you couldn’t figure it out yourself, the second “head” attached to Ross' body is nothing less but the head of his penis. Ross plays with the phrase “thinking with your penis”.\n\nThis also keeps the Greek mythology theme running with the earlier Medusa reference, as the two heads could refer to a hydra, an ancient serpent-like creature that possessed many heads.\n\nNotice how the first four bars are subtly sprinkled with words relating to the human anatomy (ass, bone, wrist, head, mind).\n\nIt’s also a pretty obvious reference to his short piece in “Monster”.", "annotation_id": "63980", "url": "https://genius.com/63980", "state": "accepted", "votes": 142, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "I’m makin’ love to the angel of death Catchin’ feelings, never stumble, retracin’ my steps (Uh) [Outro] Uh", "vault_lines": ["Catchin' feelings, never stumble, retracin' my steps (Uh)"], "meaning": "A reference to a few things.\n\n\nRoss is living life on the edge as he lives fast and may die young. This could be a sort of cousin to the saying “flirting with death”\nCould be a literal reference to the character in the Runaway film, as Kanye’s lover could be seen as an “angel of death.”", "annotation_id": "62557", "url": "https://genius.com/62557", "state": "accepted", "votes": 349, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Getting 2Pac money twice over Still a real nigga, red Coogi sweater, dice-roller", "vault_lines": ["Getting 2Pac money twice over", "Still a real nigga, red Coogi sweater, dice-roller"], "meaning": "Even though Ross makes a lot of money – like 2Pac, one of the best selling artists of all time – he still stays true to himself. It’s also a play on the ‘2’, in 2Pac’s name.\n\nIn addition, when Tupac died, it was rumored that he only had one hundred thousand dollars in his bank account, Ross boasts that he makes twice that amount of money on a single feature.\n\nAfter referencing Tupac in the first line, he references Biggie in the second line, which is not a coincidence since Biggie and Pac are often named in one sentence. The former liked to wear Coogi sweaters (well, not only sweaters and play Cee-lo:", "annotation_id": "59767", "url": "https://genius.com/59767", "state": "accepted", "votes": 242, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Hater talkin’ never made me mad Never that, not when I’m in my favorite paper tag Therefore, G4s at the Clearport", "vault_lines": ["Hater talkin' never made me mad", "Never that, not when I'm in my favorite paper tag", "Therefore, G4s at the Clearport"], "meaning": "Haters never upset Ricky. Especially when he’s in his favorite brand new car, fresh off the lot with the paper plates.\n\n“Clearport” is a nickname for the Teterboro Airport outside of New York City. Mainly used by people like Jay Z and Rick Ross so they won’t get mugged by fans at regular airports.", "annotation_id": "59737", "url": "https://genius.com/59737", "state": "accepted", "votes": 113, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Lookin’ at my bitch, I bet she give your ass a bone", "vault_lines": ["Lookin' at my bitch, I bet she give your ass a bone"], "meaning": "If you happen to be glancing at Rick’s lady, she’ll give you a boner (erection).\n\nAlso a great play on words, involving “bitch” (female dog) and “bone,” as dogs like to chew on bones.", "annotation_id": "56095", "url": "https://genius.com/56095", "state": "accepted", "votes": 395, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Lookin’ at my wrist, it’ll turn your ass to stone", "vault_lines": ["Lookin' at my wrist, it'll turn your ass to stone"], "meaning": "The jewelry on his wrist is like Greek Mythology’s Medusa, gazing upon her would turn onlookers to stone. Rozay wears a watch by Versace, since their logo is the head of Medusa.", "annotation_id": "54104", "url": "https://genius.com/54104", "state": "accepted", "votes": 300, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "We still speedin’, running signs like they don’t matter, uh", "vault_lines": ["We still speedin', running signs like they don't matter, uh"], "meaning": "Ross references is known for speedin, and promotes his lifestyle of living fast and dying young.\n\nDespite seeing the effects of car crashes in his friend Kanye, Ross still doesn’t drive carefully.", "annotation_id": "53851", "url": "https://genius.com/53851", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Had cyphers with Yeezy before his mouth wired (Uh) Before his jaw shattered,", "vault_lines": ["Before his jaw shattered,"], "meaning": "Yeezy and Rozay met in 2002 before either was famous — Ross hadn’t even adopted the Rick Ross moniker or grown his trademark beard yet!\n\nThis was shortly before West got in a jaw-shattering car crash, made famous by “Through the Wire” – a track where Kanye rapped the original version with his mouth still wired shut.\n\nThe August 2012 Rolling Stone cover article briefly covers Ross and West’s pre-fame meeting:\n\nSometime around 1998, Ross decided to get serious about rapping. He started hanging around studios, jockeying for time on the mic […] and at one point [in 2002] found himself in the studio with a hungry young kid named Kanye West, who was producing an artist Ross was ghostwriting for. “I didn’t even know who Kanye West was,” Ross says. “I just saw a kid with a funny shirt who seemed happy to just be working.”\n\nAnd Ross really expands on their early encounter in this 2013 Hot 97 interview:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kXpvqN9dyTM", "annotation_id": "52974", "url": "https://genius.com/52974", "state": "accepted", "votes": 262, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "What happened to religion? Oh, she lose it", "vault_lines": ["What happened to religion? Oh, she lose it"], "meaning": "“Losing my religion” is a popular phrase in American slang that means acting in a way that disregards general standards of social grace, civility, or morality.\n\nAlthough the extremely pretty girl is a Christian, she hooks up with Kanye and thus “loses her religion”.", "annotation_id": "37618", "url": "https://genius.com/37618", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Text message break-ups, the casualty of tour How she gon’ wake up and not love me no more?", "vault_lines": ["Text message break-ups, the casualty of tour", "How she gon' wake up and not love me no more?"], "meaning": "The story has been told a million times: with all the excitement, groupies, partying and more, it’s really hard to keep a serious relationship for a musician on the road. Playing on the term “Casualty of war”, Ye ponders on the quick-changing nature of love as his girlfriend suddenly and apparently unexpectedly lost her love for him.", "annotation_id": "36833", "url": "https://genius.com/36833", "state": "accepted", "votes": 152, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "May the Lord forgive us, may the gods be with us In that magic hour, I seen good Christians Make brash decisions, oh, she do it", "vault_lines": ["In that magic hour, I seen good Christians", "Make brash decisions, oh, she do it"], "meaning": "The magic hour is known to be around 3-4 AM, late in the night when bars are almost closing, wherein every person who has not hooked up yet lowers their respective standards to “whatever is available” – even good Christians.\n\nAfter giving up his good values, similar to other Christians during the “magic hours”, he is pleading to a higher power – a Lord, multiple gods or whatever might be up there – for forgiveness for early consummation. Since he wants both him and the girl to be forgiven, he implies the girl is sinning just as much as Kanye is.\n\nWhile it may be coincidental, The Magic Hour was a late night talkshow hosted by Magic Johnson, a Christian who ironically contracted HIV from what can be seen as a “rash” decision.\n\nFinally, he says the final line in the rhythm of Wake Flocka Flame’s hook on “O Let’s Do It”.", "annotation_id": "36831", "url": "https://genius.com/36831", "state": "accepted", "votes": 133, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "The Lyor Cohen of Dior Homme That’s “Dior Homme,” not “Dior, homie”", "vault_lines": ["The Lyor Cohen of Dior Homme", "That's \"Dior Homme,\" not \"Dior, homie\""], "meaning": "“The Lyor Cohen of Dior Homme” is a new, smoother (though less-catchy) version of Kanye’s nickname “The Louis Vuitton Don”\n\nLyor Cohen is an American music industry executive, who has been with various record labels for 30 years. He was responsible for the endorsement deal Run DMC had with Adidas, which was one of the first big commercial deals with a rap group. Since 2016, he has worked for YouTube as Global Head of Music.\n\n“Dior Homme” is the men’s division of high-end clothing label Christian Dior (homme = man in french)\n\nA non-French speaker would probably read ‘homme’ as ‘homie’, so Kanye gives us a quick pronunciation lesson as an excuse to explain the joke in his previous line (we’ll forgive you this one time because it’s a pretty clever line).\n\nKanye’s fashion mispronunciation is also a reference to his earlier song “All Falls Down” from his debut album The College Dropout:\n\nI can’t even pronounce nothing, pass that Ver-say-see!", "annotation_id": "36813", "url": "https://genius.com/36813", "state": "accepted", "votes": 191, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "I hit the Jamaican spot, at the bar, take a seat I ordered the jerk, she said you are what you eat, haha You see, I always loved that sense of humor But tonight, you should have seen how quiet the room was", "vault_lines": ["I hit the Jamaican spot, at the bar, take a seat", "I ordered the jerk, she said you are what you eat, haha", "You see, I always loved that sense of humor", "But tonight, you should have seen how quiet the room was"], "meaning": "“Jerk” is a Jamaican style of cooking, which refers to the Jamaican jerk spice that meat is rubbed with.\n\nKanye’s girl uses some wordplay involving the common idiom “you are what you eat” to jokingly call him a jerk. However, the joke is causing a rather awkward situation; no one laughs because everyone secretly thinks it’s true.\n\nKanye is definitely not above critiquing himself, and is fully aware of his jerky-assholeness. This line can even be seen as a foreshadowing of the album’s most important song, “Runaway”, where Kanye acknowledges his “douchebaggery”.", "annotation_id": "36809", "url": "https://genius.com/36809", "state": "accepted", "votes": 274, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "I thought I was the asshole, I guess it’s rubbin’ off", "vault_lines": ["I thought I was the asshole, I guess it's rubbin' off"], "meaning": "Kanye has developed a devilish reputation with the public and the media, most notably when he rudely interrupted a young girl in a shockingly assholish move. Kanye’s girl has been hanging around him so much that she’s starting to become an asshole herself. Naturally, the line is meant ironic. Kanye doesn’t actually believe the girl is becoming a worse person by being around him.\n\nOn another level, notice the nasty wordplay involving the words “asshole” and “rubbing off” about ass wiping.\n\nThe word “asshole” seems to be Kanye’s favorite when referring to himself on this album.", "annotation_id": "36790", "url": "https://genius.com/36790", "state": "accepted", "votes": 113, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "[Chorus: Kanye West]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This hook is altered very slightly from the first one – Kanye replaces “you” with “she” in “you done learned a lot from Satan”\n\nNotice that in the first verse he refers to the female as “her”, while in the second he talks to her directly, addressing her as “you”", "annotation_id": "36762", "url": "https://genius.com/36762", "state": "accepted", "votes": 53, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Why we argue anyway? Oh, I forgot, it’s summertime", "vault_lines": ["Why we argue anyway? Oh, I forgot, it's summertime"], "meaning": "Summer heat makes you sweat when you fuck, break dishes when you fight and makes your eyes wander more due to attractive young ladies showing more flesh. It’s also when many artists go on tour: another casualty of tour.\n\nAs an alternative interpretation it could be that Kanye and his girl find any reason to fight. This interpretation is likely, since Ye implies they are arguing so frequently that he tends to forget the reason why they were even arguing in the first place.", "annotation_id": "36536", "url": "https://genius.com/36536", "state": "accepted", "votes": 172, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "When the sun go down, it’s the magic hour, the magic hour And outta all the colors that’ll fill up the skies You got green on your mind, I can see it in your eyes", "vault_lines": ["When the sun go down, it's the magic hour, the magic hour", "And outta all the colors that'll fill up the skies", "You got green on your mind, I can see it in your eyes"], "meaning": "In that magic hour I seen good Christians\nMake rash decisions, oh she do it\n\nWhile in the first verse Kanye was referring to a different meaning of the “magic hour”, in photography and cinematography, the “magic hour” is the most beautiful time of the day, when the glow of a sunset (or sunrise) lights the Earth in a pleasing way. Kanye admires the beauty of the sun and the colors of scenery, but the only color his girl thinks about is the color of money and jealousy. Kanye ain’t saying she’s a gold digger (well actually he kind of is..)\n\n‘Green’ could also be interpreted as marijuana. The latter part of the last line would suggest that the lady Ye is referring to is high and he can tell from her bloodshot eyes. However, the first meaning fits the song’s theme better: lust.", "annotation_id": "36535", "url": "https://genius.com/36535", "state": "accepted", "votes": 100, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Hood phenomenon, the LeBron of rhyme", "vault_lines": ["Hood phenomenon, the LeBron of rhyme"], "meaning": "Went from most hated to the champion God flow, I guess that’s a feeling only me and LeBron know\n\nBoth men were raised mostly by their mothers in the mid-west United States. Both have told or hinted of a past in “the hood” (LeBron actually lived in housing projects while Kanye’s upbringing was more middle-class).\n\nThe “phenomenon” label is better applied to Lebron, as his skills were evident at a young age, while Kanye’s rhyme skills have been more of a slow burner.\n\nIn real life, the two are actually good buddies:", "annotation_id": "36239", "url": "https://genius.com/36239", "state": "accepted", "votes": 197, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "I know I’m preachin’ to the congregation We love Jesus, but you done learned a lot from Satan, haha I mean, a nigga did a lot of waiting We ain’t married, but tonight I need some consummation", "vault_lines": ["We ain't married, but tonight I need some consummation"], "meaning": "A play on the idiom “preaching to the choir” which means trying to convince a person or group of a belief that they already believe in. In this case, the girl is a Christian just like Kanye, but her invocation to sinning is similar to the way Satan tempts humans to sin.\n\nConsummation is the first time a couple has sexual intercourse following their marriage, which serves to officially complete their union. Eventually, Kanye admits to her tempting and “devilish” characteristics and wants to skip the wedding bells to get right down to some sinsations. Besides, despite all the philosophies of good and right presented to us throughout life, it’s often the bad and naughty things that we tend to remember and learn from.\n\nAlso notice how Kanye leaves a 6-7 second gap between Kanye’s last stated line until he finally says “I mean a nigga did a lot of waiting”.", "annotation_id": "36222", "url": "https://genius.com/36222", "state": "accepted", "votes": 356, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "The crib Scarface, could it be more Tony?", "vault_lines": ["The crib Scarface, could it be more Tony?"], "meaning": "In the movie Scarface, the main character Tony Montana lives in a very baller home (crib). Kanye turns the noun “Tony” into an adjective, which is a fairly common way that rappers create slang phrases.", "annotation_id": "36221", "url": "https://genius.com/36221", "state": "accepted", "votes": 177, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Why you standing there with your face screwed up? Don’t leave while you’re hot, that’s how Ma$e screwed up Throwin’ shit around, the whole place screwed up Maybe I should call Mase, so he could pray for us", "vault_lines": ["Why you standing there with your face screwed up?", "Don't leave while you're hot, that's how Ma$e screwed up", "Throwin' shit around, the whole place screwed up"], "meaning": "Rapper Mase left the rap game after some great success, especially with his debut album Harlem World. Kanye doesn’t want his girl to ruin their relationship – like Ma$e did his lucrative music career – by leaving while things are still good.\n\nAfter giving up hip-hop Ma$e became a pastor, making him the go-to guy for rappers dealing with religious issues.\n\nKanye West may also be gifting us a double entendre: “hot,” referring both to Mase’s fame/success and Kanye’s lady’s anger. In other words, he advises the the girl not to leave while she’s “hot” (heated, pissed off), like Mase exiting the rap game when he was “hot” (famous, successful).", "annotation_id": "36217", "url": "https://genius.com/36217", "state": "accepted", "votes": 268, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Hard to be humble when you stunting on a jumbotron I’m looking at her like, “This what you really wanted, huh?”", "vault_lines": ["Hard to be humble when you stunting on a jumbotron", "I'm looking at her like, \"This what you really wanted, huh?\""], "meaning": "Jumbotrons are those giant screens displayed in sports arenas, which often show members of the audience – especially celebrities like Kanye. Since Kanye has been so successful as an artist he finds it hard to be modest.\n\nThe LeBron James comparison continues: both are on a jumbotron; LeBron while playing basketball, Kanye being a famous member of the audience. But even more important, both Kanye and LeBron are hated for their braggadocio when they are on the big screen – Kanye’s girl seems to be bored by this.\n\nKanye finds this confusing, as he believed his fame is what attracted her to him in the first place. What is wrong now?", "annotation_id": "36211", "url": "https://genius.com/36211", "state": "accepted", "votes": 134, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "line": "Put your hands to the constellations The way you look should be a sin, you my sensation, uh", "vault_lines": ["The way you look should be a sin, you my sensation, uh"], "meaning": "Kanye’s girl’s sexiness tempts him with sinful thoughts, leading him to believe that dressing so provocatively and looking so good should be a sin itself. In church people raise their hands to praise God, and ‘Ye implies this might be the best thing to do when you see a girl as beautiful as her.\n\nHe furthers the idea by pronouncing “sensation” as “sinsation,” thus making it sound like the girl satisfies his sinful desires.", "annotation_id": "36210", "url": "https://genius.com/36210", "state": "accepted", "votes": 386, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Devil In a New Dress", "status": "done", "targets": 42, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Kanye-west-devil-in-a-new-dress-lyrics", "genius_title": "Devil In a New Dress", "genius_id": 1207, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Diamonds", "line": "Ricky Rozay (Hey) J. Cole, Montana", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "For the intro of “Diamonds”, Montana shouts out himself, Rick Ross aka Ricky Rozay, J. Cole and the track itself.\n\nThis song is only collaboration between French Montana and J. Cole as of 2018.\n\nRoss and French have collaborated several times on each others projects as French was signed to Ross’s Maybach Music Group Label from 2012 – 2016. Some of their most notable collaborations being “Nobody” and “Stay Schemin'” which are both Ross songs.\n\nRicky Rozay is a nickname of Ross and a play on the French wine Rosé which he has endorsed in his music several times.", "annotation_id": "13540325", "url": "https://genius.com/13540325", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Diamonds", "line": "Make sure this car ride slow, Rozay, J. Cole", "vault_lines": ["Make sure this car ride slow, Rozay, J. Cole"], "meaning": "Drive slow homie.\nThe car’s gonna go slow with Ross in it.\n\n\n\nThough imagine being the poor car in the video for Fed Up, since that had Ross and DJ Khaled sat next to each other in it.", "annotation_id": "1583986", "url": "https://genius.com/1583986", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Diamonds", "line": "Staples Center, get your head full of staples, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Staples Center, get your head full of staples, nigga"], "meaning": "Since he’s in a different tax bracket, courtside seats are a normal thing for him. Also note the dual use of “staples.” The first referring to the sports arena, and the second referring to staples used to fix a gunshot wound.", "annotation_id": "1574377", "url": "https://genius.com/1574377", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Diamonds", "line": "She say I’m just like Sinatra", "vault_lines": ["She say I'm just like Sinatra"], "meaning": "Frank Sinatra is considered one of the most famous and well respected jazz and swing singers. His voice and personality had a certain uniqueness to them which make him remembered to this day.", "annotation_id": "1572152", "url": "https://genius.com/1572152", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Diamonds", "line": "It’s a real nigga revolution, real nigga revolution", "vault_lines": ["It's a real nigga revolution, real nigga revolution"], "meaning": "An adaptation of Gil Scott-Heron’s piece “The Revolution Will Not Be Televised.” Revolutionary change won’t come exactly in a concrete form but more in the form of an abstract idea within everyone.", "annotation_id": "1556021", "url": "https://genius.com/1556021", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Diamonds", "line": "Strapped with Iron Sheik", "vault_lines": ["Know I'm draped like a king, strapped with Iron Sheik"], "meaning": "A reference to the Iron Sheik, a former professional wrestler. Ross has “muscle” or a firemarm on his side.", "annotation_id": "1410697", "url": "https://genius.com/1410697", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Diamonds", "line": "Call my chick Cleopatra, she got rings on her toes", "vault_lines": ["Call my chick Cleopatra, she got rings on her toes"], "meaning": "Cleopatra was the only female pharaoh of Egypt. She had a relationship with the emperor of the Roman Empire, Caesar, so if he calls his chick Cleopatra, he indicates that he’s a king/emperor as he did in the previous line too. He also treats his chick like a queen, endowing her with as much gold as she can handle – and maybe some would say, that she was pretty hot too:", "annotation_id": "1309383", "url": "https://genius.com/1309383", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Diamonds", "line": "On some ’Ville shit", "vault_lines": ["On some 'Ville shit"], "meaning": "“Ville” being short for Fayetteville, his hometown. He mentions the Ville in many of his songs.", "annotation_id": "1289910", "url": "https://genius.com/1289910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Diamonds", "line": "Forty niggas strapped up, yeah, 49ers", "vault_lines": ["Forty niggas strapped up, yeah, 49ers"], "meaning": "A play on the NFL team, the San Francisco 49ers and the fact that French has 40 goons strapped up with 9mm pistols.", "annotation_id": "1259944", "url": "https://genius.com/1259944", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Diamonds", "line": "Playin’ with them falcons like I’m from Decatur", "vault_lines": ["Playin' with them falcons like I'm from Decatur"], "meaning": "Falcons is synonymous with birds as in cocaine. He plays with bricks of cocaine as if he is from Decatur, a suburb of Atlanta, home of the NFL football team, the Atlanta Falcons.", "annotation_id": "1258249", "url": "https://genius.com/1258249", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
