{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down South", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Jadakiss-down-south-lyrics", "genius_title": "Down South", "genius_id": 7194751, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down for Life", "line": "DJ Khaled DJ Khaled! Another one! We The Best Music!", "vault_lines": ["Here come another one, Khaled"], "meaning": "These are DJ Khaled’s trademark slogans that he often proclaims in the intro and outro of his songs since the 2000s. They appeared on the previous Grateful singles “Shining,” “I’m the One,” “To The Max,” and “Wild Thoughts.”\n\n\n\nWe The Best Music is Khaled’s record label that he founded in 2008.", "annotation_id": "12301999", "url": "https://genius.com/12301999", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down for Life", "line": "Here come another one, Khaled Here come another one, Khaled (Another one)", "vault_lines": ["Here come another one, Khaled"], "meaning": "“Another one” is one of DJ Khaled’s trademark slogans that he often proclaims in his songs along with “DJ Khaled” and “We the Best Music.”\n\nIt’s his long-standing way of announcing that he has another hit on his hands.", "annotation_id": "12267178", "url": "https://genius.com/12267178", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down for Life", "line": "The season success, 6 reasons I’m blessed Secret societies, he seems so possessed", "vault_lines": ["Secret societies, he seems so possessed"], "meaning": "In Indian culture there are 6 seasons as opposed to 4 in Western culture, due to their spiritual belief. This creates a connection to the other spiritual reference of being “possessed” and the number “6” – According to the bible, symbolizes man, human weakness, the manifestation of sin and Satan.\n\nRick Ross’s career has been plagued by allegations that he’s part of a Luciferian secret society, just like  his mentor and idol Jay-Z.\n\nJay and Rozay even collaborated on “Free Mason” and also vehemently denied any involvement with a Luciferian cult in “The Devil Is A Lie.” The allegations went so far that real Masons had to disavow any Rick Ross connection in 2012:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/y6etOKeDrmc?t=34s", "annotation_id": "12210094", "url": "https://genius.com/12210094", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down for Life", "status": "done", "targets": 3, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dj-khaled-down-for-life-lyrics", "genius_title": "Down for Life", "genius_id": 3117586, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "Red, monkey", "vault_lines": ["I'm repping for the rich, red, monkey"], "meaning": "Red Monkey jeans. $739 on Amazon\nA bit ugly for my taste", "annotation_id": "56817", "url": "https://genius.com/56817", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "Miami, FL", "vault_lines": ["Welcome to Miami, FL"], "meaning": "Rick Ross considers Miami, FL to be his home", "annotation_id": "30424", "url": "https://genius.com/30424", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "Have you running up north like a runaway slave", "vault_lines": ["Have you running up north like a runaway slave"], "meaning": "Because the Dirty South is so powerful, especially in the rap world, Bun B suggests that those who cannot hold their own (most likely the rappers that Ludacris would have smoked like Marijuana, above) should flee to the North, much like the African-American slaves of pre-Civil War American History had to\n\nFor more information on this part of History, check out the Wikipedia article on the Underground Railroad", "annotation_id": "30420", "url": "https://genius.com/30420", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "You can get your issue like your ass subscribed", "vault_lines": ["You can get your issue like your ass subscribed"], "meaning": "To “get your issue” is to receive just consequences for the sins you’ve committed. That is, it is a karmic punishment of getting “what’s coming to you.” Bun B’s song “Get Cha Issue” from his album II Trill repeats this theme toward different detrimental members of society\n\nHere, Bun B is punning on the phrase by suggesting that the “issue” is an issue of a magazine or other periodical, to which one should subscribe in order to be assured proper delivery", "annotation_id": "30419", "url": "https://genius.com/30419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "Can’t find a triller nigga than me if you had the Hubble", "vault_lines": ["Can't find a triller nigga than me if you had the Hubble"], "meaning": "Trill (True + Real) is an adjective that Bun B uses in much of his music, including his first two solo albums, Trill and II Trill. Bun B is saying that he is highly respected in the South, so much so that if you were to use a high powered space telescope such as the Hubble, you still would not be able to find someone more legitimate\n\nThis is also Bun B’s way of sending a shout-out to his hometown, Houston, TX, in that NASA, which controls the Hubble, is based there. Bun B also uses similar metaphors in many other songs", "annotation_id": "30413", "url": "https://genius.com/30413", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "Streets on fire like raw scalps with a perm", "vault_lines": ["Got the streets on fire like raw scalps with a perm"], "meaning": "The Dirty South hip-hop movement is so good, according to Bun B, that it has the “streets on fire,” meaning that the ghettos and hoods of the South are unstoppable in their raw energy\n\nTo further his point, Bun B returns to the haircare analogy with which Ludacris began the song. Hair that has been treated to too many perms can cause the scalp to burn, much like the streets “on fire”", "annotation_id": "30409", "url": "https://genius.com/30409", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "Must be on sherm", "vault_lines": ["Anybody think they can beat it must be on sherm"], "meaning": "Sherm is a spliff or cigarette dipped in PCP. It is quite a powerful drug, and like any PCP-delivery mechanism produces in its users delusions of grandeur, which can be the only explanation for someone who thinks they can beat the rappers from the Dirty South, according to Bun B", "annotation_id": "30407", "url": "https://genius.com/30407", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "The game in a choke hold No I won’t let go", "vault_lines": ["The game in a choke hold", "No I won't let go"], "meaning": "Here Rick Ross is suggesting that he controls the Rap “game” and that he is the master of Hip-Hop. Furthermore, he refuses to relinquish this position of power", "annotation_id": "30406", "url": "https://genius.com/30406", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "Khaled", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "DJ Khaled, a friend of Ross', was not particularly influential when this song was released. Therefore, other than perhaps giving a shoutout to his friend, this line is of questionable meaning", "annotation_id": "30403", "url": "https://genius.com/30403", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "If it ain’t a black truck it’s a quarter million Maz’", "vault_lines": ["If it ain't a black truck it's a quarter million Maz'"], "meaning": "Ross again returns to his automobile collection, suggesting that when you see him next, he’ll either be in a “black truck” or a Maserati that cost him $250,000", "annotation_id": "30400", "url": "https://genius.com/30400", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "E-class on the cell; discussing another mill’", "vault_lines": ["E-class on the cell; discussing another mill'"], "meaning": "Ross, who often raps about cars, is here stating that on the other end of a phone conversation he’s having waits a Mercedes E-class ready to be purchased by him, for the right price. Apparently the current owner is holding out until the price rises by at least another $1,000,000", "annotation_id": "30398", "url": "https://genius.com/30398", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "Credit cards don’t need ’em All the birds won’t feed ’em", "vault_lines": ["Credit cards don't need 'em", "All the birds won't feed 'em"], "meaning": "Ricky doesn’t want to get into debt buying fripperies for his “birds” (ladyfriends)", "annotation_id": "30393", "url": "https://genius.com/30393", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "Put ’em in a ash tray throw away the roaches", "vault_lines": ["Put 'em in a ash tray throw away the roaches"], "meaning": "Again, Ludacris continues his rappers-as-marijuana metaphor, suggesting that most other rappers are as worthless as empty ashes or “roaches,” which are the unsmokeable butts of joints", "annotation_id": "30386", "url": "https://genius.com/30386", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "Rappers get smoked like I rolled in some paper", "vault_lines": ["Cause rappers get smoked like I rolled in some paper"], "meaning": "Here, Ludacris continues his rappers-as-marijuana metaphor, shifting the imagery onto other rappers, who are now the marijuana, while Ludacris, rolling them up like joints, is the smoker (killer)", "annotation_id": "30385", "url": "https://genius.com/30385", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "Catch a contact like you inhaled Luda vapors", "vault_lines": ["So catch a contact like you inhaled Luda vapors"], "meaning": "This is the first of Ludacris' rappers-as-marijuana metaphors. Here, Ludacris is suggesting that his lyrics are so powerful that just by listening to his song, you might get a contact high, similar to if he were marijuana being smoked nearby", "annotation_id": "30384", "url": "https://genius.com/30384", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "Bathing apers", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Bathing Apes (apers) are a expensive kind of shoe, featuring a star on the side. They are also featured in the song “Crank Dat” by Soulja Boy Tell ‘Em", "annotation_id": "30382", "url": "https://genius.com/30382", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "The mule and the acres", "vault_lines": ["I'm out to get that paper, the mule and the acres"], "meaning": "40 acres and a mule was the 1865 Union solution to help former Black slaves of the Confederacy start a family farm. As Union armies occupied Confederate lands, some lands and mules were distributed, forty acres and one mule at a time, to some of the freed slaves", "annotation_id": "30376", "url": "https://genius.com/30376", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "I’m yellow with that purp like I came with the Lakers", "vault_lines": ["And I'm yellow with that purp like I came with the Lakers"], "meaning": "Purp is a particularly strong and expensive variety of Marijuana. To yellow marijuana means taking a very strong hit from a bong, filling the chamber completely with yellow smoke\n\nHere, Ludacris is playing off of the LA Lakers' colors (yellow and purple) in order to explain that he is a masterful weed-smoker, especially with expensive, strong weed", "annotation_id": "30372", "url": "https://genius.com/30372", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "Then I make ’em come around like curl activator", "vault_lines": ["Then I make 'em come around like curl activator"], "meaning": "Curl activator is a substance used to maintain a Jheri curl by keeping it greasy and preventing it from drying out\n\nThis is the second Jheri curl reference, suggesting that Ludacris' abilities to change people’s personalities goes so far that not only can he “turn lovers into haters” but can turn them right back around, much like curl activator does to Jheri curled hair", "annotation_id": "30371", "url": "https://genius.com/30371", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Down in tha Dirty", "line": "My car just got a Jheri curl the paint’s still drippin’", "vault_lines": ["My car just got a Jheri curl the paint's still drippin'"], "meaning": "A Jheri curl was a popular hairstyle for African Americans in the ‘80s. It required two different 'wash-and-wear’ treatments, the second being a chemical treatment\n\nHere Ludacris is setting the scene for his later hair analogy also associated with the Jheri curl by suggesting that his car’s paint, just redone, is dripping much like the chemical treatment would leave hair", "annotation_id": "30370", "url": "https://genius.com/30370", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
