{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "Sniper Gang Music 2, yeah Sniper Gang Music 2, yah feel me", "vault_lines": ["Sniper Gang Music 2, yeah Sniper Gang Music 2, yah feel me"], "meaning": "Sniper Gang is kodak’s clothing line, streetwear and merchandise brand", "annotation_id": "15760351", "url": "https://genius.com/15760351", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "Where we play football and sell dope, man", "vault_lines": ["Where we play football and sell dope, man"], "meaning": "Florida is a state known for football. A cleveland.com ranking confirmed that Florida is the most represented state in the NFL.\n\nHowever, Florida is also known to be instrumental in the drug game, especially South Florida. The southeastern region of Florida is known for it’s ports, an area where drug traffickers smuggle cocaine and other drugs into the country.\n\nThe documentary Cocaine Cowboys explains how notorious the South Florida area was for drug dealing. Allegedly, much of Miami’s skyline and other popular attractions were funded through drug money.", "annotation_id": "14204789", "url": "https://genius.com/14204789", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "Got it off credit cards, shit, yeah, larceny", "vault_lines": ["Got it off credit cards, shit, yeah, larceny"], "meaning": "Kodak is saying that a lot of the items and “wealth” that he was able to possess were through fraud.  According to the Sun Sentinel, Florida is the leading state in the U.S. for fraud; especially credit card fraud.\n\nLarceny is just a fancy word for “taking others' personal property”", "annotation_id": "14204632", "url": "https://genius.com/14204632", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "Been jumped off the porch, since 14, I had lawyers", "vault_lines": ["Been jumped off the porch, since 14, I had lawyers"], "meaning": "“Jumping off the porch” is a slang term used in Florida that says that you have been living a tough life. Whether you are involved in dealing drugs, theft, or hustling in any kind of way. You weren’t sheltered by your parents, and you grew up fast.", "annotation_id": "14204588", "url": "https://genius.com/14204588", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "The whole Tri county, I love the Tri county but I hate everybody", "vault_lines": ["The whole Tri county, I love the Tri county but I hate everybody"], "meaning": "Lil Kodak is expressing his loyalty for the Tri-County area of Florida made up of Miami-Dade, Broward, and Palm Beach County. These three counties are better known as South Florida.", "annotation_id": "14204551", "url": "https://genius.com/14204551", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "But it’s still a struggle so you can’t get too comfortable ’Cause niggas see that money coming and they try to come for you But they don’t know that this famous shit mean nothin’ to you Still keep a gun or two and I still dump at you", "vault_lines": ["But it's still a struggle so you can't get too comfortable", "'Cause niggas see that money coming and they try to come for you", "But they don't know that this famous shit mean nothin' to you", "Still keep a gun or two and I still dump at you"], "meaning": "The people from Kodak’s hood may take his wealth as a sign of him becoming soft—even though Kodak is financially stable, he’s still a project baby, willing to defend himself the same way he did when he was in the hood.\n\nIt’s like, it’s like once a nigga got in this lil situation everybody stop fucking with me, dawg\nThey throwing salt on my name\n\nIn January 2018, Kodak ran into trouble with the law after being charged with possession of a firearm by a felon (among other charges) after live-streaming on Instagram. The charges were later dropped the next month.", "annotation_id": "13937600", "url": "https://genius.com/13937600", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "I’m Jordan in this motherfucker, puttin’ mad numbers up", "vault_lines": ["I'm Jordan in this motherfucker, puttin' mad numbers up"], "meaning": "Kodak most likely references basketball legend Michael Jordan, who is considered the best basketball player of all time and set or break a large amount of records during his career.", "annotation_id": "13890707", "url": "https://genius.com/13890707", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "They gave my dawg a long sentence, he can’t even read a book", "vault_lines": ["They gave my dawg a long sentence, he can't even read a book"], "meaning": "This is a Critique of our judicial system and our education system.\nHe refers to a sentence as if it has literary meaning but it speaks of his sentence from a judge in a judicial setting.\nWhile discuss how the system set him up by making him illiterate or incapable of reading at a certain age level.", "annotation_id": "13788057", "url": "https://genius.com/13788057", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "I’m a Haitian boy, I’m draped up in Cuban link Haitian but I’m draped up in Cuban link", "vault_lines": ["I'm a Haitian boy, I'm draped up in Cuban link", "Haitian but I'm draped up in Cuban link"], "meaning": "Cuban links and jewelry are a prized item in rap, dating back to the Wu-Tang solo classic, Only Built 4 Cuban Linx, so it makes sense to be bragging about them.\n\nKodak makes a reference to his Haitian nationality, comparing this to the jewelry he’s wearing. This isn’t Kodak’s first time bringing up his ethnicity, as he also mentioned in his verse to A Boogie Wit Da Hoodie’s 2017 hit, “Drowning”;\n\nI ain’t Jamaican, bitch I’m Haitian, but I got them shottas", "annotation_id": "13730531", "url": "https://genius.com/13730531", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "Boy, I’m a young bull come charging when he running you We ain’t over there in public houses like we growin’ too", "vault_lines": ["Boy, I'm a young bull come charging when he running you", "We ain't over there in public houses like we growin' too"], "meaning": "Bulls are known for charging at full speed to attack, specifically in the sport of bullfighting. This line connects with the previous Jordan line, as Michael Jordan famously played for the Chicago Bulls.\n\n\n\nKodak Black wants his listeners to know that he has moved out of the hood since growing in the rap game and increasing his popularity.", "annotation_id": "13727454", "url": "https://genius.com/13727454", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "Livin’ like the OG slime peanut butter Nigga gotta eat, I’m talkin’ to the Last Supper", "vault_lines": ["Livin' like the OG slime peanut butter", "Nigga gotta eat, I'm talkin' to the Last Supper"], "meaning": "An “OG” is an older and established figure in the street life and even in the rap game. Kodak believes he has established enough riches and power to live the same type of lifestyle.\n\nThe Last Supper is a painting by Leonardo Da Vinci which depicts Jesus having dinner with his apostles and is one of the most recognized paintings the world. Kodak plays on peanut butter by using this to equate his metaphorical hunger to that of the Last Supper, as it could literally be his last meal due to his lifestyle.", "annotation_id": "13727278", "url": "https://genius.com/13727278", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "Livin’ like The Huxtables, but still eatin’ Lunchables", "vault_lines": ["Livin' like The Huxtables, but still eatin' Lunchables"], "meaning": "Kodak Black references the Huxtables family, from the iconic Cosby Show. The Huxtables were a very upper-middle class family with humble beginnings.\n\nLunchables are known as a very ‘suburban’ or ‘privileged’ food, and are known as a luxury only wealthier kids get to enjoy.\n\nKodak is living modestly, like the Huxtables, but he’s also balancing this with luxurious items, such as Lunchables.\n\nThese lines are similar to J.Cole’s in his “Too Deep For The Intro” track;\n\nThe other half is close to the cliff like Mrs Huxtable\nThese boys got them hoslters and clips they pack like Lunchables", "annotation_id": "13726347", "url": "https://genius.com/13726347", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "Hit a lick in a stolen car with no headlights I’ll stop at the Wing Stop, but not the red light I killed the whole DMV, no star shit", "vault_lines": ["Hit a lick in a stolen car with no headlights", "I'll stop at the Wing Stop, but not the red light", "I killed the whole DMV, no star shit"], "meaning": "Wing Stop is a chain of restaurants that focus on chicken wings.\n\n\n\nRick Ross is a lover of WIng Stop, he even owns over 9 locations:\n\nI just love Wingstop, As you can see, the energy in here is always fun. It’s always youthful. Not only that, you know they got my favorite lemon pepper wings in the world, so it’s just a natural attraction.\n\nRoss has referenced Wing Stop is several of his verses, most notably in 2014’s “The Devil Is A Lie”:\n\n Wingstop, fat boy need a 10 piece\n\n\n\nKodak will happily stop at a Wing Stop to support Ross but not a red light, as it is easier to rob and/or kill someone at a red light—trying to run away means running through the red light which may attract the cops.\n\nNote the wordplay of “lick,” being expressed with Kodak’s indulgence of Wing Stop.\n\nDMV is also an abbreviation of Department of Motor Vehicles, where drivers go to receive official documents such as their license and registration. Stealing a vehicle without headlights and running red lights would result in major driving offenses.\n\nDMV could also refer to the D.C., Maryland, and Virginia area, where Kodak may have performed outstanding shows.", "annotation_id": "13719166", "url": "https://genius.com/13719166", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "Heard I was a genius, when coming to the numbers", "vault_lines": ["Heard I was a genius, when coming to the numbers"], "meaning": "Ross is no stranger to high numbers. He has a reported net worth of $35 million dollars and has a discography of well selling albums.\n\nHis discography of nine includes three gold albums; Teflon Don, God Forgives, I Don’t and Trilla. He also has one certified platinum album: Port of Miami.\n\nHis lowest selling album Black Market sold 75,000 units.", "annotation_id": "13719020", "url": "https://genius.com/13719020", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "Yeah, bitch, I’m a Florida Boy", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, bitch, I'm a Florida Boy"], "meaning": "Ross, T-Pain and Kodak are all from Florida, they all make sure to pay homage to their home state on this track.\n\n\nRoss grew up in the Carol City neighborhood. On his 2015 album Black Market he released the song “Carol City” as a bonus track which pays homage to his old neighbor hood.\nT-Pain grew up in Tallahassee, which is the capital of Florida and the state’s 7th largest city.\nKodak grew up in Pompano Beach, a smaller city that is found along the Atlantic Coast.", "annotation_id": "13718562", "url": "https://genius.com/13718562", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "It was back in 2015, me and Baby linked But I’m just a type of young nigga, listen ’fore I speak I’d be flawed to say Wayne ain’t influence me I don’t play both sides, nigga, I keep it too G", "vault_lines": ["But I'm just a type of young nigga, listen 'fore I speak", "I'd be flawed to say Wayne ain't influence me", "I don't play both sides, nigga, I keep it too G"], "meaning": "Kodak Black has had a relationship with legendary rapper Birdman since 2015 which was when his career was in it’s early stages, however he also acknowledges that Lil Wayne was a big musical influence on him.\n\nAlthough Birdman and Wayne have been beefing for quite some time, Kodak recognizes both of their greatness and would never let that get in between his relationships with either of them.\n\nOn Kodak’s last project Project Baby 2, he had individual songs with both Wayne and Birdman.\n\n“Flaw” is Florida slang meaning to be fake.", "annotation_id": "13716698", "url": "https://genius.com/13716698", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "This ain’t a Maybach but my Audi A8 seats go way back", "vault_lines": ["This ain't a Maybach but my Audi A8 seats go way back"], "meaning": "Maybach vehicles are one of the most luxurious cars ever made and are known to have an extreme amount of room, for a price tag of about $200,000.\n\nHowever, for about $85,000, Kodak can get an Audi A8 which is still a luxury vehicle, with the 2018 version including a lot of room in the interior, paralleling a Maybach.\n\n\n\nRoss is known for his love of Maybach cars and not only named his label after the brand, but also has a Maybach series of songs. T-Pain also featured on the hook of “Maybach Music II.”\n\nKodak could be using the comparison of the two cars two symbolize himself not being quite on the same level as Ross; he is in a lower tier of cars like the Audi R8.", "annotation_id": "13716672", "url": "https://genius.com/13716672", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "Jonny Dang seen a lot of chains", "vault_lines": ["Jonny Dang seen a lot of chains"], "meaning": "Johnny Dang is a famous celebrity jeweler, best known for working with Paul Wall in creating the grillz movement. He now supplies grills and other custom jewelry to all of the biggest rappers in the game.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BebZljWBx-O/?hl=en&taken-by=johnnydangandco\n\nHowever, in 2013 Dang sued Rick Ross for $90k in unpaid fees for watches.", "annotation_id": "13716602", "url": "https://genius.com/13716602", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "Sidelines, can’t be switching up like I’m Ray Lewis", "vault_lines": ["Sidelines, can't be switching up like I'm Ray Lewis"], "meaning": "Ray Lewis is a former NFL player who played as a linebacker. Throughout his entire 17 year career, Lewis played only for the Baltimore Ravens, never switching up his team.\n\nHowever, Rick Ross could also be talking about the murder charge that Ray caught in 2000, in which he basically snitched on his friends in order to have his charges dropped. Following the street code, Ross would never do this.\n\n\n\nThis line could also refer to an incidient during the 2017-18 NFL season where Lewis was spotted on the Ravens sideline before a game, kneeling with current players. In a video response to the sideline activities Lewis states that he was not protesting, but rather praying with the team.", "annotation_id": "13716568", "url": "https://genius.com/13716568", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "line": "Old school, big-ass rims, sittin’ like Tonka Toys (Tonka Toys, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Old school, big-ass rims, sittin' like Tonka Toys (Tonka Toys, yeah)"], "meaning": "Tonka Toys is the company that makes the popular Tonka Trucks, which are all different types of toy trucks for children, which normally have large tires.\n\n\n\nOld school cars sitting on huge rims is a very popular style in Florida, as seen here by one of Kodak’s new cars, and Oldsmobile Cutlass on 30 inch gold rims\n\n\n\nhttp://s754.photobucket.com/user/sploaterboi/media/Whipsace/20170131_154445_zpshdfhvppf.jpg.html", "annotation_id": "13716523", "url": "https://genius.com/13716523", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Florida Boy", "status": "done", "targets": 20, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-florida-boy-lyrics", "genius_title": "Florida Boy", "genius_id": 3490155, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Fluorescent Ink", "line": "This ink is why they feel me This ink is why they feel me, Lord This ink is why they feel me This ink is why they feel me This ink is why they feel me, Lord This ink is why they feel me Double MG, thank you, this the real me", "vault_lines": ["This ink is why they feel me", "This ink is why they feel me, Lord", "Double MG, thank you, this the real me"], "meaning": "Leave it to Wale to make the hook have a double meaning:\n\nThe 1st meaning is obvious: the reason people like what he does/stands for is due to his rap lines (written in ink)\n\n\n\nthe 2nd meaning is less obvious and applies only to females:\nWomen love to feel/trace tattoo’s. It gives them an excuse to touch him", "annotation_id": "961684", "url": "https://genius.com/961684", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Fluorescent Ink", "line": "My relevance never ending", "vault_lines": ["Double MG pimping, my relevance never ending"], "meaning": "Ironically, Wale’s debut Attention Deficit got little attention from the masses back in 2009; it wasn’t until he hooked up with Rick Ross that he started making any waves", "annotation_id": "868019", "url": "https://genius.com/868019", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Fluorescent Ink", "line": "This ink is why they feel me", "vault_lines": ["This ink is why they feel me"], "meaning": "A pen’s ink is a central image in much hip-hop.  See this famous Rakim couplet, for example:\n\n I start to think, and then I sink\nInto the paper, like I was ink\nWhen I’m writing, I’m trapped in between the lines\nI escape when I finish the rhyme", "annotation_id": "862270", "url": "https://genius.com/862270", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Fluorescent Ink", "line": "Bonnie and Clyde story, Mick and Mallory A killer in them sheets, they bring that ish up out of me", "vault_lines": ["Bonnie and Clyde story, Mick and Mallory", "A killer in them sheets, they bring that ish up out of me"], "meaning": "Stalley mentions two infamous murdering couples in order to pun on “killing” his ladies in bed.  Bonnie Parker and Clyde Barrow were a bank-robbing, policeman-killing team in the 1930s whose exploits were immortalized in a 1967 movie\n\n\n\nMickey and Mallory Knox are the central serial-killing couple in Oliver Stone’s 1994 film Natural Born Killers.  They are portrayed by Woody Harrelson and Juliette Lewis", "annotation_id": "862259", "url": "https://genius.com/862259", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Fluorescent Ink", "line": "I got a couple old schools and some new girls They like old jewels and new pearls", "vault_lines": ["They like old jewels and new pearls"], "meaning": "Stalley uses some “old”/“new” wordplay here to discuss his myriad of girlfriends and their tastes in gifts", "annotation_id": "862257", "url": "https://genius.com/862257", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Fluorescent Ink", "line": "Little bit of Chanel, decorate your appearance Double Cs on your purses, perfect, I get the message", "vault_lines": ["Little bit of Chanel, decorate your appearance", "Double Cs on your purses, perfect, I get the message"], "meaning": "Wordplay ‘Le strikes again:\n\nWhile Wale follows the footsteps of his boss Rozay here, commenting on his girl’s accessories; Double C’s is also referring to the CC: portion of a letter or email, where you “carbon copy” another recipient so they “get the message”.\n\n\n\n^ The Chanel logo is two “C’s”", "annotation_id": "859500", "url": "https://genius.com/859500", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Fluorescent Ink", "line": "I recline in my seat, number 9s on my feet", "vault_lines": ["I recline in my seat, number 9s on my feet"], "meaning": "Wale channels his inner sneaker-head, bragging about his OG Jordan Nine’s, which were the first Jordan’s that Michael didn’t wear in a game", "annotation_id": "859496", "url": "https://genius.com/859496", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
