{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Freaky Hoe", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-freaky-hoe-lyrics", "genius_title": "Freaky Hoe", "genius_id": 2885091, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "But his daughter in the night never pierced her ears", "vault_lines": ["But his daughter in the night never pierced her ears"], "meaning": "This might be a reference to the book of Leviticus where a law is passed down that states, “You shall not make any cuts on your body.” The man whom Ross is probably talking about is a staunch Christian who believes in the Old Testament ways, thus is reason enough for the daughter to follow suit.\n\nRoss has a teenaged daughter named Toie Roberts.", "annotation_id": "8383756", "url": "https://genius.com/8383756", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "And for the record, know my best man’ll kill a bitch", "vault_lines": ["And for the record, know my best man'll kill a bitch"], "meaning": "According to his 2012 Rolling Stone cover story, Ross decided to become a correctional officer after his best friend got 10 years for trafficking cocaine and heroin. It’s possible enough time has passed for his best friend to be released from jail, so hopefully he can make the Ross' upcoming wedding but won’t have to kill anyone.", "annotation_id": "8376242", "url": "https://genius.com/8376242", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Show up at the wedding in my Timberlands", "vault_lines": ["Show up at the wedding in my Timberlands"], "meaning": "In September 2015, Ross announced he got engaged to his girlfriend, Lira Galore. There were rumors that the couple had broken up after a fight, but they later confirmed on social media that they were in fact still together.\n\nTimberland boots are typically meant for everyday use, not a formal occasion like a wedding. Maybe Ross took this GQ article a little too seriously?", "annotation_id": "8376095", "url": "https://genius.com/8376095", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Never said I wouldn’t make my mom cry (no)", "vault_lines": ["Never said I wouldn't make my mom cry (no)"], "meaning": "John Legend actually made someone close to him cry but it wasn’t his mom, it was his wife, supermodel Chrissy Teigen.\n\nAt the 2015 Golden Globes, Legend and Common won the award for Best Original Song for “Glory,” a song from the film Selma.  When the camera cut to Teigen’s reaction, the Chrissy Teigen Crying meme was born.\n\n\n\nhttps://twitter.com/chrissyteigen/status/554459113220870144?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw\n\nhttps://twitter.com/chrissyteigen/status/554459168648609792?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw", "annotation_id": "8376054", "url": "https://genius.com/8376054", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Get all the sneakers, not enough clothes", "vault_lines": ["Get all the sneakers, not enough clothes"], "meaning": "Many individuals of a low net worth are known to buy high priced footwear and neglect to buy the new clothing to compliment the new pair of shoes.", "annotation_id": "8373688", "url": "https://genius.com/8373688", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "So many false allegations, is my image tainted?", "vault_lines": ["So many false allegations, is my image tainted?"], "meaning": "Possibly a reference to Ross being sued by a woman who claims she was drugged and raped by Thaddeus “Black” James—a member of Ross' entourage.\n\nAccording to the woman, who is listed as Jane Doe in the lawsuit, she got into a van with Ross and his crew during Grammy weekend at the W Hotel in Los Angeles. She claims she was drugged and raped.\n\nRoss responded to the allegations in a statement to TMZ where he said:\n\n“It has been brought to my attention that serious allegations have been made against an independent contractor, who’s services I use often. And while I am not directly involved or implicated, as a man and a father, I take this very seriously. I don’t believe any woman’s safety should be compromised and she should never be forced to do anything outside of her will, regardless of the circumstances.”", "annotation_id": "8373496", "url": "https://genius.com/8373496", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Don’t be jealous of me, I keep enough foes", "vault_lines": ["Don't be jealous of me, I keep enough foes"], "meaning": "Even though he’s “pushing a foreign,” you shouldn’t be jealous of Rozay because he’s got a lot of enemies, like Pimpin' Curly. Seriously, do you want to see this man in your nightmares?", "annotation_id": "8373466", "url": "https://genius.com/8373466", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Inmates gave me commissary just because I’m famous Or is it ’cause I’m rich and I know what pain is?", "vault_lines": ["Inmates gave me commissary just because I'm famous", "Or is it 'cause I'm rich and I know what pain is?"], "meaning": "Commissary is a store for provisions available to inmates on occasion.\n\nWhile Ross was incarcerated, the inmates gifted him provisions because he’s rich and famous and understands pain more than the average person.\n\nThis might have been a case of karma coming back around for Ross. Back in June 2015, when Ross was arrested for possession of marijuana (something he’s repeatedly been arrested for), he tweeted saying he bailed out the inmates he met while in jail.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/rickyrozay/status/609016985398038528", "annotation_id": "8369275", "url": "https://genius.com/8369275", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Grinding ’til my new accountant is fully at ease", "vault_lines": ["Grinding 'til my new accountant is fully at ease"], "meaning": "After getting arrested for  kidnapping, aggravated battery, and aggravated assault on June 24, 2015, Ross spent three weeks in jail and was forced to put up two of his homes as well as $500,000 in cash in order to make bail. So now he needs to grind (work hard) in order to make back the money he lost while in jail to put his new accountant at ease.", "annotation_id": "8369157", "url": "https://genius.com/8369157", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Don’t you wish everything could be so perfect?", "vault_lines": ["Don't you wish everything could be so perfect?"], "meaning": "Considering how Ross is recounting his recent struggles and the somber tone of the song, Ross is likely lamenting here. He’s probably alluding to the fact that despite his “Bawse” persona and rich lifestyle, his life isn’t as perfect as it seems. Still, he considers God great for all the blessings he has received.", "annotation_id": "8368856", "url": "https://genius.com/8368856", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "So if I die before I wake Say I, I lived my way", "vault_lines": ["So if I die before I wake", "Say I, I lived my way"], "meaning": "Possibly a reference to Frank Sinatra’s “My Way” which features the lyrics, “I did it my way.”\n\nCoincidentally, a man named Ric Ross was known as the biggest Frank Sinatra fan in the world thanks to his massive collection of Sinatra records, tapes, and memorabilia. Sinatra even told the LA Times, “I’m certain that Ric Ross knows more about me than I do myself.”", "annotation_id": "8361718", "url": "https://genius.com/8361718", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Double R, Ricky Ross, bitch, we been the gang Bottom of the black market, time to rise again", "vault_lines": ["Double R, Ricky Ross, bitch, we been the gang", "Bottom of the black market, time to rise again"], "meaning": "Ross and his Maybach Music Group gang were once on top but now they’ve fallen to the bottom and are ready for a comeback. Ross and MMG’s fall from grace can be attributed to many things; the controversy that ensued after Ross' infamous “molly all in her champagne” line, the legal troubles both Ross and Meek have had, the in-fighting within the crew between Meek and Wale (#UNotMMG), and the malaise that often follows a dominant rap run.", "annotation_id": "8361629", "url": "https://genius.com/8361629", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Mind frame of that boy that was found slain Mild slang, you can tell I been around ’caine", "vault_lines": ["Mind frame of that boy that was found slain", "Mild slang, you can tell I been around 'caine"], "meaning": "The mind frame of a hustler is to make continuous money. Drug dealers are known to work a lot of night shifts and encounter face off with people that want to kill them.", "annotation_id": "8361611", "url": "https://genius.com/8361611", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Why you think I’m on the night shift with the pills?", "vault_lines": ["Why you think I'm on the night shift with the pills?"], "meaning": "Continuing from the previous line, he knows all the nice things can be gone quickly so every day he’s hustling. Much like Drake, he gets his money working the night shift.", "annotation_id": "8361590", "url": "https://genius.com/8361590", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Irritated, uneducated, still scribble my flows", "vault_lines": ["Irritated, uneducated, still scribble my flows"], "meaning": "In an interview with Billboard, Ross revealed he wrote this song in prison, so he was obviously irritated. But despite his unfortunate circumstances, he’s still and writing down new flows and rhymes.", "annotation_id": "8342038", "url": "https://genius.com/8342038", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Head of black music, do you know what rhythm is? Stick your hand in the basket, it gets venomous", "vault_lines": ["Head of black music, do you know what rhythm is?", "Stick your hand in the basket, it gets venomous"], "meaning": "Ross takes us to the world of snake charming with this line. He acts as the head of a metaphorical snake which is black music.\n\n\n\nHe asks if you know what is the meaning of following the snake’s movement in rhythm to the song because he thinks snake charmers use rhythm to charm snakes out of baskets.\n\n\n\nIf you don’t follow the rhythm, you will get stuck in the basket and a bite from a  venomous snake will be your end.\n\n\n\nIn truth, although snakes can hear, they don’t have the ears to hear the music snake charmers typically play using instruments like a pungi or a bansuri. Snake charmers only pretend to hypnotize snakes with music, in reality the snakes are reacting to the instrument in the charmers hands as if they were under attack. So it turns out, snakes don’t actually know what rhythm is.", "annotation_id": "8341805", "url": "https://genius.com/8341805", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Straight player, my chicks finish my sentences", "vault_lines": ["Straight player, my chicks finish my sentences"], "meaning": "Ross has such a good connection with his females they are able to finish his sentences. This means they have synced brains which is a connection indeed.\n\nBAWSE!", "annotation_id": "8341786", "url": "https://genius.com/8341786", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Time to squash to beef, I kill a nemesis", "vault_lines": ["Time to squash to beef, I kill a nemesis"], "meaning": "Ross implies he doesn’t end beef amicably, he just kills his enemies.", "annotation_id": "8341768", "url": "https://genius.com/8341768", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Being down and out was inconceivable When you find out the one you love is just misleading you", "vault_lines": ["When you find out the one you love is just misleading you"], "meaning": "Ross made the Forbes Hip Hop Cash Kings list in 2015, so the idea of him “being down and out” doesn’t seem like a possibility. But thanks to his legal troubles, he had to put up $2 million dollars to make bail so the “inconceivable”  came true.\n\nSeemingly a theme in the Black Market album Ross explores the issue of backstabbers whom he even made a complete song for in Black Dollar: The Mixtape.\n\nHere he once again tells us that some of the closest ones to you will be your end. It’s unclear if someone betrayed Ross in real life or if this is just a general observation. However, there has been speculation about the nature of Ross' relationship with his current girlfriend, Lira Galore, on Twitter and gossip sites. People have brought up old photos of a naked Galore sitting on Meek Mill’s lap as well as Galore tweeting about wanting to marry Wale. Galore addressed the rumors on Twitter.\n\n\n\nAppropriate Swahili proverb: Kikulacho Ki Nguoni Mwako (What ails you is within you).", "annotation_id": "8341573", "url": "https://genius.com/8341573", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Reptile black, Birkin Bamboo handle", "vault_lines": ["Reptile black, Birkin Bamboo handle"], "meaning": "This is Rick Ross' description of a Hermès Birkin bag. The bag is named after English actress and singer Jane Birkin (though she no longer wants her name associated with the crocodile version Ross is likely referencing). A Birkin bag can cost anywhere from $11,550 to $150,000.\n\nDue to the reptile reference, it’s likely Ross is referring in particular to the Hermes Alligator Crocodile Bag, which you can buy for $79,590. Cheap!", "annotation_id": "8341475", "url": "https://genius.com/8341475", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "The jubilation, the euphoria Pushing a foreign in the morning, pick your daughter up Drop your seed off, let the weed up Slow motion, fast forward till we speed up", "vault_lines": ["Drop your seed off, let the weed up", "Slow motion, fast forward till we speed up"], "meaning": "In reference to how awesome Ross' life is, he’s filled with joy because he drives one of his many foreign-brand vehicles after he wakes up.\n\n\n\nHowever, his joy doesn’t stop there, he also gets to pick up his daughter, Toie Roberts.\n\n\n\nThe use of the word “seed” here means his child. So after he drops off his daughter, he smokes his weed.\n\nDouble Entendre: Seed here also means the impurities in weed. He connects this to letting the weed up in smoke—minus the impurities of seeds which he just dropped off.\n\nAs for speed, the vehicle he’s probably in goes this fast.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HKsQMGVqQ3U", "annotation_id": "8340986", "url": "https://genius.com/8340986", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "God is great, first and foremost, uhh", "vault_lines": ["God is great, first and foremost, uhh"], "meaning": "Ross opens the album affirming his love of God. Ross has mentioned God in other songs as well, opening “Ten Jesus Pieces” saying, “God forgives, he’s so honorable.” Also worth noting that his fifth studio album was titled God Forgives, I Don’t. Ross talks about his belief in God in this interview on the BET show Lift Every Voice.", "annotation_id": "8340739", "url": "https://genius.com/8340739", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Tryna make a living, remain spiritual", "vault_lines": ["Tryna make a living, remain spiritual"], "meaning": "Ross talked about his belief in God in this interview on the BET show Lift Every Voice.", "annotation_id": "8338621", "url": "https://genius.com/8338621", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "My inner Andre 3000, uhh", "vault_lines": ["My inner Andre 3000, uhh"], "meaning": "Ross is referring to one half of the rap duo OutKast, Andre 3000. Andre previously worked with Ross on the song “Sixteen” off God Forgives, I Don’t.\n\nAndre is often cited as one of the greatest rappers of all time. He is know for his unique style, wordplay, and thought-provoking lyrics.", "annotation_id": "8329910", "url": "https://genius.com/8329910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Time will come when all that nice shit disappears", "vault_lines": ["Time will come when all that nice shit disappears"], "meaning": "Fast money and it’s byproducts tend to be temporary as more often than not your past catches up with you—be it in the form of enemies or the law. This is something Ross is currently experiencing with his legal woes.", "annotation_id": "8327797", "url": "https://genius.com/8327797", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Now accept these words as they came from Eminem", "vault_lines": ["Now accept these words as they came from Eminem"], "meaning": "In an interview with Rolling Stone Ross explained this line:\n\n\nRolling Stone: You say, “Now accept these words as if they came from Eminem.” Do you think he gets away with saying things that other rappers would get criticized for?\n\nRoss: I think that’s a huge possibility. That’s why I said that line, so maybe this question would come up. But I’m not really into a white-black race card. Some of my best friends are white.\n\n\nRoss has some history with namedropping Eminem. After his beef with 50 Cent, Ross tried baiting Eminem into a beef. He even told MTV News that he wanted to battle Em. He taunted Em and 50 in a vlog where he called Em a “munkey” (monkey) and a “hunkey” (honkey). Em never really responded to Ross.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eCKB9TLq28A", "annotation_id": "8327119", "url": "https://genius.com/8327119", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "All I wanted was some Belaire", "vault_lines": ["All I wanted was some Belaire"], "meaning": "Belaire is a reference to Luc Belaire Rose, a type of sparkling wine that Ross is basically an ambassador for since he raps about it about as often as he raps about Wingstop.\n\nGQ did a writeup about the brand and Ross' affiliation with it.", "annotation_id": "8326753", "url": "https://genius.com/8326753", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Assassinate Trump like I’m Zimmerman", "vault_lines": ["Assassinate Trump like I'm Zimmerman"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is saying that he would murder Donald Trump just like George Zimmerman assassinated Trayvon Martin, but this time, it is the other way around.\n\nIn an interview with Rolling Stone, Ross explained the lyric saying:\n\nI would never advocate violence on Trump or anyone. It’s lyrical assassination. That’s me being a poet, putting words together in my art form, with no violence in my heart at all. Clarify that. Matter of fact, my cameraman was Trump’s caddy at his golf course for five years, and he says Trump is cool as fuck.\n\nYouTube personality Mark Dice claims that, because of this line, he was able to convince Walmart to remove Ross' album from their shelves. Dice called up Walmart (in a video he posted on his YouTube)  questioning why they would sell an album that threatens to kill a presidential candidate.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p8fdtQGK3tM", "annotation_id": "8326322", "url": "https://genius.com/8326322", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Democratic party sentenced to the pendulum Killing them, I voted for Andre Benjamin", "vault_lines": ["Democratic party sentenced to the pendulum", "Killing them, I voted for Andre Benjamin"], "meaning": "Ross seems to be referring to how the Democratic party was able to take control of the house, the senate, and the White House in 2008 when President Obama was elected only to lose their majorities in the house and senate in the following elections (thus the pendulum swinging back on them).\n\nSince the Democrats can’t consistently win, Ross would rather cast his vote for Andre 3000: The only man who can save us from the nonsense of the Republicans. Andre, who is mentioned twice in this verse, worked together  before on “Sixteen” off Ross' God Forgives, I Don’t.\n\nRoss doesn’t often talk about politics, but he did sample a President Obama speech in his video for “Presidential.”", "annotation_id": "8325778", "url": "https://genius.com/8325778", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "3 weeks in the hole, as if a nigga heinous As I’m pacing in the cell, all these picture’s painted", "vault_lines": ["3 weeks in the hole, as if a nigga heinous", "As I'm pacing in the cell, all these picture's painted"], "meaning": "Ross spent some time in jail after being arrested on assault and kidnapping charges before posting bond. They had him locked down as if he was a malicious person who actually acted upon those crimes, something he thinks about this as he paces up and down his cell.\n\nAccording to the arrest warrant, Ross was having work done on his house in Atlanta (which was formerly owned by Evander Holyfield) by groundskeeper Jonathan Zamudio. Ross and his bodyguard (Nadrian James) got into a fight with Zamudio because Ross was reportedly upset Zamudio had people over the night before for a birthday party (Zamudio lived in a guest house on the property). According to the arrest warrant, Ross and James pistol-whipped  Zamudio with a glock 9mm gun, chipped two of his teeth, and held him against his will.  All of which actually does sound kinda heinous.", "annotation_id": "8325774", "url": "https://genius.com/8325774", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Now the trophy on the mantle, well, I got a Phantom", "vault_lines": ["Now the trophy on the mantle, well, I got a Phantom"], "meaning": "No matter what happened in the past, Ross is winning now and he considers having a Phantom Rolls Royce the equivalent of having a trophy on the mantle.", "annotation_id": "8325741", "url": "https://genius.com/8325741", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Egotistical ways I embraced back in middle school If the chick was game, we would run a train Send her home on a bus, then forget her name But now the bitches be the realest ones I done cried on her shoulder when I’m feeling numb", "vault_lines": ["Egotistical ways I embraced back in middle school", "If the chick was game, we would run a train", "Send her home on a bus, then forget her name", "But now the bitches be the realest ones", "I done cried on her shoulder when I'm feeling numb"], "meaning": "“Running a train” is a sexual act where a group of men have sex with the same women one after another, something Ross apparently did while in middle school.\n\nFast forward to today, Ross has matured and realizes the importance of a woman. He even expresses that he cried on one’s shoulder when he was at his lowest point. Ross’s new found perspective may have a lot to do with his recent engagement to fiancé, Lira Galore.", "annotation_id": "8325735", "url": "https://genius.com/8325735", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "You can have the ex, I got enough hoes You can have my next, once we a month old", "vault_lines": ["You can have the ex, I got enough hoes", "You can have my next, once we a month old"], "meaning": "Ross is a firm believer in the “on to the next one” lifestyle. He offers his old girl because he has enough women in his life to suffice. He even offers his next girl because he knows he’ll be done with her in a months time.\n\nIn real life, Ross is currently dating model Lira Galore. The couple was engaged at one point, although at this time it’s unclear whether or not they’re still planning on getting married.", "annotation_id": "8325726", "url": "https://genius.com/8325726", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "line": "Beefing with broke niggas really like pulling my teeth Get away from me! You looking so suspicious Light pocket, heavy hearted, you simple minded niggas", "vault_lines": ["Beefing with broke niggas really like pulling my teeth", "Get away from me! You looking so suspicious", "Light pocket, heavy hearted, you simple minded niggas"], "meaning": "The “broke niggas” line is likely a shot at long time rival, 50 Cent who recently filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy. Although filing for Chapter 11 doesn’t always mean you’re broke, Ross did not hesitate to bring up 50’s financial woes in a recent interview with The Breakfast Club.\n\nI’d probably be in my emotions as well if my first album was titled Get Rich or Die Tryin', and I filed bankrupt.\n\n50 filed for bankruptcy after a jury ordered him to pay Lastonia Leviston—Rick Ross' baby mama—$5 million dollars in damages (another $2 million was tacked on afterward) for releasing a sex tape of Leviston. 50 released the tape after he bought it from Maurice Murray—Leviston’s former boyfriend and co-star in the sex tape.\n\nRoss and 50’s beef goes back to 2009. The beef became public when Ross talked about 50 and his baby momma’s house mysteriously burning down on “Mafia Music.” The beef came at a turbulent time for Ross, when it seemed like the combination of being exposed as a former correctional officer and beefing with 50 (who had never really “lost” a beef at that time) would sink his career. However, Ross weathered the storm and became a bigger rap star than ever before, even as his feud with 50 raged on.\n\nAfter dying down for quite some time, things between them have heated up again in 2015. 50 continued to taunt Ross in 2015 by posting a photo on Instagram of him hanging out with the alleged victim of Ross' kidnapping and assault case, Jonathan Zamudio.", "annotation_id": "8325712", "url": "https://genius.com/8325712", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Enterprise", "status": "done", "targets": 34, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-free-enterprise-lyrics", "genius_title": "Free Enterprise", "genius_id": 2371930, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "Hear me clearly: If y’all niggas fear me, just say y’all fear me", "vault_lines": ["Hear me clearly: If y'all niggas fear me, just say y'all fear me"], "meaning": "In the line “Hear me clearly: If y’all niggas fear me, just say y’all fear me,” Jay-Z is addressing his critics and detractors directly. He’s essentially challenging them to be honest about their feelings toward him. If they’re intimidated or feel threatened by his success, influence, or power, he’s saying they should own up to it rather than hiding behind rumors, attacks, or false narratives.\n\nThis ties into the larger themes of the verse:\n1.  Facing opposition: Jay-Z reflects on how people have tried to bring him down, whether through violence (squeezing the metal) or tarnishing his name (rumors of Lucifer).\n2.  Acknowledging fear: The line suggests that their hostility stems from fear or insecurity, not genuine grievances.\n    3.  Calling out hypocrisy: By referencing “He without sin shall cast the first stone,” he highlights the double standards of those judging or attacking him while ignoring their own flaws.\n\nOverall, he’s asserting his dominance and resilience, dismissing the “fairytales” spun about him, and calling for honesty and accountability from those who oppose him.", "annotation_id": "33729328", "url": "https://genius.com/33729328", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "I stopped writing, so fuck it, I’ll do it mentally", "vault_lines": ["I stopped writing, so fuck it, I'll do it mentally"], "meaning": "In Freemason ritual, one is supposed to recite the oath from memory with no written prompts. Writing the ritual is forbidden. So although Rick is showing off how great his rap game is, he is also referencing a key element of Masonic ritual here.", "annotation_id": "33464414", "url": "https://genius.com/33464414", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "This is for the soldiers that see the sun at midnight You dig?", "vault_lines": ["This is for the soldiers that see the sun at midnight"], "meaning": "Rick was only trying  to tell listeners  to be aware that  what they going to spit into, are something  very  difficult to understand,  those are subliminal bars .. that needs high IQ to understand\n\nRelating it as to see the sun at the midnight", "annotation_id": "28884093", "url": "https://genius.com/28884093", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "Ancient wisdom valuable like gifts of gold", "vault_lines": ["Ancient wisdom valuable like gifts of gold"], "meaning": "Here  rick was trying to tell listeners  , like  the kind of life they living worthy\nTrying to compare it with Gold , and how ancient  people  used to treat Gold like something special,  important  and the one you can’t afford to loose", "annotation_id": "28884007", "url": "https://genius.com/28884007", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "Allow my flow time to sink into the tempo", "vault_lines": ["Allow my flow time to sink into the tempo"], "meaning": "Musically, he’s saying: let me find the groove. But this bar should also be heard as “Allow my flow time to sink into the temple” – as in, the listener’s head.\n\nThirdly, as he just referenced Solomon in the bar before (this notation is in half bars, mind you) it’s very plausible the author referenced the other kind of temple; King Solomon’s Temple – which is a major focal point for Freemasonry and masonic mythology.\n\nIt’s an elegant triple entendre, but combined with the flower / the seal of a king and the seal of approval Solomon gave to the builder of his Temple, (who is a central figure in Masonry)  this might be the first Double Triple in history.", "annotation_id": "25802494", "url": "https://genius.com/25802494", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "We the lost symbols,", "vault_lines": ["We the lost symbols,"], "meaning": "the Lost Symbol is a book by author Dan Brown, in which the character Robert Langdon is invited to give a lecture at the United States Capitol, at the invitation apparently from his mentor, a 33rd degree Freemason named Peter Solomon.\n\nKing Solomon is the legendary son of King David, and plays an important role in Judaism, Christianity, Islam and Freemasonry. Known for his wisdom, King Solomon is known as the builder of the first Temple in Jerusalem.\n\nA few bars down, Ross references Solomon’s Seal, a predecessor to the Star of David. It’s clear Rick Ross has at least read up on the symbolism of Freemasonry and its old-world roots.", "annotation_id": "15379125", "url": "https://genius.com/15379125", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "I understand that folks expect me to fold Community control to violate parole (No) I won’t fail, but a lot of men will", "vault_lines": ["I understand that folks expect me to fold", "Community control to violate parole (No)", "I won't fail, but a lot of men will"], "meaning": "Playing on the earlier line where he declares his understanding of the hustle and the rap game, he also states an understanding in the politics at play in his life. He is expected to give up as an uneducated black man, to fall into a circle of crime and parole violations, and perhaps because of this, he doesn’t. Instead he proves himself a master of his craft also rising with a company and basically seizing back “the means of production”.\n\nBasically he is a sore thumb to anyone who ever said he’d never amount to anything saying: fuck you, anyone else might have failed and given up, but I’m not anyone else", "annotation_id": "11881455", "url": "https://genius.com/11881455", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "Period, we masters", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The Master Mason is the title given to the person elected to oversee a Masonic jurisdiction. He presides over a Grand Lodge, and has certain rights in the constituent Lodges that form his jurisdiction.\n\nRoss, who “started in the ghetto, now [is] worldwide” is the architect, or the Master Mason, of the whole world.", "annotation_id": "4999482", "url": "https://genius.com/4999482", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "Free agents (Me), we faster (Ha)", "vault_lines": ["Free agents (Me), we faster (Ha)"], "meaning": "In professional sports, a free agent is a player who is eligible to sign with any club or franchise, i.e. not under contract to any specific team. The term is also used in reference to a player who is under contract at present, but who is allowed to solicit offers from other teams. In some circumstances, the free agent’s options are limited by league rules.\n\nThe term came into wider use after sports leagues stopped using the “reserve clause”, which allowed a repetitive option by the club to renew the contract for one (or more years) but did not allow the player to terminate it.\n\nHowever, while Ross discusses contracts, he is likely referring to the philosophy of Plato:\n\nIn sociology and philosophy, agency is the capacity of an agent (a person or other entity, human or any living being in general, or soul-consciousness in religion) to act in a world. The capacity to act does not at first imply a specific moral dimension to the ability to make the choice to act, and moral agency is therefore a distinct concept. In sociology, an agent is an individual engaging with the social structure. Notably, though, the primacy of social structure vs. individual capacity with regard to persons' actions is debated within sociology. This debate concerns, at least partly, the level of reflexivity an agent may possess.\n\nAgency may either be classified as unconscious, involuntary behavior, or purposeful, goal directed activity (intentional action). An agent typically has some sort of immediate awareness of their physical activity and the goals that the activity is aimed at realizing. In ‘goal directed action’ an agent implements a kind of direct control or guidance over their own behavior.\n\nIn short, Ross is restricted by neither contracts or social structures.", "annotation_id": "4999473", "url": "https://genius.com/4999473", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "Built pyramids,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A possible reference to the Eye of Providence, which can be seen on the reverse of the Great Seal of the United States 1$ Bill\nThis symbol is often associated with Freemasonry, since The Eye first appeared as part of the standard iconography of the Freemasons in 1797, with the publication of Thomas Smith Webb’s Freemasons Monitor\n\nPopular among conspiracy theorists is the claim that the Eye of Providence shown atop an unfinished pyramid on the Great Seal of the United States indicates the influence of Freemasonry in the founding of the United States (this also was dramatized in the 2004 Disney film National Treasure\n\n\n\nOr at least hinting to the structure of Freemasons cliques", "annotation_id": "4850568", "url": "https://genius.com/4850568", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "I embark on life, my path is all math", "vault_lines": ["I embark on life, my path is all math"], "meaning": "This line could refer to three things:\n\n\nThe math is referring to money, since acquiring more money adds to your wealth. This line also ties into the idea of Freemasonry because it is said that one of their beliefs and practices is to acquire as much money as possible in their lifetime.\nRick Ross is obviously not a real free mason, but he is however, using the idea of Free Masonry as a metaphor of what he does with rap music.\nSecond possibility is that math refers to Sacred Geometry.   The G at the center of the Freemason square and compass.\nSomething less likely is that Rick Ross is referring to the Pathway of Supreme Mathematics, a piece of 5 Percenter terminology.", "annotation_id": "3342669", "url": "https://genius.com/3342669", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "A lot of great thinkers and a lot of great inventors", "vault_lines": ["A lot of great thinkers and a lot of great inventors"], "meaning": "Great man like these (below) came with ideas of building pyramids.Not this association of illuminate that people say a pyramid represents.\n\n\n\nAccording to Redford, “The Egyptians began using the pyramid form shortly after 2700 B.C., and the great heyday of constructing them for royalty extended for about a thousand years, until about 1700 B.C.” The first pyramid was built by King Djoser during Egypt’s Third Dynasty. His architect, Imohtep, created a step pyramid by stacking six mastabas, rectangular buildings of the sort in which earlier kings had been buried. The largest and most well-known pyramids in Egypt are the Pyramids at Giza, including the Great Pyramid of Giza designed for Pharaoh Khufu.\nhttp://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2008/03/080328104302.htm\n\nACCEPTED COMMENT: They were concerned with how it was built….Edison is the person who discredited and exploited the man who wanted and did understand why it was built….It’s purpose isn’t just a huge structure to hold bodies…Fast forward to modern times, and it’s given that title in the same era mile tall structures are built solely for business,tourist attractions, and just blatant trivial competition to “own the sky”.\n\n…And they’re much older than they claim…putting them right in the timeframe of the inhabitance of the land’s visitors.", "annotation_id": "2942244", "url": "https://genius.com/2942244", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "Speak in cryptic codes", "vault_lines": ["speak in cryptic codes"], "meaning": "A metaphor for rap, because it is usually not straight forward – it leaves the listener to figure out every song in its entirety.\n\nThis ties into Free Masonry because the masons are known to write books in hieroglyphic like language so only they can read it until someone else figures it out.\n\nThis is also a reference to the esoteric nature of lyrics written by enlightened rappers. Francis Bacon was one of the first to use this form of writing: ‘hidden in plain sight’.", "annotation_id": "705491", "url": "https://genius.com/705491", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "My Jesus-piece flooded But thou shall not covet—keep your eyes off my cupboard I’m a bad motherfucker, it’s Hov, just say you love it", "vault_lines": ["But thou shall not covet—keep your eyes off my cupboard", "I'm a bad motherfucker, it's Hov, just say you love it"], "meaning": "Jay lets it be known that he’s not into the devil-worshipping thing. He uses wit when he rhymes about his “Jesus piece” (jewelry for those who don’t know) being flooded (with diamonds) but warns (in scripture lingo) that others shouldn’t crave after his possessions and advises them against eyeing what he has\n\nInteresting enough, that lyric seems like typical tough-guy bravado but it’s actually plain ol’ good advice: Don’t lust after what others have- get on your grind and get your own!\n\nHe ends the verse telling those who hate to just concede and expose their masked admiration for his accomplishments", "annotation_id": "48667", "url": "https://genius.com/48667", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "Fuck all these fairytales Go to Hell, this is God engineering", "vault_lines": ["Fuck all these fairytales", "Go to Hell, this is God engineering"], "meaning": "Jay writes off the rumors as “fairy tales”, created within his haters’ imaginations. But this is the part I really like (brilliant use of double entendre): Telling his haters to “go to Hell”, Jay proudly claims that his rise is “God engineering.” “God Engineering” is another way of saying “God’s will” is to be done. So in the context of the rhyme, Jay states that whatever happens in life is greater than himself, you or I- that it’s God’s will\n\nYou don’t have to be a religious person to understand this belief or embrace it. In fact, people are sometimes burdened by their religious beliefs. God engineering is simply the belief in a higher power- and/or that our lives are subject to a master plan; fate or destiny", "annotation_id": "48665", "url": "https://genius.com/48665", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "Niggas couldn’t do nothing with me, they put the devil on me I’d have preferred niggas to squeeze the metal on me Rumors of Lucifer, I don’t know who to trust Whole world want my demise—turn the music up", "vault_lines": ["Niggas couldn't do nothing with me, they put the devil on me", "I'd have preferred niggas to squeeze the metal on me", "Rumors of Lucifer, I don't know who to trust", "Whole world want my demise—turn the music up"], "meaning": "Conspiracy theorist screaming Illuminati\nThey can’t believe this much skill is in the human body\n\nI got a lot of money. When people get a lot of money, they start getting those rumors, like Willie Smith. That’s ridiculous.\n\n\nAll of the rumors of devil worshiping or him being involved in a secret society has Jay suspicious of anyone attempting to get close to him. He claims that the “whole world” just wants to witness his downfall.", "annotation_id": "48663", "url": "https://genius.com/48663", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "It’s amazing that I made it through the maze that I was in Lord, forgive me, I never would’ve made it without sin Holy water, my face in the basin Diamonds in my Rosary shows He forgave him", "vault_lines": ["It's amazing that I made it through the maze that I was in", "Lord, forgive me, I never would've made it without sin", "Holy water, my face in the basin", "Diamonds in my Rosary shows He forgave him"], "meaning": "Jay reveals his own amazement in being able to overcome his past circumstances. But the tone in which he delivers the rhyme and its context within the verse shows that his amazement is not just directed at himself but at a higher power. We know that’s the case because in the next line Jay states that he could not have turned his life around without sin AND God’s forgiveness\n\nThis is where Jay really gets deep…\n\nJay rhymes about his face being in “holy water” which is used in a religious setting for baptism. Baptism signifies cleansing or a rebirth. This lyric reflects back to the rhyme before it in which Jay spoke of sinning in order to escape the “maze” or circumstances he was in. Once God grants his plea for forgiveness, Jay makes a transition (which we’re witnessing the work-in-progress now) and experiences a rebirth on the road to redemption\n\nRosary are a string of beads used in devotional prayer. When Jay says that diamonds are in his Rosary he’s giving listeners a visual that we all recognize. A diamond represents wealth. Wealth is not just about money, it’s about abundance; whether of wisdom, experience, love, etc. This is all Jay’s way of saying that his wealth – in every sense of the word, is a blessing from God\n\nForget about Creflo Dollar and Eddie Long, Jay-Z is the man to go to hear the true gospel of prosperity!", "annotation_id": "48660", "url": "https://genius.com/48660", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "He without sin shall cast the first stone So y’all look in the mirror, double-check your appearance Bitch, I said I was amazing Not that I’m a Mason", "vault_lines": ["He without sin shall cast the first stone", "So y'all look in the mirror, double-check your appearance", "Bitch, I said I was amazing", "Not that I'm a Mason"], "meaning": "The first line in this part of the lyric is Jay’s take on a well known Bible verse. The actual Bible verse is basically stating that no one is without sin and therefore unfit to judge. Jay is stating that his critics should look at themselves in the mirror and “double check y'all appearance” instead of judging him\n\nJay then makes clear that in rhymes he spat in the past, he said that he was “amazing” NOT “a Mason”.\nClever homophone.", "annotation_id": "48657", "url": "https://genius.com/48657", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "This is a Hail Mary pass, y’all interfering", "vault_lines": ["This is a Hail Mary pass, y'all interfering"], "meaning": "In religious/spiritual terms, the Hail Mary is a prayer\n\nI won’t go into too much detail here but in Catholicism, a Hail Mary is an intercession between a believer and the Virgin Mary in which the Holy Mother a.k.a., Mother of Christ, acts as a mediator, sending a prayer to God on the behalf of the believer\n\nA Hail Mary pass in football is when the quarterback throws a long pass downfield in a desperate attempt to score a touchdown before time runs out on the clock\n\nHere, Jay rhymes about those who attempt to interfere with his spiritual connection to a higher power, also a double entendre\n\nAlso in football, ‘pass interference’ is illegal for the defense. He’s saying his opponents have to cheat to try and stop him. That adds to the double entendre.", "annotation_id": "48656", "url": "https://genius.com/48656", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "So— So I “JFK” Join forces with the kings", "vault_lines": ["So— So I \"JFK\"", "Join forces with the kings"], "meaning": "i.e., Join Forces with the Kings. Get it? It means he aligned with the people in the know, the people that are actually trying to do the right thing, and is willing to be a Batman-esque figure in the rap game, knowing that it will garner a certain level of hate from unenlightened souls, but at least SOME of those souls will see the light, and in turn project light themselves.\n\nThis could also be a reference to LeBron’s decision to come to Miami. LeBron joined forces with Wade & Bosh in order to win championships. LeBron reportedly rapped this line when he threw a housewarming party when he first moved to Miami.", "annotation_id": "23553", "url": "https://genius.com/23553", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "If I ever die Never let it be said I didn’t win", "vault_lines": ["Never let it be said I didn't win"], "meaning": "Joh Legend has an impressive awards catalogue of six Grammys, three Soul Train awards, a BET award and a MOBO.\n\nIt also alludes to the fact that they’ve lived their lives exactly how they’ve intended the whole time and some how its continued to lead them to the top. At the point they would rather die than live any other way.", "annotation_id": "23504", "url": "https://genius.com/23504", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "Bitch, I’m red-hot I’m on my third six, but a devil I’m not", "vault_lines": ["I'm on my third six, but a devil I'm not"], "meaning": "Likely a reference to Jay’s penchant for expensive cars, either the Mercedes Benz 600, or the Maybach 62.\n\nTwitter claimed it was a bar count: Jay is on the 18th bar of his verse (3 x 6 = 18)", "annotation_id": "23406", "url": "https://genius.com/23406", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "All-black diamonds, times were hard (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["All-black diamonds, times were hard (Woo)"], "meaning": "The black diamond is a play on words. times were hard relates to the fact that black is a color of depression and sadness but on the other hand diamonds are the hardest rock on earth, it also parallels with the previous line in all white represents holiness, and as said before, black is a color of sadness.", "annotation_id": "23009", "url": "https://genius.com/23009", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "I’m iconic in the field like Solomon’s Seal (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["I'm iconic in the field like Solomon's Seal (Woo)"], "meaning": "Solomon’s Seal is a sort of mystical Star-of-David thing, based on a ring worn by King Solomon which, in Biblical mythology, allowed him to control demons\n\nOr, alternately, it’s a flower in the lily family–which one is more likely to be found in a field?\n\nIn either case, Pretty Ricky resembles one", "annotation_id": "23008", "url": "https://genius.com/23008", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Free Mason", "line": "My top back like JFK They wanna push my top back like JFK (Ayy)", "vault_lines": ["They wanna push my top back like JFK (Ayy)"], "meaning": "The top of his car is back (i.e it’s a convertible) like JFK was shot on the head (top back)\n\nHe’s at risk of getting shot in the head (his top pushed back) like Kennedy\n\n\n\nSince this song is about conspiracies, you just knew a JFK reference would pop up.", "annotation_id": "22883", "url": "https://genius.com/22883", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
