{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "[Intro: Pimp C]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The words are taken wholesale from Pimp’s last interview. The Youtube video apparently inspired Vinylz to create the beat, and he takes Pimp’s words and samples them, adding effects to create the haunting atmosphere.", "annotation_id": "8081637", "url": "https://genius.com/8081637", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "In the villa in Venice, sippin’ vino", "vault_lines": ["in the villa in Venice, sippin' vino"], "meaning": "In Villa like that in Venice (Venice is a town in Italy, also another follow-up to Italy in his verse) Jay-Z is going to drink some Vino (which means Wine in English). He is talking about living a wealthy life in one of the most luxurious countries in Europe, where he could drink Wine. Italian Wines are famous for being one of the most gorgeous types of this drink over the whole world.\n\nLiving in expensive, wealthy Villa, with beautiful woman, and sipping with her the best Wines in the world, yea, it sounds like being a Jay-Z.", "annotation_id": "3183068", "url": "https://genius.com/3183068", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "I bet the hoes on my tour, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I bet the hoes on my tour, nigga"], "meaning": "He’s betting the girls that show up to his tours on the previous bar:\n“Look at me, I’m pure nigga”\n\n\n\nRoss is referencing the movie Almost Famous here. There is a scene in that flick where the tour managers and artists are playing poker.  They proceed to wage cash, beer and the “band-aids”.  The “band-aids” were basically traveling groupies.", "annotation_id": "1989071", "url": "https://genius.com/1989071", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "[Verse 1: Rick Ross]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Ross has featured Jay-Z on a number of his songs in the past (“Hustlin' Remix”, “Maybach Music”, “Free Mason”, “3 Kings”), but this is the first time Jay has featured Ross.\n\nEarlier in 2013, Ross instagrammed his studio session with Hova.\n\nhttp://instagram.com/p/XK0e3EhvJh/\n\nIn an MMG Vlog, Ross spoke on the MCHG collaboration.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2_Xv-gdGNMQ\n\nhttps://twitter.com/chancetherapper/statuses/388291505913610240", "annotation_id": "1952740", "url": "https://genius.com/1952740", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Look at me, I’m pure, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Look at me, I'm pure, nigga"], "meaning": "A cocaine pun – Ross makes plenty of dope, both music and cocaine. But don’t take my word for it:\n\nI can make cocaine\n\nRoss is also referencing how real he is and could also referring to his race/ethnicity, considering the Pimp C intro.", "annotation_id": "1949690", "url": "https://genius.com/1949690", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "[Verse 2: Jay Z]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jay had intended to cover Italian-themed material in “Living So Italian,” a song that was dropped from 2011’s Watch The Throne. The beat (not this one) was constructed but Jay never dropped his verse. This verse is a good indicator of what that material would’ve sounded like.", "annotation_id": "1946786", "url": "https://genius.com/1946786", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "My bitch whip cost a hundred grand", "vault_lines": ["My bitch whip cost a hundred grand"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is in a relationship with model Shateria L. Moragne, he probably bought her an expensive car (Hundred grand = $100,000) as a sign of his love.\n\n\n\nThe Boss waited till his own song came out, though, to reveal exactly which make he bought her:", "annotation_id": "1946295", "url": "https://genius.com/1946295", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Shoppin’ bags, I’m a tourist, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Shoppin' bags, I'm a tourist, nigga"], "meaning": "It’s not uncommon for a tourist to shop when on vacation, and of course Rick Ross has Money Bags to spend. He can just enjoy his time in Europe shopping and blowing some money!\n\nMost of the luxury brands that rappers idolize come from Europe. The rich from all around the world come to Europe to buy the best in luxury brands:\n\n\nFrance: Louis Vuitton, Cartier, YSL, Louboutin etc.\nItaly: Gucci, Prada, Fendi, Dolce & Gabbana etc.\nUK: Burberry, Manolo, Jimmy Choo, Alexander McQueen etc.\nSwitzerland: Audemars Piguet, Rolex, Bally, Hublot etc.\n\n\n\nBy shopping in Europe, Rick Ross is implying that he’s actually spending a lot of money on luxury brands, not just rapping about it.\n\nSince he is a tourist, Europe is far from home. Knowing the struggle most black people from poor backgrounds have, Rick could also be implying as a “tourist” he shouldn’t even be there with the circumstances he has faced in life. But since he is there, he may as well ball.", "annotation_id": "1945465", "url": "https://genius.com/1945465", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Bad bitch, she a masterpiece Got a bad bitch, she a master; peace", "vault_lines": ["Bad bitch, she a masterpiece", "Got a bad bitch, she a master; peace"], "meaning": "In “Picasso Baby,” Jay namechecks the Mona Lisa, a famous painting of Da Vinci’s known for its subject’s subtly beautiful smile.\n\n\n\nSleeping every night next to Mona Lisa\nThe modern day version\nWith better features\n\nJay’s all sweet with it, but Rick’s saying his bitch is a masterpiece for, er, different reasons. She’s a master piece of ass. Also she can “mash the piece,” or knows how to fire a gun. Hov confirms this by adding a gun sound to the end of the song.\n\nThe first line “Bad bitch, she a masterpiece” is a reference to his watch. Specifically the Rolex model ‘Masterpiece’. Which he refers to as being ‘bad’. The Masterpiece is a popular model among rappers, as numerous rappers have made reference to it.\n\nThe second line is indeed about his girl. So the pun is that he says the same line twice (although he switched it up) but have a different meaning.\n\nRoss also says the word “piece” differently the second time, as if he was saying “peace” – an allusion to the ending of the song. A bit of subtle wordplay by The Boss.", "annotation_id": "1942612", "url": "https://genius.com/1942612", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "A 100 keys at the piano", "vault_lines": ["A 100 keys at the piano"], "meaning": "“100 Keys” is a piano-heavy track by Big Sean, also featuring Rick Ross; a real piano only has 88 keys.\n\n\n\nIs also a reference to an amount of coke (100 kilos).", "annotation_id": "1942513", "url": "https://genius.com/1942513", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Not bad for a mulignan Y’all know like we know, I got it", "vault_lines": ["Not bad for a mulignan", "Y'all know like we know, I got it"], "meaning": "Keeping with the Neapolitan theme, mulignan (pronounced mool-ee-YAH-no, derived from Italian melanzana, “eggplant”) is a derogatory term that Italian-Americans have historically used to refer to blacks.\n\nThere’s a nice wordplay on mulignan and “y'all know,” both sounding very similar, using the sounds /ya/ and /no/. Jay also pronounces ‘we know’ to sound exactly as ‘vino’ two lines above.\n\n\n\nThis is a play on his “Otis” line, “Not bad for some immigrants,” which would fit in, considering the migrant past of Italian Americans in New York. Jay acknowledges various struggles in his music: the struggles of minorities, and the struggles of individuals within those communities.", "annotation_id": "1942499", "url": "https://genius.com/1942499", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Paesano", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Paesano literally means ‘countryman’ in Italian, informally used as a term of endearment and respect in Italian American culture. It shows that the mob bosses respect him and think highly of him regardless of race, as seen with the mulignan line below.", "annotation_id": "1942356", "url": "https://genius.com/1942356", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Red vert, you see me slide", "vault_lines": ["Red vert, you see me slide"], "meaning": "“Vert” is short for convertible. Rick Ross is probably referencing his 1973 Chevy Impala convertible seen in the “Don’t Like (Remix)” and “Love Sosa (Remix)” videos. Being that its a vert you can see who is in it easily. You see him sliding in his red vert.", "annotation_id": "1942290", "url": "https://genius.com/1942290", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "El Padrino,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "He is the El Padrino, the Godfather of this rap game. He is untouchable.\n\n\n\nHe is chilling in Venice, Italy, in his own mansion, drinking expensive wine. It’s a carefree life, he doesn’t have to think about the Barzini Meeting.\n\nThe theme of El Padrino will surface again on track 15, La Familia\n\nHead of the family\nEl Padrino\n\nAlso note the use of alliteration with the letter V.", "annotation_id": "1942276", "url": "https://genius.com/1942276", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Lucky Luciano is what they call me,", "vault_lines": ["Lucky Luciano is what they call me,"], "meaning": "Continuing Jay’s references to Italy and Italian culture, Charles “Lucky” Luciano was a famous Italian-American mobster considered to be the father of modern organized crime in the United States. This reference validifies Jay’s claim that he’s “got it,” because Lucky definitely had it made when he was at the top of his gangster game. Hov once even had his own Commission like Luciano.\n\n\n\nOne of the many nicknames Hov has acquired over the years is “Lucky Lefty.” Running with the Mafioso themes scattered throughout Hov’s raps, it shows him drawing comparisons to himself and Luciano. Although highly successful, Luciano was eventually imprisoned because he couldn’t leave the gangster life behind. The left-handed Hov is lucky to have escaped the hood before meeting a similar fate; having instead moved successfully into the legitimate music and business worlds.", "annotation_id": "1942246", "url": "https://genius.com/1942246", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "200 in the dash, we gonna rev it (skirt)", "vault_lines": ["200 in the dash, we gonna rev it (skirt)"], "meaning": "This could have a double meaning:\n\n\nOne meaning could be that Jay has 200 of some multiple of dollars in the glove compartment (which is part of the dashboard) of his car. i.e. $200,000; $200,000,000. etc. For all we know, the currency could be in Euros, since much of the verse has to do with being in Italy.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAnother meaning could be that when Jay-Z revs the engine of his Lamborghini, he goes up to the 200 kilometers per hour (or 124.274 MPH) mark on the spedometer (which is also a part of the dashboard).", "annotation_id": "1942234", "url": "https://genius.com/1942234", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Fuck with me, you know I got it Fuck with me, you know I got it Sexy bitch, I hope she ’bout it Come fuck with me, you know I got it Fuck with me, you know I got it Fuck with me, you know I got it Sexy bitch, I hope she ’bout it Come fuck with me, you know I got it", "vault_lines": ["Come fuck with me, you know I got it"], "meaning": "Ross is telling anyone of influence to rock with him, because he’s incredibly rich and successful within the industry. He may also be courting customers, both rappers speak about selling drugs in their music. Or, threatening enemies. “It” might be a weapon.\n\nThis could also be a reference to the line that’s constantly being said in rap songs: “it ain’t trickin' if you got it”. Obviously…Ross has got it, though, he doesn’t have it as good as Jay.", "annotation_id": "1942207", "url": "https://genius.com/1942207", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "“A little over a year ago I was in bondage. And now I’m back out here, reaping the blessings, and getting the benefits that go along with it. Everything that’s out here for kings like us. The reason why we like this—this jewelry and this diamonds and stuff—they don’t understand, it’s because we really from Africa. And that’s where all this stuff come from. And we originated from kings, you know what I’m saying? So don’t look down on the youngsters because they wanna have shiny things. It’s in our genes, know what I’m saying? We just don’t all know our history, so…”", "vault_lines": ["Y'all know like we know, I got it"], "meaning": "The intro features a recording of late Southern rapper Pimp C talking about his period of being incarcerated, and trying to get back acclimated to the world on the outside of a prison cell.\n\nHe goes on to explain that the reason a lot of black people try to wear diamonds and flashy jewelry is because we come from a long line of kings in our African ancestry. He explains that it’s “in our genes” to try to wear jewelry because all of us black men are kings and we can’t help it. If you look at Africa’s natural resources, most countries were rich in gold and silver! Reason why Europeans wanted to take over the continent.\n\nBut asking why rappers always talk about their stuff is like asking why Milton is forever listing the attributes of heavenly armies. Because boasting is a formal condition of the epic form.\n\nHere is the video of the interview. Skip to 1:13. Allegedly, this was his last interview before he died.\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_embedded&v=JK3HLJk8iMY", "annotation_id": "1942194", "url": "https://genius.com/1942194", "state": "accepted", "votes": 175, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Cent’anni, ciao bella", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Cent'anni” is a traditional Italian toast meaning “health for 100 years”, (“cento” meaning “hundred” and “anno” meaning “year”) and “ciao bella” is an Italian greeting towards a woman, “Hi beautiful”. In case you couldn’t tell, Jay is mad cultured.\n\n\n\nThe way that Jay Z pronounces cent'anni (and even spells it, on the lyrics sheet at right), is “Chin Don,” making a possible reference to deceased mobster Vincent “Chin” Gigante, who was, as it happens, a Don.", "annotation_id": "1942185", "url": "https://genius.com/1942185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Come money dance with the good fellas", "vault_lines": ["Come money dance with the good fellas"], "meaning": "A “money dance” is an Italian wedding tradition used to help offset the cost of the lavish celebrations. As guests arrive they slip an envelope of cash into a purse the bride carries on her wrist. Every male guest is expected to dance with the bride and contribute their share. In 2012 Chicago rapper Lil Mouse popularized\n\nJay’s inviting y'all to come celebrate life (cent'anni!) with his goodfellas – a term used in the Mafia to determine a trustworthy person. Presumably Hov’s goodfellas include members of the Roc-A-Fella Records and Roc Nation crews he has kept around him throughout his career.\n\n\n\nBeginning the verse’s Mafia references, this line also alludes to the infamous organized crime movie GoodFellas. Often regarded as one of the greatest films of all time, it starred Hov’s friend Robert De Niro – which cleverly links this line to the dinero wordplay in the next two.", "annotation_id": "1942161", "url": "https://genius.com/1942161", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Hov just landed in Rome, nigga All hail, Caesar’s home, niggas", "vault_lines": ["Hov just landed in Rome, nigga", "All hail, Caesar's home, niggas"], "meaning": "When Jay lands in exotic vacation spots like Rome, he walks around like he owns the place, even referring to himself in third person, much like a ruler would.\n\nThe reference to Julius Caesar, head of the Roman Empire, is an allusion he has used before:\n\nYou can call me Caesar / In a dark Czar\n\n\n\nInterestingly enough, Caesar often referred to himself in the third person in his writing (see The Gallic Wars). Rather than say “I just landed…”, perhaps Jay is using his nickname (Hov) as a reference to the stylistic device (called Illeism: It is the act of referring to oneself in the third person instead of the more appropriate first person..) that Caesar employed in his work.", "annotation_id": "1942143", "url": "https://genius.com/1942143", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Reeboks on, I just do it, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Reeboks on, I just do it, nigga"], "meaning": "“Just Do It” is the slogan of Reebok competitor, Nike. The fact that Ross is referencing Nike’s slogan could be ironic, or it could be a reference to the endorsement deal he once had with the company.\n\n\n\nThis verse was recorded earlier in 2013 (presumably in March, when he instagrammed his studio session with Hov) while Ross was still endorsed by Reebok. However, the song was released in July, a few months after he had been dropped from his Reebok endorsement for his now infamous molly raps.\n\nDespite being dropped from Reebok, Ross maintains a good relationship with them. After this song dropped in late July, Ross reiterated his good relationship with Reebok, saying that he would “make an announcement when the time is right”. Ross' good relations with Reebok is perhaps why Ross left these lyrics unchanged. Rumors surfaced that Ross had re-signed with Reebok after being pictured with their CEO.", "annotation_id": "1942140", "url": "https://genius.com/1942140", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Geechi niggas with satin sheets", "vault_lines": ["Geechi niggas with satin sheets"], "meaning": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_embedded&v=6lBzkFjdJrQ\n\n“Geechee” or “Geechy” refers to the Gullah Geechee people who are direct descendants of African slaves that inhabit the barrier sea islands off the coast of Georgia, North and South Carolina, and Florida. Ross usually uses the word in reference to being country or ghetto.\n\nSatin is known as a luxurious, silky smooth material for bed sheets. Ross has the disposable income to use on a good night’s sleep. So basically, Ross is a luxurious hood nigga.\n\n\n\nAlso a reference to Wale’s Chain Music.", "annotation_id": "1942132", "url": "https://genius.com/1942132", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "I don’t bop, I do the money dance", "vault_lines": ["I don't bop, I do the money dance"], "meaning": "Ross doesn’t concern himself with frivolous dance fads like the “bop”, he’s too busy doing the money dance, which is usually a dance at weddings where male guests pay to dance briefly with the bride, or sometimes female guests pay to dance with the groom. But in this case, Ross' money dance is just a celebratory dance because Ross is stacking millions.\n\n\n\nhttps://twitter.com/rickyrozay/status/359022943717691392", "annotation_id": "1942122", "url": "https://genius.com/1942122", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Plays across the Verrazano", "vault_lines": ["Plays across the Verrazano"], "meaning": "Bridge that connects boroughs of Staten Island and Brooklyn in New York City. The word Verrazano has an italian origin continuing the mafioso theme of this verse\n\nHis music plays across NYC, and the coke gets transferred over the Verrazano, too.", "annotation_id": "1942069", "url": "https://genius.com/1942069", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Black Jack in a casino", "vault_lines": ["Black Jack in a casino"], "meaning": "Jay-Z is playing with the fact that Robert De Niro is the protagonsit of the movie Casino. Jigga’s also an African American (“black Jack”).", "annotation_id": "1942067", "url": "https://genius.com/1942067", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Hov keep gettin’ that dinero, got it Even if a nigga gotta rob it, get it?", "vault_lines": ["Hov keep gettin' that dinero, got it", "Even if a nigga gotta rob it, get it?"], "meaning": "A continuation from the previous mention of the film Goodfellas – couple of things going on here:\n\n\nAn allusion to Robert De Niro who starred in Goodfellas; the way Jay pronounces “rob it” makes it sound like “Robert”.\n“Dinero” means money in Spanish.\n\n\n\nThe line is especially fitting when you take into account the plot of the movie Goodfellas: watching the rise and fall of a crime family. Jay is also keeping up with the Italian scheme here.\n\nFun Fact: Hov is neighbors with DeNiro in the Tribeca neighborhood in Lower Manhattan, NYC, and they had a recent falling out, so this could be subliminal shots directed at Rob also.", "annotation_id": "1942063", "url": "https://genius.com/1942063", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "line": "Peel off in a Lamborghini Countach", "vault_lines": ["Peel off in a Lamborghini Countach"], "meaning": "Peeling out is when you make your tires burn rubber. Not just any car though, a Lamborghini Countach.\n\n\n\nLike Hov’s boy Kanye said in “Illest Motherfucker Alive”:\n\n1985 white Lamborghini Countach, 2 of ‘em", "annotation_id": "1942005", "url": "https://genius.com/1942005", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "status": "done", "targets": 28, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Jay-z-fuckwithmeyouknowigotit-lyrics", "genius_title": "FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt", "genius_id": 182976, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Fuckin’ Problems (Remix)", "line": "I get the cheddar here, baby girl a blood but she kinda cute Let a nigga gang bang in the found blue (Su-woo)", "vault_lines": ["I get the cheddar here, baby girl a blood but she kinda cute", "Let a nigga gang bang in the found blue (Su-woo)"], "meaning": "Rick Ross has a lot of money and riches. Even though the girl is a Blood, he still thinks she is kind of cute. Rick Ross tries to play personas in his rap style of gangsters. Here he tries to represent being a Crip.", "annotation_id": "3077990", "url": "https://genius.com/3077990", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Fuckin’ Problems (Remix)", "line": "Talking down, she wanna ball like Cyndi Lauper", "vault_lines": ["Talking down, she wanna ball like Cyndi Lauper"], "meaning": "Cynthia Ann Stephanie “Cyndi” Lauper is an American singer, songwriter, actress, and LGBT rights activist whose career has spanned over four decades. Her debut solo album She’s So Unusual was an instant commercial success.\nDue to how long her career has stretched, she makes a lot of money. He basically is referring to her wealth.", "annotation_id": "3077969", "url": "https://genius.com/3077969", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Fuckin’ Problems (Remix)", "line": "110 Maserati Burning down the highway, burning on some Marley", "vault_lines": ["Burning down the highway, burning on some Marley"], "meaning": "Stalley is driving his Maserati at a high speed of 110mph (hence, burning down) on the highway while smoking Marijuana, a drug often associated with Bob Marley.", "annotation_id": "1481892", "url": "https://genius.com/1481892", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Fuckin’ Problems (Remix)", "line": "Red beamer and it’s brand new", "vault_lines": ["Red beamer and it's brand new"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is known to be a car collector, and has now added to his collection by purchasing a brand new Red BMW.", "annotation_id": "1481867", "url": "https://genius.com/1481867", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
