{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Still the biggest boss", "vault_lines": ["Dope boy, big calm, still the biggest boss"], "meaning": "A reference to Rick Ross' song “The Boss” from his sophomore album.", "annotation_id": "30803953", "url": "https://genius.com/30803953", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "A tub full of money, I still can’t relax", "vault_lines": ["A tub full of money, I still can't relax"], "meaning": "Between all his business ventures and his music career, Rick Ross is estimated to be worth $150 million, per Forbes, and he is still hungry for more", "annotation_id": "30120616", "url": "https://genius.com/30120616", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Breaks my heart (Haha) God did", "vault_lines": ["Breaks my heart (Haha)"], "meaning": "Jay repeats the line “breaks my heart” which was the first line of the song, by Khalid. Jay ends his verse with “breaks my heart,” so as to bring the song full circle.\n\nThe line is a double entendre, as Jay is also stating that God “broke” his heart in order to humble Jay through different trials in his life.", "annotation_id": "27914953", "url": "https://genius.com/27914953", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Nosedive, they just prayin’ that I crash (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Nosedive, they just prayin' that I crash (Huh)"], "meaning": "A nosedive refers to a plane going down front-first while crashing, and Rozay refers to his haters who hope that he crashes as well", "annotation_id": "27901585", "url": "https://genius.com/27901585", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "I cleaned up la madrina with the same soap, huh Me and Loro talk ’bout how we slang dope, huh", "vault_lines": ["I cleaned up la madrina with the same soap, huh", "Me and Loro talk 'bout how we slang dope, huh"], "meaning": "According to Young Guru, the descriptions of selling drugs with ‘Loro’ (Spanish for parrot) and cleaning ‘La Madrina’ (Spanish for godmother) refers to the nicknames of Jay-Z’s longtime friends and business partners, Juan and Desiree Perez.\n\n“La Madrina” also references Lorine Padilla, the actual La Madrina, the South Bronx matriarch and former “First Lady” of the Savage Skulls gang, which is the subject of a 2020 documentary\n\nhttps://www.gq.com/story/jay-z-god-did-verse-dj-khaled\n\nhttps://www.imdb.com/title/tt11905748/", "annotation_id": "27799940", "url": "https://genius.com/27799940", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "And bloke and ’nem from London, Harrow Road, Weston Inn", "vault_lines": ["And bloke and 'nem from London, Harrow Road, Weston Inn"], "meaning": "“Bloke” is a common slang term primarily used in the United Kingdom, Ireland, Australia, New Zealand, and South Africa. The word derives from a group of cockney slang — originating in East London.\n\nHov further makes a reference to the North-West London borough, Harrow. However, Hov also mentions Harrow Road which is a route that runs through Wembley. Surprisingly, the road begins just outside of South Harrow.", "annotation_id": "27751672", "url": "https://genius.com/27751672", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Don’t wan’ see me with the ring? You better play D", "vault_lines": ["Don't wan' see me with the ring? You better play D"], "meaning": "Wayne basically saying he’s one of the top names in the industry. Which he is. You only have to read his incredible portfolio of accolades and accomplishments to understand how influential he is. He puts the challenge to new names up and coming. You want my ring, my spot? You try and stop me.", "annotation_id": "27745624", "url": "https://genius.com/27745624", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Or I can go and buy a bank, I know my money’s safe", "vault_lines": ["Or I can go and buy a bank, I know my money's safe"], "meaning": "Rick Ross did incredible word play here, as he said: ‘he could go and buy a bank because his money is safe.’ Not only is a safe somewhere, you keep your money safe. But money is kept in a safe.", "annotation_id": "27590243", "url": "https://genius.com/27590243", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "(Where we at Gu?)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Here, JAY-Z is shouting out Young Guru, who has been his recording engineer since 1999.\n\nOn September 4, 2022, Young Guru posted a photo on Instagram, where he praised JAY-Z for doing his verse on “GOD DID” in just one take. The photo acted as proof, showing the single take in question on the screen. Beside the photo, he explained how talented you have to be able to do a verse in one take:\n\nDoing a song in one take is just a bonus of the level of talent. The song is what is important. You have to realize how much work is done before he goes in the booth. Questions like, “are these the right words”, “are you going in the right direction”, “is this the best flow” are all answered and addressed mentally way before you record. Then you say the verse over and over in the control room to memorialize it. Does jay do EVERY verse in one take? No. But has he done A LOT of verses in one take? Hell yes!!!\n\n\n\nThe photo Young Guru posted on Instagram, where you can see JAY-Z’s single take on the screen.", "annotation_id": "27455515", "url": "https://genius.com/27455515", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Somehow, I’ll out-fox every box they’ll try to throw me in (Come on)", "vault_lines": ["Somehow, I'll out-fox every box they'll try to throw me in (Come on)"], "meaning": "PROPOSED SUGGESTION: Triple Entendre\n\nFoxy Brown reference from their rumored sexual relationship in the 90s. Hov references this also on ‘Picasso Baby’ – “I never stuck my c*ck in the fox’s box”. Foxy later suggested that Jay took her virginity at 15 years old when he would’ve been 27, which Hov denies.\n\nReference – Foxy Brown, any other female rumors (every box), notably Aaliyah, and the box that corporate America tries to put in.", "annotation_id": "26582668", "url": "https://genius.com/26582668", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "They was tired of me winnin’, now they dead sleep", "vault_lines": ["They was tired of me winnin', now they dead sleep"], "meaning": "Wordplay on tired, dead and sleep.\nPeople were tired of Wayne winning and now the haters have given up on hating him, because Wayne keeps winning. They are dead to him.", "annotation_id": "26679178", "url": "https://genius.com/26679178", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "I never wanted to be the state custodian (Come on) The laws are draconian (Come on, Hov)", "vault_lines": ["I never wanted to be the state custodian (Come on)", "The laws are draconian (Come on, Hov)"], "meaning": "Statute of Limitations Drug Talk: This is my favorite Jigga. With the line, “never wanted to be the state custodian,” he seems to be distancing himself from the neoliberal billionaire talk that I’m not into: I welcome his getting back into his smooth-criminal bag with open arms. Rarely is someone that deep in the game able to leave it unscathed, and he’s secure enough to be able to be amazed at his own success. GQ\n\nA Custodian guards, protects, or maintains what they’ve been put in charge of. Therefore, even with his status as a billionaire and what he stands to lose with whatever he says. He decides to say it as it is anyway – Thus, not being a custodian of the state and calling out the criminal injustice (Draconian Laws) created to stalk black people\n\nDraconian Laws come from the first Athenian Legislator known as Draco around 7th century BC who legislated severe and overly excessive punishments (such as death) for crimes committed at that time in Athens irrespective of how minor or grave the crimes were.\n\nAnd Draco himself, they say, being asked why he made death the penalty for most offences, replied that in his opinion the lesser ones deserved it, and for the greater ones no heavier penalty could be found. by Plutarch, Life of Solon", "annotation_id": "26754227", "url": "https://genius.com/26754227", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "For those who make the laws, I’ma always have smoke for them", "vault_lines": ["For those who make the laws, I'ma always have smoke for them"], "meaning": "According to the HOV DID explanation of the lyrics posted on Hov’s official YouTube channel, this line is referring to his fight or continuous fight against the authorities who make the laws that create criminal injustice.\n\nThe New York Times, Opinion with the title – Jay-Z: The Criminal Justice System Stalks Black People Like Meek Mill which has been credited to Jay Z as the writer is a good example of Jay Z’s fight against injustice.\n\nIronically Meek Mill who was helped by Hov, later worked together with Hov to get 21 Savage released from ICE custody.\n\nHe will clash with those who make the laws he says. He calls that clash by the slang term smoke which is also a play on the smoke he now sells legally. It’s deep! (HOV DID)", "annotation_id": "26753894", "url": "https://genius.com/26753894", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Hov did", "vault_lines": ["Hov did (Hov did)"], "meaning": "By starting this verse with “ Hov did”, Jay-Z is calling himself a god. This is nothing new for Jay, since Hov stands for Jay-Hova, which comes from Jehova. Jehova is a name for the Christian/Jewish/Muslim God", "annotation_id": "26679190", "url": "https://genius.com/26679190", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "In memory of Teelo", "vault_lines": ["In memory of Teelo"], "meaning": "During a Twitter Spaces conversation with DJ Khaled and Rob Markman on Aug 31, 2022, Jay Z confirmed that Teelo is the brother of Hov’s childhood friend and business partner, Tyran “Ty Ty” Smith.\n\nRest in peace Teelo, that’s Ty Ty’s brother, Trance.", "annotation_id": "26621047", "url": "https://genius.com/26621047", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "They played themselves", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“You played yourself” is a popular quote from Khaled that has gained traction as an internet meme since 2016.", "annotation_id": "26620073", "url": "https://genius.com/26620073", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "So leave me in the dark, swimmin’ with the sharks", "vault_lines": ["So leave me in the dark, swimmin' with the sharks"], "meaning": "DJ Khaled once hilariously got lost on the ocean in the dark on his jet ski and documented it on snapchat\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMHCW1RIqBk", "annotation_id": "26618619", "url": "https://genius.com/26618619", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "I fucked the world and when I did, I practiced safe sex", "vault_lines": ["I fucked the world and when I did, I practiced safe sex"], "meaning": "Fucking the world is a common sentiment in hip-hop, popularised by 2Pac’s song “Fuck The World”:\n\nThey tryna say that I don’t care\nI woke up screamin' “Fuck the world!”\n\nThis connects to the earlier 2Pac reference in the verse with “only God can judge us.”\n\nCompare Wayne’s family planning here to how he fucked the world in “No Love”:\n\nit’s “Fuck the world,” get a child out her\n\nAnd fitting the track’s religious theme, he’s more loyal to the world than he was on “6 Foot 7 Foot”:\n\nMarried to the money, fuck the world; that’s adultery\n\nThe safe sex may be a reference to “Lollipop” (2008):\n\nShe say I got great sex\nSafe sex is great sex, better wear a latex\n‘Cause you don’t want that late text, that 'I think I’m late’ text\"\n\nIn 2021, Wayne was in a viral video in which he forgot those were his lyrics.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7OgFzPUYY4Q", "annotation_id": "26618600", "url": "https://genius.com/26618600", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Please, forgive me, God did", "vault_lines": ["Please, forgive me, God did"], "meaning": "Rozay makes a slick reference to his 2012 album, God Forgives, I Don’t.", "annotation_id": "26603257", "url": "https://genius.com/26603257", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "You never know how this shit could go Me and Biggs probably got too big if they ain’t book that load (What’s up Hoffa?) Hindsight is 20/20 Though he’s gettin’ plenty money, lookin’ back now this shit is funny", "vault_lines": ["You never know how this shit could go", "Me and Biggs probably got too big if they ain't book that load (What's up Hoffa?)", "Though he's gettin' plenty money, lookin' back now this shit is funny"], "meaning": "Kareem “Biggs” Burke was the third founder of Roc-A-Fella records, along with Jay & Dame Dash. While he is mostly known as Biggs, he has also been referred to as “Hoffa” on more than a few occasions.\n\nIn 2012, Burke was sentenced to five years in prison on charges related to possession and conspiracy to distribute marijuana, but was released after only four. Here he is in 2018 talking to Vlad TV about the case.\n\nhttp://youtu.be/YVYncxja7d4\n\nThis connects to Jay’s earlier line about weed now being readily available, indicating that you never know how life might turn out. “Hindsight is 20/20” is an idiom that refers to the ease and clarity of understanding past decisions in the present moment; in this case, the risks of going to jail over a substance that is now legal. While Biggs can never get those four years back, that he now makes plenty of money even after what happened seems comical, to say the least.\n\nJay-Z has talked about his frustration at Hoffa’s situation before, most notably on the final verse of Smile.", "annotation_id": "26598430", "url": "https://genius.com/26598430", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "I got lawyers like shooters Workin’ pro bono for him as a favor ’cause I throw them Ms", "vault_lines": ["I got lawyers like shooters", "Workin' pro bono for him as a favor 'cause I throw them Ms"], "meaning": "Jay Z compares his lawyer to a sniper due to the lawyer’s ability to present genuine legal issues when defending a client. It’s worth noting that pro bono is another legal term derived from the Latin phrase, “for the public good.” It is widely used to describe when someone undertakes professional labor for free, implying that Jay’s lawyer may be performing cases for him for free as a favor because of how well he has paid them in the past.\n\nThis is evident on February 2019, after ICE (Immigration and Customs Enforcement) arrested and sent 21 Savage to jail for overstaying his visa in the United States after it expired. During an interview, 21 Savage said that after being imprisoned, he phoned Meek Mill, who then contacted Jay Z, and got assistance after Jay Z added his lawyer as Savage’s defendant. He went on to compliment Jay and clarify more about his assistance, saying:\n\nHe ain’t just doing that shit for anybody cause your rap. You gotta be from a certain clot. I don’t feel like he’s just doing that for anybody. I ain’t Roc Nation or none of that shit", "annotation_id": "26590364", "url": "https://genius.com/26590364", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Sky is the limit, every day I reach", "vault_lines": ["Sky is the limit, every day I reach"], "meaning": "Wayne references his song “Ride for My Niggas (Sky is the Limit)” from Da Drought 3.\n\nWeezy tells us that every day he is able to achieve the kind of things that most people spend their lives working on.", "annotation_id": "26584471", "url": "https://genius.com/26584471", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "I’m now careful with the sentences, them only jail bars I like (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["I'm now careful with the sentences, them only jail bars I like (Woo)"], "meaning": "Wordplay. Getting sent to jail is a jail sentence and jails have bars to keep the prisoners in. But rap lyrics are often referred to as bars, and a bar might be a sentence – a complete group of words.\n\nJay-Z has to be careful with what he raps about, because it can be used by the government to convict him, resulting in a jail sentence. Jay-Z, along with many other rappers, is trying to push for a law that will make rap lyrics inadmissible in court without clear evidence that the lyrics are literal. Drakeo the Ruler was infamously imprisoned for three years on the strength of some violent braggadocio lyrics on a 2016 freestyle, even though there is no evidence that he did anything and he was eventually acquitted.\n\nhttps://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/jay-z-meek-mill-big-sean-fat-joe-back-law-limiting-rap-lyrics-at-criminal-trials-1286594/", "annotation_id": "26589004", "url": "https://genius.com/26589004", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "These ain’t songs, these is hymns ’cause I’m him It’s the Psalm 151, this New Testament The book of Hov (The book of Hov) Jesus turned water to wine, for Hov, it just took a stove", "vault_lines": ["These ain't songs, these is hymns 'cause I'm him", "It's the Psalm 151, this New Testament"], "meaning": "Psalms is a book containing lyrics to ancient Hebrew religious hymns included as part of the Christian Old Testament as well as the Jewish Tanakh. In Christian and Jewish tradition, there are only 150 Psalms – hence Hov (also a religious reference – Jay-hova / “Jehovah”, a name for the Abrahamic God) is writing the 151st, his own hymn to his miraculous success. He then escalates this to a new New Testament and picks out a title for the new Bible book about him.\n\nThe New Testament of the Christian Bible is concerned with the teachings of Jesus Christ, who, as the second person in the Trinity, is God – God can be referred to as “Him” or “He”, which is the sense in which Jay uses it in “hymns ‘cause I’m Him”. Hov’s comparing himself to Jesus, who turned water to wine at a party in the way Hov miraculously turned cooking crack on the stove into a business empire.\n\nA real “Psalm 151” does exist in the Septuagint, an early Greek translation of the Old Testament that includes parts later excised. The Psalm is written from the point of view of David, celebrating his victory over Goliath, and praising God for choosing him to become a great leader over his tall, better-looking brothers – thematically similar to the story Jay is recounting in “GOD DID”.\n\nGod anointed me with holy oil;\nGod made me leader for his people,\nruler over the children\nof his covenant.", "annotation_id": "26587594", "url": "https://genius.com/26587594", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Some new niggas out of pocket, talkin’ exotic You barely been to the Baham— (Haha) that’s another topic", "vault_lines": ["Some new niggas out of pocket, talkin' exotic", "You barely been to the Baham— (Haha) that's another topic"], "meaning": "The line starts out with some wordplay – out of pocket is a phase that means either losing money in a transaction, or behaving inappropriately. Hov suggests his poor enemies/obnoxious haters are so far out of the pocket that they’re exotic, then plays that association into the exotic holiday destination of the Bahamas – while most people would be happy to spend a week in a hotel there, the Carters have their own island. Exotic is also slang for marijuana, transitioning from the weed theme of the previous bars.\n\nHov then playfully cuts off his association because he remembers the Bahamas are a tax haven. Citizens of the Bahamas do not pay tax on income or capital gains and foreign businesses can be incorporated there. This has made the Bahamas the third richest country in the Americas, after the USA and Canada.\n\nWhile tax avoidance is legal, it’s an exploitation of loopholes that goes against what taxation is supposed to achieve – to keep money in the local economy and pay for infrastructure that businesses use to achieve their fortunes in the first place. Hov’s change of topic is a joking attempt to correct bringing up a dirty secret. Topic may be a pun on tropics.\n\nThe Bahamas were also a popular place for sourcing illicit drugs, though Jay-Z’s own connect was in St. Thomas.\n\nMonogram is the name of Jay-Z’s luxury medicinal marijuana brand. On the red carpet, stars usually dress up in suits and tuxedos. It’s not uncommon for a gentleman to have a pocket square with his initials monogrammed on them.", "annotation_id": "26585017", "url": "https://genius.com/26585017", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Monogram in my pocket off the red carpet You see the face I made that night, shit is that shockin’ Odds wasn’t great, we’d even be alive Gotta be crazy to y’all niggas, we surprised Shit is too much how we grew up (Grew up) Shit don’t even feel real to us (Damn)", "vault_lines": ["Monogram in my pocket off the red carpet", "You see the face I made that night, shit is that shockin'", "Odds wasn't great, we'd even be alive", "Gotta be crazy to y'all niggas, we surprised", "Shit is too much how we grew up (Grew up)", "Shit don't even feel real to us (Damn)"], "meaning": "Hov’s so shocked that he and his friends survived the street life, and can now legally smoke Monogram, their own brand of marijuana. He’s as shocked as Hov looked on the night of the special screening of “The Harder They Fall,” a film for which Hov was producer. Hov spotted Kelly Rowland, former Destiny’s Child group-mate of his wife Beyoncé, and made a shocked facial expression that will forever live in internet infamy.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/netflix/status/1448691828740354049?s=20&t=yJllwYHtrz1i4Yt7mCSFmA\n\nJay’s amazement is such that his success doesn’t even feel real, but it could also be the emotional unavailability caused by his rough background, or the impostor syndrome felt by working class and/or Black people who gain success.\n\nAlso note the smart play on “odds” and “even”.", "annotation_id": "26583543", "url": "https://genius.com/26583543", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "I’m at the cap table, what the splits is? Not that cap table, boy, we live this", "vault_lines": ["I'm at the cap table, what the splits is?", "Not that cap table, boy, we live this"], "meaning": "Hov uses double entendre to stress that he appears on capitalization tables (colloquially known as “cap tables”), which is a document that details the ownership splits in a company – while others might be capping (lying) about their own business achievements.\n\nSince the the capitalization industry jargon is obscure, Hov plays on the sound of cap table and splits – it evokes the gambling association of crap table and split bets.\n\nNote also the wordplay on cap and LIDS (live), as in the name of the chain of baseball cap stores. A cap is also called a lid, as in the top of a bottle.", "annotation_id": "26583480", "url": "https://genius.com/26583480", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "They said they don’t know me internationally, niggas on the road did I see a lot of Hov in Giggs", "vault_lines": ["They said they don't know me internationally, niggas on the road did", "I see a lot of Hov in Giggs"], "meaning": "Giggs is a rapper from Peckham, London, considered one of the Godfathers of the UK Rap scene. Jay-Z sees himself in his success and musicianship, anointing Giggs the Jay-Z of the UK.\n\nGiggs is from the roads, a London term meaning he comes from the streets and street life. So when Jay mentions “niggas on the road”, it’s referring to hustlers in Britain recognising him as one of their own – and, as Hov sees himself in Giggs, he recognises them as like minds in return.\n\nNote the wordplay with “Giggs” (gigs) and “on the road”, creating the image of touring. This goes with the less cryptic reading of the line, which is that – despite what people say – Jay can go on international world tours and have people know him.", "annotation_id": "26582996", "url": "https://genius.com/26582996", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "All this pain from the outside, inspired all this growth within So new planes gettin’ broken in Highest elevation of the self They done fucked around and gave the right niggas wealth", "vault_lines": ["All this pain from the outside, inspired all this growth within", "So new planes gettin' broken in", "Highest elevation of the self", "They done fucked around and gave the right niggas wealth"], "meaning": "An extremely dense passage of double-meanings and wordplay.\n\n1) Plane refers to Jay’s private plane, an emblem of his wealth. His new planes are broken in (when he takes them for a ride for the first time), he reaches his highest elevation (physically in the sky), and he makes a playful pun that he and his associates are the “right niggas” (“Wright niggas”, referring to the Wright Brothers who pioneered flight.)\n\n2) Jay-Z owns the Paper Planes clothing brand. “[N]ew Planes getting broken in” refers to the process of wearing a new piece of clothing until it becomes more comfortable.\n\n3) Jay is also undergoing a spiritual ascension, in preparation for becoming God in the following lines: “new planes” refers to the concept of the astral plane, an esoteric concept of a higher form of existence beyond mortality and the physical. The growth to his highest spiritual elevation is an ascent to Godhood.\n\n4) Jay’s getting high on weed – see “growth within” and “highest elevation”. A paper plane is slang for a joint made out of rolling paper like the Monogram No.03.", "annotation_id": "26582912", "url": "https://genius.com/26582912", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "E was down ten for this We just got his ten back then went back like, “Where the interests is?” Em light up the O3 We let y’all do the zazas, OG for the OGs", "vault_lines": ["E was down ten for this", "We just got his ten back then went back like, \"Where the interests is?\"", "Em light up the O3"], "meaning": "“E” and “Em” is Emory Jones. Emory is Jay’s childhood friend who was incarcerated for 10 years for drug dealing. Jay helped Emory get an early release by writing the judge to say he was giving Emory a job at Jay’s clothing brand, Rocawear.\n\nhttps://thejasminebrand.com/2010/11/20/the-story-behind-jay-zs-boy-emory-jones-his-early-prison-departure/\n\nJay boasts that not only did he get ten years of Emory’s life back from prison, he got those years back with interest – not only is Em able to enjoy his life more now that he’s rich and successful, getting off the streets has almost certainly extended his lifespan.\n\nZaza is a trashy type of weed popular with younger people. OG is an older, higher quality strain of marijuana. Jay suggests that he, and Emory, (as OGs, “Original Gangstas”), smoke up to celebrate all they achieved – enjoying the good weed themselves, since they earned it.\n\nHov shills his luxury medicinal marijuana brand, Monogram. 03 is one of Monogram’s products.  Note that the OG Handroll is a pre-roll that is small enough to fit in your pocket on the red carpet.", "annotation_id": "26582785", "url": "https://genius.com/26582785", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Out the mud, they gotta face you now, you can’t make up this shit", "vault_lines": ["Out the mud, they gotta face you now, you can't make up this shit"], "meaning": "“Out the mud / they gotta facial (face you) / make up” describes the kind of beauty routine someone might get if they have access to an expensive spa – taking off a mud face mask to finish off a facial, then having makeup reapplied. The wordplay plays into the theme of going public – this would be the kind of treatment a celebrity might have done before a public appearance – as well as creating the imagery of beauty and luxury.\n\nHov’s critics now have to face the fact that they can no longer criticize him for selling weed, because it’s now legal and he’s doing so through legitimate channels.\n\nYoung Guru’s take on the line:\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/reel/ChuhEnQscRo/?igshid=YmMyMTA2M2Y=", "annotation_id": "26582705", "url": "https://genius.com/26582705", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Back and forth on this turnpike, really took a toll on ’em", "vault_lines": ["Back and forth on this turnpike, really took a toll on 'em"], "meaning": "In his hustling days Jay travelled down south to sell drugs. He would have to take the NJ Turnpike, which is a toll road – expensive! But it also “took a toll” on his Timbs – a figure of speech meaning it wore them down.", "annotation_id": "26582690", "url": "https://genius.com/26582690", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "OG sold to those, you called kingpin If those your drug lords, then who are we then?", "vault_lines": ["If those your drug lords, then who are we then?"], "meaning": "The drug kingpins you thought were at the top of the food chain bought from “O.G.” Juan Perez, Jay’s partner in the 40/40 club and president of Roc Nation Sports. O.G. and, by association, Jay, were the top of the food chain. Rumor has it that O.G. sold to the drug kingpin Alpo Martinez (referenced by Jay before in “La Familia”), who supplied product to the mid-level Harlem dealer Rich Porter – Alpo believed Rich was stealing from him and murdered him on 3 January 1990.\n\nOG is also a strain of high end weed from Jay-Z’s marijuana brand, Monogram. The brand is such a luxury product that the wealthy patrons that buy it are considered kingpins – in turn, what does that make Jay-Z?", "annotation_id": "26582650", "url": "https://genius.com/26582650", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "I just got a million off a sync Without riskin’ a million years tryna get it out the sink (Woo) Hov did", "vault_lines": ["I just got a million off a sync", "Without riskin' a million years tryna get it out the sink (Woo)"], "meaning": "Jay is referring to a sync licensing deal, which is when music is synchronised with visual media – e.g. his song being played in a movie, TV show or commercial). Sync placements are notoriously lucrative – not only do artists get paid for every time the song is used (for example, every time a commercial airs on every channel), the media itself serves as an advertisement for the song, often sending it up the charts.\n\n Making money off of a sync is far less risky then cooking crack in a sink, which could’ve lead to jail time for many years.", "annotation_id": "26582309", "url": "https://genius.com/26582309", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Me and Meek could never beef, I freed that nigga from a whole bid Hov did (Hov did)", "vault_lines": ["Me and Meek could never beef, I freed that nigga from a whole bid", "Hov did (Hov did)"], "meaning": "Jay is referencing Meek Mill’s 2022 departure from Roc Nation Management to WME. Jay states there are no hard feelings on his part, and there cannot be any on Meek’s part as Jay helped get Meek out of jail in 2018.\n\nMeek was still attached to the Roc empire when this song was released – his nonprofit Reform Alliance (a joint effort with Hov to protect rappers and other musicians and artists from unjust imprisonment) is still part of Roc Nation, as is his record label imprint Dream Chasers Records.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/status/1563081890587783169", "annotation_id": "26582297", "url": "https://genius.com/26582297", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Judge it how you judge it, say we goin’ corporate Nah, we just corner boys with the corner office", "vault_lines": ["Judge it how you judge it, say we goin' corporate", "Nah, we just corner boys with the corner office"], "meaning": "Jay references his ascension from hustling on the block – boys on a street corner – to hustling in the corporate world – in a corner office.\n\nJay and his associates haven’t forgotten their roots or changed. They’re drug dealers and corner boys that have made it – not a corporation.", "annotation_id": "26582282", "url": "https://genius.com/26582282", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Now the weed in stores, can you believe this, Ty? I put my hustle onto Forbes, can you believe this guy? Then we said, “Fuck it,” took the dope public", "vault_lines": ["Now the weed in stores, can you believe this, Ty?", "I put my hustle onto Forbes, can you believe this guy?", "Then we said, \"Fuck it,\" took the dope public"], "meaning": "Jay took the same principle of selling dope in his past to start a venture with the luxury medicinal marijuana company Monogram. The brand has been featured in Forbes magazine multiple times:\nhttps://www.forbes.com/sites/willyakowicz/2020/12/10/jay-zs-cannabis-brand-monogram-launches-with-50-hand-rolled-joints/?sh=327c7b6660df\n\nJay’s amused by the audacity of being able to peddle a previously illegal product as blatantly and legitimately as he now can – it proves how great he is as a hustler. Monogram is owned by TPCO, which is a “public” company on the market, $GRAMF – hence, took the dope public.\n\n“Can you believe this guy?”, as a phrase, is a very New York figure of speech, serving to reinforce his connection to the city he once sold drugs to. It may also be a reference to Jay-Z’s 2022 interview with Kevin Hart for his Peacock interview show Hart To Heart:\n\nI was actually on Facetime with Ty, and I was saying, ‘I don’t think people realize that we just sit around all the time and [say], like, “Can you believe this shit? I can’t believe this shit.”\n\n“Ty” is Jay’s longtime friend and cousin Tyran “Ty Ty” Smith, who’s been a part of Jay Z’s life from the streets all the way to corporate America.", "annotation_id": "26582268", "url": "https://genius.com/26582268", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "I turned the cocaína into champagne, huh", "vault_lines": ["I turned the cocaína into champagne, huh"], "meaning": "One of Jesus' most famous miracles is turning water into wine. Jay turns this into a boast about going for a criminal activity to a legitimate business – his champagne brand, Ace of Spades. He sold a share of it to LVMH. Whatever the legality of what he’s selling, it’s always something in demand.\n\nThe imagery also implies a journey from doing drugs in the hood to sipping champagne at lavish parties – continuing the theme of Hov turning a negative into a positive.", "annotation_id": "26582241", "url": "https://genius.com/26582241", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "I left the dope game with my record clean, huh", "vault_lines": ["I left the dope game with my record clean, huh"], "meaning": "Here, Jay boasts about never being caught for selling drugs. His criminal record is clean. Leaving the dope game with a “clean” record is such a rare feat that Jay-Z acknowledges it here. Record can also be a reference to the “paperwork” people get when they turn informant.\n\nRecord is a double entendre for recorded music, sometimes pressed onto vinyl records. While commercial-quality vinyl recordings are pressed from molten plastic, ‘clean’ records do exist as raw material for a record cutting process called lathing. The imagery is of a blank slate onto which Jay can write his recording career.\n\nNote also the juxtaposition between “dope” (drugs) and “clean” (a word which refers to someone who is abstinent from drugs). Hov is perhaps presenting his choice to quit selling drugs as analogous to quitting using.", "annotation_id": "26582223", "url": "https://genius.com/26582223", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Nobody touched the billi’ until Hov did", "vault_lines": ["Nobody touched the billi' until Hov did"], "meaning": "Triple entendre.\n\n1) Jay-Z was named the first rapper to become a billionaire in 2019. However, this is somewhat semantic – Dr. Dre claimed to be the “first billionaire in hip-hop” in 2014, but does not consider himself a rapper as he does not write his own lyrics. It’s possible Dre was exaggerating – he was worth “only” $800 million after the sale of Beats, making Hov the first to hit the billion.\n\nAfter he achieved this, the next rapper to achieve billionaire status was Kanye West in 2020, who has boasted about being richer than Hov despite evidence to the contrary.\n\n2) Jay-Z is also stating in this line that nobody touched G.O.A.T status until he did (the term “billy” referencing a male goat).\n\n3) Bill Gates reached out to Jay in 2008 to help him with a video short for his keynote speech to Microsoft. In the video he contemplates becoming a rapper and raps a version of Jay-Z’s “Big Pimpin” while Jay-Z records him and directs him in the studio. In this way, Jay was the first rapper to touch (impact) the “Billy” Gates – who also happened to be a billionaire.\n\nJay also might have been the first to impact and have a relationship with famed billionaire Warren Buffet as they linked in 2010 and provided an interview to Forbes.", "annotation_id": "26582209", "url": "https://genius.com/26582209", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Nigga, God did His thing when He made me", "vault_lines": ["Nigga, God did His thing when He made me"], "meaning": "Wayne interpolates the chorus of his September 2018 track “Took His Time”, off his album Tha Carter V, on which he raps:\n\nMomma said God took his time when he made me\nGod took his time when he made me\n\nhttps://youtu.be/tVF6pvCwSFk?t=133\n\nThe shift in emphasis from “took his time” to “did his thing” suggests he’s less an act of God’s craft and more an act of God’s sudden inspiration and genius.\n\n“God did” is also a play on the name of the song, and the title of the album.", "annotation_id": "26580919", "url": "https://genius.com/26580919", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "I know only God can judge us, I say that for Brittney", "vault_lines": ["I know only God can judge us, I say that for Brittney"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne acknowledges God as the ultimate judge while expressing his support for Brittney Yevette Griner’s release. Griner is an American professional basketball player for the Women’s National Basketball Association and in February 2022, she was detained in Russia for bringing hashish oil into the country. On August 4, 2022, she plead guilty  and was sentenced to nine years in a Russian prison on top of a one million dollar fine, accentuating the cruel judgement she received from the court. Brittney Griner is a victim of illegitimate justice since she was judged by someone other than God.\n\nThe term “Only God Can Judge Me” is rather prominent, having been coined by 2Pac in his February 1996 song, “Only God Can Judge Me:”\n\nOnly God can judge me (That right?)\nOnly God can judge me now\nNobody else (Nobody else)\n\nLil Wayne has also mentioned the term “Only God Can Judge Me” in his 2013 song, “Scream and Shout (Hit-Boy Remix):”\n\nOnly God can judge me, case closed", "annotation_id": "26580558", "url": "https://genius.com/26580558", "state": "accepted", "votes": 76, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Breezy what the business is We pushin’ Fenty like fentanyl, the shit is all legitimate (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["Breezy what the business is", "We pushin' Fenty like fentanyl, the shit is all legitimate (Woo)"], "meaning": "“Breezy” is the nickname of Jay-Z’s business partner, Jay Brown, who is the manager of Rihanna. Hov has previously referenced Brown by this nickname in the 2007 song “Success”:\n\n“I got these niggas, Breezy, don’t worry about it”\n\nAs Jay-Z’s entertainment company Roc Nation manages Rihanna, they’re instrumental in putting together the deals for her Fenty brand, which sells makeup and lingerie – a long way from the illegal business of selling drugs.\n\n\n\nFitting the drug-dealing theme, Fentanyl is a notoriously potent opioid that is favoured by dealers for its low cost compared to heroin, and therefore commonly used to press cheap illegitimate opioids. Jay plays on that by saying “this shit is all legitimate”. America’s love affair with Fentanyl came about due to drugs companies irresponsibly pushing opioid painkillers in order to make profit – all legitimately.\n\nA more famous Breezy is Chris Brown, who is known for his attempt to push Robyn Rihanna Fenty out of his moving Lamborghini in 2009. Considering Jay’s classy tone throughout the rest of the song, is it an intentional sick joke?", "annotation_id": "26579021", "url": "https://genius.com/26579021", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "How many billionaires can come from Hov crib? Huh I count three, me, Ye and Rih Bron’s a Roc boy, so four, technically (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["How many billionaires can come from Hov crib? Huh", "I count three, me, Ye and Rih", "Bron's a Roc boy, so four, technically (Woo)"], "meaning": "This is an evolution from a line Jay raps on Drake’s 2013 song “Pound Cake”:\n\nI’ve done made more millionaires than the lotto did\nDame made millions, Biggs made millions\nYe made millions, Just made millions\nLyor made millions, Cam made millions\n\nJay mentored many people who became millionaires, but in “GOD DID” he considers that his investments in others have created billionaires. It demonstrates that even in the last 10 years Jay’s wealth and influence has grown exponentially.\n\nThe musician billionaires listed in this lyric are Jay-Z himself, Kanye West (a signee of Jay’s Roc-A-Fella Records in 2002) and Rihanna (who Jay arranged to get signed to Def Jam in 2005). The fourth billionaire is LeBron James, the legendary basketball player, who is Roc La Familia due to his close ties with Jay-Z since the early 2000s – he can be seen with Jay on this magazine cover:\n\n\n\nJay-Z uses a flutter of poetic devices on the topic of basketball here. Lebron James has… 4 rings.  A technical is a type of foul in basketball.  A rock is also slang for basketball.\n\nThe “Hov crib” line is a double entendre – it also refers to Jay-Z himself and the fact that not many billionaires could come from Marcy Projects.\n\n“Crib” also sounds like “rib,” a reference to Adam giving up one of his ribs to allow God to create Eve. Like Adam, Hov had to give up parts of himself to create the billionaires he spawned.", "annotation_id": "26578834", "url": "https://genius.com/26578834", "state": "accepted", "votes": 117, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "With the odds stacked against me, I could crack Da Vinci", "vault_lines": ["With the odds stacked against me, I could crack Da Vinci"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne exhibits his bravery and willingness to withstand all difficulties, by alluding to The Da Vinci Code as an example. The Da Vinci Code is a novel written by Dan Brown and later on, adapted into a film that debunks a conspiracy hypothesis concerning the secret reality of Jesus' sexual relationship with Mary Magdalene.\n\nIt’s also worth quoting that Lil Wayne has pertained to Da Vinci in several of his songs. Like September 2018’s “Mona Lisa” featuring Kendrick Lamar, named after Da Vinci’s masterpiece, the Mona Lisa. He also name-dropped Da Vinci in October 2009’s “Ice Cream” where he states:\n\nAhem, flow like syringes\nYeah, I’m in my mode, got a code like Da Vinci’s\nI was in the trenches, now I’m in the Trump", "annotation_id": "26578591", "url": "https://genius.com/26578591", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "But I’m so cultivating, everybody replicate me, nigga, face facts Dreadlocks, face tats, I’m the apex", "vault_lines": ["But I'm so cultivating, everybody replicate me, nigga, face facts", "Dreadlocks, face tats, I'm the apex"], "meaning": "Wayne himself comments on a common observation throughout the hip-hop world that his appearance (face tats, dreads, grills, etc.) set an image for an entire wave of rappers such as Lil Pump and Lil Uzi Vert to adapt and use. Wayne’s choice of rap name and rapping style has been replicated as often as his appearance.\n\nDespite this wave becoming more popular, Weezy remains the trendsetter or “apex”, and reminds his audience that he is the reason why most of these rappers even exist.\n\nWayne further references the generation of rappers he inspired on Lil Pump’s “Be Like Me.”", "annotation_id": "26578461", "url": "https://genius.com/26578461", "state": "accepted", "votes": 78, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Please, Lord forgive me for what the stove did", "vault_lines": ["Please, Lord forgive me for what the stove did"], "meaning": "Jay references his infamous past as a drug dealer in Brooklyn. His actions had a huge negative impact on not only the community he grew up in but also on his personal relationships.\n\nA stove can be used to turn powder cocaine into crack, which is much more addictive and has far worse effects on a community.", "annotation_id": "26578367", "url": "https://genius.com/26578367", "state": "accepted", "votes": 77, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "GOD DID", "line": "Next time we have a discussion who the G.O.A.T., you donkeys know this Forgive me, that’s my passion talkin’ (Haha) Sometimes I feel like Farrakhan (Haha) talkin’ to Mike Wallace (Haha) I think y’all should keep quiet", "vault_lines": ["Next time we have a discussion who the G.O.A.T., you donkeys know this", "Forgive me, that's my passion talkin' (Haha)", "Sometimes I feel like Farrakhan (Haha) talkin' to Mike Wallace (Haha)", "I think y'all should keep quiet"], "meaning": "The farm animal wordplay on “G.O.A.T.” and “donkey” is almost too trivial to mention.\n\n“Donkey” is an insult referring to a stubborn idiot who won’t accept Jay is the G.O.A.T. / Greatest Of All Time (hence Jay’s apology for getting too passionate in the following lines), but Mark 11:1-11 of the Gospels states that Jesus rode a donkey into Jerusalem – the first event in what would be the final days of Jesus’s mortal life, a story known as the Passion of Christ. When Hov jokes “that’s my Passion”, he’s therefore imagining himself as Jesus triumphantly riding into the city on his haters and doubters.\n\nIn one memorable exchange from a 1996 “60 Minutes” interview with Mike Wallace, Nation of Islam Minister Louis Farrakhan responds to Mr. Wallace’s suggestion that Nigeria is the “most corrupt nation in the world” by pointing out the USA’s own corruption, emphasizing that the USA should not consider itself to be in a position of moral superiority and “should keep quiet” before it seeks to condemn other nations. At the end of the clip, Minister Farrakhan takes a conciliatory tone with Mr. Wallace, stating, “I didn’t mean to be so fiery. That’s my passion.”\n\nhttps://youtu.be/bvfEj7RobQ0?t=14", "annotation_id": "26578131", "url": "https://genius.com/26578131", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
