{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Ghostwriter", "line": "Any nigga with cheddar, I bet I can make it better", "vault_lines": ["Any nigga with cheddar, I bet I can make it better"], "meaning": "Very reminiscent of Jay Z’s line in “Ride or Die”:\n\n S. Carter, ghost writer, and for the right price\n I can even make YO' shit tighter", "annotation_id": "8670729", "url": "https://genius.com/8670729", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Ghostwriter", "line": "Publishing was something I would have to earn back", "vault_lines": ["Publishing was something I would have to earn back"], "meaning": "In many record deals, the publishing (profits from selling a record) is kept with the label. Since publishing is a lucrative business, labels hold this over the artists' heads and push them to make more albums for the means to buy the rights back for their favorite albums.", "annotation_id": "8364369", "url": "https://genius.com/8364369", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Ghostwriter", "line": "Any pressure, we come with sticks like they do at Philippe’s", "vault_lines": ["Any pressure, we come with sticks like they do at Philippe's"], "meaning": "*A stick is an AK-47 assault rifle (especially in South Florida, where Ross grew up)\n\n\n\n*Philippe Chow’s is a high-end Chinese restaurant, where your meal comes with chopsticks\n\n\n\n(could also be a reference to their signature chicken satays, which come with sticks inside them)\n\n\n\nRoss combines a threat with a reference to a luxury restaurant—he’s willing to bring out an AK, but he wants you to know he’s enjoying fine dining in the meantime.", "annotation_id": "8349563", "url": "https://genius.com/8349563", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Ghostwriter", "line": "Charging for the verses, throw in the chorus", "vault_lines": ["Charging for the verses, throw in the chorus"], "meaning": "Something which has not been hidden from the public eye or ear is the cost of guest verses per rapper. Some rappers have been open enough to put it in songs too.\nRick Ross' rates for a verse could possibly be within this range. Ross However is courteous enough to serve a free chorus if you need one.", "annotation_id": "8347513", "url": "https://genius.com/8347513", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Ghostwriter", "line": "Fat boy behind a lot of your favorite flows, man", "vault_lines": ["Fat boy behind a lot of your favorite flows, man"], "meaning": "As dictionary.com states,\n\na person who writes one or numerous speeches, books, articles, etc., for another person who is named as or presumed to be the author.\n\nIn other words, a ghostwriter is an uncredited writer. Rick Ross (Fat Boy) is letting you know that he created the flows of some of your favorite artists.", "annotation_id": "8334452", "url": "https://genius.com/8334452", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Ghostwriter", "line": "So many successful entertainers with tax problems", "vault_lines": ["So many successful entertainers with tax problems"], "meaning": "A list of rappers who have been hit with tax liens and evasion charges.\n\n\n\n\nMC Hammer Owed the government: $1,400,000\nIt took until 2011 , but the IRS finally demanded some money from MC Hammer’s earnings in 1996 and ‘97, which was the period during which he was $13 million in debt first totaling around $800,000, the charge increased in 2014.\n\n\n\nJermaine Dupri How much: $3,595,004.64 total.\nIn 2008, Dupri got popped for failing to pay his taxes since 2006, and a few years later , got hit with an additional lien for $500,000.\n\n\n\nSnoop Dogg How much: $546,270.29\nIn 2013 , Snoop was hit with his second lien, with his first coming in 2008 to the tune of $476,000.\n\n\n\nJa Rule How much: $1,100,000\nThis was a pretty terrible coincidence for Ja. He got out of prison in February 2013 after serving 20 months for a gun possession charge, and then was immediately hit with a tax evasion charge.\n\n\n\nLauryn Hill How much: $900,000\nIn 2013, the ex-Fugees MC got nabbed for not paying taxes from 2005 to 2007, but fortunately, she was able to pay it back before getting punished.\n\n\n\nLil Kim How much: $126,725.12\nIn 2014, it was revealed that the Queen Bee had failed to pay her taxes for the last three years.\n\n\n\nFat Joe How much: $200,000\nJoe didn’t pay taxes on $3 million he earned between 2007 and 2010, so he eventually had to serve four month s and prison and pay back a fifteenth of his earnings.\n\n\n\nMaster P How much: $237,027.41\nIt took the IRS thirteen years to catch him, but in 2013, P was hit for not paying taxes in 1999 and 2000.\n\n\n\nTyga How much: $91,648\nThis one wasn’t too major, as it just stemmed from unpaid taxes on his earnings from his song Bouncin' On My Dick.\n\n\n\nFlo Rida How much: $1,197,655 in 2014, and $1,040,777 in 2012.\nThe IRS placed liens on his house both times, but he managed to pay them off.\n\n\n\nLil Wayne How much: $7,000,000 in 2009, $12,000,000 in 2014.\nWayne’s owed more money than anyone else on this list, but lucky for him, he’s also super rich so, he’s had no problem paying these off in the past.", "annotation_id": "8327087", "url": "https://genius.com/8327087", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Ghostwriter", "line": "DMX went homeless, and heard he back robbing", "vault_lines": ["DMX went homeless, and heard he back robbing"], "meaning": "DMX is no stranger to trouble with the law, but this is probably one of the stranger accusations to be levied against the 44-year-old. Accused of Alleged Gas Station Theft\n\n\n\n“Rozay won’t be a old nigga with back problems” Rick Ross  is referring to having to pay back taxes; tax evasion is a common crime for once successful entertainers.", "annotation_id": "8326983", "url": "https://genius.com/8326983", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Ghostwriter", "line": "Ghostwriter, blunt lighter Write a rapper’s song, then go buy a home Lyrics they recite, these are words I own", "vault_lines": ["Lyrics they recite, these are words I own"], "meaning": "Ross is saying that he has ghostwritten for many rappers, and everything that those rappers say are Ross' words, since he wrote them.\n\nIn a recent interview at The Breakfast Club, he explained more about his ghostwriting:\n\nI really think that’s on the artist themself. I’ve never discussed the people I’ve written raps for. Like I said, I’ve been in the studio with some of the biggest artists and everybody who know Rozay know I could do 10 songs in a day worth of work, so if you don’t think I have any input sitting in the studio, you’re a donkey, ya dig? But at the end of the day, what’s important is making dope music, and if you got a relationship with that artist, that’s more important than any credit for a little record that’s gonna come and go.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fVHqbgc9NeE", "annotation_id": "8326615", "url": "https://genius.com/8326615", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
