{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Heavy Artillery", "line": "I get my money, smoke extensive like it’s Friday", "vault_lines": ["I get my money, smoke extensive like it's Friday"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is highlighting his success in accumulating wealth. Rick Ross is a rapper, entrepreneur, and founder of the record label Maybach Music Group. Known for his luxurious lifestyle and business acumen, Ross often raps about wealth, success, and his business ventures, which includes the ownership of multiple Wingstop franchises.\n\nThe line also nods to the 1995 classic film Friday, starring Ice Cube and Chris Tucker. The film is centered around two friends who spend a Friday in their neighborhood smoking marijuana and dealing with various humorous situations.", "annotation_id": "32478336", "url": "https://genius.com/32478336", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Heavy Artillery", "line": "[Chorus] Oh, they got guns 45s, machine guns, and heavy artillery", "vault_lines": ["45s, machine guns, and heavy artillery"], "meaning": "Sample of the Song “Just a Prisoner” by Billy Paul (1971)\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wK6b5KTKLOg#t=440\n\n(7:20)", "annotation_id": "4738019", "url": "https://genius.com/4738019", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Heavy Artillery", "line": "I still’ll pull it, still’ll bully niggas on the Strip (Still)", "vault_lines": ["I still'll pull it, still'll bully niggas on the Strip (Still)"], "meaning": "Game is notorious for being a bully in the rap game. He was on the cover, and was featured twice, in the rap feud documentary Beef 3 (explaining his feud with G-Unit, and his feud with Yukmouth.)\n\n\n\nGame is also front and center on the cover of Beef IV\n\n\n\nRecently, he filmed himself beating up G-Unit affiliate 40 Glocc\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GrYWkNJn1Sk%E2%80%8E", "annotation_id": "2680793", "url": "https://genius.com/2680793", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Heavy Artillery", "line": "I’m sittin’ sideways like I’m in my driveway", "vault_lines": ["I'm sittin' sideways like I'm in my driveway"], "meaning": "Sittin' sidewayz is a song by the master of glamorous teeth, Paul Wall. It’s about posting up in your parked car with the door open.\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SfPLcQhXpCc\nDiamond teeth not required, but they help.", "annotation_id": "2680778", "url": "https://genius.com/2680778", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Heavy Artillery", "line": "Eminem wasn’t Dr. Dre’s only sick nigga", "vault_lines": ["Eminem wasn't Dr. Dre's only sick nigga"], "meaning": "When Eminem first broke onto the scene and into the mainstream with Slim Shady LP, parents, critics and government officials assumed that he was deranged in some way due to his twisted sense of humor.\n\n\n\nGame let’s us know that, Dr. Dre isn’t only responsible for mentoring Em, but Game as well. Another take on the line could be that Eminem is often described as a sick MC with his mic abilities, Game is informing us that Em isn’t the only one with the mic abilities that was tutored by Dre.", "annotation_id": "631307", "url": "https://genius.com/631307", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Heavy Artillery", "line": "Something like C-Murder on Worldstar when I dump out, what?", "vault_lines": ["Something like C-Murder on Worldstar when I dump out, what?"], "meaning": "Reference to the surveillance footage shown on WorldstarHipHop.com of C-Murder after an altercation with a nightclub owner, which led to him shooting a man.\n\nThe footage was later used against him in court, where he was found guilty of second-degree murder. He is currently serving a life sentence.", "annotation_id": "325504", "url": "https://genius.com/325504", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Heavy Artillery", "line": "First 48, they don’t find me? Case closed", "vault_lines": ["First 48, they don't find me? Case closed"], "meaning": "Referring to the A&E TV show, The First 48, where homicide detectives are profiled in the first 48 hours of an investigation, attempting to find a lead in the case.", "annotation_id": "325445", "url": "https://genius.com/325445", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Heavy Artillery", "line": "Murder game cold as Aspen", "vault_lines": ["Murder game cold as Aspen"], "meaning": "Referring to Aspen, Colorado, a city known for it’s mountains and winter sports activity. A cold fucking town.", "annotation_id": "325444", "url": "https://genius.com/325444", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Heavy Artillery", "line": "Insane in the membrane like Soul Assassins", "vault_lines": ["Insane in the membrane like Soul Assassins"], "meaning": "“Insane in the membrane” refers to the Cypress Hill song of the same name.\n\nSoul Assassins refers to a collection of musicians and graphic artists associated with Cypress Hill.", "annotation_id": "325439", "url": "https://genius.com/325439", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Heavy Artillery", "line": "Yeah, I Blake Griff niggas, make stiff niggas", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I Blake Griff niggas, make stiff niggas"], "meaning": "Another basketball reference, this time to Oklahoma Sooner turned L.A. Clipper, Blake Griffin. He’s saying he’s a showstopper – he’s so good, you can’t stop him. All you can do is watch.\n\nGriffin is also famous for jamming down powerful dunks on top of defenders – this is known as posterizing, as those dunks will be made into posters. When he does this, he literally makes them into stiff posters (also they are stiff when he dunks on them as they are trying to take a charge). In the same way, Game makes people stiff with his gun by shooting them up, he “Blake Griffs” them.\n\n\n\nThe name of The Game’s record label, The Black Wallstreet, actually refers to the Greenwood neighborhood of Tulsa, Oklahoma, which was home to many prominent black businessmen. Greenwood is three hours northwest of Griffin’s hometown of Oklahoma City.", "annotation_id": "325436", "url": "https://genius.com/325436", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Heavy Artillery", "line": "Bust shots in the Cavalier like I ball with Baron", "vault_lines": ["Bust shots in the Cavalier like I ball with Baron"], "meaning": "Referring to committing a drive-by shooting in a Chevrolet Cavalier, as well as basketball player Baron Davis, who joined the Cleveland Cavaliers in February of 2011.\n\n\n\nDavis is also the godfather of The Game’s son.", "annotation_id": "325430", "url": "https://genius.com/325430", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Heavy Artillery", "line": "I speed off doing 90 with Tha Carter blarin’", "vault_lines": ["I speed off doing 90 with Tha Carter blarin'"], "meaning": "Referring to Tha Carter, the fourth album by Lil Wayne, released in 2004.\n\n\n\nTha Carter is largely considered to be Lil Wayne’s breakout album.", "annotation_id": "325427", "url": "https://genius.com/325427", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
