{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hell Yeah", "line": "Sock over the sneakers, she with me I’m dippin hard", "vault_lines": ["Sock over the sneakers, she with me I’m dippin hard"], "meaning": "An unique figure of speech for using a condom. Rozay compares his male member to a athletic shoe – high performance.\n\n\n\nRoss also plays on the word dipping:\n- leaving a club with a girl\n- proceeding to ‘dip’ deeply into his catch", "annotation_id": "1672511", "url": "https://genius.com/1672511", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hell Yeah", "line": "Beautiful is today, can’t imagine tomorrow Beautiful is today, can’t imagine tomorrow", "vault_lines": ["Beautiful is today, can’t imagine tomorrow"], "meaning": "Even in dire, hellish, circumstances, the day is beautiful. But that’s not all. Ross rejoices because God’s love is real and his faith is strong. He is not fearful or hopeless about the quality of our future. It will be so beautiful that it is impossible to imagine it.\n\nBars like this remind us that Ross is essentially a peaceful, wise, old soul. Part of being “Rich Forever” is grasping that serenity is the best route to a deep and lasting happiness. Lessons like these are a major theme in Ross' music beyond this feature.\n\n\n\nPhilosophically, Ross has much in common with Lao Tzu, who in verse 30 of the Tao Te Ching writes:\n\n“He understands that the universe\nIs forever out of control,\nAnd that trying to dominate events\nGoes against the current of the Tao.\nBecause he believes in himself,\nHe doesn’t try to convince others.\nBecause he is content with himself,\nHe doesn’t need others' approval.\nBecause he accepts himself,\nThe whole world accepts him.\"", "annotation_id": "1587402", "url": "https://genius.com/1587402", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hell Yeah", "line": "Till the day that I’m gone Just know that the game mine", "vault_lines": ["Till the day that I’m gone", "Just know that the game mine"], "meaning": "Ross promises to be Rich Forever.", "annotation_id": "1577482", "url": "https://genius.com/1577482", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hell Yeah", "line": "Fire all in her eye, coming to take the flame", "vault_lines": ["Fire all in her eye, coming to take the flame"], "meaning": "As Ross looks deep into his woman’s eyes, he is attracted to what he sees.\n\nHer spirit burns bright, like fire. And in that brief moment in which their eyes meet, Ross is deeply moved by her positivity, ambition, and soul.\n\nPerhaps that is what Ross saw in Shateria Moragne-El, whom he was dating around the time when this song was released in 2013.\n\n\n\nThe idea that the eyes are a window to the soul most likely comes from the Gospel of Matthew, 6:22-23, KJV, which reads:\n\n“The light of the body is the eye if therefore thine eye be single, thy whole body shall be full of light.\n\nBut if thine eye be evil, thy whole body shall be full of darkness. If therefore the light that is in thee be darkness, how great is that darkness!\"", "annotation_id": "1577422", "url": "https://genius.com/1577422", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hell Yeah", "line": "Got me runnin’ the streets, tell me sky is the limit", "vault_lines": ["Got me runnin' the streets, tell me sky is the limit"], "meaning": "Rozay alludes to the Biggie classic, Sky’s the Limit.\n\nHustlers are on the streets of hell looking wistfully toward the heavens.", "annotation_id": "1481986", "url": "https://genius.com/1481986", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hell Yeah", "line": "Father which art in heaven Hallowed thy be thy name", "vault_lines": ["Father which art in heaven", "Hallowed thy be thy name"], "meaning": "On the intro to “Bury Me a G” from MMG’s Self Made Vol. 2 album, Ross says:\n\n“They told me it’s never too late for prayer! Oh Lord! Pray for us niggas, because we ready to die ‘bout this shit.”\n\nRoss takes his relationship with God seriously, and uses prayer as a tool for spiritual growth. By starting his verse with the first two lines of the “Lord’s Prayer”, Ross suggests that through respectful, and sincere prayer, we can be heard by God from here on Earth.\n\nHis point compliments the theme of the song, which is best surmised by Barthe’s question: “Are we living in Hell here? Hell Yeah!”\n\nEven though we are living in “Hell here”, Ross asserts that God’s love is both real and good.", "annotation_id": "1476098", "url": "https://genius.com/1476098", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hell Yeah", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Stacy-barthe-hell-yeah-lyrics", "genius_title": "Hell Yeah", "genius_id": 114929, "source": "api"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hella Smoke", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-hella-smoke-lyrics", "genius_title": "Hella Smoke", "genius_id": 7475179, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello (Remix)", "line": "Hello from the outside At least I can say that I’ve tried To tell you I’m sorry, for breaking your heart But it don’t matter, it clearly doesn’t tear you apart anymore", "vault_lines": ["Hello from the outside", "At least I can say that I've tried", "To tell you I'm sorry, for breaking your heart", "But it don't matter, it clearly doesn't tear you apart anymore"], "meaning": "In contrast with “Someone Like You,” Adele plays the role of the heartbreaker here. But twist: after all this time, she’s still obsessing about it, and he doesn’t seem to be bothered. So who’s really the heartbreaker?\n\nIn a tweet, Adele described her last album as “a break-up record,” and she sees 25 as the record where she makes up with herself. It’s possible that in this song, she’s making amends with her younger self; she wants to apologize for her past mistakes, but that younger self no longer exists.\n\nIf “Hello” addresses Adele’s estranged father, perhaps this is his apology for what he did. Here he recognizes that he hurt her when he left, and that he thinks everything’s okay because now she’s moved on.", "annotation_id": "8445550", "url": "https://genius.com/8445550", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello (Remix)", "line": "Hello from the other side I must’ve called a thousand times To tell you I’m sorry, for everything that I’ve done But when I call you never seem to be home", "vault_lines": ["Hello from the other side", "I must've called a thousand times", "To tell you I'm sorry, for everything that I've done", "But when I call you never seem to be home"], "meaning": "Adele has tried to reach out and apologize to the one she hurt. She wants to atone for her mistakes, but he won’t give her that opportunity.\n\nIn her 2015 Rolling Stone cover story, Adele said the line is not intended to be as morbid as it sounds.\n\nIt sounds a bit morbid, like I’m dead […] But it’s actually just from the other side of becoming an adult, making it out alive from your late teens, early twenties.\n\n\n\nHello!? Adele is calling you, but she keeps getting your voicemail.\n\nWe know Adele is calling from California (maybe her offer on that Malibu mansion went through?), so she’s saying hello “from the other side” of the pond to someone back in England. She’s also on the other side of pain and experience. In 21, her heart was broken. Now, she’s on “the other side”: she’s breaking another’s heart.", "annotation_id": "8445548", "url": "https://genius.com/8445548", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello (Remix)", "line": "I’ve forgotten how it felt before the world fell at our feet There’s such a difference between us And a million miles", "vault_lines": ["There's such a difference between us", "And a million miles"], "meaning": "The differences between Adele and the subject of “Hello” (maybe her father? maybe an ex-lover? maybe her former self?) have driven them apart and cause the figurative space that exists between them.\n\nBut that’s not all: Adele makes it a point to let us know that there is “a million miles” of physical space as well.\n\nOf course, she is taking poetic license here, as the circumference of the Earth (at the equator) is only 24,902 miles.", "annotation_id": "8445546", "url": "https://genius.com/8445546", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello (Remix)", "line": "I’m in California dreaming about who we used to be When we were younger and free", "vault_lines": ["I'm in California dreaming about who we used to be", "When we were younger and free"], "meaning": "Adele is homesick in California, fantasizing about the good old days with her beloved. These memories stay on her mind like a recurring dream—she hasn’t completely moved on.\n\nIt’s an allusion to the song “California Dreamin” by The Mamas and the Papas. Adele has drawn comparisons to lead singer Mama Cass Elliot due to her sultry, soulful voice. It’s interesting that Sia, who wrote several rejected songs for 25, covered “California Dreamin” for the movie San Andreas.\n\n“California Dreamin” is significant not only because of the connection Adele has to the Mamas & Papas but also because the last verse of “Dreamin” reflects the impending dissolution of a relationship, which is also referenced throughout “Hello” and much of 21.", "annotation_id": "8445545", "url": "https://genius.com/8445545", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello (Remix)", "line": "Hello, can you hear me?", "vault_lines": ["Hello, can you hear me?"], "meaning": "For those who literally can’t hear Adele, Molly Lou Bartholomew created a beautiful ASL interpretation of the song.\n\nAlthough not the song’s theme, the repeating chorus line “Hello from the other side” represents the world of the hearing impaired, fantastically captured by the singer.\n\nhttps://vimeo.com/144355064", "annotation_id": "8445541", "url": "https://genius.com/8445541", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello (Remix)", "line": "They say that time’s supposed to heal ya But I ain’t done much healing", "vault_lines": ["They say that time's supposed to heal ya", "But I ain't done much healing"], "meaning": "Adele doesn’t agree with the expression “time heals all wounds.” In those 25-odd years, she has never gotten over the pain of that abandonment.\n\nIt has been said, ‘time heals all wounds.’ I do not agree. The wounds remain. In time, the mind, protecting its sanity, covers them with scar tissue and the pain lessens. But it is never gone. – Rose Kennedy\n\nMark Evans, Adele’s father, left his wife and daughter in 1990, but he only started making serious attempts at reconciliation in 2013 after the staggering success of 21 and his own cancer diagnosis.", "annotation_id": "8445538", "url": "https://genius.com/8445538", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello (Remix)", "line": "I was wondering if after all these years You’d like to meet, to go over everything", "vault_lines": ["You'd like to meet, to go over everything"], "meaning": "Many wonder exactly who the “you” is. In classic form, Adele says it’s no one specific:\n\nIt’s about friends, ex-boyfriends, it’s about myself, it’s about my family. It’s also about my fans as well.\n\nHowever, many have speculated that it may be about her father Mark Evans, who claims he reconnected with his daughter while she was writing 25. Mark had left Adele and her mother when Adele was only a child and they were estranged for decades, but after his own cancer diagnosis and the birth of Adele’s son Angelo, Evans reached out.\n\n\n\nAdele at 10 and her father Mark Evans", "annotation_id": "8445536", "url": "https://genius.com/8445536", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello (Remix)", "line": "Hello, it’s me", "vault_lines": ["Hello, can you hear me?"], "meaning": "The song’s first line matches the melody of Lionel Richie’s iconic line, “Hello, is it me you’re looking for? from his own “Hello.”\n\n\n\nWhen asked about the similar titles on BBC1, Adele said “We’ve definitely got to do something, me and Lionel,” and proposed a competitive hello-off. And then it kind of happened. Kind of.\n\nIn an interview on Beats 1 Radio she said that “Hello, it’s me” and the other phone call references throughout the song were meant to express her feelings about her comeback. Adele said she wanted to call all her fans, friends, family, and even strangers to tell them about the news. Adele chose the song as the first single to start a conversation with her fans.", "annotation_id": "8445535", "url": "https://genius.com/8445535", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello (Remix)", "line": "Or give it to you live, Beethoven on the Fifth", "vault_lines": ["Or give it to you live, Beethoven on the Fifth"], "meaning": "Ross uses a spin on the common concert phrase “coming to you live” to say he will address his issues in person. Clever wordplay is then utilized to build a metaphor around Beethoven’s famous Fifth Symphony.\n\nThe symphony is performed by a live orchestra which plays off the first part of this line. However, Beethoven’s Fifth Symphony is also know as the “Victory Symphone”). which suggests a second meaning. Ross will face any issue in person and be victorious.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_4IRMYuE1hI", "annotation_id": "8334775", "url": "https://genius.com/8334775", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello (Remix)", "line": "Renzel, aka Ricky Rubin", "vault_lines": ["Renzel, aka Ricky Rubin"], "meaning": "Rick Ross, who derived his stage name from drug dealer kingpin “Freeway” Rick Ross, goes by a ton of different names including Rick Ross, Ross, Rozey, & Renzel. It would seem Ross is adding legendary record producer Rick Rubin to the list of aliases.", "annotation_id": "8334482", "url": "https://genius.com/8334482", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello (Remix)", "line": "Poverty line hang lower than my pants", "vault_lines": ["Poverty line hang lower than my pants"], "meaning": "His pants hang quite low; but not as low as the poverty line. Of all the levels of wealth, the poverty line would be the among the lowest, and, while Mr. Ross’s pants do sag low, they don’t hang down quite that far. The rhyme of “France” and “pants” should further support the extent of his richness, the south of France being a notably swanky place to travel to.", "annotation_id": "8109604", "url": "https://genius.com/8109604", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello (Remix)", "line": "Tears fall all the way to the south of France", "vault_lines": ["Tears fall all the way to the south of France"], "meaning": "Various locations in France have been used in music to depict great emotion: from Kanye’s naked jaunts in the south, to Ross' Ferrari fondling in the south, to LCD Soundsystem using the middle of France as a point of bored escapism.", "annotation_id": "8071413", "url": "https://genius.com/8071413", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello (Remix)", "status": "done", "targets": 12, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-hello-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "Hello (Remix)", "genius_id": 2342379, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello, Good Morning (Remix)", "line": "Man, the last time I checked, I was bubbling out ’Bout to turn down shows out in Dublin now", "vault_lines": ["Man, the last time I checked, I was bubbling out", "'Bout to turn down shows out in Dublin now"], "meaning": "The Billboard Bubbling Under Hot 100 Singles chart, also known as Bubbling Under the Hot 100, is a weekly chart that lists the most popular songs that are not yet on the main Billboard Hot 100.\n\nNicki Minaj is referring to how she used to only get her songs on the Bubbling Under Billboard Hot 100 chart instead of the main Billboard Hot 100 chart, now she’s rich and famous enough to be able to cancel shows in other countries, she’s also likely saying she can get her songs into the Billboard Hot 100 chart rather than just the Bubbling Under chart now.", "annotation_id": "33108078", "url": "https://genius.com/33108078", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello, Good Morning (Remix)", "line": "Got that shimmy shimmy ya, shimmy ya, shimmy yo", "vault_lines": ["Got that shimmy shimmy ya, shimmy ya, shimmy yo"], "meaning": "This a reference and homage to “ODB” also known as “ol’ dirty bastard”: and this is referring to one of his biggest songs to date known as “shimmy ya”: which in the song he repeats that at the beginning of his verses which the shimmy is a reference to club dancing.  So Nicki is making a slick reference to another rap MC.", "annotation_id": "28741180", "url": "https://genius.com/28741180", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello, Good Morning (Remix)", "line": "But did I kill a Queen?", "vault_lines": ["But did I kill a Queen?"], "meaning": "Nicki allegedly threw shots at Lil' Kim when she made the claim “But did I kill a Queen?”. People also thought that she made fun of the tragic death of Alexander McQueen, who shared a moment with Lil Kim on an awards show.", "annotation_id": "27288766", "url": "https://genius.com/27288766", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello, Good Morning (Remix)", "line": "I ain’t coming out for less than a hunnid thou’", "vault_lines": ["I ain’t coming out for less than a hunnid thou'"], "meaning": "Diddy really wanted Nicki on this song, and they both argued about what her pay rate would be to be featured. They both finally settled that $100,000 would be sustainable.", "annotation_id": "25934968", "url": "https://genius.com/25934968", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello, Good Morning (Remix)", "line": "Young money, I do it for the youth dem", "vault_lines": ["Young money, I do it for the youth dem"], "meaning": "Young Money is the label that signed Nicki and now the crew that she rolls with. Nicki frequently uses Jamaican creole in her lyrics, and the “youth dem” in this context means “the guys”. That’s how many Jamaican guys refer to their homies.\n\n\n\nNicki and Sean Kingston in Jamaica", "annotation_id": "98513", "url": "https://genius.com/98513", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello, Good Morning (Remix)", "line": "Your baby momma cry for me like Jodeci", "vault_lines": ["Your baby momma cry for me like Jodeci"], "meaning": "“Cry For You” is a song by Jodeci\n\nP. Diddy is having sex with your baby momma and she’s crying his name", "annotation_id": "72864", "url": "https://genius.com/72864", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hello, Good Morning (Remix)", "line": "Yeah, it’s the Teflon Don", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, it’s the Teflon Don"], "meaning": "Rick Ross’s nickname – purloined from Jon Gotti, who earned the nickname by hiring thug-ass defense lawyers..\n\n\n\n..also the name of Rick Ross' latest album", "annotation_id": "72863", "url": "https://genius.com/72863", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
