{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Back", "line": "Fornicate in my fortress, 40k still my mortgage", "vault_lines": ["Fornicate in my fortress, 40k still my mortgage"], "meaning": "A mortgage is a special type of loan for buying property where monthly payments are spread over a number of years.\n\nRoss wants the listener to know that not only is he living in his 7 million dollar home, but fornicates there as well. He fornicates in mansions while he is still paying.\n\nHis segment on MTV Cribs is a must watch.", "annotation_id": "1300092", "url": "https://genius.com/1300092", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Back", "line": "How we rose from the sewers, funny now I’m the shit", "vault_lines": ["How we rose from the sewers, funny now I'm the shit"], "meaning": "Double meaning of “shit.” Ross uses to both mean the worst of things (“sewers” where shit and other ends up) to the best (“the shit”).", "annotation_id": "975783", "url": "https://genius.com/975783", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Back", "line": "I look in my fridge, my shit lookin’ scarce I got a few kids, we need some shit on the shelf I get a knock at the door, they say my rent overdue", "vault_lines": ["I look in my fridge, my shit lookin' scarce", "I got a few kids, we need some shit on the shelf", "I get a knock at the door, they say my rent overdue"], "meaning": "Ross noted that he wrote this song from the mindset of “somebody who may not have much”.\n\nThis person knows that he has no food, but has a family to feed. On top that, he’s been given an eviction notice because he has failed to pay his rent.\n\n\n\nErgo, this man will resort to desperate measures in order to feed his family and keep a roof over their heads. In the next line, he notes how his partners sell dope, to make sure they don’t fall into the same situation he has.", "annotation_id": "963502", "url": "https://genius.com/963502", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Back", "line": "These niggas won’t hold me back These niggas won’t hold me back These niggas won’t hold me back These niggas won’t hold me back These niggas won’t hold me back These niggas won’t hold me back These niggas won’t hold me back These niggas won’t hold me back These hoes won’t hold me back These hoes won’t hold me back These niggas won’t hold me back These niggas won’t hold me back", "vault_lines": ["These hoes won't hold me back"], "meaning": "Ross told MTV News what “hold me back” means in July 2012:\n\nThat’s the struggle, the angle I wanted to take on that record once again from somebody who may not have much, but that don’t determine where you end up in the game. From struggle to triumph, as long as you stay loyal with your clique and your family, you can overcome whatever. That’s how I feel.", "annotation_id": "952234", "url": "https://genius.com/952234", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Back", "line": "When you feedin’ your circle, watch your shipments improve", "vault_lines": ["When you feedin' your circle, watch your shipments improve"], "meaning": "Ross channels his mafioso side for this line, as he’s referring to how those in the Cosa Nostra (Mafia) usually have an inner circle of people that they conduct important mafia business. If you “feed” (support) said circle, they will do the same for you; which in turn give you better drug shipments.\n\nMore universally, if you support those closest to you (in Ross’s case, MMG), your money and success will get bigger, too.", "annotation_id": "925532", "url": "https://genius.com/925532", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Back", "line": "All my exes deported, shout-out Texas, New Orleans", "vault_lines": ["All my exes deported, shout-out Texas, New Orleans"], "meaning": "He gets rid of his ex-girlfriends the way the US government deports illegals. The ex/Texas rhyme is a reference to the famous country song “All My Ex’s Live in Texas” by George Strait.\n\n\n\nThe New Orleans shout out is mirrored in the song’s video.", "annotation_id": "883072", "url": "https://genius.com/883072", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Back", "line": "Then I start sippin’ purple, got my shit screwed", "vault_lines": ["Then I start sippin' purple, got my shit screwed"], "meaning": "Ross drinks purple drank, or codeine, which gets him drunk/slizzard. When saying he’s screwed, Ross refers to the chopped & screwed style of music, which is famous in Texas, where purple drank was originated.\n\n\n\nIn addition, he could also be making a reference about the purple covers of chopped and screwed albums/mixtapes.", "annotation_id": "883064", "url": "https://genius.com/883064", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Back", "line": "I multiplied my hustle, stimulated my mind Motivated my niggas and we’ll never divide, NO!", "vault_lines": ["I multiplied my hustle, stimulated my mind", "Motivated my niggas and we'll never divide, NO!"], "meaning": "Used his hustle to increase more than his pockets. He increased his education and respect. Now his group will always be faithful.\n\nAlso a reference to Tupac’s classic line:\n\nNiggas never die\nWe just retaliate with hate until we multiply", "annotation_id": "881729", "url": "https://genius.com/881729", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Back", "status": "done", "targets": 11, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-hold-me-back-lyrics", "genius_title": "Hold Me Back", "genius_id": 80009, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Down", "line": "Being a black male in America is a charge", "vault_lines": ["Being a black male in America is a charge"], "meaning": "A theme played off by many rappers, simply being a man of African American descent you are almost sure to get in trouble with the law. Jay Z illustrates this subject very well on his song off the Black Album, 99 Problems.\n\nThere have been many different reports that show black males have a much higher chance of going to prison than whites, or Hispanic’s. Black males have a 28% chance of going to prison while white males only have a 4% chance! That is a huge disparity, and goes to show that there is racial profiling involved in the justice system.\n\nHere is another article that speaks on the issue.", "annotation_id": "3128417", "url": "https://genius.com/3128417", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Down", "line": "I’ll stalk your nigga, chalk your nigga If I thought your nigga was a cop oh nigga Working for the cops came in for the talk oh nigga If somebody finna’ snitch, stop your nigga", "vault_lines": ["I'll stalk your nigga, chalk your nigga", "If I thought your nigga was a cop oh nigga", "Working for the cops came in for the talk oh nigga", "If somebody finna' snitch, stop your nigga"], "meaning": "You better not let Rozay find out one of your homies is a snitch, or there will be consequences.\n\nThis line could have something to do with some of what Ross says earlier in the verse about his friend in jail. These could be related because if Rozay found out someone snitched on his friend, there would definitely be some kind of repercussions.", "annotation_id": "3128382", "url": "https://genius.com/3128382", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Down", "line": "I remember the days when I used to jack my dick And had to catch a bus, to go check a flick", "vault_lines": ["And had to catch a bus, to go check a flick"], "meaning": "Ross reminisces about his previous, less fortunate life before he became rich and famous. At the time this compilation album was released, Rozay had only been relevant in the music industry for about a year after the release of his debut album Port of Miami was certified gold.\n\nThe days before Rick Ross could get any bitch he wanted to, or drive whatever car he wanted, wasn’t to long ago I suppose.", "annotation_id": "3128311", "url": "https://genius.com/3128311", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Down", "line": "I was raised in these streets Still one of the coolest cats you could ever meet", "vault_lines": ["I was raised in these streets", "Still one of the coolest cats you could ever meet"], "meaning": "Rozay was forced to grow up in the dangerous streets of Carol City. There is a belief that most people are a product of their environment, however Rozay is not. Despite the violence and drug use all throughout Ross' childhood, he is still a cool dude.", "annotation_id": "3125442", "url": "https://genius.com/3125442", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold Me Down", "status": "done", "targets": 4, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-hold-me-down-lyrics", "genius_title": "Hold Me Down", "genius_id": 413065, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "I sold more dope than I sold records You niggas sold records, never sold dope So I ain’t hearing none of that street shit ’Cause in my mind, you motherfuckers sold soap", "vault_lines": ["'Cause in my mind, you motherfuckers sold soap"], "meaning": "Push is a MC who has built his career on delivering honest details about his past days of dealing coke. His name (Pusha T) even stands for “Push-A-Ton” which relates to selling and moving cocaine.\n\nHe’s essentially communicating that he’s not hearing any “dope” or raw hip hop currently. Phonies posing as street dealers in the rap game brag about distributing cocaine but have never sold the actual product. Essentially, they’re selling “soap”— a watered down version of the real thing.", "annotation_id": "16303212", "url": "https://genius.com/16303212", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "We was made in His image, dialing and it’s much clearer", "vault_lines": ["We was made in His image, dialing and it's much clearer"], "meaning": "Tying in with the previous line he refers to being a kingpin, as to why dealers are made in his image. The reason for how he can see God’s face in the mirror, is a pun on being a “Drug Lord”, as Scarface relative to the “Kingpin lifestyle,” like Scarface. Also whiteness, represents purity and goodness in Christianity, the properties cocaine happens to share.\n\n\n\nSo the image gets much\" clearer\", when costumers dial his phone asking for drugs. He is morally compelled to feel as if he is their “healer”, for their daily troubles.\nThis is similar when Christians get on their knees to receive their “Sunday Fix”, addicts follow in a manner. They get high off of “dopamine” in the spiritual energy from Southern Baptist services, the exact feeling users and dealers get off their activities. A drug dealer and spiritual healer are one, they have the same demographic. The church and streets provide the “service”, different messages. Most ex-crack dealers often turn to God, to change their life to become preachers.", "annotation_id": "2508852", "url": "https://genius.com/2508852", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "Like Scarface but it’s God’s face in that mirror", "vault_lines": ["Like Scarface but it's God's face in that mirror"], "meaning": "https://players.brightcove.net/4863540648001/HklmClHkx_default/index.html?videoId=5236765247001\n\nOf course the street layer, which would be Scarface’s, would be throughout all of my music. But there is a conscious element to all of my music. Like there is always an explanation, it’s always regret, it’s always knowing right and wrong, you know what I’m saying? I always acknowledge right and wrong and Hold On is one of those songs where it’s an emotional record and I’m really trying to get it across that man, I want you to understand that I have a heart, do you know what I’m saying? I have a heart and I actually do understand that it weighs on my conscious sometimes.", "annotation_id": "2314680", "url": "https://genius.com/2314680", "state": "verified", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "Everything that it seems, hear my passion again Was never my dream, the immaculate win", "vault_lines": ["Everything that it seems, hear my passion again", "Was never my dream, the immaculate win"], "meaning": "https://players.brightcove.net/4863540648001/HklmClHkx_default/index.html?videoId=5236769518001\n\nI really was never supposed to be a rapper, man. Like, I didn’t start off rapping. It was always something that my brother was into and I feel like it all just fell into my lap and I never dreamed this. I never dreamed about it. It used to be a joke that I was never going to have the incredible ‘getting a record deal’ story. There was no amazing battle that I did to get a record deal, it just happened to be my best friends lived up the street and they ended up being super producers and my brother was a really good writer and has been a really good writer since a child. I simply just was hanging around the studio and you sort of – when you are around it you sort of know the criteria and the standard for raps, and that was how I learned. I learned by being around my brother and being around Pharrell and knowing what was whack by their standards. So I just tried to write. I tried to write a verse one day and everybody liked it and that is how I became the other half of Clipse.", "annotation_id": "2314650", "url": "https://genius.com/2314650", "state": "verified", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "God body and mind, food for the soul", "vault_lines": ["God body and mind, food for the soul"], "meaning": "God body is a reference to the Nation of Gods and Earths adjective used to describe a man who has Knowledge of Self.\n\n\n\nJay Z recently had a similar line on Heaven\n\nArm, leg, leg, arm, head – this is God body", "annotation_id": "2276581", "url": "https://genius.com/2276581", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "If you slipping you fall, I got you, my nigga, hold on If you right or you wrong, if you riding, come on By the end of this song, I got you, my nigga, hold on I got you, my nigga, hold on I got you, my nigga, hold on If you right or you wrong, if you riding, come on By the end of this song, I got you, my nigga, hold on I got you, my nigga, hold on I got you, my nigga, hold on I got you, my nigga, hold on I got you, my nigga, hold on", "vault_lines": ["If you slipping you fall, I got you, my nigga, hold on", "By the end of this song, I got you, my nigga, hold on", "I got you, my nigga, hold on"], "meaning": "A repetition of Ross' lines from his verse, which emphasize the theme of this song: Ross and Pusha looking out for and watching over over their people, like real Bosses do. No matter how bad a situation might seem, Ross and Pusha leave no man behind – they always have their boys covered and protected.", "annotation_id": "2274212", "url": "https://genius.com/2274212", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "If you slip and you fall I got you, my nigga, hold on If you right or you wrong, if you riding, come on", "vault_lines": ["If you slip and you fall I got you, my nigga, hold on", "If you right or you wrong, if you riding, come on"], "meaning": "Ross takes responsibility for his boys, because it his duty of being the Baous: if ever one of his people falters or fails, Rozay will be there to pick him up. Ross stresses the importance of loyalty, and will always have his people’s back; through the good times and the bad times.", "annotation_id": "2274191", "url": "https://genius.com/2274191", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "[Outro: Rick Ross, Pusha T & Together]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The outro by both Push & Ross symbolises the epitome of the song – the street life is harder than it seems. Often the drug dealing, street lifestyle is glorified throughout hip hop and people don’t understand the struggle. This song is an anthem for the kids on the street; the feeling when your best friend got caught by the cops on his 3rd strike, the feeling when your homie has been in for 5 years and can’t take it anymore, wanting to SNITCH to get out.\n\nPush, Ross & Ye capture that emotion, to say “It’s okay man, we got you”", "annotation_id": "2269901", "url": "https://genius.com/2269901", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "Scoring from the heights but I wanted mine purer", "vault_lines": ["Scoring from the heights but I wanted mine purer"], "meaning": "He was making a mountain of money but he stopped selling that cut of cocaine to get his shit purer, which undoubtedly would be more expensive. This also connects with the next line, as both him and Hitler were after their version of the purest.\n\n\n\nBit of drug dealing wordplay as well, he can be as high as the sky and still make a shit ton of money (scoring the heights) but he chose pure money. The heights here also refer to Washington Heights.\n\nPUSHHH", "annotation_id": "2269486", "url": "https://genius.com/2269486", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "[Kanye West harmonizes for 25 seconds]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Classic Yeezy autotune. Kanye’s recent Yeezus album; produced concurrently with My Name Is My Name; has liberal use of autotune, especially the track Blood On The Leaves, which has many similarities to this song (the actual melody, production by Hudson Mohawke, autotune; and Kanye West)\n\nYe is employing a gibberish technique that many producers and songwriters use when in the creative process.  It’s used to get ideas flowing for brainstorming potential melodies for a record.  Most people who use this technique don’t actually use the nonsensical musical vomit on the song itself but Yeezus made an exception because THIS PUSHA T!\n\nHere is Ryan Leslie using the same technique in a demo song.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uHodee8Kfgg\n\nGo deeper into this snippet of musical masterpiece here.", "annotation_id": "2267829", "url": "https://genius.com/2267829", "state": "accepted", "votes": 117, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "The judge and the jury, the jewellery mad froze", "vault_lines": ["The judge and the jury, the jewellery mad froze"], "meaning": "Playing off the previous reference to “white” people, Push implies the judge and the jury are out to get him. As he spits in Exodus 23:1\n\nWhere the blood and the battle scars?\nWe the ones that judge juggling the gavels on\n\nNote the play on jury/jewellery. While the jury’s out to sent him the to slammer, he’s iced out from all the drug money.", "annotation_id": "2265932", "url": "https://genius.com/2265932", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "So I’m labeled the rebel, nigga get on my level", "vault_lines": ["So I'm labeled the rebel, nigga get on my level"], "meaning": "Ties together with the line before as the song “Rebel Of The Underground” was featured on 2Pac’s album 2Pacalypse Now.", "annotation_id": "2265894", "url": "https://genius.com/2265894", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "[Verse 2: Rick Ross]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Rick Ross was part of the featured guests that has been annouced pretty early.This particular guest has been made public in April already in an interview. They also collaborated earlier this year on the track “Millions”.", "annotation_id": "2265870", "url": "https://genius.com/2265870", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "Crack dealer living like a hoop star Black marble, white walls in my new spot Four chains, big studs, a nigga too fly", "vault_lines": ["Crack dealer living like a hoop star", "Black marble, white walls in my new spot", "Four chains, big studs, a nigga too fly"], "meaning": "Rick Ross was a former cocaine dealer, and the profits from crack sales allowed him to live a comfortable life, like an NBA star.\n\nRoss describes in detail what his drug money allowed him to afford: rich home interiors and copious amounts of jewelry.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/rickyrozay/statuses/376141899259592704", "annotation_id": "2265817", "url": "https://genius.com/2265817", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "Built a school in Ethiopia, should enroll in myself", "vault_lines": ["Built a school in Ethiopia, should enroll in myself"], "meaning": "Rick Ross has been successful as a rapper and as a result he has a lot of money. He has done what many famous celebrities have done and donated to charity, in this case he helped build a school in Ethiopia. Ross is exaggerating the irony of his own lack of a complete education even though Ross actually graduated from Miami Carol City Senior High School. And received a full ride scholarship to play football at Albany State University.", "annotation_id": "2265605", "url": "https://genius.com/2265605", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "Fuck copping them foams, when you copping the home", "vault_lines": ["Fuck copping them foams, when you copping the home"], "meaning": "Rozay has grown up, he’s way past obsessing over “foams” (Foamposites) and graduated to bigger things, like real estate.\n\n\n\nReminds you of what he said in MMG Untouchable\n\nI’m buying real estate and I’m building my plans", "annotation_id": "2265597", "url": "https://genius.com/2265597", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "Nah, I ain’t got nothing in common with yous Pain in my heart, it’s as black as my skin", "vault_lines": ["Nah, I ain't got nothing in common with yous", "Pain in my heart, it's as black as my skin"], "meaning": "Push has seen and done alot of questionable things in his life. Unrelatable unless you’ve lived with him, side by side. As a result he has a heart that’s “black” and hardened by too many experiences. His heart has experienced as much darkness as his skin tone.\n\n\n\nThe phrase “Pain In My Heart” could be a reference to the Otis Redding song of the same name. This isn’t the first time Otis would be referenced in a song by a member of G.O.O.D. music.", "annotation_id": "2265594", "url": "https://genius.com/2265594", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "Got rich selling hope to the hopeless", "vault_lines": ["Got rich selling hope to the hopeless"], "meaning": "#Selling Hope VS Selling Dope\nDrugs are an escape for many people stuck in the hood due to the pleasant high they create. Pusha made his riches helping people “forget” the realities they see in poverty, unemployment, gang banging and such.\n\n\n\nAdditionally, the same drugs he’s pushing are going to get him out of the hood and towards a better life, and so even in his dealing, people around him see a way out of the hood, and this actually attracts more people to get into drug-dealing in the hopes of moving on up.", "annotation_id": "2265582", "url": "https://genius.com/2265582", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "We ain’t the same color clarity of diamond, nigga", "vault_lines": ["We ain't the same color clarity of diamond, nigga"], "meaning": "Color and Clarity are two properties that determine the value of precious gems. Along with Cut and Carat these four C’s are used to appraised the cost and value of diamonds.\n\nPusha is saying that if you were a diamond, you wouldn’t be in the same class as him.", "annotation_id": "2265152", "url": "https://genius.com/2265152", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "We were born to be kings, only major league teams", "vault_lines": ["We were born to be kings, only major league teams"], "meaning": "A somewhat common motif in rap that has been hashed on is that Africans were born to be kings: Pusha shared the same sentiment earlier on this song and Pimp C’s sampled intro on “FuckWithMeYouKnowIGotIt” (which features Ross) goes in detail on how blacks were born to succeed.\n\nRoss and his crew are no different – they were destined for greatness. Ross compares MMG’s success in the rap game to the baseball hierarchy: in American professional baseball, there are minor baseball leagues to help players prepare for the pros. However, Ross and his crew are highly successful, so they only play in the major league (of the rap game).", "annotation_id": "2265135", "url": "https://genius.com/2265135", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "I was pissing my shorts, having rich nigga thoughts", "vault_lines": ["I was pissing my shorts, having rich nigga thoughts"], "meaning": "Even when Ross was just a tyke that wasn’t even potty-trained, he was still dreaming big.\n\n\n\nRoss has always been a big dreamer; it was portrayed in the beginning skit of the Aston Martin Music video, where a young version of Ross is dreaming of the day he’ll own an Aston Martin.", "annotation_id": "2265118", "url": "https://genius.com/2265118", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "No witness, no Jehovahs", "vault_lines": ["no witness, no Jehovahs"], "meaning": "He makes a dope play off of the religion Jehovah’s Witness; he doesn’t leave any witnesses for his illegal activities and therefore doesn’t have the police knocking on his door. Jehovah’s Witness' are infamous for knocking on peoples doors, trying to convert them!", "annotation_id": "2265104", "url": "https://genius.com/2265104", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "No woman, no child,", "vault_lines": ["No woman, no child,"], "meaning": "Playing off of the stereotype that African-American men abandon their families he’s also saying he doesn’t implicate women or children in his illegal schemes, therefore possibly having to leave them behind to keep them safe.\n\n\n\nThink with the following Scarface line, its a reference to Tony Montana’s code of ethics in the film Scarface when he refused to detonate a bomb because he had a ‘No Wife, No Kids’ policy\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZcQtUdZ5Afs", "annotation_id": "2265100", "url": "https://genius.com/2265100", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "Young king bury me inside a glass casket Windex wipe me down for the life after", "vault_lines": ["Young king bury me inside a glass casket", "Windex wipe me down for the life after"], "meaning": "Instead of a traditional casket, Rozay wants to be buried in one that is made of glass because:\n\n\nEveryone can see him\nIt’s uncommon, so he’d stand out like the Boss he is\nIt’s presumably more expensive and would show his wealth\n\n\n\n\n\nWindex is a glass/hard-surface cleaner that would make his glass casket sparkling clean and easy for people to see him.\n\nIt also represents the purification/forgiveness after all the “dirt” of dealing and street life that eventually elevated Ross to where he is today.", "annotation_id": "2251392", "url": "https://genius.com/2251392", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "Aryan, blonde hair, blue-eyed like the Führer", "vault_lines": ["Aryan, blonde hair, blue-eyed like the Führer"], "meaning": "Push compares the purity of the cocaine that he moves with the Nazi ideology of racial purity.\n\nThe “Aryan” and “Heights” combination is also reminiscent of a Nas line in “Triple Beam Dreams”:\n\nWhere I’m from ain’t far from Washington Heights to cop Aryan", "annotation_id": "2244237", "url": "https://genius.com/2244237", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "Overnight I seen a nigga go get a Carrera Two weeks later I had to be that boy pallbearer", "vault_lines": ["Overnight I seen a nigga go get a Carrera", "Two weeks later I had to be that boy pallbearer"], "meaning": "RIP LIL SNUPE\n\n\nThe young 18 year old rapper was shot twice and mortally wounded with two shots to the torso area.\n\nRick has seen some crazy things, overnight someone going from not having a pot to piss in, to owning an expensive Porsche Carrera sports car. But, as fast as the money comes, it can all go away. Two weeks later Rick was at Lil Snupe’s funeral.\n\nLil Snupe’s homie Meek Mill even tweeted the line:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MeekMill/statuses/400798999596523520", "annotation_id": "2238192", "url": "https://genius.com/2238192", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "Descended from kings, we at it again Just hand me the crown, I’m active again", "vault_lines": ["Descended from kings, we at it again", "Just hand me the crown, I'm active again"], "meaning": "Perhaps referencing Nas‘ joint N.I.G.G.E.R:\n\nDescendant of Kings, it’s necessary I bling\n\nAfrican Americans were taken from their homeland where they were Kings of their own country, and were forced in to slavery.  But Pusha-Ton has come back from being forced and beaten down to become the King of the rap game, just like his ancestors were actually Kings in Africa.", "annotation_id": "2237571", "url": "https://genius.com/2237571", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "They tipping the scale for these crackers to win No reading, no writing, made us savage of men They praying for jail but I mastered the pen", "vault_lines": ["No reading, no writing, made us savage of men", "They praying for jail but I mastered the pen"], "meaning": "For a very long time, black members of American society were denied the same treatment as whites.  One of the reason why there is still such an equality gap between blacks and whites is that schools were segregated by race, and blacks were not taught to read and write to the same extent as whites.\n\nHe plays off the term “crackers”, implying white people but furthermore, on “scales” and “crack”-ers, which is a smart drug reference.\n\nIt has even been recently proved that government is more responsive to low white graduation rates than black.  Black people were at one point seen as illiterate savages, but Push has risen above that.\n\n\n\nSome people believe that this gap and supposed lack of care about black education is evidence of the government secretly wanting to imprison black members of society. Push could have at one point been destined for jail, but he says he has mastered the pen.  This can be seen two ways:\n\n\nHe mastered the art of writing and reciting raps, essentially beating the white man at his own game\nHe mastered the penitentiary – and there’s no chance of him going there now.", "annotation_id": "2237548", "url": "https://genius.com/2237548", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "line": "[Produced by Hudson Mohawke & Kanye West]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Hold On” definitely falls under some of Hudson Mohawke’s softer works thanks to the sample, piano, and autotuned vocals that Kanye supplies to the track. Despite these, the song still utilizes common trap sounds that one could come to expect from Hudson, such as claps and those deep 808’s.\n\nWhile most people know Hudmo for his unique trunk-rattling bangers as a solo producer or his unique trunk-destroying bangers as a part of TNGHT, he’s actually got a softer side, as can be heard on tracks like “The One” from Cruel Summer and more recently Drake’s “Connect.”\n\nAs of late, Kanye has been working with producers more akin to making trap music, such as Hudmo and Travis Scott, no doubt to help get those hard hitting drum sounds. But, he comes back over those drums and does put his Kanye touch on them, making a beat that is truly special.", "annotation_id": "2232238", "url": "https://genius.com/2232238", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold On", "status": "done", "targets": 29, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Pusha-t-hold-on-lyrics", "genius_title": "Hold On", "genius_id": 218132, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold You Down (Remix)", "line": "There goes my baby", "vault_lines": ["There goes my baby"], "meaning": "This is a reference to Usher’s song of the same name. And they say DJ Khaled has a self-promotion problem. Of course Charlie Wilson has an oldie’s song of the same name, but it is a sad song, and is probably not the intended reference here.\n\nRegardless, usher is saying that he wants to brag about his new girl.", "annotation_id": "6317496", "url": "https://genius.com/6317496", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold You Down (Remix)", "line": "So I found her, fly shit to jump up out her", "vault_lines": ["So I found her, fly shit to jump up out her"], "meaning": "Another transcription error here. He clearly says “fly shit TO jump up out her.” This gives more meaning to the sentence to. This would mean that he is saying fly shit ( presumably designer line clothing) is going to look like it’s jumping out of her her, because he buys her so much. He treats his rider right.", "annotation_id": "6317462", "url": "https://genius.com/6317462", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold You Down (Remix)", "line": "We the best Yeah, we the best at holding it down", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, we the best at holding it down"], "meaning": "“We the Best Music Group” is DJ Khaled’s record label. Similar to most other rappers with a group or a label, he makes a point of sprinkling references to it throughout his songs.\n\nA prime example of this would be Lil Wayne’s “young moolah baby” screeches.", "annotation_id": "4732826", "url": "https://genius.com/4732826", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold You Down (Remix)", "line": "See we be Bonnie and Clyde", "vault_lines": ["See we be Bonnie and Clyde"], "meaning": "Bonnie and Clyde were an American couple responsible for dozens of robberies, murders, and assaults. They are used in hip-hop as a symbol of real gangsters working together, as well as a tight relationship.\n\nThey have been referenced in many rap songs, such as “Me and my Girlfriend,” “97' Bonnie and Clyde,” and “‘03 Bonnie and Clyde.”\n\nEminem and Jay Z (both contributing with songs above) have also referenced them multiple other works.", "annotation_id": "4729241", "url": "https://genius.com/4729241", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold You Down (Remix)", "line": "Boy, baller hood billionaire", "vault_lines": ["boy, baller hood billionaire"], "meaning": "He is a hood billionaire, meaning he started from the bottom and in a rough place, but he is now successful.\n\nAlso notice the alliteration used between “Black bottle, boy, baller…billionaire.”", "annotation_id": "4729224", "url": "https://genius.com/4729224", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold You Down (Remix)", "line": "Black bottle,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Rick Ross has been trying to start Luc Belaire rosé as a new trendy drink through his music. It is not hard to notice if you look at pictures the rapper posts, such as these:\n\n\n\n\n\nRick Ross is not the first hip hop artist to try and endorse drinks, as evident with Jay Z and Ace of Spades.", "annotation_id": "4729207", "url": "https://genius.com/4729207", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hold You Down (Remix)", "line": "I have been through hell, had ups and downs", "vault_lines": ["I have been through hell, had ups and downs"], "meaning": "During the beginning of his career, Ace Hood had been through personal, professional, and financial issues. In a complex magazine interview, he describes his past issues regarding getting evicted:\n“My family going through things, dealing with eviction notices. I got an eviction notice at my condo. My girl called me and she was upset telling me what happened. I was trying to work it out with the landlord to get an extension and pay everything off.”", "annotation_id": "4341903", "url": "https://genius.com/4341903", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
