{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustle hard french remix", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dada-usa-hustle-hard-french-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "Hustle hard french remix", "genius_id": 50201, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "Magnum cost me (Twenty-two)", "vault_lines": ["Don't tote no (Twenty-twos), Magnum cost me (Twenty-two)"], "meaning": "Rick’s use of the number 22 is a double entendre. 21 is the minimum drinking age in the USA and by metaphorically associating himself to the age of 22 instead, Rick is asserting that he has more than just a newfound legal accessibility to alcohol.\n\nP.S When this song was published, Rick himself was not 22 but 30 years of age.\n\n.22 is also the most popular rifle calibre and carries connotations of toughness, dominance,  freedom and autonomy. Rick’s pairing suggests that these attributes also also projected onto him.  A variant of the Magnum pistol also comes chambered in .22.", "annotation_id": "31135590", "url": "https://genius.com/31135590", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "We never steal cars, but we deal hard", "vault_lines": ["We never steal cars, but we deal hard"], "meaning": "Instead of stealing cars, which Ross considers too petty to do, probably due to the low amount of money involved in selling a stolen car, his crew instead deals crack cocaine- “hard” which allows them to make more money.", "annotation_id": "22555166", "url": "https://genius.com/22555166", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "My roof back (Roof back), my money right (My money right)", "vault_lines": ["My roof back (Roof back), my money right (My money right)"], "meaning": "Rick has lowered the roof in his convertible car.  In doing this he is showing off his extreme wealth- not many people get to drive convertibles at an age like his.", "annotation_id": "22555151", "url": "https://genius.com/22555151", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "Who the fuck you think you fuckin’ with?", "vault_lines": ["Who the fuck you think you fuckin' with?"], "meaning": "This line is a nod to Denzel Washington’s epic rant toward the end of Training Day, where (among other things) he yells at the crowd, “Who the fuck you think you fuckin' with?”", "annotation_id": "21995265", "url": "https://genius.com/21995265", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "Jose Canseco just snitchin’ because he’s finished", "vault_lines": ["Jose Canseco just snitchin' because he's finished"], "meaning": "Jose Canseco released a tell-all book about steroids.", "annotation_id": "14644492", "url": "https://genius.com/14644492", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "Seven-forty-five, white-on-white, that’s fuckin’ Ross", "vault_lines": ["Seven-forty-five, white-on-white, that's fuckin' Ross"], "meaning": "BMW 745 is a luxurious car.\n\nWhite is also a very rare color for expensive cars to come in, because Ross drives such a noticeable car he is easily identifiable  anytime he is driving by.", "annotation_id": "12727522", "url": "https://genius.com/12727522", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "They flyin’ over—Pacific, to be specific", "vault_lines": ["They flyin' over—Pacific, to be specific"], "meaning": "His drugs (birds) are flying over the Pacific ocean, probably to Southeast Asia. Note the progression from the last verse, where they were flying over the Atlantic.\n\nAlternatively, if it really were literal chickens, you would typically import bred roosters from regions where cockfighting is popular, like Southeast Asia.", "annotation_id": "29407", "url": "https://genius.com/29407", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "I touch work, like I’m Convertible Burt (Burt)", "vault_lines": ["I touch work, like I'm Convertible Burt (Burt)"], "meaning": "Rozay sold drugs like Richard “Convertible Burt” Simmons, who was the mentor of Boobie Williams (who was Ross' mentor).\n\n\n\nThis is Convertible Burt, along with Don King & Mike Tyson.", "annotation_id": "28715", "url": "https://genius.com/28715", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "Lil’ mama super thick (Thick), she say she (Twenty-two)", "vault_lines": ["Lil' mama super thick (Thick), she say she (Twenty-two)"], "meaning": "Continuing with the theme of rhyming “22” with “22”, Ross denotes that his female friend is 22 years of age.\n\nThese “22” lines were used as criticism against Ross, because of it’s extreme simplicity.", "annotation_id": "28481", "url": "https://genius.com/28481", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "Triple C’s, you know it’s fact, we holdin’ sacks", "vault_lines": ["Triple C's, you know it's fact, we holdin' sacks"], "meaning": "Also known as the Carol City Cartel, Triple C’s is a group founded by Ross", "annotation_id": "28480", "url": "https://genius.com/28480", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "I feed ’em steroids to strengthen up all my chickens (My chickens)", "vault_lines": ["I feed 'em steroids to strengthen up all my chickens (My chickens)"], "meaning": "A play on illegal cockfights, somewhat common in South Florida (owners use steroids to give their birds an advantage)\n\n\n\nRozay uses “chicken” to mean a kilogram of cocaine, which he cuts with other substances to swell up the quantity.\n\nOr so they can be extra juicy when he serves them at Wing Stop", "annotation_id": "28391", "url": "https://genius.com/28391", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "Whip it real hard— whip it— whip it real hard", "vault_lines": ["Whip it real hard— whip it— whip it real hard"], "meaning": "To cook cocaine into crack, you have to whip it in the kitchen with baking soda before you start cooking\nThere is a double meaning to this line: he whips the coke up with baking soda to make crack. He’ll make more money this way so he’s whipping the coke in the sense he’s making it work harder like whipping a horse.\nIf you count the cars in the previous line as a pun with “horsepower” moving weight faster like a racetrack horse.", "annotation_id": "28347", "url": "https://genius.com/28347", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "Major league—who catchin’? Because I’m pitchin’ (I’m pitchin’)", "vault_lines": ["Major league—who catchin'? Because I'm pitchin' (I'm pitchin')"], "meaning": "Besides the baseball context, “pitch” can also refer to selling drugs (or hyping them up, as in a “sales pitch”)\n\n“Pitching” can also refer to being the “top” in a sexual relationship, which makes this line a little bit “umm..”", "annotation_id": "28334", "url": "https://genius.com/28334", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "We in Room (Two-twenty-two)", "vault_lines": ["She seen 'em (Twenty-twos), we in Room (Two-twenty-two)"], "meaning": "Using Travelocity™, Ross bagged a 2nd-floor hotel room (with beach view!) for only $199!\n\nPerhaps he chose room 222 because he dreams of being a special guest star on the ‘70s comedy-drama Room 222\n\n\n\n(One kilogram, incidentally, is equal to 2.20462 pounds)", "annotation_id": "28331", "url": "https://genius.com/28331", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "Lot of drug-dealin’ ‘round me goin’ down in Dade County (Woah)", "vault_lines": ["Lot of drug-dealin' 'round me goin' down in Dade County (Woah)"], "meaning": "Miami is notorious for it’s cocaine dealing, hence why the city is nicknamed M.I.YAYO, yayo being a Spanish slang word for cocaine.\n\nThe Boobie Boys were known to run Carol City’s drug scene. Ross was a member of the Boobie Boys and was supposedly Boobie Williams' protege.", "annotation_id": "25338", "url": "https://genius.com/25338", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "In the (M-I-yayo)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "MIA (short for Miami) + Yayo (Spanish slang for “cocaine”) = “M.I.YAYO”", "annotation_id": "24861", "url": "https://genius.com/24861", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "When they snatched Black, I cried for a hundred nights He got a hundred bodies, servin’ a hundred lifes", "vault_lines": ["When they snatched Black, I cried for a hundred nights", "He got a hundred bodies, servin' a hundred lifes"], "meaning": "Kenneth “Black/Boobie” Williams was the mastermind behind The Boobie Boys, a murderous drug gang that supplied Florida with cocaine in the ‘90s\n\nThe Boobie Boys got raided not too long ago and Kenny “B” is now serving 3 life sentences (not a hundred – but still a lot!) Black was supposedly Rick Ross' rap/cocaine-dealing mentor. Ross has said,\n\nTo me, Kenneth “Boobie” Williams is to the Carol City Cartel as Larry Hoover was to the Gangster Disciples in Chicago.", "annotation_id": "24858", "url": "https://genius.com/24858", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "I know Pablo (Pablo), Noriega (Noriega) The real Noriega—he owe me a hundred favors (Church)", "vault_lines": ["I know Pablo (Pablo), Noriega (Noriega)", "The real Noriega—he owe me a hundred favors (Church)"], "meaning": "Ricky’s not just friends with the rapper Noreaga (aka N.O.R.E.)..\n\n..he’s friends with the REAL Noriega – Manuel Noriega, dictator of Panama / huge coke kingpin\n\nWhen Rick Ross was asked if he knew Manuel ‘Manolo’ Noriega he stated the following:\n\nNoriega owe a hundred favors, you know what I’m sayin’? It’s just like, you know, I kick it with [Pablo] Escobar nephew. He live down here. That’s my nigga, you know what I’m sayin’? So I just meant like, you know, real ties with real niggas. That’s what that meant. I don’t know Noriega personally, but I know niggas who have met Noriega. I know niggas who was in federal prison two, three cells down from Noriega. You know what I’m sayin’? And when I talk to them, I let ‘em know you know that’s something I meant to ‘em in the movies. I’m into shit like that. So I’m gonna stand to that. That’s all that means", "annotation_id": "24856", "url": "https://genius.com/24856", "state": "accepted", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Hustlin’", "line": "I’m into distribution, I’m like Atlantic", "vault_lines": ["I'm into distribution, I'm like Atlantic"], "meaning": "Atlantic Recording Group (ARG) formerly “Atlantic Records” is a label that distributes records on a wholesale level, rather than your local record store. Rick Ross does the same with cocaine; he’s a wholesale player rather than a lowly corner salesman.\n\nHe could also mean by saying “Atlantic” that his drug game expands to outside of the US and overseas.", "annotation_id": "24855", "url": "https://genius.com/24855", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
