{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "That lean got us dozing, we forget that we the chosen", "vault_lines": ["That lean got us dozing, we forget that we the chosen"], "meaning": "Kanye refers to a proven theory that states the CIA and FBI traffic drugs to weaken the black community.\n\n\n\nYe also refers to other conspiracy theories, which believes blacks are the chosen people or even the lost tribe of Israel", "annotation_id": "34602309", "url": "https://genius.com/34602309", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "Ran up outta pills, rob that CVS", "vault_lines": ["Ran up outta pills, rob that CVS"], "meaning": "Kanye describes a situation where a desperate drug addict has run out of their supply of pills and their plan is to rob a CVS pharmacy to get more.\n\nDriven by drug addiction, a likely broke addict is willing to resort to crimes to fuel their addiction.", "annotation_id": "34602275", "url": "https://genius.com/34602275", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "Niggas getting bust over In God We Trust", "vault_lines": ["Niggas getting bust over In God We Trust"], "meaning": "“In God We Trust” is the national motto of the United States and is featured on every currency of the U.S.\n\n“Getting bust\" means being arrested or caught. So this line means people get arrested or punished over petty crimes robbing a CVS\n\nThis line highlights the lengths people go to for financial gain. Especially when paired with the line before, “rob that CVS”, where people will risk going to jail over materialistic things like store robbery", "annotation_id": "34602238", "url": "https://genius.com/34602238", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "All your codes get decoded, know the niggas you quoting", "vault_lines": ["All your codes get decoded, know the niggas you quoting"], "meaning": "A reference to JAY-Z’s 2011 memoir Decoded.\n\nIn it, Hov annotates (or decodes) lyrics to some of his most popular songs.", "annotation_id": "21053420", "url": "https://genius.com/21053420", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "God Forgives and I Don’t, pussy niggas on notice", "vault_lines": ["God Forgives and I Don’t, pussy niggas on notice"], "meaning": "This is in reference to Ross' then upcoming studio album, “God Forgives, I Don’t”, that would release about a month after this song was released.", "annotation_id": "20964616", "url": "https://genius.com/20964616", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "I’m at the fight, ringside, right next to promoters", "vault_lines": ["I'm at the fight, ringside, right next to promoters"], "meaning": "Rozay is watching a ring side fight next to big money businesses cause he is ballin'.\n\nEditors note:\n\nThis also ties into the Frank Lucas reference in the line above.\n\nIn the movie American Gangster, a depiction of Frank Lucas’s life, Lucas goes to a major boxing match with his wife, wearing the expensive fur coat and fur hat, something he doesn’t like to do because it draws attention to himself.\n\nAnyway, at the fight he gets some really close seats, right next to the stage practically.\n\nThis ends up marking the downfall of Frank Lucas, but that isn’t relevant to the explanation", "annotation_id": "872760", "url": "https://genius.com/872760", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "If we believe enough, will we ever get to know Him?", "vault_lines": ["If we believe enough, will we ever get to know Him?"], "meaning": "Kanye is asking if we simply believe in God, without living the necessary lifestyle, will that belief be able to carry us to Heaven? In he line before, he says, “We believe in God but do God believe in us?” This leads one to believe that he was asking a very complex question. We believe and faith in God, but we are not always living by what he teaches. That being said, does He (God) believe in us and our ways of life? Even though we may be of strong belief and faith, will that be enough to get us to Heaven? In the next line he says, “We forget that we the chosen.” This is a biblical reference. Kanye is challenging the lifestyles of people who claim to have a faith and belief in God but do not live accordingly. Furthermore, Kanye’s contributions to the song generate a certain recurring theme. I think it discusses specific struggles of life and how those different things can have an impact on our belief and our actions. And in these three lines Kanye may be asking another complex question: If we have belief in God, a real faith, will He see that as enough? Or will He judge us because we didn’t live up to “standards” because of our conditions in life? Does He see our pain?", "annotation_id": "872721", "url": "https://genius.com/872721", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "Frank Lucas fur,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Frank Lucas was known for wearing elaborate fur coats.\n\n\n\nThis refers to the fur outfit Frank Lucas’s wife bought him to wear to the “Fight of the Century” (the legendary fight between Muhammed Ali and Joe Frazier). Usually, Lucas dressed with a modest conservatism both as a sign of strength and to avoid attracting the attention of the law. But on that ostentatious night out he wore the outfit, law be damned.", "annotation_id": "872701", "url": "https://genius.com/872701", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "Every year gon’ be our year, every year gon’ be my year", "vault_lines": ["Every year gon' be our year, every year gon' be my year"], "meaning": "2010 was Kanye’s year. (MBDTF)\n2011 was Kanye’s year. (Watch The Throne)\n2012 will be Kanye’s + G.O.O.D.’s year. (Cruel Summer)\n2013… you already know. (Yeezus)", "annotation_id": "872485", "url": "https://genius.com/872485", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "G5,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Gulfstream V, A type of Private Jet. Rick Ross lives a luxury life\n\n\n\nG5 is also a popular stripclub in Miami. That’s why he says “make it rain, I can change the weather” right after.", "annotation_id": "872433", "url": "https://genius.com/872433", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "We believe in God, but do God believe in us?", "vault_lines": ["We believe in God, but do God believe in us?"], "meaning": "Most religions believe in a God or superior being that created this world and is watching down on us. Kanye questions whether or not possibly has faith in humanity, and at the same time questions his existence. How can he believe if he’s not there?\nSimilarly, can God believe that we’re trying to “get our lives together” when so many do the opposite?", "annotation_id": "872431", "url": "https://genius.com/872431", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "One, two, them boys coming for you Three, four, better lock your door Five, six, hide your bitch", "vault_lines": ["Five, six, hide your bitch"], "meaning": "Based off a famous nursery rhyme sang in A Nightmare On Elm Street about the serial killer Freddy coming for you.\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TcROvivYkw4\n\nIf Kanye’s competition falls asleep and slacks off, he will kill them and their career.\n\nYeezy used a VERY similar hook in his song “Livin' In A Movie”", "annotation_id": "872429", "url": "https://genius.com/872429", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "The block is at war, post traumatic stress", "vault_lines": ["The block is at war, post traumatic stress"], "meaning": "Post traumatic stress disorder is something you get after a horrible event, such as going to war or seeing your best friend get murdered. Kanye’s hometown of Chicago has a lot of murders going on, like a war on the streets. Kanye is still feeling the pain even after leaving Chicago.\n\nAlso may refer to his raps in Murder for Excellence:\n\n41 souls murdered in fifty hours", "annotation_id": "872425", "url": "https://genius.com/872425", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "line": "I wish you would nigga, I wish you would nigga I wish you would nigga, I wish you would nigga", "vault_lines": ["I wish you would nigga, I wish you would nigga"], "meaning": "The phrase “I wish you would” is used when you want to provoke someone into doing something so you can react. Kanye is daring them to test his team so he can prove them wrong. A similar line was used by Lil Wayne in “It’s Good”", "annotation_id": "872419", "url": "https://genius.com/872419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wish You Would", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dj-khaled-i-wish-you-would-lyrics", "genius_title": "I Wish You Would", "genius_id": 79532, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "You wanna do me, then you go and tell the people Knock me down, temp drops as the sun rise", "vault_lines": ["You wanna do me, then you go and tell the people", "Knock me down, temp drops as the sun rise"], "meaning": "These lines, like most lines in this song, probably refers to the Zimmerman-Trayvon incident.\n\nZimmerman slayed Trayvon Martin and then told “the people” it was in self defense. Perhaps Ross is insinuating that Zimmerman wanted to kill Martin, but acted as if that wasn’t his intent. “The people” refers simultaneously to the jurors and the state of Florida, as criminal prosecutions are brought by the state, known as “The People”.\n\nThe second part of the line again refers to Zimmerman, as he knocked Trayvon down first. Many Trayvon defenders believe that Zimmerman was the first aggressor.\n\nRoss uses a metaphor to compare Trayvon to the sun – perhaps he is implying that Trayvon’s soul rose as he died. Use of the homophone “sun” anticipates the of the use of the word “son” in the next line, making Trayvon a symbol for all young black men, including Ross’s son. The case hit home for many black families, as they believed it could’ve been their son who could’ve been killed, even President Obama once said “If I had a son, he’d look like Trayvon”.\n\nVery clever wordplay in these two lines by Ross.", "annotation_id": "2238362", "url": "https://genius.com/2238362", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "Business, big business Maybach business", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "There’s business, and then there is Maybach Business. All members of MMG make power moves.\n\n\nRick Ross is the owner of five Wingstop restaurants, is most likely sponsored by Reebok, and has endoresment deals with both Ciroc and Belaire Rose.\nMeek Mill has endorsement deals with Skull Candy, Monster, and PUMA.\nStalley has an endorsement deal with Nike.\nLike his cohort Meek, Rockie Fresh has an endorsement deal with PUMA.\n\n\n\nNobody else makes moves like MMG.", "annotation_id": "2159700", "url": "https://genius.com/2159700", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "Got on my hoodie and my motherfucking Desert Eagle", "vault_lines": ["Got on my hoodie and my motherfucking Desert Eagle"], "meaning": "Ross is wearing a Trayvon Martin-style hoodie in support of Trayvon (who was an inspiration for this song).\n\n\n\nA lot of other celebrities supported Trayvon Martin’s case by wearing a hoodie like Martin did, including Diddy, Carmelo Anthony and the whole Miami Heat team.\n\nWhile Trayvon was wearing a hoodie and was unarmed when George Zimmerman shot him to death, Rozay is prepared for creepy ass crackers and therefore carries a Desert Eagle pistol with him.", "annotation_id": "2070150", "url": "https://genius.com/2070150", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "I wanna shine just like the summertime", "vault_lines": ["I wanna shine just like the summertime"], "meaning": "Rick compares the way he wants to shine and prosper in the rap game (and life), to the way the sun shines during the summer, because the sun shines the brightest during the summertime. Ross has used this simile before.", "annotation_id": "2060245", "url": "https://genius.com/2060245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "Attempted murder on his life in that 360i", "vault_lines": ["Attempted murder on his life in that 360i"], "meaning": "This is probably referencing a BMW e60 since a BMW was used in the attempted drive-by on Rick Ross on January 28th 2013. Interestingly enough two rap legends died in this fashion.", "annotation_id": "2046628", "url": "https://genius.com/2046628", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "Dreams come true, they dream of killing me", "vault_lines": ["Dreams come true, they dream of killing me"], "meaning": "Ross proved that dreams can come true (since he made it in the rap game), but the same thing isn’t true for his enemies. They dream of killing him, but so far haven’t succeeded.\n\nYou’d think he isn’t that hard to miss, but maybe we’re all just underestimating Bawse", "annotation_id": "2045117", "url": "https://genius.com/2045117", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "All this fame and fortune leave you traumatized", "vault_lines": ["All this fame and fortune leave you traumatized"], "meaning": "Rozay denotes that money and fame can change a person that living a life of fame isn’t for everybody, because not everybody can live in the limelight.\n\nAs he goes on to denote later in the song: more money, more problems. You gotta have strong shoulders when you’re constantly in the spotlight, or you’ll end up like Lindsay Lohan. Success is heavy burden.\n\nNot long after this song was released, Miami Heat champion LeBron James explained the pressure of fame gets to him sometimes. On a Facebook post, James said:\n\nMan it’s hard to go out and have a good time these days! It’s cool though, it’s part of my life. Wouldn’t change anything(just saying). Been in the spotlight since I was 15. Sometimes u just wanna STOP!! But I refuse cause I have a commitment to the youth to inspire them!! That will keep me pushing forward and focused alone.", "annotation_id": "2043910", "url": "https://genius.com/2043910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "They say them folk gave him a heap of time", "vault_lines": ["They say them folk gave him a heap of time"], "meaning": "One of Ross' friends got a long sentence due to his criminal activities.\n\nThis could be a reference to Kenneth “Boobie” Williams, Ross' friend and mentor, who caught 100 life sentences.", "annotation_id": "2043898", "url": "https://genius.com/2043898", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "(Okay, they’re having trouble hearing you, so take your time) Creepy-ass cracka And then he said [bleep] (And he’s still following him?) Yes...", "vault_lines": ["And then he said [bleep]"], "meaning": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x78artldN2Y\n\nRachel Jeantel testified that Trayvon Martin told her that a “creepy ass cracka” (otherwise known as George Zimmerman) was following him. This signified a seminal moment in the trial as the defense was able to bring up the point that it wasn’t Zimmerman who first brought race into the trial, but it was, in fact, Trayvon Martin. This moment also seemed to exemplify a divide between Black and White America. Ms. Jeantel herself explained that in her understanding of the slang “creepy ass cracka” was not a racial slur. A majority of African Americans seemed to understand her statements while many Caucasian Americans wholeheartedly disagreed with the idea that cracka was not a racial slur. This prompted CNN to run a soon to be infamous debate on which racial epithet was worse.\n\nIn the context of this outro these statements seem to exemplify a divide between White America and Rick Ross himself. They will never be able to understand his life in this way. They will never be able to wrap their heads around the fact that he keeps two pistols in the car, that he still supports his brother in prison, and they will never see his point of view on the Trayvon situation. It can also represent the media’s seemingly tireless pursuit of slandering Ross' name (whether it be because of the date rape line or many other controversies involving him). In this way, by the way the media portrays him or the ways White America might see him, Rick Ross compares himself to Trayvon. A misunderstood African American who has already been found guilty.", "annotation_id": "2043252", "url": "https://genius.com/2043252", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "Now I’m being followed by some creepy-ass cracker Now I’m being followed by some creepy-ass cracker Stand your ground, stand your ground Stand your ground, you gotta stand your ground", "vault_lines": ["Now I'm being followed by some creepy-ass cracker", "Stand your ground, stand your ground", "Stand your ground, you gotta stand your ground"], "meaning": "A reference to the Trayvon Martin shooting case.\n\nTrayvon was followed by George Zimmerman, which lead to some sort of stand off, culminating in Zimmerman shooting Trayvon.  The death of an unarmed seventeen year old black male resulted in public outcries for justice.\n\nZimmerman was eventually acquitted of all charges, because of Florida’s Stand Your Ground Law.\n\nThe “creepy-ass cracker” lines refer to Rachel Jeantel’s testimony, which is included in this song’s outro.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/rickyrozay/statuses/356234768020549632", "annotation_id": "2042648", "url": "https://genius.com/2042648", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "Nappy-headed nigga, pocket full of crack rock Nineteen, living like a fucking rap star", "vault_lines": ["Nappy-headed nigga, pocket full of crack rock", "Nineteen, living like a fucking rap star"], "meaning": "Ross paints the picture of young ninteen year old drug dealer, and perhaps this is the same nineteen year old Ross referenced on “Pirates”.\n\nThe listener can assume the young drug dealer is black because nappy hair (curled unaltered hair) is found on people of African descent.\n\nThe kid has a lot of crack, and thus he’s able to live like a rap superstar.", "annotation_id": "2042629", "url": "https://genius.com/2042629", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "My brother caught a L and I still write him every month", "vault_lines": ["My brother caught a L and I still write him every month"], "meaning": "Rick Ross has no biological brothers, so one of his close friends was sentenced to prison time, but he still keeps in contact with him.\n\nThis is probably a reference to his Haitian Mafia Brother Kano, who was down with Ross since day 1. Gunplay also shouts him out in his song Real Niggas.", "annotation_id": "2042607", "url": "https://genius.com/2042607", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "I could see a hustler in my son’s eyes", "vault_lines": ["I could see a hustler in my son's eyes"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is a family man, he recently took his son to a Heat game, where they sat courtside.\n\nLately, it seems Ross has been grooming his son to be the Heir to the Maybach Empire.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/rickyrozay/status/352832486373482496", "annotation_id": "2042601", "url": "https://genius.com/2042601", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I Wonder Why", "line": "[Outro: Rachel Jeantel]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A friend of Trayvon Martin, Jeantel testified that she was on the phone with Martin during the incident and that they were on the phone together while Martin was at the 7-Eleven. This outro is Jeantel’s actual testimony from the Zimmerman trial.", "annotation_id": "2042599", "url": "https://genius.com/2042599", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
