{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "But it’s okay, Gotham City needs savin’", "vault_lines": ["But it's okay, Gotham City needs savin'"], "meaning": "Reference to Batman’s hometown, the fictional city has an impressive roster of villains that Batman has to tangle with.", "annotation_id": "29070120", "url": "https://genius.com/29070120", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "Get a B in blue, that’s the Jay I’m on", "vault_lines": ["Get a B in blue, that's the Jay I'm on"], "meaning": "What Fab is saying is he wants to get a billion $ like Jay Z. A clever word play as Jay Z first born child is Blue Ivy", "annotation_id": "16260432", "url": "https://genius.com/16260432", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "Two years and two billion dollar exits", "vault_lines": ["Two years and two billion dollar exits"], "meaning": "“Exits” describe how much a company was sold on for.\n\nChamillionaire is a recognised tech investor, most notably investing in Lyft, Ring, Maker Studios (sold for $700k) and Cruise (sold for $1bn)", "annotation_id": "15339774", "url": "https://genius.com/15339774", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "Runnin’ wild in the city, no direction", "vault_lines": ["Runnin' wild in the city, no direction"], "meaning": "In this line, runnin' wild\" has a dual meaning. It can mean spazzing out/going out of control, usually in a partying sense. In addition, he is using the term “run” to also refer to his prolonged success in the rap game. So T.I. takes advantage of these dual meanings, implying that he is partying like crazy in his city, and also that no one other than himself is responsible for his successful “run” in hip hop, as no one other than himself is “directing” it.", "annotation_id": "14128838", "url": "https://genius.com/14128838", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "And every day I’m on And if I wasn’t, then why would I say I’m on?", "vault_lines": ["And every day I'm on", "And if I wasn't, then why would I say I'm on?"], "meaning": "Does the start of this verse sound familiar? Fab utilizes the same rhyme structure as Eminem’s, “The Way I Am”. More recently, Royce da 5’9 and 2 Chainz paid homage to Em in the same way, on the track, “Flirt”. Interestingly, Royce has a verse on this track too.", "annotation_id": "14121984", "url": "https://genius.com/14121984", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "Your worst nightmare, Freddy Krueger, Wes Craven", "vault_lines": ["Your worst nightmare, Freddy Krueger, Wes Craven"], "meaning": "Freddy Krueger is a character created by film maker, Wes Craven, in the popular Nightmare on Elm Street series. Chamillionaire throws in some word play by comparing himself to Krueger,  who in Nightmare on Elm Street attacks people in their dreams, quite literally making him “your worst nightmare.”", "annotation_id": "13951891", "url": "https://genius.com/13951891", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "Get an M and get low, that’s the Dre I’m on", "vault_lines": ["Get an M and get low, that's the Dre I'm on"], "meaning": "Dre found “M” (Eminem) in the slums of 8 Mile, Detroit. Eminem soon blew up, and Dre who “got down” with Em, was able to “get low” and move away from his rapping career and stay under the radar as a rapper and explore more his producing and entrepreneurial side (see Beats by Dre®).", "annotation_id": "12447541", "url": "https://genius.com/12447541", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "It ain’t easy bein’ on for 30 years To see the glitter and glamour but not the blood, sweat and tears I’m an old ass youngsta, bruh, I’m a vet Sick Wid It Records, sellin’ cassettes before the internet", "vault_lines": ["It ain't easy bein' on for 30 years", "To see the glitter and glamour but not the blood, sweat and tears", "I'm an old ass youngsta, bruh, I'm a vet", "Sick Wid It Records, sellin' cassettes before the internet"], "meaning": "E-40’s musical career began in 1988 as a founder of Sick Wid' It Records. Although versions of the modern day internet existed back then, it was not released on a widespread level until the 1990s, where it then gained popularity.\n\n\n\n He first gained traction as a member of the group The Click.  Despite achieving mainstream popularity in the Bay Area in 1993 with the classic “Captain Save A Hoe”, E-40 did not receive widespread attention until 2006, when he was featured on the Lil Jon single “Snap Yo Fingers”.\n\n\n\nAt 49, E-40 does not seem to back down despite being one of oldest rappers which are relevant, which describes his “youngsta” persona. He still releases content, and has made several investments, including a line of malt liquor.", "annotation_id": "12352380", "url": "https://genius.com/12352380", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "Yeah, all gas, fast livin’ like somethin’ was speedin’ Ashy to classy now I bless ‘em like someone who sneezin’ I’m only here to give ‘em pressure, bitch, picture me squeezin’ Against the world like I was Pac, wasn’t nobody believin’", "vault_lines": ["Against the world like I was Pac, wasn't nobody believin'"], "meaning": "Trae reflects on his earlier years, when he was trying to prove himself in the rap game and move out of the streets of Houston. Trae started his career with a relatively little fan base.\n\nTrae has been involved in controversy, notably with Mike Jones at the 2008 Ozone Awards, and  Houston radio station KBXX following Trae Day 2009, painting the rapper in a negative light.  Trae had to maul through all of the internal and external obstacles to make it out from the negativity and make himself a role model in his city\n\n\n\nMe Against the World is a song by 2Pac , which highlights a similar theme of being placed down and being treated unfairly amongst the public despite attempting to speak his mind.", "annotation_id": "12351765", "url": "https://genius.com/12351765", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "Ayy Trae, let The Counselor speak E-40! The best that ever did it and got away with it Let The Counselor speak", "vault_lines": ["Ayy Trae, let The Counselor speak", "E-40! The best that ever did it and got away with it", "Let The Counselor speak"], "meaning": "E-40’s self-applied title on Twitter and Instagram is “E-40 The Counselor”.  For many years, 40 has helped up and coming artists from the Bay Area and beyond, whether it’s signing to his record label, putting them on a track or just spitting some game.", "annotation_id": "12260829", "url": "https://genius.com/12260829", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "They’ll fight back but I’ma shock ’em like Raiden", "vault_lines": ["They'll fight back but I'ma shock 'em like Raiden"], "meaning": "Raiden is one of the original characters in the ultra-violent, fighting video game series, Mortal Kombat.  Modeled after the character “Lightning” from the 1986 John Carpenter movie Big Trouble in Little China, Raiden literally shocks his opponents with the element of lightning, making him one of the most powerful characters in the long-running game franchise.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/nkSZY9cUv5s?t=8", "annotation_id": "12255919", "url": "https://genius.com/12255919", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "And I would like to thank the dead presidents For not livin’ forever-ever, forever-ever", "vault_lines": ["And I would like to thank the dead presidents", "For not livin' forever-ever, forever-ever"], "meaning": "Chamillionaire jokes that if the presidents featured on U.S. currency would have lived forever, he would not be as rich as he is right now.\n\nHis repetition of “forever-ever” is a classic interpolation of Andre 3000’s verse in Outkast’s Ms. Jackson, also used by Kanye West, Big K.R.I.T and Capital STEEZ.", "annotation_id": "12210081", "url": "https://genius.com/12210081", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "Shit (Oh-woah, woah) They say three times the charm, huh? (No-woah) Hehe, I got ya", "vault_lines": ["Hehe, I got ya"], "meaning": "This song marks the third of the series of posse cut singles named “I’m On” released by Trae tha Truth. He uses the “three time’s a charm” phrase to tell the listener that this song will top all the other singles he’s released before.\n\nThe original, “I’m On”, released in 2011, had Trae’s verse go up against five other verses from five other rappers, including Lupe Fiasco, Big Boi and Wiz Khalifa. The follow up, “I’m On 2.0”, released in 2012, had him up the ante further with nine new rappers, including J Cole, Kendrick Lamar and Big K.R.I.T..\n\nThis third installment now features twelve new rappers, along with D.R.A.M and Gary Clarke Jr. handling the chorus, and with verses from old favourites like T.I., Chamillionare and E-40, Trae hopes that this third offering will be the biggest and best of them all.", "annotation_id": "12210028", "url": "https://genius.com/12210028", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "They tried to turn us into the villains like Colin Kaepernick", "vault_lines": ["They tried to turn us into the villains like Colin Kaepernick"], "meaning": "In the United States, people are told from a young age to push for what you believe in and use the freedom of speech and expression to your advantage.\n\nThis was however flipped as ex-San Francisco 49ers quarterback Colin Kaepernick decided to kneel during the National Anthem to express his displeasure with the government.\n\nMany citizens took it as an offense to the people that fought for him to have that right, making him out to be a villain. He did, however, incite people with similar beliefs to join him.", "annotation_id": "12207666", "url": "https://genius.com/12207666", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "The weather’s gettin’ hot, Eazy Season approachin’", "vault_lines": ["The weather's gettin' hot, Eazy Season approachin'"], "meaning": "G-Eazy has labelled the time leading up to his new project, “Eazy Season.”\n\nhttps://twitter.com/G_Eazy/status/874924278604550144\n\nSo far in 2017, he released 5 songs in the span of 1 week, including three on his birthday.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/G_Eazy/status/875056762235244544\n\nAfter all of this, G announced his third studio album The Beautiful & Damned is due to drop this fall. The summer of 2017 isn’t just one of the hottest summers ever because of global warming, since Gerald plans to drop some fire tracks.", "annotation_id": "12207622", "url": "https://genius.com/12207622", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "line": "Was a stunna ’til they froze all the assets", "vault_lines": ["Was a stunna 'til they froze all the assets"], "meaning": "Reference to Birdman’s nickname ‘Stunna’, who was reportedly freezing a lot of Cash Money artists pay checks with Lil Wayne being the most known one, as the highly-anticipated Tha Carter V will not drop until Birdman pays him the money.\n\nStunna is also a term derived from the South, someone who drives nice cars and shows off their bling.", "annotation_id": "12199607", "url": "https://genius.com/12199607", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m On 3.0", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Trae-tha-truth-im-on-30-lyrics", "genius_title": "I’m On 3.0", "genius_id": 3127795, "source": "api"}
