{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "No, those ain’t Rolex diamonds, what you done to that? You fools fugazi", "vault_lines": ["No, those ain't Rolex diamonds, what you done to that?"], "meaning": "Rozay references two lines from JAY-Z’s classic track “Imaginary Player” from his 1997 sophomore album “In My Lifetime, Vol. 1”:\n\n\nThose ain’t Rolex diamonds, what the fuck you done to that?\n\nYou crazy, you fugazi, I’m loco with dough, papo\n\n\n\n\nRick Ross has collaborated with Jay on several tracks such as “Free Mason” and “Maybach Music”", "annotation_id": "37195561", "url": "https://genius.com/37195561", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "I’m so hood that Ludacris shoulda been on the original version But this is the remix,", "vault_lines": ["I'm so hood that Ludacris shoulda been on the original version", "But this is the remix,"], "meaning": "The original version of DJ Khaled’s song “I’m So Hood” features T-Pain, Trick Daddy, Rick Ross & Plies but Ludacris feels that he should’ve been on that version instead of this remix.", "annotation_id": "33023487", "url": "https://genius.com/33023487", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "Just call me the boss, always got what you needed", "vault_lines": ["Just call me the boss, always got what you needed"], "meaning": "“The Boss” is a frequent moniker Ross gives himself. A big part of Ross' rap persona is being a cocaine selling mafia affiliate (Although in his infamous beef with rival 50 Cent it was revealed he was a correctional officer, de-valuing his persona in the public’s eyes).\n\nThe moniker became further popularized in a song off Ross' 2008 album Trilla , “The Boss”:\n\nI’m on my job, ey and I ain’t gettin' off\nCause it’s just another day in the life of the goddamn boss\n\nIn 2015 rap-megastar Drake shouted out the name with a line off his song “No Tellin'”:\n\nIt’s our year (aw yeah), aw yeah\nI mean besides Ricky Ross, Aubrey the biggest boss here", "annotation_id": "15379721", "url": "https://genius.com/15379721", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "So hood like the Cadillac Ornament", "vault_lines": ["so hood like the Cadillac"], "meaning": "Big Boi is as “hood” or true to his roots as the emblem on a Cadillac.\n\nFirstly, many people in the hood drive Cadillacs including Big Boi and his partner, Andre 3000.\n\n\n\nSecondly, it’s a play on the literal meaning of the word “hood”. The ornament on a Cadillac is placed in front on of the car and on top of the hood.", "annotation_id": "2568403", "url": "https://genius.com/2568403", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "I gave you “We Takin’ Over”", "vault_lines": ["I gave you \"We Takin' Over\""], "meaning": "“We Takin' Over” is the third track on this album featuring Akon, T.I., Rick Ross, Fat Joe, Birdman and Lil Wayne; talk about classic.", "annotation_id": "2077077", "url": "https://genius.com/2077077", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "I should own a McDonald’s the way I bring the MACs out", "vault_lines": ["I should own a McDonald's the way I bring the MAC's out"], "meaning": "The Big Mac is a hamburger sold by McDonald’s, but the Mac he’s referring to is the gun (Mac 11).", "annotation_id": "2076938", "url": "https://genius.com/2076938", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "(I’m so hood!) Yeah, I wear my pants below my waist", "vault_lines": ["(I'm so hood!) Yeah, I wear my pants below my waist"], "meaning": "Sagging is wearing your pants or shorts below the waist, revealing much of the underwear. This was adopted from the United States prison system due to belts sometimes being prohibited to stop violent behavior.", "annotation_id": "2076775", "url": "https://genius.com/2076775", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "[Intro: DJ Khaled] DJ Khaled! We The Best Who? We, nigga! We The Best This the remix! The “I’m So Hood” remix! I’m makin’ mo-vies! “I’m So Hood” remix!", "vault_lines": ["\"I'm So Hood\" remix!"], "meaning": "Khaled screaming at the beginning of his songs is a true trademark. It has spawned the same question over and over again.\n\nBesides scream, what does DJ Khaled do?\n\n\n\nTyler, The Creator has even touched on this\n\nI’m harder than DJ Khaled playing the fucking quiet game\n\nIt’s not an official song unless you hear the screaming intro. Check out this compilation of Khaled screaming in the beginning of his songs.", "annotation_id": "2076743", "url": "https://genius.com/2076743", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "Mo’ money, mo’ problems,", "vault_lines": ["Mo' money, mo' problems,"], "meaning": "1997 hit by the Notorious B.I.G. “Money Mo Problems” was the second single from his album Life After Death.", "annotation_id": "1859643", "url": "https://genius.com/1859643", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "(Weezy!)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Weezy is one of Lil Wayne’s nickname, one that he used a lot more when this song was released (he tends to use Lil Tunechi a lot more now).", "annotation_id": "1341630", "url": "https://genius.com/1341630", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "I’m so Hollygrove, New Orleans (Lil Weezyana)", "vault_lines": ["I'm so Hollygrove, New Orleans (Lil Weezyana)"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne was born in Hollygrove, New Orleans on September 27th, 1982. He has represented his streets from day One!\n\nLil' Weezyana one of Lil Wayne’s many nicknames. It’s a play on the pronunciation of his home state, Louisiana.", "annotation_id": "1047681", "url": "https://genius.com/1047681", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "My fools from Haiti", "vault_lines": [", my fools from Haiti"], "meaning": "Little Haiti is a neighborhood in Miami, Florida. In the 1980s and 1990s, Little Haiti was one of the poorest areas in the city and was known for its crime and drug trade.", "annotation_id": "116543", "url": "https://genius.com/116543", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "Daddy Fat Saxxx", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A nicknam he picked up from from selling drugs. Having the “fattest sacks”  meant that he had the most drugs in his bags.\n\nHe even titled a song on his debut album “Daddy Fat Sax”.", "annotation_id": "47331", "url": "https://genius.com/47331", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "Since I heard Plies, I done bought the Phantom back", "vault_lines": ["Since I heard Plies, I done bought the Phantom back"], "meaning": "In the original “I’m So Hood” Plies said\n\nI’ll never buy a Phantom, 28s can’t fit", "annotation_id": "31351", "url": "https://genius.com/31351", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "They tryna dance with the devil in the pale moonlight", "vault_lines": ["They tryna dance with the devil in the pale moonlight"], "meaning": "This is a rendition of the famous line by The Joker in Tim Burton’s 1989 film; Batman. This incarnation of The Joker was player by Jack Nicholson.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gfMXEmCew4", "annotation_id": "29833", "url": "https://genius.com/29833", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "It go down like Frasier, I ain’t talkin’ Kelsey Grammer", "vault_lines": ["It go down like Frasier, I ain't talkin' Kelsey Grammer"], "meaning": "Weezy likens the tense situation to the Joe Frazier-George Foreman fight of 1973 during which ABC commentator Howard Cosell made the legendary call “DOWN GOES FRAZIER!”.\n\n\n\nHe’s saying this doesn’t have anything to do with Dr. Frasier Crane, the fictional psychiatrist played by Kelsey Grammer on Cheers and its spinoff, Frasier.", "annotation_id": "29704", "url": "https://genius.com/29704", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "Through the stress and strife Had to earn my stripes like I played with the Bengals (Hood!)", "vault_lines": ["Through the stress and strife", "Had to earn my stripes like I played with the Bengals (Hood!)"], "meaning": "The Cincinnati Bengals are a team in the National Football League; their helmets and logo are orange with black stripes resembling a Bengal tiger", "annotation_id": "29702", "url": "https://genius.com/29702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "line": "Calliope, Magnolia, Melpomene", "vault_lines": ["Martin Luther King, Calliope, Magnolia, Melpomene"], "meaning": "Three notorious housing projects in Central City New Orleans,famous for single-handedly sustaining a local crime rate that rivals many entire US cities'; in 2007, Central City had a murder rate of 316 per 100,000. The neighborhood’s absurdly disproportional contribution to New Orleans' status as the “murder capital” has so monopolized the attention of New Orleans Police Department that the Louisiana National Guard had to be deployed to the city, to watch over the rest of the New Orleans so that the NOPD could focus the entirety of its efforts on the Central City projects\n\nOn the other hand, the three housing developments (taken together) were once home to Juvenile, Lil Wayne, B.G., Turk, Master P (+ Lil Romeo), Baby, Silkk the Shocker, Soulja Slim AND C-Murder!", "annotation_id": "29341", "url": "https://genius.com/29341", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "status": "done", "targets": 18, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dj-khaled-im-so-hood-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "I’m So Hood (Remix)", "genius_id": 948, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m Ya Dogg", "line": "Smoked out like a player and you know the name", "vault_lines": ["Smoked out like a player and you know the name"], "meaning": "This can be a double entendre by Rozay.\n\nWhile himself is a player – evidenced by his large amount of money, his very accurate taste for women and his successful career – and everyone knows him well, this could also be a reference to a real player (i.e. an athlete).\n\n\n\nIsn’t a secret, despite being prohibited, that a lot of players from major american sports leagues smoke weed.\n\nIn the NBA, the most notorious names may be J.R. Smith, who was caught smoking a blunt and Michael Beasley, who got arrested in possession of marijuana.\n\n\n\nThe list goes on in the NFL, being headlined by Jerome Simpson, who got through in serious legal trouble because of weed.", "annotation_id": "8873232", "url": "https://genius.com/8873232", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m Ya Dogg", "line": "Shando, count my blessings", "vault_lines": ["Shando, count my blessings"], "meaning": "The term “shando” comes from worship in Pentacostal churches. When people are speaking in tongues, a common practice in some Pentecostal and Baptist churches, they have been know to start by uttering the word shando.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/PRrKw8Syah4?t=11", "annotation_id": "6375185", "url": "https://genius.com/6375185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m Ya Dogg", "line": "Dogg pound niggas, and we came to fight", "vault_lines": ["Dogg pound niggas, and we came to fight"], "meaning": "Tha Dogg Pound is the name of the West Coast hip-hop duo that consists of two of Snoop’s long-time friends; Daz Dillinger and Kurupt.\n\nRick Ross has previously collaborated with  Daz on the track “On Some Real Shit”.", "annotation_id": "6366535", "url": "https://genius.com/6366535", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m Ya Dogg", "line": "Baby, I come running Baby, I come running Just keep me coming (Keep me coming) And I come running (Running) Cause I’m your dog Baby I’m your dog So whenever you call I come running Baby, I come running Baby, I come running Just keep me coming (Keep me coming) And I come running Cause I’m your dog (I’m your dog) Baby I’m your dog (Oh, oh, oh) So whenever you call (Whenever you call) I come running (Baby, baby, baby)", "vault_lines": ["Baby I'm your dog", "So whenever you call"], "meaning": "Snoop will always be there for the girl in his life. Whenever she needs him, he’ll come running.\n\nHe describes himself as her ‘dog’, which is wordplay on his stage name; additionally a dog will always run towards their owner when their name is called.", "annotation_id": "6357100", "url": "https://genius.com/6357100", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m Ya Dogg", "line": "[Verse 3: Kendrick Lamar]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Both Snoop Dogg and Pharrell Williams recently collaborated on one of the most anticipated albums of the year, Kendrick Lamar’s To Pimp a Butterfly (Snoop on “Institutionalized,” and Pharrell on “Alright”). Kendrick returns the favor on the closing track for Bush.\n\nThe title of this song, “I’m Ya Dogg,” refers to a few lines on the previously mentioned TPAB. On the second verse of “Wesley’s Theory,” Kendrick portrays the white man trying to pimp his artistic talent, saying\n\nMy name is Uncle Sam, I’m your dog\nMotherfucker, you can live at the mall\n\nA variation of the same line appears on “Alright,” substituting Lucy for Uncle Sam.", "annotation_id": "6356021", "url": "https://genius.com/6356021", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m Ya Dogg", "line": "Freaky... freak like it’s the 80s", "vault_lines": ["Freaky... freak like it's the 80s"], "meaning": "Likely a reference to the popular Rick James song “Super Freak” which came out in 1981.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QYHxGBH6o4M", "annotation_id": "6354948", "url": "https://genius.com/6354948", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m Ya Dogg", "line": "Head in that Maybach boomin’, thank God for leg room", "vault_lines": ["Head in that Maybach boomin', thank God for leg room"], "meaning": "Kendrick’s getting a blowjob in his Maybach and there’s plenty of leg room for 5'6\" Kendrick to lay back and enjoy it.\n\n\n\nAlso, note the subtle wordplay with the vehicle and Maybach Music Group (MMG), a record label founded by Rick Ross.", "annotation_id": "6354517", "url": "https://genius.com/6354517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m Ya Dogg", "line": "I got... Rottweiler in tube sock", "vault_lines": ["I got... Rottweiler in tube sock"], "meaning": "The central metaphor of the whole song is being ‘alpha male’, or ‘top dog’ in an effort to impress/win over a girl. Kendrick uses wordplay around dog breeds for what is a rather sexually explicit verse.\n\n\n\nA rottweiler is an intelligent, loyal workdog. Socks are often used as masturbatory devices to catch cum and so Kendrick might be alluding to the fact that he is sexually charged and ready; likening his dick to a powerful and much-loved dog.", "annotation_id": "6351312", "url": "https://genius.com/6351312", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m Ya Dogg", "line": "Pitbull... lock on your neck, kiss it and you’ll die", "vault_lines": ["Pitbull... lock on your neck, kiss it and you'll die"], "meaning": "Although typically docile and loyal creatures, pitbulls are considered by the general public as vicious attack animals. Sadly, there have been cases of pitbulls attacking and killing children.\n\n\n\nWhen animals attack, a common target is to go for the throat. Here Kendrick uses the idea of a pitbull going for the neck of the girl in the song. Let’s be honest, he’s probably talking about choking a girl with his dick.", "annotation_id": "6351299", "url": "https://genius.com/6351299", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m Ya Dogg", "line": "Boxer... you take mine’s off then hallelujah", "vault_lines": ["Boxer... you take mine's off then hallelujah"], "meaning": "In this case of dog breed double entendre, Kendrick references the short-haired breed of Boxer dog from Germany. At the same time he is also talking about taking off his boxers in preparation for sex. He is excited about it.", "annotation_id": "6351260", "url": "https://genius.com/6351260", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m Ya Dogg", "line": "Search warrant for the crib, you better get a map for it Paid for, meanin’ I ain’t gotta rap for it", "vault_lines": ["Search warrant for the crib, you better get a map for it", "Paid for, meanin' I ain't gotta rap for it"], "meaning": "Ross owns a 109-room mansion in Georgia – good luck navigating that without assistance. He has full ownership of the $5.8 million mansion, so he doesn’t have to worry about mortgage payments.", "annotation_id": "6349359", "url": "https://genius.com/6349359", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m Ya Dogg", "line": "She a alley oop, I tap it off the backboard", "vault_lines": ["She a alley oop, I tap it off the backboard"], "meaning": "An alley oop occurs when a player tosses the basketball in the air and another dunks it – no backboard involved. Bagging this woman is as simple as pulling off the flashy dunk.", "annotation_id": "6349261", "url": "https://genius.com/6349261", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m Ya Dogg", "status": "done", "targets": 12, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Snoop-dogg-im-ya-dogg-lyrics", "genius_title": "I’m Ya Dogg", "genius_id": 1779940, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m a Boss", "line": "Better run that dope back (Dope back)", "vault_lines": ["better run that dope back (Dope back)"], "meaning": "Ross is comparing himself to Jerry Jones. Saying he treats his dealers the same way Jerry Jones treats his running-backs. By naming legendary running-backs above, Ross might be alluding to his dealers being legendary people in the streets.\n\nAdditionally, this line sounds like something a coach might say to a running-back. “Better run that line back!” Or something of the sort.\n\n“Lines” is also a not-said but connected word here. With running-backs running “Lines/routes”, while dealers run “dope-lines/routes” (“lines” also being synonymous with cocaine).", "annotation_id": "39565851", "url": "https://genius.com/39565851", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m a Boss", "line": "At the fight, we watchin’ Floyd, we on the floor (We on the floor)", "vault_lines": ["At the fight, we watchin' Floyd, we on the floor (We on the floor)"], "meaning": "Meek Mill is watching Floyd Mayweather Jr from his floor-level seat: “we on the floor”", "annotation_id": "37762683", "url": "https://genius.com/37762683", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m a Boss", "line": "Scared money don’t make no money", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, scared money don't make no money"], "meaning": "Meek Mill is referring the saying “you have to spend  money to make money”, which implies that being overly cautious with your money will lead to missed financial opportunities.", "annotation_id": "37762637", "url": "https://genius.com/37762637", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m a Boss", "line": "Yeah I’m the king, now where my muh’fuckin’ crown? (Huh!) Bitch, I’m a boss! (I’m a boss) Bitch, I’m a boss! (I’m a boss) Bitch, I’m a boss! (I’m a boss) Bitch, I’m a boss! (I’m a boss) Bitch, I’m a boss! (I’m a boss) Bitch, I’m a boss! (I’m a boss)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah I'm the king, now where my muh'fuckin' crown? (Huh!)"], "meaning": "", "annotation_id": "36826448", "url": "https://genius.com/36826448", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": false, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "I’m a Boss", "line": "Fuck a blog, dawg, ‘cause one day we gon’ meet (Gon’ meet)", "vault_lines": ["Fuck a blog, dawg, 'cause one day we gon' meet (Gon' meet)"], "meaning": "Diss to legendary gangster rapper, 50 Cent, who Ross been beefing with since 2008. 50 had a blog website called “thisis50.com” which posted about Rick Ross often.\n\nAn amazing example is 50’s infamous “warning shot”", "annotation_id": "30836546", "url": "https://genius.com/30836546", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
