{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Jumping Ship", "line": "I gotta driver who keep all the sticks", "vault_lines": ["I gotta driver who keep all the sticks"], "meaning": "Sticks is used as a double entendre here: Ross is saying his chauffeur handles his guns (sticks) for him and keeps them in the car, and uses the reference to his driver make a play-on-words between sticks as in guns and sticks as in cars with a stick-shift transmission, usually seen in foreign sportscars.", "annotation_id": "12713498", "url": "https://genius.com/12713498", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Jumping Ship", "line": "Big money baller, so they know just who I am A Lonzo Ball", "vault_lines": ["Big money baller, so they know just who I am"], "meaning": "The second overall draft pick of the Los Angeles Lakers in the 2017 NBA Draft, Lakers Draft Lonzo Ball is the face behind Big Baller Brand (BBB). Big Baller Brand also features Lonzo’s brothers, LiAngelo and LaMelo as well as his polarizing father, LaVar, meet the Jordan Stopper, Lavar Ball.\n\nThe BBB brand has largely been criticized due to its $495 price tag on the first signature shoes released for Lonzo, the ZO2, Lonzo’s $495 Kicks.\n\nLaVar notoriously claimed that if you can’t afford the sneakers “you aren’t a REAL big baller if you can’t afford the ZO2’s”, Lavar’s a Big Baller; Are You?\n\nRick Ross is saying his pockets are fat and that he’s a big baller just like the face of the BBB brand, Lonzo Ball.", "annotation_id": "12617252", "url": "https://genius.com/12617252", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Jumping Ship", "line": "Diddy shot, I got Cîroc all in my glass", "vault_lines": ["Diddy shot, I got Cîroc all in my glass"], "meaning": "Ciroc is brand of vodka, made in Charente Maritime of France Grapes. This vodka was popularized by many rappers, starting with Diddy when it was introduced in 2003 and handled by Sean “Diddy” Combs in an “equal-share venture” with a 50/50 profit split.", "annotation_id": "12617051", "url": "https://genius.com/12617051", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
