{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Bubble double-R", "vault_lines": ["Murder-cedes Benz or that bubble double-R"], "meaning": "Double-R reffers to Rolls-Royce, the expensive automobile brand.", "annotation_id": "26726303", "url": "https://genius.com/26726303", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I fell in love with the pen, started fuckin’ the ink (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["I fell in love with the pen, started fuckin' the ink (Huh)"], "meaning": "When he says he’s in “love with the pen” he could be talking about how he (Rick Ross) loves PENis", "annotation_id": "25310566", "url": "https://genius.com/25310566", "state": "pending", "votes": 206, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I know that showin’ emotion don’t ever mean I’m a pussy", "vault_lines": ["I know that showin' emotion don't ever mean I'm a pussy"], "meaning": "Being one of the early pioneers in melodic/sentimental rap and known for liking strippers for their personality, Drake has been the consistent subject of debate when discussing ‘soft rappers’.\n\nIn Drake’s defence against his emotional persona, he quickly follows up with a bar for his critics, and proceeds to rap one of the most memorable lines within the track…", "annotation_id": "25015343", "url": "https://genius.com/25015343", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Went and got a yacht, I’m talkin’ Carnival Cruise", "vault_lines": ["Went and got a yacht, I'm talkin' Carnival Cruise"], "meaning": "Ross is saying that the yacht that he purchased is as big as a cruise ship. He is of course using hyperbole, but the point is to convey that his boat is extremely big because he has a lot of money.", "annotation_id": "24425771", "url": "https://genius.com/24425771", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Know that I don’t make music for niggas who don’t get pussy So those are the ones I count on to diss me or overlook me", "vault_lines": ["So those are the ones I count on to diss me or overlook me"], "meaning": "Around this time Drake was heavily criticized from certain communities for talking about “soft” subject matter such as women in a vulnerable way (as exemplified with the “Drake the type of nigga to…” memes).\n\nDrake reverses this criticism onto his critics claiming that the people who diss him are people who can’t get any women, and thus can not relate to his songs about relationships.", "annotation_id": "22778496", "url": "https://genius.com/22778496", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "In this bitch, all drinks on the house like Snoopy", "vault_lines": ["In this bitch, all drinks on the house like Snoopy"], "meaning": "Wordplay from Drake comparing how all of his drinks are complimentary wherever he goes (“on the house”) to how Snoopy from the Charlie Brown cartoons famously sleeps on top of his dog house rather than sleeping inside of it.", "annotation_id": "22778485", "url": "https://genius.com/22778485", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "The hustle’s an art, I painted what I would think", "vault_lines": ["The hustle's an art, I painted what I would think"], "meaning": "Regarding the previous line, Ross’ “fell in love with the pen”, line can also mention how his love for the hustle of the game and dedication placed him where is today in his career. He sees his past achievements in both his personal and professional life as a canvas and describes his life experiences behind the mic using lyrics as his muse.", "annotation_id": "22189201", "url": "https://genius.com/22189201", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Mink coats make your woman wanna fornicate Rozay and Drake, I’m getting cake, nothin’ short of great (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Mink coats make your woman wanna fornicate", "Rozay and Drake, I'm getting cake, nothin' short of great (Huh)"], "meaning": "“Cake” is slang for money and fornication is the act of two unmarried people who have sexual intercourse. On Drake’s September 2011 track, “Free Spirit,” Ross also mentions fornication:\n\nI fondle the money, fornicate with a fortune", "annotation_id": "18559731", "url": "https://genius.com/18559731", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Huh You know I love this YOLO, you only live once", "vault_lines": ["You know I love this", "YOLO, you only live once"], "meaning": "Drake used the term YOLO more prominently on the Take Care hit single, “The Motto”:\n\nYou only live once: that’s the motto, nigga, YOLO\n\nDrake and Rick Ross also had plans to release a joint mixtape titled YOLO but they were eventually scrapped. Drake told MTV News in June 2012 that he was considering changing the mixtape’s title to something else, confirming there was something in the works:\n\n[YOLO] has become so … it’s like an epidemic. So I hope by the time we finish it, it would still hold weight and substance […] We might have to call it something different now ‘cause that’s just so attached to 'The Motto.’", "annotation_id": "18559653", "url": "https://genius.com/18559653", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "(M-M-Maybach Music)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Maybach Music Group is a record label imprint founded by rapper Rick Ross. Some of the most notable rappers from Maybach Music include Rick Ross himself, Meek Mill, and French Montana.", "annotation_id": "14365057", "url": "https://genius.com/14365057", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Young-ass nigga, lifelong dreams", "vault_lines": ["Young-ass nigga, lifelong dreams"], "meaning": "Drake may be referring to his days as Jimmy Brooks on Degrassi: The Next Generation.  During his time as Jimmy Brooks, he wrote 12 pages worth of lyrics in a purple spiral notebook titled “10 Mack Commandments”.\n\n\n\nA page directly from Drake’s Degrassi notebook, showing a list of songs and signatures of his real name.\n\nAt the commencement of Drake’s “Degrassi”-era, his mother was still the sole income of his household. His “lifelong dreams” as a 15-year-old may have been to become wealthy enough to support himself and his mother. Drake also discusses this topic on “10 Bands” of 2015’s “If You’re Reading This It’s Too Late”:\n\nI was payin' mama’s rent when was I turning 17", "annotation_id": "14364645", "url": "https://genius.com/14364645", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "[Intro: Choir & Rick Ross]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "While it may sound like a classic sample-based Just Blaze production, no samples were technically used. In an interview with Stereogum, Drake had this to say about the production:\n\n…Just Blaze did the beat. It was so amazing because it was a gospel sample and the gospel group didn’t want to clear the sample, so we had a choir come in and re-sing the sample. The new beat versus the old beat, the new beat is so much better…\n\nHere is a very rare video of the choir session that was arranged for the production of this track:\n\nhttp://www.dailymotion.com/video/x17ujbj_lord-knows-session_music", "annotation_id": "2498527", "url": "https://genius.com/2498527", "state": "accepted", "votes": 108, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Lookin’ for the right way to do the wrong things (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Lookin' for the right way to do the wrong things (Huh)"], "meaning": "Doing ‘the wrong things’ would be things like partying with strippers, doing drugs, drinking heavily, smoking weed, etc. (Things society would be against).\n\n\n\nDrake’s therefore trying to figure out the ‘right way’ to approach these habits, so as not to fall victim to them.", "annotation_id": "516221", "url": "https://genius.com/516221", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "It’s your worst nightmare, it’s my first night here", "vault_lines": ["It's your worst nightmare, it's my first night here"], "meaning": "Drake kicks off this hard hitting track by stating that he is the worst nightmare for his fellow rappers. This is due to the fact that he is young, fresh, original and aiming to reach the top of the rap game.\n\nStating that it’s his ‘first night here’ implies that Drizzy hasn’t been around long (this is his sophomore album after all) but is already a nightmare to the opposition – ‘here’ being the rap scene.", "annotation_id": "516209", "url": "https://genius.com/516209", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Lord knows, Lord knows, I’m heavy, I got my weight up", "vault_lines": ["Lord knows, Lord knows, I'm heavy, I got my weight up"], "meaning": "Could refer to a couple of things:\n\n\nDrake has got his money up.\nGaining weight in boxing is to fight in a different weight class, so he’s moving up in the sense that he’s competing with a different class of rappers as he is consistently ranked high on MTV’s Hottest MC’s Lists.\nPerhaps Drake is alluding to increased the meaning in his lyrics since we last saw him on Thank Me Later.\nDrake has literally gotten bigger by working out.", "annotation_id": "490847", "url": "https://genius.com/490847", "state": "accepted", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Haffa and Jordan my only role models", "vault_lines": ["Haffa and Jordan my only role models"], "meaning": "Two of his closest friends. Jordan is a member of OVO and is usually seen with the crew.\n\nThere was speculation on this line for years, but it’s been solved since his IG post .", "annotation_id": "478254", "url": "https://genius.com/478254", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Talking strippers and models that try to gain attention Even a couple pornstars that I’m ashamed to mention", "vault_lines": ["Talking strippers and models that try to gain attention"], "meaning": "A few strippers and models have tried to have sex with Drake, just for the mediatic boost of being in a relationship with a celebrity like him.\n\nMost famously, artist and former pornstar Sophie Brussaux, who claims to have a child by Drake, is at the center of his beef with Pusha-T, who has attacked Drake for what Push sees as his failure to adequately step up as a father.\n\n2021 Update: Sophie Brussaux it turns out WAS pregnant with Drake’s son. She in fact did have a brief stint as a “soft-core” porn actress with some solo videos.\n\n\n\nDrake and Sophie went on a now infamous date in Amsterdam in 2017.\n\n\n\nIn the past, Drake has also received negative attention for allegedly thirstily messaging pornstar Mia Khalifa on Instagram.", "annotation_id": "470405", "url": "https://genius.com/470405", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "And this girl right here, who knows what she knows?", "vault_lines": ["And this girl right here, who knows what she knows?"], "meaning": "Drakes met a girl who comes off as sketchy… Not knowing whether this girl is a gold digger or not, as he’s dealt with too many of them since becoming famous. (As he mentions later)\n\nDrake doesn’t trust these types of women, from previous experience, as detailed on his song, “The Resistance”, with:\n\nMaybe cause a girl I thought I trusted was who set the whole shit up\n\nReferring to being robbed and a trusted girl setting it up.", "annotation_id": "451240", "url": "https://genius.com/451240", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Destined for greatness, but got a place in Jamaica (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["Destined for greatness, but got a place in Jamaica (Woo)"], "meaning": "It’s a play on how he’s “destined” for greatness but already owns a home in Jamaica; as well as the idea that that is his “destination” (as in actual physical destination).\n\nRoss' intention with this line appears to be to say that even though he’s rich (and going to get richer), he still owns a house in a country with a significant population of people in poverty (Jamaica).\n\nLike another line in this same verse; this is an example of sophisticated ignorance.\n\nAny future changes to this annotation should take a leaf out of TV Tropes' book and use the Rule Of Cautious Editing Judgment.", "annotation_id": "450132", "url": "https://genius.com/450132", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Roberson, boost the rate up, it’s time that somebody paid up", "vault_lines": ["Roberson, boost the rate up, it's time that somebody paid up"], "meaning": "Gee Roberson is one of Drake’s managers from the famous management and production company, Hip Hop Since 1978.", "annotation_id": "449778", "url": "https://genius.com/449778", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Mixtape legend, underground kings", "vault_lines": ["Mixtape legend, underground kings"], "meaning": "Earlier in the album, Drake dealt with the underground king theme at length.  As for “mixtape legend”, he mentioned that too on the same song\n\nUnderground kings is a reference in part to UGK, an influential and popular Houston rap duo featuring Bun B (who Drake collaborated with on Uptown and Put It Down) and the late Pimp C. Drizzy has spoken on their influence on him time and time again (in part possibly why Drake is on Put It Down).\n\n\n\nAs for ‘mixtape legend’ – “So Far Gone” is what made Drake not only famous, but rich.", "annotation_id": "440847", "url": "https://genius.com/440847", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Trill nigga, for real", "vault_lines": ["Trill nigga, for real"], "meaning": "Trill, a term coined by Houston rappers, is a combination of “true” and “real”.", "annotation_id": "437913", "url": "https://genius.com/437913", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I wonder if they’d ever survive in this era In a time where it’s recreation To pull all your skeletons out the closet like Halloween decorations", "vault_lines": ["I wonder if they'd ever survive in this era", "In a time where it's recreation", "To pull all your skeletons out the closet like Halloween decorations"], "meaning": "Well speaking of greats from the past, Jay’s still doing alright, and even six-feet under Biggie Smalls and Pac are as popular as they ever were. Then there’s also the small matter of one Marshall Mathers.\n\nDrake elaborated on this idea in a recent Billboard interview:\n\n\n I genuinely feel like the first one that really launched a career in this time period. I say it on my album, “They take the greats from the past and compare us/But I wonder if they’d ever survive in this era.” And that shit to me [is] everything. Not even the leaks-I’m just talking about how malicious and intrusive the Internet is, and how essential and necessary the Internet is in our lives, in our careers. Twitter is an opinion with no consequences because you can be anybody, you can hide from anybody, you don’t have to see anybody – but you can reach anybody. And that shit is crazy\n\n These sites that are driven on tearing artists down daily… I remember back in the day – there were like two or three individuals on the radio, and one publication that would attack rappers and artists. And if you didn’t make that, you were good. There wasn’t really that much else to fuck you over. Lord only knows what people got away with – I can’t imagine. I commend any artist thriving in this generation, because it’s difficult. You have to have thick skin and just care about your craft, and know that there’s a thousand positive people for every one negative person\n\n It’s crazy how fast it’s all moving. Artists from the past, they had a different grind. It was more pavement, hitting the streets. Maybe it was harder work, I’ll never know. But for this generation it’s very hard work, upkeep, to be successful", "annotation_id": "437456", "url": "https://genius.com/437456", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "A lot of niggas came up off of a style that I made up But if all I hear is me, then who should I be afraid of?", "vault_lines": ["A lot of niggas came up off of a style that I made up", "But if all I hear is me, then who should I be afraid of?"], "meaning": "Drake’s style is heavily influenced by R&B. He includes plenty of singing and emotion in his songs (as heard in 2009’s So Far Gone). Although they may have jacked his style, he’s confident that they can’t compete with him.\n\nIt also means if competitors in the game are “coppin” a style he’s generated then he has nothing to fear. He’s supplying the style that they are “jocking” so all he’s seeing & hearing his himself basically being funneled through other artist, why would he then be worried?\n\nA dope punchline on the surface even though if you look deeper this argument is not entirely true since it´s widely recognised that Kanye West with 808s & Heartbreaks made that style up. That album is more of singing/R&B from a rapper and deals with the same themes as Drakes album – Love, loneliness, heartbreak and being introspective on that level. Drake has also citied 808s & Heartbreaks as his biggest influence.", "annotation_id": "435875", "url": "https://genius.com/435875", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I never hear the disses they try and point out to me But it’s whatever if somebody won’t make it a thing", "vault_lines": ["I never hear the disses they try and point out to me", "But it's whatever if somebody won't make it a thing"], "meaning": "Drake, from a November 2011 Village Voice article:\n\n To be honest with you, I coined the phrase ‘Dis me, and you’ll never hear a reply for it,’ so for me to go in would be silly.  But at the same time, I’m a competitive rapper, so I mean, nobody could ever take a direct shot at me, say my name or do a full song about me, and I sit idly by and take it. Unless it was wack, then I’d just be like, ‘You sound stupid,’ but if it was somebody with bars? I’m going back for sure\n\nWell, let’s see if he stands by those words in his beef with Common…", "annotation_id": "435782", "url": "https://genius.com/435782", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I’m going trigger happy just to see my niggas happy", "vault_lines": ["I'm going trigger happy just to see my niggas happy"], "meaning": "Continuing the metaphor of “killing” the rap game. Drake is gonna kill everything to make sure his homies are living good too.\n\nWe hope you didn’t forget what his name stood for:\n\nDo\nRight\nAnd\nKill\nEverything", "annotation_id": "435468", "url": "https://genius.com/435468", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "It’s the red bottom boss (Huh), came to buy the bar (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["It's the red bottom boss (Huh), came to buy the bar (Huh)"], "meaning": "Rick Ross wears Louboutins, which are known for their signature red-lacquered soles. This isn’t the first time Ricky’s worn red bottoms", "annotation_id": "435077", "url": "https://genius.com/435077", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Every day, them hammers bang, whippin’ yay like Anna Mae", "vault_lines": ["Every day, them hammers bang, whippin' yay like Anna Mae"], "meaning": "Ross is whipping up the crack in the kitchen. This may be a reference to Tina Turner (born Anna Mae Bullock) and the famous whipping (abuse) she suffered at the hands of Ike Turner.\n\nIt could also be from the movie Which Way Is Up? starring Richard Pryor. In the movie there is a scene in which he and his wife Anne Mae use a whip during sex.", "annotation_id": "434968", "url": "https://genius.com/434968", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Only fat nigga in the sauna with Jews", "vault_lines": ["Only fat nigga in the sauna with Jews"], "meaning": "Ross mentions here that he is rich enough to be conducting inside business deals with the real power players – it is a meme (if troubling) idea that Jews are rich and control Hollywood\n\nRoss is likely referring to the cultural institution of Jewish bathhouses in Miami—(the city with the second-most Jews per capita in the United States)—most famously the Russian Turkish Bath on Collins Ave in Miami Beach.\n\n\n\nAdditionally, Drake is half-Jewish, and Rozay is based in Miami, which is home to lots of retired Jewish people. Also possibly being some clever wordplay – ‘a pig in a sauna with jews’.\n\nThis is a perfect example of sophisticated ignorance.", "annotation_id": "434888", "url": "https://genius.com/434888", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "But Weezy and Stunna my only role models", "vault_lines": ["But Weezy and Stunna my only role models"], "meaning": "Young Money Entertainment CEO is Lil Wayne aka Weezy\n\n\n\nYoung Money is an imprint of  Cash Money Records, which is founded by the hand rubber extraordinaire , Birdman a.k.a. Stunna\n\n\n\nAll joking aside, Birdman’s label has an extraordinary discography, from New Orleans classics beloved in the city but relatively unknown elsewhere to international smashes", "annotation_id": "434754", "url": "https://genius.com/434754", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I’m hearing all of the jokes, I know that they tryna push me", "vault_lines": ["I'm hearing all of the jokes, I know that they tryna push me"], "meaning": "Drake hears all the jokes about him being emo and soft because of his lyrics. He knows haters are trying to push him to throw shots back, but he makes music for his fans to love and not the haters\n\nThe use of the word “push” here may not be accidental – rapper Pusha T had some lyrics that many regarded as a diss towards the Canadian wonder, though the Clipse member has denied it.  The lyrics in question mention “Rappers on their sophomores, acting like they boss lords” and “The talk don’t match the leather, the swag don’t match the sweaters”", "annotation_id": "434631", "url": "https://genius.com/434631", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Bought a white Ghost, now shit is gettin’ spooky Very, very scary like shit you see in the movies", "vault_lines": ["Bought a white Ghost, now shit is gettin' spooky", "Very, very scary like shit you see in the movies"], "meaning": "Drake plays on the meaning of “ghost” to describe the Rolls-Royce Ghost he bought himself.  This line is, um, “reminiscent” of this Jay-Z classic\n\nThis plays into the scariness theme of the previous couplet", "annotation_id": "434613", "url": "https://genius.com/434613", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "YM, I remain that forever In the same place my brother Wayne at forever", "vault_lines": ["YM, I remain that forever", "In the same place my brother Wayne at forever"], "meaning": "Lots of talk recently that Drake might leave Young Money especially after OVOXO came about. Drake is letting everyone that they are misinformed\n\n\n\nDrizzy recently expanded on his relationship with Weezy in an interview with Billboard:\n\nCan you talk a bit about your relationship with Lil Wayne?\n\n That’s my brother. Over music, over everything. He’s responsible for everything that’s going on around me. But of course, it’s amazing. There’s a constant competitive vibe because that’s what rap is and we both love the same things in rap. In the back of my mind I’m always like, “I got to rap better than Wayne.” And I can’t say I know, but I feel like I put enough good music out there that he also feels like he’s … got to rap better than everybody. We play off each other, we make each other better. He said something in an interview that was so true that I respected him for it so much. They tried to corner him with question like, “Drake’s put out so much beastly music, do you feel like you created a monster?” And he’s like, ‘No, that’s my artist.“ And he’s right. I’m his soldier. That’s all I ever want to be for Wayne", "annotation_id": "434592", "url": "https://genius.com/434592", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "Get some shake, a brick in the press And chef it like Mrs. Fields, they makin’ the cookie stretch", "vault_lines": ["Get some shake, a brick in the press", "And chef it like Mrs. Fields, they makin' the cookie stretch"], "meaning": "Mrs. Fields!:\n\n\n\n‘Shake’ and ‘brick’ are slang words in the drug world.\n\nDealers (that want to buy gifts for their daughters) have to stretch their product to make more money, similar to how cooks working for Mrs. Fields have to stretch the cookie dough. (Dough is also slang for money)", "annotation_id": "434492", "url": "https://genius.com/434492", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "line": "I’m a descendant of either Marley or Hendrix I haven’t figured it out ’cause my story is far from finished", "vault_lines": ["I'm a descendant of either Marley or Hendrix", "I haven't figured it out 'cause my story is far from finished"], "meaning": "Bob Marley and Jimi Hendrix were respectively visionary reggae and psychedelic rock musicians.  Drake has long talked of his admiration of Hendrix. Not to mention, in his second mainstream hit, “Over”, he makes sure to add his allusion to Marley’s own “Trenchtown Rock”: “One thing ‘bout music: when it hits, you feel no pain.”\n\n“My story is far from finished” also refers to the fact that both of the aforementioned legends are now deceased (cut down in their prime, even), but Drake does not see this same fate befalling him.\n\n\n\nSidenote: Like Drake, both Bob Marley and Jimi Hendrix were mixed race.", "annotation_id": "434486", "url": "https://genius.com/434486", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Lord Knows", "status": "done", "targets": 46, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-lord-knows-lyrics", "genius_title": "Lord Knows", "genius_id": 55695, "source": "api"}
