{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "Teflon Don", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This song was published 4 months before the Teflon Don album as a teaser.\n\n\n\nA still from the Mafia Music 2 music video.", "annotation_id": "31544117", "url": "https://genius.com/31544117", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "Ahh, 2 mill on my second home Still stepping on a couple bricks of that methadone", "vault_lines": ["Ahh, 2 mill on my second home", "Still stepping on a couple bricks of that methadone"], "meaning": "Check the use of words for doubles (2 mill', second, couple).\n\nSubtle link between bricks and home. The second line suggests he’s still in the dope game (bricks of meth) even as a major label artist.", "annotation_id": "31544048", "url": "https://genius.com/31544048", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "You know we hustle to the key of life", "vault_lines": ["You know we hustle to the key of life"], "meaning": "Most likely a reference to the highly regarded Stevie Wonder album from 1976.", "annotation_id": "31543725", "url": "https://genius.com/31543725", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "Kilograms were the key to my success", "vault_lines": ["Kilograms were the key to my success"], "meaning": "Kilograms of coke are usually called ‘ki’s’, so it’s a key pun.\n\nAlso sets the tone for the rest of the song and mafia references throughout.", "annotation_id": "31543589", "url": "https://genius.com/31543589", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "The moral of the story was Ain’t no happy ending for a thug I think of 2Pac, I think of Biggie now Look how they getting down, I went and bought a 50 Cal We all go when we gotta go", "vault_lines": ["The moral of the story was", "Ain't no happy ending for a thug", "I think of 2Pac, I think of Biggie now", "Look how they getting down, I went and bought a 50 Cal", "We all go when we gotta go"], "meaning": "Tupac Shakur and Biggie Smalls are both legendary hip hop artists who were fatally murdered in the 1990s.\n\nIn a 2013 interview with Power 106, Rick Ross spoke about his thoughts on Tupac and Biggie:\n\nTupac and Big, I feel, are the two most important rappers of all time […] As a fan of the culture side, you know what they brought to the game, um, on B.I.G side from the style and the lifestyle and the culture and he way he put it on the screen […] And Tupac, the message and the power and the voice and he showed you the potential…\n\nA .50 caliber handgun is an extremely powerful handgun that fires bullets measuring half an inch in diameter. Some popular variants are the Smith & Wesson Model 500 and the Desert Eagle.", "annotation_id": "12749269", "url": "https://genius.com/12749269", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "As Salamu Alaykum to paper Walaikum Salam Im the don inshallah, now pour me my Dom", "vault_lines": ["As Salamu Alaykum to paper", "Im the don inshallah, now pour me my Dom"], "meaning": "As-Salamu Alaykum means peace be upon you in Arabic, Walaikum Salam is a general response.\nMost of the time this is used as a greeting.\n\nInshallah is another common Arabic word, meaning you hope something will happen with the power of Allah (God). Here Ross hopes to be remembered as a Don (mafia boss).\nClever use of two muslim/Arabic terms.\n\nDom refers to Dom Perignon.\n\n\n\nNot so clever to mention alcohol and the Islam in one sentence though.", "annotation_id": "997400", "url": "https://genius.com/997400", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "RIP Paul Castellano, Myer Lansky, Bumpy Johnson, Al Capone T-Roger, Tookie Williams, Carlo Gambino and last but not least John Gotti Teflon Don (Ross, Ross, ross)", "vault_lines": ["RIP Paul Castellano, Myer Lansky, Bumpy Johnson, Al Capone", "T-Roger, Tookie Williams, Carlo Gambino and last but not least John Gotti"], "meaning": "Ross is paying respect to all of the mobsters listed that have been killed (except for Meyer Lansky, he died naturally).\n\nThis track was supposed to be on his fourth LP, Teflon Don, so he’s giving a shout out to the album.\nThe name of the album, Teflon Don, is also a nickname of the notorious don John Gotti.\n\nHe then mentions himself, implying he wants to be remembered like them.", "annotation_id": "997358", "url": "https://genius.com/997358", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "I got a method for getting money like RZA do", "vault_lines": ["I got a method for getting money like RZA do"], "meaning": "RZA is the Abbott of Wu-Tang and was a major force in creating the Wu-Tang brand.\n\nMethod Man is also a member of Wu-Tang, hence the line, “I got a method”.\n\nRick Ross has done 2 songs with Wu-Tang members. “Molasses” with Rakewon and Ghostface Killah and “Audio Meth” with only Raekwon.\n\nRZA is also a legendary producer who makes plenty of money producing music.", "annotation_id": "444347", "url": "https://genius.com/444347", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "Resurrected Big Poppa in the physical", "vault_lines": ["Resurrected Big Poppa in the physical"], "meaning": "Ross is comparing himself to Biggie Smalls here.\n\nRoss has compared himself to the legendary Biggie on more than one occasion, and he’s not the only one to make that comparison.\n\nIn 2014, Ross recreated one of Biggie’s most famous songs with the help of Diddy (BIG’s former right hand man), furthering the eerie similarities between the two.", "annotation_id": "399598", "url": "https://genius.com/399598", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "My vocal like an atom bomb in the Avalon Black roses for singing the saddest song", "vault_lines": ["Black roses for singing the saddest song"], "meaning": "Rozay is known for his immense, hoarse, brassy roar. Here, he describes it in almost hyperbolically epic terms, comparing it to an atomic explosion when he records his vocals on the Avalon 737SP Compressor/Tube Pre-Amp.\n\n\n\n\n\nRick also continues to draw metaphors for the gravity of his rhymes, now comparing his verses to black roses that would accompany sadness.", "annotation_id": "221622", "url": "https://genius.com/221622", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "I bust my nut and holla lucky me Its tatted in my vein I remain sucker free", "vault_lines": ["I bust my nut and holla lucky me", "Its tatted in my vein I remain sucker free"], "meaning": "Ross delights in the fact that he is not beholden to the women with whom he has sex. He claims it’s an innate quality of his to bust a nut and sever all connections, an attribute which he describes as being “sucker-free” because he frees himself of women after they suck his dick. “Sucker-free” is also used to mean “no-nonsense” and “one who does not suffer fools.”", "annotation_id": "221619", "url": "https://genius.com/221619", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "With 20 g’s in my denim jeans If she fucking me she lucky just to get some Chuck E. Cheese", "vault_lines": ["With 20 g's in my denim jeans", "If she fucking me she lucky just to get some Chuck E. Cheese"], "meaning": "He walks around with $20,000 in his pockets. If a woman is obviously sleeping with him for money, Ross will make sure she is minimally rewarded. The amount of money she’d come back with would be laughably small, so he compares it to the ridiculous mascot of a tedious children’s restaurant\n\n\n\nAlso, notice the “cheese” wordplay; cheese as in both the mascot’s last name and a slang term for money.", "annotation_id": "221618", "url": "https://genius.com/221618", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "Oh please let a nigga breathe Dilated pupils are the seeds of a niggas greed", "vault_lines": ["Oh please let a nigga breathe", "Dilated pupils are the seeds of a niggas greed"], "meaning": "Ross wants freedom from the paparazzi, whose camera flashes make his pupil dilate. This yearning from attention is echoed in Kanye West’s “The Glory.”\n\nCameras flash so much that I gotta do that yayo dance.\n\n\n\nAdditionally, Ross seems to imply that greed can take root if a person is constantly hounded for attention.", "annotation_id": "221615", "url": "https://genius.com/221615", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "Whips weaving through traffic the triple black centipedes", "vault_lines": ["Whips weaving through traffic the triple black centipedes"], "meaning": "The fleet of cars Ross and his entourage drive around in is shiny and black with many segments, causing it to resemble one of these:\n\n\n\nRoss also refers to 2 popular hip hop themes of driving slow coined by Drive Slow. And the black pride movement of All Black Everything.", "annotation_id": "221613", "url": "https://genius.com/221613", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "line": "Plenty cream my women pose like figurines", "vault_lines": ["Plenty cream my women pose like figurines"], "meaning": "With the addition of money to his situation, Ross finds that women are more inclined to spend time with him and do what he says.\nRoss often reference high end luxury cars which often have figurines on the hood of the car. Emphasized in the next line.", "annotation_id": "221612", "url": "https://genius.com/221612", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music II", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-mafia-music-ii-lyrics", "genius_title": "Mafia Music II", "genius_id": 40238, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "The home invasion done by niggas in your bloodline", "vault_lines": ["The home invasion done by niggas in your bloodline"], "meaning": "Ross may be taking cues here from Rule #3 of Biggie’s “Ten Crack Commandments” – even your own family may blast you given the opportunity to steal your wealth.", "annotation_id": "16280727", "url": "https://genius.com/16280727", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Glock .40, even when I shower Chrome .22 in my swimmin’ towel", "vault_lines": ["Glock .40, even when I shower", "Chrome .22 in my swimmin' towel"], "meaning": "Ross has to be careful. Despite his prominence in the rap game, he’s always prepared to defend himself. Even when showering in the privacy of his own home or bathing by the pool, Ross keeps a strap.", "annotation_id": "16280693", "url": "https://genius.com/16280693", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Match the watch in the face", "vault_lines": ["I got a new style of shoes, match the watch in the face"], "meaning": "Rozay is most likely rocking the watch of one of the many brands of shoes he most recently signed a deal with (Prada, Saint Laurent Paris, Givenchy, and Balenciaga) after being released by Reebok.", "annotation_id": "3011315", "url": "https://genius.com/3011315", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "I ordered the Wraith", "vault_lines": ["I bought more jewels, I ordered the Wraith"], "meaning": "In December of 2013 Rick Ross purchased a Rolls Royce Wraith, which has a starting price of $288,600. He was so proud of the new addition to his collection he even made a video of its delivery.\n\n\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lrds0ju1qNM", "annotation_id": "2930375", "url": "https://genius.com/2930375", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Time to elevate, we re-open shop", "vault_lines": ["Time to elevate, we re-open shop"], "meaning": "WE DID IT, WE DID IT! WE SO FAR FROM FINISHED!\n\nDespite the celebration, they still work as if they just started from the bottom. The game does not have any place for complacency.", "annotation_id": "2882683", "url": "https://genius.com/2882683", "state": "accepted", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "I studied Kenneth Williams, I’m one hell of a student", "vault_lines": ["I studied Kenneth Williams, I'm one hell of a student"], "meaning": "Kenneth “Boobie” Williams was the founder and leader of the Boobie Boys gang. Ross was not only part of his gang, but was allegedly Boobie’s right hand man and protege.", "annotation_id": "2875218", "url": "https://genius.com/2875218", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Remarkable hustle, my niggas comin’ home", "vault_lines": ["Remarkable hustle, my niggas comin' home"], "meaning": "Ross kept the high risk business investments alive while some of his team faced time in prison. They are coming home to more money than ever before; a remarkable feat by the BAWSE.", "annotation_id": "2868494", "url": "https://genius.com/2868494", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Throw a yellow flag, pussy nigga body drops", "vault_lines": ["Throw a yellow flag, pussy nigga body drops"], "meaning": "This line continues on the Football reference made in the previous: The yellow flag is a yellow cloth used in American football (and lacrosse) by game officials to identify and sometimes mark the location of penalties or infractions that occur during regular play. Rozay drops yellow flags to symbolise who he wants dropped, and the job gets done.", "annotation_id": "2866776", "url": "https://genius.com/2866776", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Patek Philippe, platinum Audemars", "vault_lines": ["Patek Philippe, platinum Audemars"], "meaning": "The Patek Philippe and Audemars are both luxury watches, you most likely see the average joe waltzing around in one of these types of watches as they are incredibly expensive. Since this line comes directly after Rozay boasting about buying a new house in Phoenix that has a 10 car garage, its obvious that Rick is gloating about his immense wealth.", "annotation_id": "2864185", "url": "https://genius.com/2864185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "[Produced by Bink!]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "During an interview with XXL Magazine B!nk explained how the track came about. It’s an interesting story that sheds light on Dr. Dre’s infamous Detox record:\n\n\nWell it’s crazy. It started over a year ago; I did the record for Dr. Dre, for Detox. Dr. Dre hired Ross to write his verse for him. So Ross wrote a verse for him and a verse for himself, and then he was supposed to put Rihanna on the hook. But, you know, Dre never revisited the record, and then Ross came back about a month and a half ago and wanted the record for himself. So I had to ask the Good Doctor for clearance. I actually made it WITH Dre; I was in Vegas with Dre working on it at the time. They brought Ross in later.\n\nActually, Khaled called me. He was just ranting and raving about Ross' verse—his exact words were, “Ross caught the Holy Spirit.” He kept saying that Ross had caught the Holy Ghost, “We gotta have that, we gotta have that.” So I was like, you know, I gotta call up Big Homie and see if he’ll let it go, ‘cause I knew he was back in Detox mode again. I didn’t know if he was gonna hold onto it or not this time around.\n\nDre was basically like, “Ask him to send you a copy of the record, and if you like it, I’m with you. I'ma let you make the call.” Which is flattering to me, but at the same time that’s what Big Homie told me. So I called them, they sent me the record, which they was very nervous about, because of course these days for some reason, when you send a rapper the music, they feel like their lyrics is worth more than the beat, and they don’t send the song back. [Laughs]\n\nI still have yet to hear it. ‘Cause when I heard it, it was just Ross and Mavado, but now they added Sizzla to it, so I still haven’t heard Sizzla’s verse. There’s a serious disconnect, to be honest with you, with the music. It’s not like it used to be.\n\n\nhttps://twitter.com/rickyrozay/statuses/446774795599101953", "annotation_id": "2859469", "url": "https://genius.com/2859469", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Niggas hatin’, though they studied my moves I’m like Farrakhan, in view of hundreds of Jews", "vault_lines": ["I'm like Farrakhan, in view of hundreds of Jews"], "meaning": "Louis Farrakhan (head of Nation of Islam) is known to be very critical of Jews and many of his comments have been deemed antisemitic by the Anti-Defamation League.  Ross is simply out here making big money moves and Jews, of course, wanna be a part of that.", "annotation_id": "2859390", "url": "https://genius.com/2859390", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Bought a spot in Anguilla just for me and my ho", "vault_lines": ["Bought a spot in Anguilla just for me and my ho"], "meaning": "Ross bought property in Anguilla, a British overseas territory in the Caribbean, just for him and his girl.", "annotation_id": "2857466", "url": "https://genius.com/2857466", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Her sweet potato pies, oh me, oh my Showin’ no remorse, watchin the others cry", "vault_lines": ["Her sweet potato pies, oh me, oh my", "Showin' no remorse, watchin the others cry"], "meaning": "Continuing off the past line, Ross talks about the aunt’s sweet potato pies in order to paint the picture of her as an innocent mother who lost her life because of mindless gang violence.\n\nThe protagonist of the story shows no sympathy or contrition over his killings of his extended family members, as he witnesses others grieve.", "annotation_id": "2856071", "url": "https://genius.com/2856071", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Movin’ bricks like it’s Black Friday", "vault_lines": ["Movin' bricks like it's Black Friday"], "meaning": "#Rozay is selling a lot of drugs.\n\nA brick in street terms means a kilogram of some kind of drug, most likely crack cocaine. Black Friday is a day every year where major retailers offer promotional sales for a limited time. Usually a LOT of sales are made on Black Friday. Here is an article showcasing how Walmart had 10 million transactions in 4 hours.", "annotation_id": "2855512", "url": "https://genius.com/2855512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Wale a genius, Meek Mill a superstar", "vault_lines": ["Wale a genius, Meek Mill a superstar"], "meaning": "Ross is shouting out Wale and Meek Mill two rappers signed to Ross' Maybach Music Group. In 2011, both Wale and Meek Mill signed to Ross' MMG. Shortly after signing, both artists were featured on Self Made Vol. 1 which is a compilation album featuring some of MMG’s new artists.\n\n\n\nBoth Wale and Meek Mill then began working on their own solo projects under MMG, Dreamchasers by Meek Mill and Ambition by Wale. Also, Wale refers to himself as the Maybach Poetic Genius. He does this all throughout the song Legendary on the Ambition album. Ross is probably trying to garner some exposure for the two talents with this salute to the two.", "annotation_id": "2854348", "url": "https://genius.com/2854348", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "A lot of yellow tape, where that Obama care?", "vault_lines": ["A lot of yellow tape, where that Obama care?"], "meaning": "There is some clever word play in this line. Yellow tape is usually associated with some sort of crime scene, be it homicide, suicide, etc. The wordplay comes to surface when Ross says ‘why don’t Obama care?’. Obamacare, is an act which goal is to provide affordable and good quality healthcare, mostly aimed at less fortunate families. However, Obamacare fails to do what it has been advertised, it does not help the poorest of the poor. You can read about Obamacare’s failure to do what is intended here.\n\n\n\nRoss is imposing the question of why Obama isn’t helping the people deep in poverty. Usually, impoverished areas are linked to crime, thus Ross speaking about ‘yellow tape’. Here are some quick facts about the connection between poor areas and crime.", "annotation_id": "2852552", "url": "https://genius.com/2852552", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Two attempts on my life, they threatened venues", "vault_lines": ["Two attempts on my life, they threatened venues"], "meaning": "Rick Ross has had a couple attempts on his life. Most notably a drive-by shooting in Florida.\n\n\n\nRozay also references the Florida drive-by in the song Supreme where he says:\n\nTried to murder me while in mine so that’s how I survived", "annotation_id": "2852461", "url": "https://genius.com/2852461", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Bill Belichick, coachin’ and callin’ the shots", "vault_lines": ["Bill Belichick, coachin' and callin' the shots"], "meaning": "Rozay is a boss. He runs shit and is the main dude in charge, like legendary New England Patriots head coach Bill Belichick.\n\n\n\nNot the first time he’s mentioned the Patriots, in “Pop That” he takes a shot and mentions Spy Gate:\n\nFilm it, film it this bitch want me to film it\nBalling, balling like I play for New England", "annotation_id": "2852417", "url": "https://genius.com/2852417", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "It’s death row, conspiracy theories", "vault_lines": ["It's death row, conspiracy theories"], "meaning": "This line refers to 2Pac’s record label, Death Row Records, as well as all the conspiracy theories that surrounded his death and who killed him.", "annotation_id": "2850209", "url": "https://genius.com/2850209", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "So he put a hit on his cousin and auntie", "vault_lines": ["So he put a hit on his cousin and auntie"], "meaning": "Ross uses this story to show the listener that the and greed that people have in the hood tears families apart, causing people to spill their own blood.\n\nContinuing off the past line, the listener can assume that the protagonist of this story is possibly a former inmate, and has a “GABOS” mindset.\n\nThus, he wants his own cousin (and that cousin’s mother) dead because he believes they stole from him.", "annotation_id": "2850052", "url": "https://genius.com/2850052", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "Five shots to the stomach, 2Pac gift pack", "vault_lines": ["Five shots to the stomach, 2Pac gift pack"], "meaning": "In the infamous story,  2Pac was robbed and shot five times by three men in the lobby of Quad Recording Studios in Manhattan. Ross is gonna give you five shots to the stomach for screwing him over, what he calls the 2pac gift pack.", "annotation_id": "2849985", "url": "https://genius.com/2849985", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mafia Music III", "line": "GABOS, game ain’t based on sympathy", "vault_lines": ["GABOS, game ain’t based on sympathy"], "meaning": "GABOS, as Ross explains is an acronym for “Game Ain’t Based On Sympathy”.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3A1w7idxJpU\n\nThe term originated in the Miami prison system, and has gone on to a wider constituency from prisons to crime-ridden urban areas.\n\nIt’s a gladiatorial code based on strength and respect, where the most powerful inmate rules by sheer force of strength. The system is ruled by those with the strength (physical or in terms of respect) to control those around them and enforce their will. It describes the foundational outlook of inmate culture: to show an ‘every man for himself’, ‘no mercy’ attitude.\n\nThe term came to light in Louis Theroux’s Miami Mega Jail documentary.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_lEPsVDiLVs", "annotation_id": "2818717", "url": "https://genius.com/2818717", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
