{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marathon", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-marathon-lyrics", "genius_title": "Marathon", "genius_id": 7453673, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "Whippin’ work, it smelling like they dinner rolls", "vault_lines": ["Whippin' work, it smelling like they dinner rolls"], "meaning": "“Whipping work” is slang for cooking crack cocaine. Powder cocaine is dissolved in a mixture of water and either ammonia or baking soda. It’s then heated, cooled and cut up in small rocks. This process gives off a strong odor but as a professional, Ross gotten used to the smell, even comparing it to sweet dinner rolls.\n\nRoss also praised “whipping work” on the chorus of his 2009 song “B.M.F. (Blowin' Money Fast)”:\n\nI think I’m Big Meech, Larry Hoover\nWhippin' work, hallelujah", "annotation_id": "14970354", "url": "https://genius.com/14970354", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "2 Chainz, that’s your best answer Cup filled with pink, I’m supporting breast cancer I might do that walk for the 5k", "vault_lines": ["2 Chainz, that's your best answer", "Cup filled with pink, I'm supporting breast cancer", "I might do that walk for the 5k"], "meaning": "“Pink” is lean, a mix of codeine and soda, although the drink is traditionally purple. Pink is also the symbolic color for breast cancer awareness—director of the Color Association of the United States, Margaret Welch said:\n\nThe profile on pink is playful, life-affirming. We have studies as to its calming effect, its quieting effect, its lessening of stress. [Pastel pink] is a shade known to be health-giving; that’s why we have expressions like ‘in the pink.’ You can’t say a bad thing about it.” Pink is, in other words, everything cancer notably is not.\n\nA 5k run/walk is an event done to raise money for charity. In case 2 Chainz is willing to do the walk, he can go to the Annual Pink Ribbon Walk in his hometown of Georiga.", "annotation_id": "14969558", "url": "https://genius.com/14969558", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "Stuntin’ on them bitches like Birdman", "vault_lines": ["Stuntin' on them bitches like Birdman"], "meaning": "Birdman is Lil Wayne’s mentor and father figure in the music world. One of Birdman’s well-known nicknames is Stunna and surely is proud of using it. His third studio album was named 5 Star Stunna and has songs like “Born Stunna,” “Stunna” and the Big Tymers classic “#1 Stunna.”\n\nTo “stun” on someone means to flex on someone. Wayne used the same analogy on his 2006 collaboration with Birdman titled “Stuntin' Like My Daddy,” where he likens himself to his mentor:\n\nAnd I be stuntin' like my daddy, stuntin' like my daddy\nStuntin' like my daddy, I be stuntin' like my daddy\nI’m the Young Stunna, stuntin' like my daddy\nStuntin' like my daddy, I be stuntin' like my daddy", "annotation_id": "14963249", "url": "https://genius.com/14963249", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "We sell that white, sell that brown, call that rice and gravy", "vault_lines": ["We sell that white, sell that brown, call that rice and gravy"], "meaning": "“White” is slang for cocaine and “brown” is slang for heroin. Wayne doesn’t want to get caught selling drugs by the police so he gives his products food nicknames, respectively by their color.", "annotation_id": "14961483", "url": "https://genius.com/14961483", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "All you talk is shit, nigga, halitosis", "vault_lines": ["All you talk is shit, nigga, halitosis"], "meaning": "Halitosis  is chronic bad breath that cannot go anyway. It’s caused by bad oral hygiene, such as dental issues, infections, dry mouth and smoking tobacco. 2 Chainz takes “talking shit” to a literal standing point, comparing hate to bad breath.", "annotation_id": "14961404", "url": "https://genius.com/14961404", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "All my hoes pretty, all my niggas brazy", "vault_lines": ["All my hoes pretty, all my niggas brazy"], "meaning": "Bloods replace all words that start with “c” or with a “c” sound to “b.” It’s been claimed that Wayne had ties to the Bloods since he was a teenager, during his Hot Boyz days. While he hasn’t validated that claim, he has repped the gang on songs like “Go Brazy,” “Blooded” and has a mixtape titled Piru Dreams—in reference to the Piru Street Family, who makes most of the original Blood alliances in Los Angeles.", "annotation_id": "14960992", "url": "https://genius.com/14960992", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "Put it on the scale, watch it do numbers", "vault_lines": ["Put it on the scale, watch it do numbers"], "meaning": "When drugs are purchased, it’s weighed on a scale to make sure one’s receiving the correct amount. This ties into the “key/ki” wordplay in the line below.\n\nHowever, the line may serve as a metaphor for Wayne’s music. He has multiple platinum and gold albums under his belt—with his biggest being his critical acclaimed Tha Carter III, released in 2008. It sold over three million copies in the U.S—the highest of his career so far—and features Grammy-winning hits like “A Milli” and “Lollipop.”", "annotation_id": "14957790", "url": "https://genius.com/14957790", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "Red bottoms only for the centerfolds", "vault_lines": ["Red bottoms only for the centerfolds"], "meaning": "Red bottom soles are a trademark feature of Christian Louboutin shoes, each of which can run up $600 a pair.\n\nA “centerfold” is a term used to describe a beautiful woman. It refers to the centre of a magazine, usually with a double page spread of an incredibly beautiful (and usually naked) woman. The term was allegedly coined by Hugh Hefner, Playboy kingpin.\n\nRoss gives his expensive gifts only to his most beautiful woman.", "annotation_id": "12733018", "url": "https://genius.com/12733018", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "They on that bad batch, too much pork around it", "vault_lines": ["They on that bad batch, too much pork around it"], "meaning": "It’s kind of ambiguous here whether he says bitch or batch, or even whether he means both, but there are explanations for both of them:\n\n\nThis girl’s a bad bitch and saying she has so much pork, or meat on her ass.\nBad batch refers to there being too much baking soda in the crack. Bacon is made from pork, which is a homophone for bakin' (bakin' soda)", "annotation_id": "1267026", "url": "https://genius.com/1267026", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "Wet work, got the kitchen stanking", "vault_lines": ["Wet work, got the kitchen stanking"], "meaning": "Cocaine has a strong odor when cooked on the stove.", "annotation_id": "1253524", "url": "https://genius.com/1253524", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "Man, fuck all you niggas, pause!", "vault_lines": ["Man, fuck all you niggas, pause!"], "meaning": "Pause is the new “No Homo” used in the hiphop community. It dismisses any sexual or homosexual double meaning to something you say.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2YN_n2WuZhQ\n\nAlso notice the long pause before the word “pause”", "annotation_id": "1253447", "url": "https://genius.com/1253447", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "We call a undercover, a pig in the blanket", "vault_lines": ["We call a undercover, a pig in the blanket"], "meaning": "Double Entendre:\n\n\nPig in a blanket is a snack food. A “pig” is derogatory word for a police officer, and being it’s a undercover officer the  pig is in a blanket or “undercover”. Which leads us to a pig in a blanket!\nLil' Waynes claiming to be a cop killer.  The sheets they put over homicide victims would make a “pig” in a blanket\n\n\n\nThe ‘Pig in a blanket’ phrase was first coined in Spice 1’s 2010 song Smoke ‘em like a blunt", "annotation_id": "1253388", "url": "https://genius.com/1253388", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "Playing with them keys like Stevie Wonder", "vault_lines": ["Playing with them keys like Stevie Wonder"], "meaning": "This is a pun with the words “keys” and “kis”. Kis refers to kilos (kgs) of cocaine, and keys refers to piano keys like Stevie Wonder once skillfully played. So Wayne compares his skill with moving cocaine like Stevie on the piano.", "annotation_id": "1253199", "url": "https://genius.com/1253199", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "Call me Tunechi Lee or don’t call me at all", "vault_lines": ["Call me Tunechi Lee or don't call me at all"], "meaning": "It seems Tunechi/Tunechi Lee (short for Lionches) is Lil Wayne’s preferred nickname these days.\n\nPrevious, Wayne would say Weezy F baby please say the baby. If not don’t say it at all.", "annotation_id": "1253165", "url": "https://genius.com/1253165", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "Ted DiBiase in Versace loafers", "vault_lines": ["Ted DiBiase in Versace loafers"], "meaning": "World Wrestling Entertainment (WWE) legend Ted DiBiase’s nickname in the ring was “The Million Dollar Man”.", "annotation_id": "1253008", "url": "https://genius.com/1253008", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "Shoot you in ya head like Abraham Lincoln", "vault_lines": ["Shoot you in ya head like Abraham Lincoln"], "meaning": "In 1865, John Wilkes Booth shot President Abraham Lincoln in the head while the president was watching the play Our American Cousin at the Ford Theater.", "annotation_id": "1252832", "url": "https://genius.com/1252832", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "line": "Got ya bitch tip-toeing like Gregory Hines", "vault_lines": ["Got ya bitch tip-toeing like Gregory Hines"], "meaning": "Gregory Hines was a famous American dancer. He was known for his skill in tap dancing. He also starred in the movie The Cotton Club based in Harlem.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7N9E15P8604", "annotation_id": "1252825", "url": "https://genius.com/1252825", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Marble Floors", "status": "done", "targets": 17, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/French-montana-marble-floors-lyrics", "genius_title": "Marble Floors", "genius_id": 99610, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mark My Words", "line": "So familiar like Tookie Williams, I’m in the building", "vault_lines": ["So familiar like Tookie Williams, I'm in the building"], "meaning": "In gang culture Crips refer to each other as “Cuzz” which is short for cousin. Tookie Williams is the founder of the Crip Nation, which would make him family to all who follow his guidelines to being stand up men.\n\nhttps://images.app.goo.gl/2CTw4Hc9DEFoxp2P8", "annotation_id": "17786568", "url": "https://genius.com/17786568", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mark My Words", "line": "Young niggas in front the buildings tryna make a living", "vault_lines": ["Young niggas in front the buildings tryna make a living"], "meaning": "Nip in many interviews often told the story about how him and his friends and brother started out selling music out the back of his car in front of Slauson Tees (At the time. It’s now his own clothing store, The Marathon Clothing) on Crenshaw and Slauson.", "annotation_id": "17060425", "url": "https://genius.com/17060425", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mark My Words", "line": "Faced with this decision it’s a funny feeling Knowing when you tell the truth they gon’ come and kill you", "vault_lines": ["Faced with this decision it's a funny feeling", "Knowing when you tell the truth they gon' come and kill you"], "meaning": "It’s a funny feeling knowing as much as Nip knows about the shady secrets of industry and government, knowing that if he tells these truths about these industries, he could risk being hunted down and killed.\n\nFast forward 2019, Nip is shot and killed on March 31st. Theorists on his death say that Nip was killed because he had plans of releasing a documentary about the holistic doctor, Dr. Sebi’s trials when he was taken into court for distributing medicines not approved by medical professionals. The case with his medicine however was that it was curing people of aids, cancer and all other dangerous diseases that the medical officials supposedly didn’t have the cure to. Dr. Sebi had two trials and beat both.", "annotation_id": "17022815", "url": "https://genius.com/17022815", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mark My Words", "line": "Suburban tinted, five or less percentage", "vault_lines": ["Suburban tinted, five or less percentage"], "meaning": "Nip uses a clever play on words to represent how he’s risen in class and status. His rise in status has given him a “suburban tint.” He then goes on to say “five of that’s percentage” to allude to a his car being tinted because of his hard work to get to this point in his life.\n\n\n\nA lot of celebs get 5% tint on their cars, usually to keep people from looking inside. Do to them being famous, it can be annoying having everyone recognize you while you just want to go home.", "annotation_id": "17022763", "url": "https://genius.com/17022763", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mark My Words", "line": "Look, grind strenuous, cash out continuous Mogul in the making, autonomous black businesses", "vault_lines": ["Mogul in the making, autonomous black businesses"], "meaning": "Nipsey pictures what black excellence looks like at it’s peak. Cashing out money and black businesses owned by blacks only.", "annotation_id": "17022712", "url": "https://genius.com/17022712", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mark My Words", "line": "’85 Cutlass, I’ll make my swerves", "vault_lines": ["'85 Cutlass, I'll make my swerves"], "meaning": "Here Nipsey mentions his favorite car, a 1985 Oldsmobile Cutlass. Old school cars are a popular item in Nipsey’s home town of Los Angeles, and are starting to make their return to rap.", "annotation_id": "10837417", "url": "https://genius.com/10837417", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
