{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mastermind Unfolds Like A True Crime: The Album’s Top Lyrics", "line": "The disbandment of the Boobie Boys gang", "vault_lines": [". The Bawse starts off by callously describing the disbandment of the Boobie Boys gang"], "meaning": "Following Boobie Williams' arrest & sentencing, the Boobie Boys began to disband. Ricky rapped about his capture in his debut single, Hustlin':\n\nWhen they snatched Black, I cried for a hundred nights\nHe got a hundred bodies, serving a hundred lives", "annotation_id": "2875229", "url": "https://genius.com/2875229", "state": "verified", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Mastermind Unfolds Like A True Crime: The Album’s Top Lyrics", "line": "Throughout the album, Ross speaks on his days with the Boobie Boys, but no lyric is more powerful than this", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Rozay was once not only a prominent member of the Boobie Boys gang, he was the founder’s right hand man. He refers to the Boobie Boys (mainly their leader, Kenneth Williams) a couple times throughout the album:\n\n\n“I studied Kenneth Williams, I’m one hell of a student”\n“Plenty money orders, that what uncle Kenny taught us”", "annotation_id": "2875202", "url": "https://genius.com/2875202", "state": "verified", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
