{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "You know what happen when G.O.O.D. Music and MMG get together, right?", "vault_lines": ["You know what happen when G.O.O.D. Music and MMG get together, right?"], "meaning": "G.O.O.D. Music is the record label that Pusha T is signed to and MMG is an abbreviation of Maybach Music, a record label founded by Rick Ross.", "annotation_id": "19857910", "url": "https://genius.com/19857910", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "My record sales ain’t much as theirs And we still ride the same coupes How we still fuck the same hoes Why we still buy the same clothes How we both got the same watch I’m just keepin’ y’all on y’all toes", "vault_lines": ["My record sales ain't much as theirs", "And we still ride the same coupes", "How we still fuck the same hoes", "Why we still buy the same clothes", "How we both got the same watch"], "meaning": "I’ve got to say these things because people still talk about how much an album sold. But the music business has changed so much.\n\nEverybody is so victimized— all the platinum rappers. Everybody’s been stolen from, everybody ain’t got no money, everybody in foreclosure, and everybody’s in debt. Everybody’s thinking, “what happened?” Nobody ever took no car for me or none of that shit.\n\nI wish all the talk about numbers would stop because I feel like other guys would be appreciated so much more. Look at Sean Price— rest in peace. Look at how everybody is like, “Yo, dude is nice.”\n\nOh really, now? When you get into the numbers game, that stops the conversation.", "annotation_id": "8507113", "url": "https://genius.com/8507113", "state": "verified", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "Y’all gotta beg my pardon on it But this shit sound like God, don’t it?", "vault_lines": ["Y'all gotta beg my pardon on it", "But this shit sound like God, don't it?"], "meaning": "I relate God to power a lot— and powerful feelings, powerful energy.\n\nIf God had a human voice he would sound like James Earl Jones.", "annotation_id": "8507111", "url": "https://genius.com/8507111", "state": "verified", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "Millions-millions in the ceiling, millions-millions in the ceiling Millions-millions in the ceiling, millions-millions in the ceiling", "vault_lines": ["Millions-millions in the ceiling, millions-millions in the ceiling"], "meaning": "Who the fuck knows how many millions you can hide in the ceiling before it caves in on you?\n\n“Millions in the ceiling actually” comes from a guy around my way who was like five years older than me.\n\nWhen we were younger there was a conversation going, thinking we got money, and he was like, “Man, if you haven’t got a quarter million put up – I am not talking about a quarter million that you play with, I am talking about you need a quarter million put up. Put it up in the ceiling— then you play with this bag over here. Now you have got some coins.”", "annotation_id": "8507109", "url": "https://genius.com/8507109", "state": "verified", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "I got this I got that, I got that I got this", "vault_lines": ["I got this I got that, I got that I got this"], "meaning": "In using epanodos (which Ross has done before), Ross tells the listener what he has – drugs of course.\n\nBut he censors himself, so he refers to the drugs as only “this” and “that”. As he said in the previous line – he wishes he could say more but can’t, because he knows the feds are listening.", "annotation_id": "1468925", "url": "https://genius.com/1468925", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "Pop tags when I’m paranoid ’Cause the pawn shop was my paradise", "vault_lines": ["Pop tags when I'm paranoid", "'Cause the pawn shop was my paradise"], "meaning": "https://players.brightcove.net/4863540648001/HklmClHkx_default/index.html?videoId=5237590745001\n\nFirst of all, it is a play on the P’s. When you are flowing in records like this you are trying to still say a lot. You are trying to still say a lot and you are still trying to make sense. And playing off the P’s sort of helps that go along with people understanding what you’re saying. But the meaning behind it is just that popping tags is something that people can associate with and you got something fresh and you got something new. And it is basically saying like you are always going to feel this feeling. The pawn shop used to be a place where you might frequent to get some money, you might be pawning a chain or pawning a watch or whatever. So I have been there before and I have been here and I am trying to keep this feeling going.", "annotation_id": "1461511", "url": "https://genius.com/1461511", "state": "verified", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "Did drugs with Donatella,", "vault_lines": ["Did drugs with Donatella,"], "meaning": "Donatella refers to Donatella Versace. The current Vice-President of the Versace Group – a high end fashion label from Italy. Her older brother, the founder of the Versace label, Gianni, was murdered, and afterwards she became creative director and thanks to Gianni’s will, her daughter, Allegra inherited 50% of the company. Ross is chilling with the wealthiest people in the world and you know what those parties are like right…", "annotation_id": "1398839", "url": "https://genius.com/1398839", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "Soft loafer preferred,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Adds a touch of luxury to any outfit. Ross is no different and prefers soft supple leather on his loafers.", "annotation_id": "1398814", "url": "https://genius.com/1398814", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "Make a nigga mash on the gas, floor", "vault_lines": ["Make a nigga mash on the gas, floor"], "meaning": "This can work both ways;\n\n\nHis music is so terrifying it’ll make someone drive away scared\nThis is the type of music you ride to (at least when you have a good sound system) and wanna put the pedal to the medal. The music people asked for.", "annotation_id": "1398710", "url": "https://genius.com/1398710", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "Blue tops, blue tops,", "vault_lines": ["Blue tops, blue tops,"], "meaning": "Different batches of crack are known by the colors on the top of their vials. Blue tops, orange tops, etc.  Let Raekwon tell you all about it\n\nBlue tops is a reference to crack or cocaine, but also a direct reference to HBO show The Wire – which Pusha constantly refers to. A full breakdown of ‘The Wire’ slang\n\nHe references the show multiple times throughout the mixtape. As well as his upcoming album “My Name Is My Name”, which is a direct quote from Marlo Stanfield, a prominent character on the show.\n\n\n\nAlso begins another use of Alliteration with the letter B", "annotation_id": "1398686", "url": "https://genius.com/1398686", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "This big face is in Blu-Ray And these black diamonds like Boondocks", "vault_lines": ["And these black diamonds like Boondocks"], "meaning": "His watch, most likely a Rolex is in “High Definition” – much like a blu-ray disc. See his two Rolex’s on instagram here. The black diamonds he posses on the bezel of the watch are like Thugnificent’s that he wears in the TV comic show – The Boondocks.\n\n\n\nInterestingly enough the initial story line of the show matches up with how Pusha’s early life went. He was born in New York City, in the Bronx and then moved to Virginia Beach and a predominately white suburb. In the TV show the Freeman family having moved from the South Side of Chicago, Illinois to the fictional, peaceful and mostly white suburb of Woodcrest.\n\nShouts out to Butters who helped with the team effort in Editor chat on this one!", "annotation_id": "1398627", "url": "https://genius.com/1398627", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "I was dead pop when that powder came For that knot saved in that shoebox", "vault_lines": ["I was dead pop when that powder came", "For that knot saved in that shoebox"], "meaning": "“If a player is all-in and their bet is called or raised by other active players, remaining bets go into a side pot. Since the main pot cannot be added to, it is termed a dead pot.” c\n\nHe was all in and people were coming for the drugs he was selling.\n\nIn addition, the knot is referring to a method of storing cocaine; Pusha got some hidden in his shoebox.", "annotation_id": "1398596", "url": "https://genius.com/1398596", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "And them hoes with angel faces, cryin’ loud with ill intentions Just so I can buy them Christians Have ‘em shittin’ on all they bitches, ah!", "vault_lines": ["And them hoes with angel faces, cryin' loud with ill intentions", "Just so I can buy them Christians", "Have 'em shittin' on all they bitches, ah!"], "meaning": "Love the wordplay here.\n\nHe juxtaposes good and evil by referring to girls with angel faces – meaning they look gorgeous but have ill intentions. Furthermore displayed in the Bible when angels have sinned they are deemed Fallen Angels and are often depicted shrieking – hence they’re cryin' loud. Something else to pay attention to is that another name for the Devil is; The Fallen Angel.\n\n\n\nAlso note the play on Christians, as this ties in with angels as a reference to the faith. To complete the homonym on Christians this line implies he’s going to buy them Christian Louboutin shoes so they can stunt on all the other bitches. It is also interesting to note Christian Louboutins are often referred to as Red Bottoms – the colour is associated with the devil.\n\n\n\nThis basically creates a paradox – they may be Christian shoes but the devil is wearing them!!!", "annotation_id": "1398576", "url": "https://genius.com/1398576", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "I restore the feelin’ of when niggas made a killin’ Hidin’ choppas in the closet, half a million in the ceiling", "vault_lines": ["I restore the feelin' of when niggas made a killin'", "Hidin' choppas in the closet, half a million in the ceiling"], "meaning": "He’s bringing back the real drug dealer street element that he feels has been absent from the rap game. Back when cocaine was at it’s most popular point, the drug lord made more money than anyone else in the world!\n\nPusha actually dealt drugs when 90% of the emcees who spit about it never did – ironically one of those “imposters” is said to be Rick Ross who features on this track and has collaborated with Pusha in the past.", "annotation_id": "1398505", "url": "https://genius.com/1398505", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "Arm & Hammer in the kitchen", "vault_lines": ["Got the razor on the counter, Arm & Hammer in the kitchen"], "meaning": "Arm & Hammer is a brand of baking soda. The baking soda is mixed with the cocaine to create *crack – something entirely different to cocaine.\n\n\n\nAdding baking soda to cocaine actually makes it cheaper. See, by chopping and cooking the coke with baking soda it decreases the purity thus making it less expensive, and now anyone can afford a costly drug like cocaine in the form of crack (and addict don’t have to scrape up more than $5 to get there fix). Crack is actually much more profitable than cocaine even though it is cheaper to buy. This is because you are still getting coke and baking soda, so the baking soda is overpriced.", "annotation_id": "1398457", "url": "https://genius.com/1398457", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "Finger fuck a Ferrari, south of France early morning", "vault_lines": ["Finger fuck a Ferrari, south of France early morning"], "meaning": "The Bawse is using the finger push start button in his Ferrari in the south of France (probably in hotspots like Cannes, Saint Tropez, etc) in the wee hours of the morning after a long night out. He may also be referring to changing gears with the car’s paddle shifters located behind the steering wheel, as witnessed by his gestures in the video.\n\n\n\n\n\nAlso,notice Ross’s use of an alliteration:\n\n“Used to fiddle my finger ‘til I found me a fortune\nFinger fuckin’ Ferrari, souf of France early morning”\n\nRoss employs alliteration quite often, and his emphatic delivery makes them especially noticeable.", "annotation_id": "1398431", "url": "https://genius.com/1398431", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "This that shit that y’all wanted This shit cook up hard, don’t it?", "vault_lines": ["This shit cook up hard, don't it?"], "meaning": "Nice way to start the track, with a double entendre!\n\n\nFirstly imagine he’s the dealer – yourself the customer. He’s talking about cooking up his cocaine (hard) and how good it is.\nSecondly imagine he’s the rapper – yourself the listener. This is the music the fans have been waiting so long for – dope music.", "annotation_id": "1398394", "url": "https://genius.com/1398394", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Millions", "line": "Call my Phantom the holy ghost Church on chrome wheel tires, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Call my Phantom the holy ghost", "Church on chrome wheel tires, nigga"], "meaning": "None other than Rolls Royce makes both The Phantom & The Ghost models. Also note the play on holy ghost & church", "annotation_id": "1398330", "url": "https://genius.com/1398330", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
