{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money and Powder", "status": "done", "targets": 10, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-money-and-powder-lyrics", "genius_title": "Money and Powder", "genius_id": 2296152, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I was on top when that shit meant a lot Still on top like I’m scared of the drop Still on top and these niggas wanna swap", "vault_lines": ["I was on top when that shit meant a lot", "Still on top like I'm scared of the drop", "Still on top and these niggas wanna swap"], "meaning": "These lines are subliminal disses at Kendrick. Drake often used the name of Kendricks label at the time “Top Dawg Entertainment” to sub Kendrick. He says he still on “Top” & “they wanna swap”. As in, trade or spar. Swap also could be a reference to the now closed “Compton Swap Meet” a once famous flea market in Compton. Then he references Slauson & Watts in the next line, which are two famous neighborhoods in Los Angeles County, such as Compton. Drake also say states “Still on top like I’m scared of the drop”. Which was one of Drake’s pokes at Kendrick during their beef. Telling him to “drop something”, see Push Ups & Taylor Made Freestyle. Not to mention, this beat also has a West Coast feel to it, which can be another dig.", "annotation_id": "39881645", "url": "https://genius.com/39881645", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "When you niggas thinkin’ small in the mall with a rat (Rat)", "vault_lines": ["When you niggas thinkin' small in the mall with a rat (Rat)"], "meaning": "Ross is saying that his doubters and enemies aren’t even close to being on his level here. By “rat” he means someone who snitches, but this line is also a play on words with the term mallrat, used to describe someone who loiters around the mall all day. The phrase was popularized by the 1995 Kevin Smith cult classic film “Mallrats,” starring Jason Lee, Ben Affleck, and Shannen Doherty.", "annotation_id": "17523087", "url": "https://genius.com/17523087", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Bury my motherfuckin’ Chase Bank, time to bounce (Bounce)", "vault_lines": ["Bury my motherfuckin' Chase Bank, time to bounce (Bounce)"], "meaning": "Making a play on the track’s theme of burying Drake and Rick Ross with their money to take it to the afterlife, Ross expands the metaphor, claiming that he wants his whole bank account to be buried with him. Chase Bank is a national bank that deals with credit cards, mortgages, retirement planning and much more.", "annotation_id": "17396611", "url": "https://genius.com/17396611", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Rickey Smiley’s in Decatur with the yé Lil’ nigga, just another state case", "vault_lines": ["Rickey Smiley's in Decatur with the yé", "Lil' nigga, just another state case"], "meaning": "Ricky Smiley is a stand-up comedian and actor, who’s known for his prank phone calls. Rick Ross has featured on Smiley’s TV programme The Rickey Smiley Morning Show, and when Ross was hospitalized in March 2018, there was a segment in the Hip Hop Spot category of the show that addressed rumours of how he faced the health scare.\n\nDecatur is a city in Georgia; in January 2017, Ross opened a new Wingstop in the city. The store was the company’s 1000th branch opened and Ross has often mentioned his love for Wingstop; for example, in “The Devil is a Lie”, he rapped:\n\nWingstop, fat boy need a 10 piece\n\n“Yé” is a slang term meaning cocaine—Ross is talking about small-time jokers (Ricky Smileys) selling coke in Decatur who are about to be state cases because of the foolish moves they make.", "annotation_id": "17396506", "url": "https://genius.com/17396506", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I really gotta put a couple niggas in they place Really just lapped every nigga in the race", "vault_lines": ["I really gotta put a couple niggas in they place", "Really just lapped every nigga in the race"], "meaning": "This could be a dig at former rappers that Drizzy’s had beef with, most recently being Pusha T.\n\nDrake’s compared the rap game to a race before on “Sacrifices”:\n\nIt’s a marathon, not a sprint, but I still gotta win the race, yeah\n\nBy saying he lapped other rappers in the race that is the rap game, Drake is indicating that what is new for other rappers, he has already accomplished, as he is that far ahead.", "annotation_id": "17396448", "url": "https://genius.com/17396448", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I really might tat “Realest Nigga” on my face", "vault_lines": ["I really might tat \"Realest Nigga\" on my face"], "meaning": "Over the years, Drake has faced criticism for being fake, especially when he was accused of having a ghostwriter in the form of Quentin Miller, who threatened to expose him. The allegations were first made by Philadelphia rapper Meek Mill which sparked the two’s now dead beef. Funkmaster Flex even contributed to the scandal, airing a whole series of “reference tracks” that Drake later used. However, Drake defended himself:\n\nMusic can be a collaborative process. I need, sometimes, individuals to spark an idea so that I can take off running. […] I’m not ashamed.\n\nThat said, Drake here says that he’s the most real rapper in the scene, and this self-given title could be made even more credible with his honesty in the ghostwriting scandal.", "annotation_id": "17396418", "url": "https://genius.com/17396418", "state": "accepted", "votes": 52, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Rich niggas and I’m really bein’ modest", "vault_lines": ["Rich niggas and I'm really bein' modest"], "meaning": "As of 2019, Rick Ross' net worth was estimated to be around $35 million. He’s made a lot of this money from his label Maybach Music Group, as well as making classic hits like “The Devil is a Lie” and “Stay Scheming”. However, Ross ironically claims that he’s actually being modest, and that he has a lot more to boast about.", "annotation_id": "17396401", "url": "https://genius.com/17396401", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "The homies quiet, but we all smoke the loudest (Loudest)", "vault_lines": ["The homies quiet, but we all smoke the loudest (Loudest)"], "meaning": "Here, Rick Ross juxtaposes the slang term of “smoking loud”, which means smoking high-quality marijuana, with his homies keeping their cool.", "annotation_id": "17396391", "url": "https://genius.com/17396391", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I got two phones, one need a charge", "vault_lines": ["I got two phones, one need a charge"], "meaning": "Drake references Kevin Gates’s November 2015 song “2 Phones”:\n\nI got two phones, one for the plug and one for the load\nI got two phones, one for the bitches and one for the dough\nThink I need two more, line bumpin’ I’m ring, ring, ringin’\n\nThe line also references Drake’s July 2015 track, “Charged Up,” which features a iPhone charging screen on it’s cover art. On the diss track aimed at Meek Mill, Drake rapped:\n\nI’m charged up\nOh no we eatin', niggas ain’t about to starve us\nNah, niggas ain’t about to starve us\nI’m charged up\n\nDrake often juggles work and his personal relationships, and has a phone for each part of his life. Just like a phone in need of a charge, when Drake focuses too much of his time on one aspect of his life, the other suffers.", "annotation_id": "17396360", "url": "https://genius.com/17396360", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Thought my raps wasn’t facts ’til they sat with the bars", "vault_lines": ["Thought my raps wasn't facts 'til they sat with the bars"], "meaning": "Drake alludes to Meek Mill’s July 2015 Twitter rant in which he infamously accused Drake of working with a ghostwriter and not writing his own raps. He quickly responded with two diss tracks against Meek, “Charged Up” and “Back to Back,” the latter of which received a Grammy nomination. Drizzy is widely considered to have won the unexpected beef with Meek and re-established himself as a true lyricist despite the accusations.\n\nIn late 2018, Drake and Meek officially ended their beef. However, the ghostwriting accusations were still a topic of concern for others, such as Pusha-T, who dissed him on his May 2018 track, “Infrared”:\n\nThe game’s fucked up\nNiggas' beats is bangin', nigga, ya hooks did it\nThe lyric pennin' equal the Trumps winnin'\nThe bigger question is how the Russians did it\nIt was written like Nas, but it came from Quentin\n\nOn the same day as the song’s release, Drake replied with “Duppy Freestyle”:\n\nWhoever supposedly makin' me hits\nBut then got no hits sound like they need me\nMy hooks did it, my lyrics did it\nMy spirit did it, I’m fearless with it, yeah\n\nOthers came to the defense of Drake’s writing ability, such as Boi-1da, who, in an April 2018 interview, said:\n\nI think Drake is literally one of the greatest songwriters of all time, and he writes his own songs. And for someone to try and discredit somebody who I’ve literally seen write songs like he’s writing an essay, it bothers me because I’m close to it. I know he writes his own songs.", "annotation_id": "17396345", "url": "https://genius.com/17396345", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Thinkin’ back to the fact that they dead", "vault_lines": ["Thinkin' back to the fact that they dead"], "meaning": "In September 2017, OVO affiliate, Anthony Soares, was gunned down in Toronto. In honor of his late friend, Drake had a custom jewelry piece made by Seattle Gold, with the inscription “Rest In Piece Anthony Soares.”\n\n\n\nDrake shared a message on the loss of his friend through a September 2017 Instagram post:\n\nRIP to one of our family members…our brother… I still can’t even believe this morning was real. It was a honor to have shared years together and I will always keep your memory alive", "annotation_id": "17396306", "url": "https://genius.com/17396306", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Yeah, they twins, I could tell they ass apart", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, they twins, I could tell they ass apart"], "meaning": "Identical twins are two people who share a womb and originate from a single zygote that split. They typically share the same physical characteristics and are indistinguishable to most people.\n\nWhile figuring out the identity of a twin is considered difficult for some, Drake’s expertise with women allows him to accurately decide. He also boasts that he had sexual intercourse with each of the sisters, allowing him to distinguish them.\n\nDrake previously rapped about identical twins during his feature on Rihanna’s January 2016 track, “Work”:\n\nIf you had a twin, I would still choose you\n\nDrizzy also likely references the Lejonhjärta twins, Elizabeth and Victoria. They are identical twin sisters from Sweden who have been affiliated with Drake and modeled for OVO for years. They even appeared in the “Nice for What” music video.", "annotation_id": "17396287", "url": "https://genius.com/17396287", "state": "accepted", "votes": 56, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "In the next life, I’m tryna stay paid When I die, put my money in the grave", "vault_lines": ["In the next life, I'm tryna stay paid", "When I die, put my money in the grave"], "meaning": "Drake revives the ancient notion that you can bring belongings with you beyond death, and claims that he wants to be buried with his money to take it to the afterlife.\n\nDrake rapped contrary lyrics on “Pop That” (which also featured Rick Ross):\n\nMoney coming, money going, ain’t like you can take it with you\n\nHe also discussed his prerogative about money on his The Weeknd assisted November 2011 track, “Crew Love”:\n\nTell them I'ma need reservations for 20\nI’ve never really been one for the preservation of money, nah\nMuch rather spend it all while I’m breathing", "annotation_id": "17396245", "url": "https://genius.com/17396245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I got the hookup and there’s really no limit (Limit)", "vault_lines": ["I got the hookup and there's really no limit (Limit)"], "meaning": "Ross fires off a quick reference to the 1998 movie I Got The Hook Up, which was written by and starred Master P.\n\nI Got the Hook Up was just one contributing factor in the legendary run that Master P’s No Limit Records had in the year 1998.", "annotation_id": "17396221", "url": "https://genius.com/17396221", "state": "pending", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "You could DM my accountant My per diem six figures and I’m countin’", "vault_lines": ["You could DM my accountant", "My per diem six figures and I'm countin'"], "meaning": "“Per Diem” (Latin for per day or for each day) refers to the amount of money an organisation gives its employees to cover their daily expenses; i.e. lodging, meals & incidental expenses.\n\n\n\nRick Ross is boasting of a wealthy lifestyle in which he spends 6 figures ($100,000 or more) daily for the previously outlined expenses.", "annotation_id": "17396183", "url": "https://genius.com/17396183", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I got Lil Max with me, he the wave", "vault_lines": ["I got Lil Max with me, he the wave"], "meaning": "In this line, Drake kills two birds with one stone by simultaneously shouting out his young OVO affiliate Max and the incarcerated New York City rapper Max B, the latter of whom is known for coining the terms “wave” and “wavy” in hip-hop lexicon.\n\nMax B’s status as the originator of the term wave became a hotly debated topic after it sparked a Twitter feud between Kanye West and Wiz Khalifa and eventually led to West including Max B on West’s Life of Pablo track “Siiiiiiiiilver Surffffeeeeer Intermission.”\n\nDrake has previously shouted out Max, notably on 2018’s “Elevate”:\n\nI see the future when I get wavy (Wavy God!)", "annotation_id": "17396121", "url": "https://genius.com/17396121", "state": "accepted", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Ross got it (Maybach Music)", "vault_lines": ["Ross got it (Maybach Music)"], "meaning": "Maybach Music Group (MMG) is a record label imprint of Atlantic Records founded by featured artist Rick Ross. Originally, MMG was an imprint of Def Jam Recordings until 2011 when it moved to Warner Music Group and finally Atlantic in 2013. Alongside Ross, MMG is home to Wale and Meek Mill, the latter of which featured Drake on November 2018’s “Going Bad.”\n\nRick Ross and Drake have collaborated many times before, most notably on Ross' August 2012 track, “Diced Pineapples,” April 2012’s “Stay Schemin',” with Wale, and Drake’s November 2011 track “Lord Knows.” The duo also notably appeared alongside Lil Wayne on DJ Khaled’s May 2011 release “I’m On One,” April 2013 track “No New Friends” and French Montana’s June 2012 collaboration “Pop That.”", "annotation_id": "17396113", "url": "https://genius.com/17396113", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Nine figures was the goal ’til I hit it", "vault_lines": ["Nine figures was the goal 'til I hit it"], "meaning": "Rick Ross’s net worth is currently estimated to be approximately $35 million, indicating that Ross still needs a significant amount of money in order to achieve his prior goal of $100 million.\n\nHe previously rapped about his net worth on his June 2017 track “Glory of War,” when his net worth was in the hundred thousands:\n\n8 figure nigga, tell me how those numbers sound\nHit the Bay and let Berner know that I want a pound", "annotation_id": "17396109", "url": "https://genius.com/17396109", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Niggas wanna swap like it’s Slauson or Watts", "vault_lines": ["Niggas wanna swap like it's Slauson or Watts"], "meaning": "The Slauson Super Mall, also known as the Slauson Swap Meet, is a famous shopping center located at the intersection of Slauson and Western Avenues in Los Angeles.  Drake references the “swap meet” to make a play on words as he boasts that his adversaries would like to “swap” places with him.", "annotation_id": "17396102", "url": "https://genius.com/17396102", "state": "pending", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Got the trap jumpin’ like Zion when I rebound it Then I’m out (Then I’m out)", "vault_lines": ["Got the trap jumpin' like Zion when I rebound it", "Then I'm out (Then I'm out)"], "meaning": "Zion Williamson is a former one-and-done Duke basketball star and rising basketball sensation. As a freshman in college, Zion averaged 8.9 total rebounds per game, an impressive accomplishment in collegiate athletics.\n\nHe is also considered one of the most athletic players to enter the draft in years and has a max vertical jump of approximately 40 inches, which set a new record for Duke.", "annotation_id": "17396090", "url": "https://genius.com/17396090", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Mob ties, so I’m always good where I’m at Word to Junior, Jazzy, Baby J", "vault_lines": ["Mob ties, so I'm always good where I'm at", "Word to Junior, Jazzy, Baby J"], "meaning": "Drake probably references his June 2018 track “Mob Ties,” where he severed ties with everyone that may have been disingenuous towards him. Drake implies that he’d rather stay where he is than change for someone else.\n\n“Mob” is also short for mafia: that could be a nod to Drake’s other song on the EP, “Omertà”, which takes its name from the Law of Omertà, a code of silence used by the mafia when dealing with the government and often imposed to common people, so they won’t denounce some committed crimes in order not to being persecuted or murdered.\n\nAlso, “Junior (J. Prince Jr.),” “Jazzy (Jas Prince),” and “Baby J” are the nicknames of the sons of respected Houston rap executive James Prince (aka J. Prince).  According to Vice:\n\nJas Prince discovered Drake from the days of the Toronto rapper releasing tracks on MySpace. […] Jas brought Drake not only to Lil Wayne, but also to his father. That’s why J. Prince’s name is included as executive producer for Thank Me Later & Nothing Was The Same.\n\nJ. Prince is a known defender and protector of Drake and other artists. For instance, in March 2019, after YBN Almighty Jay, whose crew YBN has been put under J. Prince’s protection as well, was beaten and robbed in New York City, J. Prince–in a lengthy Instagram post–called upon his “mob ties” to get the chain back.\n\n\n\nAccording to a subsequent Instagram post by the older Prince, he and his son Jas did ultimately get YBN Almighty Jay’s chain back:\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BvMqzZZFdGD/", "annotation_id": "17396019", "url": "https://genius.com/17396019", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I got my niggas ‘cross the street livin’ large", "vault_lines": ["I got my niggas 'cross the street livin' large"], "meaning": "Drake lives on a secluded street in Hidden Hills, a small town near Calabasas, California. His neighborhood is home to a wide variety of stars and celebrities, since the houses are lavish and expensive.\n\nDrizzy previously mentioned sharing success with those who supported him on his June 2018 track, “Elevate”:\n\nSlowly, my mouth is goin' off, I don’t know patience\nI got a chance then my niggas got it too like it’s contagious\n\nMoreover, this could allude to famous rapper Kanye West, who also lives in Hidden Hills. The two were engaged in a back-and-forth rap beef in 2018, which included multiple subliminal disses and Tweets.\n\nDrake previously rapped about his close proximity on his January 2016 track, “Summer Sixteen”:\n\nNow I got a house in LA, now I got a bigger pool than Ye\nAnd look man, Ye’s pool is nice, mine’s just bigger’s what I’m saying\nI’m that nigga’s what I’m saying", "annotation_id": "17394564", "url": "https://genius.com/17394564", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I used to save hoes with a mask and a cape Now I’m like, “Nah, love, I’m good, go away”", "vault_lines": ["I used to save hoes with a mask and a cape", "Now I'm like, \"Nah, love, I'm good, go away\""], "meaning": "Drake compares himself to a superhero in these lines as vigilantes typically wear disguises to conceal their identity in comics.\n\nDrake echoes the sentiments of E-40’s Classic 1993 track “Captain Save A Hoe”:\n\nLook up in the sky it’s a bird it’s a plane\nWhat’s that nigga’s name?\nCaptain save a ho, mayne!\nMore faster than a speeding bullet\nTo put on his cape his card\n\nDrake also potentially alludes to the consistent themes of female empowerment and self love that is present throughout his discography. In April 2018, Drake released “Nice For What” which reached critical acclaim for its inspiring themes and positive depiction of women. The accompanying visuals were also praised for showing strong, independent women that were able to rise to fame and fortune on their own.\n\nIn addition, he juxtaposes the way he used to prefer the company of needy women to his newfound interest in independent females. He rapped a similar line on his 2016 track “Redemption”:\n\nWhy do I want an independent woman\nTo feel like she needs me?—I lost my way\n\nDrake also references Future’s famous 2018 text interaction with an Instagram model who was attempting to play him. The woman, Shamartess Monique Whisett, claimed that he invited her to his house for sexual intercourse but when she refused, he wouldn’t purchase plane tickets for her to return home. She called him out in texts, to which he replied:\n\nI’m good luv. Enjoy", "annotation_id": "17393851", "url": "https://genius.com/17393851", "state": "accepted", "votes": 105, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "Yeah, okay Lil CC on the beat, mmm Yeah, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Lil CC on the beat, mmm"], "meaning": "Drake shouts out American model Cydney Christine, who serves as the main producer on the track. She confirmed her status as the primary producer after the initial release was teased.\n\nThis track marks the first collaboration between Drake and Christine, as well as the first time she has been credited as a producer.", "annotation_id": "17393717", "url": "https://genius.com/17393717", "state": "accepted", "votes": 179, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "line": "I got hoes that I’m keepin’ in the dark", "vault_lines": ["I got hoes that I'm keepin' in the dark"], "meaning": "This line could be a reference to the controversy surrounding Sophie Brussaux, a pornstar that Drake secretly had a child with. Pusha T infamously made the world aware of this fact on his May 2018 diss track “The Story Of Adidon”:\n\nSince you name-dropped my fiancée\nLet ‘em know who you chose as your Beyoncé\nSophie knows better as your baby mother\nCleaned her up for IG, but the stench is on her\nA baby’s involved, it’s deeper than rap\nWe talkin’ character, let me keep with the facts\nYou are hiding a child, let that boy come home\nDeadbeat mothafucka playin' border patrol, ooh\n\nDrake then responded to these allegations on his June 2018 track “Emotionless”:\n\nI wasn’t hidin' my kid from the world\nI was hidin' the world from my kid\nFrom empty souls who just wake up and look to debate\nUntil you starin' at your seed, you can never relate", "annotation_id": "17392949", "url": "https://genius.com/17392949", "state": "accepted", "votes": 83, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Money in the Grave", "status": "done", "targets": 25, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Drake-money-in-the-grave-lyrics", "genius_title": "Money in the Grave", "genius_id": 4629364, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monkey Suit", "line": "Once I’m on they ass now, I picked up that poetry Remaster my old flows, let me bust this door key", "vault_lines": ["Remaster my old flows, let me bust this door key"], "meaning": "PROPOSED SUGGESTION: Remaster my old flows, let these bustas know it’s me", "annotation_id": "14359077", "url": "https://genius.com/14359077", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monkey Suit", "line": "Backed ‘em up from out my hood cause they flags confederate First they give it to us, then they lock up us for selling it", "vault_lines": ["Backed ‘em up from out my hood cause they flags confederate", "First they give it to us, then they lock up us for selling it"], "meaning": "Here, he explains the historical development of the hood, which is basically an alienated community of minorities living in poverty. His next line references the various accounts of the United States' and its local governments' corroboration with a plethora of cartels and political groups in facilitating drug smuggling. There is plenty of documented evidence to show the extent the U.S. went in order to allow cocaine to flood the inner cities in the 80’s and beyond", "annotation_id": "12285558", "url": "https://genius.com/12285558", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monkey Suit", "line": "’Bout to jump out in that monkey suit, that monkey suit Act bananas, that what monkeys do, what monkeys do You get stupid, we get funky too, get funky too ’Bout to jump out in that monkey suit, that monkey suit Nigga, pull up in that black van, nigga, I ain’t lyin’ Jump out in that monkey suit, nigga, I ain’t lyin’ I pull up in that black van, nigga, I ain’t lyin’ Jump out in that monkey suit, nigga, I ain’t lyin’ ’Bout to jump out in that monkey suit, that monkey suit Act bananas, that what monkeys do, what monkeys do You get stupid, we get funky too, get funky too ’Bout to jump out in that monkey suit, that monkey suit", "vault_lines": ["'Bout to jump out in that monkey suit, that monkey suit", "Act bananas, that what monkeys do, what monkeys do", "You get stupid, we get funky too, get funky too", "Nigga, pull up in that black van, nigga, I ain't lyin'", "Jump out in that monkey suit, nigga, I ain't lyin'", "I pull up in that black van, nigga, I ain't lyin'"], "meaning": "The meaning of “monkey suit” here is all black clothing—and a ski mask—to cover identities or to be harder to see at night, whilst committing crimes.\n\nPlies used a similar line in his 2008 song “Bushes”:\n\nGot on my monkey suit layin in this wet grass\n\nBruno links the metaphorical meaning of monkey with the literal with the term “bananas”. Going bananas means to go crazy, which he will do when he hops out of his black van. Whereas real life monkeys eat bananas.", "annotation_id": "11538849", "url": "https://genius.com/11538849", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monkey Suit", "status": "done", "targets": 3, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Bruno-mali-kidd-monkey-suit-lyrics", "genius_title": "Monkey Suit", "genius_id": 3030011, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Ah", "vault_lines": ["Know I'm gonna do, ah, it's the new now"], "meaning": "Lion scream", "annotation_id": "41281517", "url": "https://genius.com/41281517", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "[Verse 3: JAY-Z]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "To this day, this verse is often considered JAY-Z’s most polarizing verse, with some loving it and others hating it, with common criticisms being that he has lame and weak bars such as the first two:\n\nSasquatch, Godzilla, King Kong, Loch Ness\nGoblin, ghoul, a zombie with no conscience\nQuestion, what do all these things have in common?\nEverybody knows I’m a motherfucking monster\n\nThis verse is heavily memed in rap communities and is considered JAY-Z’s worst verse.", "annotation_id": "28559441", "url": "https://genius.com/28559441", "state": "pending", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "I’m livin’ in the future so the present is my past", "vault_lines": ["I'm livin' in the future so the present is my past"], "meaning": "Kanye has mentioned living in/seeing the future many times, like in this iconic interview, and in songs like Saint Pablo\n\nSo don’t worry about me, I’m fine\nI can see a thousand years from now in real life\nSkate on the paradigm and shift it when I feel like", "annotation_id": "28468736", "url": "https://genius.com/28468736", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "I smell a massacre Seems to be the only way to back you bastards up", "vault_lines": ["Seems to be the only way to back you bastards up"], "meaning": "The last two lines create a scenario in which Jay-Z is about to massacre people right after rapping his verse. He smells a massacre means he is about to be the one massacring people", "annotation_id": "26685705", "url": "https://genius.com/26685705", "state": "pending", "votes": -41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Murder, murder in black convertibles, I Kill a block, I murder the avenues, I Rape and pillage your village, women and children", "vault_lines": ["Murder, murder in black convertibles, I", "Kill a block, I murder the avenues, I", "Rape and pillage your village, women and children"], "meaning": "Jay-Z begin this verse with a fairly mild reference to fictional monsters.\nHe then turns it up by referencing his own experiences and dreams.\nHe then turns it up even more with these lines by saying that he will do horrible things.", "annotation_id": "26685696", "url": "https://genius.com/26685696", "state": "pending", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "All I get is these vampires and bloodsuckers", "vault_lines": ["All I get is these vampires and bloodsuckers"], "meaning": "1) At the time of this albums release, the teen vampire romance movies and books series Twilight was incredibly popular.\n\n2)Jay-Z is also portraying vampires as much lesser monsters than himself\n\n3)bloodsuckers as in leeches – people who want to use Jay-Z because he is rich and famous.", "annotation_id": "26685676", "url": "https://genius.com/26685676", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "None of you niggas have seen the carnage that I’ve seen I still hear fiends scream in my dreams", "vault_lines": ["None of you niggas have seen the carnage that I've seen", "I still hear fiends scream in my dreams"], "meaning": "Jay-Z was a drug dealer and as such has seen many things. The people he sold drugs to became fiends. He can still hear their screams in his dreams. This is comparable to PTSD of a returned soldier.", "annotation_id": "26685666", "url": "https://genius.com/26685666", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "My presence is a present, kiss my ass", "vault_lines": ["My presence is a present, kiss my ass"], "meaning": "Kanye talks about his ass (and kissing it) all throughout My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy, for example, on “POWER”:\n\nTell them Yeezy said they can kiss my whole ass\nMore specifically, they can kiss my asshole\n\nAnother example is “Gorgeous”:\n\nTell ‘em hug and kiss my ass, X and O", "annotation_id": "25606804", "url": "https://genius.com/25606804", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Now she claiming that I bruised her esophagus", "vault_lines": ["Now she claiming that I bruised her esophagus"], "meaning": "The esophagus is a hollow, muscular tube that connects the throat to the stomach. The esophagus is about 8 inches long. In a true hip-hop fashion, this line is about a long member West allegedly possesses.", "annotation_id": "23864074", "url": "https://genius.com/23864074", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Sasquatch, Godzilla, King Kong, Loch Ness Goblin, ghoul, a zombie with no conscience Question, what do these things all have in common? Everybody knows I’m a motherfuckin’ monster", "vault_lines": ["Sasquatch, Godzilla, King Kong, Loch Ness", "Goblin, ghoul, a zombie with no conscience", "Question, what do these things all have in common?", "Everybody knows I'm a motherfuckin' monster"], "meaning": "Jay-Z starts his verse off by listing multiple infamous monsters. Throughout his verse, Jay-Z alludes to a total of nine different monsters.\n\nHe lists two urban legends, Sasquatch and the Loch Ness Monster. While there have been “sightings” of these creatures, there has been no scientific or substantial evidence of their existences.\n\nHe also mentions Godzilla and King Kong, both of whom are fictional monsters from movies. These monsters also appear in a movie together, in the form of the 1963 movie King Kong vs. Godzilla. They’ve also appeared in the March 2021 remake of the movie, Godzilla vs. Kong.\n\nJay-Z finishes this set of four lines by repeating Kanye’s line from the chorus to further exaggerate the message of the song.", "annotation_id": "22365496", "url": "https://genius.com/22365496", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "So I, I am headed home (Headed home)", "vault_lines": ["So I, I am headed home (Headed home)"], "meaning": "The theme of loss and running back to familiarity and safety (symbolised by ‘home’) is a common theme in Bon Iver lyrics and in fact is a central theme of his breakout album For Emma, Forever Ago. The idea of not being to “last these shows” is also used in the title track of the album.\n\n(Running home, running home, running home, running home…)\n\nIn theme with “Monster”, Bon Iver wistfully sings about a time when he wasn’t caught up in the lights, a simpler, happier time.", "annotation_id": "13104372", "url": "https://genius.com/13104372", "state": "accepted", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Chick came up to me and said, ”This the number to dial If you wanna make your number one your number two now”", "vault_lines": ["Chick came up to me and said, \"This the number to dial", "If you wanna make your number one your number two now\""], "meaning": "Kanye broke up with Amber Rose in July 2010 and started dating Kim Kardashian in May 2012. However, Amber Rose claims that Kanye and Kim were romantically involved in 2010 when her and Ye were together.\n\nCould the mystery “chick” who gave Kanye her number be Kim Kardashian? Talk about a booty call.", "annotation_id": "8754887", "url": "https://genius.com/8754887", "state": "accepted", "votes": 82, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "[Video directed by Jake Nava]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The “Monster” music video was released in June 2011, seven months following a leak of the unfinished product (which almost made Kanye shelve it). It features all four rappers amongst the midsts of sheer gore, primarily the corpses of white models – a potential stab at Ye’s then-recent ex Amber Rose. This could also be a satire of video vixens: instead of dancing attractive women, they’ve all been massacred. Lovely.\n\nDirected by Jake Nava, best known for his work with Beyoncé, the video has been interpreted as a multitude of tributes to classic horror films such as Saw, American Psycho and Michael Jackson’s “Thriller” video. There are also some more obscure references to the character of Doctor Frankenstein and Bill Stoneham’s “The Hands Resist Him”.\n\n\n\nThe depicted misogyny and gothic style garnered a huge amount of controversy. The video was banned from MTV, alongside a backlash from activist Ann Simonton who started a petition to have it removed from online circulation. However, there has been some support for the piece. Brandon Soderburg argued for Kanye’s freedom of artistic expression, so not to dilute the effect.", "annotation_id": "8468691", "url": "https://genius.com/8468691", "state": "accepted", "votes": 73, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Conquer, stomp ya, stop your silly nonsense", "vault_lines": ["Conquer, stomp ya, stop your silly nonsense"], "meaning": "Carrying on from his sentiment on “Dig A Hole”, he throws the challenge down to the multitude of rappers who were taking shots at him at the time (the list is always long and illustrious).\n\nOne of Jay’s favourite take-downs is to debase his competitor so they feel less “grown” or important as him. Calling it “silly nonsense” makes it sound childish.\n\n\n\nSquashing unnecessary beef is mature.", "annotation_id": "8142411", "url": "https://genius.com/8142411", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "But really, really, I don’t give an F-U-C-K “Forget Barbie, fuck Nicki, sh-she’s fake” “She on a diet,” but my pockets eatin’ cheesecake", "vault_lines": ["But really, really, I don't give an F-U-C-K", "\"Forget Barbie, fuck Nicki, sh-she's fake\"", "\"She on a diet,\" but my pockets eatin' cheesecake"], "meaning": "Nicki takes on the voice of her critics in these two lines. They complain about her fake character, claiming she puts on this face for her fans but in reality is like a diet cheater, stuffing cheesecake into her pants.\n\nThere is a double meaning here as cheese is another term for money. Her pockets are fat (rich) while she remains skinny and successful, like Barbie.", "annotation_id": "6736858", "url": "https://genius.com/6736858", "state": "accepted", "votes": 216, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "[Verse 2: Kanye West]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This verse ranked #14 on Complex Magazine’s list of the best Kanye verses. With Kanye’s braggadocio and wordplay, it’s easy to see how it stands among his best.", "annotation_id": "4293503", "url": "https://genius.com/4293503", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "[Produced by Kanye West, Mike Dean & Plain Pat]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The song begins with an ominous, distorted vocal roar, sung by Justin Vernon of Bon Iver, with Vernon declaring:\n\nI shoot the lights out\n\nWhich sets up the dark atmosphere of the track. The intro of the song is followed by a brief verse given by Ross, delivered in the rapping style of boom bap with his deep voice complementing the beat. The song ends in a spooky, hoarse vocal riff delivered by Vernon, reminiscent of the track “Thriller”. An extended catchy vocal riff, closes the track.\n\nThe song heavily features bombastic tribal drums, and paranoia-inducing strings that create a feeling of tension. The beat of “Monster” has been described as a “hyperventilating death rattle”, the song was permeated with dark, urgent-sounding moans.\n\n\n\nThis monstrous beat has been reused for a freestyle by Kendrick Lamar, another by Nitty Scott and has been sampled numerous times. Interesting to note is that it’s one of the few beats, by Kanye standards, without a sample.", "annotation_id": "2564570", "url": "https://genius.com/2564570", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "And I, I wouldn’t last these shows", "vault_lines": ["And I, I wouldn't last these shows"], "meaning": "This could refer to the situation that the late Michael Jackson was faced with when he announced his monster 50-date concert series at the O2 Arena at London. Most skeptics believed he wouldn’t have the energy to last that long (and unfortunately Michael passed away before he can prove the skeptics wrong or right). This can refer to Kanye’s suicidal thoughts or the times he cried on stage while singing “Hey Mama” in honor of his mother.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CGVE5QMAFNM\n\nIt could also refer to Kanye not attending and participating in any more telethons or award ceremonies (the shows). He knows he’s outspoken and wouldn’t last anymore in them when everyone knows his pedigree. He’s a monster, he can’t be tamed.\n\nFurthermore, he had to cancel his national tour with Lady Gaga as a result of Taylor-gate.", "annotation_id": "1666540", "url": "https://genius.com/1666540", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "[Verse 4: Nicki Minaj]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Many consider this to be Nicki Minaj’s greatest verse of all time, including Kanye himself. Interestingly, My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy was released on the same day as Nicki’s debut album, Pink Friday, which eventually went platinum. It featured another great Nicki/Kanye collaboration on the song “Blazin'.”\n\nNicki has since named this verse as her “breakout moment”:\n\nI held my own and I stayed true to my crazy animation, and it’s like I didn’t have to take my fun stuff [out]… I was able to incorporate [the real] Nicki Minaj on a record with JAY-Z and Kanye West. And I think people know that’s a difficult task in itself.\n\nHipHopDX named Nicki’s feature 2010’s “Verse of the Year” and, in May 2013, Complex Magazine gave it “Best Rap Verse of the Last 5 Years,” beating out verses on songs like Kendrick Lamar’s 2012 single, “m.A.A.d city,” Lil Wayne’s “A Milli,” released in 2008, as well as Kanye and JAY-Z’s own 2011 hit “Niggas in Paris.”\n\n\n\nOn January 13, 2016, Adele was asked if she was able to spit some bars to this song on The Late Late Show with James Corden. She rapped Nicki Minaj’s verse to “Monster” bar-for-bar and even waved to the careers she’s killed. You can watch the video here. When Nicki saw this she was ecstatic, even calling Adele “Mad Ratchet.”", "annotation_id": "1281332", "url": "https://genius.com/1281332", "state": "accepted", "votes": 565, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "I’ma need to see your fuckin’ hands at the concert I’ma need to see your fuckin’ hands at the concert", "vault_lines": ["I'ma need to see your fuckin' hands"], "meaning": "Kanye’s live shows are stuff of legend. His 2008 Glow In The Dark Tour featured  Rihanna, Lupe Fiasco, N.E.R.D, Santigold and Nas, running from April to December.\n\nAlthough he never toured for MBDTF, he played a string of festival dates. His 2011 Coachella set is regarded as one of the best performances of his career.", "annotation_id": "1189987", "url": "https://genius.com/1189987", "state": "accepted", "votes": 205, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "I heard the beat, the same raps that gave the track pain", "vault_lines": ["I heard the beat, the same raps that gave the track pain"], "meaning": "This is a callback to 808s & Heartbreak and Kanye’s influence on the rap game.\n\nRappers using auto-tune/opening up emotionally became popular post-808s. The likes of Drake, Childish Gambino, J. Cole and more were all influenced by Kanye.", "annotation_id": "946565", "url": "https://genius.com/946565", "state": "accepted", "votes": 114, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "And my only focus is stayin’ on some bogus shit Arguin’ with my older bitch, actin’ like I owe her shit", "vault_lines": ["And my only focus is stayin' on some bogus shit", "Arguin' with my older bitch, actin' like I owe her shit"], "meaning": "Kanye likes to stay on top on “bogus” (untrue/fake) shit. He only keeps it real.\n\nAt the time this song was recorded, Kanye was still with Amber Rose and just two years earlier had broken up with fiancé Alexis Phifer.\n\nIt seems Amber and Alexis didn’t get along. According to Vibe, Amber said this about Alexis on radio:\n\nYou see Kanye’s other ex, he was with her for six years, took her the same places and no one even remembers her name now…", "annotation_id": "587449", "url": "https://genius.com/587449", "state": "accepted", "votes": 66, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Hide ’til it’s bright out", "vault_lines": ["Hide 'til it's bright out"], "meaning": "Monsters are typically nocturnal creatures—vampires and werewolves avoid the sun and bright light.\n\n\n\n“Hide ‘til its bright out” is the opposite of what a monster would do. Celebrity “monsters” come into their own when the spotlight is on them.", "annotation_id": "576017", "url": "https://genius.com/576017", "state": "accepted", "votes": 163, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "I think big, get cash, make ’em blink fast Now look at what you just saw, this is what you live for Ah, I’m a motherfuckin’ monster!", "vault_lines": ["I think big, get cash, make 'em blink fast", "Now look at what you just saw, this is what you live for", "Ah, I'm a motherfuckin' monster!"], "meaning": "In music videos (such as “Super Bass”), Nicki often blinks fast when she embodies her alter ego of Barbie. Here, she’s making people blink fast because we can’t believe our eyes or our ears.\n\n\n\nAs Nicki wraps up her monster verse, she encourages listeners to take a second look. Not only do her cash and physical looks draw attention, but her rapping ability is now undeniable. She knows it, and that’s why she closes out with the declaration “AHHH! I’m a motherfucking monster!” Jay Z and Kanye have all declared it already in the song, but Nicki ultimately has the final word.", "annotation_id": "519773", "url": "https://genius.com/519773", "state": "accepted", "votes": 311, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Pull up in the monster, automobile gangsta", "vault_lines": ["Pull up in the monster, automobile gangsta"], "meaning": "Nicki starts off her verse by exploiting the first of three different meanings of “monster.” In this case, “monster” means big. Furthermore, Nicki is starting up her verse like dramatically arriving on the scene in a car.", "annotation_id": "492868", "url": "https://genius.com/492868", "state": "accepted", "votes": 254, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Gossip, gossip, nigga, just stop it", "vault_lines": ["Gossip, gossip, nigga, just stop it"], "meaning": "Kanye West is a controversial figure. In 2009, Kanye interrupted Taylor Swift’s VMA speech. This led to intense media backlash against Kanye, who as a result left the United States to live in Europe. Kanye would rather people listen to his music than pay attention to gossip columns.", "annotation_id": "448518", "url": "https://genius.com/448518", "state": "accepted", "votes": 195, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Mix that Goose and Malibu, I call it “Malibooyah” Goddamn, Yeezy always hit ’em with a new style", "vault_lines": ["Mix that Goose and Malibu, I call it \"Malibooyah\"", "Goddamn, Yeezy always hit 'em with a new style"], "meaning": "Grey Goose is a premium vodka made in France, while Malibu refers to Malibu Rum from Barbados. Mixing 40 and 42 proof liquors together would give any drink a serious edge!\n\nYeezy’s new concoction is just one example of his ability to create new trends. Ever since he broke into in hip hop speeding up soul samples, Kanye’s changed the landscape of music/pop culture.\n\nThis is also a callback to “Good Life” where Kanye raps:\n\n50 told me, go ‘head, switch the style up\nAnd if they hate then let 'em hate and watch the money pile up", "annotation_id": "437149", "url": "https://genius.com/437149", "state": "accepted", "votes": 124, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "I, I crossed the limelight And I’ll, I’ll let God decide, ’cide", "vault_lines": ["I, I crossed the limelight", "And I'll, I'll let God decide, 'cide"], "meaning": "Justin Vernon (the lead singer of Bon Iver) knows he’s sinned. God will be the judge for him in the end. However, this outro also applies to Kanye.\n\nKanye has screwed up plenty of times. He faced suicidal thoughts after his mother passed away and fled the United States after the Taylor Swift incident.  Yet he still continues to perform and will let God be the only one to judge him.", "annotation_id": "227650", "url": "https://genius.com/227650", "state": "accepted", "votes": 149, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Matter who you go and get, ain’t nobody cold as this", "vault_lines": ["Matter who you go and get, ain't nobody cold as this"], "meaning": "In Dante’s Inferno, the devil is trapped in ice on the last circle of Hell, destined to freeze forever. Is Yeezy the devil? Is Yeezy worse than the devil?\n\nThis degree of coldness also describes how merciless Kanye is when rapping.", "annotation_id": "227646", "url": "https://genius.com/227646", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "[Outro: Justin Vernon & Charlie Wilson]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "West first got into contact with Vernon when he called him with the intention of sampling his song “Woods.”\n\nThe two artists became friends while playing games of basketball together, and West introduced him to Jeff Bhasker, one of his main producers.\n\nAlong with Justin Vernon, Charlie Wilson harmonizes in the background here.", "annotation_id": "161919", "url": "https://genius.com/161919", "state": "accepted", "votes": 141, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Hotter than a Middle Eastern climate, violent", "vault_lines": ["Hotter than a Middle Eastern climate, violent"], "meaning": "The Middle East is covered by a a lot of desert, and is literally the hottest geographical area on earth. Politically, it is also one of the most tumultuous areas on earth.\n\nNicki is not only saying she is hotter than a Middle Eastern climate in a literal sense, but that her rhymes are as potent as weapons of war.", "annotation_id": "70754", "url": "https://genius.com/70754", "state": "accepted", "votes": 189, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Nicki on them titties when I sign it That’s how these niggas so one-track-minded", "vault_lines": ["Nicki on them titties when I sign it", "That's how these niggas so one-track-minded"], "meaning": "Even female fans ask Nicki to sign their tits. Nicki said in an interview that she carries around a pink marker just for this purpose.\n\nImmediately after rapping about “titties”, Nicki takes aim at the guys with only one thing on their mind: sex. Note also the double meaning here as she criticises other rappers who only drop one track at a time, or whole records that only feature one hot track.", "annotation_id": "70324", "url": "https://genius.com/70324", "state": "accepted", "votes": 190, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "I shoot the lights out", "vault_lines": ["I shoot the lights out"], "meaning": "Light is a motif in Kanye’s work—it appears throughout this album and on songs like “Flashing Lights.”\n\n“All of the Lights,” a song about Kanye’s fame, precedes “Monster” in My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy. The first line in this song transitions from the public Kanye to a darker side of Ye.", "annotation_id": "60516", "url": "https://genius.com/60516", "state": "accepted", "votes": 207, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Everybody know I’m a motherfuckin’ monster", "vault_lines": ["Everybody know I'm a motherfuckin' monster"], "meaning": "Kanye brags about his skills on the mic! After his comments about George Bush and the Taylor Swift incident, many labeled Kanye a “monster,” or variation of.", "annotation_id": "60456", "url": "https://genius.com/60456", "state": "accepted", "votes": 266, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "You could be the king, but watch the queen conquer", "vault_lines": ["You could be the king, but watch the queen conquer"], "meaning": "In chess, the King is generally considered most important piece to the game, as losing the King means losing the game. However, there isn’t a whole lot that the King can do, meanwhile, the Queen is the desirable piece in the game because it is capable of many different moves. Nicki argues that like the Queen in chess, she’s in control here and not the men around her.\n\nHowever, historically queens have often been seen as inferior to kings, but Nicki wants to let us know that she’s just as capable, maybe even more so than her male counterparts in the rap game. In fact, some of the most accomplished monarchs in history have been women. Furthermore, it’s common for male artists in the Hip-hop genre to call themselves “kings,” and Nicki plays on this idea but instead focuses on her femininity.\n\nNicki was also raised in Queens, New York City, and has lots of pride for her hometown, even once she called herself the “Queen of Queens” because of her dominating influence over the city. Nicki has maintained this status and identity of being “the Queen” or the “Queen of Rap” for much of her career, and in 2018 she even named an album after it.\n\nNicki also uses the word “conquer” to tie-back to an earlier line from Jay-Z.", "annotation_id": "58783", "url": "https://genius.com/58783", "state": "accepted", "votes": 255, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Okay, first things first, I’ll eat your brains Then I’ma start rocking gold teeth and fangs ‘Cause that’s what a motherfuckin’ monster do", "vault_lines": ["Okay, first things first, I'll eat your brains", "Then I'ma start rocking gold teeth and fangs", "'Cause that's what a motherfuckin' monster do"], "meaning": "Continuing the monster theme, zombies love to eat brains. Nicki is going to take all your knowledge (eat/pick your brain) about conquering the music industry, as she declared in the previous line. The “your” could be referring to Jay-Z or Kanye; two of the biggest moguls in hip-hop with the most knowledge to consume.\n\nGiving or getting brain is also a reference to oral sex, and so “eat your brains” may be an allusion to monstrous sexual prowess. In other words, Nicki is a “monster”–dominant in the bed.\n\nWearing gold fangs as fronts was a New York trend throughout the ‘90s.", "annotation_id": "57476", "url": "https://genius.com/57476", "state": "accepted", "votes": 195, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Nonsense, none of you niggas know where the swamp is", "vault_lines": ["Nonsense, none of you niggas know where the swamp is"], "meaning": "Although Florida probably isn’t a bad guess…\n\n\n\nThe line refers to the idea that other rappers and listeners may claim to have an idea of his upbringing/the ghetto, but don’t actually have any connection to what he has experienced", "annotation_id": "50034", "url": "https://genius.com/50034", "state": "accepted", "votes": 75, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Profit, profit, nigga, I got it", "vault_lines": ["Profit, profit, nigga, I got it"], "meaning": "In 2010, Kanye was reportedly worth around 30 million dollars.\n\nMy Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy, apart from achieving critical acclaim, was successful commercially. It sold 496,000 copies in its first week and had the fourth-best sales week of 2010. It also debuted at #1 on the Billboard 200 and is certified 2X platinum—meaning it sold 2 million copies.", "annotation_id": "50032", "url": "https://genius.com/50032", "state": "accepted", "votes": 185, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Kanye West samples, here’s one for example", "vault_lines": ["Kanye West samples, here's one for example"], "meaning": "Early Kanye production was very sample-heavy. He often used sped-up, chipmunk-sounding samples like the Luther Vandross' “A House Is Not A Home\" on Twista’s “Slow Jamz.”\n\nIronically, “Monster” happens to be one of only two songs on My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy that contains no credited samples or elements of other songs (“All of the Lights” is the other).", "annotation_id": "49630", "url": "https://genius.com/49630", "state": "accepted", "votes": 243, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "And I’m all up, all up, all up in the bank with the funny face And if I’m fake, I ain’t notice ’cause my money ain’t", "vault_lines": ["And I'm all up, all up, all up in the bank with the funny face", "And if I'm fake, I ain't notice 'cause my money ain't"], "meaning": "Nicki is known for the funny faces she makes in music videos. She is also referring to laughing all the way to the bank, a common idiom that depicts one’s joy in making a (perhaps unlikely) profit. The phrase is a variant of “cry all the way to the bank”, suggesting the money was wrongly earned. An example of a face she might give a teller:\n\n\n\nThis line is also reminiscent of the opening clown bank robbery scene of The Dark Knight. Jay Z references the film in the next song.\n\nNicki also addresses those who circulate rumours of her cosmetic surgery by saying, “Who cares? I’m rich!”", "annotation_id": "48814", "url": "https://genius.com/48814", "state": "accepted", "votes": 194, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "All I see is these fake fucks with no fangs Tryna draw blood from my ice-cold veins", "vault_lines": ["All I see is these fake fucks with no fangs", "Tryna draw blood from my ice-cold veins"], "meaning": "Following the vampire line, these vampires try to suck blood from him but they can’t.\n\n\n\nRappers try to destroy his career but nothing hurts him because he’s a monster with ice in his veins (who else would do alliterative fake fuck?).\n\nAlso, notice the double entendre of ice-cold. He means both that he’s remorseless and that he’s just got rich in his blood–he was born to be great, unlike these other fake fucks.", "annotation_id": "44553", "url": "https://genius.com/44553", "state": "accepted", "votes": 84, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Know that motherfucker, well, what you gon’ do now? Whatever I wanna do, gosh, it’s cool now Know I’m gonna do, ah, it’s the new now Think you motherfuckers really, really need to cool out", "vault_lines": ["Know that motherfucker, well, what you gon' do now?", "Whatever I wanna do, gosh, it's cool now", "Know I'm gonna do, ah, it's the new now", "Think you motherfuckers really, really need to cool out"], "meaning": "This is an interpolation of Napoleon Dynamite in the 2004 comedy film Napoleon Dynamite.\n\nGosh! was his catchprase. Kanye relays this line with similar intonation.\n\nhttp://youtu.be/w5X4UTXESMY", "annotation_id": "42051", "url": "https://genius.com/42051", "state": "accepted", "votes": 166, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Bitch, I’m a monster, no-good bloodsucker Fat motherfucker, now look who’s in trouble", "vault_lines": ["Fat motherfucker, now look who's in trouble"], "meaning": "Ross starts his short verse with a self-deprecating spree, taking pride in his villainous self. He directly announces his arrival as the boss of the rap game to intimidate his rivals.\n\n“Fat motherfucker” means he’s eating as in making money and being successful as a rapper. Rick Ross is also a hefty guy himself, who weighed around 350 pounds at the time of this song’s release. Since then, he lost over 100 pounds due to his self-branded exercise program, RossFit:\n\nMan, you know me — I was just a proud, fat boss my whole life, you dig? I always enjoyed that. That’s just always been who I am, but the older we get, we have to take care of our health first and foremost. We’re having families, we’re having kids, we have loved ones who depend on us […] that’s what RossFit’s all about.", "annotation_id": "35075", "url": "https://genius.com/35075", "state": "accepted", "votes": 381, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Pink wig, thick ass, give ’em whiplash", "vault_lines": ["Pink wig, thick ass, give 'em whiplash"], "meaning": "Nicki is known for her brightly colored hair and her prodigious booty. When Nicki walks by, men stare so intensely they hurt their necks.\n\nFrom an interview with Hype Hair Magazine:\n\nI am a very visual person and when I look in the mirror, and I see green or I see pink, it does something to my attitude. It makes me feel positive.\n\n\n\nThere’s also a “whiplash” effect between this line and the next—with almost every word rhyming, and the exact same rhythm (sort of set outside the flow of the verse), you get a deja vu or double-take effect. Emphasized by the echo vocal effect on those lines.", "annotation_id": "35074", "url": "https://genius.com/35074", "state": "accepted", "votes": 227, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "As you run through my jungles, all you hear is rumbles", "vault_lines": ["As you run through my jungles, all you hear is rumbles"], "meaning": "Rumble in the Jungle was the famous 1974 boxing match between Muhammad Ali and George Foreman in  Kinshasa, Zaire (now Democratic Republic of the Congo).\n\nRozay owns the rap game (it’s his jungle) and will knock out anyone who tries to run through it. Rick Ross was featured on The Game’s “Ali Bomaye,” where the 1974 fight was also used as a metaphor for the rap arena.", "annotation_id": "35072", "url": "https://genius.com/35072", "state": "accepted", "votes": 113, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "With a bad bitch that came from Sri Lanka", "vault_lines": ["With a bad bitch that came from Sri Lanka"], "meaning": "Nicki’s friend M.I.A. was born in London to Sri Lankan parents and moved to Sri Lanka when she was six months old. Nicki is also featured on M.I.A.’s “Teqkilla.” She and Nicki are known for collaborating and shouting each other out in interviews.\n\nM.I.A.’s fiancé at the time—producer, musician, and Seagrams heir Benjamin Bronfman—also contributed to this track.", "annotation_id": "34987", "url": "https://genius.com/34987", "state": "accepted", "votes": 335, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "And I’ll say, bride of Chucky, it’s child’s play Just killed another career, it’s a mild day", "vault_lines": ["And I'll say, bride of Chucky, it's child's play", "Just killed another career, it's a mild day"], "meaning": "After a bit of a bragging spree, Nicki brings the verse back again to the monster theme of the song. Chucky is the murderous doll who first made an appearance in the film titled Child’s Play (1988). In the fourth film of the series, Jennifer Tilly voiced a vengeful female serial killer spirit inside Chucky’s female counterpart, Bride of Chucky (1998).\n\nNicki often portrays herself as a (Barbie) doll, and in Child’s Play, it is the doll that is the killer monster. Nicki is used to killing others' careers just as Chucky went on a killing spree in his films. However, she casually says it’s a slow day for her, with only one death on her list.\n\nI could just imagine a Barbie becoming like a Chucky and you think it’s all cute but the head starts spinning or something crazy and the kids just took that and ran with it.\n\nLil Chuckee, is also one of Nicki’s fellow Young Money members. He has a record label called Child’s Play Records.\n\n\n\n‘Cheesecake’ also follows into ‘Chucky’ here; Chuck E. Cheese is a franchise which is definitely “child’s play”, being a children’s entertainment area and restaurant.", "annotation_id": "34878", "url": "https://genius.com/34878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 214, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "All I see is these niggas I made millionaires Millin’ about, spillin’ they feelings in the air", "vault_lines": ["All I see is these niggas I made millionaires", "Millin' about, spillin' they feelings in the air"], "meaning": "From here to the end of the verse, Jay is taking subliminal shots at former Roc-a-fella artist Beanie Sigel, who has lately taken to releasing diss songs aimed at him and Kanye. “Feel It In The Air” is the name of a Sigel song, which Jay puns on here.\n\n\n\nIt could also refer to Dame Dash, but he hasn’t done any “feelings spilling” recently (here’s Dame flipping out cause he wasn’t informed of a Jay-related Def jam meeting)\n\n\n\nA third possibility is rapper Peedi Crakk, a somewhat disgruntled former Roc-a-fella employee, who once planned to release an album titled Camel Face Hunting Season\n\n\n\nA fourth possibility is former Jay-Z crony, DeHaven. As in:\n\nFuck DeHaven for caving, that’s why we don’t speak made men ain’t supposed to make statements.\n\n\n\nA fifth possibility is former Jay-Z mentor, Jaz-O who released the Jay-Z disses “Ova” and “Go Harder”. As in:\n\nDame made millions, even Jaz made some scraps…he could’ve made more but he ain’t sign his contract.\"", "annotation_id": "34790", "url": "https://genius.com/34790", "state": "accepted", "votes": 126, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Everybody want to know what my Achilles heel is Love, I don’t get enough of it", "vault_lines": ["Everybody want to know what my Achilles heel is", "Love, I don't get enough of it"], "meaning": "The Achilles heel (a phrase of Greek origin) is a deadly weakness that could actually or potentially lead to a downfall. Jay’s killing everyone with his monstrous lyrics, so his enemies need to find his Achilles heel to take him down.\n\nAccording to Homer’s The Iliad, Achilles was known as one of the greatest warriors who ever lived. Jay is most likely alluding once again to his own greatness, as well as admitting that, yes he does have a weakness, love. He may be a monster, but he is still mortal.\n\n\n\nHe pinpoints his weakness as his need for love from the masses (I suppose Beyoncé isn’t giving him enough love).", "annotation_id": "34788", "url": "https://genius.com/34788", "state": "accepted", "votes": 147, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Uh, the best living or dead hands down, huh Less talk, more head right now, huh", "vault_lines": ["Uh, the best living or dead hands down, huh", "Less talk, more head right now, huh"], "meaning": "While MCs like Lil Wayne have claimed to be the best rapper alive, Kanye takes it one step further and claims to be the best rapper of all time, dead or alive.\n\nThe second part of this couplet is very similar to Big Sean’s lyric on his song “Made,” which Kanye produced:\n\nI need less friends, more bread\nLess talk, more head\n\nThe song was released two months before “Monster.”\n\nThe wordplay/transition from the hook: “I’m-a need to see your fucking hands at the concert” into “The best living or dead hands down, huh” could be intentional or coincidence.", "annotation_id": "34785", "url": "https://genius.com/34785", "state": "accepted", "votes": 151, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Hair dresser from Milan, that’s the monster ’do Monster Giuseppe heel, that’s the monster shoe", "vault_lines": ["Monster Giuseppe heel, that's the monster shoe"], "meaning": "Milan is one of the fashion capitals of the world. A queen like Nicki requires the highest of fashion, which only a place like Milan can provide. She is known for her unique style and interest in fashion.\n\nGiuseppe Zanotti is a high-end shoe designer and is one of her favourite brands.\n\nHere is Nicki sporting a monster hairdo:", "annotation_id": "34708", "url": "https://genius.com/34708", "state": "accepted", "votes": 166, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Besides, Ye, they can’t stand besides me I think me, you, and Am’ should ménage Friday", "vault_lines": ["Besides, Ye, they can't stand besides me", "I think me, you, and Am' should ménage Friday"], "meaning": "“Am” was Kanye’s girlfriend at the time, model Amber Rose. Their breakup came a few months before the song’s release.\n\nThe line also references Nicki’s stage name Minaj, which is a play on the phrase “ménage à trois” (a threesome), and her album Pink Friday.\n\n\n\nKanye later referenced these lines on his 2018 track “Violent Crimes” saying:\n\nI hope she like Nicki, I make her a monster\nNot havin' ménages, I’m just bein' silly\n\nThe track’s outro features a voicemail from Nicki Minaj in which she gives the idea for the 2018 lyric:\n\nI’m saying it like\nI want a daughter like Nicki, aw, man, I promise\nI'ma turn her to a monster, but no ménages”\nI don’t know how you saying it but, let ‘em hear this", "annotation_id": "34687", "url": "https://genius.com/34687", "state": "accepted", "votes": 391, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "So let me get this straight, wait, I’m the rookie? But my features and my shows ten times your pay? 50K for a verse, no album out Yeah, my money’s so tall that my Barbies got to climb it", "vault_lines": ["So let me get this straight, wait, I'm the rookie?", "But my features and my shows ten times your pay?", "50K for a verse, no album out", "Yeah, my money's so tall that my Barbies got to climb it"], "meaning": "A response to her detractors, and more specifically, Lil Kim, who has been around for over a decade.\n\nEven though Nicki at this stage was still the new kid on the block, she made more money than any other female rapper in the game. Receiving 50k for a single guest verse is a lot for an artist yet to debut an album.\n\nNot only is she raking in big money, but Nicki also has an extremely strong connection with her fanbase, whom she affectionately calls “Barbies” or “Barbz.” She explained the origin of the term and the importance of her fans to Sway in 2011.\n\n\nIt’s definitely a movement at this point… People really identify with me through that and I definitely don’t think that when we say ‘Barbie,’ we’re thinking of the plastic little doll with the blond hair anymore. It’s really how we’ve come to define ourselves, but there’s so many surrounding circumstances.\n\nI always promote the girls to have their own stuff, be go-getters…don’t depend on a man for anything, going to school is a big criteria. When you connect with your friends in a way that they feel like they’re a part of a certain club, it boosts their self-esteem because they’re no longer a fan, they become a friend.", "annotation_id": "34679", "url": "https://genius.com/34679", "state": "accepted", "votes": 329, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Young Money is the roster and a monster crew", "vault_lines": ["Young Money is the roster and a monster crew"], "meaning": "Young Money Entertainment, founded by Lil Wayne, signed Nicki in 2009. Other signees include Drake, Tyga, and Gudda Gudda.\n\nNotice two uses of the word “monster” here. Not only is “monster” used to describe their talent, but also their popularity and influence in the rap game.\n\n\n\nNicki recently revealed another meaning of ‘the roster’: she also meant ‘Rasta’ as this is how she used to call Lil Wayne because of his dreads.\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/BLqs1LMjFY4/", "annotation_id": "34677", "url": "https://genius.com/34677", "state": "accepted", "votes": 145, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Tony Matterhorn, dutty wine it, wine it", "vault_lines": ["Tony Matterhorn, dutty wine it, wine it"], "meaning": "Tony Matterhorn is a Jamaican dancehall reggae DJ who gained popularity in the U.S. with his song “Dutty Wine” (2006). Nicki is aiming for the unexpected crossover success that Matterhorn had.", "annotation_id": "34676", "url": "https://genius.com/34676", "state": "accepted", "votes": 135, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "And my eyes more red than the devil is And I’m ’bout to take it to another level, bitch", "vault_lines": ["And my eyes more red than the devil is", "And I'm 'bout to take it to another level, bitch"], "meaning": "Red eyes are often associated with evil, hence the used of the devil in the simile of the first line.\n\nIn a collaboration with Mic, Genius found MBDTF to be Kanye’s darkest album. He makes further references to the devil on “Devil In A New Dress” and “See Me Now.”", "annotation_id": "34673", "url": "https://genius.com/34673", "state": "accepted", "votes": 191, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Chi nigga, but these hoes love my accent", "vault_lines": ["Chi nigga, but these hoes love my accent"], "meaning": "Kanye plays on “shy” and “chi”—a reference to a character trait of his (yes! Kanye’s shy) and his hometown of Chicago. Women love his Midwest accent.\n\nYeezy raps “accent” with an exaggerated Midwest twang. This seems to call back to the first lyric on the album.", "annotation_id": "34634", "url": "https://genius.com/34634", "state": "accepted", "votes": 133, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Yeah, I’m in that Tonka, color of Willy Wonka", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I'm in that Tonka, color of Willy Wonka"], "meaning": "Tonka is a company that makes toy trucks, usually yellow in color. This plays off of her opening line as Tonka trucks are literal automobiles, while the “monster” Nicki is in is a killer song.\n\nWilly Wonka is the candy mogul from the children’s book Charlie and The Chocolate Factory. If something is the “color of Willy Wonka” it could mean:\n\n\nPurple: the color of Wonka’s jacket and the color of the Lamborghini Murcielago Nicki drives (well, that’s pink, but close enough).\nYellow, or another bright candy color: the Wonka character and the candy manufacturer are generally associated with vibrancy. Furthermore, Tonka trucks are typically yellow.\nGold: The tickets used to get into Wonka’s factory are gold. This further pushes the yellow wordplay.\nBrown: Willy Wonka’s main product is chocolate, which is brown, like Minaj’s skin tone. Combined with the royal colors of purple and gold, this makes for a powerful metaphor.\n\n\n\nEither way, Nicki has found herself a “golden ticket” in this track—along with being a huge success, it effectively launched her mainstream popularity and is widely recognized as one of her best verses to date.", "annotation_id": "34596", "url": "https://genius.com/34596", "state": "accepted", "votes": 193, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Head of the class and she just won a swallowship", "vault_lines": ["Head of the class and she just won a swallowship"], "meaning": "Since she’s the “head” (top) of the class, she just won a “swallowship” (swallow+ scholarship) to help pay her ‘education.’ Also, “head” is a term for oral sex.", "annotation_id": "34593", "url": "https://genius.com/34593", "state": "accepted", "votes": 277, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "’Cause you’ll never get on top of this So, mami, best advice is just to get on top of this Have you ever had sex with a pharaoh? Ah, put the pussy in a sarcophagus", "vault_lines": ["'Cause you'll never get on top of this", "So, mami, best advice is just to get on top of this", "Have you ever had sex with a pharaoh?", "Ah, put the pussy in a sarcophagus"], "meaning": "There’s no way anyone will “top” Yeezy in the rap game, so it’s easiest for females to simply get “on top” of him (in the sexual term). Clever wordplay.\n\nKanye intentionally uses the term “mommy” to refer to his female companion, because it sounds like “mummy,” which is not only another type of monster, but also sets up the Ancient Egypt theme.\n\nHere, Kanye likens himself to a pharaoh. Pharaohs were viewed by the Egyptians as literal sons of the gods, walking the earth in human form. With Kanye’s ego and Yeezus persona, it’s not too far a stretch for him to think sex with him is like sleeping with the son of God.\n\nKanye offers to “beat the pussy up” until he “kills” the pussy, which will be put to rest in its Egyptian tomb. This is a pun on the propensity of ancient Egyptians to mummify and bury cats (pussy) in the same way they did people.\n\nTechnically, Kanye’s off here: If you look at the root of the word sarcophagus, you will find that it means (sarco) flesh (phagus) eating—flesh eating. By putting the pussy in a sarcophagus, Kanye would actually be eating out the pussy.", "annotation_id": "34591", "url": "https://genius.com/34591", "state": "accepted", "votes": 518, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Bought the chain that always give me back pain Fuckin’ up my money so, yeah, I had to act sane", "vault_lines": ["Bought the chain that always give me back pain", "Fuckin' up my money so, yeah, I had to act sane"], "meaning": "Kanye is parodying/living up to the common hip hop trope of buying a large chain. His massive 24K Horus head chain allegedly cost him $300,000. Certainly looks like it would cause back pain…\n\nKanye later released a song called “Chain Heavy.”", "annotation_id": "34587", "url": "https://genius.com/34587", "state": "accepted", "votes": 98, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "Do the rap and the track, triple-double, no assists", "vault_lines": ["Do the rap and the track, triple-double, no assists"], "meaning": "In basketball, a triple double refers to achieving double digit numbers in three of the following categories: scoring, rebounds, blocked shots, steals, and assists.\n\nKanye writes, raps, and produces his own tracks. Whereas typically a hip hop artist either produces records or raps, Ye doesn’t need assistance since he can take a track from concept to completion by himself (doing both the rapping and producing).\n\nThis seems somewhat hypocritical, considering 26 writers and 12 producers worked on this album.\n\n\n\nKanye playing basketball in Hawaii during the recording of MBDTF.", "annotation_id": "34586", "url": "https://genius.com/34586", "state": "accepted", "votes": 212, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Monster", "line": "[Intro: Justin Vernon]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The intro and the outro are performed by indie-folk singer Justin Vernon, who records as Bon Iver (pronounced Bone-ee-VARE). “Bon Hiver” is French for “good winter”.\n\n\n\nTwo other songs on this album also have the Bon Iver touch—  “Lost in the World” samples Bon Iver’s song “Woods,” and “Dark Fantasy” has Justin Vernon’s background vocals.", "annotation_id": "34583", "url": "https://genius.com/34583", "state": "accepted", "votes": 212, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
