{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Mama’s stressing, seen them ratchets in the dresser She not asking questions She seen the vests and seen other kids in the casket restin’", "vault_lines": ["She seen the vests and seen other kids in the casket restin'"], "meaning": "In Queensbridge NY and in other hoods in the United States, violence is commonplace with kids toting rifles and hiding ratchets aka firearms in the dresser.\n\nIt’s a violent lifestyle that many people in the hood don’t get out of, and the mothers are almost always the victims of grieving and seeing their sons and daughters pass too soon.", "annotation_id": "15817374", "url": "https://genius.com/15817374", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "And half the team got the choppers with the shoulder slings It ain’t a rust, stay in the trap ’til you a hundred up", "vault_lines": ["And half the team got the choppers with the shoulder slings", "It ain't a rust, stay in the trap 'til you a hundred up"], "meaning": "Half of Ross’s team or crew have http://www.urbandictionary.com/define.php?term=chopper  or AK-47s with the shoulder slings/strap.\n\n‘It ain’t a rust’ = It’s not to tarnish someone’s rep.\n\nStay in the http://www.urbandictionary.com/define.php?term=Trap (definition #3) until you get a hundred dollars or more.  So, in the rap game rappers will throw shots not necessarily to tarnish the other rapper’s reputation.  They stay in the game to make a hundred dollars or more.\n\nAlso:  Half of his team got or understood the ‘choppers’, referring to the line “Next crib I build got to cross state lines” when Ross ‘chopped up’ words (hidden in his cypher).", "annotation_id": "8926651", "url": "https://genius.com/8926651", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Raw deal, rob a nigga like it’s on my appeal", "vault_lines": ["Raw deal, rob a nigga like it's on my appeal"], "meaning": "http://www.urbandictionary.com/define.php?term=raw+deal  or an unfair treatment.\n\nRoss says “it’s on” and appeal means “an urgent request”.\n\nWhat’s the unfair treatment?  He’ll robbed a nigga by throwing with http://www.yourdictionary.com/urgency  headcrackers.\n\nConnecting to Ross’s lines in verse 1:  “never let a man take your jewels”  Jewels can refer to gold chains or manhood.  Ross will rob a nigga’s jewels.\n\n‘My appeal’ can be a play on words for Mass Appeal, which is Nas’s record label.", "annotation_id": "8922403", "url": "https://genius.com/8922403", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "It ain’t arrest, they ain’t a pussy ’til it’s time to bust", "vault_lines": ["It ain't arrest, they ain't a pussy 'til it's time to bust"], "meaning": "This last line covers both possible themes of the verse:  1) FBI trying to catch Ross.  2) Someone thinks Ross and Nas are homo because they’ve been seen wearing products from Marc Jacobs.\nhttp://www.upscalehype.com/2012/04/nas-wearing-marc-by-marc-jacobs-fuchsia-harvey-twill-blazer-in-the-don-video/ ;\n\n\n\nIt ain’t arrest = no arrest made until really busted for a crime.\n\nThey ain’t a pussy = not homo.. until it’s time to bust.  We find out if a person ‘man’s up’ when it’s time to bust shots.", "annotation_id": "8912092", "url": "https://genius.com/8912092", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "We ’bout that moolah, rich shooters", "vault_lines": ["We 'bout that moolah, rich shooters"], "meaning": "Moolah is slang for money.\n\nRich shooters refer to crap shooters, a dice game (mentioned in Ross’s verse).  Nas and Ross know how to gamble.\n\nOf course it could also mean:  Nas is a Don and he knows how to shoot a gun.  A rich shooter.", "annotation_id": "8900959", "url": "https://genius.com/8900959", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Marc Jacob, chocolate diamonds, they for my white bitch", "vault_lines": ["Marc Jacob, chocolate diamonds, they for my white bitch"], "meaning": "Ross gives his girl a Chocolate Diamond, a recent trend in the fashion industry. They are actually sold by Le Vian, rather than its competitor Marc Jacobs.\n\n\n\nBy being white, Ross reverses the traditional stereotype of an African-American “gold digger” having an attraction for normal diamonds, usually white.", "annotation_id": "8900913", "url": "https://genius.com/8900913", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Fly nigga from the other side, hecklers, PKs, 9's and .45's 7.62's, deer-hunting rifle shells, life is hell", "vault_lines": ["Fly nigga from the other side, hecklers, PKs, 9's and .45's", "7.62's, deer-hunting rifle shells, life is hell"], "meaning": "Nas emphasises his stature by means of his gun knowledge; Heckler and Koch handguns, PK machine guns, nine milimeters, .45 caliber Colt pistols and 7.62 deer hunting ammunition.\n\n\n\nHe has often had a close affinity for guns, as seen with the likes of “Got Ur Self A…” and “I Gave You Power”. Nas also expresses a negative feeling towards life, a significant allusion in regards to his early career.", "annotation_id": "8870963", "url": "https://genius.com/8870963", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "You wouldn’t get that from me, yo, you’d overlook me Looking like I just get to that money and stay in some pussy", "vault_lines": ["Looking like I just get to that money and stay in some pussy"], "meaning": "After letting someone know where ‘he’s coming from’ or point of view, Nas says he’ll be overlooked or someone will not consider his point of view, someone will not consider his warning.  That someone will think that Nas is just into getting money and pussy.\n\nHis point of view, from the lines before, is that he will put holes or he can put holes into someone.", "annotation_id": "8870880", "url": "https://genius.com/8870880", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Learn how to draw the line from when we hang with the shooters Strippers, dealers, and killers, leeches and opportunists", "vault_lines": ["Learn how to draw the line from when we hang with the shooters", "Strippers, dealers, and killers, leeches and opportunists"], "meaning": "Coming from Queensbridge, Nas knows the ways of the streets and warns any means of opposition with the strength of his crew. He uses an isocolon to convey this.\n\nNas also puts some wordplay in motion with the phrase “to draw the line”, meaning to set a limit, and the word “hang”. Taking the expressions in a more literal sense, this could be a potential reference to the popular game Hangman.", "annotation_id": "8870761", "url": "https://genius.com/8870761", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Calculating, touching money, these niggas masturbating", "vault_lines": ["Calculating, touching money, these niggas masturbating"], "meaning": "People gaining monetary success tend to crave for more, enjoying remembering themselves  how much money they own, giving them pleasure like a self-serving would do. This is mental, ego masturbation.\n\nSuccess is one tough poison.", "annotation_id": "8522195", "url": "https://genius.com/8522195", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "But we won’t self-indict ourselves Be our own prosecutor, won’t 25-to-life ourselves", "vault_lines": ["But we won't self-indict ourselves", "Be our own prosecutor, won't 25-to-life ourselves"], "meaning": "Nas isn’t the type to get caught slipping bragging about the “activities” he engages in nor would he snitch on himself. Those who do have pretty much done the prosecutor’s job for them and have set themselves up for the dreaded “25 years to life”", "annotation_id": "8420421", "url": "https://genius.com/8420421", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Got a pretty real dope life", "vault_lines": ["Got a pretty real dope life"], "meaning": "Though the line is not grammatically correct the poor sentence structure is used to emphasize the triple entendre on “pretty”.\n\n“Pretty” is used as an adverb meaning “very”, Nas has a very real dope life.  Also, meaning “beautiful” for a more broader aspect of his life.\n\nThe third meaning plays off the previous line where Nas describes how he looks. Well, put together males are often called “Pretty Boys” or simply “pretty.” Based on this description, Nas has a pretty boy lifestyle.", "annotation_id": "8393315", "url": "https://genius.com/8393315", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Some homies of yours bangin’, affiliation on their personal time Conflict with business can hurt down the line It’s something that you ain’t have nothing to do with Your man confrontation end up getting you hit", "vault_lines": ["Some homies of yours bangin', affiliation on their personal time", "Conflict with business can hurt down the line", "It's something that you ain't have nothing to do with", "Your man confrontation end up getting you hit"], "meaning": "Nas speaks on a common scenario often discussed in the rap community. When young rappers from the streets become famous they often continue to associate with their street friends. Because their friends are still living the street life, their friends' baggage can negatively affect them. From a business standpoint, rappers could lose sponsors and do jail time due to their affiliations. Rappers are vulnerable during scheduled appearances (shows, signings, video shoots etc.) to get caught by their friends' rival gangs.", "annotation_id": "8393311", "url": "https://genius.com/8393311", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Closest friends do us best, regardless how I feel", "vault_lines": ["Closest friends do us best, regardless how I feel"], "meaning": "A reference to the Meek Mill and Drake beef. When Meek originally called out Drake for using a ghostwriter, Ross tweeted “Drake>>>>>Meek”,\n\n\n\nand later deleted the tweet. Although as a rapper Ross may feel Drake is better, his friendship with Meek was stronger through MMG so Rick Ross is staying on Meeks side.", "annotation_id": "8340861", "url": "https://genius.com/8340861", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Dice game, head crackers, time to get it back", "vault_lines": ["Dice game, head crackers, time to get it back"], "meaning": "“Dice game, headcrack, it’s time to get it back”\n\nHe’s referring to a headcrack in the dice game “Cee-lo”. A headcrack is to roll a 4-5-6, which is an automatic win. Once he rolls a headcrack, he’ll get the money that he lost back.", "annotation_id": "8326200", "url": "https://genius.com/8326200", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Double-M umbrella, the feds call it tax shelter", "vault_lines": ["Double-M umbrella, the feds call it tax shelter"], "meaning": "Ross' record imprint, MMG, could be seen as a way of avoiding tax by paying it through his company. The “umbrella” metaphor means it is both a large company encompassing various different businesses, and also it “shelters” Ross from tax, like a real umbrella shelters one from the rain.", "annotation_id": "8289814", "url": "https://genius.com/8289814", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Where I come from it’s ruthless, air you out from the Ubers", "vault_lines": ["Where I come from it's ruthless, air you out from the Ubers"], "meaning": "By keeping up with modern technology, Nas has grown more sophisticated. He now commits his drive-by shootings from cars bought with the Uber taxi rank service.", "annotation_id": "8289809", "url": "https://genius.com/8289809", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Weed so loud they got surround sound Smoking good shit ’cause they tryna take me back to some hood shit", "vault_lines": ["Weed so loud they got surround sound", "Smoking good shit 'cause they tryna take me back to some hood shit"], "meaning": "“Loud” is a common description of very strong weed, or in the literal sense Nas is using as a metaphor, a surround sound system on a TV.\n\n\n\nKing Chip’s use of the term on Just What I Am is especially memorable.", "annotation_id": "8277957", "url": "https://genius.com/8277957", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Never let a man take your jewels And if he do, double back, make sure he make the news", "vault_lines": ["And if he do, double back, make sure he make the news"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is saying that never let a man rob you of your chains and other jewels.\n\nJewels is also another name for you “manhood” so Rick Ross is saying never let a man take your manhood away.\n\nAnd if he does, you make sure he’s on the news by, what I presume, catching a body, as Nas would say.", "annotation_id": "8238803", "url": "https://genius.com/8238803", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "Any club, any fly affair If they start shooting all they remember is Nas was there", "vault_lines": ["Any club, any fly affair"], "meaning": "Shootings have occurred at two of Nas' concerts; once during his 2005 show at London’s Brixton Academy (there’s some shaky video footage) and once following his 2014 show with ScHoolboy Q & Flying Lotus near Denver.\n\nPeople may be quick to blame an artist and/or their lyrics if something bad goes down at their show. Nas, however, has always been open about his opposition to gun violence. As he rapped (from the perspective of a gun) on “I Gave You Power”:\n\nI seen some cold nights and bloody days\nThey grab me and bullets spray\nThey use me wrong so I sing this song til this day", "annotation_id": "8229863", "url": "https://genius.com/8229863", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "line": "You could die young out here Mess around and die before 21 out here", "vault_lines": ["Mess around and die before 21 out here"], "meaning": "They say the good die young and with the opening line to his verse Nas reflects on how dangerous things are in the streets— especially in his native Queensbridge Housing Projects.\n\nThings are so bleak that simply making it to see 21-years old is an accomplishment. Nas is no stranger to this truth. His best friend and DJ Willie “Ill Will” Graham, was killed at age 19. His brother, Jungle, was also shot twice growing up.\n\nOn “Life’s a Bitch,” from his debut album Illmatic, Nas begins echoing a similar sentiment.\n\nI woke up early on my born day; I’m 20, it’s a blessing\nThe essence of adolescence leaves my body, no I’m fresh and\nMy physical frame is celebrated ‘cause I made it", "annotation_id": "8220651", "url": "https://genius.com/8220651", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "One of Us", "status": "done", "targets": 21, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-one-of-us-lyrics", "genius_title": "One of Us", "genius_id": 2371931, "source": "api"}
