{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pinned to the Cross", "line": "Racists killing for cotton, niggas fighting for honor", "vault_lines": ["Racists killing for cotton, niggas fighting for honor"], "meaning": "Ross is referencing the racist history of African slavery in the Unites States. Cotton was among the most prominent and profitable crops which slaves picked and were often brutalized and killed over.\n\nMeanwhile, Black people were fighting for their freedom and right to be treated with basic dignity and humanity.", "annotation_id": "24471077", "url": "https://genius.com/24471077", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pinned to the Cross", "line": "Van Jones go get a broom, never making no laws", "vault_lines": ["Van Jones go get a broom, never making no laws"], "meaning": "Ross is referencing CNN contributor Van Jones and the “mess” he made regarding his involvement in helping the Trump administration with police reform. After Trump announced his police reform project following the death of George Floyd, Jones publicly praised Trump and the project, but never disclosed he was involved with secretly working with Trump’s son-in-law and senior adviser Jared Kushner to craft it. Jones' interview with Inside Politics: “The executive order is a good thing, mainly because you saw the support of law enforcement there… There is movement in the direction of a database for bad cops. We have never had a federal database for bad cops, that’s why all these cops go all over the place doing bad stuff… The chokeholds, that’s common ground now between Nancy Pelosi and Trump. Good stuff there.”\nBut then Jones denied that he was involved in crafting the police reform order, tweeting: “I have never been included in any meetings about police reform (not by phone, zoom, nada). I didn’t know what was in the EO until the day it was released.”", "annotation_id": "23398720", "url": "https://genius.com/23398720", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pinned to the Cross", "line": "When you’re forced to be fearless, strangers consider you careless Detrimental to my upbringing, I felt so abandoned", "vault_lines": ["When you're forced to be fearless, strangers consider you careless"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is referencing growing up in the ghettos filled with crime and poverty. Often times children must witness traumatic events (murders, robberies, assaults, divorce, homelessness, etc.) before their brains are fully equipped with the knowledge to heal. This evokes survival instincts in children as they do not have the emotional IQ to handle traumatic events. The results often times are hostility towards others to prove courage, earn a level of respect or instill fear. Strangers with no background knowledge of ones prior traumatic experience can consider him careless.", "annotation_id": "22987451", "url": "https://genius.com/22987451", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pinned to the Cross", "line": "Now we burning the streets, Christ to the cross Our position is blatant (Blatant), rightfully lost Let’s empower the people, no need for signin’ petitions Thieves and Revelations, scriptures keep on blinding these Christians I’m in search of the truth (Truth), we each have a flaw (Flaw)", "vault_lines": ["Now we burning the streets, Christ to the cross", "Our position is blatant (Blatant), rightfully lost", "Let's empower the people, no need for signin' petitions"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is speaking on how Christianity was used to brainwash and enslave the black community. Africans would be forced to convert into Christianity by their Slave Owners. Often times these slave owners would use scriptures to instill fear into slaves. Slave owners would twist scriptures such as Ephesians 6:5-6. This scripture was detrimental to Africans in pursuing their freedom as they would pray to God for freedom from their masters hoping that God would one day set them free. Enabling a victim mentality believing that they could not set themselves free and had no power over their life.  “Thieves and Revelations keep on blinding these Christians”  You see the religion today being misused today to disguise hidden agendas and fool people into buying into ideologies and beliefs blindly. Rick Ross is in search of the truth as he knows he cannot take others words for face value as we each have flaws", "annotation_id": "22987145", "url": "https://genius.com/22987145", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pinned to the Cross", "line": "Still I wish her the best, Ray Charles in the flesh", "vault_lines": ["Still I wish her the best, Ray Charles in the flesh"], "meaning": "Ross claims to be the modern day embodiment of Ray Charles, aka “The Genius”. The 17-time Grammy Award winner is regarded as a legend, an innovator who fused a wide breadth of musical genres in the 1950’s and 60’s, and whose lasting impact on popular music reverberates into the present day.", "annotation_id": "20601038", "url": "https://genius.com/20601038", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pinned to the Cross", "line": "Pavarotti to some, Quincy Jones to the rest", "vault_lines": ["Pavarotti to some, Quincy Jones to the rest"], "meaning": "Ross is equating himself to three highly revered figures in music history, each of whom occupy a unique place in the pantheon of music’s legends. Charles could be understood to represent innovation and genius. Luciano Pavarotti, one of the finest operatic tenors of the 20th century, represents mastery. Quincy Jones represents music production and business savvy.", "annotation_id": "20600944", "url": "https://genius.com/20600944", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pinned to the Cross", "line": "Muddy Waters to marble, set my city aflame", "vault_lines": ["Muddy Waters to marble, set my city aflame"], "meaning": "This is a double entendre: Rick Ross is referencing both blues legend Muddy Waters and the place of their birth—he and Waters hail from the marshlands of the Mississippi Delta, one of the poorest regions in the United States. “Marble” is employed as a status symbol, an expensive stone used as a building material in the dwellings of the rich. “Muddy Waters to marble” thus signifies a journey from poverty to luxury.", "annotation_id": "20595294", "url": "https://genius.com/20595294", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pinned to the Cross", "line": "I’m watchin’ for Karen, she watchin’ bird watchers", "vault_lines": ["I'm watchin' for Karen, she watchin' bird watchers"], "meaning": "Karen is the generic name for white women who call the police for fallacious public disturbances—usually initiated by racist triggers of the subconscious. The lyric references the Central Park bird watching incident where Amy Cooper called the police on a black man, Christian Cooper (no relation), an avid bird watcher, after Mr. Cooper requested that she leash her dog in an area of Central Park where it was required in order to protect bird habitat.", "annotation_id": "20586635", "url": "https://genius.com/20586635", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pinned to the Cross", "line": "Soon as Dre hit the roof, she filed for divorce", "vault_lines": ["Soon as Dre hit the roof, she filed for divorce"], "meaning": "Rick Ross commenting how Dr. Dre’s wife waited until he became a billionaire with Beats headphones to divorce and reach a settlement to take half his money where she is arguing that she signed the prenup due to distress and would want to take more of Dr. Dre’s fortune for herself", "annotation_id": "20586589", "url": "https://genius.com/20586589", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pinned to the Cross", "line": "Such a body of work, my debut was a classic HOV gave me a verse, so that kept it attractive", "vault_lines": ["Such a body of work, my debut was a classic", "HOV gave me a verse, so that kept it attractive"], "meaning": "Ross recalls his debut album Port of Miami, which was released in 2006 and featured a verse from JAY-Z on the remix of the lead single, “Hustlin'.” In 2019, Ross released the sequel to his debut with Port of Miami 2.\n\n“J-Hova,” or “Hova,” is one of JAY-Z’s nicknames—he used the moniker as the title for his December 1999 record, “Hova Song.” The nickname is a play on the Hebrew word for God; Jehovah.", "annotation_id": "20538275", "url": "https://genius.com/20538275", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pinned to the Cross", "line": "Terry Crews is another coon who was basically bought", "vault_lines": ["Terry Crews is another coon who was basically bought"], "meaning": "In the midst of the Black Lives Matter protests in 2020, Terry Crews faced backlash for enforcing the notion of “black supremacy”. Because of this, people on Twitter began labelling him as a “coon” or “Uncle Tom”.\n\nhttps://www.insider.com/terry-crews-twitter-black-supremacy-reactions-2020-6", "annotation_id": "20527613", "url": "https://genius.com/20527613", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pinned to the Cross", "status": "done", "targets": 11, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-pinned-to-the-cross-lyrics", "genius_title": "Pinned to the Cross", "genius_id": 5842972, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pirates", "line": "Got on my mother pearl, she fuckin’ up father time", "vault_lines": ["Got on my mother pearl, she fuckin' up father time"], "meaning": "This is a play on words as “Mother Pearl” refers to the Rolex Datejust watch which is nicknamed the “Mother of Pearl.” He then created a double-metaphor by saying “she fuckin’ up father time” as watches are used to keep time, and the “Mother of Pearl” is typically regarded as a feminine watch for women, which explains why it is “fuckin’ up father time.”", "annotation_id": "37744324", "url": "https://genius.com/37744324", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
