{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pirates", "line": "Steppin’ off the Sonoma with the black duffle bag filled", "vault_lines": ["Steppin' off the Sonoma with the black duffle bag filled"], "meaning": "Likely the GMC Sonoma truck", "annotation_id": "933362", "url": "https://genius.com/933362", "state": "accepted", "votes": -4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pirates", "line": "Had to fuck with the Haitians and break a kilo to crumbles", "vault_lines": ["Had to fuck with the Haitians and break a kilo to crumbles"], "meaning": "Haiti has long been a primary shipping point for cocaine to get into the United States, as this 1988 U.S. government report spells out.  Ross' city of Miami has a large community of Haitians (many of whom live in the city’s Little Haiti neighborhood), and Ross implies a relationship between these two facts here", "annotation_id": "933355", "url": "https://genius.com/933355", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pirates", "line": "I multiply what I manage, I manage to multiply", "vault_lines": ["I multiply what I manage, I manage to multiply"], "meaning": "Ross uses a poetic device known as epanados here, where words are repeated in reverse order", "annotation_id": "933353", "url": "https://genius.com/933353", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pirates", "line": "Seen so many things, be preposterous not to record it", "vault_lines": ["Seen so many things, be preposterous not to record it"], "meaning": "Ross is probably referencing what he saw as a member of the Boobie Boys. Ross was known to be Kenneth “Boobie” Williams' right-hand man/protege, and often references them in his raps.", "annotation_id": "933348", "url": "https://genius.com/933348", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pirates", "line": "Babies be havin’ babies, I’m talkin’ ’bout how I grind", "vault_lines": ["Babies be havin' babies, I'm talkin' 'bout how I grind"], "meaning": "A baby is a half a ki, and if Ross can cook and flip a half to a whole – that’s how he can make a baby have a baby. It’s clear he’s talking about cocaine because he’s referencing how “how he grinds” – and we all know Ross raps about coke.\n\n\n\n\n\nCould also be a reference to Terry Tate’s 1989 hit “Babies Having Babies”.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2s338u1Rrvg", "annotation_id": "933340", "url": "https://genius.com/933340", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pirates", "line": "I’m rollin’ the dice, four, five, six Young nigga, nineteen, four or five bricks", "vault_lines": ["I'm rollin' the dice, four, five, six", "Young nigga, nineteen, four or five bricks"], "meaning": "Rick Ross compares his metaphoric victories in life (rolling four, five, six is a winning combination in the meme dice game cee-lo) to the up-and-coming status of a young man making his first big moves in the drug game", "annotation_id": "933337", "url": "https://genius.com/933337", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pirates", "line": "Gotta silence the lambs, get on my Buffalo Bill", "vault_lines": ["Gotta silence the lambs, get on my Buffalo Bill"], "meaning": "Ross threatens his enemies by comparing himself to the fictional serial killer Jame “Buffalo Bill” Gumb from the movie and novel The Silence of the Lambs.  Another notable reference to this movie in rap is Showbiz & A.G.’s 1992 classic song “Silence of the Lambs”", "annotation_id": "933318", "url": "https://genius.com/933318", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pirates", "status": "done", "targets": 22, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-pirates-lyrics", "genius_title": "Pirates", "genius_id": 80545, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Play Your Part", "line": "I understand that Prada lives in her inner sole So the devil that droves in her got her control Anne Hathaway", "vault_lines": ["I understand that Prada lives in her inner sole", "So the devil that droves in her got her control"], "meaning": "Wale laments over a woman who values material possessions more than her love for others.\n\nUsing the sole/soul homonym to describe her inner beauty, or lack thereof, he also makes reference to the Anne Hathaway movie, “the Devil Wears Prada.”\n\n\n\nEssentially, Wale makes the point that this woman is fueled by negative character associations, instead of genuine goodness.\n\nAnne Hathaway was also the name of Shakespeare’s wife.\n\n\n\nIn an astonishing double entendre, Wale also compares himself in his poetic ability to Shakespeare, commonly considered the greatest writer of all time by invoking Anne Hathaway.\n\nGenius.", "annotation_id": "648882", "url": "https://genius.com/648882", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Play Your Part", "line": "I’m simply fuckin’ with bitches that know they whole position", "vault_lines": ["I'm simply fuckin' with bitches that know they whole position"], "meaning": "He is just messing with girls that know their role, which is to provide him with good sex and to look good on his side when he goes out.", "annotation_id": "550153", "url": "https://genius.com/550153", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Play Your Part", "line": "And sack chasing, getting yacht cake up", "vault_lines": ["and sack chasing, getting yacht cake up"], "meaning": "“And sack chasing getting yacht cake up” meaning saving money to buy him a yacht.", "annotation_id": "550144", "url": "https://genius.com/550144", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Play Your Part", "line": "Told my jeweler make her freeze like I was tryna stick her up", "vault_lines": ["Told my jeweler make her freeze like I was tryna stick her up"], "meaning": "Meek told his jeweler to ice his girlfriend up [and/or adorn his girlfriend with jewelry]\nMeek is also playing the most used word in the Bank Robbers' Dictionary; “Freeze!”, which is usually accompanied by a stick-up.", "annotation_id": "113517", "url": "https://genius.com/113517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Play Your Part", "line": "A lot of women is real, some bitches Robin Given I never give ’em no liquid, no pot to piss in", "vault_lines": ["A lot of women is real, some bitches Robin Given", "I never give 'em no liquid, no pot to piss in"], "meaning": "Wale respects the ladies who deserve it, but disses those who “cry rape” – as did Robin Givens, ex-wife of Mike Tyson (some accuse her of having married Mike to get at his money)\n\n\n\nWale won’t give these golddiggers the time of day! (nor any “liquid” – meaning sperm or currency)\n\nNote the “liquid/piss” play – “a pot to piss in” being a conventional way to say “a home”", "annotation_id": "81022", "url": "https://genius.com/81022", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Play Your Part", "line": "Triple white Ferrari, yeah that’s the Easter Bunny", "vault_lines": ["Triple white Ferrari, yeah that's the Easter Bunny"], "meaning": "The Ferrari is all-white.. like the Easter bunny, who is traditionally all-white as well\n\nJust as even the whitest Easter bunny might have some cute, pink furry paws, Ricky might have some off-colored rims (purple perhaps?)", "annotation_id": "80283", "url": "https://genius.com/80283", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
