{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Walk a day up in my tennis, my soul is possessed", "vault_lines": ["Walk a day up in my tennis, my soul is possessed"], "meaning": "Here tennis refers to his tennis shoes.\n\nSo if you switch places with Wale and look through his perspective you would see that he has many demons.\n\nAlso note the play on soul and shoe sole.", "annotation_id": "1837874", "url": "https://genius.com/1837874", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Lupe, gotta tell ‘em how you livin’", "vault_lines": ["Lupe, gotta tell 'em how you livin'"], "meaning": "Rick asks Lupe how he’s living, more than likely after some kind of poor decisions he has made in his life. This could also mean how the area he’s in is living, more than likely Chicago, Lupe’s hometown.", "annotation_id": "1837846", "url": "https://genius.com/1837846", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Lust apparel who dream of Rolls but can’t Accord Or afford a Dodge or a Ford", "vault_lines": ["Lust apparel who dream of Rolls but can't Accord", "Or afford a Dodge or a Ford"], "meaning": "Some women show made lust for high value apparel and dream of owning a Rolls Royce, but can’t afford to buy basic cars.\n\nAccord takes on two meanings. It means to grant status, and also refers to the low end car.\n\nWale continues the vehicle wordplay with the words “afford” and “a Ford.”", "annotation_id": "1837657", "url": "https://genius.com/1837657", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Where I’m from, I guess that’s how it goes", "vault_lines": ["Where I'm from, I guess that's how it goes"], "meaning": "Whether we like it or not, this kind of activity goes on everyday in impoverished places, it’s almost normal to the people who live there.", "annotation_id": "1837546", "url": "https://genius.com/1837546", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "I think that might be a better description", "vault_lines": ["I think that might be a better description"], "meaning": "Lupe is saying that whatever corruption their may be, it is up to the peoples since of morals to rise up and stop it. Something we seem to have a lack of. So in essence it is just as much our fault we let this happen as the people who put us here. (or maybe we just need to start realizing it)", "annotation_id": "1837054", "url": "https://genius.com/1837054", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Golly, why they watchin’ me?", "vault_lines": ["Golly, why they watchin' me?"], "meaning": "Lupe has been creating quite a Fiasco the past couple of years.\n\nFrom getting kicked off stage at the inaugural ball, supporting Occupy Wall Street, and much criticism to “Words I Never Said” by the media.\n\n\n\nIt appears Lupe will not do these things only when he has immunity; “olly olly oxen free”. We must rely on the people to make changes; government is not always so successful at making the proper ones.\n(hence the reason he is being watched … does Lupe have a secret file like Jay-Z too?)", "annotation_id": "1837039", "url": "https://genius.com/1837039", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Rich niggas makin’ poor decisions", "vault_lines": ["Rich niggas makin' poor decisions"], "meaning": "The listener can assume that Rick Ross is obviously talking about himself, perhaps because of his now infamous molly rap.", "annotation_id": "1836105", "url": "https://genius.com/1836105", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "That’s a poor nigga making rich decisions", "vault_lines": ["That's a poor nigga making rich decisions"], "meaning": "Eluding from the last line, Lupe is saying the rich continue to push their morals aside to rake in more money, although they may not realize it, they cannot afford to keep breaking people down to do so. Just as the rich keep buying more schemes the poor keep themselves stuck in their vicious cycle of killing themselves(whether it be drugs, violence, prostitution) to make a profit.", "annotation_id": "1836037", "url": "https://genius.com/1836037", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Finna D-O-P-E, bob and weave and that boxer boxin’ free", "vault_lines": ["Finna D-O-P-E, bob and weave and that boxer boxin' free"], "meaning": "The line refers to the rope-a-dope, the fighting style made famous by Muhammad Ali in his legendary fight against George Foreman.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EptkotqPIOo\n\nThe technique involves getting into a defensive stance and allowing the opponent to repeatedly hit you until he tires himself out.\n\nLupe is comparing his fight against poverty and the system to the Ali vs Foreman fight. And there were indeed several similarities between the two.\n\nBoth Lupe and Ali were the underdogs, and for both of them there was a more attractive path to take. For Ali, punching back seemed like the glorious thing to do, and in the poverty stricken projects, drug dealing was the more lucrative method used to fight the system (this is where the D-O-P-E comes in).\n\nHowever they both resisted the norm and found a cleverer way to win the situation. Ali put his guards up and tired Foreman out, while Lupe stayed away from the drugs and found a way out through hip hop.", "annotation_id": "1836014", "url": "https://genius.com/1836014", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Label whores who fuck a sale and suck a store", "vault_lines": ["Label whores who fuck a sale and suck a store"], "meaning": "Girls love the brands that cost more; in their mind it makes them more attractive or desirable.\n\nIn fact, they will fuck a sale and suck a store instead of a man. This turns around a few lines later, when they come back to the man’s knob (penis).\n\n\n\nWale’s also comparing the music industry (i.e. Record Labels) to ‘whores’ who insist on old-school practices, focus on sales and stocking physical store shelves, even though the Internet Era has changed the game and provided independent artists with sharper tactics. Food for thought: what are the top sellers of physical music in the US? Answer: Walmart, Target, and Best Buy\n\n\n\nAs it turns out, most of these department stores see marginal revenues from selling music anyway, leading to shrinking “music” sections at the stores (tried buying an indie album at Best Buy lately?) and effectively making Major Record Labels their bitch. Hence a big reason why mainstream music sucked so much in the 2000s post-Napster/piracy aftermath; we were cutting poppy “glam rap” records for the execs who sell us cheap clothes.", "annotation_id": "1835824", "url": "https://genius.com/1835824", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "But I’ll tell you ’bout this hate dude", "vault_lines": ["But I'll tell you 'bout this hate dude"], "meaning": "Wale may not be able to relate to all the struggles of the hood, but he can tell you about its consequence and impact;\n\nThings like drugs and poverty play very divisive roles in the projects; they create power struggles and hate within the people. Wale is presumably calling for unity.", "annotation_id": "1835814", "url": "https://genius.com/1835814", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Coveting cars over community", "vault_lines": ["Coveting cars over community"], "meaning": "We value cars over a sense of community. Hedonism and materialism (of the type often promoted in rap videos) over family, friends and neighbors. By driving around in status symbol cars, these values then infect the next generation further weakening the fabric of communities.\n\nAlso in car dominated cities like Los Angeles there are sharp physical divides between different demographics. As well as promoting sedentary lifestyles, cars make it harder to foster community compared to areas dominated by pedestrians.\n\n#Compare Los Angeles:\n\n\n\n#to Copenhagen:\n\n\n\nNo prizes for guessing which city spends more on rims.", "annotation_id": "1835759", "url": "https://genius.com/1835759", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Poor niggas makin’ rich decisions Can’t afford ‘em but you still gon’ get ’em", "vault_lines": ["Poor niggas makin' rich decisions", "Can't afford 'em but you still gon' get 'em"], "meaning": "Lupe is referring to people who buy things they want rather than things they need.\n\n\n\nThen again, there’s another way to think about this line.\n\nPoor nigga’s makin rich decisions could refer to you being broke but making a wise decision. Not rich as in wealth but rich as in high quality or Smart.\n\nIn that case, the “Cant afford ‘em” would refer to kids. (note that it’s plural not singular like buying an object)\n\nSo this talks about how so many poor people don’t have the means to take care of them, but will still make sacrifices and raise kids.", "annotation_id": "1835717", "url": "https://genius.com/1835717", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "It’s like D-O-C a lottery, that D-O-C be lock and key", "vault_lines": ["It's like D-O-C a lottery, that D-O-C be lock and key"], "meaning": "D-O-C is abbreviated in one form as “drug of choice”. Going back to the ‘odds’ line, Lupe says drug dealing is like a lottery, because so few of them actually make it.\n\nOn top of that its highly dangerous, as it can often lead you to prison (lock and key). D-O-C is also an abbreviation for the Department of Corrections, which oversees the imprisonment of convicts.\n\n\n\nThis could also be a comment on the number of people  wrongly thrown in jail. At times the DOC imprisons people on a whim, as if they were choosing names from a lottery.", "annotation_id": "1835707", "url": "https://genius.com/1835707", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "They say her mother never played her role I heard her mother always on the roll", "vault_lines": ["They say her mother never played her role", "I heard her mother always on the roll"], "meaning": "This talks about the cycle that can play from generation to generation. This girl’s mother never played her part because she was  herself trapped in the above mentioned cycle.\n\n“Rolling” is slang for getting high on ecstasy.\n\nGetting high as a mother is bound to have a negative influence on the children. Seeing their mother in that state, the children are likely to do the same thing when they have kids, thus perpetuating the cycle.\n\nFrom the way Ross pronounces/slurs that last word, it could be either “roll” or “road”. In the case of the latter, the mother is one of many women caught up in Ricky’s touring lifestyle – no wonder she’s wearing gold, that’s how it goes in Ross' world. In this case, “road” probably makes more sense than “roll”, since we’re talking about somebody’s mother and poppin molly/e is more typically associated with kids these days.", "annotation_id": "1835693", "url": "https://genius.com/1835693", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Her mother always wearin’ gold", "vault_lines": ["Her mother always wearin' gold"], "meaning": "This was bound to have a negative influence, and would have no doubt instilled a sense of vanity and materialism in her daughter.", "annotation_id": "1835687", "url": "https://genius.com/1835687", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Another fish in the bowl", "vault_lines": ["Another fish in the bowl"], "meaning": "Like a fish in a bowl she is trapped in the vicious cycle created by her decisions and the hood.\n\nShe makes money by showing off her body to men, and doesn’t know how to get out because that is all she knows.\n\n\n\nAlso similar to Lupe’s line in “Animal Pharm”. These people are kept in the bowl intentionally by those with all intentions of profiting from them. They do they’re best to keep them there so they know no better.", "annotation_id": "1835675", "url": "https://genius.com/1835675", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Now she strippin’ for that pot of gold", "vault_lines": ["Now she strippin' for that pot of gold"], "meaning": "She is stripping with ambitions to one day amass enough money to finally leave and find a better life, but this dream of hers only keeps her running, and she becomes trapped", "annotation_id": "1835668", "url": "https://genius.com/1835668", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Buyin’ jewelry but you know your vision", "vault_lines": ["Buyin' jewelry but you know your vision"], "meaning": "Lupe is referencing the current rise in price of silver and gold that was been occurring for more than a decade. People buy gold and silver at the current price, wait some years until the price of gold/silver has risen, and sell it for a profit. In 2000 gold was around $300 an ounce, but now it is around $1500 an ounce… That’s a huge profit margin.", "annotation_id": "1835661", "url": "https://genius.com/1835661", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Now early 30s, my blood pressure’s incredible Medical, yeah nigga, I’m tellin’ you", "vault_lines": ["Now early 30s, my blood pressure's incredible", "Medical, yeah nigga, I'm tellin' you"], "meaning": "This is a continuation of the previous lines. Now that children develop their bad eating habits; their blood pressure reaches astounding height’s that otherwise would never be reached or at least at a much later age.\n\nKind of ironic with Rick Ross on the track huh?\n\n\n\nLupe, however, is in his early 30’s (as of 2013 he is 31), but he is still in a good physical shape and has good health. He’s a contradiction to the typical person who normally isn’t in good health by this age.\n\nAll of this is basically plays into the good/poor decisions thing. Lupe makes the right choices. Even with food.", "annotation_id": "1835638", "url": "https://genius.com/1835638", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Yeah, on a mission, hah Maybe part two, yeah!", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, on a mission, hah", "Maybe part two, yeah!"], "meaning": "Here Lupe states maybe he could make a part 2 that would state a more postive outcome for the people who make decisions, the people who make decisions that get them ahead in life.\n\nBut considering this is Lupe….he might be stating here that he’s throwing out the idea of actually continuing Food and Liquor II: The Great American Rap Album Pt.1 and could make Pt.2.\n\nOf course as you remember he canceled it, and considering Lupe with his statement’s (ahem retiring cough) I doubt it could mean much.", "annotation_id": "1835586", "url": "https://genius.com/1835586", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "And I’ll show you where they raise a tool", "vault_lines": ["And I'll show you where they raise a tool"], "meaning": "Tool takes on two meanings here:\n\n\nA gun.\nA superficial person who only reflects the views of someone else, thus being used as a tool. This refers to the millions who listen to rap and follow its dictums without thinking for themselves.", "annotation_id": "1835512", "url": "https://genius.com/1835512", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Yeah, poor niggas makin’ rich decisions That shit right there is more efficient", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, poor niggas makin' rich decisions", "That shit right there is more efficient"], "meaning": "The poor have been brainwashed to pursue the rich person’s lifestyle (the lust for vanity over community). This is more efficient for the rich because they make the laws and can keep the poor in their environment (fish bowl) with the illusion that we live in an equal opportunity society.", "annotation_id": "1835468", "url": "https://genius.com/1835468", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Whole hand cards but nothin’ that you can build with? That sound like bullshit from out a bull mouth", "vault_lines": ["Whole hand cards but nothin' that you can build with?", "That sound like bullshit from out a bull mouth"], "meaning": "In a game of cards each person is dealt a specific collection of cards, called “hand”. Here the hand is being compared to the talents and abilities that you inherit at birth.\n\nSome people use their surroundings as an excuse not to make something out of their lives. They focus the negative without realizing that they too have abilities (whole hand) that they could be utilized to make a better life.\n\nGoing back to the previous lines, the “bull” could also be the system, that puts down the people and denies them a well suited job even if they have the adequate skills (the whole hand).\n\nIn a clever line using both the literal and figurative meanings of bullshit, Lupe argues that this is straight up bs, coming directly from the source.\n\n\n\nAlso, bullshit is the name of a card game, where every time someone suspects you of bluffing your cards, they say “bullshit.”  Lupe is really going crazy with these card references.", "annotation_id": "1835459", "url": "https://genius.com/1835459", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "If we hold it close together, we’ll never fold", "vault_lines": ["If we hold it close together, we'll never fold"], "meaning": "More of the cards metaphor. In poker folding is giving up the game prematurely.\n\nLupe is saying that if you hold strong with what you have you’ll never be brought down.\n\n\n\nAlso, Lupe pronounces “close” like “clothes”, which is a play on the word “fold”, and ties into the previous line about keeping out the cold, since clothes keep us warm.", "annotation_id": "1835334", "url": "https://genius.com/1835334", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Lord help us, my generation come to an end", "vault_lines": ["Lord help us, my generation come to an end"], "meaning": "The 80s was the epidemic of crack cocaine. Chances are many of the people, involved in either the trafficking or the usage of the product, are either dead or in jail.", "annotation_id": "1835168", "url": "https://genius.com/1835168", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "I can show you what them blocks buy I can tell you ’bout my block ties I can take you to the far side", "vault_lines": ["I can tell you 'bout my block ties", "I can take you to the far side"], "meaning": "Like most rappers, Ross claims he knows the streets. He can take you along the innermost projects and show you the hidden world of the hood.\n\nWhat them blocks buy: Ross knows the deep details of the drug game. Need to know how much coke is selling for on x street? Rozay is your man.\n\nBlock ties: Ricky knows all about them gangs and street affiliations. It’s required reading.", "annotation_id": "1835127", "url": "https://genius.com/1835127", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "It’ll have a nigga late for the labels or the latest shoes", "vault_lines": ["It'll have a nigga late for the labels or the latest shoes"], "meaning": "This is a criticism of African-Americans that we’ve seen all too often. Many grow complacent and pass up opportunities to get out of their predicament, because of their focus on material items.\nYou’d surprised at the amount of competition for brand ownership that takes place in very unlikely places.\n\nHere label could also refer to music companies. So a potential rapper is giving up his music career because he was too busy blowing his money on shoes.", "annotation_id": "1835124", "url": "https://genius.com/1835124", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "I’m from an era where gold trinkets could buy attention", "vault_lines": ["I'm from an era where gold trinkets could buy attention"], "meaning": "Wale was raised in the late 80s and the early 90s. Now we may have gotten more or less used to the association of hip hop and jewelry, in that era the big chains and jewels were new and gold covered rappers like Slick Rick were novelty acts that attracted a lot of attention.\n\nThis upbringing serves as an explanation for today’s culture of materialism, because the cultures and role models of those days no doubt had a major affect on today’s rappers.", "annotation_id": "1835070", "url": "https://genius.com/1835070", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Once I got hot, they only good option to vent", "vault_lines": ["Once I got hot, they only good option to vent"], "meaning": "More wordplay from Wale. Vent could refer to the releasing of emotions, and the literal pathway of heating that’s found in households and buildings.\n\nOnce he starts achieving greatness, to let off the “heat” they have to just vent.", "annotation_id": "1835053", "url": "https://genius.com/1835053", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Cause we all selfish but livin’ shallow, how we gon’ swim?", "vault_lines": ["Cause we all selfish but livin' shallow, how we gon' swim?"], "meaning": "Wordplay on the meanings of the word ‘shallow’, which refers both to water level and personality.\n\nWale is saying that a superficial and selfish person cannot live with same meaning and dignity as others.\n\nAlso note the way he says “selfish.” He skews it a tad to sound like shellfish to fit the “how we gon' swim when it’s shallow” concept. (Random fact: Shellfish tend to be bottom feeders so shallow water would be a no-no.)", "annotation_id": "1835050", "url": "https://genius.com/1835050", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Caught up in the game now", "vault_lines": [", caught up in the game now"], "meaning": "Several possible meanings:\n\n\nHe’s jailed. This goes back to the “DOC be lock and key”.\nHe’s caught up in the hustling game, which can often keep people “locked” forever in that state of living.\nNow he’s transitioned to being caught up in the rap game, something he loves and that provides much more freedom.", "annotation_id": "1835040", "url": "https://genius.com/1835040", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Yeah, uh, can’t tell you ’bout that H dude", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, uh, can't tell you 'bout that H dude"], "meaning": "An “H Dude” is a either a heroin addict or a homeless person, both of which names have an H in them .\n\nThe themes of poverty and drugs are a prominent part of gangsta rap; Wale doesn’t claim to be thug, and can’t really tell you much about the subjects.", "annotation_id": "1835034", "url": "https://genius.com/1835034", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Look at how we came out like olly olly oxen free", "vault_lines": ["Look at how we came out like olly olly oxen free"], "meaning": "In hide and seek if someone says “olly olly oxen free”, it means you can come out without getting caught.\n\nAlso notice how Lupe pronounces “olly, olly”; he makes it sound like “Ali, Ali”, so it could be an extension of the Muhammad Ali reference.", "annotation_id": "1834935", "url": "https://genius.com/1834935", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Up out them rings like it was Rocky III", "vault_lines": ["Up out them rings like it was Rocky III"], "meaning": "A nod to this scene from Rocky 3, where Rocky throws Thunderlips out of the ring\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHqawQp2nsM#t=6m00s", "annotation_id": "1834926", "url": "https://genius.com/1834926", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "If you don’t believe in God then at least believe in odds", "vault_lines": ["If you don't believe in God then at least believe in odds"], "meaning": "He continues with religious lines. If belief in a God is not going to motivate you to do good and live a straight life, then at least take a look at the odds of what you are trying to do. He is also carrying on the card wordplay… Seeing that poker is a game of odds literally.\n\nThe chances of you “making it” as a drug dealer are very low, and you are more likely to end up dead or in jail. The chances of you getting into college are much higher.\n\nHe’s basically asking you not to use atheism as an excuse to do wrong, because even statistics say that a person of high morals does better in society.\n\n\n\nThis concept is very much in tune with the Pascal’s Wager, which was an argument developed by Blaise Pascal of seventeenth-century France that considers the entirety of human existence to be a cruel game, where every human bets his/her life on whether God exists or not.", "annotation_id": "1834920", "url": "https://genius.com/1834920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "This house of ours is just a house of cards Just without the yards, and nice adjacent parks", "vault_lines": ["Just without the yards, and nice adjacent parks"], "meaning": "A “house of cards” is a house that is always on the brink of collapse. But in the ghetto, most people can’t even afford to live in a house with a yard regardless of how unstable it is, because they live in an apartment in the middle of the hood. This means they have to deal with all of the disfunction and none of the benefits that come from owning a house.\n\n\n\nAnd knowing that Lupe is politically minded and often sneaks references into his lyrics, there’s a good chance that he’s referencing the popular show House of Cards, a political drama revolving around the House of Representatives.", "annotation_id": "1834868", "url": "https://genius.com/1834868", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "We was born black, but that shit’ll make you dark", "vault_lines": ["We was born black, but that shit'll make you dark"], "meaning": "A black person, especially a poor one, generally faces more challenges in life, and often the harsh circumstances make him/her turn to other “darker” means of living, like drug dealing or prostitution.", "annotation_id": "1834853", "url": "https://genius.com/1834853", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Even the tepee is a full house Simple shelter keep you out the cold", "vault_lines": ["Even the tepee is a full house", "Simple shelter keep you out the cold"], "meaning": "The teepee is a conical tent usually associated with Native Americans. When you get poor enough to be out on the cold streets looking for shelter, even a small cloth tent can be viewed as a full and proper house.\n\nThis line ties into the the card metaphor started in the previous lines. In poker a Full House is a hand that contains three matching cards of one rank and two matching cards of another rank. Its a rare hand that comes to the more fortunate players.\n\n\n\nIn addition, the word “tepee” can also be heard as “TP”, the acronym for toilet paper, which plays off of the preceding line that dissembles the word “bullshit.” Bullshit and toilet paper is a pretty natural progression.\n\nBonus: “TP” also forms a double entendre in its own line, as many pranksters enjoy throwing numerous rolls of toilet paper all over neighboring families' residences, or, “Full Houses.” Excuse an additional toilet paper pun: Lupe wiped the floor with this one.", "annotation_id": "1834831", "url": "https://genius.com/1834831", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Mind on the club just to find a little love, my regards", "vault_lines": ["Mind on the club just to find a little love, my regards"], "meaning": "This last card reference (clubs being one of the 4 suits) makes this line a double entendre: Lupe feels that the emptiness felt by many is the motivation behind frequenting clubs", "annotation_id": "1834816", "url": "https://genius.com/1834816", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Yeah, yeah, poor decider since like four, five or Sugar-coated colored edibles Instead of buildin’ up a habit in them vegetables", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, yeah, poor decider since like four, five or", "Instead of buildin' up a habit in them vegetables"], "meaning": "4 and 5 are important years in terms of forming eating habits that could influence your life long health.\n\nAdditionally, 4 to 5 PM is the time when kids make the most important food decisions, as at this time they have their evening snacks, which could range from teeth ruining candy to immunity boosting vegetables.\n\nThis fits the theme of poor decision making, and the candy vs veggies decision could represent any important decision in life where you have to choose between quick gratification and the more difficult path that will ultimately reap its rewards.\n\n\n\nCompare the amount of processed food in this weekly shop for a family in North Carolina (costing approximately $330)\nwith this weekly shop for a family in Cairo, Egypt (costing approximately $65):\n\n\n\nfrom the book: What I Eat, by Peter Menzel and Faith D'Aluisio\n\nAdditional nuances:\n\n\n“4, 5” could be a .45 caliber weapon. This is fits in well with the topics of the song, and the decision would be between a gun toting drug dealing life that provides easy money, and a life of hard work and ambition.\nThe sugar coated edible could refer to an easily digestible radio friendly song, something Lupe tries not to make. This ties into the previous line of “that ain’t why they watchin' me”, implying that there’s more to his music than the typical things that artists use to attract followers.", "annotation_id": "1834790", "url": "https://genius.com/1834790", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Young niggas catchin’ dope charges", "vault_lines": ["Young niggas catchin' dope charges"], "meaning": "Teenagers living in the hood have to resort to dealing drugs to make money to survive. Here Ross says that a lot of these teenagers get caught and are charged with dope charges", "annotation_id": "1834787", "url": "https://genius.com/1834787", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Young niggas havin’ no fathers", "vault_lines": ["Young niggas havin' no fathers"], "meaning": "Young niggas won’t have fathers because their fathers are caught up in gang lives or have been killed by another rival gang member.\n\nOr, perhaps the father decided to abandon the child, thus leaving em fatherless.", "annotation_id": "1834782", "url": "https://genius.com/1834782", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "[Outro: Lupe Fiasco] Ay Ricky, I’ma flip the mission How ‘bout poor niggas makin’ rich decisions?", "vault_lines": ["Ay Ricky, I'ma flip the mission", "How 'bout poor niggas makin' rich decisions?"], "meaning": "In the intro, Rozay says “rich niggas making poor decisions”, but Lupe, always positive, wants to switch this around – instead, poverty-stricken men making choices that will better their lots in life\n\nOn the level of an even deeper social commentary, Lu observes that some poor men focus solely on the materialistic (copping new Js and upgrading their rides), while the wealthy sometimes seem to be “empty” inside, devoid of real emotions\n\nIf one uses the interpretation that Rick Ross' “poor decision” was the infamous UOENO “Date Rape” rap, this can be considered Lupe comforting him, because Rozay started from a hood, as poor as possible, but became rich. To make a poor decision that costs a lot, you need to be rich in the first place, and Ross made rich decisions when he pursued rapping and achieved fame and wealth from it.", "annotation_id": "1834780", "url": "https://genius.com/1834780", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Young thugs with so much talent Young thugs havin’ no balance", "vault_lines": ["Young thugs with so much talent", "Young thugs havin' no balance"], "meaning": "A low bank balance is the motivating factor for many broke people who go into illegal activities. Ross mourns the talent lost in these young thugs who choose the wrong life.\n\nBalance takes on another meaning as well. The dangerous circumstances around them also creates an unstable life with no balance.", "annotation_id": "1834768", "url": "https://genius.com/1834768", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Even with a handful of hearts", "vault_lines": ["Even with a handful of hearts"], "meaning": "The cards again – hearts are one of the four suits in a deck of cards. Even if you’re a loving person, it’s tough to not grow jaded as a Black person in America\n\n\n\nA deck is also split between red and black cards. The cards with hearts are not black, but with when the odds are against you, your chances look dark even when you have a bunch of red cards.\n\nNote that a handful of hearts could equal a flush, which is a great hand to have in poker.", "annotation_id": "1834766", "url": "https://genius.com/1834766", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Rappers influence your shootin’ sprees Turn around and publish bars like it ain’t got shit to do with me Easy to record so ruthlessly, rich niggas", "vault_lines": ["Rappers influence your shootin' sprees", "Turn around and publish bars like it ain't got shit to do with me", "Easy to record so ruthlessly, rich niggas"], "meaning": "This is the crux of Lupe’s verse. He believes that the poorest decisions rappers make is to glorify a violent culture and then turn  their back to it and say “it’s only entertainment.” Interesting that he is sparking this dialogue on a song with Rick Ross, the kingpin of fabricated violent culture.\n\nRecently, Lupe had a twitter debate with another scholarly MC, Talib Kweli. Talib believed that rappers should not be blamed for violence; the culture from where they stem from that created their outlook should be blamed and altered. Lupe on the other hand, respectfully disagreed, saying that rap lyrics are now embedded in that very culture from where other problems arrise.\nhttps://twitter.com/TalibKweli/statuses/318079666051227649\nhttps://twitter.com/LupeFiasco/statuses/318080227769208834\n\nLupe discusses Rick Ross and the topic of this song in this interview.\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UYbW_CwlvaM#t=6m20s\n\nThe line, “Easy to record so ruthlessly”, could be a possible reference to Eazy-E and Ruthless Records.. While not necessarily the first gangsta rappers, the Ruthless Record’s release of N.W.A.’s “Straight Outta Compton” brought the genre notoriety and introduced the music to a global audience.", "annotation_id": "1834737", "url": "https://genius.com/1834737", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "’Cause when your P-O-V is poverty", "vault_lines": ["'Cause when your P-O-V is poverty"], "meaning": "Great wordplay. P-O-V is an acronym for Point of View, and also is the first three letters of Poverty. The moment in life when poverty becomes your only perspective.", "annotation_id": "1834732", "url": "https://genius.com/1834732", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "I can tell you ’bout the Mach Five", "vault_lines": ["I can tell you 'bout the Mach Five"], "meaning": "In Speed Racer, the 2008 car racing film based on a Japanese Manga with the same name, the title character drove a car called the Mach Five.\n\nNow rappers like to brag about their expensive cars, but Rozay takes it a step further by declaring that he has access to a car that doesn’t even exist.\n\nIts kind of like saying he has the batmobile.", "annotation_id": "1834727", "url": "https://genius.com/1834727", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "Huh, rich niggas makin’ poor decisions Rich niggas makin’ poor decisions Rich niggas makin’ poor decisions Damn, check Rich niggas makin’ poor decisions All my little homies up in prison I’ma let you know just how I’m livin’", "vault_lines": ["I'ma let you know just how I'm livin'"], "meaning": "Ross could be alluding to the fact that poor decisions are now accepted and welcomed in society, whether you are a rapper or not.\n\nAlso note the juxtaposition of “rich” being used with “poor,” even though poor in this case doesn’t refer to a lack of wealth, but rather a bad decision.", "annotation_id": "1834713", "url": "https://genius.com/1834713", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "line": "The beloved T-Rex says “Grown man bars is somethin’ you gotta deal with”", "vault_lines": ["The beloved T-Rex says", "\"Grown man bars is somethin' you gotta deal with\""], "meaning": "Jail (grown man bars) is just something you have to deal with when you’re poor and in the hood.\n\nThis line begins the card metaphor that continues throughout the verse. In a card game each person is dealt a certain number of cards. This is similar to the circumstances you are born in.\n\nConsidering the next few lines, the beloved tyrant could be the system, which tries to tell you that you don’t matter.\n\n\n\nAlso, T-Rex is a battle rapper who always starts his verse with the phrase, “Grown man bars is something you gotta deal with”\n\n\n\nLupe calls him ‘beloved’, taking a page out of Loaded Lux’s book, who calls his opponents his beloveds upon battling.\n\nhttp://youtu.be/u1-z2hxXxKg?t=36m52s\n\nLupe is a pretty big fan of this battle, and has given it the ultimate validation by tweeting about it:\n\nthe last verse…chills… SMACK/ URL PRESENTS LOADED LUX VS CALICOE – YouTube … #ThatWork\n\n\nLupe Fiasco (LupeFiasco) on Twitter", "annotation_id": "1834644", "url": "https://genius.com/1834644", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Poor Decisions", "status": "done", "targets": 52, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Wale-poor-decisions-lyrics", "genius_title": "Poor Decisions", "genius_id": 160756, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "Ass so fat need a lap dance", "vault_lines": ["Ass so fat need a lap dance"], "meaning": "Referencing the hook from the 2011 Tyga song, “Lap Dance”.\n\nBenzo, batman, thick ho, lap dance\nAss so fat need a lap dance\nAss so fat I need a lap dance", "annotation_id": "17747764", "url": "https://genius.com/17747764", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "I’m 5'5\" but I could 6-9", "vault_lines": ["I’m 5'5\" but I could 6-9"], "meaning": "Wayne makes a play on numbers. While he is only the average male height he is still tall enough to 69. 69 is a sexual position that involves the couple laying down into a 69 position where they are facing each other crouches performing oral sex on each other, this would obviously be easier if one was taller but Wayne can still do it well despite only being 5'5.\n\nWeezy has rapped about being successful despite not being tall is previous songs, such as Rick Ross’s 2008 song “Luxury Tax”:\n\nThey said I couldn’t play football, I was too small\nThey said I couldn’t play basketball, I wasn’t tall\nThey said I couldn’t play baseball at all\nAnd now every day of my life, I ball", "annotation_id": "14100496", "url": "https://genius.com/14100496", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "Motherfucker I’m on my skateboard Watch me do a trick, ho", "vault_lines": ["Motherfucker I'm on my skateboard", "Watch me do a trick, ho"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is a avid lover of skateboarding. He even has his own private skate park in his house.\n\nIn an interview with ESPN he shows off his skating prowess and explains:\n\nThe moment I decided I wanted to skate I was watching Alex Miller a lot of T.V like I didn’t even know you could do this, I didn’t even know you could jump off stairs and I didn’t know you could skateboard at this age, where’s you parents?\n\nHe also explains:\n\nYou know in music we have restrictions, we have limits that you can’t do this because you want to be considered this or you can’t do this cause you’re gonna be considered that. In skateboarding you can just be free. What I can get from skateboarding that I can’t get from music is natural satisfaction.\n\nOn the cover of his Dedication 4 mixtape he is depicted holding a skateboard.", "annotation_id": "14100436", "url": "https://genius.com/14100436", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "Shout out to Uncle Luke (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to Uncle Luke (Huh)"], "meaning": "“Pop That” samples the 1992 track “I Wanna Rock (Doo Doo Brown)” by rapper Uncle Luke. Ross shouts him out to credit the owner of the original sample.", "annotation_id": "14072571", "url": "https://genius.com/14072571", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "Bitches know I’m that nigga, talkin’ four-door Bugatti", "vault_lines": ["Bitches know I'm that nigga, talkin' four-door Bugatti"], "meaning": "Bugatti is a high-end French car manufacturer popular among rappers. Ross is an avid car collector (you can view his collection here) he owns, several Maybachs, Lamborghinis, BMWs, and of course Bugattis.  His love for cars is so great he even named his Maybach Music Label after the Maybach brand.\n\n\n\nA few months prior to this song’s release in January, 2013  Ross also appeared on the popular Ace Hood song “Bugatti” which also featured Future.", "annotation_id": "14072232", "url": "https://genius.com/14072232", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "Dropped Take Care, bought a mothafuckin’ crib", "vault_lines": ["Dropped Take Care, bought a mothafuckin' crib"], "meaning": "Drake is referencing his second studio album, Take Care which was released on the 15th of November, 2011 through Cash Money Records.\n\nThe sophomore album sold over 631,000 copies in its first week and in August of 2015 it was reported that the album had sold over 4,000,000 copies.\n\nThe album has gone 4X platinum in the U.S due to its very high sale numbers.", "annotation_id": "13232699", "url": "https://genius.com/13232699", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "Bitch, I ball like two eyelids", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, I ball like two eyelids"], "meaning": "Here, Wayne uses the homophone of “I” and “eye” to describe how much he balls. According to his net worth, he definitely has the ballin' aspect covered, much like an eyelid covers an eyeball. Wayne goes on to use other ocular metaphors later in his career.\n\nIf you don’t see what I’m sayin'\nI give your ass a fuckin' eye exam", "annotation_id": "11665026", "url": "https://genius.com/11665026", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "It’s Weezy F, fuck hoes", "vault_lines": ["It's Weezy F, fuck hoes"], "meaning": "One of Lil Wayne’s many nicknames is Weezy F Baby. In this case the F is for “fuck hoes.” It’s been used for “finisher”, “fuck you', and various others.", "annotation_id": "1894698", "url": "https://genius.com/1894698", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "Top off like Wallace", "vault_lines": ["Top off like Wallace"], "meaning": "https://players.brightcove.net/4863540648001/HklmClHkx_default/index.html?videoId=5237526654001", "annotation_id": "1773952", "url": "https://genius.com/1773952", "state": "verified", "votes": 60, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "Got a Brinks truck in my pocket (Pocket)", "vault_lines": ["Got a Brinks truck in my pocket (Pocket)"], "meaning": "A Brinks truck is one that transports large amounts of money to/from places. Essentially, he has a lot of money in his possession.", "annotation_id": "1045116", "url": "https://genius.com/1045116", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "And, nigga, you’s a square And I will twist you like in a Rubik’s", "vault_lines": ["And I will twist you like in a Rubik's"], "meaning": "A Rubik’s Cube is a square puzzle. The goal is to twist in order to match all the colors on every side. Wayne will fuck you up to look like this:\n\n\n\nHe has also used the twist/Rubik’s pun in Birdman’s song “Fire Flame.” .", "annotation_id": "1024491", "url": "https://genius.com/1024491", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "Car’s tinted like Barack", "vault_lines": ["Car's tinted like Barack"], "meaning": "His windows are tinted super dark black just like the President’s windows so no one can see inside.\n\n\n\nThis line may also be referring to the colour of Barack Obama’s skin, meaning he is not fully black but has a ‘tinted’ skin colour, shaded white.", "annotation_id": "964136", "url": "https://genius.com/964136", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "Sorry that’s a habit, smoke a spliff and then I vanish", "vault_lines": ["Sorry that's a habit, smoke a spliff and then I vanish"], "meaning": "Rozay hits it and quits it: smoke’s a bitch’s weed, and then displays his disappearing act.\n\n\n\nOne could even say that he kept a “magician theme” over the last few lines: rabbits, disappearing, smoke?", "annotation_id": "944305", "url": "https://genius.com/944305", "state": "pending", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "Shout out my bitches too (Huh) We the 2 Live Crew Two for me, two for you (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["Shout out my bitches too (Huh)", "We the 2 Live Crew", "Two for me, two for you (Woo)"], "meaning": "Ross puns on the name of his Miami rap forbears, 2 Live Crew.  The Crew’s head honcho was Luther Campbell, a.k.a. Uncle Luke, whose solo work is sampled in this very song", "annotation_id": "927296", "url": "https://genius.com/927296", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "What you twerkin’ with?", "vault_lines": ["What you twerkin' with?"], "meaning": "Twerkin' is dancing that involves showing off your rear…ah hell, just go and watch some\n\nBesides being about twerking, it’s a play on words: “What you working with?” has commonly been used in rap songs to inquire about how well a woman can move her woman parts. French is asking her a question, knowing that the answer will come in the form of POPPING THAT.", "annotation_id": "927285", "url": "https://genius.com/927285", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "Bitch! Stop talkin’ that shit And suck a nigga dick for some Trukfit", "vault_lines": ["Bitch! Stop talkin' that shit", "And suck a nigga dick for some Trukfit"], "meaning": "Wayne is referencing one of the most famous chants in all of bounce music; the New Orleans-created subgenre where Cash Money Records first made its name.  He has called back to this chant on previous occasions. This time, Wayne uses it to reference his own clothing line. (The original is “suck a nigga dick for an outfit.”)", "annotation_id": "926171", "url": "https://genius.com/926171", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "I am rich like a bitch", "vault_lines": ["I am rich like a bitch"], "meaning": "This line makes no sense, partly because it was first said by Lil B. Weezy is infamously known for biting lyrics occasionally, so this shouldn’t be too shocking.\n\nLil B said this line in his song “Pretty Bitch” at the 2:48 mark.\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vAYrMJWvC44\n\nLil B and Lil Wayne worked together on Wayne’s “Grove Street Party” which is on Weezy’s Sorry 4 the Wait mixtape. Lil B spit a very…based verse, and it may have impressed Wayne to the point of using one of his lines.\n\nMost likey, this is actually a reference to “I’m RICHHHHH BITCH”, the classic saying from Chappelle Show.\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kMFJBd40Awo", "annotation_id": "852007", "url": "https://genius.com/852007", "state": "accepted", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "I’m a beast, I’m off the leash", "vault_lines": ["I'm a beast, I'm off the leash"], "meaning": "Very reminiscent of Wayne’s verse on We Takin' Over:\n\nI am the beast, feed me rappers or feed me beats  I’m untamed, I need a leash", "annotation_id": "844634", "url": "https://genius.com/844634", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "It’s Truk the world It’s Truk yo girl", "vault_lines": ["It's Truk the world", "It's Truk yo girl"], "meaning": "Wayne likes to use the word “Truck” instead of “fuck” as the slogan for his clothing brand Trukfit.\n\nHe uses it the exact same way in “My Homies Still.”", "annotation_id": "844073", "url": "https://genius.com/844073", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Pop That", "line": "YMCM beat that pussy up, stop playin’", "vault_lines": ["YMCM beat that pussy up, stop playin'"], "meaning": "Young Money Cash Money Billionaires (YMCMB) is Wayne’s label.\n\n\n\nHe plays off the last letter in “YMCMB”. Instead of stating the “B” he uses a word that starts with the letter “b”. .", "annotation_id": "843982", "url": "https://genius.com/843982", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
