{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Atlanta housewives takin’ pictures in my section", "vault_lines": ["Atlanta housewives takin' pictures in my section"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is centered in Atlanta. He’s referring to connection with the A List of Atlanta, housewives or possibly connection with the Real Housewives of Atlanta. Ross’ section, referring to his grand sections of clubs and at basketball games, emphasizing his extravagant wealth.", "annotation_id": "16744064", "url": "https://genius.com/16744064", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Cartier, Hublot, I could name a dozen", "vault_lines": ["Cartier, Hublot, I could name a dozen"], "meaning": "Rozay hasn’t been one to shy away from showing off his expensive luxury watch and jewelry collection and is saying he has owned so many, he could go on naming more than just a couple of these luxury watchmaker brands.\n\n\n\nCartier, pronounced (car-tee-ay) is a French luxury goods company that specializes in jewelry manufacturing, watchmaking and retailing. Founded 1847, in Paris, France, by Louis-François, Cartier is one of the most prominent brands in luxury watchmaking.\n\n\n\nHublot, pronounced (who-blow) is a Swiss luxury watchmaker founded in 1980 by Carlo Crocco.\nHublot, located in Nyon, Switzerland, solely produces wristwatches and is also one of the leaders in the watchmaking industry.", "annotation_id": "12127486", "url": "https://genius.com/12127486", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Club LIV, Louis shots on a Sunday", "vault_lines": ["Club LIV, Louis shots on a Sunday"], "meaning": "Club Liv is a famous nightclub in Miami (the city being referenced throughout this song), and Louis shots are a reference to Louis XIII which is the most expensive grade of Remy Martin cognac. It’s about 15 times more expensive than XO grade cognac. It retails for about $2,500 a bottle but a shot in an expensive bar or club can cost upwards of $300.\n\n\n\n\n\nThe Teflon Don at Club Liv (Love how the white chick is just staring at him. He gon' tap that tonight ;)", "annotation_id": "536493", "url": "https://genius.com/536493", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Platinum 21, it’s time to go and spoil shorty", "vault_lines": ["Platinum 21, it's time to go and spoil shorty"], "meaning": "Kamal’s 21 (formerly Platinum 21) is a strip club in Atlanta, GA. Ross likes to tip the strippers generously.", "annotation_id": "536250", "url": "https://genius.com/536250", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Flossin’ out in Compton like I got a cape", "vault_lines": ["Flossin' out in Compton like I got a cape"], "meaning": "He flosses his jewelry in a part of the country that’s well-know for it’s dangerous streets. He acts as if he’s invincible and no one can or will try to jack him.", "annotation_id": "535651", "url": "https://genius.com/535651", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "RoseMo tried to fight it but barely, niggas fade", "vault_lines": ["RoseMo tried to fight it but barely, niggas fade"], "meaning": "Brick Squad affiliate and gang member RoseMo 700 “allegedly” knocked out The Game late summer 2011 in a LA mall. He continued “handing out fades” like a barbershop until he was murdered in Inglewood November 2011. “But barely niggas fade” refers to how people don’t fight anymore; they shoot.", "annotation_id": "535639", "url": "https://genius.com/535639", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Grandfather deal for the Godfather", "vault_lines": ["Grandfather deal for the Godfather"], "meaning": "The ‘grandfather (clause)’ deal might refer to some sort of court arrangement that his money allowed him to work out with the judge. Grandfather clause is a legal term used to describe a situation in which an old rule continues to apply to some existing situations, while a new rule will apply to all future situations.\n\nThis line is more than likely in reference to the Maybach Music Group record deal Rick Ross signed with Warner Bros. From an era where rap labels were born with of 50/50 splits, distribution only ventures, favorable royalties and alike (ala Roc-a-fella, G-Unit, Cash Money); Ross claims to have some of these stipulations grandfathered in to his Contract. A rare occasion in today’s economy.", "annotation_id": "533068", "url": "https://genius.com/533068", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Back of the Phantom, couldn’t fathom, I was such a student", "vault_lines": ["Back of the Phantom, couldn't fathom, I was such a student"], "meaning": "While riding in the back of a Rolls Royce Phantom luxury car (probably for the first time w/ his new cash lifestyle) he found it hard to fathom this new life style he was about to embrace that came with the money, especially since he still felt like a student of the rap game.", "annotation_id": "533067", "url": "https://genius.com/533067", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Can’t talk snow, where the soft at? Your man got murked but you squashed that Hope you know what we call that I think you know just what we call that...", "vault_lines": ["Can't talk snow, where the soft at?", "Your man got murked but you squashed that", "Hope you know what we call that", "I think you know just what we call that..."], "meaning": "Another shot at Jeezy?? This is referring to the situation where allegedly BMF ordered Jeezy to rob Gucci and as a result one of Jeezy’s homie got killed which eventually led to the situation being squashed. We all know retaliation is a must but the situation was squashed so Rozay is just pointing out the fact that Jeezy is basically pussy.", "annotation_id": "532255", "url": "https://genius.com/532255", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Count a million cash, can you blog that?", "vault_lines": ["Count a million cash, can you blog that?"], "meaning": "Ross addresses his internet haters who go online to talk bad about him. He counts a million dollars in cash as a testament of his success, and staying behind the computer talking shit on blogs isn’t going to help you see such cash.", "annotation_id": "531601", "url": "https://genius.com/531601", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Now these thugs actors all of a sudden", "vault_lines": ["Now these thugs actors all of a sudden"], "meaning": "The same rappers who claim to be thugs are in Hollywood (i.e. Ice T’s “Cop Killer”). Now Ice plays a cop on Law and Order. (Whatever you have to do to pay the bills!)\n\nThis is also a subtle shot at Young Jeezy who starred in his  own mini-movie entitled .38. This shot could also be directed towards Ross' former rival 50 Cent, who started as a gangsta rapper, and eventually starred in movies such as Get Rich or Die Tryin and All Things Fall Apart.", "annotation_id": "531568", "url": "https://genius.com/531568", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "I’m screamin’ YOLO in the V-I", "vault_lines": ["I'm screamin' YOLO in the V-I"], "meaning": "Ross is screaming “The Motto” while balling in the V-I.\n\nHe leaves us guessing as to what these letters mean. They could refer to a VI[P] section, a G6 private jet, his Rolls Royce Phantom VI, or even the Virgin Islands.", "annotation_id": "531566", "url": "https://genius.com/531566", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Your shit pushed back cause it ain’t buzzin’", "vault_lines": ["Your shit pushed back cause it ain't buzzin'"], "meaning": "Young Jeezy shot? TM103 seemed to have almost ten release dates!\n\n\n\nAlso a possible shot at FIFTY\n\n\n\nAside from the obvious music industry reference, having one’s ‘shit pushed back’ is a reference to shooting someone (in the head). A buzz cut is a popular haircut.", "annotation_id": "531524", "url": "https://genius.com/531524", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Me and niggas your type never exchange numbers", "vault_lines": ["Me and niggas your type never exchange numbers"], "meaning": "Ross doesn’t exchange numbers with lames or strangers as he said in “Triple Beam Dreams”:\n\nRule number 1: I can’t do business with a stranger", "annotation_id": "531519", "url": "https://genius.com/531519", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "White sheet, yellow tape, where your dawgs at?", "vault_lines": ["White sheet, yellow tape, where your dawgs at?"], "meaning": "Referring to the police tape at a crime scene and the cloth used to cover the homicide victim. Where were your homeboys when the guns popped off?", "annotation_id": "531515", "url": "https://genius.com/531515", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Stop dealin’ with them niggas, you need one soldier", "vault_lines": ["Stop dealin' with them niggas, you need one soldier"], "meaning": "Ross is saying that his girl of interest needs to stop messing with men who have no future and be with him instead.\n\nAnd when paired with the preceding line, this is a nice combination of wordplay, flow and compound rhymes.", "annotation_id": "531458", "url": "https://genius.com/531458", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "She wanna cum, maybe come over", "vault_lines": ["She wanna cum, maybe come over"], "meaning": "Ross is using wordplay with “cum/come” to describe that his girl of interest is very horny and wants to orgasm–and that if she does–then she should come over to his house to do the deed.", "annotation_id": "531452", "url": "https://genius.com/531452", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Order some more bottles because I’m on one", "vault_lines": ["Order some more bottles because I'm on one"], "meaning": "Ross wants to continue partying and drinking to keep his buzz going.\n\nHe is also referring to DJ Khaled’s “I’m On One”, where he appeared on the track with both Drake and Lil' Wayne.", "annotation_id": "531378", "url": "https://genius.com/531378", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Cigarette speedboats but he’s still with the thugs", "vault_lines": ["Cigarette speedboats but he's still with the thugs"], "meaning": "This is continuing the question of whether or not Ross is dealing drugs since he is still seen with thugs even though he can afford expensive luxuries such as cigarettes speedboats (so called because they are very narrow to cut through the water at speed)", "annotation_id": "531375", "url": "https://genius.com/531375", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Worth forty Ms, is he still dealin’ drugs?", "vault_lines": ["Worth forty Ms, is he still dealin' drugs?"], "meaning": "Ross is referring to people who still wonder if he’s dealing drugs even though his net worth is worth $40 million dollars.\n\nRoss is actually worth $25 mills, so perhaps he has answered his own question if he’s still dealing drugs – he’s legally worth $25 million but claims to be worth $40 million, so that $15 million difference is the undeclared profit made from selling drugs.", "annotation_id": "531366", "url": "https://genius.com/531366", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Huh! A hundred mil ain’t enough Got a hundred women, gotta deal with it, love", "vault_lines": ["Huh! A hundred mil ain't enough", "Got a hundred women, gotta deal with it, love"], "meaning": "He is saying that making $100 million dollars isn’t enough, he wants to make more. This goes with the concept of the song in terms of being rich forever (which I don’t know about you, but $100 million is cool with me).\n\nHe is also saying that he has and/or is pimpin' 100 different women, and that the current chick (calling her “love”) he’s with has to deal with it.\n\nThese lines are also connected with wordplay of “100” (or, uh, I guess you could actually call that number-play?)", "annotation_id": "531362", "url": "https://genius.com/531362", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Lucian Grainge from the ghetto,", "vault_lines": ["Lucian Grainge from the ghetto,"], "meaning": "Grainge is the Chairman and CEO of Universal Music Group, where Island/Def Jam is a subsidiary among the list of their other numerous acquired labels. Grainge was the individual that offered Ross his deal with Island/Def Jam. Ross has the same mentality and power as the CEO, but with a less suburban upbringing.", "annotation_id": "531330", "url": "https://genius.com/531330", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "They call me Mr. Roberts when I’m in the bank", "vault_lines": ["They call me Mr. Roberts when I'm in the bank"], "meaning": "Ross is referring to his real name, William Leonard Roberts II.\n\n\n\nNot to mention, everyone properly addresses him since he holds a large amount of cash in their bank, which helps supply everyone’s salary.", "annotation_id": "531309", "url": "https://genius.com/531309", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "You only live once", "vault_lines": ["You only live once"], "meaning": "“You only live once: that’s the motto nigga, YOLO” Is the name of the collaborative mixtape Rick Ross and Drake are working on. #YOLO", "annotation_id": "531191", "url": "https://genius.com/531191", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "line": "Digits like the Dodgers when it come to franks", "vault_lines": ["Digits like the Dodgers when it come to franks"], "meaning": "Rick Ross pulls off a triple entendre:\n\n\n\nDodger Dogs are the highest selling hot dogs (aka franks) at any Major League Baseball stadium.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWealthy Dodger’s owner and chairman Frank McCourt, bought the team for $430 million. That’s a lot of digits. (The Dodgers have since filed for Chapter 11 Bankruptcy, which goes against the idea of being rich forever)\nIf you don’t pay taxes, you are known as a tax dodger. This is a common for those who obtain their money through “nontraditional” ways such as selling drugs.\n\n\n\n$100 bills are called Franklins, or franks for short because they feature a picture of Benjamin Franklin.", "annotation_id": "531103", "url": "https://genius.com/531103", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Forever", "status": "done", "targets": 26, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-rich-forever-lyrics", "genius_title": "Rich Forever", "genius_id": 62985, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Then I gave Meek Mill a Range Rover", "vault_lines": ["Then I gave Meek Mill a Range Rover"], "meaning": "Ross gifted Meek Mill a black Range Rover for Meek’s 25th birthday in 2012. https://youtu.be/PFfidLoxPro", "annotation_id": "26875700", "url": "https://genius.com/26875700", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "All my shit went gold, nigga", "vault_lines": ["All my shit went gold, nigga"], "meaning": "Ross is no stranger to earning gold certifications.  His 2006 debut Port of Miami is platnium, his sophomore album Trilla is gold, his third studio album while not gold did sell 434K copies. His 4th and 5th albums Teflon Don and God Forgives, I Don’t also both went gold.\n\nIronically, Mastermind did not get any certifications although it has sold 400K copies.  Even more unfortunately he hasn’t had a gold album since this line was recorded.", "annotation_id": "14101138", "url": "https://genius.com/14101138", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Screamin’ in my sleep, I know the Lord hear me", "vault_lines": ["Screamin' in my sleep, I know the Lord hear me"], "meaning": "Ross is suggesting that in his sleep, he finds himself haunted by the acts that he’s either observed or committed in the past, ie. the death that he’s witnessed on the streets.\n\nWhilst Ross does boast about his struggle on the rise to the top, as it isn’t considered “gangsta” to be afraid of these pasts, he hides the realities until he cannot escape them in his dreams. He hides his fear from the world, however…\n\n“O Lord, you have examined my heart and know everything about me.”\n\n - Psalm 139: 1-5\n\nWe can infer how it is impossible to escape the omniscient nature of the Lord and subsequently, Ross cannot repress his fear of his past and hide it from God.\n\nIn short, Ross is haunted by his past and the Lord knows it, there’s not hiding from him.", "annotation_id": "2987121", "url": "https://genius.com/2987121", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Got the cubans, got the boats, got the zoes, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Got the cubans, got the boats, got the zoes, nigga"], "meaning": "He’s talkin about getting a shipment of drugs", "annotation_id": "2878076", "url": "https://genius.com/2878076", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "AK’s in his heyday, nigga", "vault_lines": ["AK's in his heyday, nigga"], "meaning": "A heyday is a period of great prosperity and productivity. Without his AK’s, his days just wouldn’t be as great as they could be.", "annotation_id": "2877985", "url": "https://genius.com/2877985", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "If you don’t know I’m finna break it down", "vault_lines": ["I'm finna break it down"], "meaning": "MasterMind has a heavy 90s vibe—but even moreso is heavily influenced by The Notorious BIG—Rozay remade BIG’s Nobody track, and recruited Diddy to be the album’s executive producer.\n\nThus, it is no coincidence that the album’s introductory lines are a rendition of Biggie’s ultra famous line, “If you don’t know, now you know, nigga!”", "annotation_id": "2874573", "url": "https://genius.com/2874573", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "You still smokin’ weed on your car chase", "vault_lines": ["You still smokin' weed on your car chase"], "meaning": "This is a subliminal hit, because although Ross isn’t directly saying it, he’s implying that while you are still in a car chase smoking weed, he’s doing molly and cocaine among other things whilst on a chase of his own, metaphorically and literally saying he’s on to bigger and better and harder things.", "annotation_id": "2868917", "url": "https://genius.com/2868917", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "It’s hard for a young black executive Can’t you see we’re all fucking relatives?", "vault_lines": ["It's hard for a young black executive", "Can't you see we're all fucking relatives?"], "meaning": "Ross emphasizes the difficulty in being black and successful in a white driven society, so blacks need to work together to help each other.", "annotation_id": "2863439", "url": "https://genius.com/2863439", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "New Presidential had that pavé", "vault_lines": ["New Presidential had that pavé"], "meaning": "The lyric is New Presidential had that pavé.\n\nPavé is a way to cover something in diamonds. Presidential refers to a Rolex.\n\nSo Ross has a new Presidential watch covered in diamonds like this one:", "annotation_id": "2860556", "url": "https://genius.com/2860556", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Cocaine worth much more than gold, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Cocaine worth much more than gold, nigga"], "meaning": "Actually, the validity of this claim is highly variable and dependent on the region and point in time in which the cocaine is sold.\n\nPrice of gold for example, reached nearly $1900/oz during the height of the U.S. Recession.\n\n\n\nWhile cocaine prices in the U.S are on a steady decline. The price for cocaine per gram is now supposedly ~$20 when in the 80s, a gram of white could have costed up to $600.\n\nRicky Rozay’s claim that “cocaine is worth much more than gold” is an exaggeration at best and cannot be validated unless the context is fully understood.", "annotation_id": "2859783", "url": "https://genius.com/2859783", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "[Produced by Black Metaphor]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "During an interview with XXL Magazine Black Metaphor explained how the track came about:\n\n\nIt all came together last year. My manager had hit me and told me that Ross had recorded to one of my tracks. So I was like, cool. But as the year went by, I didn’t get any word from him. So I think he had a couple release dates that they pushed back. So when they pushed it back the final time, they contacted me on December 23rd, right before Christmas, telling me that they was going to use it.\n\nI’m really one of them producers who just make a lot of beats. So I just happened to find a good sample Average White Band, “Soul Searching” and I wanted to take it and give it kind of a musical feel. At the same time it’s still hard. I still wanted the drums to be on. [But] He definitely took it to another level.\n\nIt’s cool; the process is pretty simple and straightforward. It wasn’t a whole lot of difficulty or anything. I’m definitely looking forward to doing more work with him in the future.", "annotation_id": "2859460", "url": "https://genius.com/2859460", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Told Warner Brothers that the game’s over", "vault_lines": ["Told Warner Brothers that the game's over"], "meaning": "Rick Ross' label, MMG, moved from Warner Brothers to Atlantic Records in late December – still under the same Warner Music Group umbrella, though.", "annotation_id": "2857622", "url": "https://genius.com/2857622", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "I’m pullin’ off the car lot screaming: “God’s great!”", "vault_lines": ["I'm pullin' off the car lot screaming: \"God’s great!\""], "meaning": "Ross subscribes to the Gilded Age belief that one’s personal wealth represents favor in God’s eyes:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ykn8YcIbmfo\n\nA shoutout to MMG’s Rockie Fresh song “God is Great” from Self Made 3.", "annotation_id": "2857592", "url": "https://genius.com/2857592", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Young mogul,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Pretty self explanatory, Ross impressively put together one of the hottest coalitions in rappers in the game today, Maybach Music Group. Hard to argue calling him mogul when you look at all the work that’s been spawned from his formation of MMG.", "annotation_id": "2854593", "url": "https://genius.com/2854593", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Chasing me a hundred million, that’s inshallah Fresh up out the Feds system, welcome home Jabar", "vault_lines": ["Chasing me a hundred million, that's inshallah", "Fresh up out the Feds system, welcome home Jabar"], "meaning": "Insha'allah is Arabic for “If God (Allah) wills it” usually said when you are hopeful for something (e.g “I’m gonna ace this test tomorrow insha'allah”) so Rozay is hopefully gonna  get himself that 100 mil.\nAlso Ross' long time friend Jabar is welcomed back and pretty quick at that! Last we heard, Jabar was facing twenty-five years! Note Jabar is a practicing Muslim considering the following line hence Rozay saying “insha'allah”\nThis line and the next line probably refers to how convicts turn to religion (and especially convert to Islam) while in prison.", "annotation_id": "2854501", "url": "https://genius.com/2854501", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Bo Jackson, I’m a switch hitter", "vault_lines": ["Bo Jackson, I'm a switch hitter"], "meaning": "Bo Jackson is a retired professional baseball and football player. He is the only athlete to be named an All-Star in two major American sports. He was named the greatest athlete of all time by ESPN.\n\n\n\nIn baseball, a switch-hitter is a player who bats both right-handed and left-handed – right-handed against left-handed pitchers, and left-handed against right-handed pitchers. Ross can do his thing on the rapping end to make money, but don’t doubt his business smarts as well.\n\nWe also know Ross is a huge 2Pac fan, so this may be a nod to his classic “How Do U Want It” In it, Pac says:\n\nI’m hittin' switches on bitches like I been fixed with hydraulics.", "annotation_id": "2853785", "url": "https://genius.com/2853785", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Gangsta, nigga Rich is gangsta, nigga This is gangsta, nigga Yeah, rich is gangsta, nigga You know where we came from Look where we at", "vault_lines": ["Rich is gangsta, nigga", "This is gangsta, nigga", "Yeah, rich is gangsta, nigga", "You know where we came from", "Look where we at"], "meaning": "The title of this song, “Rich Is Gangsta”, was a quote that Ross used in his explanation of why he canceled the 2012 MMG tour.\n\nAnd to me, Rich Is Gangsta. Loyal is gangsta. You understand? Family is gangsta. If that’s not gangsta, I don’t wanna be gangsta. I'ma BOSS.\n\nhttp://youtu.be/2Pymwqq7Gk0?t=3m43s", "annotation_id": "2853417", "url": "https://genius.com/2853417", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "I just upped my stock, fuck them cops If you love hip-hop, bust them shots", "vault_lines": ["If you love hip-hop, bust them shots"], "meaning": "These lines were used at the end of the “Mastermind Intro”, and serve as the introductory lines of album’s first verse.", "annotation_id": "2853409", "url": "https://genius.com/2853409", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "I watch him pray five times a day, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I watch him pray five times a day, nigga"], "meaning": "One of the 5 pillars of Islam includes praying 5 times a day. Muslims are required to pray facing towards Mecca 5 times a day.", "annotation_id": "2852554", "url": "https://genius.com/2852554", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "They want me face down on the pavement", "vault_lines": ["They want me face down on the pavement"], "meaning": "This is not an exaggeration – Ross was the victim of an attempted drive-by\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FT6dIcMi0qw\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_I3-rO587nc", "annotation_id": "2852325", "url": "https://genius.com/2852325", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Gang members claimin’ I need to make a payment", "vault_lines": ["Gang members claimin' I need to make a payment"], "meaning": "Rick Ross upset gang members when he dropped his 2010 song “B.M.F.”, where he name-drops criminal masterminds Big Meech and Larry Hoover. He later released a mixtape with his face in a 6 point star, the logo of the Gangster Disciples. GD members wanted Ross to pay for the privelage of using their name\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qg4xHfam6aM", "annotation_id": "2852276", "url": "https://genius.com/2852276", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "I’m spittin’ like this bitch a mixtape ’Til they seen a Ghost with a temp plate", "vault_lines": ["I'm spittin' like this bitch a mixtape", "'Til they seen a Ghost with a temp plate"], "meaning": "Often rappers' mixtapes are seen as superior to their albums. This was even the case with Ross himself when he dropped his mixtape Rich Forever (which was very well-received) in 2012 to build up hype for his incoming album God Forgives, I Don’t (which was seen as a bit of a disappointment by many fans).\n\nThis is mainly because labels force artists to compromise their work and do things such as get popular features, make catchy hit singles, etc. Which is an issue that doesn’t occur with mixtapes due to them being released for free; therefore they have no label interference and allow the artist to do what they truly want.\n\n\n\nRoss promises that this time his album will be completely raw and uncompromised, hence he’s “spitting like it’s a mixtape,” though this time it’s earning him the big bucks—enough to buy a Rolls Royce Ghost", "annotation_id": "2851317", "url": "https://genius.com/2851317", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "No Adidas but I rock a brick a day", "vault_lines": ["No Adidas but I rock a brick a day"], "meaning": "Playing off of the previous line, Rick Ross references one of Run D.M.C.’s most popular songs, “My Adidas”. The crew was known to frequently kick it in Adidas jumpsuits.\n\n\n\nRozay, however, isn’t rocking the Adidas but rather claims to be rocking a brick of cocaine on the daily. Rick Ross frequently makes references in his songs to selling cocaine and even titled a song after the act.", "annotation_id": "2850707", "url": "https://genius.com/2850707", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Your man is priceless, if your man’s loyal Better give that man a raise or end up paying for it", "vault_lines": ["Your man is priceless, if your man's loyal", "Better give that man a raise or end up paying for it"], "meaning": "The Bawse praises loyalty in his music biz dealings as well as his street ones.  DJ Khaled recently talked about how he and Ross value that quality:\n\nEver since [the “We Takin' Over” video shoot in 2007], me and Ross been loyal to each other and there’s nothing he won’t do or nothing I won’t do for each other. We don’t say the word ‘no’ to each other. We just do it", "annotation_id": "2850651", "url": "https://genius.com/2850651", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "If you cut it, call it Jam Master Jay", "vault_lines": ["If you cut it, call it Jam Master Jay"], "meaning": "Jam Master Jay = DJ for Run-D.M.C.(cuts and scratches records)\n\n\n\nCut = term for how cocaine is mixed with other substances", "annotation_id": "2850636", "url": "https://genius.com/2850636", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Feds tore apart the squad, nigga That’s why I had to play the part, nigga That wasn’t me, it was a job, nigga It gets deeper, that was just a start, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Feds tore apart the squad, nigga", "That's why I had to play the part, nigga"], "meaning": "Here, Rick Ross refers to his past as a CO and his motivation of becoming one.\n\nKenneth “Boobie” Williams was not only Ross' mentor, but he was also the leader of the Boobie Boys – the infamous drug gang based in Carol City. The feds apprehended him and he was given a life sentence. After Boobie’s arrest, the Boobie Boys gang slowly disintegrated.\n\nIn an interview with Rolling Stone, Ross admitted that he was told take a job after one of his close friends was arrested. Although he did not truly want to, Ross decided to “take one for the team” and take a job as a CO in order to look after his incarcerated gang-mates. Like a true BOSS, Ross was always putting his people’s needs first, his needs second.\n\nOn the release of Mastermind, Ross confirmed that this line was about his past as a CO in an interview with MTV. He went on to add\n\nI wouldn’t change nothing ‘cause to me it’s about who Rozay is. […] People know it’s authentic right here. They know it’s real. So it was nothing that I was personally threatened with. I was just ready to move on. Let’s go get this money. Let’s make this music. And right now, to feed my family, I’d do it again. To feed my kids, I’d go get it. We gon’ get it. […]", "annotation_id": "2850459", "url": "https://genius.com/2850459", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Relatively easy, we can go to war Fuck it, we can go to war", "vault_lines": ["Relatively easy, we can go to war", "Fuck it, we can go to war"], "meaning": "When meeting with us here at Rap Genius, Ross denoted what truly makes him ready to go to war!", "annotation_id": "2850457", "url": "https://genius.com/2850457", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Talk about the jewels outta nigga reach Nigga, please, so I came back with a bigger piece", "vault_lines": ["Talk about the jewels outta nigga reach", "Nigga, please, so I came back with a bigger piece"], "meaning": "At the beginning of the Rick Ross – 50 cent beef, 50 stated that Rozay’s chains were either fake or rented and had court records to prove it. Rozay’s now suggesting that they can check again.\n\nIn effect, if you think there are “jewels outta reach” (i.e. out of his price range), you’re mistaken, as he has returned with an even bigger piece of jewelry.", "annotation_id": "2850361", "url": "https://genius.com/2850361", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Elevators like Frank’s on Scarface", "vault_lines": ["Elevators like Frank's on Scarface"], "meaning": "Referring to the classic kingpin movie, Scarface:\n\nIn the film, Frank is a wealthy drug lord, meaning he lives up in high penthouses that require elevators to ascend to. Ross has made so much money off of rapping and drugs, he is living the same lifestyle as Frank, meaning he too lives in skyscraper penthouses.", "annotation_id": "2850245", "url": "https://genius.com/2850245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Before the crib, you gotta clear the guard’s gate", "vault_lines": ["Before the crib, you gotta clear the guard's gate"], "meaning": "He’s got such a nice house that before it you must go through a guards gate, while also implying that the card is there for his protection, because someone is always trying to knock him off.\n\n\n\nRoss relates this to Scarface in the next line.", "annotation_id": "2849963", "url": "https://genius.com/2849963", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Rich Is Gangsta", "line": "Like a G, I gave the Cartier to Wale", "vault_lines": ["Like a G, I gave the Cartier to Wale"], "meaning": "Rick Ross welcomed Wale to MMG by giving him a 44K Cartier watch\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YYosr9cpIQ4", "annotation_id": "2823668", "url": "https://genius.com/2823668", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
