{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Running the Streets", "line": "Maybach Music", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This is the well-known ad-lib of Maybach Music Group, a record label founded by Rick Ross in 2009. It is voiced by Jessica Gomes.", "annotation_id": "31247879", "url": "https://genius.com/31247879", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Running the Streets", "line": "But would you still be fuckin’ with me If I was wearin’ the same jeans for a week? If I was hungry and I ain’t have nothing to eat? Would you, yeah, would you still think about it when you up?", "vault_lines": ["But would you still be fuckin' with me", "If I was wearin' the same jeans for a week?", "If I was hungry and I ain't have nothing to eat?", "Would you, yeah, would you still think about it when you up?"], "meaning": "A Boogie Wit Da Hoodie deals with the fake love that affects successful artists like him. The young rapper raises doubts about the reasons why his girl loves him, a topic he already adressed in his very popular January 2016 song “My Shit.”\nOn the song, he raps:\n\nTrust me, it get ugly\nIf I wasn’t gettin' money would you still want me?\nIf I told you I was broke would you start actin' funny?\nThis is all I ever wanted, you can’t take it from me\n\nOn the verified annotation for these lyrics, A Boogie explains:\n\nIt’s self-explanatory. Let’s say you talking to a girl, that’s exactly what it means. Trust me, it gets ugly. That’s real shit right there. If I was pop, a lot of girls wouldn’t want to talk to me.", "annotation_id": "17796198", "url": "https://genius.com/17796198", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Running the Streets", "line": "MAC-11, dirty money on my prayer rug", "vault_lines": ["MAC-11, dirty money on my prayer rug"], "meaning": "A MAC-11 is a handheld machine pistol that has a very fast rate of fire for a handheld weapon.\n\n\n\nRoss juxtaposes two wrongs, his weapon and “dirty money,” or money obtained through illegal actions, with his prayer rug, which is used for purer things in life, such as religion.\n\nRoss has mentioned owning this type of weapon many times before, including on “Game Ain’t Based On Money”:\n\nMAC-11 next to Grammy invitations", "annotation_id": "17784920", "url": "https://genius.com/17784920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Running the Streets", "line": "They back on the rampage, like Quentin", "vault_lines": ["They back on the rampage, like Quentin"], "meaning": "Quinton Jackson is a professional MMA fighter who is nicknamed “Rampage”. Jackson speaks on how he got the “Rampage” nickname here.", "annotation_id": "17782991", "url": "https://genius.com/17782991", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Running the Streets", "line": "Went from sleepin’ on the floor to pissin’ Moët", "vault_lines": ["Went from sleepin' on the floor to pissin' Moët"], "meaning": "Moët & Chandon is a luxury brand of champagne that is very expensive. Bottles of the champagne start at around $50 and increase in price with higher quality and age.\n\n\n\nRoss uses his ability to constantly drink Moët as a symbol for his wealth and changed lifestyle since coming up in the rap game.", "annotation_id": "17778091", "url": "https://genius.com/17778091", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Running the Streets", "line": "Peyton Manning with the poems, go look at the stats", "vault_lines": ["Peyton Manning with the poems, go look at the stats"], "meaning": "Peyton Manning is a retired NFL quarterback who is considered to be one of the greatest quarterbacks of all time.\n\n\n\nManning holds many passing records in the NFL, such as the most career touchdowns thrown and most touchdowns thrown in one season. Ross compares his discography to Manning’s record stats, saying that he has a track record in the rap game comparable to Manning’s illustrious career in football.", "annotation_id": "17778078", "url": "https://genius.com/17778078", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Running the Streets", "line": "Quick step, then I plant, just like I’m Durant", "vault_lines": ["Quick step, then I plant, just like I'm Durant"], "meaning": "Ross references basketball player Kevin Durant, who is considered to be one of the greatest players of his generation and one of the most prolific scorers in NBA history.\n\n\n\nDurant was also mentioned by Drake on another track on this album, “Gold Roses”:\n\nAll smiles, Kevin Durant trials\nHad to blow it on the court, I must have blew a milli'\nI’m walkin' on all charges, that’s my new Achilles", "annotation_id": "17778071", "url": "https://genius.com/17778071", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Running the Streets", "line": "Dyslexic, spell Dade", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Dyslexia is a learning disorder that can affect your ability to read and write correctly, and often results in those affected disordering the letters of a word in their perception. This would imply that a dyslexic person would read “Dade” as “dead.”\n\nMiami-Dade County, where Denzel is from, is also a county with one of the highest income inequalities and a large amount of racial disparities.\n\nWith Miami-Dade being so problematic, Zel is saying the words dead and Dade are interchangeable.", "annotation_id": "17778015", "url": "https://genius.com/17778015", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Running the Streets", "line": "Street sweeper clean up the streets like Cascade", "vault_lines": ["Street sweeper clean up the streets like Cascade"], "meaning": "Cascade is a common household cleaner used for dishes.\n\nHere Curry speaks on the short memory of the streets. Once the street cleaner comes by, the memory of the murder or shooting that has just occurred is wiped away with the cleaning.\n\nHe also references one of his many aliases, Denny Cascade. Denzel first portrayed this persona on his 2013 debut album Nostalgic 64. The record features an eponymously-named track, “Denny Cascade.”", "annotation_id": "17777854", "url": "https://genius.com/17777854", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Running the Streets", "line": "Shippin’ ’em off to San Quentin", "vault_lines": ["Shippin' 'em off to San Quentin"], "meaning": "Curry references the unincorporated town of San Quentin, California, which is home to the San Quentin State Prison. The prison is a maximum security prison and has housed many of the most dangerous criminals in America. The prison is also the oldest high-security prison in California, and is the state’s only facility that conducts the death penalty.", "annotation_id": "17777813", "url": "https://genius.com/17777813", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Running the Streets", "line": "Like Stanley Yelnats, he caps, his head red", "vault_lines": ["Like Stanley Yelnats, he caps, his head red"], "meaning": "Stanley Yelnats is a fictional character and protagonist in the story Holes. One noteworthy characteristic of his is his red hat, which he wears constantly, even while digging holes as a punishment for his accused crime.\n\n\n\nCurry also mentioned Yelnats on his “Shawshank” single.\n\nSpitting like a semi, only get me semicolon\nCouple 0’s like I’m Stanley Yelnats", "annotation_id": "17777774", "url": "https://genius.com/17777774", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
