{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "I feel right at home,", "vault_lines": ["I feel right at home, y'all sitting right at home"], "meaning": "In response to the previous line, Andre 3000 explains how he’s been fighting tough conditions for his entire life since the odds were always stacked against him—with his back against the wall, leaving him completely trapped with no other option but to fight forward,\n\nWith his back against the wall, he is completely trapped with no other option but to fight forward. Instead of panicking or giving up, he finds home in the pressure since he has faced unfair struggles his entire life.", "annotation_id": "41791711", "url": "https://genius.com/41791711", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "When", "vault_lines": ["I miss the days of old when one could hold his gal on his arm"], "meaning": "‘when’, not ‘and’", "annotation_id": "37359283", "url": "https://genius.com/37359283", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Hahahah, it’s funny because, we could’ve short-changed you (Huh!) But because we love you, we go that extra mile Break it down for ’em, André, huh!", "vault_lines": ["Hahahah, it's funny because, we could've short-changed you (Huh!)", "But because we love you, we go that extra mile", "Break it down for 'em, André, huh!"], "meaning": "These artists could have shortchanged us, the fans, with shorter 16-bar verses and left us wanting more out of them both, so instead they both went extra hard for double the time and gave the fans more than plenty from them both. This little boast leads as a perfect segue into the Andre 3000 directed guitar solo and outro, of what could have been a shorter track and thankfully, wasn’t.", "annotation_id": "34200917", "url": "https://genius.com/34200917", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Everything I ever wore was once worn before", "vault_lines": ["Everything I ever wore was once worn before"], "meaning": "He reminisces on how time has changed. He grew up on second-hand clothing, goodwill style shopping and anything him and or his family could do to save money. All a rough childhood lifestyle that helped him develop into the multi-millionaire artist that he is today.", "annotation_id": "34200839", "url": "https://genius.com/34200839", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Smooth as John Coltrane cruising in the Cadillac Seville, feel my life on the real", "vault_lines": ["Smooth as John Coltrane cruising in the Cadillac", "Seville, feel my life on the real"], "meaning": "John Coltrane was an American Jazz saxophonist, band leader, and composer with a predilection for blues and jazz music from his youth. Known as one of the most influential jazz artists of all time. “Smooth jazz” being a common phrase used to describe his music, as well as how the Cadillac Seville drives. Nothing but smooth.", "annotation_id": "34200780", "url": "https://genius.com/34200780", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "I’m in my room, booming Drawin’ LL Cool J album covers with Crayolas on construction paper", "vault_lines": ["I'm in my room, booming", "Drawin' LL Cool J album covers with Crayolas on construction paper"], "meaning": "The lyric “in my room” is a reference to LL Cool J’s song “I need love” in which LL raps “When I’m alone in my room…”\n\nThe use of the word “Booming” is a reference to LL’s song “The Boomin' System”\n\nLL Cool J had three albums out up until 1989 which were “Radio, BAD (which had \"I need love” on it) and Walking With  a Panther\"\n\nhttps://genius.com/Ll-cool-j-i-need-love-lyrics\n\nhttps://genius.com/Ll-cool-j-the-boomin-system-lyrics", "annotation_id": "27475803", "url": "https://genius.com/27475803", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Summer ’88, or was it ’89? Or was it wintertime? Ah, never mind", "vault_lines": ["Summer '88, or was it '89?", "Or was it wintertime? Ah, never mind"], "meaning": "Shouts out to Andre 3000, the greatest storyteller in rap… Of course, everyone knows what he is saying here!!\n\nHe is telling us when he was 16 (a reference to song title) but can’t recall the year properly and then he says never mind… For a grown man past is passed no matter what so what can he do, he sure though can’t change it so ‘aw, never mind’…", "annotation_id": "24158240", "url": "https://genius.com/24158240", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "[Interlude: André 3000]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "In witty fashion, Andre gives a long winded monologue about how one verse isn’t enough to say what you want to say, but in doing so he wastes the time he has for his verse.", "annotation_id": "19485320", "url": "https://genius.com/19485320", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Welch’s", "vault_lines": ["As if we can name 'em; hint-hint—it ain't, um, Welch's"], "meaning": "Welch’s is a popular brand of grape https://s3.amazonaws.com/filepicker-images-rapgenius/sAZYe8cGT8iQtbLzrHtq_welch’s.jpgjuice\n\nI dont know where to go with this annotation because I’m high, but someone else should finish it off as I believe it has some meaning to it…. It’s a funny line..", "annotation_id": "19468763", "url": "https://genius.com/19468763", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "How’s he God if he lets Lucifer let loose on us?", "vault_lines": [", how's he God if he lets Lucifer let loose on us?"], "meaning": "God is supposedly all knowing, all kind and all powerful. Contrary to the initial statement, Lucifer which is another word used when calling the “devil” was God’s creation. Secondly He had all the power and ability to stop Lucifer but according to the bible he just banished the Serpent to roam free on Earth and tempt mankind. So 3 stacks here questions God’s kindness and Being.", "annotation_id": "13133991", "url": "https://genius.com/13133991", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "It’s funny because, it’s been on my mind lately Having a dope beat, a dope idea Sixteen bars ain’t enough! How the fuck can I squeeze my whole life into a sixteen-bar verse? You know, so many different levels, to living your life and Depicting, with your wordplay Exactly what life means to you, and sixteen ain’t enough I know y’all gonna feel me on this one You gotta feel me on this one, huh!", "vault_lines": ["I know y'all gonna feel me on this one", "You gotta feel me on this one, huh!"], "meaning": "Rozay describes the concept of the song—16 bars is not enough to express himself.\n\nIn an interview, Ross expanded:\n\nBasically it was just when you get some music that you really love or a track that you really love, sometimes sixteen bars is not enough to really express yourself. We just went to that next level. I think for people to hear Andre do what he did; I'ma let y'all count how many bars he did as well as of course what I did, man. He pulled out the guitar and banjo on this one and he sang a little bit on the end of it. It just broke all the rules of making a standard record.", "annotation_id": "1669571", "url": "https://genius.com/1669571", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Three Stacks on the beat and the kicks mean", "vault_lines": ["Three Stacks on the beat and the kicks mean"], "meaning": "3 Stacks refers to Andre 3000 being on the track. Ross confirmed the guitar solo at the end was played by Andre himself.\n\nAlso, Rozay has a mean shoe game because he was sponsored by Reebok until his controversial molly raps.", "annotation_id": "963135", "url": "https://genius.com/963135", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "And they deserve it all, don’t settle for what ain’t yours", "vault_lines": ["And they deserve it all, don't settle for what ain't yours"], "meaning": "He’s connecting back to the beginning of his verse where he talked about his homeboy cheating. Here he is saying that women deserve it all, so give them everything. Don’t instead pursue a woman who is already taken (something that Dre personally had done to him).\n\nAndre could also want women to not settle for chasing after a man’s money—but for them to be independent and to have it all by being self made.", "annotation_id": "947636", "url": "https://genius.com/947636", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "I break their laws ’til they see out our window", "vault_lines": ["I break their laws 'til they see out our window"], "meaning": "They meaning the government and these corrupted politicians. People of the ghetto break their laws because they live in the harsh times of this strife life. They make the laws that help corrupt us further but Andre is gonna keep breaking their laws until they see what we see, live like how we live, do things the way we do things because the system is fucked up.", "annotation_id": "947615", "url": "https://genius.com/947615", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "And not set off these", "vault_lines": ["And not set off these"], "meaning": "The fame hits him, (through out the verse he is telling a story through his time before fame), thats why there is so much “before…before…before” in the earlier lines. In the previous line Andre is reminiscing about the times when these things did not happen. He tries not to set off people because they will try to ruin him. That’s the price of his then newly found fame.", "annotation_id": "947602", "url": "https://genius.com/947602", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "While I’m jelly beans descending Into the palm of a child, looks up at mama and smile With such a devilish grin, like, “Where the hell have you been?”", "vault_lines": ["while I'm jelly beans descending", "Into the palm of a child, looks up at mama and smile", "With such a devilish grin, like, \"Where the hell have you been?\""], "meaning": "The legendary triple entendre:\n\n\nThe mother looks in the kids hand and sees crack cocaine and tells the kid where the hell have you been…connecting to the next line that says “selling is a sin” meaning kids aren’t suppose to be hustling at that young of an age.\nAndre’s message goes through his music and into the ears of a child as easily as jellybeans into their palm. Hearing his message is much better for your health than a sugar filled treat though.\nHe is drawing comparisons to his current career which constitutes very few features and appearances and a child receiving jellybeans as a treat. As Hip Hop fans, we are only being allowed small sweet portions of what 3 Stacks has to offer.  When he does eventually surface the questions is always “where they hell have you been”. & their sweet taste always leaves us wanting more.", "annotation_id": "947598", "url": "https://genius.com/947598", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Because the odds Have always been stacked against me when back’s against the wall", "vault_lines": ["Have always been stacked against me when back's against the wall"], "meaning": "A double entendre.\nIt’s a play on his name .. Andre 3 Stacks. 3 is an odd number, plus he is saying that he fought his way out of adversity.\n\nThe Greek Gods cant mess with him now. He has proven his ability to persevere.", "annotation_id": "947579", "url": "https://genius.com/947579", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "And all I wanted was one, sixteen ain’t enough Talking that fast money, fifteen every month When your people labelled poor, that motivated me more", "vault_lines": ["And all I wanted was one, sixteen ain't enough", "Talking that fast money, fifteen every month", "When your people labelled poor, that motivated me more"], "meaning": "All he wants is a million, sixteen bars aren’t enough to spit how big that desire is.\n\nHe “talks” fast money—the words he spit earn him lots of money. He “talks” fast money—he also raps about earning large amounts of money\n\nDouble Entendre from the great Rozay!\n\nHis people (black people) are stereotyped to be poor. This only fuels his desire to prove them wrong.\n\n“All he wanted was one, 16 ain’t enough” could also mean that like all poor people all he wish he had was a million dollars,  but he’s on 16 million and he making fast money so even 16 milly doesn’t satisfy his money making ways anymore.", "annotation_id": "944337", "url": "https://genius.com/944337", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Steaming my marijuana", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Steaming marijuana helps it restore its original moisture when it becomes dry.", "annotation_id": "944208", "url": "https://genius.com/944208", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Does your daddy think you perfect? Does he know for certain? Does he know how you act when you pull back all them curtains?", "vault_lines": ["Does your daddy think you perfect? Does he know for certain?", "Does he know how you act when you pull back all them curtains?"], "meaning": "One of the greatest fear of a father is that his daughter will turn out to be a freak. Take former Cowboys quarterback Bernie Kosar and his offspring who is now a pornographic actress.", "annotation_id": "940188", "url": "https://genius.com/940188", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "All that shit that used to be cool, ain’t cool anymore", "vault_lines": ["All that shit that used to be cool, ain't cool anymore"], "meaning": "The drive of human beings to constantly move forward causes stylish trends in dress, music, behavior, etc. to fade, resurface, fade again, and die.\n\n\n\nAll that shit that he perceived as cool when he was younger: the fame, the paparazzi and shit, isn’t cool anymore.\n\nIt’s also worth noting how at the beginning of the verse he tells of in his youth he was idolising LL Cool J, who was at the peak of his popularity or “coolness”. The level of popularity Cool had back in the day isn’t his anymore, basically saying he isn’t he centre of attention like he used to be. It just shows times changing.", "annotation_id": "940174", "url": "https://genius.com/940174", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "I miss the days of old when one could hold his gal on his arm", "vault_lines": ["I miss the days of old when one could hold his gal on his arm"], "meaning": "Before the fame. When he could freely walk out with his lady friend and not have paparazzi taking pictures of them together.\n\nAndre 3000’s soul is from a time and place that is surely not our own.\n\n\n\nWhenever it is, it is more simple, and beautiful.", "annotation_id": "940171", "url": "https://genius.com/940171", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "I take the fall, to make them all, treat humankind more gentle", "vault_lines": ["I take the fall, to make them all, treat humankind more gentle"], "meaning": "Andre compares himself to the Christian Jesus, in that he sacrificed any kind of simple, private life to be a Rap Star whose rhymes uplift mankind. And according to Christian lore, just as Jesus sacrificed his life for all the world.", "annotation_id": "940166", "url": "https://genius.com/940166", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "That noose on us won’t loosen up, but loose enough to juice us up Make us think we do so much and do it big Like they don’t let us win, I can’t pretend But I do admit, it feel good when the hood pseudo-celebrate", "vault_lines": ["That noose on us won't loosen up, but loose enough to juice us up", "Make us think we do so much and do it big", "Like they don't let us win, I can't pretend", "But I do admit, it feel good when the hood pseudo-celebrate"], "meaning": "Here, the extremely prejudicial noose that once hung around black people to lynch them has loosened up a bit to make them think they are free. It makes the hood think it’s succeeding when someone becomes a rapper or a baller but Dre knows it’s not real success, especially not in the eyes of everyone else.\n\nIn the next couple of lines it becomes clear that despite knowing that their success is superficial, it’s still as good as it gets in the hood so he’ll celebrate too. The prefix pseudo- is used to mark something as false, or pretending to be something it is not.\n\nNotice how he uses the words “juice us up” and “do it big”…. He’s referring to Tupac and Biggie, as Tupac played a role in the movie “Juice”.", "annotation_id": "937956", "url": "https://genius.com/937956", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Before my crumbs would cake up", "vault_lines": ["Before my mama wake up, before my crumbs would cake up"], "meaning": "Cake is a regularly used synonym for money, so in this case “caking up” would mean “making money.”", "annotation_id": "937947", "url": "https://genius.com/937947", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Hence, why every time we dine, we eat until our belly aches Then go grab the finest wine and drink it Like we know which grape and which region it came from", "vault_lines": ["Hence, why every time we dine, we eat until our belly aches", "Then go grab the finest wine and drink it", "Like we know which grape and which region it came from"], "meaning": "In the previous line he said it feels good when the hood pseudo celebrate. Pseudo means not real, which means the celebration isn’t real and every time we eat, we eat like pigs and eat as much as we can and then go drink some wine that we don’t even know where it came from but because it’s a “celebration” and we think we somehow “made it” we eat and drink like it but its all really fake and just a simple way out to say we have bettered ourselves in some kinda way.", "annotation_id": "934768", "url": "https://genius.com/934768", "state": "accepted", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Better put away a penny for the rainy days Pick and roll, give and go, fuck a fade away", "vault_lines": ["Better put away a penny for the rainy days", "Pick and roll, give and go, fuck a fade away"], "meaning": "Rozay suggests that he should save—not just for the times when he no longer makes that cash like he does now, but so that he goes out with a bang, and memorably, rather than sifting through the palms of the limelight, to be a forgotten figure in the cut-throat world of entertainment.\n\nAlso “pick AND roll” and “fade away” are basketball terms, which flow into the next line about Scottie Pippen, one of the most famous basketball players, ever.", "annotation_id": "934614", "url": "https://genius.com/934614", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Yeah, something tells me we ain’t in Kansas anymore", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, something tells me we ain't in Kansas anymore"], "meaning": "Andre is continuing the running narrative of the song. First, he starts off describing the ease and innocence of childhood, painting the scene with all of the “before” lines.\n\nThen he starts to grow up and come to terms with the grittiness of life (having to protect himself from intruders, bad education, oppression in the ghetto).\n\nThen he speaks about how his worth is infinite because throughout it all, selling drugs and making music to hustle, he persevered, and is now on the other side of the struggle. The success and the fame—he obtained that.\n\nBut now with fame, he sees that it isn’t all what it was cracked up to be, hence the next line, it “ain’t cool anymore”. With fame, he has no privacy.\n\nSo he’s not in Kansas anymore, which is a reference to the famous movie, Wizard of Oz. Not being in Kansas refers to being a place or situation that is outside your comfort zone or scope of understanding – strange surroundings. In the film, Kansas is the land of innocence and carefree—like Andre was at the beginning. However, now he’s in the real world now.", "annotation_id": "934106", "url": "https://genius.com/934106", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "She’s yelling, “Selling’s a sin,” well, so is telling young men That selling is a sin if you don’t offer new ways to win", "vault_lines": ["She's yelling, \"Selling's a sin,\" well, so is telling young men", "That selling is a sin if you don't offer new ways to win"], "meaning": "Dre criticizes those who would scold drug dealers, hustlers, etc., without offering an alternative way to make a living for people in their situation because the most important thing for the young man is to live and prosper, survive and thrive. Man, that sounds boring when we say it.\n\nThe child’s mother is scolding him, saying he is a sinning but she is not putting money in his pocket or paying for his education or helping him achieve any status in life so she is the original sin for bringing him into a world with nothing to offer so the only thing he is left with is selling so how can she judge him.\n\nBasically, if you can’t offer a viable alternative to hustling—don’t judge the lifestyle. The judgement aspect makes it a sin.", "annotation_id": "934094", "url": "https://genius.com/934094", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "I don’t budge, don’t want much, just a roof and a porch And a Porsche, and a horse, and unfortunately, but of course An assortment of torches that scorches the skin When they enter, intruders whose tutors did A lousy job", "vault_lines": ["I don't budge, don't want much, just a roof and a porch", "And a Porsche, and a horse, and unfortunately, but of course", "An assortment of torches that scorches the skin", "When they enter, intruders whose tutors did"], "meaning": "A clever reversal by Andre—his wishes sound modest at first, but almost immediately graduate into super-rich dreams. It can also show how he has ascended in life, starting off by just wanting shelter, then a nicer house, then the lavishness of fancy cars and horses.\n\nHe says “unfortunately, but of course” before bringing up the guns (referred to as torches) to show that he would rather not have to deal with these things, but knows that that’s the way the world is.\n\nHe then goes on to say that the reason the world is like that (and the reasons these people he refers to have resorted to a life of crime) is the “tutors who did a lousy job”, which is a reference to the poor education system. It’s “unfortunate” because if these “intruders” had received a good education they may have ended up in a position where they wouldn’t have to rob and steal. But, “of course”, this is the world we live in, and so Dre recognises that he needs to protect him self from the evils of the world (the next line).", "annotation_id": "934080", "url": "https://genius.com/934080", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Whitney died night before the Grammys – damn, what a memory", "vault_lines": ["Whitney died night before the Grammys – damn, what a memory"], "meaning": "Even the news of the singer’s death didn’t stop the annual Grammy party by her mentor, music mega-mogul Clive Davis.", "annotation_id": "934069", "url": "https://genius.com/934069", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Floor seat for the Heat", "vault_lines": ["Floor seat for the Heat"], "meaning": "Ross is a big enough booster of his hometown NBA team that he felt he deserved a championship ring.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/rickyrozay/status/216212073309413376", "annotation_id": "934061", "url": "https://genius.com/934061", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Double M G’s, double M fees", "vault_lines": ["Double M G's, double M fees"], "meaning": "Ross pithily mentions both his Maybach Music Group posse and his multi-million dollar accounts in one short line.", "annotation_id": "934045", "url": "https://genius.com/934045", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Eisenhower status, Etta James on the dash", "vault_lines": ["Eisenhower status, Etta James on the dash"], "meaning": "Rozay is listening to some classy music by one of the greatest American R&B singers, Etta James.  James did indeed have her first hits in the waning years of the Eisenhower administration.", "annotation_id": "934042", "url": "https://genius.com/934042", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "We the Last Poets so this is a world premiere", "vault_lines": ["We the Last Poets so this is a world premiere"], "meaning": "Ross compares his originality to that of the late-1960’s spoken-word artists The Last Poets, whose rhyming style and black consciousness often gets them nods as forefathers of hip-hop.", "annotation_id": "934029", "url": "https://genius.com/934029", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Ol’ Dirty Bastard", "vault_lines": ["Yacht to Yachtmasters, Ol' Dirty Bastard"], "meaning": "Ol' Dirty Bastard\nRozay acknowledges that he himself is an old and dirty child without a father.\n\nWhether or not this is true is not important. The life of William Roberts has ceased. It is only the character Rick Ross’s that matters.", "annotation_id": "933660", "url": "https://genius.com/933660", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Do he think I’m 2Pac ’cause I’m Black and put the words in? Does he know his daughter might have caught a real merman? Yeah", "vault_lines": ["Do he think I'm 2Pac 'cause I'm Black and put the words in?", "Does he know his daughter might have caught a real merman? Yeah"], "meaning": "Dre asks if the girls father think he is a thug/gangsta like Tupac just because he raps? This shows the stereotypical image some people have of rappers, being that Andre is nothing like that\n\n\n\nThe last sentence has two interpretations, depending on how you understand the last word.\n\n\n\nMerkman — Going back to the 2Pac reference, Andre is questioning if his girlfriend’s father knows that she is dating a merkman, which slang for a killer or a thug. No father wants to see his daughter date a ‘bad boy’, but some do simply out of rebellion and to spite their father.\n\nMerman — In this interpretation, Andre is reinforcing that he is actually unique and rare, like a merman (a male mermaid). It is a way of showing that he is special, and how he is unlike most other rappers. He’s quite a catch!\nAlso, it is interesting to note the word “catch” being that a merman is part fish", "annotation_id": "933634", "url": "https://genius.com/933634", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Living like Scottie Pippen, dribble riddles for vittles", "vault_lines": ["Living like Scottie Pippen, dribble riddles for vittles"], "meaning": "Ross is saying he doesn’t want to be broke, rhyming for chump change. NBA Hall of Famer Scottie Pippen was erroneously reported to be bankrupt and is now playing exhibition games in the Philippines, for a lot less than he made in the NBA. This may have left Ross to think he was broke.", "annotation_id": "933484", "url": "https://genius.com/933484", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Alarms, when cameras snap-snap-snap Return fire, pap-pap-pap pap pap-pap pap pap They’ll learn why, mere privacy, so essential They won’t make no laws,", "vault_lines": ["alarms, when cameras snap-snap-snap", "Return fire, pap-pap-pap pap pap-pap pap pap", "They'll learn why, mere privacy, so essential", "They won't make no laws,"], "meaning": "Paparazzi will not understand how important his privacy is until he responds to their camera shots with gunfire. Also he states if the government won’t make paparazzi invading your privacy against the law, he will break they law and take the fall using force until they choose to treat him better.", "annotation_id": "933419", "url": "https://genius.com/933419", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "A dolphin gon’ shake his fin, regardless if he gets in Or out of water, most important thing for him is to swim And Flipper didn’t hold his nose, so why shall I hold my tongue?", "vault_lines": ["A dolphin gon' shake his fin, regardless if he gets in", "Or out of water, most important thing for him is to swim", "And Flipper didn't hold his nose, so why shall I hold my tongue?"], "meaning": "Some things are just natural; like a dolphin flapping his fins. Andre compares the dolphin to the hustler from the previous line: a dolphin will shake his fin (do what he has to do), regardless if he gets in (legal means) or out of water (illegal means such as selling drugs) most important thing for him is to swim (moves forward in life and prosper).\n\nSo like Flipper, the famous dolphin who could communicate with humans via is nose – Andre has to spit rhymes – natually.\n\n\n\nBasically, a dolphin will swim no matter what and a hustler will hustle – legally or illegally. Similar to how dolphins have to come up for air, a hustler can’t hold his tongue and has to come up and speak about why he does what he does.\n\nIn terms of the dolphin comparison, Andre is basically saying that nothing is wrong with the boy’s aim, he just has to change the target. He needs money so he will flap his fins regardless of if he is in the water. The “in” refers to making money the right way.", "annotation_id": "933359", "url": "https://genius.com/933359", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Y’all can’t measure my worth But when you try, you’ll need a ruler made by all the Greek gods", "vault_lines": ["But when you try, you'll need a ruler made by all the Greek gods"], "meaning": "Rulers, (wooden sticks with measurements), can measure worth, but you’ll need a big Ruler to measure all of his money. He says you’ll need a Ruler (a leader exercising domination) made by all the Greek Gods, (also  rulers). It’s a play on the definitions of ruler.\n\nHis worth can’t be measured by mere mortals.  He is saying that his worth is close to infinite, (immeasurable), therefore you would need a ruler of equal value to come to a definite answer.", "annotation_id": "933034", "url": "https://genius.com/933034", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Y’all sitting right at home All Kellygreen with envy", "vault_lines": ["All Kellygreen with envy"], "meaning": "Kelly green’s a popular shade of green.\n\n\n\nGreen is usually the color used to represent jealousy & envy.\n\nAndre is talking about the haters who are envious of what he has but are just sitting at home and not going through hardships like he did or not willing to work when their back is against the wall.", "annotation_id": "932162", "url": "https://genius.com/932162", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Hell just fell three thousand more degrees cooler", "vault_lines": ["Hell just fell three thousand more degrees cooler"], "meaning": "There are 3 possible interpretations to this line:\n\nDre could referring to the lines just before these talking about wines, where he mentions that we grab the finest wines and we’re quick to pay for fancier (and thus, more expensive) wine under the assumption that it will be better (but without knowing for sure since you aren’t a sommelier). Hell and He’ll is just a play on word meaning that the black man feels 3000 times cooler to be drinking the fancy wine!\n\nHe could also be suggesting he’s going to hell and when he does it will be 3000 degrees ‘cooler’. Now the reference here is that on his smash hit Hey Ya his name was Andre “Ice Cold” 3000, thus hell becomes ‘cooler’ (plus, we all know he is a very cool person)…and I think we all understand the 3000 reference.\n\nOr, he could be referring to the old saying about hell freezing over, “this could only be real when hell freezes over”.", "annotation_id": "932136", "url": "https://genius.com/932136", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Before lil’ Marc was Jacob", "vault_lines": ["Before lil' Marc was Jacob"], "meaning": "Marc Jacobs, an American fashion designer.", "annotation_id": "931998", "url": "https://genius.com/931998", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "We in every hood, nigga: government cheese", "vault_lines": ["We in every hood, nigga: government cheese"], "meaning": "Government cheese is processed cheese provided to welfare and food stamp recipients, thus it’s in every hood.\n\nThis could also hold the meaning that the music is in (inside) every hood dude like government cheese is inside them (from consimption).  So every hood nigga feels the music.", "annotation_id": "931855", "url": "https://genius.com/931855", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Before we’d tour them shake clubs", "vault_lines": ["Before my voice would break up, before we'd tour them shake clubs"], "meaning": "Atlanta is famously known as the Strip Club capital of the US. Shake club = strip club.\n\nIf Andre was going to the strip clubs at the age of 14/15, he might have had a reaction like this:", "annotation_id": "931790", "url": "https://genius.com/931790", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Rolling like Mick Jagger, the women just getting badder", "vault_lines": ["Rolling like Mick Jagger, the women just getting badder"], "meaning": "Jagger is the famous frontman for the Rolling Stones.\n\n\n\nHe is using the double meaning of rolling to say he is both walking around with a bunch of money and fame (like Mick Jagger, and another part of the pun is that he is the front man for the Rolling Stones) and that he is also rolling up a lot of joints, and rockstars are notoriously known to have a thing for drugs…\n\nAnd Rick Ross may be getting into the Honky Tonk Women.", "annotation_id": "931568", "url": "https://genius.com/931568", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "All I see is the money, cream, Eric Clapton", "vault_lines": ["All I see is the money, cream, Eric Clapton"], "meaning": "Rozay is talking about money, (C.R.E.A.M.) and also talking about the band that Eric Clapton was in, Cream, a 1960s British rock supergroup consisting of bassist/vocalist Jack Bruce, guitarist/vocalist Eric Clapton, and drummer Ginger Baker.", "annotation_id": "931518", "url": "https://genius.com/931518", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "Dressed like Sammy Davis,", "vault_lines": ["Dressed like Sammy Davis,"], "meaning": "Ross is dressed like Sammy Davis, a famous American entertainer and actor who played “The Candy Man.” He’s known for his charisma and sense of style.", "annotation_id": "931503", "url": "https://genius.com/931503", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Sixteen", "line": "When sixteen ain’t enough When sixteen ain’t enough Ooh, yeah Aw yeah-eah-eah-eah-eah-eah-eah When sixteen ain’t enough When sixteen ain’t enough Ooh, yeah Aw yeah-eah-eah-eah-eah-eah-eah", "vault_lines": ["When sixteen ain't enough"], "meaning": "Rap verses are generally sixteen bars, or measures, long.  For the non-musicians in the crowd, see here for the definition of a bar.\n\nAndré and Ross don’t seem to share the same idea as Masta Ace and Ed O.G on the topic of verse-length.", "annotation_id": "931452", "url": "https://genius.com/931452", "state": "accepted", "votes": 70, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
