{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "Produced by Jake One", "vault_lines": ["[Produced by Jake One"], "meaning": "The track was produced by Jake One, who’s become a favorite of Rozay’s since he produced the street anthem “3 Kings” featuring Dre and Jay Z. He also produced “Dope Dick” and “Money Dance” on Black Market and another three songs on the Black Dollar mixtape. A particularly productive work week for the producer, he tells Genius: “Everything is just whatever style I happen to be into at the time. I go thru phases. I actually made ‘Money Dance,’ ‘Dope Dick,’ and ‘Smile Mama Smile’ all in the same week.”\n\nComposed of chopped-up sounds played on the Rhodes and Memorymoog keyboards, the arrangement and added background vocals give the track the feel of an old soul sample without actually being one. The vocals were the result of another session of singers working on imitating the group harmonies of ‘60s and '70s protest music or “struggle group harmonies.”\n\nJake Uno agreed that the vocals, “seem to work really at making a track with no samples sound like something off a ‘70s record. That’s kind of been something I’ve worked really hard at over the years. I really don’t want people to know exactly what I’m doing (sampling or not) when they hear a beat of mine.”", "annotation_id": "8756943", "url": "https://genius.com/8756943", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "Just smile The world had to make me a man, but I’ll always be your child Mama just smile, oh Most importantly, I want you to be proud Mama just smile!", "vault_lines": ["The world had to make me a man, but I'll always be your child", "Mama, just smile, oh", "Most importantly, I want you to be proud"], "meaning": "Without a father in the picture, the streets raised Young Renzel. But he’ll always be his mother’s son, and he just wants to make her proud.\n\n\n\nThere’s a sense of urgency in Cee-Lo’s voice, most likely stemming from his fall from grace in the wake of an ill-advised Twitter rant while facing rape charges in 2014. There’s no question he’s also singing these lyrics to his own mother, even releasing a song titled “Mother May I” on his 2015 Heart Blanche album.", "annotation_id": "8466702", "url": "https://genius.com/8466702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "Mama, always depend on my mama Just bought another Benz for my mama I always keep it real with my mama She harder than a rock Throw her parties on the block for my mama", "vault_lines": ["Mama, always depend on my mama", "Just bought another Benz for my mama", "I always keep it real with my mama", "She harder than a rock", "Throw her parties on the block for my mama"], "meaning": "Ross and his moms have a special relationship. She’s tough, but he can always depend on her and be honest with her.\n\nEternally grateful, he’s more than happy to shower her with Mercedes Benzes and throw block parties in her honor. Rozay loves his Benzos, counting a Mercedes S-Class and a CLS among his large car collection.", "annotation_id": "8466670", "url": "https://genius.com/8466670", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "Turn me onto real estate, we bought us 50 homes", "vault_lines": ["Turn me onto real estate, we bought us 50 homes"], "meaning": "Rozay’s mom introduced him to the real estate game  and they’ve bought dozens of properties throughout Miami.\n\nIn July of 2015, the Jasmine Brand reported that a woman named Norbert Martins filed suit against Ross and his mother in Broward County court. In 2011, the alleged victim was visiting Ross’s tenant at the time when she tripped on the rail of the house’s iron gate. She evidently suffered from severe pain and suffering, and four years later wanted over $15,000 in damages.", "annotation_id": "8466270", "url": "https://genius.com/8466270", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "It made me stronger, made me quicker on my feet All the gain she gave me, the love that she would teach", "vault_lines": ["It made me stronger, made me quicker on my feet", "All the gain she gave me, the love that she would teach"], "meaning": "The struggles growing up made Ross into the man he is today. But instead of seeing them as handicaps, he’s flipped them into assets. [Pun intended.] His mother showed him love as a kid, and in doing so set an example for him to follow.\n\nThe phrase brings to mind the African-American proverb, “Each one teach one”—a slave era expression encouraging slaves who learned to read or write to teach those who hadn’t.", "annotation_id": "8466200", "url": "https://genius.com/8466200", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "Or that we always stand in line, I’m talkin’ all of us", "vault_lines": ["Or that we always stand in line, I'm talkin' all of us"], "meaning": "Like most poor kids, Ross spent lots of time standing in line. Whether it’s waiting for the bus, in the food line, or the welfare line, being broke means standing in lots of lines.\n\n\n\nIn Ross’s case, it was the whole family, which included his older sister Tawanda Roberts who now helps run the Rozay business empire.", "annotation_id": "8466136", "url": "https://genius.com/8466136", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "Just smile The world had to make me a man, but I’ll always be your child Mama, just smile, oh Most importantly, I want you to be proud Mama just smile!", "vault_lines": ["The world had to make me a man, but I'll always be your child", "Mama, just smile, oh", "Most importantly, I want you to be proud"], "meaning": "Without a father in the picture, the streets may have raised Young Renzel but he’ll always be his mother’s son and he just wants to make her proud of him.\n\n\n\nThere’s a sense of urgency in Cee-Lo’s voice, most likely stemming from his fall from grace in the wake of an ill-advised Twitter rant while facing rape charges in 2014. There’s no question he’s also singing these lyrics to his own mother, even including a song on his 2015 comeback album titled “Mother May I.”", "annotation_id": "8465966", "url": "https://genius.com/8465966", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "Word on the streets, they say that nigga get a lot of cake I take the time, I kiss both cheeks on my mama’s face", "vault_lines": ["Word on the streets, they say that nigga get a lot of cake", "I take the time, I kiss both cheeks on my mama's face"], "meaning": "It’s well known on the streets that Ross is getting money or cake. But even though he’s out hustling, he still takes the time to show his mother affection.", "annotation_id": "8465820", "url": "https://genius.com/8465820", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "40 M’s in my Timbs, still ridin’ rims", "vault_lines": ["40 M's in my Timbs, still ridin' rims"], "meaning": "While alluding to both his large $40 million net worth and the practice of stashing money in your shoes for safety, Rozay makes it clear that despite being rich he still keeps it hood.", "annotation_id": "8465685", "url": "https://genius.com/8465685", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "Think about it, think about it", "vault_lines": ["Think about it, think about it"], "meaning": "Keying off the song’s opening conversation with his mother asking him to stop sipping syrup, Ross is now asking the listener to process the depth of what he’s trying to reveal in the song.", "annotation_id": "8465565", "url": "https://genius.com/8465565", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "That’s why I throw a pinkie ring up when I post a pic It’s for my niggas on they dick, “Look what this nigga did!”", "vault_lines": ["That's why I throw a pinkie ring up when I post a pic", "It's for my niggas on they dick, \"Look what this nigga did!\""], "meaning": "Ross is known for flossing on Instagram, and throwing up his pinkie ring is one of his signature moves. He does it so others on their grind can see how he came up and be inspired to do the same.", "annotation_id": "8465550", "url": "https://genius.com/8465550", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "Positivity is all I got to give When we was kids, it was steal now it’s time to live", "vault_lines": ["Positivity is all I got to give", "When we was kids, it was steal now it's time to live"], "meaning": "Despite his rough upbringing, Rozay’s letting his mother know he’s still all about doing the right thing.\n\nIn the streets, a lot of beef is settled with weapons. Though as a youth Rozay may have resorted to pulling out a gun and ending someone’s life, now that he’s a grown man he’s all about living life.", "annotation_id": "8465414", "url": "https://genius.com/8465414", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "Never had the chance to tell my dad I was a Yankee fan", "vault_lines": ["Never had the chance to tell my dad I was a Yankee fan"], "meaning": "Though Rozay doesn’t talk about his father much, we know that he was largely absent from his life growing up. Ross touches on the subject on Wale’s “Ambition,”\n\nMy father was missing, War Lord Oliver North\n\nIt appears Ross’s father was possibly also a Yankee fan, but due to their strained relationship he was never able to tell his father that he was as well.", "annotation_id": "8465188", "url": "https://genius.com/8465188", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "First time me in a batting cage, I knew I had a gift Which I had to skip, I’m at the car wash trying to make a tip", "vault_lines": ["First time me in a batting cage, I knew I had a gift", "Which I had to skip, I'm at the car wash trying to make a tip"], "meaning": "Though a talented baseball player, due to circumstances growing up Ross had to focus on making money and not potentially playing baseball.\n\nRoss did however excel enough at football in high school to earn a scholarship to Georgia’s Albany State University, but dropped out before graduating.", "annotation_id": "8377140", "url": "https://genius.com/8377140", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "No, I never really mind that we caught the bus", "vault_lines": ["No, I never really mind that we caught the bus"], "meaning": "Taking a bus is the lowest form of transportation in a city of luxury automobiles like Miami. Ross is trying to let his mom know that it’s okay that she didn’t have a car and they had to take public transpo.", "annotation_id": "8373825", "url": "https://genius.com/8373825", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "True love is unconditional, money don’t make the man", "vault_lines": ["True love is unconditional, money don’t make the man"], "meaning": "There’s an old quote attributed to the father of gospel music Thomas A. Dorsey:\n\n“Money doesn’t make the man. Some people have money, and some people are rich.”\n\nAs he grows older and wiser, Ross has come to terms with his father’s absence and realized that life isn’t just about money.", "annotation_id": "8373816", "url": "https://genius.com/8373816", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "Who really gonna be your shoulder when your kidney’s gone Who really gonna be your rider when the Bentley’s gone? Or when they singin’ like a choir in a Christmas song?", "vault_lines": ["Who really gonna be your shoulder when your kidney's gone", "Who really gonna be your rider when the Bentley's gone?", "Or when they singin' like a choir in a Christmas song?"], "meaning": "Ross is realizing the person who is most loyal to you is your mother. When the tides change and things turn for the worst, his mother is always there. When he loses his money and expensive toys (like a Bentley), the one person he knows will be there is his mother.\n\nRoss takes the term “rider,” which refers to close companions, and cleverly plays off the car theme to say “friends leave when you lose your wealth.” He does the same with the word “singing,” which is slang for telling on someone to the police.", "annotation_id": "8353683", "url": "https://genius.com/8353683", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "I went unconscious, I woke up to see my mama smile She told me no more promethazine, that’ll make her proud Think about it, damn I had to think about it Gimme a second, mama, lemme think about it I went unconscious, I woke up to see my mama smile She told me no more promethazine, that’ll make her proud Think about it, damn I had to think about it", "vault_lines": ["She told me no more promethazine, that'll make her proud", "Think about it, damn I had to think about it", "Gimme a second, mama, lemme think about it"], "meaning": "Promethazine, commonly referred to as lean, syrup, or sizzurp is a very serious narcotic which has has been linked to the deaths of several rappers\n\nOn Oct. 14th, 2011, Ross suffered two seizures on separate flights en route to Memphis for a show. The first seizure forced a return to Ft. Lauderdale, while the second seizure forced an emergency landing in Birmingham, Alabama.\n\nIncreased usage of promethazine can cause seizures, especially when mixed with alcohol. Ross’s high weight and excessive partying with both alcohol and syrup were most likely big factors in the cause of his seizures. He acknowledged as much in an interview with Big Boy and another on Good Morning America while promoting Black Market, pointing to the seizures as the turning point in changing his lifestyle.\n\nRozay’s mom, a registered nurse, would know a thing or two about the dangers of promethazine. As a mother she’s concerned for her son’s health and wants him to stop drinking lean.", "annotation_id": "8348244", "url": "https://genius.com/8348244", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "You always was a black queen, mama You showed me all the best things, mama", "vault_lines": ["You always was a black queen, mama", "You showed me all the best things, mama"], "meaning": "When saying “black queen, mama” Ross borrows the same vocal inflection that Tupac used on “Dear Mama” as a nod to hip-hop’s greatest mama anthem.\n\nAnd even as a crack fiend, mama\nYou always was a black queen, mama\n\nRozay shows appreciation again by saying that his mother showed him the best things in life.", "annotation_id": "8325666", "url": "https://genius.com/8325666", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Smile Mama, Smile", "line": "I am at love Your flesh and your blood Your unconditional love inside of me (L-E-T---M-E---S-E-E---Y-O-U---S-M-I-L-E) Mama, just smile (L-E-T---M-E---S-E-E---Y-O-U---S-M-I-L-E) Mama, just smile (L-E-T---M-E---S-E-E---Y-O-U---S-M-I-L-E) Mama, just smile (L-E-T---M-E---S-E-E---Y-O-U---S-M-I-L-E) (L-E-T---M-E---S-E-E---Y-O-U---S-M-I-L-E)", "vault_lines": ["I am at love", "Your flesh and your blood", "Your unconditional love inside of me"], "meaning": "Even though Rozay grew up in the streets without a father, he’s famous for telling tales of his struggles. Now that he’s more mature he doesn’t need to resort to violence to earn money. His mother can look at him and be proud of the man he’s become. Even though he’s famous, he’s still her child and nothing can change how much he loves her.\n\nAs the song fades out, Cee-Lo can be heard in the background spelling out “L-E-T M-E S-E-E Y-O-U S-M-I-L-E” which translates to “Let me see you smile.”", "annotation_id": "8324849", "url": "https://genius.com/8324849", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
