{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Splash Brothers", "line": "Yeah, on double M G, I’ll fuck a rapper’s wife She ain’t even my type but I’ll make the sacrifice", "vault_lines": ["On double MG, I'll fuck a rapper's wife"], "meaning": "“Double M G” refers to Maybach Music Group (MMG), the label founded by Rick Ross and home to artists like French Montana. Drake is not directly associated with MMG, but he has collaborated with both Rick Ross and French Montana frequently, and by swearing on the label name, he stress on the seriousness of his statement. These bars highlights Drake’s approach when he tries to antagonize his enemies particularly by targeting that said enemy’s romantic partner, especially by hinting at or toying with the idea of perceived infidelity.\n\nHere are several instances where Drake has been rumored to involve himself with women connected to his rivals:\n\n\nSerena Williams (linked to Common): During Drake and Common’s feud, their tension was reportedly fueled by Drake’s connection with Serena Williams, whom Common had been romantically involved with.\nBlac Chyna (linked to Tyga): Drake seemingly targeted Tyga by flirting with Blac Chyna, Tyga’s former partner, and referencing her in subliminal shots during their beef.\nRihanna (linked to Chris Brown and A$AP Rocky): Drake’s relationship with Rihanna added fuel to his long-standing rivalry with Chris Brown and deintely lead to great tension in his relationship with Rocky.\nKim Kardashian (linked to Kanye West): The speculation surrounding “In My Feelings” being about Kim Kardashian deeply upset Kanye West. The tension escalated when fans speculated that the lyric “Kiki, do you love me?” referred to Kim, who has the nickname “Kiki.” Kanye’s reaction indicated the effectiveness of Drake’s tactic.  https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GYM9HP7fpLs", "annotation_id": "34235982", "url": "https://genius.com/34235982", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
