{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Ordered her the filet, told ’em, “Butterfly it, she’ll love it.” She used to soda and nuggets, she really just out here thuggin’", "vault_lines": ["Ordered her the filet, told 'em, \"Butterfly it, she'll love it.\"", "She used to soda and nuggets, she really just out here thuggin'"], "meaning": "Butterflying a steak filet is when the chef delicately cuts slice from the middle of the filet downward until the steak resembles butterfly wings.\n\n\n\nIt is often only requested at high class restaurants, Drake is predicting her excitement to get to enjoy the higher class foods he notes by saying how shes used to eating less classy and expensive things like soda and nuggets.", "annotation_id": "18923383", "url": "https://genius.com/18923383", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Spaghetti bolognese in the Polo Lounge", "vault_lines": ["Spaghetti bolognese in the Polo Lounge"], "meaning": "The Polo Lounge is considered a hotspot for Hollywood eating. Obviously a lot of famous people like Drake eat there.\n\nHowever, spaghetti bolognese is not served at the Polo Lounge. Drake probably means Rigatoni Bolognese, served at lunch and dinner, which costs $56.\n\n\n\nRigatoni (pictured above) is an entirely different type of pasta than spaghetti (pictured below), so Drake either doesn’t know his pasta or he has enough clout to get an exclusive meal.", "annotation_id": "17616863", "url": "https://genius.com/17616863", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Watch the body tilt when you hit the head (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Watch the body tilt when you hit the head (Huh)"], "meaning": "In his Decoded interview with Life + Times, French states that it means like when you take the leader of something out, everyone else falls. Hence the next line, “tryna beat the feds.” .\n\nIn a more literal sense, French can attest to being shot in the head because it happened to him. His attacker suffered the same fate, dying because of it. .", "annotation_id": "618973", "url": "https://genius.com/618973", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Million-dollar meetings in a Polo Lounge", "vault_lines": ["Million-dollar meetings in a Polo Lounge"], "meaning": "In repeating this line with the article “a” in lieu of “the,” Drizzy is emphasizing a second meaning. It’s possible that he is implying that not only is he going to million dollar meetings, but he’s doing it quite casually (as most big name rappers do) and he’s simultaneously sporting some Polo loungewear. The designer apparel would naturally be associated with and a direct product of Drake’s fame and fortune.\n\nIn saying this, he’s following up on the line “might look light,” for he’s saying that though his attire seems unfit for the occasion, he and his crew are important people (that happen to be able to get you gunned up quick.)\n\nAs Bear Bryant once candidly said: “When you get in the end zone, act like you’ve been there before.”", "annotation_id": "555586", "url": "https://genius.com/555586", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "(Huh! Bitch, you wasn’t with me shootin’ in the gym)", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, you wasn't with me shootin' in the gym"], "meaning": "Bawse repeats the line for emphasis. Kobe’s wife wasn’t there when he was putting in the hard work to be famous, shooting thousands of jumpers in the gym. So now she doesn’t deserve ½ of what she didn’t work for.\n\n\n\nIn 2013, Vanessa Bryant responded to these lyrics in an Instagram comment and claimed Drake had apologized to her in a text.\n\n\nI love when immature kids quote a rapper that has never been friends with Kobe and knows nothing about our relationship. Just shows how gullible they are. I don’t need to be in the gym. I’m raising our daughters, signing checks and taking care of everything else that pertains to our home life. But thanks for your concern over an immature quote.\n\nI really wish people would stop THINK and then realize that they are being sucked into someone’s clear intention to monetize and gain attention off of our family’s heartache. This is real life. I hold down our home life so my husband can focus on his career. It’s a partnership.\n\nSince my husband isn’t friends with Drake, we received a text of an apology forwarded from his manager. It’s done and over with and it’s now time for people to THINK before speaking.", "annotation_id": "541830", "url": "https://genius.com/541830", "state": "accepted", "votes": 166, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Tell Lucian I said “fuck it,” I’m tearin’ holes in my budget", "vault_lines": ["Tell Lucian I said \"fuck it,\" I'm tearin' holes in my budget"], "meaning": "As in Lucian Grainge, the CEO of Universal Music Group, of which Young Money is a subsidiary.\n\nDrake is letting his boss know he doesn’t care if he’s exceeding his album budget, because a more quality product will ultimately garner him more profit.\n\nOn January 26, 2020, at the Universal Grammy afterparty, Lucian was asked about this line to which he replied:\n\nWhenever Drake calls up and says he needs more money for his project, I give it to him…because he’s the greatest.", "annotation_id": "538296", "url": "https://genius.com/538296", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Stay schemin’ Niggas tryna get at me, dawg", "vault_lines": ["Niggas tryna get at me, dawg"], "meaning": "Borrowed from Nas' unreleased track “Day Dreamin (Stay Schemin)” Nas originally found the inspiration for his hook in Kurtis Blow’s 1982 song “Daydreamin”.", "annotation_id": "532400", "url": "https://genius.com/532400", "state": "accepted", "votes": 95, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Then gave my nigga Penthouse another 30 (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Then gave my nigga Penthouse another 30 (Huh)"], "meaning": "This is not the first time Montana has shouted out his incarcerated friend.  Penthouse’s name comes up “What You Want From Me”\n\nOne writer tells us that “Penthouse” is also known as “Penthouse P” and that he was one of the biggest drug figures on Long Island", "annotation_id": "532392", "url": "https://genius.com/532392", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Niggas lost mills tryna beat the feds (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Niggas lost mills tryna beat the feds (Huh)"], "meaning": "See here for more on rappers' grudging respect for federal crime-fighting forces in general (as opposed to local or state police).\n\nSpecifically, he is referring to the millions he spent personally to beat a murder charge.", "annotation_id": "532388", "url": "https://genius.com/532388", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "From the hoopty coupe to that Ghost, dawg (Huh) Pigeons on the roof like Ghost Dog (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["From the hoopty coupe to that Ghost, dawg (Huh)", "Pigeons on the roof like Ghost Dog (Huh)"], "meaning": "Along with a bit of internal rhyming, French makes nice use of one of RG’s favorite literary devices, the homophone. The first “Ghost, dog” refers to the extremely expensive Rolls-Royce Ghost, which Montana now claims to be driving in place of his old, beat-up car.\n\n\n\nThe second iteration of the phrase refers to the movie called Ghost Dog: The Way of the Samurai, a 1999 film directed by Jim Jarmusch which starred Forest Whitaker as a hit man who only communicates by homing pigeon, and thus keeps a stable of birds.  Montana’s pigeons are of a different kind entirely – it’s slang for a kilo of coke\n\n\n\nPS, as Willy Staley for the NEW YORK MOTHERFUCKING TIMES has noted (in an article that mentions us!), he’s NOT saying “fanute” (this is a mondegreen). French even cleared it up with us!\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NFotPdXD_XY", "annotation_id": "532378", "url": "https://genius.com/532378", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "I’m just hittin’ my pinnacle, you and pussy identical You like the fuckin’ finish line; we can’t wait to run into you", "vault_lines": ["You like the fuckin' finish line; we can't wait to run into you"], "meaning": "The young Drizzy taking age-related shots at Common, who at the time of this song was a few months from 40.\n\nDrake and Common did eventually run into each other at the 2012 Grammys. But according to Common, they squashed the beef once they meet face to face:\n\nWe had a talk, we had a conversation. It was a face-to-face, man-to-man, a positive thing. It was a good conversation. I’ve learned to respect him even more. I already thought he was a talented guy, but just from the conversation we had, I hold even more respect for him. We saw each other and the conversation was initiated. It was just a respectful conversation that needed to be had, so he knew that I’m not at him trying to destroy him as a human being. It was a Hip Hop battle to me. I had to put all of those things into Hip Hop… I gave him a pound, gave him a boom and it was all love.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ryO-eugtEBc\n\nDrake and Common were eventually spotted together at the 2012 NBA All-Star game after the beef ended:", "annotation_id": "532373", "url": "https://genius.com/532373", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Might look light, but we heavy though You think Drake will pull some shit like that? You never know", "vault_lines": ["Might look light, but we heavy though", "You think Drake will pull some shit like that? You never know"], "meaning": "While Drake and his cohorts may not appear to be tough, they may well surprise you with weapons and/or violence.  There’s also a pun here on Drizzy’s light-skinned appearance – the rapper is famously biracial.  Below with his mother, Sandi Graham:", "annotation_id": "532322", "url": "https://genius.com/532322", "state": "accepted", "votes": 63, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Double M, I got Gs out in California (Huh!)", "vault_lines": ["Double M, I got Gs out in California (Huh!)"], "meaning": "Ross puns on the name of his label, Maybach Music Group or MMG, suggesting that the Maybach Music folks make money (G’s) and/or know real “g’s” – gangsters – on the West Coast", "annotation_id": "532298", "url": "https://genius.com/532298", "state": "accepted", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Dwight Howard on the post, dawg (Huh) My niggas got the powder through the post, dawg (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Dwight Howard on the post, dawg (Huh)", "My niggas got the powder through the post, dawg (Huh)"], "meaning": "Montana stays “posted up” at his drug corner similar to how NBA big man Dwight Howard stays posted up in the basketball sense of the term\n\n\n\nContinuing with the homophones, as above, the second “post” here refers to sending drugs via the postal service", "annotation_id": "531510", "url": "https://genius.com/531510", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Gave my nigga Max 7-5 (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Gave my nigga Max 7-5 (Huh)"], "meaning": "Referring to his pal, Dipset associate Charlie “Max B” Wingate, who is currently serving a 75 year sentence on armed robbery, kidnapping, and murder charges", "annotation_id": "531506", "url": "https://genius.com/531506", "state": "accepted", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Guess every team doesn’t come complete with niggas like ours", "vault_lines": ["Guess every team doesn't come complete with niggas like ours"], "meaning": "Drake is part of Young Money Cash Money Billionaires, a team run by Birdman, Lil Wayne, Nicki Minaj, Tyga, Busta Rhymes, et. al.\n\nHe could be taking swipes at G.O.O.D. Music, since Common is affiliated with that crew.\n\nAlso, Pusha T is signed to G.O.O.D. Music, and he has his own feud with Drake, as evident from “Sweet Freestyle” and “Don’t Fuck With Me.”", "annotation_id": "531503", "url": "https://genius.com/531503", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Bag her like we in Publix, and take her ass out in public", "vault_lines": ["Bag her like we in Publix, and take her ass out in public"], "meaning": "Publix is a supermarket similar to Kroger or Whole Foods Market. The chain is Southern based and especially heavy in Florida, where it originated.  A long way from Toronto, Drake likely dropped the name for a little authenticity, because he often talks about being in Miami.\n\nDrake sounds a little bit like his labelmate:\n\n“And I got her nigga – grocery bag!”", "annotation_id": "531484", "url": "https://genius.com/531484", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Me and my man Oliver North, that’s how I roll around", "vault_lines": ["Me and my man Oliver North, that's how I roll around"], "meaning": "Oliver is fellow Canadian OVO member Oliver El-Khatib, who Drake calls one of his closest friends and is Drizzy’s brand manager.  The “North” presumably comes from his Canadian heritage (he grew up in both London and Toronto), and is a pun on the controversial political figure Oliver North.  El-Khatib is pictured below:", "annotation_id": "531469", "url": "https://genius.com/531469", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "That’s why I see no need to compete with niggas like y’all", "vault_lines": ["That's why I see no need to compete with niggas like y'all"], "meaning": "Drake’s producer and close friend Noah “40” Shebib responded to Common over Twitter:", "annotation_id": "531388", "url": "https://genius.com/531388", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "I just ask that when you see me you speak up, nigga, that’s all Don’t be duckin’ like you never wanted nothin’", "vault_lines": ["I just ask that when you see me you speak up, nigga, that's all", "Don't be duckin' like you never wanted nothin'"], "meaning": "Drake accused Common of avoiding him and addressed this at the Cali Christmas Concert after “Sweet” dropped.\n\nDrake and Common did eventually run into each other at the 2012 Grammys. But according to Common, they squashed the beef once they meet face to face:\n\nWe had a talk, we had a conversation. It was a face-to-face, man-to-man, a positive thing. It was a good conversation. I’ve learned to respect him even more. I already thought he was a talented guy, but just from the conversation we had, I hold even more respect for him. We saw each other and the conversation was initiated. It was just a respectful conversation that needed to be had, so he knew that I’m not at him trying to destroy him as a human being. It was a Hip Hop battle to me. I had to put all of those things into Hip Hop… I gave him a pound, gave him a boom and it was all love.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ryO-eugtEBc\n\nDrake and Common were eventually spotted together at the 2012 NBA All-Star game after the beef ended:", "annotation_id": "531372", "url": "https://genius.com/531372", "state": "accepted", "votes": 79, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Shawty wanna tell me secrets ’bout a rap nigga I tell that bitch it’s more attractive when you hold it down", "vault_lines": ["Shawty wanna tell me secrets 'bout a rap nigga", "I tell that bitch it's more attractive when you hold it down"], "meaning": "Here Drake alludes to the possible reason for Common’s beef:  They both have been romantically linked to tennis star Serena Williams.  See here for a rundown on the Drake-Serena relationship rumors\n\n\n\n\n\nIn an interview with VladTV after the beef, Common admitted it was a “war over a girl” saying:\n\nUnfortunately the war might have been over a girl, even though at the time I never said that. But it wasn’t totally that, it was more or less, ‘Do you respect me? Because I actually like you as an artist.’\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XlfqfLm27n8\n\nDrake could also be referring to radio host Charlamagne. With them too having had intercourse with the same girls, they now know certain things about each other when it comes to ‘the bedroom’ because of pillow talk.\n\nhttp://youtu.be/5X8JouGD_fo?t=3m34s", "annotation_id": "531314", "url": "https://genius.com/531314", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Back when if a nigga reached it was for the weapon Nowadays niggas reach just to sell they record", "vault_lines": ["Back when if a nigga reached it was for the weapon", "Nowadays niggas reach just to sell they record"], "meaning": "Drake continues the Common-bashing here. Com’s single “Sweet” was released 1 ½ months before his ninth album, The Dreamer/The Believer, which sparked people’s interest as to who he was talking about. Here Drake suggests Common dissed him simply because he wanted to sell records by igniting a beef with Drake for no real reason.", "annotation_id": "531226", "url": "https://genius.com/531226", "state": "accepted", "votes": 74, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "It bothers me when the gods get to actin’ like the broads", "vault_lines": ["It bothers me when the gods get to actin' like the broads"], "meaning": "Aimed at rapper Common. Common dissed Drake in the song “Sweet”–something he at first denied, and then grudgingly admitted.\n\nThis line establishes the verse as Drake’s counter-punch and indicates that Common is acting like a woman.  The “acting” word choice is a nod to Common’s burgeoning career as a thespian. The phrasing also gives Common respect for what he’s done in the rap game, just calling him a bitch for what he’s doing now\n\nWhile Drake has not directly confirmed that this section of the song is about Common, French Montana, who also appears on this track, has told interviewers to “read between the lines” to understand the tune.", "annotation_id": "531146", "url": "https://genius.com/531146", "state": "accepted", "votes": 160, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Stay Schemin’", "line": "Kobe ’bout to lose a hundred fifty Ms", "vault_lines": ["Kobe 'bout to lose a hundred fifty Ms"], "meaning": "Kobe’s wife Vanessa filed for divorce in 2011 and seemed poised to make a killing off the split from the then-highest paid player in the NBA. Similar to Tiger and Elin' divorce, but Elin got 110 million! In the end, the couple didn’t go through with the divorce.", "annotation_id": "531133", "url": "https://genius.com/531133", "state": "accepted", "votes": 132, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
