{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "The Lion Roars", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Stephen-marley-the-lion-roars-lyrics", "genius_title": "The Lion Roars", "genius_id": 3553626, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "The More I Get", "line": "My past was illusive", "vault_lines": ["My past was illusive"], "meaning": "A corrections officer yes, but should that invalidate his artistic output? As Jesus said,\n\nHe without sin cast the first stone.", "annotation_id": "1723989", "url": "https://genius.com/1723989", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "The More I Get", "line": "Time for a revolution And it won’t be televised, say goodbye to my future", "vault_lines": ["Time for a revolution", "And it won't be televised, say goodbye to my future"], "meaning": "The revolution is found in the music. Ross references the song “The Revolution Will Not Be Televised” by Gil Scott-Heron:\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rGaRtqrlGy8\nTelevision is montered and controlled by corporations, while music is commercialized as well, it has more freedom for social change.\n\nRoss speaks of how his personal future is gone, and by becoming the Biggest Bawse in Hip Hop, he has sacrificed his personal life for his music and fans.", "annotation_id": "1723984", "url": "https://genius.com/1723984", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "The More I Get", "line": "And made me write the song that took me all night long Started as a poem at first but kept going", "vault_lines": ["And made me write the song that took me all night long", "Started as a poem at first but kept going"], "meaning": "The discovery of music as a way to become wealthy without risking incarceration.\n\nIt began as reciting simple poems, but soon after listening to and subsequently memorizing Eric B & Rakim’s Paid in Full, Ross became invested in Hip Hop.", "annotation_id": "1723978", "url": "https://genius.com/1723978", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "The More I Get", "line": "Girls used to laugh at the way I talked", "vault_lines": ["Girls used to laugh at the way I talked"], "meaning": "Girls laughed at both his voice, which I’m sure was still bossy back in the day, as well as the jokes he’s made.\n\nRolling Stone Magazine on the voice of Hip Hop’s Biggest Bawse\"\n\nRick Ross' voice can only be described as a rich, booming baritone that sounds like a steamroller running over a million dollars in slow motion.", "annotation_id": "1438159", "url": "https://genius.com/1438159", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "The More I Get", "line": "Like the watch glass must of stared at Medusa", "vault_lines": ["Like the watch glass must of stared at Medusa"], "meaning": "Both this mixtape & Kanye’s Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy were released in 2010, so it is not clear wether this or his guest verse on Devil in a New Dress was rapped first.\n\nBoth contain allusions to the infamous gaze:\n\nLookin at my wrist it’ll turn your ass to stone", "annotation_id": "1400845", "url": "https://genius.com/1400845", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "The More I Get", "line": "Polished a man’s shoes sitting on that throne", "vault_lines": ["Polished a man's shoes sitting on that throne"], "meaning": "End of Becoming Ross Pt 1.\n\nBoss is a state of mind. The study of being a boss is Bawseology, in which Rozay has a PHD.\n\n\n\nRoss began his career in stacking paper as a shoe-shiner, a boy who shines rich mens shoes for tips.\n\n\n\nOne day Ross would be on that same throne, tossing a Jesus Piece to the youngin' like Mean Joe Green would a jersey.", "annotation_id": "1366213", "url": "https://genius.com/1366213", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "The More I Get", "line": "So from that day on I was on my own", "vault_lines": ["So from that day on I was on my own"], "meaning": "Then he got signed to Suave-House and eventually met GUNPLAY and formed a lifelong criminally inspired prolific music empire.\n\n\n\nTriple C’s to Maybach Music.\n\nNot to mention Rozay’s High School football career. He had coaches right?\n\nThe Maybach Music Group CEO graduated from Carol City Senior High School and later attended historically black college Albany State University in Albany, Georgia on a football scholarship.", "annotation_id": "1366206", "url": "https://genius.com/1366206", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "The More I Get", "line": "My nappy hair corduroys and my shirt seemed small", "vault_lines": ["My nappy hair corduroys and my shirt seemed small"], "meaning": "The perpetually dapper Rick Ross describes his “look” before he learned the craft of the rhyme and became the Biggest Bawse.\n\n\n\nJust as the High School ID of Lil B proved, the immortal were once just like us, corduroys and all.", "annotation_id": "1366142", "url": "https://genius.com/1366142", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
