{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Uh, wake up in the world and I’m just another nigga Call it public housin’ when you next door to the killers On them corners, it gets better as you go", "vault_lines": ["Call it public housin' when you next door to the killers", "On them corners, it gets better as you go"], "meaning": "https://players.brightcove.net/4863540648001/HklmClHkx_default/index.html?videoId=5236746150001", "annotation_id": "2876601", "url": "https://genius.com/2876601", "state": "verified", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Can’t believe we did it", "vault_lines": ["Can't believe we did it"], "meaning": "Mastermind was an album that had its fair share of delays, and many thought that due to the amount of delays the album may get shelved. The album was slated for a December 17th 2013 release, but the album did not drop officially until March 4th 2014.", "annotation_id": "2870129", "url": "https://genius.com/2870129", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Double M, we handle business", "vault_lines": ["Double M, we handle business"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is calling himself and Lil Wayne masterminds (“Double M” meaning masterminds), and that they are the big dogs handling business.\n\n\n\nAlso referring to MMG (Ross' Label) saying that they handle business.", "annotation_id": "2861279", "url": "https://genius.com/2861279", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "J.U.S.T.I.C.E. League", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "During a 2014 interview with XXL Magazine, Justice League explained how the track came about:\n\n\nRoss has sort of chosen to keep fucking with us because the chemistry is obviously there. Whether it be during the beginning stages of his projects or the ending stage of his project, we always kind of get in with him or get in contact with him about final touch ups or whatever. I think we all know that there’s a chemistry there.\n\nRoss tends to gravitate towards the music that we make that most people don’t know what to do with. They might be scared of it or they just don’t know what to do with it. He has a history of just picking off the beaten path basically. He’ll pick the tracks that nobody else picks and everyone is mad at us because we didn’t send them that beat or whatever. Most of the time, they just didn’t take that beat.\n\nThe hook was sung by Betty Idol. You gotta remember that the beat was made in 2013. So it was the twenty year anniversary of “93 ‘til Infinity.” So we did that. It was paying homage. That decade year long classic. We got Betty Idol to write and perform the hook on it. It was undeniable. We just went ahead and sent it off to Ross. You know Ross likes to push the boundaries, like we said earlier. Once you hear the hook? Ah, shit. That shit’s crazy.", "annotation_id": "2859528", "url": "https://genius.com/2859528", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Go against the odds youngin’, go and get a job Another country boy they want back on the farm", "vault_lines": ["Go against the odds youngin', go and get a job", "Another country boy they want back on the farm"], "meaning": "Possibly a nod to slavery times, from the previous line he is talking to a kid going against the odds.\nStatistics are against him and Rozay states they just want them back on the farm to pick cotton.\n\n\n\nMost likely the “yougin'” he’s talking about is African American, getting a job is going against the odds, as statistics say African Americans typically end up in prison before they turn 25—right where they want you.", "annotation_id": "2857155", "url": "https://genius.com/2857155", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Free as a bird, spoken word in my verse", "vault_lines": ["Free as a bird, spoken word in my verse"], "meaning": "‘Free as a bird’ is a common simile referring to the carelessness of one’s actions. Rozay can do whatever he wants. In his own words:\n\nThese niggas won’t hold me back – Rick Ross – “Hold Me Back”\n\n\n\nSpoken Word, generally speaking, is a common form of poetry. It generally focuses on the meanings of the words in a piece of work. Ross is telling stories in his verses, not just relying on a beat to give his song meaning.\n\nNotice the multi in this line:\n\nFree as a bird, spoken word in my verse.", "annotation_id": "2856265", "url": "https://genius.com/2856265", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "I lived on Billboard, where the fuck to go next?", "vault_lines": ["I lived on Billboard, where the fuck to go next?"], "meaning": "Rick Ross has been very successful on the Billboard charts during the span of his career—he’s had 13 singles as a lead artist and 12 as a featured artist chart in the top 100 as well as having four #1 albums and two #2 albums.\n\nAfter achieving all this success on the charts already, Ross ponders what can he do next?", "annotation_id": "2855201", "url": "https://genius.com/2855201", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Go get the yellow tape, it’s well orchestrated", "vault_lines": ["Go get the yellow tape, it's well orchestrated"], "meaning": "Yellow tape is used in a crime scene to keep bystanders at bay and prevent them from trespassing. Weezy also appeared on a Fat Joe song of the same name.", "annotation_id": "2854383", "url": "https://genius.com/2854383", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Grind that motherfucker ’til it’s yellow brick road", "vault_lines": ["Grind that motherfucker 'til it's yellow brick road"], "meaning": "Referring to the famous film The Wizard of Oz: Rozay’s referring to grinding “bricks” as in cocaine, which can sell for the price of gold. This is how he had to try to make it at first hustling on the corners.\n\n\n\nThe music video also stars Wood Harris, known for playing drug kingpin Avon Barksdale in the The Wire. The video alludes to a cycle of drugs, violence, death, and incarceration that Ross managed to escape. http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jEy9ygty4JQ", "annotation_id": "2854372", "url": "https://genius.com/2854372", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Money over bitches, first nigga with a Wraith", "vault_lines": ["Money over bitches, first nigga with a Wraith"], "meaning": "Rolls Royce released a new line of its luxurious cars called the Rolls Royce Wraith in 2014.It has been reported that this will be the fastest and most luxurious British car to be built by Rolls-Royce Ross claims he was the first nigga to own one of them", "annotation_id": "2851268", "url": "https://genius.com/2851268", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Niggas hatin’ like it’s Salt Lake City", "vault_lines": ["Niggas hatin' like it's Salt Lake City"], "meaning": "Urban Dictionary says the meaning of Salty is:\nSALTY—to be pissed, upset\nFor example:\nShe was salty because she lost the game.\n\nSo basically he’s saying many haters or angry or salty about his success, making them envious.\nSo he’s comparing the amount of haters he has to, Salt Lake City. Get it?\nClever wordplay from “The Boss himself.\n\nHistorical Connection: Salt Lake City is where the Mormons finally settled after being hated on across the continental United States of America.", "annotation_id": "2851117", "url": "https://genius.com/2851117", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "But to make a long story short, I need a shoulder ‘Cause the devil on one, the other one, I’m lookin’ over", "vault_lines": ["But to make a long story short, I need a shoulder", "'Cause the devil on one, the other one, I'm lookin' over"], "meaning": "To have a devil on ones shoulder is figurative language for having an inner-wicked voice attempting to influence you negatively. And the phrase I need a shoulder means having someone there for you to help.\n\nWayne is burdened by the devil on his shoulder and the constant need to look over his “other one” for potential threats; hence, he needs someone else’s shoulder to lie on for support. It’s good to have someone keep you company and help you when you’re being plagued by your inner-demons.\n\n\n\nNote: the implication that he needs another shoulder for support because the devil is weighing down on his.", "annotation_id": "2850786", "url": "https://genius.com/2850786", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Tunechi", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Tunechi is one of Wayne’s many nicknames. He received this nickname from his grandmother and has been using it very frequently lately.", "annotation_id": "2850480", "url": "https://genius.com/2850480", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "On my knees prayin’, niggas shootin’ in the church", "vault_lines": ["On my knees prayin', niggas shootin' in the church"], "meaning": "Even while he is in Church praying for an end to violence, there are hits being carried out inside that very Church. This exemplifies just how hard the it is in the hood, Churches are supposed to be a place of peace and repentance, but in the hood they are just another place of sin and murder.", "annotation_id": "2850456", "url": "https://genius.com/2850456", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Get a bad bitch that posts up like comments", "vault_lines": ["Get a bad bitch that posts up like comments"], "meaning": "Wayne makes his hoes post up on street corners to sell themselves and bring him back the money they’ve earned.\n\n\n\nOf course, this being Wayne, he feels the need to tie this into a metaphor/punchline. This time playing on the word “post” and making it relate to posting comments online.", "annotation_id": "2850442", "url": "https://genius.com/2850442", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Came from the hood, ain’t nothing changed Still lemon pepper on my motherfuckin’ wings!", "vault_lines": ["Came from the hood, ain't nothing changed", "Still lemon pepper on my motherfuckin' wings!"], "meaning": "Ross loves his Wingstop, he even owns several of them.\n\n\n\nFun fact: This marks the 3rd Wingstop reference for Ross on Mastermind. More specifically, the second Lemon Pepper wing reference.", "annotation_id": "2850222", "url": "https://genius.com/2850222", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "200 acres estates, a young nigga made it", "vault_lines": ["200 acres estates, a young nigga made it"], "meaning": "He recently purchased the largest mansion in Georgia, 54,000 square-foot with 109 rooms and sits on 235 acres. It was previously owned by retired boxer Evander Holyfield.", "annotation_id": "2850203", "url": "https://genius.com/2850203", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Can’t trust none of these niggas, I murk one of these niggas Then bury one of these niggas, still got dirt under my fingers That ain’t a threat, that’s a bet ‘Cause they comin’ at my neck like the best a man can get", "vault_lines": ["Can't trust none of these niggas, I murk one of these niggas", "Then bury one of these niggas, still got dirt under my fingers", "That ain't a threat, that's a bet", "'Cause they comin' at my neck like the best a man can get"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne feels like he’s surrounded by shifty characters—he can’t tell the real from the fake. He’s not afraid to kill someone who wronged him; he’ll even bury their body. He’s so casual about it that hasn’t even bothered to scrub the dirt from under his fingernails after burying the last guy he killed.\n\n\n\nHe uses a simile to compare the people who are “coming at his neck” to the motto of razor company Gilette (“the best a man can get”). Razors are used on the face and neck, which Wayne cleverly relates to the rappers that are coming for his neck, or coming to kill him or take his throne in the rap game.", "annotation_id": "2850192", "url": "https://genius.com/2850192", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Smokin’ on that loud, pumpin’ up that volume", "vault_lines": ["Smokin' on that loud, pumpin' up that volume"], "meaning": "Double Entendre:\n\n1) Weezy is smoking more often, likely to cope with the fact that he feels he’s putting out mediocre music as of late.\n\n2) Wayne’s weed is strong, or loud, so he plays on the word volume to illustrate that his marijuana is now better than ever.", "annotation_id": "2850136", "url": "https://genius.com/2850136", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Get it crackin’ like an eggshell in this motherfucker, make omelettes", "vault_lines": ["Get it crackin' like an eggshell in this motherfucker, make omelettes"], "meaning": "“Get cracking” is an expression meaning to act quickly. Wayne relates this to the literal cracking of “eggshells,” and eggs are a primary ingredient in the popular breakfast meal, “omelettes.”\n\nHe also spits a similar line on his 2006 song, “Cannons”.\n\nNiggas know I’m sick, I don’t spit, I vomit, got it?\nOne egg short of the omelet", "annotation_id": "2850093", "url": "https://genius.com/2850093", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Thug Cry", "line": "Thug cry for Mac Dre, nigga, throwin’ up Thizz", "vault_lines": ["Thug cry for Mac Dre, nigga, throwin' up Thizz"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is shouting out Mac Dre, who was one of the legends of the Hyphy movement in Hip-Hop, who helped popularize the term thizz, which refers to the effects of and the consumption of ecstasy, and features its own hand signal (which Mac Dre is displaying in the image above).\n\nMac Dre often used the term in his music and founded his own record label called Thizz Entertainment.  Unfortunately Mac Dre was shot and killed in Kansas City, Missouri on November 1st, 2004.", "annotation_id": "2850001", "url": "https://genius.com/2850001", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
