{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Trilla Intro", "line": "Shout out to Bun B", "vault_lines": ["shout out to Bun B"], "meaning": "Southern rapper Bun B, who has collaborated with Ross multiple times. He was one half of the legendary duo UGK, with the late Pimp C.", "annotation_id": "2831024", "url": "https://genius.com/2831024", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Trilla Intro", "line": "Shout out to Jay-Z", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to Lil' Jay, shout out to Jay-Z"], "meaning": "Jay-Z signed Rick Ross to Def Jam, so Ross salutes him for that.", "annotation_id": "1427291", "url": "https://genius.com/1427291", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Trilla Intro", "line": "Shout out to E-Class", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to E-Class"], "meaning": "A shoutout to E-Class the producer, Ross' former money manager. They eventually had a falling out, however.", "annotation_id": "1281531", "url": "https://genius.com/1281531", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Trilla Intro", "line": "I’m paranoid it’s too much parafonalia Public prosecutors got me preparin’ for failure Picture pimp, picture me pimpin’ the pen", "vault_lines": ["I'm paranoid it's too much parafonalia", "Public prosecutors got me preparin' for failure", "Picture pimp, picture me pimpin' the pen"], "meaning": "Note the amazing alliteration in these lines with the letter P, and it continues into the next line.\n\nThere’s too many items for making crack around him—makes him paranoid that he may go to jail because he’s already going to court hoping to win, but prepared to lose and do jail time. Even if he’s in jail you already know Rozay will be pimpin' hard.", "annotation_id": "1277483", "url": "https://genius.com/1277483", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Trilla Intro", "line": "With all these pretty scriptures that I can pimp with my pen Oh no the Lord is my light and my savior", "vault_lines": ["Oh no the Lord is my light and my savior"], "meaning": "He says his lyrics are just like “scriptures”, words to help guide one’s life.\n\nThe Lord is my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear? the Lord is the strength of my life; of whom shall I be afraid?\n\nPsalm 27:1", "annotation_id": "842699", "url": "https://genius.com/842699", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Trilla Intro", "line": "Shout out to Trick Daddy, I got love, fool We both millionaires, let’s make some thug moves", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to Trick Daddy, I got love, fool", "We both millionaires, let's make some thug moves"], "meaning": "Ross is saluting Trick Daddy, who would feature Ross on his early albums before Ross blew up. Ross was a HUGE Trick Daddy fan before he got into a beef with him.", "annotation_id": "842696", "url": "https://genius.com/842696", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Trilla Intro", "line": "Shout out to Gucci Pucci", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to Gucci Pucci"], "meaning": "Gucci Pucci is Ross’s manager. He’s the one in the maybach with Ross in the BMF video\n\n\n\nRozay loves his Gucci Pucci shout outs", "annotation_id": "842689", "url": "https://genius.com/842689", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Trilla Intro", "line": "Shout out to Kenneth Williams", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to Kenneth Williams"], "meaning": "Kenneth Griffith Williams was a notorious gang leader in NY in the 90’s.", "annotation_id": "842687", "url": "https://genius.com/842687", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Trilla Intro", "line": "Shout out to Big Meech", "vault_lines": [", shout out to Big Meech"], "meaning": "Big Meech, the former leader of the Black Mafia Family.", "annotation_id": "251950", "url": "https://genius.com/251950", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Trilla Intro", "line": "Shout out to Larry Hoover", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to Larry Hoover"], "meaning": "Larry Hoover, former leader of the Gangster Disciples. He’s locked up now though.", "annotation_id": "251949", "url": "https://genius.com/251949", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Trilla Intro", "status": "done", "targets": 11, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-trilla-intro-lyrics", "genius_title": "Trilla Intro", "genius_id": 40268, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "Triple beam dreams", "vault_lines": ["You seein' me blurry, triple beam dreams"], "meaning": "Triple beam refers to a triple beam balance, a highly accurate scale that Rick would likely use to weigh product.", "annotation_id": "20148067", "url": "https://genius.com/20148067", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "[Verse 1: Nas]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The verse tells the story of his ultimately left-behind drug dealer phase, leading us to the place where he would drop that and become the MC of today. He’s not regretful of his decisions, but as he grows older, Nas gets that it had to be that way for him to hit his peak as a person and especially as a rapper.\nCan’t say I disagree.", "annotation_id": "7623773", "url": "https://genius.com/7623773", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "[Produced by J.U.S.T.I.C.E. League]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "It´s a really dark production. The drums on here are hard-hitting and the synths have a really eerie and sinister vibe that fits the album theme perfectly. It features crazy choice of pads – it´s a basic hip-hop beat underneath. The bells, strings and transition effects on this track helps give this song a real movie-soundtrack feel. Which is complemented with Nas and Rozay’s vivid storytelling.", "annotation_id": "2787769", "url": "https://genius.com/2787769", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "Ralph Lauren Blue Label as I’m gettin’ high", "vault_lines": ["Ralph Lauren Blue Label as I'm gettin' high"], "meaning": "Ross, Blue Label is for women…\n\n\n\nDo we have ourselves a crossdresser?\n\nKidding…\n\nContinuing the drug and fashion reference, Ross shouts out Polo in connection to his high grade, Blue Label OG Kush marijuana.", "annotation_id": "552057", "url": "https://genius.com/552057", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "The secret life of G’s", "vault_lines": ["Before the evil, the secret life of G's"], "meaning": "The secret life of G’s, where “G’s” can, and knowing Nas, does refer to both gangsters and 1,000s of dollars, which each can be used interchangeably and go hand in hand. The secret life of G’s, in this case, takes you into the world of a young dealer struggling to compete with his competitors, a lifestyle that is often overlooked in the eyes of everyday people. Nas opens up to expose the level of competition in the drug trade around Queens back in the 1980’s, which acts as a testament to the harshness of survival in Queensbridge.", "annotation_id": "550445", "url": "https://genius.com/550445", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "Food on the plate, fuck em all, I could send my dog", "vault_lines": ["Food on the plate, fuck em all, I could send my dog"], "meaning": "At the end of the day, despite criticisms of “haters”, Rick Ross can afford to provide. “Food on the plate” can also be a euphemism for having much to deal with, or staying busy & productive. As a result of this realization, Rick Ross dismisses his critics, and notes that due to his newfound power and influence, could send a friend to deal with vocal “haters”", "annotation_id": "550442", "url": "https://genius.com/550442", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "Triple beam dreams, the ghetto’s my reality", "vault_lines": ["Triple beam dreams, the ghetto's my reality"], "meaning": "Although Rick Ross has ambitions to profit from the drug trade, or hustling in general, he comes to the realization that the ghetto was/is his environment, and goes on to speak about the values life in the ghetto instilled in him.", "annotation_id": "550439", "url": "https://genius.com/550439", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "I’m from where your hustle determines your salary", "vault_lines": ["I'm from where your hustle determines your salary"], "meaning": "Where Ross is from (Carol City, Florida), your hustle is directly proportional to the amount of paper stacks you attain.\n\nWhere A is one’s given hustle, and B is the amount of paper stacks.\n\n\n\nA very similar idea was made popular by English explorer and early colonist John Smith, which he bit from the Bible.\n\nIf any would not work, neither should he eat.\n\nAdditionally, Ace Hood mentioned this idea in “Hustle Hard”", "annotation_id": "539909", "url": "https://genius.com/539909", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "Picturin’ pipin’ out the seats of a Pathfinder Powerful pursuit for pussy, cash, to flash diamonds", "vault_lines": ["Powerful pursuit for pussy, cash, to flash diamonds"], "meaning": "Using the literary technique of “alliteration”, where a sentence is constructed with similar sounds to begin each word, Nas paints a picture of the hustlers life.\n\nPiping is a lining used in many expensive cars. He aspires to have custom seats in his Nissan Pathfinder, a bomb-ass SUV from the 1990’s, while getting women and oodles of cash to have diamond jewelry.\n\nBrown piping on the leather seats of a Bentley:", "annotation_id": "535833", "url": "https://genius.com/535833", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "You seein’ me blurry, triple beam dreams", "vault_lines": ["You seein' me blurry, triple beam dreams"], "meaning": "The purpose of this verse was for Nas to share with us his dealings in the crack game, although the portrait is “blurry” because he can’t give too many details, for obvious reasons", "annotation_id": "535281", "url": "https://genius.com/535281", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "And so I was", "vault_lines": ["and so I was"], "meaning": "Isn’t it crazy how these four words, seemingly just filler, are actually a double entendre?\n\n\nNas is calling himself during his crack dealing days a poor excuse AND\nThese words introduce the next line\n\n\n\nPROPOSED SUGGESTION: Might actually be a triple entendre in the sense that he is saying that he 1. was a poor excuse (for a human being for selling crack in his past) 2. blaming society for his decisions would be a poor excuse in general and 3. intro to the next line.", "annotation_id": "535269", "url": "https://genius.com/535269", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "Blamin’ society, mad, it wasn’t made fair I would be Ivy League if America played fair Poor excuse,", "vault_lines": ["Blamin' society, mad, it wasn't made fair", "I would be Ivy League if America played fair"], "meaning": "Nas starts to get mad at America, saying that he didn’t get the proper opportunity to succeed…. But catches himself, knowing that it was his decision to do everything he did.\n\nAdditionally, Nas might be utilizing some wordplay with “Ivy League” and “played fair,” therefore invoking the rich (pun intended) athletic traditions of the Ivy League\n\nIn one sense, Nas is now Ivy League as he had a fellowship named after him at Harvard and his work has been discussed by numerous college professors.", "annotation_id": "535268", "url": "https://genius.com/535268", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "My junior high school class, wish I stayed there Illegal entrepreneur, I got my grades there", "vault_lines": ["My junior high school class, wish I stayed there", "Illegal entrepreneur, I got my grades there"], "meaning": "Nas dropped out of school in ninth grade, eliminating any chance of him getting a corporate job or anything like that, to instead graduate from the school of hard knocks (sorry for the corny cliche….)\n\n\n\nNas experiences regret at this decision, because this song is providing a narrative of the times during his crack dealing days where he was unsuccessful", "annotation_id": "535256", "url": "https://genius.com/535256", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "They out there every day, some true hustlers for you I’m at it half way, none of my customers are loyal", "vault_lines": ["They out there every day, some true hustlers for you", "I'm at it half way, none of my customers are loyal"], "meaning": "The already established dealers were moving farther and farther up because their customers trusted them, and they stayed busy all day. Nas, on the other hand, wasn’t an established dealer and couldn’t get many customers", "annotation_id": "535251", "url": "https://genius.com/535251", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "I brought it back to papi, ain’t tryna take no losses He focuses on my emotionless young dealer face, then pauses He gives me powder, he has faith in Nas’ ambitions to distribute coke", "vault_lines": ["I brought it back to papi, ain't tryna take no losses", "He focuses on my emotionless young dealer face, then pauses", "He gives me powder, he has faith in Nas' ambitions to distribute coke"], "meaning": "Because, as he explained in the prior line, the coke he was trying to sell wasn’t good, he took it back to the guy he got it from. .\n\nNas tells him that he refuses to sell it because he can’t make a profit, and the man respects Nas for having the balls to do that, and knows that Nas really wants to sell it. So he gives him more coke, presumably better, which Nas continues to sell.", "annotation_id": "535243", "url": "https://genius.com/535243", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "In the bastardized nation, Magnum .45 carryin’", "vault_lines": ["In the bastardized nation, Magnum .45 carryin'"], "meaning": "The term “bastardized” serves a literal meaning here: this nation has made many people, particularly young black people, bastards, be it through their fathers being killed, or through their fathers walking out on them.\n\nPartially because of this level of deception from their own fathers, people in the hood tend to carry guns to protect themselves.", "annotation_id": "535234", "url": "https://genius.com/535234", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "Mentally sex-crazed, dysfunctional, they describe us They liars, at the end of the day, we’re fuckin’ survivors", "vault_lines": ["Mentally sex-crazed, dysfunctional, they describe us", "They liars, at the end of the day, we're fuckin' survivors"], "meaning": "Although Nas is a bit of a sex fiend, people need to stop judging him & all the rest of the black community.\n\nPeople see that they are shooting each other, selling drugs, etc., and label them as crazy or dysfunctional, not knowing that they’re doing what they’re doing simply to survive.\n\nHe is not just talking about surviving day to day, though, but also the bigger struggle against racism and for equality.", "annotation_id": "535225", "url": "https://genius.com/535225", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "A project minded individual, criminal tactics Us black kids born with birth defects, we hyperactive", "vault_lines": ["A project minded individual, criminal tactics", "Us black kids born with birth defects, we hyperactive"], "meaning": "Like Kendrick Lamar said:\n\nYou know why we crack babies\nBecause we born in the eighties\nThat ADHD crazy\n\nExcept…. Nas was born in 1973, not the eighties. But it’s the same concept: the crack epidemic had already started affected people, especially those in inner city NYC where Nas grew up.\n\nThis made the “crack babies” be born a little hyperactive, always needing something to do. Well, the thing which they found easiest to become a part of was hustling on the streets, so they did.", "annotation_id": "535221", "url": "https://genius.com/535221", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "Where I’m from ain’t far from Washington Heights to cop Aryan", "vault_lines": ["Where I'm from ain't far from Washington Heights to cop Aryan"], "meaning": "Nas was born in Queens, NYC, which was very close to Washington Heights, Manhattan.\n\n‘COP Aryan’ refers to ‘Combat Outpost Aryan’ – a U.S. military post in Afghanistan that made headlines in 2011 after soldiers there posed with a flag resembling the Nazi SS flag. Aryan is a term associated with Nazism and the Aryan Brotherhood, a white supremacist group.\n\n\n\nNas is smoothly transitioning between his critique of the United States and its history of racism towards and marginalization of blacks. ‘Cop Aryan’ has the second meaning of buying (copping) cocaine (Aryan because Aryan=white, like coke).\n\nSo, he went to Washington Heights to buy cocaine for him to distribute, like many other kids in NYC during that time.", "annotation_id": "534461", "url": "https://genius.com/534461", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "It’s time to take you to the other side The side you gotta watch your A&E cable television for homie But we live this shit", "vault_lines": ["But we live this shit"], "meaning": "A&E cable features shows like First 48, which highlights the criminal activities going on in major cities.", "annotation_id": "532246", "url": "https://genius.com/532246", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "Early 90s reminiscin’ when I had a pager Triple beam dreams: now Pat Riley my neighbor", "vault_lines": ["Early 90s reminiscin' when I had a pager", "Triple beam dreams: now Pat Riley my neighbor"], "meaning": "A double entendre from Rick Ross. It refers to both the location of his house, and that Ross is in a luxury box at the Heat games located next to the one Pat Riley is in (or sitting courtside next to him).", "annotation_id": "531475", "url": "https://genius.com/531475", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "Got the plug with the uncut Jay-Z Blue Magic nigga, what what", "vault_lines": ["Jay-Z Blue Magic nigga, what what"], "meaning": "Blue Magic is a type of heroin that was sold by infamous drug kingpin Frank Lucas. Ross has the connect with the pure heroin. Jay-Z wrote a song entitled “Blue Magic” for his album American Gangster which got it’s inspiration from the movie of the same name.\n\nFrank Lucas was famous for cutting out the middle man, making him the source in the U.S. This makes the connection to The Source magazine reference.", "annotation_id": "531345", "url": "https://genius.com/531345", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "Fuck boy, you caught up in my dream Countin’ cream on the cover of a magazine I’m the source,", "vault_lines": ["Fuck boy, you caught up in my dream", "Countin' cream on the cover of a magazine"], "meaning": "You can only wish to live the life Ross does. He’s successful and is the kingpin moving tons of dope. Ross also alludes to the fact that he made the cover of the 2011 year-end issue of The Source Magazine.", "annotation_id": "531337", "url": "https://genius.com/531337", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "line": "Rule number 1: I can’t do business with a stranger", "vault_lines": ["Rule number 1: I can't do business with a stranger"], "meaning": "This is a smart rule to live by when dealing dope. It’s easier to trust an associate than it is to trust a stranger. That random person that you sell 10 kilos to might be 5-0…\n\nIs this the New Crack Commandment?", "annotation_id": "531323", "url": "https://genius.com/531323", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Beam Dreams", "status": "done", "targets": 24, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-triple-beam-dreams-lyrics", "genius_title": "Triple Beam Dreams", "genius_id": 62989, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Platinum", "line": "100 bands on me, really that’s my sneaker money", "vault_lines": ["100 bands on me, really that's my sneaker money"], "meaning": "Rephrases a line from Play Your Part.\n\nTwenty racks, really that’s sneaker money\n\nOnly difference is 100 bands instead of 20 racks. Not an inflation-updated figure, Ross is pretty much saying he spends a lot more in shoes in 2017 than he did in 2010.", "annotation_id": "30810114", "url": "https://genius.com/30810114", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Platinum", "line": "’Cause when they land, they be whiter than the Easter bunny", "vault_lines": ["'Cause when they land, they be whiter than the Easter bunny"], "meaning": "The bands will be clean no matter where they land, i.e. laundered money. Could also be talking about clean new sneakers.\n\nRecycled metaphor from a line of his, where the same whiteness was used to describe a Ferrari.", "annotation_id": "30809897", "url": "https://genius.com/30809897", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Platinum", "line": "Had a phone call with Drake so we could fix the madness", "vault_lines": ["Had a phone call with Drake so we could fix the madness"], "meaning": "http://www.bet.com/music/2017/03/29/rick-ross-drake-meeting.html Reconciliation between the frequent collaborators, post Meek Mill ghostwriter accusations toward Drake and the ensuing back-and-forth was initiated when Ross and Drake had a phone call and a subsequent meeting.", "annotation_id": "11859179", "url": "https://genius.com/11859179", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Platinum", "line": "But I had a lawyer like them triple platinum His retainer was the change up in the kitchen cabinet", "vault_lines": ["But I had a lawyer like them triple platinum", "His retainer was the change up in the kitchen cabinet"], "meaning": "$ 500.000 is considered to be a negilgible amount of money to Ross, who keeps such amounts in his kitchen storage space. \nI’m out on bond so I gotta beat it (Lord) Half a million to my lawyer ‘cause he undefeated― Rick Ross (Ft. Yo Gotti) – Summer Seventeen", "annotation_id": "11859041", "url": "https://genius.com/11859041", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Platinum", "line": "You not a boss ’til everybody at your table ate", "vault_lines": ["You not a boss 'til everybody at your table ate"], "meaning": "This is a diss towards Birdman. This is related directly to the line on his song “Idols Become Rivals” where Rick Ross says “How the fuck, nigga, you touch half a billion, nigga\nAnd your team starvin', nigga”", "annotation_id": "11753360", "url": "https://genius.com/11753360", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Platinum", "line": "Told the detectives, “Fuck ’em” and I bought a rocket missile", "vault_lines": ["Told the detectives, \"Fuck 'em\" and I bought a rocket missile"], "meaning": "As an allure to the previous line, no suspects were found after the shooting incident. We all know Ross always keeps two guns; one registered and one to solve his issues.\n\nSo Rozay is of the opinion that he doesn’t need protection for the police. He has the ‘Stand your Ground’ law to fuel this very belief.", "annotation_id": "11630723", "url": "https://genius.com/11630723", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Platinum", "line": "Somebody tried to kill me, still I’m optimistic", "vault_lines": ["Somebody tried to kill me, still I'm optimistic"], "meaning": "In 2013, Rick Ross and his girlfriend were victims of a 360i BMW drive-by, where they had to flee for their lives. A street gang known as the Gangster Disciples recently took to  YouTube to explain its grievances with Ross, who they claim is at fault for name-dropping the gang’s chairman Larry Hoover on the chorus of his 2010 single “B.M.F. (Blowin' Money Fast).\n\nThe dangerous gang has urged the rapper to settle the matter, but Ross has openly refused to grant their requests.\n\nThreats aimed at Ross have since increased with gangs in other states like North Carolina and Alabama calling on the rapper to pay his alleged dues.", "annotation_id": "11630631", "url": "https://genius.com/11630631", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Platinum", "line": "Murals of Machiavelli", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "This follows Ross' proposition that it’s “time for the real niggas to really cash in.” Machiavelli proposes that  a general “should consider peace only as a breathing-time, which gives him leisure to contrive, and furnishes as ability to execute, military plans.” In that sense, Ross’s move to squash the beef with Drake is a move to furnish his ability to execute future moves to increase revenues in the music business. Realistically too, he preaches the need for peace in the music industry, which he believes would make artists not only work together but create an upward progression of their net worth.\n\nPROPOSED SUGGESTION: First of all, he was referring to “Makavelli” which is an alias used by the late rapper 2pac; not Niccolò “Machiavelli” the philosopher. Rick Ross even referred to Meek Mill as such as Meek Mill was gaining rap notoriety while in prison similarly to 2pac when he wrote “The Don Killuminati: The 7 Day Theory” which had an alternate title of “Don Makaveli – Killuminati: The 7 Day Theory”. Ross even made a song called “Makavell- Tupac back”.  So Murals of Makavelli is referring to 2pac. I don’t want to make the assumption that Ross is not an under-the-radar adept at history and doesn’t know of Niccolò “Machiavelli, but I can surmise that because of the mention of this particular name in songs and just its use by him in general, he is referring to 2pac. He even made a song called \"Makavelli (Tupac back)”.\n\nHowever, listening to Makavelli(Tupac), one can surmise that he drew some murals from the historical Niccolo Machiavelli.", "annotation_id": "11568859", "url": "https://genius.com/11568859", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Platinum", "line": "Double R’s on the gates, like I’m triple platinum", "vault_lines": ["Double R's on the gates like I'm triple platinum"], "meaning": "Double R’s on the gates:\nStands for Rick Ross and also symbolizes the main gate of his fortress found in his music videos\n\n\n\nLike I’m triple platinum:\nRolls Royce is made from real platinum", "annotation_id": "11540653", "url": "https://genius.com/11540653", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triple Platinum", "status": "done", "targets": 9, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-triple-platinum-lyrics", "genius_title": "Triple Platinum", "genius_id": 3019835, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triumphant (Get ’Em)", "line": "You won’t carry me, no Mariah", "vault_lines": ["You won’t carry me, no Mariah"], "meaning": "A play on Mariah’s last name, Carey. Meek is not trying to look for an easy way out.\n\nAlso, a singing term, when a voice carries, like Mariah’s does, it means it can be heard well over a certain distance.\n\nInspectah Deck of the Wu Tang Clan had a similar line in “The Mystery of Chessboxin”\n\nWell I’m a sire, I set the microphone on fire\nRap styles vary, and carry like Mariah", "annotation_id": "958969", "url": "https://genius.com/958969", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Triumphant (Get ’Em)", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Mariah-carey-triumphant-get-em-lyrics", "genius_title": "Triumphant (Get ’Em)", "genius_id": 83447, "source": "api"}
