{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Umma Do Me (Remix)", "line": "Dey say I start a lot of shit I say I finish things Jus like sum dental cream I go in niggas mouth And like da limousine I stretch a nigga out Dis ain’t watchu want now u betta take a different route", "vault_lines": ["Dey say I start a lot of shit I say I finish things", "Jus like sum dental cream I go in niggas mouth", "And like da limousine I stretch a nigga out", "Dis ain't watchu want now u betta take a different route"], "meaning": "He has a reputation for starting trouble but in his perspective he’s the one who ends it. To go in someone’s mouth means to take food out their mouth as in stop them from making money and being able to provide. Stretching someone out means killing them and leaving them layed out flat like a stretch limo.\n\n\n\nSo stay clear of T.I. if you don’t wanna end up dead.", "annotation_id": "39091685", "url": "https://genius.com/39091685", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Umma Do Me (Remix)", "line": "And let’s get dis clear (get it straight) Jus ‘tween you and me, dat apology was fa B.E.T not for D.T.P, I’m doin’ me", "vault_lines": ["And let's get dis clear (get it straight)", "Jus 'tween you and me, dat apology was fa B.E.T not for D.T.P, I'm doin' me"], "meaning": "On Sunday June 24 2007, T.I. was involved in a brawl with Disturbing Tha Peace cofounder/CEO and Ludacris’s manager, Chaka Zulu at the Sunset Tower Hotel in West Hollywood, California, Los Angeles at a luncheon called “Make It Happen.”. ATL based blogger Sandra Rose says that Chaka approached T.I. and asked him, “Do you have a problem with me? You got something you want to talk about” and T.I. allegedly responded by punching Chaka in the mouth. After which a altercation between both of their entourages took place which spilled out of the hotel and onto Sunset Boulevard. According to several sources, T.I. was loud and boastful during the event proclaiming he was the “King of Atlanta.” Chaka Zulu’s publicist called the incident “an unprovoked attack from the rapper” and said Chaka Zulu “was forced to defend himself.” Chaka Zulu stated:\n\nIt’s a shame that things like this continue to happen especially with the backdrop of an event that was about benevolence and charitable works. I’m thankful that all issues have been resolved.\n\nAfter winning the BET award for Best Hip-Hop Artist, T.I. apologized to all parties involved for his actions. Ludacris was shown in the audience clapping and smiling.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=au5KcOdHh6E&t=97s\n\nLudacris seemed to have taken a shot at T.I. for this apology in his I Get Money Freestyle:\n\nAnd ain’t no need for apologies (Why not?)\nSorry is as sorry does\n\nSo T.I. is clarifying that he wasn’t apologizing to Ludacris and his label Disturbing Tha Peace but that it was meant for BET only. Even though during his speech he said he apologizes to all parties involved.", "annotation_id": "35310570", "url": "https://genius.com/35310570", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Umma Do Me (Remix)", "status": "done", "targets": 2, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rocko-umma-do-me-remix-lyrics", "genius_title": "Umma Do Me (Remix)", "genius_id": 111937, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Us", "line": "My new home look like it’s Al Capone", "vault_lines": ["My new home look like it's Al Capone"], "meaning": "Reference to the Chicago drill scene as Al Capone was a legendary Chicago gangster", "annotation_id": "1128177", "url": "https://genius.com/1128177", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Us", "line": "Them other boys well known for letting niggas starve", "vault_lines": ["Them other boys well known for letting niggas starve"], "meaning": "A reference to the entire G-Unit era as a whole.  During that time, G-Unit was the highest selling act (50, Yayo, Buck, Game, & Lloyd Banks all went platinum, not counting the G-Unit album) and would put out albums during other artists' release dates which would force them to push back their albums (thus losing money) in order to avoid poor record sales and competition with G-Unit.  Also, 50 supposedly pushed back Styles P’s album single-handedly by speaking to Koch.  As you can see, these guys were pretty selfish, unlike rap now where many artists work together and feature each other on albums and mixtapes.\n\nThis is also a shot at Jeezy. Jeezy’s CTE label was home to Freddie Gibbs, who had never been able to release an album during his tenure under Jeezy. Jeezy’s been criticized for the way he mismanaged his artists, especially Gangsta Gibbs. By the end of 2012, Gibbs left CTE.", "annotation_id": "1127294", "url": "https://genius.com/1127294", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Us", "line": "I love this beat, aw man, I’m finna go bad", "vault_lines": ["I love this beat, aw man, I'm finna go bad"], "meaning": "Rick Ross seems to love this beat, which is why he decided to remix this on his mixtape. This is the only song off the tape in which Ross writes more than one original verse, so he really did like this beat.\n\nThis could be a reference to the first line of Ross’s first verse where he mentions Michael Jackson’s “Thriller”. Him going Bad meaning going in after “Thriller”.", "annotation_id": "1123096", "url": "https://genius.com/1123096", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Us", "line": "We eating good, not my fault, it’s Wingstop’s", "vault_lines": ["We eating good, not my fault, it's Wingstop's"], "meaning": "Although Ross is literally eating good because of the Wing Stop franchise he bought into, he is figuratively eating good too, referring to his major success in the rap game. This refers back to the previous line. While Rozay’s competitors starve, MMG has had plenty of success. They’ve released two compilation studio albums, and Meek & Wale each releasing a full studio solo album under the label.", "annotation_id": "1122637", "url": "https://genius.com/1122637", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Us", "line": "BandKamp", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "BandKamp is a production trio that “Us” producer Young Chop is a member of.\n\n\n\nThe name is a play on the famous Bandcamp web music software.", "annotation_id": "1122256", "url": "https://genius.com/1122256", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Us", "line": "Michael Jackson got them fiends just like Thrilla", "vault_lines": ["Michael Jackson got them fiends just like Thrilla"], "meaning": "Ricky uses Michael Jackson as a name for his coke, referring to Michael’s skin being nearly white or to the fact that many people considered him a “white girl” (cocaine’s nickname) when he was alive.\n\nThe fiends he serves are comparable to the zombies from Michael’s legendary “Thriller” video.\n\n\n\nThis simile was expanded on in the LEP Bogus Boys song “Zombie Land” with lines like:\n\nIf you shoot him in the arm, bet he coming back again\nThis is zombie land, this is zombie land", "annotation_id": "1122099", "url": "https://genius.com/1122099", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Us", "line": "Cash Money, I see no limits", "vault_lines": ["Higher than yours, Cash Money, I see no limits"], "meaning": "Double entendre:\n\n\nThere is no limit to amount of money Ross can acquire.\nReference to older hip hop labels Cash Money and Master P’s No Limits.", "annotation_id": "1121913", "url": "https://genius.com/1121913", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Us", "line": "Port of Miami, then I drop Trilla", "vault_lines": ["Port of Miami, then I drop Trilla"], "meaning": "His debut album was Port of Miami, followed up by his sophomore LP Trilla.", "annotation_id": "1121910", "url": "https://genius.com/1121910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Us", "line": "Beef with me, fuck boy, ’bout to go out back", "vault_lines": ["Beef with me, fuck boy, 'bout to go out back"], "meaning": "Double entendre:\n\n\nReference to his scuffle with Jeezy at the BET Hip Hop Awards, where they shoved, and took the altercation out back.\nAlso, could be a reference to the Outback Steakhouse sells steaks (beef).", "annotation_id": "1121907", "url": "https://genius.com/1121907", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Us", "line": "Ex-dope dealer worth eight figures", "vault_lines": ["Ex-dope dealer worth eight figures"], "meaning": "Although Ross is describing himself, this description seems more fitting for the person he took his name from. .\n\n\n\nAlthough no sources are given some sites suggest Ross has a net worth of $25M, and Forbes states that Ross banked $6M in 2011 and $9M in 2012 making Ross' claim seem believable.  Ross claims on other tracks to actually have $50M banked. .", "annotation_id": "1121905", "url": "https://genius.com/1121905", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Us", "line": "Did this shit right here for Baka, Preme, Styles, and Gilla", "vault_lines": ["Did this shit right here for Baka, Preme, Styles, and Gilla"], "meaning": "A reference to OVO/REPSUP members P reign, Gilla the Don, Go Styles, Chubbs, all streets niggas from Toronto. His Toronto goons. .\n\nhttp://instagram.com/p/QkYqWawjxx/\nhttps://twitter.com/OVOREPSUPCHUBBS/status/255710096284332032", "annotation_id": "1120955", "url": "https://genius.com/1120955", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
