{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "But back then dough was like a whore that Goldie love, it didn’t exist", "vault_lines": ["But back then dough was like a whore that Goldie love, it didn't exist"], "meaning": "Nas compares the lack of money he used to have to the complete lack of love “Goldie” the Pimp, from the 1973 blaxploitation film The Mac has for his women employees.", "annotation_id": "36648843", "url": "https://genius.com/36648843", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "Runnin’ so fast, on the gas, never neutral", "vault_lines": ["Runnin' so fast, on the gas, never neutral"], "meaning": "This is figurative. Ross is alluding to how fast he is growing. Typically of a car’s transmission gear; Park, Reverse, Neutral and Drive. The neutral is not intended for use when driving, so he is never on the neutral gear. Always on Drive.", "annotation_id": "29854331", "url": "https://genius.com/29854331", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "We the usual suspects The real definition of success", "vault_lines": ["We the usual suspects", "The real definition of success"], "meaning": "The Usual Suspects was a popular 1995 neo-noir/mystery crime drama starring Stephen Baldwin.\n\nRacism is still a big problem in the US. It’s an important subject in rap music too\n(many rappers discuss this in their music).\n\nThis line calls to mind historical events where racism was a huge factor, like the Trayvon Martin shooting, which evoked uproar in the African-American community, and plenty of reactions from rappers..\n\nHere’s an example of what the black community in America considers racism:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-fY20sSeikw", "annotation_id": "3029653", "url": "https://genius.com/3029653", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "Gotta keep a shooter while I’m ridin’ in the 7", "vault_lines": ["Gotta keep a shooter while I'm ridin' in the 7"], "meaning": "Ross needs protection whenever he’s outside, because he knows people are looking for him. Unfortunately, he did get shot in a drive-by shooting and crashed into a building, on the 28th of January, 2013.\n\nProtection comes in handy, but his car is still bulletproof. A 7 refers to a 70’s Chevy, A seven-seater SUV or a Mazda RX7.", "annotation_id": "3001201", "url": "https://genius.com/3001201", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "Lot of homies passed, see ’em in the future", "vault_lines": ["Lot of homies passed, see 'em in the future"], "meaning": "The ‘crack game’ is quite dangerous, and murder is common. As Ross was a drug dealer, he definitely lost some friends. Rick Ross is a Christian, therefore he believes in an afterlife where he will see his loved ones in the future.\n\nNote the wordplay between passed (past) and future.", "annotation_id": "3000973", "url": "https://genius.com/3000973", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "[Produced by The Inkredibles]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "The productions ranges from strings, bass, guitar, piano, horns, drums, percussions, and more. All instruments were mixed right so that they all have space and don’t interfere in other frequencies. To build up the hook they added extra cymbals, layers are added on the samples to give it that finished touch. It contains a sample of “A Garden of Peace” by Lonnie Liston Smith.\n\nOn the track Rozay use Jay Z´s habit of big-money talk over a beat that uses the same sample as on Jay´s classics “Dead President”, and even brings out the rapper sampled for the hook of that song (Nas). The producer renovates Ski Beatz original beat and adds a modern Southern touch.", "annotation_id": "2904339", "url": "https://genius.com/2904339", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "Now reptiles was left out, I’m ’bout a watch, what is you thinkin’? Murk you, plus the muscle that you bringin’ is nothing to me", "vault_lines": ["Now reptiles was left out, I'm 'bout a watch, what is you thinkin'?", "Murk you, plus the muscle that you bringin' is nothing to me"], "meaning": "Wordplay ties to the previous bar “crocs” – Crocodile is of course a reptile, but in this line he is referring to people that are shady. Therefore he didn´t bring them with him only his real friends. In fact now that he´s rich he can kill you and your bodyguards quick.", "annotation_id": "2904322", "url": "https://genius.com/2904322", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "Imagine this The Nazareth had to get from rags to rich", "vault_lines": ["The Nazareth had to get from rags to rich"], "meaning": "Nazareth is a word play on one his alias God Son – Jesus who is called Jesus Of Nazareth since he was born in that city. Nas used to schemin how to go from poor to rich – rag to riches.", "annotation_id": "2904274", "url": "https://genius.com/2904274", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "Comin’ from a boss, I can predict a double cross Handlers managin’ money they never come across", "vault_lines": ["Handlers managin' money they never come across"], "meaning": "Being dead broke is the root of all evil\n\nOne of the biggest reasons that rich people – especially athletes – go broke is because people they entrust to properly manage their funds ends up betraying them. Ross, as the boss he is, can spot these things from miles away.", "annotation_id": "1197007", "url": "https://genius.com/1197007", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "Penthouse suite, pretty beach, I call her Summer", "vault_lines": ["Penthouse suite, pretty beach, I call her Summer"], "meaning": "Word play from Rick Ross “Beach” meaning “Bi**h” and an actual beach. Also notice “summer” being a girls name or the time when you actually go to the beach. Check out the Rap Map to see Ross' “penthouse suite, pretty beach.”", "annotation_id": "722898", "url": "https://genius.com/722898", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "Keyser Söze in the flesh, it’s time to catch up", "vault_lines": ["Keyser Söze in the flesh, it's time to catch up"], "meaning": "Keyser Söze is a criminal mastermind in the 1995 film The Usual Suspects, just like Rick Ross.", "annotation_id": "668921", "url": "https://genius.com/668921", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "I’m club hoppin’ in Cali, shout out to Harry-O", "vault_lines": ["I'm club hoppin' in Cali, shout out to Harry-O"], "meaning": "Ross shouts out Michael “Harry-O” Harris – a businessman incarcerated on drug and attempted murder charges – who helped finance the legendary record label Death Row Records and ran the streets of LA before being imprisoned.\n\nHe has a very interesting history, watch this portion of the 2001 documentary Welcome to Death Row to learn more about him.", "annotation_id": "512898", "url": "https://genius.com/512898", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "Maybach Music", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Ricky Rozay’s record label featuring artists  Wale, Triple C’s, and Meek Mill; to name a few. A nametag Rick Ross always has in his songs, even his features.", "annotation_id": "443542", "url": "https://genius.com/443542", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "Black Philip Drummond, limousines or the Hummer", "vault_lines": ["Black Philip Drummond, limousines or the Hummer"], "meaning": "Ross talking about the 80’s tv show ‘Different Stokes’ starring Gary Coleman. Gary’s character Arnold was adopted by rich white guy Philip Drummond, who always rode in a limousine.\n\n\n\n“Limousines are the Hummer” refers to these bad boys:\n\n\n\nButterfly doors are optional.", "annotation_id": "443515", "url": "https://genius.com/443515", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "If you thuggin’ or fake and shanked on Cuban, shout out my Ricans Dealt with all of you gangstas from the roughest Jamiacans and Haitians", "vault_lines": ["If you thuggin' or fake and shanked on Cuban, shout out my Ricans", "Dealt with all of you gangstas from the roughest Jamiacans and Haitians"], "meaning": "New York being a hotbed of cultural diversity has immigrants from Cuba, Puerto Rico, Jamaicans, and Haitians, and of course each of these has their own gang or a mafia, Nas is acknowledging his Puerto Rican friends and tells that he’s been through it all, dealing with Jamaicans and Haitians gangstas who are known for being tough. Tieing to the previous bar that th dud in question is nothing to Nas he has handled guys that much tougher then him.", "annotation_id": "365476", "url": "https://genius.com/365476", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "If you follow physics, nigga, money never float Meanin’ every day I’m livin’ tryna stay afloat", "vault_lines": ["If you follow physics, nigga, money never float", "Meanin' every day I'm livin' tryna stay afloat"], "meaning": "Due to the laws of physics, as money is made from metal (coins) it has a higher density than water, it will not float when placed on water and will thus sink.\n\nRick Ross is drawing a parallel between that theory and how people will hit rock bottom in this cruel world without money to their name, so he’s living every single day with this in mind and therefore hustling extra hard to stay afloat.", "annotation_id": "355626", "url": "https://genius.com/355626", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Usual Suspects", "line": "Feed the fam, boy, I put that on these canned goods", "vault_lines": ["Feed the fam, boy, I put that on these canned goods"], "meaning": "Canned Goods sell particularly well in times of recession due to the tendency of financially-stressed individuals.\nRoss is responsible for his poor family and feeds them with the money he earns with hustling.", "annotation_id": "77682", "url": "https://genius.com/77682", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
