{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "I can have you and your team finalized", "vault_lines": ["I can have you and your team finalized"], "meaning": "Ross is simply stating that he can have you and your team killed at will if he wants to. This is supported by the gunshot sound effects in the background as these haunting words are muttered so matter-of-factly.\n\nThe way the instrumental is lowered as he says ‘finalised’ is dramatic and really adds to the effect and seriousness of this statement.", "annotation_id": "23031439", "url": "https://genius.com/23031439", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "All your kids screamin’ at Mount Sinai", "vault_lines": ["All your kids screamin' at Mount Sinai"], "meaning": "In the bible, Mount Sinai is where Moses received the Ten Commandments.\n\nHowever, Rick Ross might also be referring to Mount Sinai Hospital in NYC or Miami.", "annotation_id": "16174498", "url": "https://genius.com/16174498", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "Holy Ghost, the divine spirit My heart pure, he a real nigga", "vault_lines": ["Holy Ghost, the divine spirit", "My heart pure, he a real nigga"], "meaning": "DJ Khaled uses the phrase,\n\nRoss caught the Holy Spirit\n\nTo describe when he goes in extra hard on a song because Ross' rhymes are so inspired that they seem to come from a holy place separate from this sinning world. See his song “Holy Ghost” for more on Ross catching the Holy Spirit.", "annotation_id": "3116078", "url": "https://genius.com/3116078", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "Throwin’ curveballs in the field get you hit like Derek Jeter", "vault_lines": ["Throwin' curveballs in the field get you hit like Derek Jeter"], "meaning": "If you send shots at Meek in the streets he will get one of his shooters to shoot you down. He makes a play on the word “hit” comparing the way his shooters will hit you to Derek Jeter’s skills hitting baseballs, in this bar specifically, a curve ball. Deter Jeter is known as one of the best batters in all of baseball and has 3,316 career hits through the 2013 season.", "annotation_id": "2879235", "url": "https://genius.com/2879235", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "My loyalty respected all across the atlas", "vault_lines": ["My loyalty respected all across the atlas"], "meaning": "Ross has gained respect as one of the most popular rappers of today’s mainstream.\n\n\n\nHe’s the leader of MMG, and an icon in the sneakerhead world.", "annotation_id": "2877582", "url": "https://genius.com/2877582", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "I’m into fashion, nigga, John the Baptist", "vault_lines": ["I'm into fashion, nigga, John the Baptist"], "meaning": "John wore clothing made of camel’s hair and had a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.\n                            - Matthew 3:4:", "annotation_id": "2876537", "url": "https://genius.com/2876537", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "Selassie Selassie", "vault_lines": ["Illuminated, resurrected as Selassie"], "meaning": "Haile Selassie I was crowned the King the Ethiopia in 1930 and many people, specifically in the Rastafari movement in the Caribbean and the United States, believed he was the divine reincarnation of Jesus Christ. Ross is praying for the same divinity people believe(d) Haile Selassie had.", "annotation_id": "2876491", "url": "https://genius.com/2876491", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "Ain’t no sympathy in the inner city streets That’s all I ever heard from my older G’s", "vault_lines": ["Ain't no sympathy in the inner city streets", "That's all I ever heard from my older G's"], "meaning": "No mercy for anyone; that’s what the older niggas told him when he was younger and coming up.", "annotation_id": "2871950", "url": "https://genius.com/2871950", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "Slip a black snake in a nigga’s sheets", "vault_lines": ["Slip a black snake in a nigga's sheets"], "meaning": "Referring to the previous line, Ross believes this girl is actually a snake in his sheets here to use him for his money, or even possibly set him up for another assassination attempt. Hence, someone ‘slipped’ her in his sheets.", "annotation_id": "2869780", "url": "https://genius.com/2869780", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "Nigga with that O, I move that shit like tic-tac-toe", "vault_lines": ["Nigga with that O, I move that shit like tic-tac-toe"], "meaning": "An “O” is short for an ounce of drugs, most likely cocaine.  It’s easy for Meek to make the right moves when selling his Os as if making his moves was just a game of tic-tac-toe. Also, when playing tic-tac-toe, the most sound strategy is to put your Os on the corner. Get it? The ounces Meek buys go to the corner block for the homies to slang.", "annotation_id": "2855385", "url": "https://genius.com/2855385", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "Jesus Christ, look at this nigga’s ice Better yet, look at this nigga’s life I’m walkin’ on air", "vault_lines": ["Jesus Christ, look at this nigga's ice", "Better yet, look at this nigga's life"], "meaning": "Double entendre:\n\n– Firstly, Ross feels so blessed that he thanks Jesus for blessing him with all the opportunities in his life. He is thankful not only for his diamond chains (ice), but his entire life. Not only for giving him life in the first place, but blessing him and allowing him to improve his lifestyle and go from selling crack in the projects to buying expensive jewelry and living in mansions.\n\n\n\n– Ross literally has Jesus on his chains in the form of his Jesus pieces.", "annotation_id": "2855365", "url": "https://genius.com/2855365", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "All I ever wanted was to make scrilla Have a recordin’ session with J Dilla", "vault_lines": ["All I ever wanted was to make scrilla", "Have a recordin' session with J Dilla"], "meaning": "Apart from the obvious (hustling and ending up becoming a famous rapper), there’s some heavy juxtaposition in this line, maybe intentionally. J Dilla is known for having a discography that is anything but your average braggadocio about “making scrilla.”\n\nRoss could be saying here that he originally wanted to be a more intellectual-type artist making tracks with J Dilla, instead of rapping about money and gangbanging.", "annotation_id": "2854064", "url": "https://genius.com/2854064", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "Now I’m 30,000 feet up with my feet up, rollin’ weed up", "vault_lines": ["Now I'm 30,000 feet up with my feet up, rollin' weed up"], "meaning": "He’s flying in his private jet, rolling up that loud!", "annotation_id": "2853198", "url": "https://genius.com/2853198", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "She let me fuck early so she trustworthy Her pussy sacred so she gettin’ all the purses", "vault_lines": ["She let me fuck early so she trustworthy", "Her pussy sacred so she gettin' all the purses"], "meaning": "Since this woman let Ross get into her panties early on and didn’t make him wait, he’s going to put his trust in her and start paying for her expensive handbags.\n\nRoss has always had a thing for bossy women, however he’s established before that he never trusts them enough to let them help distribute his drugs or “touch the work.”\n\nNeeded some cash so she asked me could she move shit\nNot a chance, whips got her talking foolish\n\nHe’d much rather just fuck and buy some expensive bags/cars/clothes for her.", "annotation_id": "2851084", "url": "https://genius.com/2851084", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "Half you niggas Judas, I’m the son of Moses", "vault_lines": ["Half you niggas Judas, I'm the son of Moses"], "meaning": "Judas Iscariot was one of Jesus' 12 apostles. He is infamous for betraying Jesus by exposing him to High Priest Caiaphas' soldiers. Due to this, Judas is often referenced in Hip Hop as an example of a “snitch” or somebody who doesn’t stay true to himself or his roots. While everybody else was trying to give information to the police and ratting out their homies, Ross remains loyal and true to himself; even considering himself the son of a prophet.", "annotation_id": "2851071", "url": "https://genius.com/2851071", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "line": "Illuminated, resurrected as Selassie Bob Marley through the trumpets on the day I die", "vault_lines": ["Illuminated, resurrected as Selassie", "Bob Marley through the trumpets on the day I die"], "meaning": "A direct reference to Rastafarianism and the religion’s messiah, Haile Selassie and a reference to one of it’s many famous believers, Bob Marley, known for infusing his beliefs in his music.\n\nNot only does this pay homage to the two, but it might also imply that Ross is a believer of Rastafarianism himself, because he wants to go out to the music of one of the religion’s biggest advocators.", "annotation_id": "2851038", "url": "https://genius.com/2851038", "state": "accepted", "votes": 13, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Walkin’ on Air", "status": "done", "targets": 16, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-walkin-on-air-lyrics", "genius_title": "Walkin’ on Air", "genius_id": 365984, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Sentence should now-now be pronounced I’ll ask that you stand for sentence, please Mr. ***, it is the sentence of the court That your custody be committed to the Department of Corrections For confinement of the *** state prisons Without possibility of parole for the remainder of your life You may be seated", "vault_lines": ["You may be seated"], "meaning": "The introduction of this song contains a skit with the name and state of the criminal blurred out. It is a very ominous start to the song, and seemingly suggests that the rappers are willing to commit serious crimes and go to prison for life if need be.\n\nYet, it appears that the origin of the clip is from the sentencing of Mark Jensen for the brutal, premeditated murder of his wife Julie Carol in Wisconsin in 2008.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/3ysCH73tjrA?si=lHkGfTHruzWQ87a_&t=177\n\nCuriously, this Jensen was granted an appeal of the verdict, and the trial was overturned. But he was again found guilty in 2023 for the murder.", "annotation_id": "29576418", "url": "https://genius.com/29576418", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Nigga roll", "vault_lines": ["Tomorrow ain't promised, nigga roll"], "meaning": "nigga roll", "annotation_id": "20931212", "url": "https://genius.com/20931212", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "I’m not gon’ kill you though, haha I’m not gon’ kill you though, haha Hey Black, kill this motherfucker", "vault_lines": ["I'm not gon' kill you though, haha", "Hey Black, kill this motherfucker"], "meaning": "Ross seems to fool around by continuing the Scarface scene until the end, and imagining himself as Tony Montana.", "annotation_id": "17894877", "url": "https://genius.com/17894877", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Killers on the front line when ya war ready, huh Chopper shoot a thousand rounds when ya war ready, huh Just another mama cryin’ when ya war ready, huh Just another homicide cause we war ready, huh Killers on the front line when ya war ready, huh Chopper shoot a thousand rounds when ya war ready, huh Just another mama cryin’ cause we war ready, huh Just another homicide when you’re war ready, war, war, war", "vault_lines": ["Killers on the front line when ya war ready, huh", "Chopper shoot a thousand rounds when ya war ready, huh"], "meaning": "Tracy T’s hook outlines a number of features of a battleground, but even moreso, this hook seems to describe gang violence.\n\n\nThe first line refers to the gangster at the front of an attack, ready to begin the killing spree (ie. the battle front line).\nThe second line refers to the types of weapons the thugs are using – chopper is hood slang for an AK47, a popular assault rifle.\nThe third line refers to the mothers seeing their dead children after the war is over. The intense volume of the homicide at practice is too much for any mother to cope with, especially when it is her son paying the ultimate price.\nThe final line refers to the fact that homicides are common crimes in hoods and direct results of gang violence.", "annotation_id": "2987202", "url": "https://genius.com/2987202", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "[Verse 2: Jeezy]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A surprising feature given that Ross and Jeezy has a history of confrontation.\n\nT.I allegedly squashed the beef between the two, and said in a conversation with MTV;\n\n“I ain’t gonna say I brokered peace and got them in the studio, I simply said to one of my closest partners in the game — Young Jeezy — I simply said to him, ‘We done did as much as we can do with this. It’s gone as far as we can take it, ain’t nothing else to do’,” the Trouble Man said.\n\nTip also mentioned that Young Jeezy “ain’t the type of mother—er you can just tell what to do, but if you can present something that makes more sense then what he had, go on, he will give you the opportunity to make it make sense to him. And I think the same for Ross. They exhausted all of their other possible opportunities”\n\nAlso notably to go with the war theme of the track, gun shots can be heard throughout Jeezy’s verse.", "annotation_id": "2875569", "url": "https://genius.com/2875569", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "I ain’t out here to motherfuckin’ play", "vault_lines": ["I ain't out here to motherfuckin' play"], "meaning": "Could mean two things;\n\n\nJeezy is living the rap-lifestyle, and is one of the most succesfull rappers to come out of the South (Atlanta, GA). He is not in this rap game (Industry) to play. He is here to make money and be competitive, not to play games (fool aorund)\nJeezy has lived the street-hustler lifestyle aswell. When you are a drug dealer and hustler, you are not in it to play. You are there to feed your family and make some money.", "annotation_id": "2875368", "url": "https://genius.com/2875368", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "[Produced by Mike WiLL Made It]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "During an interview with XXL Magazine Mike explained how the track came about:\n\n\nDJ Khaled, man, he reached out to me that Ross was working on his new project. He knew me and Ross had previous hits before with “Tupac Back,” “King Of Diamonds” and “Marble Floors.” He actually did all three of those records at the same time. Khaled reached out to me and told me he had a list of who Ross wanted to work with. I [got] to work with Rick Ross, he’s a legend and his albums are always crazy. We make great music together.\n\nI came to Miami to fuck with Ross and Khaled and went to Ross’ house and went through some beats but he didn’t hear anything right away. He reached out to me and said that he needed something crazy, with the crazy 808s, the meanest beat you can possibly do. “I’m about to do a song with a game changer, someone who you might not even think I would do a song with.” I had a idea that it was Jeezy. So I went through some beats and sent it to him. He told me he heard one right away and was excited about the song. At the end it came out good. I was proud of the song. I started working on the 808s, then Jeezy sent his vocals in. We just kept mixing the record. Everybody had their opinions until down to the wire with the way they wanted to sound. Everybody just worked hard when it came to this record. We knew that it was going to be a moment in hip-hop. It’s always good to see rappers come back together when people don’t think that they work with each other anymore.", "annotation_id": "2859497", "url": "https://genius.com/2859497", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
