{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "War ready, the game just wanna take my life War ready, pussy boy, we all could die tonight War ready, fast cash above the law War ready, gas masks when them choppers talk", "vault_lines": ["War ready, the game just wanna take my life", "War ready, pussy boy, we all could die tonight", "War ready, fast cash above the law", "War ready, gas masks when them choppers talk"], "meaning": "https://players.brightcove.net/4863540648001/HklmClHkx_default/index.html?videoId=5236749151001", "annotation_id": "2852441", "url": "https://genius.com/2852441", "state": "verified", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "It’s hard to go to war with seventy million, nigga, huh?", "vault_lines": ["It's hard to go to war with seventy million, nigga, huh?"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is claiming that the entire MMG Empire is worth $70 million. You don’t go to war with just Ross – you go to war with the whole empire – all 70 million of it.\n\n70 million is a lot of money, and it can be hard to compete with the amount of resources Ross has at his disposal.", "annotation_id": "2819502", "url": "https://genius.com/2819502", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Yeah, I got that FNH with that motherfuckin’ Fendi holster", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I got that FNH with that motherfuckin' Fendi holster"], "meaning": "A gun reference, FNH is the abbreviations of a Belgian gun manufacturer called Fabrique Nationale d'Herstal.\n\n\n\nHe’s rich so why not use a holster from Italian luxury fashion house Fendi?", "annotation_id": "2778643", "url": "https://genius.com/2778643", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "I’ll never fall for what I stand for", "vault_lines": ["I'll never fall for what I stand for"], "meaning": "In reference to the quote, “A man who stands for nothing will fall for anything.” -Malcom X", "annotation_id": "2772723", "url": "https://genius.com/2772723", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Yeah, I wish Shake could see this", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I wish Shake could see this"], "meaning": "Jeezy references Shakir “Shake” Stewart, a Def Jam executive who was very instrumental in his and Ross’s careers.  Shakir committed suicide in 2008", "annotation_id": "2771808", "url": "https://genius.com/2771808", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "[Chorus: Tracy T]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Although he is uncredited, the hook is performed by MMG’s newest member, Tracy T.\n\nTracy uses the same choppy flow throughout the hook that he does in his first MMG single, “16”.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/MaybachMusicGrp/status/432177612249890817", "annotation_id": "2771611", "url": "https://genius.com/2771611", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "What’s a hassa? What’s a hassa? That’s you, haha", "vault_lines": ["What's a hassa? What's a hassa?"], "meaning": "A “hassa” is a word for somebody who always wants more than what they need and will use any method to get it. It’s derived from the Yiddish word “Chazer” meaning pig and is commonly used as an insult in the same vein as “asshole”.\n\nIn Picasso Baby, Jay-Z jokingly refers to himself as one but here, Rozay is dead serious about his enemies being hassas.\n\nRoss is also quoting 2pac who asked Nas the same question as he was quoting “Scarface”.", "annotation_id": "2770440", "url": "https://genius.com/2770440", "state": "accepted", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "I went from nothing, nigga, to sixty million, nigga Walking around in my motherfuckin’ Belaire robe, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I went from nothing, nigga, to sixty million, nigga", "Walking around in my motherfuckin' Belaire robe, nigga"], "meaning": "Rick Ross did indeed just buy Evander Holyfield’s old 235-acre property outside of Atlanta, GA. What Ross gave for it is unknown, but the listing started at $10 million. It’s not entirely unthinkable that with bidding and another $13 million in upgrades, the check ended up at around the $25 million mark.\n\nGoogle Maps provides us with an aerial (and yes, the lake on the left is part of the property):\n\n\n\nAnd here’s Ross himself standing inside it, courtesy of Meek Mill:\n\n\n\nAmong other things, the 109-room mansion includes its own movie theater, bowling alley, tennis and handball courts, a barn with room for seven horses, a dining room that can fit 100 and one of the largest pools in the U.S. at 350,000 gallons of water. Ever the giver, Ross himself says he plans to use the mansion as a facility to give underprivileged  youths an opportunity to experience activites which they normally wouldn’t have the chance to.", "annotation_id": "2770374", "url": "https://genius.com/2770374", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Heroin and quinine, cut that bitch a thousand times", "vault_lines": ["Heroin and quinine, cut that bitch a thousand times"], "meaning": "Just like coke is cut with with baking soda to make the dealer’s inventory last longer, quinine is used for the same purpose when it comes to heroin.\n\nRoss is also saying he is about that money because typically a dealer doesn’t want to cut H because he will get a reputation for weak dope, however, Ross just wants to stretch a profit so he will cut it not just a couple times but a thousand….", "annotation_id": "2770240", "url": "https://genius.com/2770240", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Shout out to the Vice Lords, shout out to my Blood nigga Shout out to them GDs, where that Crip love, nigga?", "vault_lines": ["Shout out to the Vice Lords, shout out to my Blood nigga", "Shout out to them GDs, where that Crip love, nigga?"], "meaning": "Ross should be very careful about shouting out the GD’s (Gangster Disciples), as members of that Chicago-based gang have already publicly threatened him and may have been behind  a January 2013 shooting incident", "annotation_id": "2770041", "url": "https://genius.com/2770041", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "21 gun salute out of the top of your drop-top coupe", "vault_lines": ["21 gun salute out of the top of your drop-top coupe"], "meaning": "The 21-gun salute is an act performed at military funerals where 21 shots are fired into the air as a tribute to the deceased soldier.\n\nContinuing on the theme of war and death, Jeezy is saluting the dead “real niggas” by firing shots out of his drop top coupe.", "annotation_id": "2770008", "url": "https://genius.com/2770008", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Yeah, I hope you pussy niggas war ready", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I hope you pussy niggas war ready"], "meaning": "Referencing the title of the track, “War Ready”:\n\n– Note how “war” can also be construed to sound like “are” as in “are ready”: He hopes the pussies that hate on him are ready for a war, because he is certainly ready to massacre them all.", "annotation_id": "2769777", "url": "https://genius.com/2769777", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "”Snow, you could start your own alphabet with all them Gs", "vault_lines": ["\"Snow, you could start your own alphabet with all them Gs"], "meaning": "Three possible meanings:\n\n\nHe hangs out with so many gangsters (or G’s) that there are enough around to create a whole alphabet.\nHe is making so much money (or G’s as in 10 Grand, etc.) he could create an alphabet with it.\nHe is pushing so much drugs (or G’s as in grams) that he could start an alphabet with all the grams of narcotics he possesses/sells.", "annotation_id": "2769766", "url": "https://genius.com/2769766", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Give me the K and a shovel, I’ll bury that nigga Be his pallbearer so I can carry that nigga", "vault_lines": ["Be his pallbearer so I can carry that nigga"], "meaning": "K is slang for…you guessed it AK-47. Pallbearer’s carry the someone’s casket to their grave.\n\nThere’s and allusion to both drugs and murder seeing as K may also be short for Kilo. Jeezy previously stated that he was cleaning out the evidence of being in the drug game. So he could be burying his drugs until later.", "annotation_id": "2769377", "url": "https://genius.com/2769377", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "War ready, a nigga put some change on your head Damn right, fuck around, clear my safe out I got a few digi’ scales and a couple Denzels Yeah, you motherfuckin’ right this a safe house", "vault_lines": ["War ready, a nigga put some change on your head", "Damn right, fuck around, clear my safe out", "I got a few digi' scales and a couple Denzels", "Yeah, you motherfuckin' right this a safe house"], "meaning": "Time’s a-wasting. Tick tock, tick tock, tick tock, tick tock\n\n\n\nDenzel Washington played Tobin Frost in Safe House.\n\n“Change on your head” – refers to paying to get someone killed. He will use all the money in his safe to do it too.\n\nDigi scales = digital scales and Denzels is slang for cocaine according to UD and the fact he did it in Flight.", "annotation_id": "2769344", "url": "https://genius.com/2769344", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Ridin’ clean, youngest nigga in the Medellín", "vault_lines": ["Ridin' clean, youngest nigga in the Medellín"], "meaning": "Rick Ross rides in elegant cars–nothing but the best for the MMG boss. He continues to recount how he became involved in the drug-selling game at a young age by referencing the infamous Medellín Cartel, which  was a network of drug suppliers and smugglers originating in Medellín, Colombia that operated in Colombia, Bolivia, Peru, Honduras, United States, Canada, and Europe during the 1970-80s.", "annotation_id": "2769199", "url": "https://genius.com/2769199", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Put a patch over your eye, fuck with my petty change", "vault_lines": ["Put a patch over your eye, fuck with my petty change"], "meaning": "Ross continues the chain braggadocio with a reference to the great Slick Rick, Dana Dane’s high school classmate and fellow Kangol Crew member, who was as famous for his incredible story-telling ability as he is for wearing an eyepatch and a ton of chains.\n\nSince Rick Ross is making that big money, broke rappers’s are barely able to make what he considers “petty change”.", "annotation_id": "2769168", "url": "https://genius.com/2769168", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Open up a hundred doors with all them kis”", "vault_lines": ["Open up a hundred doors with all them kis\""], "meaning": "Another double entendre:\n\n\nLiterally having a bunch of keys could open up hundreds of doors for you\nGetting into the drug business and selling kilogram’s (ki’s) opens up your life to numerous possibilities.", "annotation_id": "2769167", "url": "https://genius.com/2769167", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "We could live today Blood and die tonight cuz, war ready", "vault_lines": ["We could live today Blood and die tonight cuz, war ready"], "meaning": "Triple entendre and a great play on words done here!\n\n\n\nJeezy makes reference to both the Blood and Crip gangs who refer to their gang members with the word “blood” or “cuz/cuh”\nBecause of these streets he doesn’t know whether he will live today (blood in him) or die tonight (lack of blood)\nBoth gangs are ride or die.", "annotation_id": "2769158", "url": "https://genius.com/2769158", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Versace slippers, twenty chains, bitch I’m Dana Dane", "vault_lines": ["Versace slippers, twenty chains, bitch I'm Dana Dane"], "meaning": "First he wore ten jesus pieces, now Rick Ross doubles his chain game with twenty!\n\n\n\nRozay compares his lavish style to Dana Dane, a rapper who was known for his fashion sense.\n\nThe Bawse once again proclaims his affinity for Versace clothing, he’s been a longtime fan of the brand. Hee even owns a Versace dining set and beach towel!\n\nRick Ross’s collaborator on this track, Jeezy, also referenced Dana Dane in his 2013 hit single “R.I.P.”:\n\n Who the nigga think he is\nSlick Rick or Dana Dane?", "annotation_id": "2769104", "url": "https://genius.com/2769104", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Fuck what you heard, for that bird, I’m a dirty nigga", "vault_lines": ["Fuck what you heard, for that bird, I'm a dirty nigga"], "meaning": "Rick Ross is one of the cleanest rappers in the game, but his involvement with the bird, slang for kilo of cocaine, trade will always make him “dirty”.\n\nRozay also plays on Jeezy’s ATL roots here by referencing the iconic Dirty Bird dance made famous by former Falcons player Jamal Anderson.", "annotation_id": "2769096", "url": "https://genius.com/2769096", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "Box Chevy hit the block, run the whole fifty shots You just poppin’ ’til you know you can’t pop ’em no more", "vault_lines": ["Box Chevy hit the block, run the whole fifty shots", "You just poppin' 'til you know you can't pop 'em no more"], "meaning": "Doing drive by’s in a Box Chevy (Chevrolet Caprice ‘80) is a normal thing for Jeezy and his homies. They either squeeze their guns till they are out of bullets or until their guns get jammed on them.\n\n\n\n\nA common weapon used in a drive by is a MAC-10.\n\n\n\nAlso a reference to Rick Ross' first promo single for Mastermind that was titled “Box Chevy”.", "annotation_id": "2769088", "url": "https://genius.com/2769088", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "line": "A coward dies a thousand deaths, a real nigga dies but one", "vault_lines": ["A coward dies a thousand deaths, a real nigga dies but one"], "meaning": "Who knew Jeezy was such a literature buff? More than likely a play on Tupac’s line in “If I Die 2Nite:”\n\nA coward dies a thousand deaths\nA soldier dies but once\n\nIn Jeezy’s version of the line, he asserts that if you are afraid to walk out on those streets you have already lost. A real G like Jeezy knows that every life comes with a death sentence, so why fear it?", "annotation_id": "2769073", "url": "https://genius.com/2769073", "state": "accepted", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "War Ready", "status": "done", "targets": 31, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-war-ready-lyrics", "genius_title": "War Ready", "genius_id": 352087, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Warm Words in a Cold World", "line": "Niggas bearing arms for real, this shit like Soldier Field", "vault_lines": ["Niggas bearing arms for real, this shit like Soldier Field"], "meaning": "Wale drops a clever Double Entendre to start off his verse. He pays homage to one of Chicago’s iconic sports teams, The Bears.\n\nThe symbolism begins with the phrase, ‘bearing arms.’ This expression was made famous by the Second Amendment of the Constitution, which outlines the right to own guns. Wale then makes a simile, ‘this shit like Soldier Field.’ On the surface, a connection is drawn to the line’s literal meaning; that a field full of soldiers would contain many guns.\n\nIt takes a die-hard, deep-dish loving, Chicagoan to recognize the intended meaning of this line. As Soldier Field is the home stadium of Chicago’s infamous NFL team, known only as: DA BEARS.\n\n\n\nIt can be argued that this line contains a triple entendre; meaning that it hides a third meaning. Soldier Field’s location on the South-Side of Chicago implies that the ‘bear arms’ in question are not of the mammalian variety. Gun violence found in Chicago’s neighborhoods is no secret, as it makes the headlines of national news, along with the head-lines of rap songs.\n\nTo learn about one of the best triple entendres in rap music today, look at the genius article on Frank Ocean’s Biking. Biking is thought to be the only song with three triple entendres all by three separate artists. The tripartite meanings behind Frank’s, Tyler’s, and Jay-Z’s lines are summarized perfectly by this 50 second youtube video:\n\n\n\n3 TRIPLE Entendres in FRANK OCEAN’s Biking", "annotation_id": "24646522", "url": "https://genius.com/24646522", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Warm Words in a Cold World", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-warm-words-in-a-cold-world-lyrics", "genius_title": "Warm Words in a Cold World", "genius_id": 6926057, "source": "api"}
