{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Factory, that’s me", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Jay is most likely commenting on his incredible watch collection, and highlighting that he doesn’t put jewels (aka “bust down”) his watches. He gets high value pieces which are expensive because of the craftsmanship. Not the gems inserted…or he gets the factory set gems made by the manufacturers. Most likely Patek Phillippe – his favorite current brand. See Jay in the ultra-rare Patek 2499/101J.", "annotation_id": "39123808", "url": "https://genius.com/39123808", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Mona Lisa to me ain’t nothin’ but a bitch", "vault_lines": ["Mona Lisa to me ain't nothin' but a bitch"], "meaning": "tf did the mona lisa do to yo big ass rick?", "annotation_id": "32380360", "url": "https://genius.com/32380360", "state": "pending", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Tell him how we ceasin’ all these wars, stoppin’ violence", "vault_lines": ["Tell him how we ceasin' all these wars, stoppin' violence"], "meaning": "Despite rapping about explicit violence for most of his rap career, Meek Mill has on various occasions talked about issues that haunt his community especially violence perpetrated by residents of his home town of Philadelphia.\n\nDays after the passing of Alton Sterling and Philando Castile, who lost their lives at the hands of police on the 6th and 7th July 2016 respectively, Meek Mill took to Instagram pledging not to write about extreme violence in his music after the release of DC4:\n\n…AFTER DC4 I won’t continue to rap about extreme violence!!! But I will remain to let my people know in these terrible environments to adapt and survive at any cost because US BLACK PEOPLE ARE STILL AT WAR WITH OURSELVES AND THE SYSTEM IN REAL LIFE!", "annotation_id": "19932128", "url": "https://genius.com/19932128", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "When you bring my name up to the judge, just tell him facts Tell him how we fundin’ all these kids to go to college", "vault_lines": ["When you bring my name up to the judge, just tell him facts", "Tell him how we fundin' all these kids to go to college"], "meaning": "Even though his life might be overshadowed by controversies, one of the things Meek Mill is known for is his commitment to education and charity in his home City of Philadelphia. In November 2019 he took to twitter to express his will to improve Philadelphia schools:\n\n\n\nIn a motivational speech delivered at Overbrook High School  in Philadelphia in 2016, Meek Mill announced that he had enrolled into college himself, stating that:\n\nI took it upon myself to do that because I want to be the best at what I do. I want to be the best at what I do so I enrolled in college to educate myself, get myself more knowledge because knowledge make money, being educated makes you money and I like making money and taking care of my family.\n\nIn August 2018 he collaborated with the School District of Philadelphia to donate more than 6 000 Puma backpacks to school kids.\n\nMeek Mill also partnered with Michael Rubin to donate millions of dollars to benefit the Thurgood Marshall College Fund.", "annotation_id": "19912688", "url": "https://genius.com/19912688", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "You run a check up but they never give you leverage", "vault_lines": ["You run a check up but they never give you leverage"], "meaning": "Delving deeper into the value of ownership as mentioned in the two previous lines, Mr. Carter touches on how common it is for African-Americans (and artists) to find themselves in a position where they’ve “run a check (i.e. bill) up,” leaving them in debt to someone or something usually a corporation but anything from schools to banks to hospitals which may be privately owned; in many instances by people who are of European descent.\n\n\n\nBut rarely do African-Americans and other disenfranchised groups find themselves in positions of ownership or with assets that exceed their debts. Assets can come in the\n form of ownership of and/or equity in things like companies, real estate, etc. Many times this puts these individuals at a lifelong and sometimes generational disadvantage regarding achieving but more importantly maintaining longterm success.", "annotation_id": "16455820", "url": "https://genius.com/16455820", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "’Cause most of my niggas won’t ever work together", "vault_lines": ["'Cause most of my niggas won't ever work together"], "meaning": "In the previous line, Hov speaks on the priceless value of his 100% stake in Tidal in that it affords him the privilege of breaking bread i.e., fostering mutually beneficial relationships along with personal ones with fellow artists and other individuals that he loves and respects.\n\n\n\nHe contrasts those values/practices here by suggesting that the majority of black people in numerous industries will not work together and oftentimes, maybe especially so in the music industry you will find prominent black people, working against one another on a consistent basis.", "annotation_id": "16455487", "url": "https://genius.com/16455487", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Tryna fix the system and the way that they designed it", "vault_lines": ["Tryna fix the system and the way that they designed it"], "meaning": "Meek Mill and Jay-Z launched a criminal justice reform organization on January 24 2019 called REFORM.\n\n\n\nMeek has had a long-running feud with authorities which led him to dedicate himself to reforming criminal justice in America. Jay-Z of course is also a huge advocate for reform as he’s discussed the topic in many interviews and songs. It’s also important to mention that Micheal Rubin, Robert Kraft, and Van Jones will play major roles in organizing and pushing REFORM forward. The organization’s mission is to change laws, policies, and hearts and minds across the US. This will dramatically reduce the amount of people who are unjustly controlled by the current justice system.\n\nIf you want to know more about REFORM click here to go to their official website where you can join the alliance and connect with the organization.", "annotation_id": "16365986", "url": "https://genius.com/16365986", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Phantom 500 thousand, hundred round in a stizzy", "vault_lines": ["Phantom 500 thousand, hundred round in a stizzy"], "meaning": "Phantom is a model of Rolls-Royce. This luxury vehicle often costs just under $500,000 USD.\n\n\n\nStizzy on the other hand is a slang term for a big automatic gun such as an AK, (this particular one being able to hold several rounds of ammunition). Stick is a term often used in South Florida and the UK for gun and so Stizzy is just an adaptation to the word.", "annotation_id": "16308765", "url": "https://genius.com/16308765", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "You lay a hand on Hov, my shooter shoot for free", "vault_lines": ["You lay a hand on Hov, my shooter shoot for free"], "meaning": "Just a friendly reminder from Hov that he is still affiliated with killers who will touch you if you cross him.\n\nDuring his 2012 Barclays Center Opening Night, Jay performed a freestyle in which he rapped :\n\nShooters on my team… No really, I got shooters on my team\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=omNFHNSuz3A", "annotation_id": "15967457", "url": "https://genius.com/15967457", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "I’m from the South where they never make it this rich", "vault_lines": ["I'm from the South where they never make it this rich"], "meaning": "Rick Ross hails from Carol City, Florida, a suburb of Miami. The American South, where Ross grew up, is known for its high rates of poverty, making social mobility difficult for many, especially African-Americans who face much discrimination.\n\nRoss highlights this point to prove how his wealth and fame show how he’s bucked this trend to earn his lavish lifestyle. This line connects to an overall theme presented in this song about African-Americans reaching the pinnacle of success in hip hop despite the social odds stacked against them.", "annotation_id": "15963852", "url": "https://genius.com/15963852", "state": "accepted", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "What’s free? Free is when nobody else could tell us what to be Free is when the TV ain’t controllin’ what we see", "vault_lines": ["Free is when the TV ain't controllin' what we see"], "meaning": "Meek Mill interpolates the chorus from 1997’s “What’s Beef?” by The Notorious B.I.G.:\n\nWhat’s beef? Beef is when you need two gats to go to sleep\nBeef is when your moms ain’t safe up in the streets\n\nIt is well known that the United States of America has a complex relationship with freedom, dating back to slavery in the 1700s. The US is also referred to as “the land of the free,” according to “The Star Spangled Banner”:\n\nAnd say, doth our star-spangled banner yet wave\nO’er the land of the free, and the home of the brave\n\nMeek Mill touched on this subject in the 2017 song “Young Black America”:\n\nWe slaves in the ‘40s, still slaves in the present", "annotation_id": "15931896", "url": "https://genius.com/15931896", "state": "accepted", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Made a few mistakes but this ain’t where I wanna be", "vault_lines": ["Made a few mistakes but this ain't where I wanna be"], "meaning": "In November 2017, Meek Mill was sentenced to 2-4 years in prison for violating his probation. This followed a string of incidents dating back to 2008, which include possession of a firearm, travelling without permission from a judge in 2015, and popping a ‘wheelie’ in NYC in 2017.", "annotation_id": "15922080", "url": "https://genius.com/15922080", "state": "accepted", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Judge bangin’ that gavel, turned me to slave from a king", "vault_lines": ["Judge bangin' that gavel, turned me to slave from a king"], "meaning": "Amendment XIII Section 1 of the US Constitution states:\n\nNeither slavery nor involuntary servitude, except as a punishment for crime whereof the party shall have been duly convicted, shall exist within the United States, or any place subject to their jurisdiction.\n\nBy condemning Meek Mill to prison, the judge essentially opened the possibility of Meek being treated as a slave. This is no exaggeration either.\n\nPrisoners from California to Texas and beyond engage in either paid or unpaid labor for major goods and services such as firefighting, textiles, and customer service. As more and more prisoners continued to detest this system of “modern slavery”, they banded together all across 17 states from August 21 to September 9, 2018 for a national prison strike.", "annotation_id": "15915233", "url": "https://genius.com/15915233", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Before I’m judged by 12, put a 12 on my V", "vault_lines": ["Before I'm judged by 12, put a 12 on my V"], "meaning": "“12” refers to the 12 jurors in a criminal court case. “12” is also slang for law enforcement, originally referring to DEA narcotics officers.\n\nMeek Mill had a 10-year run-in with the justice system starting in 2008, revolving around his conviction for possession of a firearm and assaulting a police officer. Meek was released from prison on bail in April 2018. In a December 2018 Breakfast Club interview following his album release, Meek said that he was still on probation, meaning the courts could make a ruling on his case at any time, whether he was present or not. He had no idea when the court date would be so he felt his days as a free man could be numbered.\n\nMeanwhile, “12 on my V” refers to a 12-cylinder engine, commonly known as a V12 engine, found in many luxury sports cars. Meek wants to enjoy life for the time being, as his freedom could be stripped at a moment’s notice.", "annotation_id": "15915086", "url": "https://genius.com/15915086", "state": "accepted", "votes": 89, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "You know what free is, nigga?", "vault_lines": ["You know what free is, nigga?"], "meaning": "Meek Mill interpolates the intro of 1997’s “What’s Beef?” by The Notorious B.I.G.:\n\nWe here (do you know what beef is?)\nWe ain’t goin' nowhere (do you know what beef is?)\nUh-uh (ask yourself, do you know what beef is?)\n\nInterestingly, Biggie also references JAY-Z, AKA “Iceberg Slim,” who appears on this track:\n\nThe Commission:\nUncle Paulie, P. Diddy, Cease-A-Leo DeGenero (yeahhh)\nCharli Baltimore, Iceberg Slim", "annotation_id": "15911007", "url": "https://genius.com/15911007", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Enjoy your chains, what’s your employer name with the hairpiece?", "vault_lines": ["Enjoy your chains, what's your employer name with the hairpiece?"], "meaning": "Another line that references the issue of ownership & white label executives profiting off of Black music. Though this line doesn’t sound/look like much, it definitely describes how the industry is run, particularly how they lure young Black entertainers into signing to terrible deals with terrible (often old) execs…with money & jewelry.\n\nThis line, for some, is also reminiscent of GZA’s bar in “Protect Ya Neck”\nhttps://genius.com/9828", "annotation_id": "15909861", "url": "https://genius.com/15909861", "state": "accepted", "votes": 41, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "That’s a CC, E-copy", "vault_lines": ["That's a CC, E-copy"], "meaning": "CC means carbon copy when sending an Email. CCE also means Continuing Criminal enterprise which is an organisation that targets drug traffickers; so Hov saying copy could also mean ‘do you understand? I got away from the CCE’.", "annotation_id": "15909777", "url": "https://genius.com/15909777", "state": "accepted", "votes": 64, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "To this day, Grandma ’fraid what I might say They gon’ have to kill me, Grandmama, I’m not they slave", "vault_lines": ["To this day, Grandma 'fraid what I might say", "They gon' have to kill me, Grandmama, I'm not they slave"], "meaning": "Jay-Z has been outspoken on social justice issues with a focus on the rights of black Americans. He has addressed prison reforms through the “Kalief Browder story” and has championed the accumulation of wealth by minorities on songs such as “Story of O.J” as he does on this song as well.\n\nJay-Z makes mention of his maternal grandmother, Hattie White who has supposedly warned him of being outspoken on issues that martyrs before him have been murdered for.", "annotation_id": "15909044", "url": "https://genius.com/15909044", "state": "accepted", "votes": 54, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "My accountant’s so good, I’m practically livin’ tax free", "vault_lines": ["My accountant's so good, I'm practically livin' tax free"], "meaning": "United States Tax Law includes provisions and loopholes that allow the technically astute a financial advantage. JAY-Z employs the services of tax advisors to ensure he avoids unnecessary taxation and submits the minimum amount on his tax returns.\n\n\n\nFor example, in 2011 Jay-Z and Kanye West donated the Maybach used in their “Otis” music video to auction, the proceeds of which were donated to fight the East African drought disaster. This donation was tax-deductible.", "annotation_id": "15908623", "url": "https://genius.com/15908623", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Locked me in a cell for all them nights and I won’t snap, uh", "vault_lines": ["Locked me in a cell for all them nights and I won't snap, uh"], "meaning": "On Novemeber 6, 2017, Meek Mill was sentenced to 2-4 years in prison for violating his probation. Almost six months later, Mill was released after his defense team filed an appeal based on the mishandling of his arrest.\n\nAs of 2018, things looked brighter for the Philadelphian rapper. He dropped his fourth studio album Championships in late November and took on a role in a criminal justice reform organization tasked with liberating “1 million people unjustly caught in the criminal justice system.” This suggests that he wasn’t fazed by the jail time, but galvanized instead.", "annotation_id": "15908492", "url": "https://genius.com/15908492", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Or the billionaire from Marcy, and the way they got my back, uh", "vault_lines": ["Or the billionaire from Marcy, and the way they got my back, uh"], "meaning": "Meek Mill is referring to long-time friend and fellow rapper JAY-Z, who grew up in the Marcy projects in Brooklyn.\n\nFollowing Meek’s imprisonment in late 2017, JAY-Z advocated for his freedom—from saying “Free Meek Mill” at his concerts to penning an article for The New York Times about wrongdoings in the criminal justice system in which he described Meek’s sentence as “unjust and heavy-handed.”\n\n\n\nThis poster calling for Meek’s freedom was paid for by JAY-Z's​ Roc Nation.", "annotation_id": "15908488", "url": "https://genius.com/15908488", "state": "accepted", "votes": 67, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Poppin’ like Bad Boy in ’94, Big Poppa and Diddy", "vault_lines": ["Poppin' like Bad Boy in '94, Big Poppa and Diddy"], "meaning": "Bad Boy Records is a record label founded in 1993 by Sean ‘'Diddy’‘ Combs. In 1994, the label was set for it’s first release Flava In Ya Ear by Craig Mack. The track proved to be a smash-hit which sparkled the release of it’s even more successful remix featuring The Notorious B.I.G. – aka Big Poppa – who would also release his 4 x platinum classic debut album Ready To Die in '94 under the label.", "annotation_id": "15908223", "url": "https://genius.com/15908223", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Sold drugs, got away scot-free", "vault_lines": ["Sold drugs, got away scot-free"], "meaning": "Although Jay spent a considerable amount of time coke dealing, he still walked out of the drug game with a clean sheet. However, members of his drug-running crew were taken down when he was recording in London with his mentor Jaz-O in 1989. If Jay had been at home in New York he would have ended up in prison as well.\n\nIn an interview with Charlie Rose, Jay spoke on this topic:\n\nIt took a bit of luck; a personal friend of mine went to jail for twelve years, and me and him would be together every single day—so I know that same fate was awaiting me.", "annotation_id": "15908179", "url": "https://genius.com/15908179", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Sucker free, no shuckin’ me, I don’t jive turkey", "vault_lines": ["Sucker free, no shuckin' me, I don't jive turkey"], "meaning": "Here Jay-Z is referring to two sayings “shuck and jive” and “jive turkey.” The phrase shuck and jive was used throughout the 1920s (maybe earlier) to refer to someone who makes statements only meant to deceive when asked about their work/work ethic. For example, when someones manager/boss asks “what are you doing?” someone could shuck and jive around the answer in order to appear busy. It is for this reason that he also references not being a jive turkey because this is a term used throughout the 70s to describe people who made empty promises or were full of talk. It was referenced in a song by the Ohio Players and also on the shows “The Jeffersons” and “Good Times” which helped to popularize it.\n\n\n\nBy making these references Jay-Z is stating that he doesn’t have anyone around him who operates like a sucka and more importantly it isn’t possible to fool him specifically because he doesn’t engage in “jive turkey behavior.” His word is his bond.", "annotation_id": "15908126", "url": "https://genius.com/15908126", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Send a order through a hands free Kill you in 24 hours or shorter, you can’t ignore the hand speed", "vault_lines": ["Send a order through a hands free", "Kill you in 24 hours or shorter, you can't ignore the hand speed"], "meaning": "Jay is showing off how practical and well connected he is:\n\nWe just call the shots by simply moving our thumbs\n\nThis bar is also reminiscent of one of his bars on his track “The Prelude”:\n\nJust sent a million dollars through a hands free\nThat’s big money talk, can you answer me?\n\nJay uses a double entendre – whoever he’s threatening to kill can’t ignore the speed of a clock hand as it ticks away the 24 hours\n\nHowever, the context here is little bit darker. This is probably a threat to George Zimmerman who in 2012 fatally shot an unarmed, 17-year old black male named Trayvon Martin in a tragic incident that imploded the Black Lives Matter movement.\n\nIn 2017, amidst the preparation for a documentary series, produced by JAY-Z, about the incident. Zimmerman would come forward through multiple media channels threatening to feed JAY-Z to an alligator if he ever bother him or his family.\n\nJAY-Z has already aired his bloodlust for Zimmerman’s life on DJ Khaled’s track “Top Off”:\n\nMeanwhile Georgie Porgie sinnin’ and sendin’ me threats\nSave your breath, you couldn’t beat a flight of steps\nTry that shit with a grown man,\nI’ll kill that fuckboy with my own hand”\n\nand on Drake’s “Talk Up”:\n\nY'all killed X and let Zimmerman live, shhh, s-streets is done", "annotation_id": "15908053", "url": "https://genius.com/15908053", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Oh, say you can see, I don’t feel like I’m free", "vault_lines": ["Oh, say you can see, I don't feel like I'm free"], "meaning": "In these two lines, Meek Mill is referring to “The Star-Spangled Banner” which is America’s national anthem. The anthem’s first line is “oh say can you see,” however, Meek immediately states “I don’t feel like I’m free” which is a reference to living in a “free country” but constantly feeling like one’s language, dress, and overall life are consistently monitored. This line could also reference Colin Kaepernick and his NFL protest where he refused to stand for the national anthem until police brutality was addressed in 2016.", "annotation_id": "15907966", "url": "https://genius.com/15907966", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Roc Nation, half of that, that’s my piece", "vault_lines": ["Roc Nation, half of that, that's my piece"], "meaning": "Formed in 2008 after a landmark $150 million deal with touring giant Live Nation, Roc Nation is Hov’s full-service entertainment company—of which he owns 50%.\n\n\n\nAfter early beginnings as a record label, the company now houses a talent agency; a touring and concert production company; a TV/film production division; a music publishing division; a sports management division; a startup investment division; and a clothing label.", "annotation_id": "15907886", "url": "https://genius.com/15907886", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Ha-ha-ha-ha-ha, check out the bizarre (Ah) Rappin’ style used by me, the H-O-V", "vault_lines": ["Ha-ha-ha-ha-ha, check out the bizarre (Ah)", "Rappin' style used by me, the H-O-V"], "meaning": "Since this track samples Biggie’s “What’s Beef”, it is only appropriate that Jay pays homage to his late friend by borrowing this bar.\n\nHa ha ha ha ha, check out this bizarre\nRapper style used by me, the B.I.G\n\nAnd while B.I.G. popularized this lyric for 90s hip-hop fans, it actually originated with Biz Markie on his 1988 track “Biz Is Goin’ Off”.\n\nHa ha ha ha ha, check out this bizarre\nRappin' style used by me, the B.I.Z", "annotation_id": "15907843", "url": "https://genius.com/15907843", "state": "accepted", "votes": 90, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Look at my hair free, carefree, niggas ain’t near free", "vault_lines": ["Look at my hair free, carefree, niggas ain't near free"], "meaning": "It has been defended for a long time that black men and women should use their natural hair. Leaders like Malcolm X, who used to comb his hair when younger, believed it was because the negro men and women in America had been brainwashed into believing that black people are ‘inferior’ – and white people ‘superior'. This goes along with the theme of Jay’s verse,  against submission to the so-called superior race. It has become common to see him rock his natural hair for the past few years.\n\nAlso, “Care Free” and “Nair” are famous hair products.\n\nhttps://twitter.com/Young_Guru/status/1068955962764128260", "annotation_id": "15907764", "url": "https://genius.com/15907764", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Inflating numbers like we ’posed to be happy about this We was praisin’ Billboard, but we were young Now I look at Billboard like, “Is you dumb?”", "vault_lines": ["Inflating numbers like we 'posed to be happy about this", "We was praisin' Billboard, but we were young", "Now I look at Billboard like, \"Is you dumb?\""], "meaning": "In May 2018, the Jay-Z owned Tidal streaming service was accused of falsifying streams and defrauding their advertisers. The story originated from Norwegian newspaper Dagens Næringsliv that claimed to have received a hard drive that contained billions of lines of the company’s streaming data. Specifically, it claimed that Tidal posted falsely inflated streaming numbers – over 300 million streams worth – for Kanye West’s The Life of Pablo and Beyoncé’s Lemonade.\n\n\n\nJay mentions Tidal’s numbers scandal to then point out the “Billboard hits” culture of Hip-Hop today. Industry artists often boast about their album sales and Billboard hits, including a young Jay. When he gave away a million early copies of his twelfth album Magna Carta… Holy Grail as part of a deal with Samsung, Billboard did not count them as album sales for their Top 200 albums chart. Even without these sales, Jay debuted the album at number one for the 13th time in a row. Yet, as he has grown older and experienced the uncertainty that surrounds streaming numbers, he no longer sees the Billboard charts as a true sign of success.\n\nHe also may be taking a shot at 6ix9ine, a rapper known for constantly mentioning Billboard charts as the self-proclaimed king of New York.\nhttps://twitter.com/6ix9ine/status/979799519754731520\nJay seems to flip 6ix9ine’s question from “STOOPID” right back at him. This track samples “What’s Beef?” to pay homage to the other half of Brooklyn’s finest, The Notorious B.I.G.. With Biggie’s passing, Jay-Z stands alone as the king of New York. While 6ix9ine has enjoyed Billboard chart success over the past year, Jay was nominated for 8 Grammys in 2018 alone, not to mention his status as the wealthiest man in Hip-Hop.", "annotation_id": "15907581", "url": "https://genius.com/15907581", "state": "accepted", "votes": 96, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Maybe it’s the Michael Rubins or the Robert Krafts", "vault_lines": ["Maybe it's the Michael Rubins or the Robert Krafts"], "meaning": "Michael Rubin and Robert Kraft are billionaire owners of the Philadelphia 76ers and the New England Patriots. They played an integral role in getting Meek Mill released from prison.\n\n\n\nPictured from left to right: Philly native Kevin Hart, Kraft, Rubin, and Meek.", "annotation_id": "15907517", "url": "https://genius.com/15907517", "state": "accepted", "votes": 68, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "They gave us pork and pig intestines Shit you discarded that we ingested, we made the project a wave You came back, reinvested and gentrified it Took niggas’ sense of pride, now how that’s free?", "vault_lines": ["They gave us pork and pig intestines", "Shit you discarded that we ingested, we made the project a wave", "You came back, reinvested and gentrified it", "Took niggas' sense of pride, now how that's free?"], "meaning": "Slaves were forced to eat animal parts discarded by their owners; intestines became chitterlings, butt of oxen became oxtails, and pigs' tails, pigs' feet, chicken necks, smoked neck bones, hog jowls and gizzards were all similarly renamed. This developed into what is now known as “soul food,” turning food viewed by white slave owners as useless into some of the most delicious and popular American dishes.\n\n\n\nIn other words, black folks made something out of nothing only to have it stripped away again. Jay also speaks on the hood becoming a center of art and culture because of black people, despite their destitution. Hip-Hop music and culture originated in these areas (Brooklyn, Oakland, Compton, etc) that are now being gentrified and pushing out the black folk who made them great in the first place. Rap itself began as a “newspaper” for the hood and it quickly became a platform for the wholesale exploitation of black and brown youth.\n\nThe line “we made the project a wave” could be another triple entendre from Jay, referring to\n\n\nSoul food becoming a wave in the popular sense, through its gentrification, appropriation, and fetishization.\n\nLow income housing (the projects) becoming a wave in the same sense as above, through the gentrification, appropriation, and fetishization of “hood culture.”\n\nThe process of using .wav files, the industry lossless standard, when making hip-hop projects.\n\n\n\nIn all of these instances, the main theme is ownership; their inability to keep their creations because they are stripped by White America of their identity.", "annotation_id": "15907348", "url": "https://genius.com/15907348", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Just for poppin’ a wheelie, my people march through the city", "vault_lines": ["Just for poppin' a wheelie, my people march through the city"], "meaning": "Meek was arrested in August 2017 for violating his probation for popping a wheelie on his dirt bike on the streets of Manhattan whilst not wearing a helmet.\n\nThe judge sentenced him for 2-4 years prison time, but due to the controversial – and now infamous – ruling, Meek was released in April 2018.\n\nIn this line Meek acknowledges his fans and their support, by demonstrating in the streets of his hometown – Philadelphia.\n\n\n\nA large crowd gather outside the courthouse to protest for Meek’s freedom", "annotation_id": "15907339", "url": "https://genius.com/15907339", "state": "accepted", "votes": 43, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "We so different, you thought these didn’t exist (M-M) The Megalodon never seen on his wrist", "vault_lines": ["We so different, you thought these didn't exist (M-M)", "The Megalodon never seen on his wrist"], "meaning": "Ross refers to the Megalodon wrist watch, named after the extinct Megalodon shark. Scientists theorize that they were up to 80-feet in length. No one has seen the megaladon before, so he is comparing his watch to it saying that it is so rare and expensive that you have never seen it before.", "annotation_id": "15907336", "url": "https://genius.com/15907336", "state": "accepted", "votes": 96, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "From a cell to a chopper, view from the top of the city", "vault_lines": ["From a cell to a chopper, view from the top of the city"], "meaning": "Meek Mill was released from prison on the 24th April 2018, after serving five months due to a parole violation. He was taken in a helicopter provided by his friend, Michael Rubin, co-owner of the Philadelphia 76ers.\n\nMeek boarded the chopper outside the State Correctional Institution in Chester, Pa. and flew to the Wells Fargo arena to watch game 5 of the playoffs between the Sixers and Miami Heat.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PDbUqCVrrPo", "annotation_id": "15907299", "url": "https://genius.com/15907299", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Been a top dog, that’s before the K-Dots Crackin’ in ’06, immaculate showmanship", "vault_lines": ["Been a top dog, that's before the K-Dots", "Crackin' in '06, immaculate showmanship"], "meaning": "Top Dawg Entertainment came to prominence in the 2010s, particularly with the 2012 release of good kid, m.A.A.d. city by Kendrick Lamar. Lamar previously went by the moniker K-Dot.\n\nRoss released his debut studio album Port of Miami in 2006—It debuted at number one on the charts, selling 187,000 copies in its first week alone. The album’s lead single “Hustlin'” is one of his most acclaimed songs and still influential in the Miami rap scene.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JU9TouRnO84", "annotation_id": "15907245", "url": "https://genius.com/15907245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 93, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "I survived the hood, can’t no Shaytan rob me", "vault_lines": ["I survived the hood, can't no Shaytan rob me"], "meaning": "Shaytan translates to “Satan” or “the Devil” in Persian (Farsi). On “Marcy Me” Jay says he “could prolly speak Farsi,” and he has expressed an appreciation for Persian poetry in the past, even naming his daughter after the Persian poet Rumi.", "annotation_id": "15907161", "url": "https://genius.com/15907161", "state": "accepted", "votes": 69, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Two-fifty a show and they still think I’m sellin’ crack, uh", "vault_lines": ["Two-fifty a show and they still think I'm sellin' crack, uh"], "meaning": "Despite Meek making over $250,000 per show, the Feds are still skeptical about how he acquired his wealth. A timeline of Meek’s arrest history shows that many of his crimes were not heinous, yet he served serious jail time, arguably due to the justice system’s racial bias.", "annotation_id": "15907146", "url": "https://genius.com/15907146", "state": "accepted", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "On god, it’s off the head, this improv but it’s no comedy Sign I fail? Hell nah (Ha-ha-ha-ha-ha) Hahahahahahahahaha", "vault_lines": ["On god, it's off the head, this improv but it's no comedy", "Sign I fail? Hell nah"], "meaning": "These last bars of Jay’s verse are freestyled. Improv is a theatrical technique that involves spontaneous creativity instead of acting from the script given. Jay uses wordplay with “sign-I-fail?” and the famous comedian Jerry Seinfeld (pronounced SYNE-feld) who would perform at several Improv clubs before his rise to fame. He’s also most known for his classic work on the comedy show Seinfeld—a television series that would use improv often.\n\n\n\nJay has been a dominant force in the Rap game for over twenty years along with many successful business ventures, and doesn’t see failure as an option. One statement that remains true to Jay ‘til this day as said before on his tracks “U Don’t Know” and “Change The Game”:\n\nI WILL NOT LOSE, EVER!", "annotation_id": "15907140", "url": "https://genius.com/15907140", "state": "accepted", "votes": 123, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Say “Happy Thanksgiving,” shit sound like a murder to me", "vault_lines": ["Say \"Happy Thanksgiving,\" shit sound like a murder to me"], "meaning": "While most United States citizens celebrate Thanksgiving annually as a joyful day when Europeans settled in the new world, Jay is not as naïve and sees the holiday for what it really is: a method that whitewashes the mass killing of millions of Native Americans.\n\nThe genocide is just another way caucasians have established their ways onto minority races, with the topic of the mass incarceration of minority races being the central theme of this track.\n\nThe line connects to the previous bar as turkey is a common dish served during the celebration of Thanksgiving. The jive turkeys are the people who go along with the celebration without considering the horrifying truth behind the tradition. This isn’t the first time Jay has mentioned the holiday. On Watch The Throne’s opening track “No Church in the Wild”:\n\nThanksgiving disguised as a feast\nhttps://youtu.be/FJt7gNi3Nr4?t=71", "annotation_id": "15907074", "url": "https://genius.com/15907074", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "I ain’t got a billion streams, got a billion dollars", "vault_lines": ["I ain't got a billion streams, got a billion dollars"], "meaning": "Jay-Z is currently the richest rapper in the world, recently passing Diddy’s $850 million net worth for the top spot. Hov’s net worth is an estimated $900 million and when paired with his wife Beyoncé, the couple is worth over $1.25 billion.\n\nJay-Z accumulated this wealth via millions of album sales, single sales and entrepreneurial ventures. His ventures include running labels, champagne brands, and the music-streaming service TIDAL.\n\nJay-Z removed all his music from Spotify, one of the most popular streaming services in the U.S., greatly reducing the amount of streams his music gets. He did this because is an avid advocate for artists' control over their music. Jay himself has managed to keep ownership of the vast majority of his music, so that if a service does not meet his requirements for royalty payments or promotion, his catalog can simply disappear into thin air.\n\nThe line may also be a playful jab at Drake who became the first artist to pass a billion streams in a week with his 2018 album, Scorpion. On French Montana’s “No Stylist”, Drake raps:\n\nI got the game in a squeeze\nWho disagree?\nI wanna see one of y'all run up a B\n\nJay disagrees with his line, saying that he runs a B in dollars instead of streams.", "annotation_id": "15907022", "url": "https://genius.com/15907022", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "And them people stole the soul and hit niggas with 360s, huh", "vault_lines": ["And them people stole the soul and hit niggas with 360s, huh"], "meaning": "JAY-Z is referring to record labels signing artists to 360 record deals. These are exclusive recording contracts between a record company and an artist, in which—in addition to revenue from sales of the artist’s music—the label shares in other income streams such as touring, merchandise, endorsements, filmography, and more. He equates this with selling one’s soul. Even though 360 deals are appealing due to their high upfront payouts, they offer minimal profit to the artist in the long run.", "annotation_id": "15906975", "url": "https://genius.com/15906975", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Hunnid percent of TIDAL to bust it up with my Gs, uh", "vault_lines": ["Hunnid percent of TIDAL to bust it up with my Gs, uh"], "meaning": "Jay Z shouts out his music platform Tidal, which he purchased in 2015. The streaming service is very popular among artists, as it was promoted as being the first artist-owned streaming service with the goal of returning the value proposition to music. Shown below are just some of the artists who supported the service after Jay’s acquisition.", "annotation_id": "15906955", "url": "https://genius.com/15906955", "state": "accepted", "votes": 87, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "No red hat, don’t Michael and Prince me and ’Ye They separate you when you got Michael and Prince’s DNA, uh I ain’t one of these house niggas you bought My house like a resort, my house bigger than yours My spou- (Come on, man)", "vault_lines": ["No red hat, don't Michael and Prince me and 'Ye", "They separate you when you got Michael and Prince's DNA, uh"], "meaning": "JAY-Z rejects people trying to pit him against Kanye West, even if they have opposing views about President Donald Trump. Jay even broke a year-long Twitter silence to explain this line:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/kanyewest/status/1068611802030698496\n\nKanye famously tweeted pictures of himself wearing Trump’s signature “Make America Great Again” hat. In a 2018 interview on The Van Jones Show, Jay expressed disappointment with Trump’s presidency, as well as his “shit-hole” comment regarding African countries. He also convinced Meek Mill to cancel a White House meeting with Trump, and theorized that Trump’s contentious behavior might bring the conversation about systemic racism in America to the forefront.\n\nAdditionally, these lines touch on the media’s tendency to pit legendary artists like Prince and Michael Jackson against each other. On the contrary, Jay has made his love for Kanye quite clear:\n\nThat’s my brother. We’re beyond friends. Like literally, my little brother is Kanye. And like your little brother, things happen sometimes. It’s still your sibling forever. We don’t come from the same mom and dad, but I’ve watched Kanye from [when he was] without an album.\n\n–From My Next Guest Needs No Introduction with David Letterman\n\nMoreover, “Prince” is the name of Michael Jackson’s first-born son, so the “Michael and Prince’s DNA” line may not only be a parallel between the pitting of black artists against one another, but also a reference to the practice of family separation during American slavery. Historically, some African slaves that worked in the house associated themselves with their slave master, rather than field slaves. As Jay said on “The Story Of OJ”:\n\nHouse nigga, don’t fuck with me\nI’m a field nigga, go shine cutlery\n\nLastly, Jay insinuates that both his estate and his spouse, Beyoncé, are superior to Trump’s wife and property. Ironically, from 2007 to 2017, Jay rented an apartment from President Trump.", "annotation_id": "15906917", "url": "https://genius.com/15906917", "state": "accepted", "votes": 349, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "100% of Ace of Spades, worth half a B (Uh)", "vault_lines": ["100% of Ace of Spades, worth half a B (Uh)"], "meaning": "Ace of Spades is the famous Armand de Brignac champagne brand that can sell at $850 a bottle.\n\nThanks to Jay Z purchasing the brand in 2014, Ace of Spades has become a hit at clubs, bars, and restaurants all over the world. His return on investment is huge, as the brand is currently worth half a billion ($500 million), with the “B” also alluding to his billion dollar wife, Beyoncé.", "annotation_id": "15906907", "url": "https://genius.com/15906907", "state": "accepted", "votes": 107, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "In the land of the free, where the blacks enslaved", "vault_lines": ["In the land of the free, where the blacks enslaved"], "meaning": "This introductory line is an interpolation of a couplet in “The Star Spangled Banner”:\n\nO'er the land of the free\nAnd the home of the brave\n\nAmerica is often touted as the “Land of the Free,” where democracy and equality prevails.\n\nJay-Z points out that, even today, African-Americans are disadvantaged compared to the rest of the population, largely because of a bias by the American government against them. This echoes several other statements he has made in the past. In the past, he’s criticized the War on Drugs and supported Trayvon Martin, pointing out that black Americans face significant discrimination, with law enforcement often biased against them.\n\nThis is a literal reference to what is referred to as “Modern Day Slavery” or “The New Jim Crow”. That line is not a metaphor. The New Jim Crow is a book by Michelle Alexander that shows how slavery still exists through the prison system. This shocking truth is also the subject matter of a Jay-Z produced documentary released last year. Not a metaphor, literally referring to modern day slaves.\n\nPrisons are filled with black people for petty, non-violent crimes and blacks who receive extremely harsh prison sentences for the same crimes as whites and are forced into physical labor as punishment while in prison.", "annotation_id": "15906868", "url": "https://genius.com/15906868", "state": "accepted", "votes": 104, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Three-fifths of a man, I believe’s the phrase", "vault_lines": ["Three-fifths of a man, I believe's the phrase"], "meaning": "This is a reference to the 3/5th’s compromise of the US Constitution, proposed by James Wilson and Roger Sherman at the Constitutional Convention in 1787. While counting American censuses prior to the end of slavery, each slave was counted as 3/5 of a person. In other words, for every five slaves, three people were counted in the population.\n\nThis satisfied the larger, southern states, who could use large slave population to boost national representation, while not having to pay as much in taxes (issues proportionally to the population). At the same time, it somewhat appeased opponents of slavery in the Northern states, who believed that slaves should not be counted.\n\nThis compromise, which had major benefits for state power, was at the expense of African-Americans. It dehumanized African-Americans (remember, most blacks in the United States were slaves) and set the tone for their legal disenfranchisement for nearly 2 centuries.\n\nThis continues JAY-Z’s statement of irony in the previous line: America may be the land of the free, but since its inception, African-Americans have been treated differently based on the color of their skin.", "annotation_id": "15906814", "url": "https://genius.com/15906814", "state": "accepted", "votes": 130, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "I’m 50% of D’USSÉ and it’s debt free (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I'm 50% of D'USSÉ and it's debt free (Yeah)"], "meaning": "JAY-Z is the co-owner of the D'USSÉ cognac brand, and is the company’s Head of Global Strategy.\n\nIn 2011 the rap mogul approached the Bacardi spirits company, most specifically their Château de Cognac arm, with a proposition to create a new cognac blend that would not only honor the French region’s centuries of production, but also push the boundaries of traditional cognac consumption. The resulting D'USSÉ brand was monumental for celebrity brand involvement—it was not just an endorsement deal, but a complete partnership.", "annotation_id": "15906768", "url": "https://genius.com/15906768", "state": "accepted", "votes": 92, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Pot and kilo go hand in hand like we Gamble and Huff", "vault_lines": ["Pot and kilo go hand in hand like we Gamble and Huff"], "meaning": "Gamble & Huff are a legendary songwriting and production team, known for developing the sound of Philadelphia soul in the 1970s. They were inducted into the Rock & Roll Hall of Fame in 2008 and have written and produced 175 gold and platinum records.\n\n\n\nRick Ross compares the prolific duo’s intrinsic relationship to his own relationship with drug-dealing in large quantities.", "annotation_id": "15906758", "url": "https://genius.com/15906758", "state": "accepted", "votes": 42, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Screaming “gang gang,” now you wanna rat Racketeering charges caught him on a tap Lookin’ for a bond, lawyers wanna tax Purple hair got them faggots on your back", "vault_lines": ["Screaming \"gang gang,\" now you wanna rat", "Racketeering charges caught him on a tap", "Lookin' for a bond, lawyers wanna tax", "Purple hair got them faggots on your back"], "meaning": "Ross takes a shot at rapper Tekashi 6ix9ine, who was apprehended by the FBI on November 18, 2018, on racketeering charges. Following the arrest, he pled not guilty and was denied bail. On February 1, 2019, 6ix9ine changed his plea to guilty and decided to cooperate with federal police.\n\n6ix9ine affiliated himself with the Nine Trey Gangsta Bloods, a notoriously violent organization with a significant history of FBI investigations. Tekashi also sports rainbow-colored hair, which includes purple highlights.", "annotation_id": "15906755", "url": "https://genius.com/15906755", "state": "accepted", "votes": 257, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "What’s Free", "line": "Is we beefin’ or rappin’? I might just pop up with Drizzy like", "vault_lines": ["Is we beefin' or rappin'? I might just pop up with Drizzy like"], "meaning": "In 2015, Meek feuded with Drake. It all started when Meek accused Drake of using ghostwriters. This spurred diss tracks like Drake’s “Back To Back” and Meek’s “War Pain.”\n\nIn September 2018, Drake brought Meek out at the Boston stop of his tour, signaling the two had ended their feud. They even engaged in a friendly match of Table Tennis:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/vYb6QuVxytg\n\nDrake is featured on “Going Bad”—a track on this album. Meek also spoke about their reunion in a November 2018 interview with Real 92.3 LA:\n\nMe and him had actually been in contact for a year before. But me, I’m more into the real way. When we both was ready for that, we already was in contact, and he had a tour going on. If it had been my tour I would have tried to plan it the same way. We’re just representing building bridges, it’s 2019.", "annotation_id": "15904245", "url": "https://genius.com/15904245", "state": "accepted", "votes": 149, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
