{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White House", "line": "Lemme think back to ’96 When Boobie had the realest nigga feelin’ like a bitch", "vault_lines": ["When Boobie had the realest nigga feelin' like a bitch"], "meaning": "In 1996 Ross was 18 years old and a senior at Miami’s Carol City Senior High School. He was a star offensive lineman and homecoming king, and most certainly had a relationship with the Boobie Boys cocaine trafficking gang.", "annotation_id": "1672900", "url": "https://genius.com/1672900", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White House", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-white-house-lyrics", "genius_title": "White House", "genius_id": 40237, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "I’m the new 3 and I change out to my new 3s", "vault_lines": ["I'm the new 3 and I change out to my new 3s"], "meaning": "The number 3 is significant when discussing A.I., as that was his jersey number throughout most of his career. From his college days at Georgetown, Iverson had always worn the number 3, until he was traded to the Detroit Pistons during the 2008-09 season and was forced to choose a number other than #3 because Piston guard Rodney Stuckey selected it before the start of the season; Stuckey said he’d defer it to Iverson, but the NBA didn’t allow it.\n\n\n\nThe Philadelphia 76ers officially retired his jersey in on March 1, 2014.\n\nThe second half of the line, “change out to my new 3s,” refers to A.I.’s shoes, which typically had an I3 (as in “Iverson 3”) logo on the shoe. It’s also possible he’s alluding to the Air Jordan “Cement” 3 basketball sneakers.", "annotation_id": "8560978", "url": "https://genius.com/8560978", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "And you know me, in my 2-3s and my gold teeth Bitch I’m smiling, bet you see me from the nosebleeds", "vault_lines": ["And you know me, in my 2-3s and my gold teeth", "Bitch I'm smiling, bet you see me from the nosebleeds"], "meaning": "Both Malone’s Jordan 23s and his grill are flashy enough to be noticed from afar, even from the nosebleeds, the highest (and cheapest) seating sections in stadiums.", "annotation_id": "8560977", "url": "https://genius.com/8560977", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "Double OT like I’m KD,", "vault_lines": ["Double OT like I'm KD,"], "meaning": "Transitioning from the previous line, the OT now alludes to “overtime” in basketball.\n\nKevin Durant (who also goes by KD) has had success in double overtime. In his rookie season, he hit the game-winning shot at the buzzer in double-overtime against the Hawks on November 16, 2007.\n\nLater in his career, on March 21, 2014, with the Thunder down by 2 points against the Raptors, KD he would hit another game-winning three… in double-overtime.\n\n\n\nNeedless to say, KD has proven to be clutch in double-overtime.", "annotation_id": "8560960", "url": "https://genius.com/8560960", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "I’m with some white girls and they lovin’ the coca Like they’re OT", "vault_lines": ["I'm with some white girls and they lovin' the coca"], "meaning": "White girls is slang for cocaine, which the ladies Malone’s hanging with love. There’s no way they can love cocaine as much as O.T. Genasis loves it, though, as he explains on his hit track “CoCo.”", "annotation_id": "8560958", "url": "https://genius.com/8560958", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "(This shit is hard)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Hard” is a common adjective/adverb used when you vibe to a song. During his interview with Skee TV, Malone says a dangerous drinking game would be to take a shot every time he described an artist or basketball player as “hard.”", "annotation_id": "8560955", "url": "https://genius.com/8560955", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "You know how I do it, Concords on my toes", "vault_lines": ["You know how I do it, Concords on my toes"], "meaning": "Malone rocks the highly popular Jordan Concord 11s, the essential shoe for his every day life. When he played for Georgetown (before his deal with Reebok), Iverson was known for wearing this colorway.", "annotation_id": "8560952", "url": "https://genius.com/8560952", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "Started rockin’ the sleeve, I can’t ball with no Joes", "vault_lines": ["Started rockin' the sleeve, I can't ball with no Joes"], "meaning": "This is an allusion to one of Allen Iverson’s most notable accessories: the sleeve. It was first used on January 21, 2001 against the Raptors to combat the bursitis in his shooting elbow. He wore his heart on his sleeve that game (no pun intended), scoring 51 points and proving that a little inflammation ain’t gon' stop him.\n\n\n\n(Read more about the history of “the sleeve” here.)", "annotation_id": "8560949", "url": "https://genius.com/8560949", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "I need that money like the ring I never won, I won", "vault_lines": ["I need that money like the ring I never won, I won"], "meaning": "Despite Iverson’s greatness as an individual player during his career, the ultimate marker of a great player always eluded him: an NBA Championship.", "annotation_id": "8560925", "url": "https://genius.com/8560925", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "You gon’ think about me when I’m gone", "vault_lines": ["You gon' think about me when I'm gone"], "meaning": "A lot of people still buzz over Allen Iverson, especially when his name is mentioned in the NBA today. Now that he is retired, we look back at his success and see him as a headband-wearing, 6 foot guard who reinvented the crossover.\n\n\n\nPost knows that if he were to fall off, people would miss him in the same manner as Iverson’s legacy.", "annotation_id": "8560924", "url": "https://genius.com/8560924", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "When I started ballin’ I was young", "vault_lines": ["When I started ballin' I was young"], "meaning": "Iverson began his NBA career at the age of 21 and almost immediately made a name for himself.\n\nPost is currently doing the same with his rap career. He dropped out of high school when he was 18 and moved from Dallas to LA to pursue his music interests. There he met Rich of FKi Music, the Atlanta production group that helped him created “White Iverson.”\n\nThe song leaked on the Internet in 2015, way before he intended on releasing it. When he saw it was getting some traction online, he decided to officially record it. The song blew up and got the attention of several labels. He ultimately signed with Republic Records and released a song called “Too Young.”\n\n\n\nYoung Al ballin'", "annotation_id": "8560923", "url": "https://genius.com/8560923", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "White Iverson", "vault_lines": ["Rick Ross teams up with Post Malone on this record to bring you the remix of White Iverson."], "meaning": "Allen Iverson is a retired NBA player known for being one of the best of his time. Iverson was an eleven-time NBA All-Star and won the All-Star MVP award in 2001 and 2005.\n\nSince the rap game is dominated by several Black rappers (with the exception of some like Eminem), Post calls himself the White Iverson to assert his position as one of the best in the game, even if he’s white. Allen Iverson has earned another shoutout from a white rapper in Asher Roth’s “I Love College.”", "annotation_id": "8560919", "url": "https://genius.com/8560919", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "Spendin’, I’m spendin’ all my fuckin’ pay", "vault_lines": ["Spendin', I'm spendin' all my fuckin' pay"], "meaning": "It’s common for superstar athletes to spend a good chunk of the millions they earn. In Iverson’s case, however, he smashed out. According to a 2012 article by Forbes, AI is reportedly broke.", "annotation_id": "8560912", "url": "https://genius.com/8560912", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "That’s my shot, that’s my shot, that’s my shot yeah", "vault_lines": ["That's my shot, that's my shot, that's my shot yeah"], "meaning": "“That’s my shot” is a phrase basketball players yell when they’re shooting from their most comfortable spot on the court. This song became Post’s real shot at making it as a popular rapper.\n\nPost Malone’s slurred speech in this chorus may make it seem like he’s saying “that’s my shawty.” Given all the basketball references in the song, it’s much more likely he’s saying “that’s my shot, yeah.”", "annotation_id": "8560911", "url": "https://genius.com/8560911", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "I’m ballin’, I’m ballin’, Iverson on you", "vault_lines": ["I'm ballin', I'm ballin', Iverson on you"], "meaning": "In case Iverson’s a bit too old school for modern NBA fans, this quickly explains how easily he could stunt on even the best teams, which is exactly what Malone does in this song.", "annotation_id": "8560910", "url": "https://genius.com/8560910", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "I’m swaggin’, I’m swaggin’, I’m swaggin’ oh ooh", "vault_lines": ["I'm swaggin', I'm swaggin', I'm swaggin' oh ooh"], "meaning": "We can only hope that Post Malone has more swag than Nick “Swaggy P” Young.", "annotation_id": "8560908", "url": "https://genius.com/8560908", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "Saucin’, saucin’, I’m saucin’ on you", "vault_lines": ["Saucin', saucin', I'm saucin' on you"], "meaning": "Philip Champion, known in the streetball world as “Hot Sauce,” uses an ankle-breaking crossover, similar to Allen Iverson’s.", "annotation_id": "8560906", "url": "https://genius.com/8560906", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "Iced out, my neck drip, slip and fall, I wreck shit", "vault_lines": ["Iced out, my neck drip, slip and fall, I wreck shit"], "meaning": "Rozzay loves wearing chunky jewllery with many diamonds, referred to as “ice” due to the fact that they look like ice and are cold too.\nHe is wearing them on his neck, probably in the form of a chain (and a pendant) and he is so heavy that when he slips and falls, he breaks stuff when he lands on the ground.", "annotation_id": "8060221", "url": "https://genius.com/8060221", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "Giuseppe heels, them jeans tight", "vault_lines": ["Giuseppe heels, them jeans tight"], "meaning": "Bawse is referring to Giuseppe Zanotti – high-end fashion brand that his girlfriend wears.", "annotation_id": "8060184", "url": "https://genius.com/8060184", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "Backstage of Meek concert, we smoke good, had a great time", "vault_lines": ["Backstage of Meek concert, we smoke good, had a great time"], "meaning": "Meek Mill is part of MMG, which is the record label founded by Rick Ross. It’s very usual that when a member of a label is on tour, some of the other members travel with him or hang with him when they are on the same city. So because Meek is signed to Ross' label is safe to say that they are good friends and what better time to smoke than in a backstage of your homie’s concert?", "annotation_id": "8050545", "url": "https://genius.com/8050545", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "Double M we the money team, 49 and 0 comin’ round with me", "vault_lines": ["Double M we the money team, 49 and 0 comin' round with me"], "meaning": "This is a reference to boxer Floyd “Money” Mayweather Jr. who is known for his 49-0 streak and for being a show off when it comes to his money, hence the nickname and the name of his crew “The Money Team”.\n\n\n\nHe and Rick Ross are very good friends, although there was a beef between them when Floyd let 50 Cent (with whom Ross doesn’t get along for years) shoot a video in his mansion with Ross' baby mama and his kids.\nBut that seems to be water under the bridge now, given that Rozay sent Floyd $10k worth of Luc Belaire. a day before the fight between him and Manny Pacquiao to prepare for the afterparty celebration\n\n  \n\n\nAfter Floyd won, there was some speculation surrounding the fight because a good amount of people thought that it wasn’t a fight at all because while Pacquiao was trying to hit Mayweather, he was just dodging and blocking him.\n\n\n\nBut Rozay says to Floyd let the haters hate and keep going into the next thing or fight on his career because in his eyes, he is the champ.\n\nAfter all, hater talking never made him mad.", "annotation_id": "8050285", "url": "https://genius.com/8050285", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Iverson (Remix)", "line": "[Chorus: Post Malone]", "vault_lines": ["Rick Ross teams up with Post Malone on this record to bring you the remix of White Iverson."], "meaning": "This part is identical to the one found on the original version.\n\n\n\nCheck out the orginal HERE.", "annotation_id": "8050210", "url": "https://genius.com/8050210", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
