{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Sand Part II", "line": "White Maserati, full of white girl", "vault_lines": ["White Maserati, full of white girl"], "meaning": "‘White girl’ is another term for the drug, cocaine.\nRick Ross is saying that he’s carrying a load of cocaine is his white car which was probably paid for from his cocaine selling endeavours.", "annotation_id": "16158658", "url": "https://genius.com/16158658", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Sand Part II", "line": "Majored in distribution, bags fatter than Rasputia", "vault_lines": ["Majored in distribution, bags fatter than Rasputia"], "meaning": "Rasputia was one of Eddie Murphy’s characters in Norbit. In the movie she is an incredibly heavy woman as her name has become synonymous with anything large.", "annotation_id": "1816109", "url": "https://genius.com/1816109", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Sand Part II", "line": "(White, white) I count bricks to go to sleep", "vault_lines": ["(White, white) I count bricks to go to sleep"], "meaning": "Counting sheep is a mental exercise used in some cultures as a means of lulling oneself to sleep.\n\nTorch however, counts bricks to put himself to sleep.", "annotation_id": "1450575", "url": "https://genius.com/1450575", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Sand Part II", "line": "I’m in the same boat, as Usain Bolt", "vault_lines": ["I'm in the same boat, as Usain Bolt"], "meaning": "Double entendre!\n\n\nRoss blows money as fast as Usain Bolt runs, which would be pretty fucking fast considering the fact that Usain is the world’s fastest man.\nRick Ross is in the same social setting as Usain Bolt – they’re both extremely famous, black cultural figures that dominate their respective industry.", "annotation_id": "1450427", "url": "https://genius.com/1450427", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Sand Part II", "line": "Somebody told, now somebody body cold, maricon", "vault_lines": ["Somebody told, now somebody body cold, maricon"], "meaning": "Because somebody told/snitched on him, Gunplay had to murder him.\n\nOne of the stages of death is known as Algor Mortis, which is the reduction in body temperature following death. Hence, the body getting cold.\n\nMaricon is the spanish word for ‘faggot’.", "annotation_id": "1434884", "url": "https://genius.com/1434884", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Sand Part II", "line": "Two Tecs protect my treasure while I digitally measure", "vault_lines": ["Two Tecs protect my treasure while I digitally measure"], "meaning": "Gunplay protects his treasure (possibly money, or product as he continues this line) with two TEC-9s, which are blowback-operated semi-automatic handguns.\n\n\n\nHe digitally measures his product, rather than using a triple beam dream, like his boss would.", "annotation_id": "1434878", "url": "https://genius.com/1434878", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Sand Part II", "line": "I say you’re right and pray I see you hoes on Skid Row", "vault_lines": ["I say you're right and pray I see you hoes on Skid Row"], "meaning": "Ricky agrees with those who say he’s crazy, and he hopes some day he’ll meet those people in Biscayne Boulevard, Miami, located in Miami-Dade County, Florida.\n\nSkid Row is another name for it, and it was, at least back in the 90’s, noted for it’s criminal conditions.", "annotation_id": "755860", "url": "https://genius.com/755860", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Sand Part II", "line": "Black Kel-Tec, White Prosecutor", "vault_lines": ["Black Kel-Tec, White Prosecutor"], "meaning": "Ricky apparently carries a Kel-Tec handgun. Could he favor this weapons manufacturer because they are located in his home-state? When he says “White Prosecutor” he most likely means he has had a white prosecutor against him in court.", "annotation_id": "755817", "url": "https://genius.com/755817", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Sand Part II", "line": "I put the team together like I’m Meyer Lansky", "vault_lines": ["I put the team together like I'm Meyer Lansky"], "meaning": "Meyer Lansky was one of the most powerful men in America during the 1930s, 40s, and 50s. Born in Russia in 1902, he later established himself in the world of organized crime in America. Lansky served as an associate for both the Italian and Jewish Mafia. He and Lucky Luciano brought mobsters all over the country together to form the National Crime Syndicate, which is why Rozay says this line, referring to the founding of the Maybach Music Group.", "annotation_id": "755800", "url": "https://genius.com/755800", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Sand Part II", "line": "Shit in my right hand will shorten up your lifespan", "vault_lines": ["Shit in my right hand will shorten up your lifespan"], "meaning": "The weapon he is packing will kill you (end your lifespan)", "annotation_id": "578321", "url": "https://genius.com/578321", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Sand Part II", "line": "White sand White sand White sand White sand White, white sand White, white, white sand White sand White sand", "vault_lines": ["White, white, white sand"], "meaning": "Cocaine, the drug Rick Ross & his crew deal.", "annotation_id": "578302", "url": "https://genius.com/578302", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "White Sand Part II", "status": "done", "targets": 11, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-white-sand-part-ii-lyrics", "genius_title": "White Sand Part II", "genius_id": 4843, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "Quarter-mill on my mind got me livin’ like I pitch", "vault_lines": ["Quarter-mill on my mind got me livin’ like I pitch"], "meaning": "Rodger Clemens was a professional baseball player who spent the majority of his career with the Boston Red Soxs. Ross also points out that Rodger is still in the game, having retired in 2003, but made a return in 2006 and finally retired again in 2007.\n\nWith these lines, Ross alludes to Clemens' position as a pitcher on the mound and the insane amount of money he has made. CNBC estimated how much he made in a 2010 article:\n\n\nClemens joined the Yankees on May 7 of this year, but the clock started ticking on June 9 when he got called up. Since the Yankees season is based on 182 days, that means Clemens salary of $28,000,022 was prorated to 115 days. Based on this, Clemens received a total of $17,690,413 for the season.\n\nUsing that number, here’s the breakdown:\n\nPer Pitch: $10,748\nPer Inning: $175,152\nPer Start: $982,801\nPer Win: $2,948,402", "annotation_id": "22359654", "url": "https://genius.com/22359654", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "The drinks, the minks, the jewels, the house, the cars Why’d we ball so hard?", "vault_lines": ["The drinks, the minks, the jewels, the house, the cars", "Why’d we ball so hard?"], "meaning": "John is naming off things that denote wealth and status, such as: Drinks; wealthy people often get together to drink expensive spirits to their latest accomplishments, Mink; mink fur is highly valued, and people who wear it are most certainly wealthy, Jewels; rich people, specifically women, love jewels and jewelry, House; a large house denotes wealth, Cars; John specifically says cars, plural, and having many cars is often representative of wealth.\n\n\n\nJohn then goes on to question why people seek to gain wealth so intently, when material wealth doesn’t define who we are as people, our intentions do, and John conveys this in the preceding line.", "annotation_id": "2958702", "url": "https://genius.com/2958702", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "This is our song – our song for the future", "vault_lines": ["This is our song – our song for the future"], "meaning": "From the same recording as the one in the intro.\nhttp://rapgenius.com/2774465", "annotation_id": "2774488", "url": "https://genius.com/2774488", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "When was the last time we got to be 100% real, ya’ll? I don’t want ya’ll holding back nothing!", "vault_lines": ["I don't want ya'll holding back nothing!"], "meaning": "This is from a live gig of Lenny’s signature tune, Let Love Rule. Recorded March 28th 1990 at The Paradise in Boston, this recording was featured among the bonus tracks of the Let Love Rule 20th Anniversary Edition album released in 2009.", "annotation_id": "2774465", "url": "https://genius.com/2774465", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "We’ve got a lot of nerve, girl We walk around here like we own this place", "vault_lines": ["We’ve got a lot of nerve, girl", "We walk around here like we own this place"], "meaning": "The harp is played by Brandee Younger, and I think it was tracked at Electric Lady.", "annotation_id": "2767241", "url": "https://genius.com/2767241", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "Yeah, I’m not afraid to fly Here we are, in the air, barely breathing And we’re not afraid to die Who do we think we are? Baby, tell me, who do we think we are? Who do we think we are? Baby, tell me, who do we think we are?", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I’m not afraid to fly", "Here we are, in the air, barely breathing", "And we’re not afraid to die"], "meaning": "The song samples Jean Knight’s “Mr. Big Stuff” in the background vocals. Additionally, chord structure is borrowed from Marvin Gaye’s “If I Should Die Tonight.”\n\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v758jdrAlZM", "annotation_id": "2767237", "url": "https://genius.com/2767237", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "[Produced by Bink! & Co]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Co-executive producer of the album Dave Tozer spoke to The Source Magazine on producing the track:\n\nI think of that as one of the more classic soul productions on the record, but again combined with hip-hop…And Rick’s voice works perfectly as an instrument on that song. It’s where his vocal timbre lies. So that worked out really well. And we had some really interesting arrangement ideas on that – the Mellotron strings that are playing in the melodies during the Rick Ross verse, and Brandee Younger playing real harp on that…It’s almost like there are Baroque instruments on it. And one of the things I like about a lot of classic soul music and classic Curtis Mayfield is that you have this interesting combination of sounds. We have this sort of black American soul music embedded in the rhythm section of the drums and bass and piano and guitars, playing this funky 70’s era funk music. But then on top of that, you have European-inspired orchestras you know – strings, horns, French horns, harps, all over doing the glissandos that they do. And so we have a lot of that on that recording and that’s why it reminds me a lot of the classic semi-soul records, the Curtis Mayfield stuff, later Motown. It’s all over the “What’s Going On” album, Marvin Gaye…So I’m inspired by that.", "annotation_id": "2766640", "url": "https://genius.com/2766640", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "We could fall so hard", "vault_lines": ["We could fall so hard"], "meaning": "A play on the double meaning of “stars”, there are a few ways you can interpret this line. 1. First of all, people make wishes on falling stars, and this can be an allusion to all the people wishing John Legend and his crew would fall so they can take their place. 2. People fall hard for their celebrities, which is part of the magic/delusion. 3. The higher one rises, the harder the fall. This could be a warning to the rapidly rising stars to watch their backs.", "annotation_id": "1906498", "url": "https://genius.com/1906498", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "Ski trips to Vail", "vault_lines": [", ski trips to Vail"], "meaning": "Vail Mountain Resort is a high-end ski resort in the Colorado Rockies", "annotation_id": "1768848", "url": "https://genius.com/1768848", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "She get Chanel", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Chanel is a high end designer brand", "annotation_id": "1768847", "url": "https://genius.com/1768847", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "Eggplant double breasted suit from Tom Ford", "vault_lines": ["Eggplant double breasted suit from Tom Ford"], "meaning": "Tom Ford is an american fashion designer and film director.\n\nAmong his portfolio of designs, Rick chose to show out in an egg plant colored suit.", "annotation_id": "1745981", "url": "https://genius.com/1745981", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "Pink champagne,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Ace of Spades, which is Moet’s Nectar Imperial Rose is one of Rick’s drinks of choice, alongside Rozay.", "annotation_id": "1745953", "url": "https://genius.com/1745953", "state": "accepted", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "We made a lot of magic It’s the only way we know Let’s trip the night fantastic Light it up, tell me more, explore We love, we love, we love the stars", "vault_lines": ["We made a lot of magic", "It’s the only way we know", "Let’s trip the night fantastic", "Light it up, tell me more, explore", "We love, we love, we love the stars"], "meaning": "These lines feel pretty poetic, and are brilliant in a way:\n\nAt the surface, it might seem like idle chit-chat between John and his lady in an attempt to get more familiar with each other; “exploring” one another sort to speak. And they both discover there adoration for the stars.\n\nHowever, the genius of the lines lie at how he blends it all together. He starts with revealing this is taking place at night, but then he follows it by “light it up” which really fits well with the theme of the night, as night is dark, and light brings less dark. Then, he says “explore” which is what light does in a sense, it brings the unknown visible. Then he ties the ribbon eloquently when his mentions the “stars” which also blends well, as stars light up the sky at night and are explored as well.", "annotation_id": "1617920", "url": "https://genius.com/1617920", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "Who Do We Think We Are", "line": "Dodging Robin Givens,", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Rick Ross isn’t literally avoiding Robin Givens, but uses her as an example for those who “cry rape” as did Robin Givens, ex-wife of Mike Tyson.\n\nPeople accuse her of having married Mike to get at his money. She filed for divorce after three months alleging spousal abuse and was reported to have received a divorce settlement of over $10million.", "annotation_id": "1615110", "url": "https://genius.com/1615110", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
