{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Don’t Want These Problems", "line": "Catfish in the Benz, Manti Teo’s a sucker", "vault_lines": ["Catfish in the Benz, Manti Teo's a sucker"], "meaning": "Can be double entendre for Catfish in the Bend, referring to South Bend, IN, where former Notre Dame football star Manti Te'o was caught up in a Catfish, internet dating hoax.", "annotation_id": "2320008", "url": "https://genius.com/2320008", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Don’t Want These Problems", "line": "You’re now rockin’ with the best,", "vault_lines": ["You're now rockin' with the best,"], "meaning": "Khaled commonly mentions the fact that he thinks he and his crew are “the best”. He points this out on song shoutouts, and his albums We The Best and We The Best Forever.", "annotation_id": "2317457", "url": "https://genius.com/2317457", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Don’t Want These Problems", "line": "TRUUUU! You just second best, Kelly Rowland (Hahaha, I’m just playin’ babe)", "vault_lines": ["TRUUUU! You just second best, Kelly Rowland"], "meaning": "You will never be as good, or as famous as 2 Chainz.\n\nWhen he mentions Kelly Rowland he is referring to her being looked as the second best in the Destiny’s Child group, behind Beyoncé.\n\n\n\nAlso, for whatever reason; the second Kelly Rowland namecheck by two different rappers on the same song.", "annotation_id": "2316168", "url": "https://genius.com/2316168", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Don’t Want These Problems", "line": "Teflon’s on the rise,", "vault_lines": ["Teflon's on the rise,"], "meaning": "John Gotti was ‘The Teflon Don’ – head of the New York Gambino Family.  Gotti and his brothers come up from poverty. A real rags to riches story.\n\nRick Ross' 4th studio album was called The Teflon Don . Rick Ross is now calling himself The Teflon Don of this rap game.", "annotation_id": "2316120", "url": "https://genius.com/2316120", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Don’t Want These Problems", "line": "VIP section, started out from section 8", "vault_lines": ["VIP section, started out from section 8"], "meaning": "Section 8 is a program for low-income people who wish to live in privately owned housing and receive rental assistance.  The term “Section 8” is used because the federal law which created the program is Section 8 of the Housing and Community Development Act of 1974. It is also funded by the government.\n\n2 Chainz is showing his progress by telling us where he started, and where he is now. He is now popping bottles in the VIP Section of the clubs he goes to, which is a sing of wealth. And he’s loving them strippers in the club.", "annotation_id": "2315830", "url": "https://genius.com/2315830", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Don’t Want These Problems", "line": "Camouflage Mac-11, I should set an example", "vault_lines": ["Camouflage Mac-11, I should set an example"], "meaning": "The MAC-11 (Military Armament Corporation Model 11) is a subcompact machine pistol developed by American gun designer Gordon Ingram at the Military Armament Corporation (MAC) during the 1970s. Rick Ross is literally showing off his guns. Not these guns, but an actual sub machine gun. Ross is asking if he should show what he’s made of. We do know that Rick Ross is a gun freak, as he says in Ali Bomaye:\n\nI got rifles with lasers, swing it just like the majors", "annotation_id": "2315725", "url": "https://genius.com/2315725", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Don’t Want These Problems", "line": "Out in Vegas like Lamar, third floor Tropicana", "vault_lines": ["Out in Vegas like Lamar, third floor Tropicana"], "meaning": "Kendrick Lamar had a line in his “Unanymous vs Youthoracle (Round Two)” that went something like this:\n\nYou ain’t get killed in Vegas or in a Suburban\n\nThe second portion of this line, “third floor Tropicana”, refers to a very popular hotel and casino in Vegas called Tropicana. Rick Ross also mentioned Vegas in his popular single “Diced Pinapples”:\n\nBelt buckles, door handles, gold plated\nBalmain, rich denim, out Vegas\n\nRoss go big or go home guys:", "annotation_id": "2315287", "url": "https://genius.com/2315287", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Don’t Want These Problems", "line": "Uh, renegade like Em and Jay", "vault_lines": ["Uh, renegade like Em and Jay"], "meaning": "A renegade is one who rejects a religion, cause, allegiance, or group for another; a deserter or an outlaw; or rebel. We all know 2 Chainz religion is T.R.U. REALigion\n\n2 Chainz pays tribute to two of the greats, Eminem and Jay Z who made a song together called “Renegade”.", "annotation_id": "2315135", "url": "https://genius.com/2315135", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Don’t Want These Problems", "status": "done", "targets": 8, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Dj-khaled-you-dont-want-these-problems-lyrics", "genius_title": "You Don’t Want These Problems", "genius_id": 238460, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Know I Got It (Reprise)", "line": "Look at me, I’m pure nigga", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "A cocaine pun – Ross makes plenty of dope, both music and cocaine. But don’t take my word for it:\n\nI can make cocaine\n\nRoss is also referencing how real he is and could also referring to his race/ethnicity, considering the Pimp C intro.", "annotation_id": "14536623", "url": "https://genius.com/14536623", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Know I Got It (Reprise)", "line": "Geechi nigga with satin sheets", "vault_lines": ["Geechi nigga with satin sheets"], "meaning": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_embedded&v=6lBzkFjdJrQ\n\n“Geechee” or “Geechy” refers to the Gullah Geechee people who are direct descendants of African slaves that inhabit the barrier sea islands off the coast of Georgia, North and South Carolina, and Florida. Ross usually uses the word in reference to being country or ghetto.\n\nSatin is known as a luxurious, silky smooth material for bed sheets. Ross has the disposable income to use on a good night’s sleep. So basically, Ross is a luxurious hood nigga.\n\n\n\nAlso a reference to Wale’s Chain Music.", "annotation_id": "14536616", "url": "https://genius.com/14536616", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Know I Got It (Reprise)", "line": "Reeboks on, I just do it, nigga", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "“Just Do It” is the slogan of Reebok competitor, Nike. The fact that Ross is referencing Nike’s slogan could be ironic, or it could be a reference to the endorsement deal he once had with the company.\n\n\n\nThis verse was recorded earlier in 2013 (presumably in March, when he instagrammed his studio session with Hov) while Ross was still endorsed by Reebok. However, the song was released in July, a few months after he had been dropped from his Reebok endorsement for his now infamous molly raps.\n\nDespite being dropped from Reebok, Ross maintains a good relationship with them. After this song dropped in late July, Ross reiterated his good relationship with Reebok, saying that he would “make an announcement when the time is right”. Ross' good relations with Reebok is perhaps why Ross left these lyrics unchanged. Rumors surfaced that Ross had resigned with Reebok after being pictured with their CEO.", "annotation_id": "14536612", "url": "https://genius.com/14536612", "state": "accepted", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Know I Got It (Reprise)", "line": "Shoppin’ bags, I’m a tourist, nigga", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "It’s not uncommon for a tourist to shop when on vacation, and of course Rick Ross has Money Bags to spend. He can just enjoy his time in Europe shopping and blowing some money!\n\nMost of the luxury brands that rappers idolize come from Europe. The rich from all around the world come to Europe to buy the best in luxury brands:\n\n\nFrance: Louis Vuitton, Cartier, YSL, Louboutin etc.\nItaly: Gucci, Prada, Fendi, Dolce & Gabbana etc.\nUK: Burberry, Manolo, Jimmy Choo, Alexander McQueen etc.\nSwitzerland: Audemars Piguet, Rolex, Bally, Hublot etc.\n\n\n\nBy shopping in Europe, Rick Ross is implying that he’s actually spending a lot of money on luxury brands, not just rapping about it.\n\nSince he is a tourist, Europe is far from home. Knowing the struggle most black people from poor backgrounds have, Rick could also be implying as a “tourist” he shouldn’t even be there with the circumstances he has faced in life. But since he is there, he may as well ball.", "annotation_id": "14536611", "url": "https://genius.com/14536611", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Know I Got It (Reprise)", "line": "[Intro: Pimp C & Rick Ross] “A little over a year ago I was in bondage, and now I’m back out here reaping the blessings and getting the benefits that go along with it, everything that’s out here for kings like us. The reason why we like this, this jewelry and this diamonds and stuff, they don’t understand is, because we really from Africa, and that’s where all this stuff come from. And we originated from kings, you know what I’m saying? So don’t look down on the youngsters because they wanna have shiny things. It’s in our genes, know what I’m saying? We just don’t all know our history, so peace to the Midwest. Keep it P-I, you know what I’m saying?” I’m a just keep you up on this pimp shit, see what I’m saying?", "vault_lines": ["I'm a just keep you up on this pimp shit, see what I'm saying?"], "meaning": "The intro features a recording of late Southern rapper Pimp C talking about his period of being incarcerated, and trying to get back acclimated to the world on the outside of a prison cell.\n\nHe goes on to explain that the reason a lot of black people try to wear diamonds and flashy jewelry is because we come from a long line of kings in our African ancestry. He explains that it’s “in our genes” to try to wear jewelry because all of us black men are kings and we can’t help it. If you look at Africa’s natural resources, most countries were rich in gold and silver! Reason why Europeans wanted to take over the continent.\n\nBut asking why rappers always talk about their stuff is like asking why Milton is forever listing the attributes of heavenly armies. Because boasting is a formal condition of the epic form.\n\nHere is the video of the interview. Skip to 1:13. Allegedly, this was his last interview before he died.\nhttp://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_embedded&v=JK3HLJk8iMY", "annotation_id": "14536602", "url": "https://genius.com/14536602", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Know I Got It (Reprise)", "line": "[Chorus]", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "If you listen closely you can hear a bill counter in the background of this hook. It refers to what he said about counting money with Hov which he said in his last verse.\n\nFornicating, counting money with a fuck face", "annotation_id": "2888144", "url": "https://genius.com/2888144", "state": "accepted", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Know I Got It (Reprise)", "line": "Top down in my white Corniche Ever see these Hermès seats?", "vault_lines": ["Top down in my white Corniche", "Ever see these Hermès seats?"], "meaning": "Corniche refers to Rolls-Royce Corniche, a discontinued Rolls Royce model. Rozay is known for his love and addiction to discontinued cars – he pimped his out with custom Hermès seats.", "annotation_id": "2885294", "url": "https://genius.com/2885294", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Know I Got It (Reprise)", "line": "Peelin’ paper like a bank teller Me and Hova in the bank, hella", "vault_lines": ["Peelin' paper like a bank teller", "Me and Hova in the bank, hella"], "meaning": "Both with net worths of over a few mill, Jay Z and Rick Ross both can handle money like a bank person.\n\nIt’s only right Rick mentions Jay on this due to the fact that the original track was on Magna Carta… Holy Grail\n\nCome money dance with the good fellas\nHov keep gettin' that dinero, got it", "annotation_id": "2885219", "url": "https://genius.com/2885219", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Know I Got It (Reprise)", "status": "done", "targets": 8, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Rick-ross-you-know-i-got-it-reprise-lyrics", "genius_title": "You Know I Got It (Reprise)", "genius_id": 365960, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "That’s why I’m movin’ all elusive These boys on they last resort and it’s givin’ us all inclusive", "vault_lines": ["These boys on they last resort and it's givin' us all inclusive"], "meaning": "Drake here drops a double entendre. The other guys competing with Drake are now on their last legs after all their other attempts have failed. But when he says all inclusive, he also shows the gap between him and his competition financially as an all-inclusive resort is much cheaper than the non-inclusive resorts Drake stays at.", "annotation_id": "37868394", "url": "https://genius.com/37868394", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "Yayo so clean, I told ’em call it, “Ajax”", "vault_lines": ["Yayo so clean, I told 'em call it, \"Ajax\""], "meaning": "“Yayo” is slang for cocaine, and Ajax is a brand of household cleaner. When cocaine is considered “clean,” it’s in its purest form—meaning it isn’t cut with any other substances like baking soda or talcum powder to save costs. Essentially, Ross only sells high quality product.", "annotation_id": "37256829", "url": "https://genius.com/37256829", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "Damn, how can I forgive like this? I gotta dead a lot of shit just to live like this I had to fuck a lot of girls to get a kid like this I had to get a lot of cribs to get a crib like this", "vault_lines": ["Damn, how can I forgive like this?", "I gotta dead a lot of shit just to live like this", "I had to fuck a lot of girls to get a kid like this", "I had to get a lot of cribs to get a crib like this"], "meaning": "Drake is using the word “crib” as a double entendre. Initially, it refers to houses or luxury homes, suggesting that he had to work hard, invest, and buy multiple properties to be able to afford his current lifestyle. Metaphorically, it reflects the idea that achieving a high level of success requires gaining experience, and putting in effort and resources over time.\n\nDrake currently owns a $100-million mansion called The Embassy in his hometown of Toronto, which is the “crib” he is likely referring to in this line.", "annotation_id": "34114548", "url": "https://genius.com/34114548", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "All my chains look like snakes, that’s some real diamondbacks", "vault_lines": ["All my chains look like snakes, that's some real diamondbacks"], "meaning": "Weezy F shows off his vintage wordplay here, comparing his collection of snake chains to that of a Diamondback Rattlesnake. More people are bitten by the Western Diamondback each year than any other snake in the USA.", "annotation_id": "26461719", "url": "https://genius.com/26461719", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "Brrrt, may I speak to pussy niggas? I just call it like I see it, I ain’t even lookin’, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Brrrt, may I speak to pussy niggas?", "I just call it like I see it, I ain't even lookin', nigga"], "meaning": "This could be a callback to a lyric on Wayne’s December 2010 hit, “6 Foot 7 Foot,” where he raps:\n\nAnd I call it like I see it, and my glasses on", "annotation_id": "25145559", "url": "https://genius.com/25145559", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "I got sentenced, took some time and it was easier than simple", "vault_lines": ["I got sentenced, took some time and it was easier than simple"], "meaning": "In March 2010 Wayne got sentenced to 1 year in prison at Rikers Island for a weapons charge from a July 2007 incident. While Wayne was away there was a big ‘Free Weezy’ movement in the hip-hop world, and he was released in November after serving 8 months, going right back to running the rap game.\n\nWhile in jail, Wayne was mostly held in protective custody away from other prisoners, and his album I Am Not a Human Being became the first album since 2Pac’s 1995 album Me Against The World to reach the top position on the Billboard 200 while its artist was in prison. Safe to say his jail time was fairly easy.", "annotation_id": "24032128", "url": "https://genius.com/24032128", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "Yeah, they plottin’ on me but they gotta do somethin’ Told my dawg two-somethin’ just to shoot somethin’ (M-M—) Left them niggas stretched out on the train tracks Rather you than me, here come that payback Got ‘em lookin’ for that paper that they can’t tax", "vault_lines": ["Rather you than me, here come that payback", "Got 'em lookin' for that paper that they can't tax"], "meaning": "Ross likely makes a nod here back to his ninth studio album, Rather You Than Me which was released in March 2017.", "annotation_id": "23987829", "url": "https://genius.com/23987829", "state": "accepted", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "Whip that big body Maybach like it’s a little Pontiac", "vault_lines": ["Whip that big body Maybach like it's a little Pontiac"], "meaning": "In his video for “Laugh Now Cry Later,” Drake displays concept cars.\nhttps://twitter.com/WordOnRd/status/1294162910932078592?s=20", "annotation_id": "23879902", "url": "https://genius.com/23879902", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "Hialeah celebrated when Fidel died", "vault_lines": ["Hialeah celebrated when Fidel died"], "meaning": "Hialeah, the sixth largest city in Florida, has the highest percentage of Cuban and Cuban American residents of any city in the United States, at 73.37% of the population, making them a typical and prominent feature of the city’s culture.\n\nWhen Cuban dictator Fidel Castro died, the city erupted into a huge street party.", "annotation_id": "23878911", "url": "https://genius.com/23878911", "state": "pending", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "Yeah, two-point-two for the Rafael Nadal", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, two-point-two for the Rafael Nadal"], "meaning": "This is likely a reference to the limited edition RM 27-04 watch made by Swiss watch company Richard Mille in partnership with Spanish Tennis player Rafael Nadal. In July 2021, Drake is known to have purchased one of these watches, of which only 50 were made available to the public. The watch was made to celebrate the 10 year anniversary of Richard Mille’s sponsorship of Nadal, who himself is known to wear some incredibly expensive sports watches during his Tennis matches.\n\n‘Two point two’ is most likely referring to a price of $2.2 million that Drake paid for the watch. The word ‘point’ could also be referring to a point in a game of Tennis.\n\n\n\nhttps://www.instagram.com/p/CQ-ZPPJjmPj/?utm_source=ig_embed\n\nDrake wearing the watch\n\n\n\nRafael Nadal wearing the watch", "annotation_id": "23876337", "url": "https://genius.com/23876337", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "Unthinkable when I think of the way these niggas been actin’ Yeah, I never did you nothin’ and you play like we family, huh? Next thing, you wanna shoot me down, it can’t be love Not sure where you was tryna send it, it can’t be up That day you sounded like a bitch, you fancy, huh?", "vault_lines": ["Unthinkable when I think of the way these niggas been actin'", "Yeah, I never did you nothin' and you play like we family, huh?", "Next thing, you wanna shoot me down, it can't be love", "Not sure where you was tryna send it, it can't be up", "That day you sounded like a bitch, you fancy, huh?"], "meaning": "Drake is sending shots at Swizz Beatz, who reportedly did an Instagram Live with Busta Rhymes back in June 2020 while being intoxicated and seemingly called Drake a “pussy” over a possible record leaking and threatened to “shooting down” Drizzy’s newly acquired private jet. Even though Swizz did apologize the next day while saying “it’s all love”, Drizzy still remember the disrespect.\n\nSo here, Drake strung together “Unthinkable”, the title of a song he wrote with Alicia Keys, who is Swizz’s wife and interpolated ”Fancy”, a song from his own album Thank Me Later that he featured Swizz Beatz on the chorus.", "annotation_id": "23876246", "url": "https://genius.com/23876246", "state": "accepted", "votes": 72, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "Her ears popped, she lick my lollipop and my teardrops, Tunechi", "vault_lines": ["Her ears popped, she lick my lollipop and my teardrops, Tunechi"], "meaning": "Here Lil Wayne is suggesting she was so into his music, she “licked” him down below and on the face.\n\nLil Wayne has tear drop tattoos on his face. They’re controversial because a teardrop on the face can represent someone you’ve killed – Wayne has avoided this fact. https://www.stotkertattoo.com/tips/why-does-lil-wayne-have-a-teardrop-tattoo.html\n\nHe also released “Lollipop” on “Tha Carter III” in 2008:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2IH8tNQAzSs", "annotation_id": "23875398", "url": "https://genius.com/23875398", "state": "pending", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "I got bitches doin’ lines, I’m Adidas to ’em, nigga", "vault_lines": ["I got bitches doin' lines, I'm Adidas to 'em, nigga"], "meaning": "Lil Wayne is suggesting he has girls “doin' lines” which could be a reference to cocaine. He is comparing himself to adidas as they are iconic for everyone wearing their 3 lines. Also they release successful lines of clothing.", "annotation_id": "23875379", "url": "https://genius.com/23875379", "state": "pending", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "Been spazzin’ since CB was chunkin’ up the deuces, nigga Your shit was boo-boo, excuses, excuses, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Been spazzin' since CB was chunkin' up the deuces, nigga", "Your shit was boo-boo, excuses, excuses, nigga"], "meaning": "Chris Brown dropped the popular track “Deuces” in 2011 which Drake also featured on the remix. Drake is implying he has been doing his thing for over ten years now.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=11aMClGbrKE", "annotation_id": "23875368", "url": "https://genius.com/23875368", "state": "accepted", "votes": 49, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "M-M—, M-M—", "vault_lines": ["Back to the real niggas glowin' in the dark (M-M—)"], "meaning": "This is the famous adlib for Maybach Music Group – voiced by Jessica Gomes.", "annotation_id": "23875355", "url": "https://genius.com/23875355", "state": "accepted", "votes": 59, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "Catalog is immaculate Still runnin’ the game, don’t ask me about the practice", "vault_lines": ["Still runnin' the game, don't ask me about the practice"], "meaning": "Describing his dominance over the rap industry, Drake references NBA Hall of Famer Allen Iverson’s infamous “practice” press conference from 2002. When a reporter asked Iverson about missing one practice, he expressed his displeasure at the question, responding with the following:\n\nWe’re talking about practice. Not a game! Not a game! Not a game! We’re talking about practice. Not a game; not the game that I go out there and die for and play every game like it’s my last, not the game, we’re talking about practice, man. I mean, how silly is that? We’re talking about practice.\n\nAs a basketball icon and NBA MVP, Iverson was upset that his premier status and willingness to practice were called into question. The lengthy nature of Iverson’s response also may have been triggered by the murder of his best friend, Rahsaan Langeford, seven months prior to the press conference. Like AI, Drake finds that his position or “run” atop the rap game will only continue and that “running” in practice is not of other’s concern.", "annotation_id": "23875131", "url": "https://genius.com/23875131", "state": "accepted", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "Patti LaBelle, who knew that we would sell pies?", "vault_lines": ["Patti LaBelle, who knew that we would sell pies?"], "meaning": "Patti LaBelle is an American R&B and Soul artist known for  songs such as “If Only You Knew” and “On My Own.” In 2015, she began selling sweet potato pies at Walmart stores throughout the United States. Following a viral review from social media personality James Wright, the pies quickly sold out. In the years following the video, the pies continue to be a commercial success, with more than 36,000 sold each day.\n\nRoss uses pies as a double entendre to reference Patti LaBelle’s sweet potato dessert and drug trafficking. A pie is a slang term for a kilogram of cocaine, an amount which he expresses disbelief in his ability to sell. Ross himself drew his rap name from drug kingpin “Freeway” Ricky Ross, who established an extensive distribution network throughout Los Angeles in the 1980s and ‘90s.", "annotation_id": "23874932", "url": "https://genius.com/23874932", "state": "accepted", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Rick Ross", "song": "You Only Live Twice", "line": "Ho, you go on vacation, don’t ask me about relaxin’ Not sure if you know but I’m actually Michael Jackson The man I see in the mirror is actually goin’ platinum", "vault_lines": ["Ho, you go on vacation, don't ask me about relaxin'", "Not sure if you know but I'm actually Michael Jackson", "The man I see in the mirror is actually goin' platinum"], "meaning": "Drake compares his success in the music industry to legendary pop icon Michael Jackson. Jackson is remembered today for his live performances, his memorable record releases and the consistency of his music after departing from the The Jackson 5 group to start a solo career. After the King of Pop’s untimely death in 2009, his legacy was succeeded by his posthumous music, including his appearance on Drake’s 2018 album Scorpion, on the track “Don’t Matter to Me.” The song samples a once unreleased Jackson track and highlighted the similarity of both singers' careers. In 2016, Drake broke MJ’s record for most American Music Award nominations, establishing both as significant figures at the top of their respective genres.\n\nInterestingly enough, Drake boasts an appearance on the 2016 2 Chainz track “Big Amount,” rapping:\n\nMichael Jackson talkin' to me in my dreams\nAnd he say, “You bad and you know it”\nBetter shamone with my check then\nI’m a J. Prince investment\n\nIn those lines, Drake mentions the hit 1987 single “Bad,” similar to how he slyly mentions Jackson’s “Man in the Mirror” from 1988 on this track. Here, Drake uses the track’s title to boast his countless platinum certifications and is crowned as the top certified singles artist of the RIAA.", "annotation_id": "23874414", "url": "https://genius.com/23874414", "state": "accepted", "votes": 61, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
